{"name": "Middleware Trap Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A five-step structured test to determine whether an AI company's position is structurally durable or merely renting its place in the stack by mapping dependencies and evaluating each against hyperscaler absorption risk.", "when_to_apply": ["Our main model provider just announced features that overlap with our core product and I need to assess how exposed we are", "I'm evaluating an AI startup for investment and need to determine if their defensibility is real or just a thin wrapper around someone else's capability", "We build on top of a foundation model API and I need to present our board with an honest assessment of platform risk", "Our differentiation story is 'domain expertise' but I'm not sure that holds up against what the big cloud providers are building", "We need to decide whether to double down on our current stack position or pivot before a hyperscaler absorbs our layer"], "principles": ["If you don't own the layer below you or the customer relationship above you, you are borrowing time", "Every dependency must be evaluated for both provider incentive and provider ability to absorb your functionality", "Domain context claims must be stress-tested against the three-layer taxonomy before accepting them as moats", "Analyze your position from the hyperscaler's perspective: do you generate more tokens for them, or sit between them and a customer they could serve directly?", "The goal is to find positions where hyperscaler incentives align with your existence rather than your replacement"], "steps": ["Draw your stack: map every dependency your product requires \u2014 model, cloud, data sources, distribution channels", "For each dependency, ask two questions: Does the provider have the incentive and ability to absorb my functionality? Is the provider now building the context layer I thought was my moat?", "For each 'yes,' ask what you still own if that provider moves into your space \u2014 distinguish borrowed value (UX, integrations) from domain context, then classify that context using the three-layer test (structural, operational, or proprietary)", "Check your remaining position against the four durable positions: proprietary context that can't rationally be platformized, infrastructure agents depend on, workflow depth with real switching costs, or trust and verification", "Look at your position from the hyperscaler's perspective: does your existence generate more tokens for them, or do you sit between them and a customer they could serve directly at higher margin?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189945797", "title": "Most AI companies are renting their position. These 4 prompts tell you if yours is one of them.", "date": "2026-03-19T13:04:27.195Z"}], "date": "2026-03-19T13:04:27.195Z", "id": "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "core_question": "Is my AI company building something structurally durable, or am I just renting a position that a hyperscaler will absorb the moment it becomes worth their attention?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The company's entire product sits on top of OpenAI's API, adds a fine-tuned workflow for legal contract review, and sells to mid-market law firms. Walking through the stack: model dependency is total (OpenAI), cloud is AWS, distribution is direct sales. OpenAI has both incentive and ability to build a legal template layer \u2014 they've already shipped custom GPTs. The claimed moat is 'we understand legal workflows,' but under the three-layer test, the context is structural (publicly available contract templates and clause libraries), not proprietary. What survives if OpenAI moves? The customer relationships with 200 law firms and 18 months of their marked-up clause data. That's real but thin \u2014 it's operational context at best, and the switching cost is low because firms haven't restructured their workflows around the tool. From the hyperscaler's perspective, this company sits directly between OpenAI and a high-value vertical they'd love to serve at higher margin. Verdict: this is a rented position with a 12-18 month window before absorption risk becomes acute.", "related_frameworks": ["three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "the-weekend-hack-test", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-context-platform-compound", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "two-ai-economies-framework", "the-distribution-trap", "building-vs-buying-ai-response"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Layer Enterprise Context Taxonomy", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that sorts enterprise domain context into three tiers \u2014 structural, operational, and proprietary \u2014 each with a distinct defensibility profile and recommended strategic response.", "when_to_apply": ["We're deciding what enterprise data to share with our AI platform partner versus what to keep in-house", "Our vertical AI startup claims 'domain expertise' as the moat \u2014 but I need to know how long that actually lasts as foundation models improve", "A company in our portfolio says their institutional knowledge is their defensibility, but I suspect most of it is just system integration knowledge that platforms will absorb", "We're negotiating a partnership with an AI infrastructure provider and need to know which data creates competitive exposure if shared", "I'm trying to figure out whether our operational knowledge is actually proprietary or just not-yet-commoditized"], "principles": ["Structural context (system connections, permissions, data locations) is commodity plumbing that platform providers absorb within 12\u201324 months", "Operational context defensibility is determined by rate of change: slowly-updating operational knowledge loses its window within one to two model generations; rapidly-updating knowledge stays defensible longer", "Proprietary context (data and judgment that exists nowhere else and would create competitive exposure if shared) is the only tier worth defending as a strategic moat", "Platforming proprietary context means giving the provider access to the thing that makes you competitively unique \u2014 today that is a partnership, in future model generations it may not be"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189945797", "title": "Most AI companies are renting their position. These 4 prompts tell you if yours is one of them.", "date": "2026-03-19T13:04:27.195Z"}], "date": "2026-03-19T13:04:27.195Z", "id": "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "core_question": "When we say our 'domain context' is our moat against platform providers, which parts of that context are actually defensible and which will be absorbed into the platform within two years?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The company's claimed moat is 'deep understanding of healthcare billing workflows' \u2014 but decomposed through the taxonomy, most of that is structural context (which EHR systems connect to which clearinghouses, permission models, data locations) that any platform provider will absorb once they ship native integrations. The operational knowledge \u2014 billing code selection heuristics, denial pattern recognition \u2014 updates slowly enough that it's one model generation from commoditization. The only genuinely proprietary tier is their accumulated dataset of 200,000 real denial-overturn outcomes paired with the clinical judgment calls that drove them. That dataset exists nowhere else and would create competitive exposure if shared with a model provider. Strategic response: platform the structural and slow-updating operational layers freely, but the denial-overturn dataset is the thing worth defending \u2014 and platforming it means handing the model provider the one asset that makes the company irreplaceable.", "related_frameworks": ["middleware-trap-diagnostic", "four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "the-context-platform-compound", "the-weekend-hack-test", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-three-layer-analysis", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "double-compression-loop"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four Durable Positions Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of the four AI stack positions where non-hyperscaler companies can build structurally durable businesses because the hyperscalers' incentives align with their existence rather than their replacement.", "when_to_apply": ["Our product works today but I keep worrying that OpenAI or Google will just build this into their platform next quarter", "We're an AI startup trying to decide which layer of the stack to build our core product around for long-term defensibility", "Evaluating whether a portfolio company's AI positioning survives the next two rounds of platform consolidation", "We've been told we're in the middleware trap and need to figure out which adjacent durable position to migrate toward", "A founder is pitching us on AI infrastructure \u2014 how do I tell if their position gets stronger or weaker as the hyperscalers advance?"], "principles": ["Position 1 (Proprietary Context): Survive only in the proprietary context tier \u2014 structural and slowly-changing operational context will be absorbed by platform providers", "Position 2 (Agent Infrastructure): Becoming a service that agents call regardless of who wins the orchestration war is more durable than competing in orchestration directly", "Position 3 (Workflow Depth): Integration depth that encodes institutional workflows creates switching costs the model provider cannot easily replicate \u2014 value a product that gets harder to remove over time, not just better to use", "Position 4 (Trust and Verification): The governance gap between agent capability adoption and auditability is the largest currently unoccupied structural position in the stack", "Companies should target one of these four positions; straddling exposed middleware while also holding a durable position (as Perplexity does) creates asymmetric risk"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189945797", "title": "Most AI companies are renting their position. These 4 prompts tell you if yours is one of them.", "date": "2026-03-19T13:04:27.195Z"}], "date": "2026-03-19T13:04:27.195Z", "id": "four-durable-positions-framework", "core_question": "Where in the AI stack can my company build a position that hyperscalers are incentivized to support rather than replace?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This compliance automation startup sits in Position 4 \u2014 Trust and Verification. Every time a hyperscaler ships more powerful agents, the governance gap widens, which increases demand for independent audit infrastructure. Their position strengthens as the platforms advance, which is the defining test of structural durability. By contrast, their competitor building a 'smart orchestration layer' is in exposed middleware \u2014 when Anthropic or OpenAI ships native multi-step tool use, that orchestration value collapses. The competitor should pivot toward encoding customer-specific compliance workflows (Position 3) or building the verification layer agents must call regardless of orchestrator (Position 2).", "related_frameworks": ["middleware-trap-diagnostic", "the-moat-audit", "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "the-three-layer-analysis", "the-weekend-hack-test", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-context-platform-compound", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "two-ai-economies-framework", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "developer-customers-vs-cio-customers-chasm"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "hardcoded_model_names", "note": "Found hardcoded model names: Perplexity. Replace with capability descriptions (e.g., 'a model optimized for extended reasoning')."}]}
{"name": "Four Structural Failure Modes of Production AI Agents", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumeration of four structural failure modes\u2014derived from a medical triage study but generalizable to any enterprise AI agent\u2014each with recognizable signals and domain-agnostic examples.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent's accuracy is 92% overall but I suspect it's failing on the hardest or highest-stakes cases", "An AI agent's reasoning trace says one thing but its final output does something different", "We're seeing our guardrails fire constantly on low-risk inputs but miss dangerous outputs", "Users are adding conversational context (tone, opinions, authority cues) that seems to override the structured data the agent should be prioritizing", "I need to audit a production agent for structural failure patterns before a high-stakes deployment"], "principles": ["Aggregate accuracy metrics mask failures at the tails of the decision distribution (Inverted U).", "An LLM's reasoning trace and its final output are semi-independent processes; consistent reasoning does not guarantee consistent action.", "Unstructured social context (tone, authority cues, minimization language) systematically biases outputs even when structured data is unchanged.", "Guardrails calibrated to surface-level language patterns rather than domain risk taxonomies produce inverted alerts\u2014firing on appearance of risk, missing actual risk.", "These failure modes are structural properties of LLMs in production, not domain-specific bugs."], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190065996", "title": "A Single Sentence from a Family Member Shifted an AI Diagnosis 12x. That Anchoring Bias Is in Your Agents Right Now.", "date": "2026-03-18T13:02:18.322Z"}], "date": "2026-03-18T13:02:18.322Z", "id": "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "core_question": "My AI agent looks great on average metrics \u2014 so why does it keep producing catastrophic outputs on the cases that matter most?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The active failure mode is Inverted U \u2014 the agent triages routine cases at 94% accuracy but drops to 41% on the highest-severity tier, precisely where errors are most costly. The tell: a family member's minimizing language ('he says it's probably nothing') shifted the urgency classification 12x downward even though the structured vitals data was unchanged. That's the social-context bias mode compounding with the reasoning-action divergence \u2014 the chain-of-thought correctly flagged elevated risk indicators, but the final output classification followed the conversational tone instead. The guardrails didn't catch it because they're calibrated to fire on explicit alarm language ('chest pain,' 'can't breathe'), not on the actual clinical risk profile embedded in the structured data.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "contextual-noise-library-factorial-stress-testing-for-agents", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "blast-radius-assessment", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "constraint-architecture", "the-off-manifold-probe", "ambient-failure-cascade", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "model-vs-harness-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four-Layer Agent Eval Architecture", "type": "procedure", "description": "A layered evaluation and oversight architecture for enterprise AI agents where each layer targets a specific structural failure mode, arranged from cheapest/continuous to most expensive/periodic.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm deploying an AI agent into production and need to design the evaluation infrastructure from scratch \u2014 what layers of oversight should exist?", "Our agent sometimes says one thing in its reasoning but does the opposite in its output, and we can't rely on the model to catch itself", "We're spending too much on human review of agent decisions and need the cost to decrease over time without sacrificing safety", "I need to stress-test whether my agent's decisions shift when irrelevant contextual signals like authority cues or emotional framing are injected", "We have a high-stakes agent where missing a true failure is much worse than a false alarm \u2014 how do we structure automated scoring to reflect that asymmetry?"], "principles": ["Each layer addresses exactly one failure mode: Inverted U \u2192 confidence routing; reasoning-action gap \u2192 deterministic validation; anchoring \u2192 factorial stress testing; guardrail inversion \u2192 taxonomy-based guardrail testing.", "Do not use the LLM to catch its own inconsistencies; use cheap deterministic rules outside the model.", "Bias the automated judge toward false positives in high-stakes systems to avoid missing true failures.", "Audit cases the judge passed, not just cases it flagged, to find defects the judge missed.", "Every human-caught failure becomes a permanent automated regression test, compounding the value of the eval suite over time.", "Human effort is front-loaded during build; ongoing cost shrinks as the regression suite grows.", "Confidence routing must use domain-specific anomaly signals, not just model confidence scores, because the agent is most confident on extreme edge cases."], "steps": ["Layer 1 \u2014 Confidence Routing with Progressive Autonomy: Route high-confidence, low-stakes decisions to full autonomy; route extreme or uncertain cases to human-in-the-loop; apply random audit sampling across all decisions. Add domain-specific anomaly detection to flag cases the model doesn't know it should be uncertain about.", "Layer 2 \u2014 Deterministic Validation Outside the LLM: Write rule-based checks that compare the agent's reasoning trace against its final action (e.g., 'if reasoning flags risk X AND output is low-risk, escalate'). Run these checks outside the model at near-zero cost before any output is acted on.", "Layer 3 \u2014 Continuous Eval with Failure-to-Regression Flywheel: Score every interaction with an automated judge; have human experts review flagged cases plus a random sample; audit cases the judge passed; convert every human-caught defect into a new automated regression test to shrink future human review load.", "Layer 4 \u2014 Factorial Stress Testing: Systematically inject contextual noise variations (social pressure, authority cues, minimization language, time pressure) into scenarios while holding structured data constant. If output shifts with only unstructured context changed, an anchoring vulnerability is confirmed. Test guardrails against the actual domain risk taxonomy, not keyword lists."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190065996", "title": "A Single Sentence from a Family Member Shifted an AI Diagnosis 12x. That Anchoring Bias Is in Your Agents Right Now.", "date": "2026-03-18T13:02:18.322Z"}], "date": "2026-03-18T13:02:18.322Z", "id": "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "core_question": "How do I build an evaluation and oversight system for my AI agent that catches the specific ways it actually fails \u2014 not just generic quality checks \u2014 without drowning in permanent human review costs?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "A claims-processing agent has four layers in place. Layer 1 routes extreme-value claims and anomalous claimant profiles to human review, catching the inverted-U problem where the model is most confident on the riskiest edge cases. Layer 2 runs a deterministic check outside the model: the agent's reasoning trace flagged potential fraud indicators, but its final recommendation was 'auto-approve' \u2014 that contradiction triggers automatic escalation before any payout happens. Layer 3's regression suite now contains 847 test cases, all converted from human-caught defects over six months, meaning the monthly human review load has dropped from 400 hours to under 60. Layer 4's last stress test injected authority cues ('the claimant's attorney called twice') into otherwise identical claims and found a 3.2x shift in approval likelihood \u2014 confirming an anchoring vulnerability that is now addressed with structured-data-only decision paths for high-value claims.", "related_frameworks": ["four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "contextual-noise-library-factorial-stress-testing-for-agents", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "blast-radius-assessment", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "speed-of-control", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "verification-frontier", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Contextual Noise Library (Factorial Stress Testing for Agents)", "type": "procedure", "description": "A reusable methodology for stress-testing AI agents by applying domain-general contextual variation templates to domain-specific scenarios, revealing anchoring bias and guardrail inversion that single-condition benchmarks cannot detect.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent processes a mix of structured records and free-text human inputs, and I need to know if the free text is contaminating decisions that should be driven by the structured data", "We have historical production data (claims, tickets, transcripts) and want to generate eval scenarios semi-automatically instead of hand-crafting them", "Our guardrails seem to trigger on specific words but I suspect they'd miss the actual risky patterns if phrased differently", "Single-condition benchmarks show our agent performs well, but I'm worried it won't hold up when users add emotional context, authority references, or urgency framing", "We need a reusable stress-testing methodology that works across domains, not a one-off eval tied to a specific use case"], "principles": ["The variation types (social pressure, authority cues, minimization language, time pressure, hedging qualifiers) are domain-general even though the scenarios they are applied to are domain-specific.", "Hold structured data constant across variations; vary only unstructured contextual framing to isolate anchoring effects.", "If output shifts when only unstructured context changes, the agent has an anchoring vulnerability.", "Enterprise scenarios can be generated semi-automatically from existing historical data (processed claims, transcripts, approvals) rather than hand-crafted by domain experts.", "Test guardrails against the domain's actual risk taxonomy, not against surface-level language patterns or keyword lists."], "steps": ["Identify or generate a base scenario library from historical production data (e.g., processed claims, compliance screenings, chat transcripts).", "Define a contextual noise library of variation types: social pressure from a stakeholder, authority-figure endorsement, minimization language, contradictory context, time pressure, hedging qualifiers.", "For each base scenario, mechanically apply each variation type to produce multiple versions holding structured data constant.", "Run the agent on all scenario variants and compare outputs across variations.", "Flag any scenario where output shifts despite identical structured data\u2014this signals an anchoring vulnerability.", "Separately test guardrails by constructing inputs that match the domain risk taxonomy's high-risk patterns without using surface-level danger keywords, and inputs that use danger keywords without representing actual risk."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190065996", "title": "A Single Sentence from a Family Member Shifted an AI Diagnosis 12x. That Anchoring Bias Is in Your Agents Right Now.", "date": "2026-03-18T13:02:18.322Z"}], "date": "2026-03-18T13:02:18.322Z", "id": "contextual-noise-library-factorial-stress-testing-for-agents", "core_question": "Is my AI agent actually reasoning from the structured data it's supposed to use, or is it being silently steered by the tone, framing, and social cues wrapped around that data?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "An insurance claims agent was tested on 200 base scenarios drawn from historical processed claims. Each scenario was mechanically varied six ways \u2014 adding a claimant's emotional plea, a supervisor's endorsement note, minimization language ('it's really just a minor issue'), contradictory context, urgency framing, and hedging qualifiers. Structured claim data (amounts, codes, dates) stayed identical across all variations. In 34 of the 200 scenarios, the agent shifted its approval recommendation when authority-figure endorsement or time-pressure language was present, despite zero change in the underlying claim facts. Those 34 scenarios now form a regression suite, and the harness was updated to strip unstructured framing before the decision step. Separately, guardrail testing revealed the fraud-detection module flagged inputs containing the word 'fraud' but missed actual structurally fraudulent patterns when described in neutral business language \u2014 confirming the guardrails were pattern-matching on keywords, not on the risk taxonomy.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "blast-radius-assessment", "correctness-definition-framework", "the-off-manifold-probe", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "constraint-architecture", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "judgment-over-rules-agent-architecture", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Blast Radius Assessment", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured decision rule for evaluating how much of a codebase a single AI agent task could affect, and how to scope or sequence it accordingly.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm about to hand a coding agent a task that touches shared infrastructure like the database schema or auth system", "My AI agent made a change and now multiple unrelated features are broken \u2014 how do I prevent this next time?", "I want to do a large refactor using an AI coding agent and I'm not sure how to break it up", "The agent has been running for several minutes and I'm not sure what it's changed or whether I can safely roll back", "I'm trying to decide whether to let the agent handle this change in one shot or break it into smaller pieces"], "principles": ["Ask one question before every task: how many files will this touch?", "Small blast radius (1\u20133 files): proceed directly", "Medium blast radius (4\u201310 files): break into 2\u20133 sequential subtasks with commits between each", "Large blast radius (10+ files): ask the agent to produce a step-by-step plan first, where each step can be tested independently", "Commit after every working step so rollback cost never exceeds one step", "Stop and check if the agent has been running unsupervised for more than five minutes", "Complex changes compound errors\u2014untangling is often harder than starting over"], "steps": ["Identify the task you want to give the agent", "Estimate how many files and systems the change could touch", "If 1\u20133 files: proceed and commit when done", "If 4\u201310 files: decompose into 2\u20133 smaller tasks, execute sequentially, commit between each", "If 10+ files: prompt the agent to produce a sequenced plan with independent test points before writing any code", "Execute each step, test manually (click through, try edge inputs, check on mobile), and commit before proceeding", "If agent runs more than 5 minutes without output, interrupt and review"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190800262", "title": "Your AI coding agent deleted 2.5 years of customer data in minutes. Here's why an experienced engineer couldn't stop it \u2014 and the 5 habits that would have + 5 prompts.", "date": "2026-03-16T13:01:43.351Z"}], "date": "2026-03-16T13:01:43.351Z", "id": "blast-radius-assessment", "core_question": "How much damage can this AI agent task do if it goes wrong, and how should I scope the work to keep rollback cost minimal?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "This refactor touches 14 files across the auth system, the user model, and two API routes \u2014 that's a large blast radius. Before writing a single line of code, the agent needs to produce a sequenced plan where each step can be tested independently. Step one: rename the database column with a migration and verify existing queries still resolve. Step two: update the auth middleware. Step three: update the API routes. Commit and test between each. If the agent had been turned loose on all 14 files at once, a single misunderstanding about the column rename would have cascaded silently through every downstream file, and untangling which changes were correct versus which inherited the error would take longer than starting over.", "related_frameworks": ["action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "speed-of-control", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "the-reversibility-gap", "primitives-for-reversibility", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-two-door-principle", "done-definition-pattern", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "task-decomposition-framework", "scout-vs-strike-team-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Scar Tissue Rules File", "type": "procedure", "description": "A method for building persistent AI agent memory by growing a rules file incrementally from observed mistakes rather than designing it upfront.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI coding agent keeps making the same mistake every new session even though I've corrected it before", "I'm starting a new project with an AI agent and want to set up a rules file but don't know what to put in it yet", "My CLAUDE.md or cursor rules file has grown to 300+ lines and the agent seems to ignore half of it", "I'm handing off an AI-assisted codebase to a teammate and need to transfer the tribal knowledge about what the agent gets wrong", "The agent keeps dropping security practices like input validation or credential handling unless I remind it every session"], "principles": ["Don't design the rules file upfront\u2014grow it from actual mistakes", "Start with minimal content: product description, tech stack, top two recurring errors", "Add exactly one line each time the agent makes a mistake you've seen before", "Every line in the file should exist because something went wrong without it", "The file is institutional memory, not a wish list", "Keep the file under 200 lines (ideally under 100) to avoid consuming agent context", "Include standing security and error-handling mandates that agents silently drop in later sessions"], "steps": ["Create a rules file in your project root (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, or tool-specific equivalent)", "Write five lines: what the product is, what it's built with, and the two things your agent keeps getting wrong", "Each time the agent repeats a mistake you've seen before, add one new line preventing it", "Periodically audit the file to remove redundant or outdated rules and keep it under 200 lines", "Add permanent standing rules for security (no logging credentials, row-level security) and error handling"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190800262", "title": "Your AI coding agent deleted 2.5 years of customer data in minutes. Here's why an experienced engineer couldn't stop it \u2014 and the 5 habits that would have + 5 prompts.", "date": "2026-03-16T13:01:43.351Z"}], "date": "2026-03-16T13:01:43.351Z", "id": "scar-tissue-rules-file", "core_question": "How do I make my AI agent actually remember and stop repeating the same mistakes across sessions without drowning it in a bloated instruction manual?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The agent has now hallucinated a nonexistent database column for the third time this week, generating queries against 'users.full_name' when the actual column is 'users.display_name'. That's a repeated mistake \u2014 one line goes into the rules file: 'The users table has no full_name column. Use display_name.' The file is now 47 lines, each one a scar from a real failure. No line exists speculatively. The standing security block at the bottom \u2014 'Never run DROP, TRUNCATE, or DELETE without explicit human confirmation' \u2014 stays permanently because agents silently drop safety constraints in fresh sessions.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-memory-model", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "constraint-architecture", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "blast-radius-assessment", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "intent-doc", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "done-definition-pattern", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "delegation-prompt-management-system"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Agent Supervision Model", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the role of a non-engineer using AI coding agents from 'prompter' to 'foreman'\u2014shifting the key skill from clear description to active supervision with defined operational habits.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI coding agent keeps breaking things I already had working and I don't know how to prevent it", "I'm a non-technical founder using Cursor/Replit agents and I feel like I'm losing control of my codebase", "I want to know the difference between getting better at prompting vs. getting better at managing AI agents", "My vibe-coded app works for me but I'm nervous about whether it's actually safe for real users", "I need to decide whether to keep building with AI agents myself or hire an engineer"], "principles": ["The shift from chat-based AI to agentic AI changes the primary skill from 'describe clearly' to 'supervise effectively'", "You don't need to know the trade work; you need judgment about what's happening and when to stop", "Five supervisory habits cover the majority of preventable disasters: version control, context management, rules files, blast radius control, and proactive quality demands", "Bringing in an engineer is not failure\u2014it is a transition point after demand is proven", "Interrupting an agent is not rude; it is the job", "The gap between 'works for me' and 'works for customers' requires proactive demands the agent will never raise unprompted"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "190800262", "title": "Your AI coding agent deleted 2.5 years of customer data in minutes. Here's why an experienced engineer couldn't stop it \u2014 and the 5 habits that would have + 5 prompts.", "date": "2026-03-16T13:01:43.351Z"}], "date": "2026-03-16T13:01:43.351Z", "id": "ai-agent-supervision-model", "core_question": "I'm building real things with AI coding agents but I'm not an engineer \u2014 what skill am I actually supposed to be developing to stop things from going wrong?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The conventional framing is that non-technical builders need to get better at prompting \u2014 describe what you want more precisely and the agent will build it right. That's wrong once you're using agentic AI. The real failure mode isn't imprecise instructions; it's unsupervised execution. The builder whose agent deleted 2.5 years of customer data wasn't bad at prompting \u2014 they were absent from supervision. The shift is from 'writer giving notes' to 'foreman on a job site': you need version control before every major change (so you can roll back), context management across sessions (so the agent doesn't lose the plot), rules files that encode past mistakes (so the agent doesn't repeat them), blast radius control (so one bad run can't destroy everything), and proactive quality demands (because the agent will never volunteer 'this isn't production-ready'). Knowing when to bring in a real engineer isn't failure \u2014 it's the sign that demand is proven and the project has graduated.", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "speed-of-control", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "builder-engineer-composability-diagnostic", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "intent-engineering", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "the-reversibility-gap", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "done-definition-pattern", "pull-push-paradigm"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Speed of Control", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the organizational bottleneck for AI-era talent from 'span of control' (how many people a manager oversees) to the speed at which a talented individual can translate their judgment into executed output without organizational friction.", "when_to_apply": ["My best engineer is somehow no more productive than my average ones and I can't figure out why", "A solo founder or tiny team just shipped what my 50-person department has been building for two quarters", "We keep adding headcount but output doesn't scale \u2014 it actually feels like it's slowing down", "I'm trying to figure out whether to restructure teams now that AI tools let individuals do so much more alone", "Our top performers keep leaving to go independent, and they seem to produce more on their own than they did here"], "principles": ["Span of control is the wrong metaphor for AI-era talent management", "The bottleneck is not headcount or hierarchy but the speed at which judgment becomes execution", "Organizational overhead (alignment meetings, cross-functional syncs, stakeholder management) taxes high-performers disproportionately", "AI tools allow talented individuals to route around coordination costs entirely", "The question is not how to find extraordinary people but how to stop making extraordinary people look ordinary"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references a full framework called 'Speed of Control' and a five-question diagnostic for measuring talent suppression, but the detailed framework steps and the five diagnostic questions are not included in the provided excerpt \u2014 they appear to be in the audio/podcast content referenced by the briefing.", "source_posts": [{"id": "190757490", "title": "Executive Briefing: One solo founder just sold for $80M in 6 months. Your 50-person department is building the same thing for Q3. + the framework that explains why", "date": "2026-03-15T15:01:05.763Z"}], "date": "2026-03-15T15:01:05.763Z", "id": "speed-of-control", "core_question": "Why do my best people seem to produce the same output as everyone else, and is the organization itself the thing making them ordinary?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this team needs better talent \u2014 the VP keeps saying 'we need 10x engineers.' That's wrong here. The three strongest engineers on the team each spend 60% of their week in alignment meetings, cross-functional syncs, and stakeholder reviews. A solo founder with the same technical ability and AI tools shipped a comparable product in six months because her judgment-to-execution latency was measured in minutes, not sprint cycles. The bottleneck isn't the talent \u2014 it's that the organization converts every individual's judgment into a committee process before anything gets built. The real question isn't 'how do we hire better?' but 'how do we stop converting extraordinary people into ordinary output?'", "related_frameworks": ["talent-suppression-diagnostic", "coordination-tax-audit", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "the-weekend-hack-test", "double-compression-loop", "magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references a full framework called 'Speed of Control' and a five-question diagnostic for measuring talent suppression, but the detailed framework steps and the five diagnostic questions are not included in the provided excerpt \u2014 they appear to be in the audio/podcast content referenced by the briefing.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "Talent Suppression Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A five-question structured audit executives can run to determine how much high-caliber talent their organization is suppressing through coordination overhead and organizational structure.", "when_to_apply": ["A top performer just left and is now shipping faster alone than they ever did with our team", "We're losing senior people to solo consulting or one-person startups and I need to understand why the org can't retain them", "Our 50-person team is being outpaced by a 3-person AI-augmented startup doing the same thing", "I suspect our organizational structure is the bottleneck, not our talent quality", "We need to restructure teams because AI tools are changing what one person can accomplish"], "principles": ["Correctness, not volume, is the scarce resource in AI-era talent", "Performance reviews typically do not measure the actual variable that makes extraordinary performers extraordinary", "Organizational compromise tax (the cost of translating individual judgment into multi-team execution) is now directly visible by comparison to solo AI-augmented output"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post explicitly references 'five questions you can run this week' as a diagnostic, but the text of those five questions is not included in the provided excerpt \u2014 they appear to be delivered in the podcast/audio portion of the briefing.", "source_posts": [{"id": "190757490", "title": "Executive Briefing: One solo founder just sold for $80M in 6 months. Your 50-person department is building the same thing for Q3. + the framework that explains why", "date": "2026-03-15T15:01:05.763Z"}], "date": "2026-03-15T15:01:05.763Z", "id": "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "core_question": "Are my best people actually performing at their potential, or is the organization itself the thing suppressing them \u2014 and would I even know the difference?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "The engineering lead who left six months ago just shipped a competing product solo in 14 weeks \u2014 the same scope your 12-person team has been working on since Q1. The gap isn't talent; it's that her in-company work was 30% actual engineering judgment and 70% translating that judgment through review cycles, alignment meetings, and cross-team dependency negotiations. The organization's compromise tax was consuming the exact capability that made her extraordinary. The performance review never captured this because it measured output volume, not correctness rate \u2014 and correctness is the variable AI-augmented individuals can now fully exploit without organizational friction.", "related_frameworks": ["coordination-tax-audit", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "the-weekend-hack-test", "capability-dissipation-gap", "double-compression-loop", "ai-leverage-audit", "magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company", "scout-vs-strike-team-framework"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post explicitly references 'five questions you can run this week' as a diagnostic, but the text of those five questions is not included in the provided excerpt \u2014 they appear to be delivered in the podcast/audio portion of the briefing.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Two-Door Principle", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between agent-facing and human-facing interfaces to the same data, arguing that effective AI-augmented systems require both doors into shared data rather than relying on chat alone.", "when_to_apply": ["I built a personal knowledge system that my AI agent can query, but I still find myself opening spreadsheets to actually see what's going on", "My chat-based AI workflow works but feels like looking through a straw \u2014 I can ask questions but can't get the big picture", "I'm designing a system where both my family and an AI agent need to manage the same shared information", "I need to decide whether to add a visual dashboard layer on top of my agent's data or just improve my prompts", "My agent has all the right data but the humans in the loop keep falling back to their old tools instead of using the AI interface"], "principles": ["Every extension needs both an agent door (for reasoning, writing, cross-referencing) and a human door (for visual scanning, quick edits, sharing)", "A chat window is a keyhole \u2014 you can ask one question but cannot see the landscape", "Both sides must read and write to the same underlying data source", "The agent does what agents do best (holding timelines, cross-referencing, noticing patterns); the human does what humans do best (visual scanning, judgment, social context)", "Neither interface alone is sufficient \u2014 each solves problems the other cannot"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190066386", "title": "You built an AI memory system. Now your agent needs hands. Here are 6 extensions that compound \u2014 from household knowledge to the job hunt.", "date": "2026-03-13T13:00:28.565Z"}], "date": "2026-03-13T13:00:28.565Z", "id": "the-two-door-principle", "core_question": "Why does my AI workflow feel claustrophobic even though the agent technically has access to everything it needs?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This household logistics system is a one-door design. The agent can cross-reference the family calendar, meal plan, and grocery list through chat \u2014 but nobody in the household actually uses it because you can't glance at next week's meals while editing Thursday's dinner. The chat window is a keyhole: perfect for asking 'what conflicts does Tuesday have?' but useless for the spatial scan a human does when planning a week. The fix isn't a better prompt \u2014 it's adding the second door: a visual dashboard that reads and writes to the same data the agent uses, so the human can drag Thursday's dinner to Friday while the agent simultaneously notices the grocery list needs updating.", "related_frameworks": ["four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "pull-push-paradigm", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "front-end-composability-four-capabilities", "your-data-your-questions", "four-layer-agent-memory-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four Principles of Agent Extension Design", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of four named design principles for determining when and how to extend an AI memory system, evaluating each use case against time-bridging, cross-category reasoning, proactive surfacing, and the human judgment line.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm considering building a new structured memory table for my AI agent and want to know if it's actually worth it", "My AI memory system has a lot of stored data but isn't generating useful insights \u2014 it feels like a glorified spreadsheet", "I'm designing an agent extension and need to decide what the agent should do autonomously versus what it should flag for me", "I want to evaluate whether a personal AI system is crossing the line from helpful pattern recognition into making decisions I should be making", "I'm brainstorming new domains (health tracking, job search, finances) to add to my AI second brain and need a filter for what will actually compound"], "principles": ["Time-bridging: an agent's memory doesn't decay \u2014 anywhere value comes from linking events spread across months or years is agent territory", "Cross-category reasoning: the power isn't in any single table but in connections between tables that no human would cross-reference manually", "Proactive surfacing: the most valuable answers are to questions you didn't ask \u2014 design tables for what you want the agent to notice, not just what you want to look up", "Agent surfaces, human decides, agent executes: the agent handles memory and pattern recognition; the human handles judgment \u2014 blur this line and you build something you won't trust"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190066386", "title": "You built an AI memory system. Now your agent needs hands. Here are 6 extensions that compound \u2014 from household knowledge to the job hunt.", "date": "2026-03-13T13:00:28.565Z"}], "date": "2026-03-13T13:00:28.565Z", "id": "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "core_question": "Should I extend my AI memory system into this new domain, and if so, what makes it worth the agent's attention rather than just being another database I never use?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "A proposed household maintenance memory table passes three of four principles: it time-bridges (linking the furnace filter replacement 11 months ago to the efficiency drop today), it enables cross-category reasoning (connecting the water bill spike to the irrigation schedule and the plumber visit), and it supports proactive surfacing (the agent notices the warranty expires in 30 days without being asked). But the proposed 'auto-schedule repair appointments' feature fails the fourth principle \u2014 that's a judgment call about budget, timing, and vendor trust. The agent should surface the warranty expiration and recommend action; the human decides whether to call. Strip the auto-scheduling, keep the surfacing, and this extension compounds.", "related_frameworks": ["the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "pull-push-paradigm", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "your-data-your-questions", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "delegation-scope-expansion-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Pull/Push Paradigm", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between conversational clients (pull \u2014 you ask and the agent reasons across your data) and autonomous agents (push \u2014 they scan your data on a schedule and surface what's urgent before you ask), clarifying when each interface type is appropriate.", "when_to_apply": ["I have an AI system that works great when I ask it things, but I keep missing time-sensitive information because I forget to check", "Designing a system where the same knowledge base needs both on-demand querying and proactive alerting", "Trying to decide whether to build a chatbot interface or a scheduled autonomous agent for a recurring workflow", "My AI memory system has valuable data but I only benefit from it when I happen to think of the right question", "I want my agent to monitor something and notify me rather than waiting for me to ask"], "principles": ["Conversational clients handle pull: the user asks a broad question and the agent reasons across data in the moment", "Autonomous agents handle push: they scan data on a schedule and surface what's urgent before you think to ask", "Same database, different interfaces \u2014 the data doesn't care which model is reading it", "Pull is sufficient when the user will remember to ask; push is necessary when the value comes from catching what the user forgot", "Both modes are valuable and complement each other rather than substitute for each other"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190066386", "title": "You built an AI memory system. Now your agent needs hands. Here are 6 extensions that compound \u2014 from household knowledge to the job hunt.", "date": "2026-03-13T13:00:28.565Z"}], "date": "2026-03-13T13:00:28.565Z", "id": "pull-push-paradigm", "core_question": "Should my AI system wait for me to ask the right question, or should it watch my data and tell me what I need to know before I think to ask?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This is a push problem, not a pull problem. The team built a conversational interface over their job application tracker, but the real value isn't answering 'what's the status of my applications?' \u2014 it's catching that a recruiter replied 18 hours ago and the response window is closing. The user will never remember to ask at the right moment. Same database, but the right interface is an autonomous agent scanning on a schedule and surfacing the time-sensitive signal before it decays.", "related_frameworks": ["the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "scout-vs-strike-team-framework", "cascade-scanner-gap-close-pattern", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "your-data-your-questions", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Coordination Tax Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured calendar analysis that categorizes all work hours into three buckets\u2014coordination overhead, verifiable execution, and genuine residual judgment\u2014to calculate the fraction of a role that is automatable or already obsolete.", "when_to_apply": ["I spend most of my week in meetings and writing documents but I can't point to what I actually created", "We're planning which team functions to hand off to AI agents and need a principled way to prioritize", "My role feels important but I suspect most of what I do is just moving information between people who should already have it", "I want a concrete, calendar-based measurement of how much of my job is automatable versus genuinely irreplaceable", "We're restructuring headcount and need something more rigorous than gut feel about which roles are at risk"], "principles": ["Work hours fall into exactly three categories: coordination (meetings, translation artifacts, state synchronization), verifiable execution (tasks agents can already do or will within 18 months), and the genuine residual (zero-to-one judgment, political navigation, moral judgment, aesthetic taste).", "The coordination category exists not because the work is inherently valuable, but because human bandwidth and context limits create a translation problem that would disappear if everyone shared perfect context.", "What feels like 'judgment' is usually a composite of verifiable preparation work plus a thin layer of genuine non-verifiable judgment on top\u2014the audit separates these.", "Use the actual calendar from the prior week, not the idealized job description, as the data source.", "The residual is what matters: identify what your role would look like if the coordination tax dropped to zero, then build a migration sequence for delegating coordination tasks to agents."], "steps": ["Pull up your calendar from last week\u2014the actual version, not the idealized one.", "Count hours where the primary purpose was transferring information from someone who had it to someone who needed it (status updates, syncs, standups, one-on-ones for context transfer)\u2014label these 'coordination.'", "Count hours spent creating artifacts whose primary purpose was translating your knowledge into a form someone else could act on (PRDs, specs, briefs, decks, tickets, handoff documents)\u2014also label these 'coordination.'", "Count hours spent doing the thing you were actually hired to do\u2014creating something that didn't exist before\u2014label these as either 'verifiable execution' (testable, measurable output) or 'genuine residual' (non-verifiable judgment, vision, or insight).", "Calculate your personal coordination tax percentage and compare it to the 60\u201370% benchmark.", "From the residual bucket, map what your role would look like if you spent 30 hours per week on it instead of your current allocation.", "Identify which coordination tasks are ready for agent delegation now, which need a verification layer first, and what the handoff protocol looks like for each\u2014producing a concrete migration sequence."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190572530", "title": "80 out of every 200 employees exist to manage handoffs that agents are eliminating + the coordination tax audit to find yours", "date": "2026-03-12T13:03:29.157Z"}, {"id": "185421882", "title": "My honest field notes on scaling agents past the demo phase + 6 rules from teams running hundreds", "date": "2026-01-26T14:03:32.389Z"}], "date": "2026-01-26T14:03:32.389Z", "id": "coordination-tax-audit", "core_question": "How much of my actual work week is genuine high-value contribution versus coordination overhead that agents could eliminate \u2014 and what would my role look like if I only did the part that matters?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "Last week's calendar shows 42 hours logged. 14 hours were meetings whose sole purpose was transferring context between people who didn't share it \u2014 standups, cross-team syncs, the Monday status review. Another 12 hours went to translation artifacts: writing a PRD that reformatted engineering context for a product review, updating tickets so another team could pick up work, building a deck that restated what was already in Slack threads. That's 26 hours \u2014 62% \u2014 pure coordination tax, right at the benchmark. Of the remaining 16 hours, 11 were verifiable execution (drafting copy, building a prototype, running analysis) that an agent handles today with a verification layer. The genuine residual is 5 hours: deciding which market to enter, navigating a political conflict between two VPs, and making an aesthetic call on brand direction. The migration sequence starts with the translation artifacts \u2014 the PRD and ticket-writing are agent-ready now with a human review checkpoint.", "related_frameworks": ["genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "ai-leverage-audit", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "work-primitives", "weekly-delegation-audit", "talent-suppression-diagnostic"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Double Compression Loop", "type": "reframe", "description": "Challenges the standard 'AI automates X% of tasks' forecast by arguing that reducing human headcount makes remaining work more verifiable, which in turn expands what agents can handle\u2014creating a compounding loop that makes static task-percentage models systematically undercount displacement.", "when_to_apply": ["An analyst says AI will automate X% of jobs in our industry and I suspect that underestimates what actually happens", "We're planning our automation roadmap and need to understand why the second and third rounds of displacement will be faster than the first", "Leadership is treating AI adoption as a one-time headcount reduction rather than a compounding structural change", "Our workforce planning model assumes the org chart stays roughly the same shape with fewer people in each box", "We're debating whether coordination-heavy roles are safe from automation because they involve 'human judgment'"], "principles": ["The current distribution of tasks in an organization is not a natural set of things that need to be done\u2014it is an artifact of human coordination requirements.", "Reducing human headcount does not just eliminate coordination roles; it simultaneously makes remaining work more machine-inspectable and testable (more verifiable), expanding what agents can handle.", "The loop compounds: fewer humans \u2192 less coordination \u2192 fewer coordination roles \u2192 remaining work more verifiable \u2192 agents handle more \u2192 fewer humans still.", "Standard forecast models are wrong by design because they treat the task distribution as fixed; the distribution itself changes when the org structure changes.", "Work expressed as code or code-like artifacts (machine-inspectable, testable) is the key threshold that determines agent eligibility."], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "190572530", "title": "80 out of every 200 employees exist to manage handoffs that agents are eliminating + the coordination tax audit to find yours", "date": "2026-03-12T13:03:29.157Z"}], "date": "2026-03-12T13:03:29.157Z", "id": "double-compression-loop", "core_question": "Are our AI displacement forecasts fundamentally wrong because they assume the current shape of work stays fixed while only the performers change?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "A consulting firm's forecast says AI will automate 30% of a 200-person department's tasks. That number is wrong because it treats the current task distribution as fixed. Forty of those 200 people exist primarily to manage handoffs, approvals, and coordination between the other 160. When the first wave of automation removes 30 roles, the coordination load drops disproportionately \u2014 eliminating the need for another 15 coordination roles that weren't in the original automation scope. Simultaneously, the remaining work becomes more machine-inspectable (fewer handoff artifacts, more codified processes), which pushes the verifiability threshold down and makes a second wave of agent-eligible work appear that didn't exist in the original task inventory. The real displacement number isn't 30% \u2014 it's 55-65%, and it arrives in two compressed waves rather than one.", "related_frameworks": ["coordination-tax-audit", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-factory-curve", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "edge-first-automation-framework", "work-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Genuine Judgment vs. Coordination Overhead Disguised as Judgment", "type": "distinction", "description": "Separates true non-verifiable judgment (conviction, empathy, structural bets with long-latency feedback) from complex coordination overhead that merely feels like judgment because it involves synthesis of many inputs\u2014arguing the latter is automatable once verifiability barriers are removed.", "when_to_apply": ["Someone argues a senior role is safe from AI because it requires 'strategic judgment' or 'years of experience'", "We're designing human-agent task splits and struggling to decide what stays with the human versus what gets delegated", "A domain like brand strategy or investment selection is claimed to be inherently subjective and unmeasurable", "Leadership is resisting automation of a function because the work 'requires synthesis that only a human can do'", "We need to figure out which parts of an executive's week are genuinely irreplaceable versus expensive coordination overhead"], "principles": ["Most 'judgment-heavy' roles are composites: a large layer of verifiable preparation work plus a thin layer of genuine non-verifiable judgment on top.", "The verifiability barrier in many 'judgment' domains is not the nature of the decision itself but the opacity of the decision process\u2014agent harnesses make reasoning inspectable.", "True non-verifiable judgment has long-latency feedback (outcomes only apparent years later), involves commitments that constrain future optionality, or requires non-dataset context such as empathy or political navigation.", "Work bundled as 'one role does both' was organizationally efficient, not structurally indivisible\u2014agent harnesses unbundle preparation from judgment.", "The test: could the outcome be measured against data if the feedback loop were faster and cleaner? If yes, it is verifiable preparation masquerading as judgment."], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "190572530", "title": "80 out of every 200 employees exist to manage handoffs that agents are eliminating + the coordination tax audit to find yours", "date": "2026-03-12T13:03:29.157Z"}], "date": "2026-03-12T13:03:29.157Z", "id": "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "core_question": "Is this role genuinely irreplaceable because of true judgment, or is it just that the preparation work underneath is so complex that it feels like judgment?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The VP of Pricing claims their work is pure judgment \u2014 reading the market, sensing customer willingness to pay, making calls competitors can't replicate. But decomposing the role reveals that 80% of the time goes to synthesizing competitor pricing data, segmenting customers by usage patterns, and modeling elasticity scenarios \u2014 all verifiable against revenue outcomes within a quarter. The genuine judgment layer is thin: it's the decision to hold price during a downturn when the board is panicking, a commitment that constrains future positioning and won't show results for 18 months. The synthesis work feels like judgment because it requires holding many inputs simultaneously, but faster feedback loops and agent harnesses make it inspectable and automatable. The role should be unbundled: agent-driven pricing analysis with a human retaining only the conviction-level strategic bets.", "related_frameworks": ["the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "coordination-tax-audit", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "task-decomposition-framework", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "harness-decision-audit", "work-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Verifiability Spectrum", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-tier classification system for categorizing work by how amenable it is to automated or structured verification, determining readiness for AI harness delegation.", "when_to_apply": ["I want to delegate a complex knowledge work process to AI agents but I'm not sure which pieces can actually be checked automatically", "My team says this work requires too much judgment for AI \u2014 but I suspect we're overestimating the judgment component", "I'm building a multi-agent system and need to decide where to invest in verification infrastructure first", "We have AI generating outputs but no systematic way to know which outputs we can trust without full human review", "I'm trying to figure out what skills on my team will still matter once AI handles more of the execution"], "principles": ["Tier 1 (machine-checkable): outputs have binary correctness signals \u2014 code compiles or doesn't, tests pass or fail, formal proofs verify or don't", "Tier 2 (expert-checkable with clear criteria): outputs can be evaluated by a judge agent without fully automated checking \u2014 informal proofs, engineering designs, legal briefs addressing relevant precedents", "Tier 3 (genuinely judgment-dependent): original theory-building, aesthetic choices, strategic decisions depending on values rather than facts", "A Tier 1 harness can operate effectively in Tier 2 \u2014 domain-specific verification machinery is not required, only enough signal to distinguish progress from non-progress", "What looks like holistic judgment (Tier 3) is almost always an assemblage of sub-tasks, each more verifiable than the composite", "The portion of work people think is Tier 3 but is actually Tier 2 is much larger than expected, and growing"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190401002", "title": "The jagged frontier was a measurement error \u2014 here's what actually smoothed it, why it's accelerating, and 3 prompts to map where your work sits on the new spectrum", "date": "2026-03-11T13:02:39.456Z"}], "date": "2026-03-11T13:02:39.456Z", "id": "the-verifiability-spectrum", "core_question": "Which parts of this work can I actually verify well enough to hand off to AI agents, and which parts am I fooling myself about needing human judgment?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "A legal team assumes contract review is Tier 3 \u2014 'it requires judgment.' Decomposed, 80% of the work is Tier 2: checking whether specific clauses address required regulatory provisions, flagging deviations from standard terms, verifying cross-references. The genuinely Tier 3 portion \u2014 advising whether an unusual indemnification structure is strategically acceptable given the client's risk posture \u2014 is perhaps 10% of the total effort. The remaining 10% (does this clause parse correctly, do defined terms match) is pure Tier 1. The team has been protecting the entire workflow from delegation based on the small Tier 3 slice.", "related_frameworks": ["verification-frontier", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "verification-capability-triage", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "correctness-definition-framework", "task-decomposition-framework", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "harness-decision-audit", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Jagged Frontier as Measurement Error", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the 'jagged frontier' observation \u2014 that AI is brilliant at some tasks and inexplicably bad at others \u2014 as an artifact of single-shot, single-agent interaction rather than a property of AI intelligence itself.", "when_to_apply": ["We tested AI on a complex task and it failed badly, so we're concluding the task is 'not ready for AI'", "Our AI capability assessment shows wildly uneven performance across task types and we're using that to draw automation boundaries", "We're designing an AI agent but defaulting to single-prompt interactions because that's how chat interfaces work", "Leadership is asking which job functions are 'safe from AI' based on current benchmark limitations", "We're seeing compounding errors in longer AI tasks and assuming it's a fundamental model limitation"], "principles": ["Jaggedness in AI outputs reflects the absence of organizational structure, not the shape of AI intelligence", "Single-turn, single-agent interaction removes all the scaffolding that human professionals rely on \u2014 drafting, revision, peer review, iterative correction", "Error propagation in serial one-shot inference is a structural flaw, not a capability ceiling", "The relevant comparison is not 'AI vs. human in one shot' but 'AI with organizational structure vs. human with organizational structure'", "Applying organizational design principles (roles, handoffs, verification, iteration) to AI agents addresses the root cause of jaggedness"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190401002", "title": "The jagged frontier was a measurement error \u2014 here's what actually smoothed it, why it's accelerating, and 3 prompts to map where your work sits on the new spectrum", "date": "2026-03-11T13:02:39.456Z"}], "date": "2026-03-11T13:02:39.456Z", "id": "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "core_question": "Are the tasks where AI fails actually beyond its capability, or am I just throwing a single prompt at problems that no human would solve without drafting, review, and iteration either?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The team concluded AI 'can't do' complex financial analysis because GPT-4 produced errors in a single-shot prompt asking it to synthesize a 40-page earnings report. But a human analyst doesn't do that in one pass either \u2014 they read, highlight, draft notes, cross-check figures, revise, and get peer review. When the same task was decomposed into a pipeline \u2014 one agent extracting figures, another cross-referencing against historical data, a third drafting the synthesis, and a fourth reviewing for inconsistencies \u2014 the error rate dropped to near-human levels. The 'jaggedness' wasn't a capability ceiling; it was the absence of organizational scaffolding that humans take for granted.", "related_frameworks": ["the-jagged-frontier-framework", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "task-decomposition-framework", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "capability-surface-mapping", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Rejection as a Competency (Recognition-Articulation-Encoding)", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-dimension framework for developing and scaling the skill of rejecting AI output, classifying rejection capability into Recognition (detecting errors), Articulation (explaining why precisely), and Encoding (making constraints persist).", "when_to_apply": ["We keep correcting the same AI outputs but never seem to build lasting improvement in our AI workflows", "Our best domain expert is leaving and their ability to spot bad AI output will walk out the door with them", "How do I assess whether my team is actually ready to verify AI-generated work at scale, not just one-off?", "We have great prompt engineers but our quality control on AI output is inconsistent across the team", "I want to design a training program that develops AI quality judgment, not just AI usage skills"], "principles": ["Recognition depends on domain experience and cannot be shortcut \u2014 it requires years of exposure to failures in the field", "Articulation is what converts personal taste into an organizational asset \u2014 a vague rejection stays in one head, a precise constraint can scale", "Encoding is where most organizations fail \u2014 constraints must be made durable and retrievable, not left in chat logs or email threads", "The three dimensions are sequential and interdependent: without recognition, articulation is impossible; without articulation, encoding is meaningless", "Inside a domain expert's boundary, AI is a force multiplier; outside it, AI is a confidence multiplier \u2014 which is worse", "Generation scales with compute; taste scales with rejection \u2014 compute is a commodity, taste is an asset"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "190322675", "title": "Your prompts are disposable. Your rejections compound. Here's the skill nobody is developing (+ the guide kit to start)", "date": "2026-03-10T13:02:00.755Z"}], "date": "2026-03-10T13:02:00.755Z", "id": "rejection-as-a-competency-recognition-articulation-encoding", "core_question": "My team keeps catching the same AI mistakes over and over \u2014 how do I turn individual quality instincts into organizational capability that actually persists?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "This team has strong Recognition \u2014 their senior engineers consistently spot when the AI generates plausible-but-wrong database migration scripts. But they're stuck at level one. When asked why a particular output was wrong, the answer is 'it just felt off' or 'that's not how we do it here.' That's a Recognition-Articulation gap. Until those engineers can state precisely which constraint was violated ('this migration drops the index before checking for active read replicas, which violates our zero-downtime deployment invariant'), the knowledge stays locked in individual heads. And even if they articulate it, without Encoding \u2014 writing it into the system prompt, the rules file, the evaluation rubric \u2014 the next junior engineer or the next AI session will reproduce the same error. The organizational bottleneck isn't detection; it's the failure to convert taste into durable constraint.", "related_frameworks": ["scar-tissue-rules-file", "constraint-architecture", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "experiential-debt", "verification-capability-triage", "correctness-definition-framework", "the-five-specification-primitives", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "definition-of-done-rubric", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "capability-dissipation-gap", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Verification Frontier", "type": "distinction", "description": "A mental model that distinguishes between the domain areas where an organization can reliably verify AI output (creating value) versus areas where it cannot (creating compounding, invisible risk).", "when_to_apply": ["We're rolling out AI across multiple departments and I need to know which ones can actually catch AI mistakes versus which ones are just trusting the output", "Our quality has been subtly declining since we started using AI but nobody can pinpoint where or why", "We stopped hiring juniors because AI handles that work now \u2014 is that going to bite us later?", "A senior expert just left and I'm worried about what we can no longer verify in their domain", "Leadership wants to expand AI-generated output to customer-facing work and I need to assess whether we have the taste to catch errors before they ship"], "principles": ["The frontier of AI value is identical to the frontier of an organization's taste \u2014 where verification capacity extends, AI creates value; where it doesn't, AI creates risk", "The frontier expands as encoded rejections accumulate and contracts when expert capacity leaves the organization", "Output that looks right but isn't right is more dangerous than output that looks wrong, because it bypasses verification triggers", "Eliminating junior hiring shrinks the future verification frontier by cutting off the pipeline that develops recognition"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "190322675", "title": "Your prompts are disposable. Your rejections compound. Here's the skill nobody is developing (+ the guide kit to start)", "date": "2026-03-10T13:02:00.755Z"}], "date": "2026-03-10T13:02:00.755Z", "id": "verification-frontier", "core_question": "Where exactly does our ability to tell good AI output from bad AI output actually end \u2014 and what's happening in the territory beyond that line?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The marketing team's AI-generated blog posts look polished and get approved quickly \u2014 but nobody on the team has deep enough domain expertise to catch when the AI fabricates a statistic or subtly mischaracterizes a competitor's product. That's beyond the verification frontier. Meanwhile, the engineering team's AI-generated code gets caught by tests and senior review \u2014 that's inside the frontier. The real danger isn't the engineering use case; it's the marketing one, because the output looks right and sails through without triggering anyone's recognition that something is off. And the company just laid off two senior content strategists, which means the frontier in marketing is actively contracting.", "related_frameworks": ["rejection-as-a-competency-recognition-articulation-encoding", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-capability-triage", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "experiential-debt", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "ambient-failure-cascade", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "blast-radius-assessment", "capability-dissipation-gap", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Intent Engineering", "type": "procedure", "description": "A discipline for structuring AI agent instructions around outcomes, values, constraints, and failure modes rather than just desired outputs, in order to close the gap between what you specify and what you actually mean.", "when_to_apply": ["Delegating a multi-step task to a long-running autonomous AI agent", "An AI agent technically completed the goal but did something I would never have approved along the way", "Writing instructions for an agent that will operate with minimal human supervision over hours or days", "Deploying an agent with access to external systems, credentials, or the ability to communicate on my behalf", "I keep finding edge cases where the agent does something dumb that feels obvious to a human but was never explicitly stated"], "principles": ["Specify not just the terminal goal but the value hierarchy governing acceptable paths to that goal", "Make constraints explicit \u2014 the model cannot infer your implicit norms, and optimization pressure will find paths you didn't think to block", "Define escalation conditions: specific situations where the agent should stop and yield control to a human rather than improvise", "Explicitly resolve what happens when the goal and a constraint conflict, since without direction the goal wins by default", "Treat goal specification as an engineering artifact \u2014 something designed, reviewed, tested, and iterated \u2014 not just a prompt", "What you leave implicit is where misalignment lives"], "steps": ["State the terminal goal and its priority level (e.g., important but not urgent enough to justify shortcuts)", "Answer: What would I NOT want the agent to do, even if it accomplished the goal? \u2014 make this your explicit constraint set", "Answer: Under what circumstances should the agent stop and ask rather than proceed? \u2014 make this your explicit escalation policy", "Answer: If the goal and a constraint conflict, which wins? \u2014 make this your explicit value hierarchy", "Specify any resource or permission boundaries the agent must not exceed", "Define the fallback behavior if the primary path fails (e.g., roll back and notify rather than attempt workarounds)"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189324514", "title": "Claude blackmailed its developers. GPT-5.3 helped build itself. The safety system is holding better than you think + a problem diagnostic and intent engineering kit", "date": "2026-03-09T13:02:54.787Z"}, {"id": "188532649", "title": "Klarna saved $60 million and broke its company. The missing layer is what I'm calling intent engineering + 2 prompts to find yours", "date": "2026-02-24T14:02:05.683Z"}], "date": "2026-02-24T14:02:05.683Z", "id": "intent-engineering", "core_question": "How do I tell an AI agent what I actually want \u2014 not just the goal, but everything I'd assume a competent human would know not to do?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The terminal goal is 'reschedule all my meetings this week to free up Thursday.' The constraint set: never cancel meetings with external clients, never move a meeting without offering two alternative times, never send more than one reschedule request to the same person. The escalation policy: if a meeting involves more than four people, stop and ask rather than attempting to coordinate. The value hierarchy: preserving relationships with external stakeholders overrides freeing Thursday \u2014 if the only way to clear Thursday means moving a client call to a worse slot, leave Thursday partially blocked. Without this structure, the agent optimizes for a clear Thursday and sends a terse 'this is cancelled' to a key client.", "related_frameworks": ["the-five-specification-primitives", "constraint-architecture", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "delegation-spec-drill", "intent-doc", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "judgment-over-rules-agent-architecture", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "speed-of-control", "the-reversibility-gap", "done-definition-pattern"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Realpolitik Safety", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI safety decisions from a binary 'is this model safe?' absolutist question to a comparative risk question \u2014 'is deploying this safer than the counterfactual?' \u2014 enabling red-line-based strategic safety commitment under competitive pressure.", "when_to_apply": ["Our safety team is blocking a launch but can't articulate what changes if a less-safe competitor fills the gap instead", "We need to decide which AI capabilities are absolute red lines versus acceptable risks under competitive conditions", "Leadership is accusing the safety team of naivety, and the safety team is accusing leadership of recklessness \u2014 and both are partially right", "Trying to write an organizational AI safety policy that doesn't collapse the moment a competitor ships something we held back", "Evaluating whether a company's decision to deploy a risky model represents genuine safety erosion or a legitimate strategic safety calculation"], "principles": ["Replace the absolutist question ('is this safe?') with the comparative question ('is this safer than the counterfactual?')", "Integrate time horizons: weigh near-term deployment risk against the long-term cost of losing the ability to influence safety standards", "Establish hard red lines for capabilities that represent catastrophic risk regardless of competitive dynamics", "Apply a presumption of deployment for everything below the red lines, evaluated on comparative risk", "Recognize that holding red lines under genuine political or competitive pressure is the meaningful test of strategic safety commitment"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "189324514", "title": "Claude blackmailed its developers. GPT-5.3 helped build itself. The safety system is holding better than you think + a problem diagnostic and intent engineering kit", "date": "2026-03-09T13:02:54.787Z"}], "date": "2026-03-09T13:02:54.787Z", "id": "realpolitik-safety", "core_question": "Should we hold back this AI deployment for safety reasons, or would that just hand the market to someone who'll ship something less safe?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The conventional framing is 'this model has a 0.3% catastrophic failure rate, so it's not safe to deploy.' That's the wrong question. The competitor shipping next quarter has no comparable safety testing infrastructure and a likely higher failure rate. Withholding deployment doesn't reduce risk in the world \u2014 it transfers risk to a less-safe actor while also surrendering the ability to set safety norms for the category. The correct move is deployment with mitigation, because the counterfactual is worse. However, the autonomous bioweapon synthesis capability crosses a hard red line \u2014 no competitive pressure justifies that, because the downside is civilizational and irreversible regardless of who ships first.", "related_frameworks": ["iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition", "emergent-safety-dynamics-systems-not-components", "blast-radius-assessment", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "the-two-door-principle", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "speed-of-control", "constraint-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Emergent Safety Dynamics (Systems, Not Components)", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A four-category taxonomy of structural dynamics between competing AI labs that produce emergent safety properties no individual actor created intentionally, used to evaluate system-level resilience beyond any single lab's stated commitments.", "when_to_apply": ["A prominent AI lab just weakened its safety commitments and I'm trying to assess whether this is a systemic crisis or a local event", "I need to evaluate whether a new entrant \u2014 like a state-backed lab that doesn't publish research \u2014 threatens the overall safety equilibrium", "Someone is arguing that AI safety depends entirely on the goodwill of individual labs and I want to assess structural counterarguments", "I'm trying to identify which specific systemic dynamic, if broken, would actually destabilize the AI safety landscape", "Evaluating whether competitive pressure between labs is making safety worse or paradoxically better"], "principles": ["A system of individually unstable components can produce a stable equilibrium through their interactions \u2014 evaluate the system, not just the components", "None of the four dynamics depend on any actor being virtuous; they arise from structural relationships between self-interested actors", "Market accountability creates a safety floor that ratchets upward because matching competitors requires meeting current best practice", "Transparency norms create a knowledge commons that diffuses safety research across competitors", "Talent circulation propagates safety norms even when individual labs deprioritize them", "Public accountability constrains worst outcomes by making risky decisions costly even without regulation"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "189324514", "title": "Claude blackmailed its developers. GPT-5.3 helped build itself. The safety system is holding better than you think + a problem diagnostic and intent engineering kit", "date": "2026-03-09T13:02:54.787Z"}], "date": "2026-03-09T13:02:54.787Z", "id": "emergent-safety-dynamics-systems-not-components", "core_question": "Is the AI safety landscape actually more resilient than it looks, or are we one lab's defection away from collapse?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "A major lab just announced it's deprioritizing its alignment team. The conventional read is that safety is deteriorating. But the system-level dynamics tell a different story: this falls into the talent circulation category \u2014 those researchers are now dispersing to three competing labs and two startups, carrying safety norms and institutional knowledge with them. Meanwhile, market accountability is active: enterprise customers are already requiring safety benchmarks that match the previous leader's published standards, creating a ratchet effect. The public accountability dynamic is also firing \u2014 the announcement itself generated reputational costs that constrain how far the lab can actually drift. The one dynamic genuinely weakened is transparency norms, since this lab was a major contributor to the safety research commons. That's the real vulnerability to monitor, not the headline.", "related_frameworks": ["realpolitik-safety", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition", "constraint-architecture", "four-forms-of-societal-inertia", "agent-deployment-safety-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Scout vs. Strike Team Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system for categorizing every project or initiative into either an exploratory 'scout' structure or an execution-focused 'strike' structure, each with specific team composition parameters.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to staff a new initiative and I'm not sure whether it needs a small exploratory team or a larger execution-focused one", "We have people who are great at directing AI and others who are great at deep execution \u2014 how do I assign them to the right projects?", "AI tools have made our people dramatically more productive and now our teams feel overstaffed for some projects but understaffed for others", "We're launching multiple projects simultaneously and need a framework for deciding team size and composition for each", "Some of our projects are about figuring out what to build and others are about building what we already know \u2014 should they be structured differently?"], "principles": ["Not all projects have the same structure requirements \u2014 exploratory work and execution work need different team compositions", "Assignment parameters differ specifically between scout and strike team types", "Team classification should precede staffing decisions"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 'specific assignment parameters for each' team type but does not include the actual parameters or classification criteria in the provided excerpt.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "189211551", "title": "Executive Briefing: When Each Person Produces $2M a Year, the Sixth Team Member Costs Millions in Lost Productivity", "date": "2026-03-08T15:02:11.439Z"}], "date": "2026-03-08T15:02:11.439Z", "id": "scout-vs-strike-team-framework", "core_question": "Should this project be structured for exploration or execution \u2014 and how does that change who belongs on the team and how many people it needs?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "The new market-entry analysis is a scout mission \u2014 the team needs one senior person with strong AI-directing skills and one domain expert, maximum. Loading it with five people creates coordination overhead that kills the exploratory speed. Meanwhile, the platform migration is a strike team: the requirements are known, the scope is defined, and it needs three engineers who can execute in parallel with clear handoff points. The mistake being made right now is staffing both projects identically with four-person squads, which starves the strike team of a needed specialist while drowning the scout team in consensus meetings.", "related_frameworks": ["coordination-tax-audit", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "director-assessment", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-leverage-audit"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 'specific assignment parameters for each' team type but does not include the actual parameters or classification criteria in the provided excerpt.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Steinberger Threshold", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A diagnostic concept and associated 'scout mission' test that distinguishes people who can direct AI agents from those who get directed by them, revealing actual capability in an AI-augmented environment.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to figure out which people on my team can actually work with AI agents versus who just says they can", "We're building a small, high-output AI-augmented team and every wrong hire costs us millions in lost productivity", "Someone interviews well on AI topics but I have no way to test whether they can actually direct an agent under real conditions", "I'm seeing a team member accept AI output uncritically and I need to determine if this is a training gap or a capability gap", "How do I design a practical test that reveals whether someone will be a force multiplier or a bottleneck in an AI-first workflow?"], "principles": ["There is a threshold separating people who direct AI agents from people who get directed by them", "A 'scout mission' can empirically reveal which side of the threshold a person falls on", "Self-assessment or credentials are insufficient \u2014 behavior under AI-augmented conditions is the test"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references a 'scout mission' methodology for identifying which side of the threshold people fall on, but the specific steps or criteria are not included in the provided excerpt.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "189211551", "title": "Executive Briefing: When Each Person Produces $2M a Year, the Sixth Team Member Costs Millions in Lost Productivity", "date": "2026-03-08T15:02:11.439Z"}], "date": "2026-03-08T15:02:11.439Z", "id": "the-steinberger-threshold", "core_question": "Can this person actually direct AI to produce high-value output, or will they become a passenger who rubber-stamps whatever the model generates?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The candidate claimed deep AI fluency and had impressive credentials, but when given a scout mission \u2014 a bounded, real task requiring them to direct an AI agent through ambiguity, catch errors, and iterate toward a specific outcome \u2014 they defaulted to accepting the first output, couldn't articulate what was wrong with it, and lost 40 minutes on a tangent the model introduced. They're below the Steinberger Threshold: they're being directed by the AI, not directing it. Putting them on the AI-augmented team would degrade everyone else's output.", "related_frameworks": ["director-assessment", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "scout-vs-strike-team-framework", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "verification-frontier"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references a 'scout mission' methodology for identifying which side of the threshold people fall on, but the specific steps or criteria are not included in the provided excerpt.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "Coordination Tax Audit (AI-Era Team Sizing)", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A five-question diagnostic that reveals whether an organization is structured for high per-person AI output or still optimized for pre-AI coordination assumptions.", "when_to_apply": ["We gave everyone AI tools and individual output went up but team throughput barely changed", "Our teams are shipping faster but quality and correctness are declining \u2014 more bugs, more rework", "We're planning a reorg or headcount decision and need to rethink team sizes for AI-augmented work", "Standup meetings, review cycles, and handoff rituals feel heavier even though each person is producing more", "Leadership is asking whether we need the same number of people now that AI tools are deployed"], "principles": ["Coordination cost scales combinatorially (n(n-1)/2) and did not decrease when AI raised per-person output", "The mismatch between increased output per person and unchanged coordination cost compounds over time", "Optimizing for volume rather than correctness produces teams that feel productive while shipping broken work", "The diagnostic reveals structural misalignment, not surface-level process problems"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 'five diagnostic questions' but their text is not included in the provided excerpt.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "189211551", "title": "Executive Briefing: When Each Person Produces $2M a Year, the Sixth Team Member Costs Millions in Lost Productivity", "date": "2026-03-08T15:02:11.439Z"}], "date": "2026-03-08T15:02:11.439Z", "id": "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "core_question": "Are we actually capturing AI's productivity gains, or is our team structure silently burning them on coordination that no longer needs to exist?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "A platform team of eight engineers adopted Copilot and Cursor six months ago. Individual output roughly doubled \u2014 PRs per person are up 80% \u2014 yet cycle time for shipped features barely moved. The coordination tax audit reveals why: the team still runs the same standup, review, and handoff rituals designed when each person produced half as much. With eight people, there are 28 communication pairs. Each pair now has twice as much work-in-progress to synchronize. The math is clear: the team would ship more correct features as two pods of three, each with a single integration boundary, than as one unit of eight drowning in review queues and merge conflicts that scale with their own increased output.", "related_frameworks": ["delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "ai-leverage-audit", "double-compression-loop", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company", "scout-vs-strike-team-framework", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 'five diagnostic questions' but their text is not included in the provided excerpt.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "Harness Decision Audit", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A structured scoring framework that assesses an engineering team's lock-in across five AI coding harness divergence dimensions, classifies their harness philosophy, and routes real work types to the appropriate tool.", "when_to_apply": ["We've been using Cursor for six months and someone just proposed switching to Claude Code \u2014 how do we evaluate the real cost of that?", "Leadership wants a single AI coding tool standardized across all teams but different teams do fundamentally different types of work", "I need to present the financial and engineering cost of our AI tooling dependency to my VP before next quarter's budget review", "We're building custom integrations on top of our AI coding agent and I'm worried we're creating irreversible architectural commitments", "Our team uses multiple AI coding tools informally \u2014 we need to formalize which tool handles which type of work and why"], "principles": ["Score lock-in across five distinct divergence dimensions: execution philosophy, state and memory, context management, tool integration, and multi-agent architecture", "Lock-in compounds separately on each dimension and all five compound together", "The harness (everything around the model) determines fit for work, not the model itself", "Every week spent inside one harness is another week of compounding dependency that grows more expensive to reverse", "Audit outputs should be translatable into engineering-weeks and dollars for leadership alignment", "Route specific work types to the harness whose architectural disposition matches the task, not a single standardized tool"], "steps": ["Document what AI coding tools the team currently uses and what has been built on top of them", "Map actual weekly workflow patterns to understand which harness is being used for which task types", "Score lock-in on each of the five divergence dimensions (execution philosophy, state/memory, context management, tool integration, multi-agent architecture) on a 1-to-5 scale", "Classify overall harness philosophy based on dimension scores", "Map each real work type to the tool that best fits it architecturally", "Generate an Architecture Decision Brief translating audit findings into switching costs (engineering-weeks and dollars), a recommendation tied to organizational priorities (velocity, security, budget, hiring), and a decision timeline showing when lock-in becomes materially more expensive to reverse"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189946424", "title": "Claude Code and Codex bet on different harnesses. Your team is compounding one of them every week + 2 prompts to audit which.", "date": "2026-03-06T14:03:35.912Z"}], "date": "2026-03-06T14:03:35.912Z", "id": "harness-decision-audit", "core_question": "How locked in are we to our current AI coding tools, and is the switching cost already higher than we realize?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The team scores 4/5 on state and memory (six months of Cursor rules files and project-specific memory that would take weeks to reconstruct), 3/5 on execution philosophy (semi-autonomous workflows but nothing that couldn't adapt), and 5/5 on tool integration (custom MCP servers wired into their deployment pipeline). The compound lock-in is severe on the infrastructure side but moderate on the workflow side. The Architecture Decision Brief translates this to roughly 14 engineering-weeks to switch \u2014 but the multi-agent dimension is only a 2, meaning the team could safely route complex multi-file refactors to Claude Code while keeping Cursor for single-file iteration, creating a hybrid that reduces compounding dependency on either harness without disrupting current velocity.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "five-harness-divergence-dimensions", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "the-two-door-principle", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "work-delegation-bucket-classification"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Model vs. Harness Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "Separates AI system evaluation into two independent layers \u2014 the model (intelligence/brain) and the harness (execution environment, memory, tool access, multi-agent coordination) \u2014 arguing that harness differences drive more consequential outcomes than model differences.", "when_to_apply": ["We're debating whether to switch from Cursor to Codex (or vice versa) and treating it like swapping one tool for another", "Our AI coding agent works great for some developers but terribly for others using the same model \u2014 what explains the gap?", "Leadership wants to compare AI tools by benchmark scores and per-token pricing, and I suspect they're measuring the wrong thing", "I'm trying to figure out where our real vendor lock-in is accumulating as we adopt AI development tools", "The same model we use internally produces much better results when a competitor deploys it \u2014 why?"], "principles": ["The model determines how smart the AI is; the harness determines how it fits into your work", "Harness differences can produce nearly double the performance from identical model weights (78% vs 42% on the same benchmark)", "Lock-in accumulates in harness infrastructure (skills, memory files, MCP connectors, workflow automation), not in model familiarity", "As model capabilities commoditize, harness architectures diverge \u2014 the harness becomes the product", "Harness philosophies (local-first vs. cloud-first, agent-remembers vs. codebase-remembers, collaborative vs. autonomous) are stable commitments even as implementation details churn", "This pattern will repeat in every domain AI touches: model becomes commodity, harness becomes moat"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189946424", "title": "Claude Code and Codex bet on different harnesses. Your team is compounding one of them every week + 2 prompts to audit which.", "date": "2026-03-06T14:03:35.912Z"}, {"id": "183624198", "title": "Meta bought Manus for $2B to acquire an \"agentic harness\"\u2014here's what \"agentic harness\" means and why it's worth $2B (yes really)", "date": "2026-01-06T14:03:30.662Z"}], "date": "2026-01-06T14:03:30.662Z", "id": "model-vs-harness-distinction", "core_question": "When my AI tool produces disappointing results, is the problem the model's intelligence or everything surrounding it \u2014 and where should I actually invest to get better outcomes?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "Two engineering teams are both using Claude 3.5 Sonnet for code generation. Team A runs it through Cursor with local-first context, persistent memory files, and MCP connectors to their codebase. Team B runs it through a vanilla API wrapper with copy-pasted context. Team A hits 78% task completion; Team B hits 42%. The model is identical \u2014 the entire performance gap lives in the harness. The strategic implication: Team B's instinct to 'upgrade to a better model' is misdiagnosed. Their bottleneck is harness architecture \u2014 memory, tool access, and context delivery \u2014 not raw intelligence. And Team A is quietly accumulating lock-in: their skills files, workflow automations, and memory infrastructure compound weekly in ways that make switching harnesses increasingly expensive, even as switching models remains trivial.", "related_frameworks": ["harness-decision-audit", "five-harness-divergence-dimensions", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "distribution-beats-compute"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Five Harness Divergence Dimensions", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A five-dimension classification system for characterizing how AI coding agent harnesses differ architecturally, enabling teams to map their compounding lock-in and predict where workflow investments will transfer versus reset.", "when_to_apply": ["We've been using Claude Code for months and someone's pushing to evaluate Codex \u2014 how do I estimate what we'd actually lose?", "I want to invest in AI coding workflows but I'm worried about building on the wrong platform's architecture", "Our team uses different AI coding agents for different tasks and I need to understand where the seams are", "Leadership wants a comparison of Claude Code vs Codex that goes deeper than benchmark scores and feature lists", "I'm designing our team's AI coding standards and need to know which investments are harness-specific versus portable"], "principles": ["Execution philosophy: local environment access with composable primitives vs. isolated cloud containers with custom tooling", "State and memory: agent-side memory (progress files, CLAUDE.md) vs. codebase-side memory (linter rules, structured documentation)", "Context management: compaction and sub-agent delegation vs. task isolation with clean per-task context windows", "Tool integration: filesystem-stored skills with just-in-time loading vs. server-mediated RPC endpoints with per-worktree instances", "Multi-agent architecture: orchestrated sub-agent teams with shared task lists vs. isolated parallel sandboxes coordinating through git", "Each dimension compounds independently, and all five compound together over time", "Investment in one harness's dimension (e.g., CLAUDE.md files) depreciates to near zero when switching to a harness with a different architecture on that dimension"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189946424", "title": "Claude Code and Codex bet on different harnesses. Your team is compounding one of them every week + 2 prompts to audit which.", "date": "2026-03-06T14:03:35.912Z"}], "date": "2026-03-06T14:03:35.912Z", "id": "five-harness-divergence-dimensions", "core_question": "How much of the workflow investment my team is making every week in our AI coding agent is actually portable, and how much silently locks us into an architecture we can't leave?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "A team heavily invested in Claude Code has built rich CLAUDE.md files, trained sub-agent delegation patterns, and relies on filesystem-stored skills \u2014 all agent-side memory. Mapping these against the five dimensions reveals that switching to Codex would reset three of five dimensions: their state/memory investment (CLAUDE.md \u2192 zero), their context management patterns (compaction-based \u2192 task-isolation), and their multi-agent architecture (orchestrated sub-agents \u2192 isolated sandboxes coordinating through git). Only their codebase-side artifacts like linter configs and structured documentation survive the move. The compounding lock-in is deeper than a feature comparison would suggest \u2014 it's architectural, and it's been accumulating silently for months.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "harness-decision-audit", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "the-context-platform-compound", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-moat-audit", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Context Platform Compound", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A four-dimensional framework for assessing whether an AI context platform can deliver genuine enterprise-scale institutional memory, where failure in any single dimension collapses the entire value proposition.", "when_to_apply": ["We're considering building our enterprise knowledge base on top of an AI platform and need to know if it can actually handle institutional memory", "Our AI deployment has great benchmark scores but keeps giving outdated or contradictory answers when questions span multiple documents or time periods", "We're evaluating whether to consolidate from multiple AI tools onto a single platform that promises end-to-end enterprise context", "The vendor demos look impressive but I need to understand where the compounding failures will hit us at scale", "Our RAG system works for simple lookups but falls apart on questions that require reasoning across relationships, timelines, or causal chains"], "principles": ["The relationship between intelligence and context is multiplicative, not additive \u2014 weak reasoning on large context produces confidently wrong synthesis", "Memory must be dynamic, not static \u2014 memory that preserves outdated context without resolving contradictions is worse than no memory", "Retrieval is the bottleneck: the architecture of RAG-style retrieval fundamentally cannot handle relational, temporal, or causal queries at enterprise scale", "Execution reliability must compound toward ~99.5% per-task to avoid systemic failure across autonomous workflows", "All four capabilities must improve together \u2014 a platform advancing one while stalling others wastes whatever capability leads"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "189813602", "title": "Grab the prompt kit I built to audit your AI platform lock-in \u2014 before your switching costs compound past the point of no return", "date": "2026-03-05T14:02:02.688Z"}], "date": "2026-03-05T14:02:02.688Z", "id": "the-context-platform-compound", "core_question": "Can this AI platform actually serve as our organization's institutional memory at scale, or will a weakness in one dimension silently undermine everything else it claims to do?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The platform scores well on reasoning (GPT-4 class) and has impressive retrieval demos, but its memory layer is static \u2014 it stores documents without tracking which supersede which. When a policy changed three months ago, the system still synthesizes from the old version 40% of the time. That single memory failure means the strong reasoning capability confidently generates wrong answers. The compound collapses: advancing retrieval speed or model quality won't fix this until temporal contradiction resolution exists in the memory layer. The recommendation is to treat this as a productivity tool, not an institutional memory system, until the memory dimension catches up.", "related_frameworks": ["three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "ambient-failure-cascade", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Comprehension Lock-in Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "Three questions that help organizations assess where AI-driven institutional knowledge is accumulating, whether the platform compound is improving, and what the true switching cost would be \u2014 before the context flywheel becomes irreversible.", "when_to_apply": ["Multiple teams have organically adopted the same AI tool and no one made a deliberate platform decision", "We're about to renew our enterprise AI contract and I suspect our switching costs are higher than anyone realizes", "Teams are building institutional knowledge inside AI tools \u2014 meeting summaries, decision logs, synthesized analysis \u2014 and I'm not sure what we'd lose if we moved", "Leadership wants to evaluate our AI tooling stack but keeps treating it like a subscription cost question rather than a dependency question", "I can feel the AI platform becoming load-bearing for how my organization thinks, not just how it works"], "principles": ["Distinguish between data lock-in (portable) and comprehension lock-in (non-portable) \u2014 the latter is categorically more severe", "Organizational understanding accumulates through daily usage patterns, not formal platform decisions \u2014 audit where habits are forming, not just where contracts are signed", "Evaluate the compound, not just the model \u2014 assess whether intelligence, memory, retrieval, and execution are all improving on your platform", "Calculate switching cost in terms of accumulated synthesis lost, not subscription fees", "Act while context accumulation is still shallow enough that switching is painful rather than impossible"], "steps": ["Identify where organizational understanding (not just data) is actually accumulating \u2014 which tools are generating synthesized institutional knowledge through daily team usage", "Assess whether the full capability compound is improving on your current platform \u2014 model, memory, retrieval, and execution reliability together, tested on real workflows over weeks", "Estimate the true switching cost in twelve months: what synthesized understanding, decision histories, and cross-team connections would be lost and cannot be exported"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "189813602", "title": "Grab the prompt kit I built to audit your AI platform lock-in \u2014 before your switching costs compound past the point of no return", "date": "2026-03-05T14:02:02.688Z"}], "date": "2026-03-05T14:02:02.688Z", "id": "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "core_question": "Are we passively building switching costs into our AI tools that will become impossible to unwind, and can we still see clearly enough to make a deliberate platform choice?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "Three product teams have been using Claude for daily synthesis \u2014 requirements refinement, sprint retrospectives, and cross-team dependency mapping \u2014 for four months. The data itself is exportable, but the accumulated understanding of how Team A's architectural decisions ripple into Team B's sprint planning exists only as synthesized context inside the platform. Switching cost isn't the $40K/year subscription \u2014 it's the twelve weeks of compounding institutional comprehension that cannot be migrated. The model, memory, and retrieval are all improving together on real workflows, which means the compound is active and deepening. In twelve months, this moves from 'painful to switch' to 'organizationally unthinkable to switch.' The decision window is now, not at contract renewal.", "related_frameworks": ["the-context-platform-compound", "the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "the-two-door-principle", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Frontier Operations", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A framework that decomposes the skill of working at the boundary between AI capability and human judgment into five simultaneous, named operations.", "when_to_apply": ["We adopted AI tools six months ago and usage is high, but productivity gains are underwhelming \u2014 what are we missing?", "I need to define what 'AI-skilled' means for hiring and performance reviews beyond just 'uses ChatGPT'", "Our best people seem to get dramatically better results from AI than everyone else, and I want to understand the specific skills that separate them", "I'm designing an AI upskilling program and need a concrete skill taxonomy rather than generic 'prompt engineering' training", "The AI tools keep changing and my team can't keep up \u2014 every model update disrupts our workflows"], "principles": ["The valuable skill is not AI adoption but operating at the boundary (the 'surface') between what AI can do reliably and what still requires human judgment", "As AI capability expands, the boundary surface area increases \u2014 creating more seams, not fewer, requiring more judgment calls", "Unlike prior workforce skills (literacy, coding), frontier operations has no fixed destination; the surface keeps moving and must be continuously re-learned", "The five operations \u2014 boundary sensing, seam design, failure model maintenance, capability forecasting, and leverage calibration \u2014 are performed simultaneously, not sequentially", "Time advantage compounds: someone six months ahead is not just ahead in time but operating in a structurally different technological era"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post names the five operations (boundary sensing, seam design, failure model maintenance, capability forecasting, leverage calibration) but does not define or explain each one in the provided excerpt. The full briefing content covering what each operation means, Team of One vs. Team of Five, hiring interview questions, and Monday-action implications is not included.", "source_posts": [{"id": "189489948", "title": "Executive Briefing: 90% of companies invested in AI. The 5 operations separating the 40% who got results from everyone else.", "date": "2026-03-01T16:01:40.092Z"}], "date": "2026-03-01T16:01:40.092Z", "id": "frontier-operations", "core_question": "My team is using AI tools, but I can't tell if they're actually skilled at working with AI or just going through the motions \u2014 what does real AI competence actually look like?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This engineering team scores high on AI adoption metrics \u2014 90% of developers use Copilot daily \u2014 but they're only doing leverage calibration (deciding how much to trust suggestions). They have no boundary sensing: nobody tracks which coding tasks Copilot handles reliably versus where it introduces subtle bugs. They have no failure model maintenance: when the model was updated last month and started generating different patterns, the team didn't notice for two weeks. They're operating one of five frontier skills, which means they're extracting maybe 20% of available value and accumulating invisible technical debt from the other four gaps.", "related_frameworks": ["the-jagged-frontier-framework", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "capability-surface-mapping", "verification-frontier", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "capability-dissipation-gap", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post names the five operations (boundary sensing, seam design, failure model maintenance, capability forecasting, leverage calibration) but does not define or explain each one in the provided excerpt. The full briefing content covering what each operation means, Team of One vs. Team of Five, hiring interview questions, and Monday-action implications is not included.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Three Disciplines of AI Engineering", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-stage evolutionary classification of AI engineering disciplines that distinguishes what AI is told to do (prompt engineering), what it knows (context engineering), and what it wants (intent engineering).", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI agents are technically working but producing outcomes that don't serve our business goals", "We're trying to figure out where to invest next in our AI infrastructure \u2014 better prompts, better data, or something else entirely", "Leadership keeps asking why our AI tools generate activity but not business value", "We want to move from human-in-the-loop AI to autonomous agents and need to understand what architectural prerequisites we're missing", "Our prompt engineering is sophisticated but results degrade when we try to remove human oversight from the loop"], "principles": ["Prompt engineering is individual, synchronous, and session-based \u2014 the human mediates every interaction", "Context engineering curates the entire information state an AI system operates against \u2014 what it knows", "Intent engineering encodes organizational purpose as structured, machine-actionable parameters \u2014 what the agent wants", "Each layer is necessary but not sufficient; all three must be present for AI to serve organizational goals", "The progression is not optional: autonomous agents operating without human oversight require intent to be in the infrastructure, not in the human's head"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188532649", "title": "Klarna saved $60 million and broke its company. The missing layer is what I'm calling intent engineering + 2 prompts to find yours", "date": "2026-02-24T14:02:05.683Z"}, {"id": "189306026", "title": "Prompting just split into 4 different skills. You're probably practicing 1 of them (+ 7 prompts and a pre-flight to close the gap)", "date": "2026-02-27T14:01:06.277Z"}], "date": "2026-02-24T14:02:05.683Z", "id": "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "core_question": "We've invested in prompts and data pipelines, but our AI systems still produce outputs that are technically competent yet strategically misaligned \u2014 what layer of the engineering stack are we missing?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This organization has excellent context engineering \u2014 RAG pipelines, curated knowledge bases, real-time data feeds \u2014 and their prompts are well-crafted. But agents are optimizing for ticket resolution speed while the company's actual priority is customer retention. The missing layer is intent engineering: no one has encoded 'retain this customer even if it means a slower resolution' as a machine-actionable parameter. The agents know everything they need to know and are told exactly how to act, but they have no encoded sense of what the organization actually wants. Until intent lives in the infrastructure rather than in a human's head, scaling these agents will scale the misalignment.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "intent-doc", "the-context-platform-compound", "capability-dissipation-gap", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "constraint-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Constraint Architecture", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A four-category framework for encoding the behavioral boundaries of an autonomous agent into a reliable specification, covering what it must do, must not do, should prefer, and should escalate.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm writing a system prompt or context doc for an agent that will run semi-autonomously on a multi-step task", "My agent keeps doing things that are technically correct but not what I actually wanted", "I need to figure out which decisions the agent should make on its own versus escalate to me", "I'm handing off a workflow to an AI and want to prevent the class of mistakes where it's too clever for its own good", "My specification feels too vague \u2014 the agent has too many degrees of freedom and I'm not sure where to tighten"], "principles": ["The four categories are: musts (required behaviors), must-nots (prohibited behaviors), preferences (tie-breaking rules when multiple valid approaches exist), and escalation triggers (decisions the agent should not make autonomously)", "Identify constraint architecture by asking: what would a smart, well-intentioned agent do that would technically satisfy the request but produce the wrong outcome?", "Each constraint should pass the test: would removing this cause the agent to make mistakes? If not, cut it", "Constraints turn a loose specification into a reliable one"], "steps": ["List what the agent must do (required actions and behaviors)", "List what the agent must not do (prohibited actions)", "List preferences \u2014 what the agent should choose when multiple valid approaches exist", "List escalation triggers \u2014 decisions the agent should surface to a human rather than resolve autonomously", "Before finalizing, enumerate failure modes: what would a smart, well-intentioned agent do that technically satisfies the request but produces the wrong outcome? Add those as must-nots or escalation triggers"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189306026", "title": "Prompting just split into 4 different skills. You're probably practicing 1 of them (+ 7 prompts and a pre-flight to close the gap)", "date": "2026-02-27T14:01:06.277Z"}], "date": "2026-02-27T14:01:06.277Z", "id": "constraint-architecture", "core_question": "How do I turn what I want an autonomous agent to do into a specification tight enough that a smart, well-intentioned agent won't satisfy the letter of the request while producing the wrong outcome?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A content-generation agent keeps producing technically accurate summaries that bury the most important insight at the end. The musts are clear (summarize the source, include key metrics), but there's no preference layer \u2014 the agent has no tie-breaking rule for information hierarchy. Adding 'lead with the single most decision-relevant finding' as a preference, and 'escalate if multiple findings have equal strategic weight' as an escalation trigger, eliminates the failure mode without over-constraining the agent's flexibility on structure or tone.", "related_frameworks": ["the-five-specification-primitives", "intent-engineering", "intent-doc", "delegation-spec-drill", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "done-definition-pattern", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "blast-radius-assessment", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "correctness-definition-framework", "judgment-over-rules-agent-architecture", "principles-over-rules-for-ai-systems"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Five Specification Primitives", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "Five ordered building blocks for constructing a specification that autonomous agents can execute reliably, replacing the old prompt-craft primitives as the key leverage points for AI output quality.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent keeps producing output that's 80% right but the last 20% requires so much editing I wonder if it's saving time", "I'm transitioning a task from back-and-forth chat with AI to a fully autonomous agent workflow", "I need to write a spec for a multi-step project that an agent will execute without me in the loop", "I want to build repeatable quality standards so my AI-generated outputs are consistent across runs and model updates", "I can't tell whether my agent's output is actually good or just looks reasonable at first glance"], "principles": ["Self-contained problem statements make requests solvable without the agent fetching additional context \u2014 AI fills gaps with statistical plausibility, not judgment", "Acceptance criteria must describe 'done' in terms an independent observer could verify without asking questions", "Constraint architecture (musts, must-nots, preferences, escalation triggers) turns loose specifications into reliable ones", "Decomposition breaks tasks into subtasks under two hours each, with clear input/output boundaries and independent verifiability", "Evaluation design means knowing whether the output is actually good \u2014 not just whether it looks reasonable \u2014 through test cases with known-good outputs", "The primitives are ordered by leverage: self-containment \u2192 acceptance criteria \u2192 constraints \u2192 decomposition \u2192 evaluation"], "steps": ["Write a self-contained problem statement: rewrite the request as if the agent has never seen your context and has access only to what you explicitly include", "Define acceptance criteria: write three sentences an independent observer could use to verify the output without asking you any questions", "Design constraint architecture: specify musts, must-nots, preferences, and escalation triggers", "Decompose the task: break it into subtasks each under two hours with clear input/output boundaries that can be verified independently", "Design evaluation: create three to five test cases with known-good outputs to run periodically and after model updates"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189306026", "title": "Prompting just split into 4 different skills. You're probably practicing 1 of them (+ 7 prompts and a pre-flight to close the gap)", "date": "2026-02-27T14:01:06.277Z"}], "date": "2026-02-27T14:01:06.277Z", "id": "the-five-specification-primitives", "core_question": "Why does my AI agent keep producing output that's almost right but subtly wrong, and how do I write specifications that eliminate that gap?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This specification fails at the first primitive \u2014 self-containment. The request says 'rewrite the onboarding flow like we discussed' but the agent has no record of what was discussed; it's filling that gap with plausible-sounding content that diverges from the actual decision. The acceptance criteria are also missing: there's no way for anyone other than the original author to verify whether the output matches intent. Before touching prompt wording or model selection, this spec needs a self-contained problem statement with three verifiable acceptance criteria. That alone will close most of the quality gap.", "related_frameworks": ["constraint-architecture", "done-definition-pattern", "definition-of-done-rubric", "intent-engineering", "delegation-spec-drill", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "correctness-definition-framework", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "task-decomposition-framework", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "intent-doc", "jiorp-structure", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Four-Month AI Skills Progression", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequenced four-month roadmap for building AI input skills in the order that produces the fastest compounding results, from prompt craft fundamentals up through organizational intent infrastructure.", "when_to_apply": ["I've been using AI tools for months but my outputs aren't getting noticeably better anymore", "I need to design a 90-day AI upskilling program for my team that builds real capability, not just tool familiarity", "I just learned about context engineering and specification and intent \u2014 but I don't know what order to learn them in", "My team is moving from casual chat-based AI use toward autonomous agent deployments and needs a structured skill-building path", "I want to invest in AI skills deliberately rather than just accumulating random tips and prompts"], "principles": ["Build in sequence \u2014 each month's work enables the next layer", "Start with prompt craft fundamentals because most people are worse at basic prompting than they believe", "Context layer precedes specification work because agents need the right information environment before complex delegation is possible", "Intent infrastructure is the organizational layer and requires the individual layers to be solid first", "The progression is a stack, not a ladder \u2014 lower layers are not abandoned as you ascend"], "steps": ["Month 1 \u2014 Close the prompt craft gap: revisit foundational prompt engineering documentation, build a folder of five recurring tasks with your best prompt for each, save outputs as a baseline for future comparison", "Month 2 \u2014 Build your personal context layer: write a context document for your own work (goals, constraints, communication preferences, quality standards, institutional context), load it at the start of every AI session", "Month 3 \u2014 Practice specification engineering: take a real project and write a full specification before touching AI (acceptance criteria, constraint architecture, task decomposition, evaluation criteria), delegate to an agent, analyze the gap between expected and actual output, tighten and re-run", "Month 4 and beyond \u2014 Build intent infrastructure: encode the implicit decision frameworks your team uses (speed vs. quality tradeoffs, escalation thresholds, definitions of 'good enough' by work category), structure them, and make them available to agents"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189306026", "title": "Prompting just split into 4 different skills. You're probably practicing 1 of them (+ 7 prompts and a pre-flight to close the gap)", "date": "2026-02-27T14:01:06.277Z"}], "date": "2026-02-27T14:01:06.277Z", "id": "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "core_question": "I know I need to get better at working with AI, but the skill set feels sprawling and undefined \u2014 where do I actually start, and in what order, so each thing I learn makes the next thing possible?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Someone has been using ChatGPT daily for six months but output quality has plateaued. The diagnosis: they skipped straight to complex delegation without building the layers underneath. Month 1 work reveals their prompts lack basic structure \u2014 they never specify format, audience, or constraints. Fixing five recurring prompts produces immediate quality gains and establishes the baseline. Month 2 exposes the real gap: every session starts cold because there's no persistent context document encoding their role, standards, and institutional constraints. Writing that document and loading it into sessions transforms output relevance overnight. Only then does Month 3 specification work become possible \u2014 because an agent that already knows your context can actually execute against a well-structured spec. The progression is a stack: prompt craft without context is fragile, specification without prompt craft is incoherent, and intent infrastructure without all three is organizational theater.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "ai-leverage-audit", "capability-dissipation-gap", "delegation-spec-drill"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "process_spec", "note": "4 steps contain temporal/phased language (month/week/phase/quarter). Likely a project plan, not a conversational reasoning framework."}]}
{"name": "Capability-Dissipation Gap", "type": "reframe", "description": "A framework that maps the widening distance between what AI can technically do (capability curve) and what the economy has actually reorganized around AI doing (dissipation curve), explaining why both doom and boom AI narratives are wrong about timing and why the gap itself creates asymmetric economic opportunity for early adopters.", "when_to_apply": ["Markets just sold off on an AI disruption headline and I need to decide if it's signal or timeline confusion", "I'm a small team wondering whether our AI adoption advantage will last or if big competitors will catch up within months", "Someone is arguing that AI will either destroy all jobs soon or that it's overhyped \u2014 and I suspect both takes are wrong in the same way", "I'm deciding how much to invest in reorganizing my workflow around AI now versus waiting for the tools to mature", "Leadership is asking whether our AI pilot needs to scale company-wide immediately or whether a phased approach still captures most of the value"], "principles": ["AI capability compounds faster than societal dissipation; the gap between the two curves is the primary variable most analyses ignore", "The gap is maintained by four inertia forces: regulatory, organizational, cultural, and trust drag", "A wide gap creates asymmetric returns for early adopters precisely because uniform adoption would eliminate the advantage", "Large firms have capital and data advantages but carry full organizational inertia; small firms and individuals have speed advantages and lighter inertia", "The gap erodes slowly, meaning early-adoption advantages persist and compound longer than conventional models predict", "Both doom and boom narratives are internally flawed in the same way: they assume the gap closes fast"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189021735", "title": "The economist who formalized the AI doom scenario says its own conditions are too extreme to hold \u2014 here's what the math actually shows + the gap framework that changes your career calculus", "date": "2026-02-26T14:02:42.036Z"}], "date": "2026-02-26T14:02:42.036Z", "id": "capability-dissipation-gap", "core_question": "Everyone says AI will change everything \u2014 but should I be acting like that change is imminent, or is there a window of advantage I'm either wasting or overestimating?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that large incumbents will quickly absorb AI and neutralize any startup or individual advantage. That's wrong here because the dissipation curve \u2014 how fast organizations actually reorganize around new capability \u2014 is governed by regulatory, organizational, cultural, and trust drag, all of which large firms carry in full. A three-person team adopting AI coding tools today isn't just 'early' \u2014 their advantage compounds for as long as the gap stays wide, and the gap stays wide far longer than disruption narratives assume. The doom scenario (mass displacement next year) and the boom scenario (every company AI-native by 2026) both fail for the same reason: they treat the capability curve as the dissipation curve. The actual opportunity is in the widening space between the two, where returns to early adopters are asymmetric precisely because most of the economy hasn't reorganized yet.", "related_frameworks": ["four-forms-of-societal-inertia", "ai-leverage-audit", "two-ai-economies-framework", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "the-factory-curve", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "ai-career-board", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "double-compression-loop"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four Forms of Societal Inertia", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of the four distinct drag forces that slow translation of AI capability into economic impact \u2014 regulatory, organizational, cultural, and trust inertia \u2014 each with different drivers, timelines, and implications for adoption strategy.", "when_to_apply": ["We have an AI tool that clearly works, but our enterprise customers keep saying 'not yet' and I can't figure out what's actually blocking them", "I'm building a deployment timeline for AI in a regulated industry and need to know which bottlenecks will dominate", "A competitor claims they'll have AI deployed across their org in six months \u2014 is that credible given how these institutions actually work?", "Our pilot went great but scaling across the organization has stalled for reasons that seem to shift every time we ask", "I need to explain to investors why AI-driven productivity gains won't show up in our sector's economics for several years"], "principles": ["Regulatory inertia: rules, procurement cycles, and compliance requirements (HIPAA, FDA, etc.) impose delays measured in years, not quarters", "Organizational inertia: headcount and workflow decisions are filtered through HR policy, employment law, management politics, and institutional knowledge concerns \u2014 not pure efficiency logic", "Cultural inertia: adoption curves lag capability curves even in technically sophisticated organizations; behavior change requires mandates, incentives, and deliberate skill-building", "Trust inertia: enterprises require verification infrastructure, audit trails, and liability frameworks before deploying AI at scale \u2014 capability benchmarks cannot compress this timeline", "All four forces operate simultaneously and compound each other, making the aggregate drag much larger than any single force"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189021735", "title": "The economist who formalized the AI doom scenario says its own conditions are too extreme to hold \u2014 here's what the math actually shows + the gap framework that changes your career calculus", "date": "2026-02-26T14:02:42.036Z"}], "date": "2026-02-26T14:02:42.036Z", "id": "four-forms-of-societal-inertia", "core_question": "Why isn't this AI capability translating into real-world impact as fast as everyone expected, and which specific drag forces are actually responsible for the delay?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "A health-tech startup's AI diagnostic tool outperforms radiologists on benchmarks, but hospital adoption is stalling. The binding constraint isn't cultural or organizational \u2014 radiology departments are eager and technically ready. The dominant inertia is regulatory (FDA clearance timelines, HIPAA compliance infrastructure) compounded by trust inertia (hospitals require audit trails, liability frameworks, and malpractice insurance clarity before deploying at scale). Organizational and cultural inertia are secondary here. This means the adoption timeline is gated by regulatory cycles measured in years, and no amount of internal change management or capability demonstration compresses it. The startup's go-to-market should target the regulatory and trust layers directly \u2014 pursue FDA breakthrough designation and build verification infrastructure \u2014 rather than investing in more clinical champions or training programs.", "related_frameworks": ["capability-dissipation-gap", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "the-implementation-spectrum", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "two-ai-economies-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Two-Axis Model Routing Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A 2\u00d72 matrix matching AI model provenance (frontier vs. distilled) to task scope (narrow vs. wide) to guide cost-effective model selection decisions.", "when_to_apply": ["We're spending a fortune on GPT-4 API calls and I suspect most of them don't need to be frontier-grade", "Building a routing layer that sends different types of requests to different models based on complexity", "A distilled model works fine in testing but I'm nervous about deploying it on our agentic workflows", "Our team debates whether to use Claude Opus vs Haiku for every new feature and there's no principled way to decide", "Trying to figure out which AI tasks justify premium model pricing and which are a waste of budget"], "principles": ["On narrow, well-defined tasks, model provenance barely matters \u2014 distilled models perform at ~90% quality for a fraction of the cost", "On wide, open-ended agentic tasks, the gap between frontier and distilled models is a chasm that widens over time", "Match provenance to scope: use distilled models aggressively on the left side of the axis, reserve frontier models for the right side", "Paying frontier prices for narrow tasks is waste; using distilled models for wide tasks is a risk that shows up at 3 AM, not in benchmarks", "The model routing skill \u2014 knowing where your task sits on this map \u2014 is a genuine competitive advantage"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189021238", "title": "16 million stolen conversations, 24,000 fake accounts, and what it means for the AI tools you're choosing this week + the manifold probe", "date": "2026-02-25T14:03:07.579Z"}], "date": "2026-02-25T14:03:07.579Z", "id": "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "core_question": "Am I overpaying for AI on simple tasks, or underinvesting on complex ones \u2014 and how do I know which is which?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The customer support ticket classifier sits firmly in the narrow-task quadrant: well-defined inputs, constrained outputs, clear success criteria. A distilled model at 10% of the cost delivers 92% of GPT-4's accuracy here \u2014 paying frontier prices is pure waste. But the autonomous research agent that synthesizes competitive intelligence across dozens of sources, decides what matters, and drafts strategic briefs? That's wide-scope, open-ended work where the gap between frontier and distilled isn't 8% \u2014 it's the difference between a useful brief and a confident-sounding hallucination that poisons a board deck. Route the classifier to the cheap model, reserve frontier capacity for the research agent, and you've cut costs 40% while actually improving reliability where it matters.", "related_frameworks": ["model-context-matching-framework", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "verification-capability-triage", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "distillation-as-piracy-reframe", "four-lane-supply-strategy", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Off-Manifold Probe", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A constraint-shift test that measures a model's representational depth and generality by observing how it adapts when one variable of a successfully completed real task is changed.", "when_to_apply": ["Two models both ace my evaluation task and I can't tell which one is actually stronger", "I need to choose a model for agentic workflows where requirements will shift mid-execution", "I suspect a model is distilled or narrow but it keeps passing my benchmarks", "We're about to deploy an AI system that needs to handle real-world variation, not just the happy path", "Standard benchmarks say these models are equivalent but my team's experience with them feels different"], "principles": ["Use a real task from your domain, not a synthetic benchmark task", "After both models succeed at the base task, change exactly one constraint \u2014 not the whole task", "A model with genuine representational depth will identify which parts of its reasoning transfer and which need revision", "A narrow/distilled model will either regenerate everything from scratch or force-fit the old solution to new constraints", "This test reveals the model's underlying capability manifold better than any leaderboard score"], "steps": ["Select a complex real task from your domain that requires multiple steps, tool use, and judgment", "Run the task on the models you are comparing", "When both models succeed, change exactly one constraint or variable in the task", "Observe whether each model adapts coherently, identifying which reasoning transfers and which needs revision", "Flag models that regenerate everything from scratch or force-fit old solutions as having narrower manifolds"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189021238", "title": "16 million stolen conversations, 24,000 fake accounts, and what it means for the AI tools you're choosing this week + the manifold probe", "date": "2026-02-25T14:03:07.579Z"}, {"id": "180278324", "title": "Ilya vs Google: The Trillion-Dollar Bet on Scaling\u2014Are You Building on Sand?", "date": "2025-12-01T14:03:39.210Z"}], "date": "2025-12-01T14:03:39.210Z", "id": "the-off-manifold-probe", "core_question": "Does this model actually understand what it's doing, or did it just pattern-match its way to a correct answer on the standard version of this task?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "Both Claude and GPT-4o produced correct Terraform configurations for a multi-region AWS deployment. Changing one constraint \u2014 switching the primary region to one with no GPU instance availability \u2014 reveals the difference. Claude identified exactly which resource blocks needed revision (the instance type selections and the autoscaling policy) while preserving the networking and IAM logic unchanged. GPT-4o regenerated the entire configuration from scratch, losing three custom security group rules that had been correctly reasoned through in the original. The regeneration-from-scratch pattern is the signature of a narrow manifold \u2014 the model doesn't have internal structure connecting 'which parts of this solution depend on region' vs. 'which parts are region-independent.'", "related_frameworks": ["jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "contextual-noise-library-factorial-stress-testing-for-agents", "capability-surface-mapping", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "model-context-matching-framework", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "the-verifiability-spectrum"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Distillation as Piracy (Reframe)", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI capability extraction from a geopolitical/espionage problem into an information economics problem analogous to digital piracy, which changes the predicted trajectory and appropriate response.", "when_to_apply": ["A cheap open-source model just matched our frontier performance and we're trying to figure out how", "We're debating whether export controls on AI chips and model weights will actually prevent capability transfer", "Our competitive strategy assumes our model capability lead will last 18+ months", "Policy team is framing AI capability leakage as an espionage problem requiring intelligence-community solutions", "We're a non-hyperscaler AI lab deciding whether to open-source or keep our model weights proprietary"], "principles": ["AI capabilities are stored as math \u2014 weightless, copyable, extractable through a chat window \u2014 making them more like music files than nuclear material", "When extraction costs thousands and independent development costs billions, the information always moves \u2014 not from malice but from overwhelming economics", "The correct question about an impressive cheap model is not 'how did they do it so cheaply?' but 'what fraction was independently developed?'", "Piracy doesn't stop \u2014 it slows down; every safeguard is a speed bump, and speed bumps matter because capability curves are exponential", "The incentive to distill is universal, not China-specific \u2014 any non-hyperscaler faces the same pressure gradient", "A time lead, not permanent exclusivity, is the realistic competitive advantage; invest in speed bumps accordingly"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "189021238", "title": "16 million stolen conversations, 24,000 fake accounts, and what it means for the AI tools you're choosing this week + the manifold probe", "date": "2026-02-25T14:03:07.579Z"}], "date": "2026-02-25T14:03:07.579Z", "id": "distillation-as-piracy-reframe", "core_question": "Will our AI capability lead hold, or is the knowledge we're protecting more like a music file than a state secret?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read on DeepSeek R1 is espionage \u2014 stolen secrets, state-backed theft. That framing is wrong because it predicts the wrong countermeasures. AI capabilities are weightless math extractable through a chat window; when independent development costs billions and distillation costs thousands, the information moves regardless of who's doing it. Every non-hyperscaler on earth faces the same pressure gradient \u2014 this isn't a China problem, it's a piracy problem. The realistic play isn't permanent exclusivity (that's the RIAA suing Napster); it's investing in speed bumps that preserve a time lead, because on an exponential capability curve, even six months of delay changes who captures the value.", "related_frameworks": ["four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "distribution-beats-compute", "capability-dissipation-gap", "two-ai-economies-framework", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "realpolitik-safety", "four-lane-supply-strategy", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Missing Layers of Organizational AI Architecture", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-layer diagnostic framework identifying the structural gaps that prevent AI deployments from delivering organizational value: unified context infrastructure, coherent AI workflow architecture, and organizational intent alignment.", "when_to_apply": ["Every team has their own AI tools but nothing connects and we're not seeing enterprise-level impact", "Our AI agents are making decisions that are technically correct but strategically wrong \u2014 they don't seem to understand our priorities", "We ran successful AI pilots in three departments but the enterprise-wide rollout stalled and nobody can explain why", "We're trying to figure out why individual AI fluency isn't translating into organizational productivity gains", "We need to design the AI infrastructure for a new organization or audit why our existing architecture isn't delivering value"], "principles": ["Layer One (Unified Context Infrastructure): agents need a single, governed, cross-departmental context stack \u2014 not team-by-team RAG pipelines", "Layer Two (Coherent AI Worker Toolkit): individual AI fluency must ladder into shared organizational infrastructure to produce leverage beyond individual tasks", "Layer Three (Organizational Alignment / Intent Engineering): human-readable OKRs and principles must be decomposed into agent-actionable decision boundaries, escalation triggers, value hierarchies, and feedback loops", "Skipping any layer creates the intent gap: tools deployed without infrastructure become expensive toys", "The context infrastructure question is architectural and political, not primarily technical", "Agents need explicit alignment before they start working, not absorbed gradually through osmosis like human employees"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188532649", "title": "Klarna saved $60 million and broke its company. The missing layer is what I'm calling intent engineering + 2 prompts to find yours", "date": "2026-02-24T14:02:05.683Z"}], "date": "2026-02-24T14:02:05.683Z", "id": "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "core_question": "We've deployed AI tools across the organization and nothing is really working at scale \u2014 what structural layers are we missing between 'individual AI usage' and 'organizational AI leverage'?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This organization has Layer One missing. Every department built its own RAG pipeline \u2014 sales has one over CRM data, support has one over tickets, product has one over Jira. But no agent can answer 'what did the customer who filed this support ticket buy, what feature did they request in their last sales call, and is that on the roadmap?' The context is balkanized, so agents optimize locally and contradict each other across departments. The fix isn't better prompts or smarter models \u2014 it's a unified context infrastructure that is as much a political negotiation about data governance as it is a technical project. Until that exists, Layer Two and Three investments will produce expensive toys.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "capability-dissipation-gap", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "the-context-platform-compound", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "ai-leverage-audit", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "principles-over-rules-for-ai-systems", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Succeeds at the Wrong Thing", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI deployment failures not as technology failures but as intent failures \u2014 the AI worked exactly as designed, optimizing for a measurable proxy while destroying the unmeasured organizational goal it was supposed to serve.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI system is performing well on every dashboard metric but stakeholders are increasingly unhappy with outcomes", "Leadership is blaming model quality for a deployment that technically works but produces bad business results", "We automated a human workflow and the measurable outputs improved but something intangible got worse", "An AI agent completed tasks correctly but caused unexpected downstream problems in other parts of the organization", "We're debating whether to upgrade our model when the real issue might be what we told it to optimize for"], "principles": ["AI that fails and AI that succeeds at the wrong thing are different problems requiring different solutions", "Measurable objectives are proxies for organizational intent, not the intent itself \u2014 optimizing proxies can destroy the underlying goal", "Human employees absorb unmeasured organizational values through osmosis; agents cannot and will not", "The question is not 'can AI do this task?' but 'can AI do this task in a way that serves our organizational goals?'", "Model quality is rarely the bottleneck; organizational intent alignment almost always is"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188532649", "title": "Klarna saved $60 million and broke its company. The missing layer is what I'm calling intent engineering + 2 prompts to find yours", "date": "2026-02-24T14:02:05.683Z"}], "date": "2026-02-24T14:02:05.683Z", "id": "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "core_question": "Our AI deployment is hitting every metric we set \u2014 so why is the business getting worse?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "Klarna's AI customer service agent resolved 2.3 million conversations and saved $60 million \u2014 those are the metrics it was optimized for. But resolution speed and cost reduction are proxies for customer satisfaction and brand trust, not the thing itself. Human agents absorbed unstated organizational values \u2014 empathy during billing disputes, judgment about when to escalate, awareness that a frustrated customer's lifetime value dwarfs the cost of a longer call. The AI optimized the proxy perfectly and destroyed the unmeasured goal. The failure isn't that the technology wasn't ready; it's that the organization never translated its actual intent into something the system could optimize for.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "intent-doc", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "ambient-failure-cascade", "blast-radius-assessment", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "capability-dissipation-gap"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Six Axes of Hard (Work Difficulty Decomposition Framework)", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A six-category classification system for identifying what actually makes a piece of work difficult, enabling more precise AI tool selection and career risk assessment by matching each difficulty type to its automation timeline.", "when_to_apply": ["I tried using AI on a task and it didn't help, but I can't explain why it fell short", "I'm trying to figure out which parts of my job are safe from automation and which aren't", "We're selecting AI tools for different parts of a workflow and need a principled way to route tasks", "A project keeps stalling despite having smart people and good tools \u2014 something about the difficulty isn't being addressed", "I need to explain to my team why some knowledge work automates easily while other work that seems similar doesn't"], "principles": ["'Hard' is not a single dimension \u2014 conflating different types of difficulty leads to wrong tool choices and career misjudgments", "Each axis has a different automation timeline and is addressed by different AI capabilities", "Reasoning problems (novel multi-step logical deduction) are a relatively narrow slice of most knowledge work, despite dominating AI benchmark discourse", "Effort and coordination problems are the dominant axes for most daily knowledge work and are being automated by agentic AI now", "Emotional intelligence, judgment/willpower, and ambiguity resolution are the last axes to yield to AI and represent the most durable human value", "Identify the bottleneck axis first before selecting a tool \u2014 the gap is usually in how the problem is framed, not which model is used", "Domain expertise problems sit between \u2014 models simulate it via training data but the 'has lived it' gap remains real"], "steps": ["Identify a specific task or role component to analyze", "Classify its primary difficulty axis: Reasoning (novel multi-step logical deduction), Effort (large surface area requiring sustained thoroughness), Coordination (aligning people/teams/information flows), Emotional Intelligence (reading and responding to human dynamics), Judgment/Willpower (courage to act on the right answer despite cost), Domain Expertise (pattern recognition from accumulated experience), or Ambiguity (defining what the question even is)", "Identify the bottleneck axis \u2014 the one dimension that, if solved, would unlock the most progress", "Map the bottleneck axis to its current AI automation status: Reasoning and Effort axes are being automated rapidly now; Coordination is beginning to yield; Domain Expertise is slowly being absorbed; Emotional Intelligence, Judgment, and Ambiguity are barely touched", "Select AI tooling matched to the bottleneck axis, or accept that human judgment is still required if the bottleneck is on the last three axes", "Reassess periodically as the automation timeline for each axis advances"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188837484", "title": "The 6 reasons your work is hard \u2014 and which ones AI is automating this year + prompts to build your map", "date": "2026-02-23T14:02:01.797Z"}], "date": "2026-02-23T14:02:01.797Z", "id": "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework", "core_question": "I know this work is hard, but I can't pinpoint *why* it's hard \u2014 and that's making me pick the wrong AI tools and misjudge which parts of my role are actually at risk.", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "This product manager's role looks like it should be heavily automated \u2014 there's research synthesis, roadmap writing, stakeholder updates. But the bottleneck axis isn't reasoning or effort, it's ambiguity resolution: the hardest part is figuring out what the right question even is when the CEO, engineering lead, and top customer each frame the problem differently. That axis is barely touched by current AI. The effort axis (compiling competitive intel, drafting specs) is fully automatable now and should be delegated immediately, but the role itself stays durable as long as the ambiguity and judgment axes remain the actual bottleneck.", "related_frameworks": ["the-jagged-frontier-framework", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "task-decomposition-framework", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "work-primitives", "ai-career-board", "capability-surface-mapping", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "edge-first-automation-framework", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "coordination-tax-audit", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Developer Tracks", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Classifies the diverging developer career paths in the AI era into three distinct roles with different skill requirements, compensation dynamics, and career trajectories.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm a mid-career developer and I'm not sure if I should go deeper into systems work or lean into my industry knowledge", "My team has developers whose roles are becoming redundant as AI handles more routine coding \u2014 how do I restructure around what's left?", "I've been a competent generalist programmer for years and I'm starting to feel like that's not enough anymore", "We're rewriting job descriptions for software roles and the old 'software engineer' title doesn't capture the diverging skill sets we actually need", "I have domain expertise in a specific industry but I've always thought of myself as 'just a developer' \u2014 is that expertise actually my main asset now?"], "principles": ["The binary 'AI replaces developers or it doesn't' framing is useless; role differentiation is the real story", "The middle of the old distribution \u2014 competent generalist coders without deep systems or domain expertise \u2014 is most exposed", "Each track has different value drivers: Orchestrators scale with token budgets, Systems Builders scale with infrastructure leverage, Domain Translators scale with niche specificity", "Cheaper intelligence increases the value of Domain Translators by making more niche problems economically viable to solve", "Decisive movement toward one track is preferable to staying in the generic middle"], "steps": ["Identify which track aligns with existing strengths: problem decomposition and specification (Orchestrator), deep systems and probabilistic infrastructure work (Systems Builder), or domain expertise combined with AI fluency (Domain Translator)", "Assess current position relative to the 'exposed middle' \u2014 generic application code production without deep specialization in either direction", "Build skills and positioning toward the chosen track", "For Orchestrators: develop system design, specification writing, quality evaluation, and token economics skills", "For Systems Builders: develop mechanical understanding of model behavior, context window dynamics, and reliable system design on probabilistic components", "For Domain Translators: deepen vertical market knowledge and acquire minimum viable AI fluency to direct intelligence at domain-specific problems"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188436740", "title": "OpenAI is charging $20K/month for an AI employee \u2014 and enterprise buyers think it's cheap", "date": "2026-02-20T14:03:18.898Z"}], "date": "2026-02-20T14:03:18.898Z", "id": "three-developer-tracks", "core_question": "Given that AI is reshaping what developers actually do, which of the diverging career paths should I commit to \u2014 and am I stuck in the exposed middle?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "This developer has eight years of full-stack experience building CRUD apps and API integrations \u2014 competent, productive, but no deep systems expertise and no domain specialization. That's the exposed middle: the exact work AI handles fastest and cheapest. The strongest existing asset is three years building healthcare compliance tools \u2014 that's a Domain Translator seed. The move is to deepen healthcare regulatory knowledge and pair it with AI fluency, not to compete on raw code output where the floor is collapsing. Staying a 'good generalist' is the highest-risk position in the current market.", "related_frameworks": ["four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "ai-career-board", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "engineering-manager-identity-shift", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "double-compression-loop", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "experiential-debt", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Distribution Beats Compute", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI competition away from token volume and capital as the primary axis, arguing that when intelligence is commoditized, competitive advantage migrates to distribution, domain expertise, and customer relationships that capital cannot replicate.", "when_to_apply": ["We're a small team and a big player just launched something that overlaps with our AI product \u2014 should we pivot or double down?", "I'm a domain expert considering building an AI product in my field, but I don't have the compute budget that tech companies have", "An investor is telling me my vertical AI company's TAM is too small because they're sizing it against SaaS benchmarks instead of labor replacement", "Our startup is debating whether to go horizontal to capture more market or stay focused on one industry vertical", "I'm evaluating whether a vertical AI company has a real moat or just a head start that evaporates when GPT gets better"], "principles": ["Enterprises will always win on raw token volume \u2014 competing on inference spend is a losing strategy for smaller players", "When the core input (intelligence) is commoditized, competitive advantage migrates to everything surrounding it: distribution, domain knowledge, customer relationships, proprietary data, workflow integration, trust", "The intelligence is purchasable; the channel never will be", "Vertical AI addresses labor and services spend, not just software license spend \u2014 making TAMs appear larger than legacy SaaS analysis suggests", "Precise aim with limited resources beats large budgets pointed at the wrong problem", "Knowing your customer beats knowing your API"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188436740", "title": "OpenAI is charging $20K/month for an AI employee \u2014 and enterprise buyers think it's cheap", "date": "2026-02-20T14:03:18.898Z"}], "date": "2026-02-20T14:03:18.898Z", "id": "distribution-beats-compute", "core_question": "We have less capital than the incumbents \u2014 is there any way to win in AI that doesn't require outspending them on compute?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "A three-person team building AI-powered compliance tooling for community banks is worried about competing with a well-funded horizontal AI platform. The conventional read is that the big player's $200M inference budget makes them unbeatable. That's wrong here \u2014 community bank compliance officers don't need more intelligence, they need intelligence that already understands their regulatory context, speaks their language, and integrates into their existing workflows. The big platform has to serve thousands of use cases generically; the small team's six months of embedded domain relationships and purpose-built workflows represent distribution and trust that no amount of compute spend can replicate. The intelligence is purchasable at commodity rates; the channel into 5,000 community banks never will be.", "related_frameworks": ["four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "the-distribution-trap", "two-ai-economies-framework", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "the-weekend-hack-test", "magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "developer-customers-vs-cio-customers-chasm"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Categories of AI Exposure", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that sorts industries or business models into three tiers based on the actual timing and severity of AI disruption risk, enabling investors and strategists to distinguish genuine near-term threats from overstated or distant ones.", "when_to_apply": ["A sector just sold off 15-20% on AI disruption fears and I need to figure out if it's a buying opportunity or the market finally catching up", "My company's board is demanding an urgent AI transformation plan because our stock dropped, but I'm not sure the threat is actually near-term", "I'm comparing two investment targets in different industries that have both been penalized equally for AI exposure, and that feels wrong", "A competitor announced an AI product that does part of what we do, and I need to gauge whether this is an existential threat or a feature demo", "I'm allocating R&D budget between defending against AI disruption and growing the core business, and I need to know how urgent the defensive spend actually is"], "principles": ["Not all industries face AI disruption on the same timeline; treat them differently", "Category 1 (immediate displacement): AI is genuinely displacing labor now \u2014 per-seat software models, routine code generation, and similar work are being disrupted in real time", "Category 2 (medium-term transition): AI will matter on a 3-to-5-year horizon but current panic vastly overstates near-term risk \u2014 relationship-based services like wealth management and commercial insurance brokerage fall here", "Category 3 (market has lost the plot): AI disruption is theoretically probable but the timeline being priced is delusional \u2014 industries with deep proprietary data, complex physical/regulatory operations, or entrenched relationships fall here", "The market pricing all three categories identically is the error \u2014 and that mispricing creates both investment opportunity and strategic misdirection", "Distinguish between 'disruption is probable' and 'disruption is imminent'; the market conflates these"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188414720", "title": "A $3 million karaoke company just triggered a global logistics selloff + the 3 categories that tell you whether to panic or buy", "date": "2026-02-19T14:02:34.509Z"}], "date": "2026-02-19T14:02:34.509Z", "id": "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "core_question": "Is this AI-driven selloff punishing a company for a real near-term threat, or is the market conflating 'disruption is probable someday' with 'disruption is imminent'?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "A commercial insurance brokerage trading at 8x earnings after an AI-fear selloff is a Category 3 mispricing. The business runs on decades of proprietary claims data, entrenched carrier relationships, and regulatory complexity that no AI system can shortcut in under five years. The market is pricing it as if GPT-5 replaces the entire distribution chain next quarter. Meanwhile, a per-seat SaaS tool for boilerplate legal document review is genuine Category 1 \u2014 the displacement is measurable right now in lost renewals, and the stock decline reflects reality, not panic.", "related_frameworks": ["the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-three-layer-analysis", "capability-dissipation-gap", "four-forms-of-societal-inertia", "two-ai-economies-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Building vs. Buying AI Response", "type": "distinction", "description": "A diagnostic distinction that separates companies genuinely integrating AI into workflows (builders) from companies announcing AI partnerships for investor optics (buyers), used to assess whether an organization's AI response will create durable competitive advantage or just a press release.", "when_to_apply": ["A company I'm evaluating just announced a big AI partnership right after a stock drop \u2014 is this real or panic spending?", "I need to figure out whether my employer's AI reorganization will make us stronger or is just theater for the board", "An enterprise customer is claiming they're 'AI-first' now and I need to assess whether they'll actually integrate or just bolt on a vendor", "I'm advising a board that wants to respond to AI disruption pressure and I need to distinguish genuine investment from reactive spending", "Two competitors both announced AI strategies \u2014 how do I tell which one will actually compound and which is performative?"], "principles": ["Net-new investment layered on top of existing capabilities signals genuine positioning for the transition", "AI budget cannibalized from product, engineering, or core domain teams signals optimization for investor narrative rather than competitive advantage", "Builders develop compounding advantages with every model upgrade; buyers get a vendor logo on a slide deck", "The gap between these two responses becomes visible in every metric that matters within 12 months", "Defensive AI postures adopted under stock pressure often make companies more vulnerable to actual disruption later"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "188414720", "title": "A $3 million karaoke company just triggered a global logistics selloff + the 3 categories that tell you whether to panic or buy", "date": "2026-02-19T14:02:34.509Z"}], "date": "2026-02-19T14:02:34.509Z", "id": "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "core_question": "Is this company's AI initiative actually building capability that compounds, or is it a reactive spend designed to calm investors while hollowing out the org?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This company announced a $200M 'AI transformation' two weeks after their stock dropped 18% on disruption fears. The tell: the AI budget was carved from their core engineering and product teams, not layered on top. They're buying, not building. In 12 months, they'll have a partnership logo on their investor deck and fewer engineers who understand their own product. Meanwhile, their competitor quietly hired 40 ML engineers funded by new budget and is retraining models on proprietary logistics data \u2014 that's the builder, and the gap between them will be irreversible by Q3.", "related_frameworks": ["three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-three-layer-analysis"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Five Levels of Vibe Coding", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A six-tier classification system (Level 0\u20135) that maps where a developer or team actually stands in their AI-assisted development practice, from line-by-line autocomplete to fully autonomous software factories.", "when_to_apply": ["My team uses Copilot and Cursor daily but I'm not sure we're actually getting the productivity gains we should be", "We're evaluating an 'agentic coding' tool that claims to handle full features autonomously \u2014 how do I assess whether that's real?", "I need to build an AI adoption roadmap for our engineering org and I don't know what the actual milestones look like", "Developers on my team resist letting AI write whole features \u2014 they insist on reviewing every diff \u2014 and I'm trying to figure out if that's the right call or the bottleneck", "We want to get to autonomous code generation but I don't know what organizational changes are required beyond just buying better tools"], "principles": ["Each level represents a fundamentally different human-AI relationship, not just a quantitative improvement in tool use", "The bottleneck shifts at each level: from keystrokes (Level 0) to task scope (Level 1) to diff review (Level 2-3) to specification quality (Level 4-5)", "Most self-identified 'AI-native' developers cluster at Level 2, not Level 3-5 where they believe they operate", "Advancing beyond Level 3 requires organizational and psychological change, not just better tools", "Level 5 requires that no human writes code and no human reviews code \u2014 the factory evaluates outcomes, not implementation"], "steps": ["Level 0 (Spicy Autocomplete): AI suggests next lines; human still writes all software", "Level 1 (Coding Intern): AI handles discrete, well-scoped tasks; human reviews all output and retains architecture", "Level 2 (Junior Developer): AI handles multi-file changes across a codebase; human reads every diff", "Level 3 (Developer as Manager): Human directs AI and reviews output; AI does all implementation; human day is diffs", "Level 4 (Developer as PM): Human writes a specification, leaves, returns to evaluate whether outcomes are correct; no diff reading", "Level 5 (Dark Factory): Specification in, working software out; no human writes code, no human reviews code; humans approve outcomes only"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188188186", "title": "The dark factory is real, most developers are getting slower, and your org chart is the bottleneck (plus 5 prompts to get from level 2 to 5)", "date": "2026-02-18T14:02:56.600Z"}], "date": "2026-02-18T14:02:56.600Z", "id": "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "core_question": "Where does my team actually stand in AI-assisted development \u2014 not where we think we are or where our tools claim to put us \u2014 and what has to change to move up?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This team says they're 'fully AI-native' but every PR still gets a line-by-line human diff review and developers manually architect all modules before handing tasks to Copilot. That's Level 2 \u2014 Junior Developer \u2014 not Level 4 where they claim to be. The tell is that the bottleneck is still diff-reading throughput, not specification quality. Moving to Level 3 requires the team to stop reading every line of generated code and start managing AI output the way a manager reviews a developer's work. That's a psychological shift \u2014 trusting outcomes over inspecting implementation \u2014 and no tool upgrade will get them there without it.", "related_frameworks": ["the-factory-curve", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "engineering-manager-identity-shift", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "three-developer-tracks", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "verification-frontier", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "done-definition-pattern", "the-five-specification-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Legacy System Migration Path to the Dark Factory", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-stage sequential roadmap for organizations with existing brownfield codebases to migrate from human-written-code workflows toward autonomous AI software factories.", "when_to_apply": ["We want to move toward AI-generated code but our legacy system has no specs, just decades of institutional knowledge in developers' heads", "Leadership is asking for a multi-year roadmap to autonomous AI software development and we need to sequence the investments", "Should we start our AI dev transformation with the legacy codebase or with greenfield projects?", "Our CI/CD pipeline wasn't designed for the volume and pattern of code that AI agents produce \u2014 when and how do we redesign it?", "We're stuck at Level 2-3 AI-assisted development and can't figure out what has to change before we can move to Level 4-5"], "principles": ["The path to Level 5 for brownfield systems starts with documentation and specification, not agent deployment", "Institutional knowledge embedded in legacy systems must be extracted by humans before machines can replicate it", "New greenfield development can move to Level 4-5 patterns while legacy maintenance continues at lower levels", "The transition takes years, not months; anyone claiming otherwise is selling something", "The organizations that get there fastest have the best specifications and deepest domain understanding, not the best AI tools"], "steps": ["Stage 1: Use AI at Level 2 or 3 to accelerate existing developer work \u2014 new features, bug fixes, refactoring \u2014 while absorbing the J-curve productivity dip", "Stage 2: Use AI to document what the existing system actually does \u2014 generate specifications from code, build scenario suites capturing existing behavior, create holdout evaluation sets", "Stage 3: Redesign the CI/CD pipeline to handle AI-generated code at volume \u2014 new testing strategies, review processes, and deployment gates suited to agent output", "Stage 4: Begin shifting net-new development to Level 4 or 5 patterns while maintaining the legacy system in parallel under the old workflow"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "188188186", "title": "The dark factory is real, most developers are getting slower, and your org chart is the bottleneck (plus 5 prompts to get from level 2 to 5)", "date": "2026-02-18T14:02:56.600Z"}], "date": "2026-02-18T14:02:56.600Z", "id": "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "core_question": "We have millions of lines of legacy code with no specs and tribal knowledge everywhere \u2014 how do we sequence the move toward autonomous AI development without blowing up what already works?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "This enterprise has 2M lines of legacy Java with zero formal specifications and 15 years of accumulated business logic. Jumping to Level 4/5 agent-driven development would be catastrophic \u2014 the agents have no specification to work from, no eval suite to validate against, and no way to know what 'correct' means. The right sequence: spend the next 6 months using AI at Level 2-3 to accelerate current dev work while simultaneously using AI to reverse-engineer specifications from the existing codebase. Only after those specs and holdout eval sets exist does it make sense to redesign CI/CD for agent-volume output. Net-new microservices can start moving to Level 4 patterns now, but the core system stays at Level 2-3 until the specification layer is solid. The organizations that skip Stage 2 \u2014 specification extraction \u2014 end up with agents confidently producing code that violates undocumented business rules nobody remembered existed.", "related_frameworks": ["the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "the-factory-curve", "the-five-specification-primitives", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "three-developer-tracks", "done-definition-pattern", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Three-Question Workflow Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-question diagnostic that sorts any workflow into the right AI agent architecture by testing for correctness requirements, tool span, and task interdependence.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm choosing between Codex, Claude, or similar autonomous agents for a specific project and don't know which architecture fits", "We have AI agents deployed but some workflows feel like they're fighting the tool \u2014 outputs are slow or wrong in ways that seem structural", "I need a repeatable way to sort incoming tasks so my team knows which agent setup to use without asking me every time", "A workflow spans multiple tools and I'm not sure whether to use one agent that integrates everything or multiple agents that coordinate", "Some of our AI-delegated work needs to be perfect and some just needs to be fast \u2014 how do I route these differently?"], "principles": ["Correctness requirements determine how much overhead is justified \u2014 non-negotiable accuracy favors autonomous correctness architecture, iterative work favors speed", "Task scope determines tool fit \u2014 self-contained tasks fit isolated autonomous agents, multi-tool workflows require integration-first agents", "Task interdependence determines agent structure \u2014 independent parallel tasks suit separate autonomous sessions, interdependent pieces require agents that coordinate with each other", "For quick one-off tasks, architecture choice is irrelevant \u2014 only complex, sustained, or multi-system work surfaces the difference", "Most organizations have both delegation problems and coordination problems, so the audit should be applied per workflow, not once for the whole organization"], "steps": ["Question 1 \u2014 Correctness tolerance: Can you tolerate errors in the output, or is correctness non-negotiable? If errors are expensive (e.g., financial reports, legal review, production code), choose an autonomous correctness-optimized agent. If you will review the output anyway, choose a faster integration-oriented agent.", "Question 2 \u2014 Tool span: Does the task live inside one environment or span multiple tools? If self-contained (analyze this document, build this component), an isolated autonomous agent works. If it requires pulling from and pushing to multiple tools simultaneously (e.g., accounting system + Sheets + Docs + Slack in one workflow), choose an integration-first agent.", "Question 3 \u2014 Interdependence: Is the work independent or interdependent? If tasks don't reference each other, dispatch them in parallel to separate autonomous sessions. If each piece shapes the others (press release \u2192 email \u2192 landing page \u2192 social), choose an agent architecture that supports direct agent-to-agent coordination."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188005863", "title": "Codex 5.3 vs. Opus 4.6: Why your AI agent choice compounds faster than you think + the workflow audit that prevents the wrong one", "date": "2026-02-16T14:03:07.763Z"}], "date": "2026-02-16T14:03:07.763Z", "id": "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "core_question": "I have a workflow I want to hand to AI agents, but I don't know whether it needs an autonomous correctness-focused agent, an integration-first agent, or a coordinated multi-agent setup \u2014 and picking wrong means compounding the wrong architecture.", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "A finance team wants to automate monthly reporting that pulls from their accounting system, formats in Sheets, and generates a narrative in Docs. Question 1: Correctness is non-negotiable \u2014 these numbers go to the board. Question 2: The task spans three tools simultaneously, so an isolated autonomous agent won't work. Question 3: The narrative depends on the formatted numbers, which depend on the raw pull \u2014 each piece shapes the next. Verdict: this workflow demands an integration-first agent architecture with agent-to-agent coordination and correctness verification at each handoff, not a single autonomous session dispatched in isolation.", "related_frameworks": ["delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "correctness-definition-framework", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "scout-vs-strike-team-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "delegation-spec-drill", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Delegation Bet vs. Coordination Bet", "type": "distinction", "description": "A distinction between two fundamentally different AI agent philosophies \u2014 autonomous delegation (hand off a task and walk away) versus integrated coordination (agents working inside existing tools and talking to each other) \u2014 that determines which organizational muscle to build.", "when_to_apply": ["We're deciding between an autonomous coding agent like Codex and an integrated agent system like Claude with MCP \u2014 how do we choose?", "Our AI agent deployment is producing good outputs but the team spends more time copy-pasting results between tools than they save", "Leadership wants to adopt AI agents org-wide but different teams have fundamentally different workflow structures", "We picked an AI agent platform six months ago and now switching feels impossibly expensive \u2014 was our initial choice wrong?", "Should we invest in building agent-to-agent coordination infrastructure or just give each team member a powerful autonomous assistant?"], "principles": ["Delegation bet: one sufficiently capable agent working alone produces trusted output without supervision \u2014 value comes from correctness and autonomy", "Coordination bet: real work is fundamentally interdependent and agents must talk to each other and integrate with existing tools \u2014 value comes from connectivity and coordination", "The architectural starting point shapes every downstream feature, default, and assumption, making the approaches remain distinct even as they partially converge", "Integration ecosystems compound via network effects \u2014 each new tool connection makes the whole system more useful \u2014 while isolated architectures do not benefit from this flywheel", "The tool choice reshapes organizational structure (delegation culture vs. coordination culture), making switching later an operational restructuring rather than a software migration", "Both patterns are valid; the error is applying the wrong pattern to a workflow, not choosing one over the other universally"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "188005863", "title": "Codex 5.3 vs. Opus 4.6: Why your AI agent choice compounds faster than you think + the workflow audit that prevents the wrong one", "date": "2026-02-16T14:03:07.763Z"}], "date": "2026-02-16T14:03:07.763Z", "id": "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "core_question": "Should we build around AI agents that work autonomously on isolated tasks, or AI agents that plug into our existing tools and coordinate across workflows \u2014 and what does getting this wrong cost us organizationally?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This team's work is fundamentally interdependent \u2014 a single research task touches the CRM, three Slack channels, a shared doc, and a project tracker before it's 'done.' That's a coordination bet, not a delegation bet. Deploying Codex-style autonomous agents here means the team will manually stitch outputs back into their tools, which is slower than the original workflow. The right move is an integration-first agent architecture where value compounds through connectivity \u2014 each new tool connection makes every workflow faster. Choosing the delegation path would feel productive for the first week and create operational drag for the next twelve months.", "related_frameworks": ["colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "coordination-tax-audit", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "ai-leverage-audit", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Infrastructure Inversion Pattern", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification framework that identifies which position an entity occupies in a platform-building cycle\u2014platform builder, defensible tenant, or vulnerable tenant\u2014and predicts value capture based on whether the infrastructure being built is a 'dumb pipe' or an integrated intelligence layer.", "when_to_apply": ["We're debating whether the massive AI infrastructure spending is a bubble or whether we should be investing even more aggressively", "Our product is built on top of a foundation model API and I'm trying to figure out if we're building value or just renting someone else's platform", "Should we build our own infrastructure layer now or wait until the technology matures and build applications on top of whoever wins?", "I'm trying to understand why this technology investment cycle might play out differently from the dot-com fiber overbuild", "Our board thinks we can catch up on AI infrastructure in 2-3 years \u2014 I need to explain why the window might already be closing"], "principles": ["Every major economic era begins with massive infrastructure overbuilding followed by investor panic before the infrastructure's true use is discovered", "Infrastructure is never built for the thing that makes it valuable\u2014it's built for what the builders imagine, then used for what the next generation imagines", "Platform builders capture the economics of everything built on top; companies that wait become tenants paying the platform's margins", "The structural difference between dumb-pipe infrastructure (railroads, fiber) and intelligent infrastructure (AI models) changes who captures downstream value", "When infrastructure is vertically integrated with the intelligence it delivers, builders can capture application-layer value, not just hosting fees", "The window for platform-building compresses with each successive infrastructure cycle: railroads took 20 years, fiber took 10, AWS took 6, AI is moving in ~18 months", "The penalty for underbuilding in an infrastructure inversion is permanent\u2014you cannot catch up after the window closes"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "187752970", "title": "$185 billion is the down payment \u2014 the 4 skills that survive when agents code for months", "date": "2026-02-14T14:03:12.715Z"}], "date": "2026-02-14T14:03:12.715Z", "id": "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "core_question": "Am I building the platform that everyone else will pay rent on, or am I about to become a tenant on someone else's infrastructure \u2014 and is there still time to change that?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The $185 billion flowing into AI infrastructure isn't a bubble \u2014 it follows the same pattern as railroads, fiber optic, and cloud. But unlike fiber (a dumb pipe where the builders went bankrupt while Google and Netflix captured the value), AI infrastructure is vertically integrated with intelligence. The model builders aren't laying cable for someone else to use \u2014 they're building the intelligence layer itself, which means they capture application-layer economics too. A company building SaaS features on top of OpenAI's API is a vulnerable tenant: the platform builder can ship their feature as a checkbox. The 18-month window for establishing a defensible position \u2014 through proprietary data, workflow integration, or building your own infrastructure layer \u2014 is compressing faster than any prior cycle, and the penalty for waiting is permanent tenancy.", "related_frameworks": ["four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "the-moat-audit", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "four-durable-positions-framework", "distribution-beats-compute", "two-ai-economies-framework", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-distribution-trap", "double-compression-loop", "the-three-layer-analysis"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four Skills That Survive Infrastructure Inversion", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A rubric of four human capabilities\u2014taste, exquisite domain judgment, phenomenal ramp, and relentless honesty about value migration\u2014that retain or appreciate in value when AI agents automate execution-layer work.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm trying to figure out where to invest my learning time when AI capabilities seem to shift every week", "I spend most of my day on execution tasks and I'm starting to wonder if that's a career risk", "We're hiring for a role that's going to be heavily augmented by AI agents \u2014 what should we screen for?", "Someone on my team insists their value is in the craft of writing code, not in the judgment around what to build and why", "I'm advising someone who wants to wait until AI 'stabilizes' before adapting their workflow"], "principles": ["Taste\u2014the ability to distinguish competent from excellent output by hard-won instinct\u2014becomes exponentially more valuable when the cost of generating options drops to zero", "Exquisite domain judgment means specific, contextual, intuitive knowledge of how a domain actually works, accumulated through experience that cannot yet be replicated from training data", "Phenomenal ramp is the meta-skill: the ability to absorb capability changes at speed so all other skills remain applicable as the frontier moves weekly", "Relentless honesty about value migration requires actively inventorying which parts of your work are judgment versus execution and reallocating time before the market forces it", "Most people's time allocation is inverted\u2014they spend the majority of hours on execution skills that are depreciating fastest", "Waiting for AI to 'settle down' before rebuilding professional infrastructure is equivalent to waiting for cloud to 'prove itself' in 2008\u2014you pay a compounding learning-curve tax"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "187752970", "title": "$185 billion is the down payment \u2014 the 4 skills that survive when agents code for months", "date": "2026-02-14T14:03:12.715Z"}], "date": "2026-02-14T14:03:12.715Z", "id": "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "core_question": "Which of my professional skills are actually appreciating as AI takes over execution, and which ones am I clinging to out of comfort while they depreciate underneath me?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This senior engineer spends 70% of their time writing implementation code and 30% on architecture reviews and quality judgment calls. That ratio is inverted \u2014 the 70% is the fastest-depreciating slice of their portfolio. The architecture taste and domain judgment (knowing which database choice will cause pain at scale in this specific regulatory environment) are the appreciating assets. The immediate reallocation: shift to 30% execution / 70% judgment, using AI agents for the implementation layer, and invest freed hours into phenomenal ramp \u2014 staying current with how agent capabilities change the architecture options weekly. The fourth skill \u2014 relentless honesty about value migration \u2014 is the one that makes this reallocation happen at all, because without it, the engineer tells themselves 'I'm a better coder than any AI' and misses the window.", "related_frameworks": ["infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-career-board", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "experiential-debt", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "work-primitives", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "verification-frontier"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Inference Gap Analysis", "type": "distinction", "description": "A distinction between training-era and inference-era infrastructure economics that explains why bubble analyses built on training-cost math systematically underestimate agent-driven compute demand.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep seeing analysts argue that AI infrastructure spending is a bubble \u2014 are they missing something?", "We're trying to forecast compute costs for deploying autonomous agents across our enterprise and the numbers seem impossibly large", "Why do AI infrastructure spending estimates keep getting revised upward every quarter?", "Our capacity planning assumes chatbot-level inference loads but we're moving to agentic workflows \u2014 how far off are we?", "Evaluating whether hyperscaler capex announcements are rational given current AI revenue"], "principles": ["Training compute is expensive but bursty and front-loaded; inference compute is cheaper per unit but continuous and scales with deployment", "A human interacting with a chatbot generates modest inference load; an autonomous agent performing a workflow generates continuous inference at orders of magnitude higher volume", "Agent workloads multiply inference demand across every enterprise workflow simultaneously, not just individual user sessions", "Analysts who correctly called the bubble were analyzing training economics; they were wrong because the actual demand driver is inference for agents", "The shift from training-era to inference-era spending makes previously 'excessive' capex numbers look undersized when modeled against agentic workloads"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "187752970", "title": "$185 billion is the down payment \u2014 the 4 skills that survive when agents code for months", "date": "2026-02-14T14:03:12.715Z"}], "date": "2026-02-14T14:03:12.715Z", "id": "inference-gap-analysis", "core_question": "Is the current AI infrastructure buildout actually oversized, or am I analyzing the wrong demand curve by modeling training costs when the real driver is inference at agent scale?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "The bearish thesis on $185B in AI capex looks airtight if you model training economics \u2014 training runs are bursty, front-loaded, and plateau once a model ships. But that's the wrong demand curve. A single enterprise deploying 10,000 autonomous agents running continuous multi-step workflows generates inference load that dwarfs anything a million chatbot users produce. The analysts who called it a bubble were right about training math and catastrophically wrong about which compute era we're entering. That $185B isn't excess \u2014 it's the down payment on inference infrastructure that doesn't exist yet.", "related_frameworks": ["four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "two-ai-economies-framework", "the-factory-curve", "four-lane-supply-strategy", "edge-first-automation-framework", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "double-compression-loop"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The 70/30 Rule for Human-Agent Delegation", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes agent deployment design around the research-backed finding that people want 70% human control and 30% agent delegation, rather than the 0/100 full-autonomy architecture most agent systems default to.", "when_to_apply": ["We built a fully autonomous agent workflow and adoption is much lower than expected despite strong capability metrics", "Deciding how much autonomy to give an AI agent that handles tasks with social consequences like emails, calendar management, or client communication", "Our team keeps manually overriding or ignoring the agent's outputs even though they're usually correct", "Where exactly should I place human approval gates in an agentic workflow \u2014 I don't want to bottleneck everything but I can't let it run free", "Planning a phased rollout of an AI agent and need to decide what to automate now versus what to keep human-controlled"], "principles": ["People will choose a less competent human helper over a more competent AI helper when stakes are real \u2014 this is psychological, not rational", "Design for 70/30 by default: agent executes, human decides", "Build approval gates so agents draft and humans send, agents research and humans decide, agents monitor and humans act", "Trust is the actual bottleneck, not capability \u2014 governance must precede autonomy expansion", "Increase delegation incrementally as operating history and trust accumulate, not based on capability alone"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "187463064", "title": "160,000 developers are building digital employees, not chatbots + the 4 prompts I use to deploy agents safely", "date": "2026-02-12T14:01:22.306Z"}], "date": "2026-02-12T14:01:22.306Z", "id": "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "core_question": "Why are users resisting or underusing our AI agent even though it's technically capable \u2014 and how should we redesign the delegation boundary so people actually adopt it?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The conventional assumption is that the email-drafting agent should send autonomously because it achieves 95% quality parity with human-written emails. That's wrong here because the cost of a bad email is social and reputational \u2014 exactly the domain where people choose a less competent human over a more competent AI. The architecture should be agent-drafts, human-sends: the agent composes and suggests timing, but the send button stays with the person. Adoption will climb because the user feels control over the risk surface, and over three months of operating history with zero embarrassing sends, the approval gate can be relaxed to auto-send for routine confirmations while keeping human review on anything external or senior-facing.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-agent-supervision-model", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "blast-radius-assessment", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "the-reversibility-gap", "speed-of-control", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agent Deployment Safety Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A seven-principle sequential checklist for deploying AI agents safely, structured around the failure patterns separating successful agent deployments from disasters.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm about to give an AI agent access to our company email, CRM, or cloud accounts and want to make sure I'm not exposing critical systems", "We had an agent do something unexpected in production and I need to figure out which safeguard was missing", "My team wants to deploy an autonomous agent but I need to define the right permission scope and approval checkpoints", "I'm setting up an agent for the first time and need a structured checklist to avoid the obvious deployment mistakes", "People on my team are frustrated that our new agent deployment is making things slower \u2014 should we kill it or keep going?"], "principles": ["Start with high-frequency, low-stakes tasks where the cost of failure is recoverable before expanding to consequential ones", "Design for approval gates rather than autonomy \u2014 every task needs a human checkpoint until reliability is proven", "Isolate aggressively: dedicated instances, throwaway accounts, no connection to irreplaceable data", "Treat third-party skill/plugin marketplaces like early npm \u2014 vet contributors and read code before installing", "Specify precisely \u2014 vague constraints cause models to fill gaps with unpredicted behavior", "Build audit trails outside the agent's own access so monitoring cannot be compromised by the monitored system", "Budget for a J-curve learning period \u2014 agents make workflows harder before they make them easier, and most people quit in the trough"], "steps": ["Start with friction removal tasks (email triage, briefings, calendar) rather than high-stakes or ambiguous tasks", "Design approval gates at every task boundary: agent drafts, human approves before action", "Isolate the agent on dedicated hardware or cloud instance with throwaway accounts and no access to critical data", "Vet all third-party skills/plugins before installation \u2014 check contributor, read code, do not rely solely on automated scanners", "Write precise specifications with explicit constraints, objectives, and communication boundaries before granting permissions", "Build an external audit trail that logs all agent actions in a system the agent cannot access or modify", "Allocate time for iterative tuning through the learning curve before evaluating whether the deployment is working"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "187463064", "title": "160,000 developers are building digital employees, not chatbots + the 4 prompts I use to deploy agents safely", "date": "2026-02-12T14:01:22.306Z"}], "date": "2026-02-12T14:01:22.306Z", "id": "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "core_question": "How do I deploy an AI agent into a real workflow without it silently breaking things, leaking data, or creating a mess I can't undo?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The team wants to deploy an agent that processes customer refund requests end-to-end. Running through the checklist: refund processing is high-stakes and irreversible \u2014 it fails Principle 1, so the agent should start on refund email triage and draft generation instead, with a human approving every actual refund action (Principle 2). The agent needs a throwaway service account with read-only CRM access and no write access to the payment system (Principle 3). The Zapier integrations it relies on haven't been code-reviewed (Principle 4 violation). The specification says 'handle refunds appropriately' \u2014 that's a gap-filling invitation; it needs explicit dollar thresholds, escalation criteria, and communication templates (Principle 5). Logging is currently inside the agent's own workspace \u2014 that audit trail is compromised by design and must move to an external system (Principle 6). Finally, the team expects full productivity in week one; they need to budget 4-6 weeks of iterative tuning before judging ROI (Principle 7).", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "speed-of-control", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "agentic-system-gut-check", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "the-reversibility-gap", "constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "ambient-failure-cascade", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "System Prompts as Onboarding Documents", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes system prompts from configuration files (lists of rules) to onboarding documents (context, purpose, and reasoning) to produce better model judgment in unanticipated situations.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent handles happy-path inputs fine but breaks on anything ambiguous or unexpected", "I have a system prompt that's grown to 50+ rules and the model still does weird things in edge cases", "I'm building a customer-facing AI product and need it to handle situations I can't predict in advance", "The model is being overly literal or refusing reasonable requests because my instructions are too restrictive", "I'm writing agent instructions for the first time and want to avoid the rule-list trap"], "principles": ["Start with narrative context before any rules: what is this application, who uses it, what are they trying to accomplish", "Explain the 'why' behind each constraint, not just the 'what'", "Claude generalizes from principles better than it interpolates from exhaustive lists", "Gaps in your system prompt get filled by judgment, not refusal \u2014 so establish clear goals and let the model infer", "Add specific rules only when the model's default judgment produces something you don't want", "Test with ambiguous, off-topic, and conflicting inputs \u2014 not just happy paths"], "steps": ["Write a paragraph establishing context: what is this application, who are the users, what are they trying to accomplish", "Describe what a successful interaction looks like", "List only the constraints that actually matter, with the reasoning behind each", "Omit rules for situations you haven't encountered yet \u2014 let judgment fill those gaps", "Test edge cases, ambiguous inputs, and conflicting scenarios to validate that intent was internalized, not just rules followed", "Add targeted rules only where testing reveals the model's default judgment diverges from your goals"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "186228903", "title": "My breakdown of Claude's 80-Page Constitution + 3 prompts to use it properly", "date": "2026-02-06T14:02:34.987Z"}], "date": "2026-02-06T14:02:34.987Z", "id": "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "core_question": "Why does my AI keep doing the wrong thing in situations I didn't explicitly cover, and how do I build instructions that produce good judgment instead of brittle compliance?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The conventional approach to system prompts is to enumerate rules: 'Don't discuss competitors. Don't give medical advice. Always respond in JSON.' That's wrong \u2014 it's a configuration file, and the model treats gaps as undefined behavior. A system prompt that opens with 'You are the support assistant for Acme Health, a telemedicine platform used by patients aged 40-70 who are often anxious about symptoms and need reassurance alongside accurate triage guidance. A successful interaction leaves the patient feeling heard and knowing exactly what to do next' gives the model enough context to handle the competitor question, the off-topic question, and the edge case you never anticipated \u2014 because it knows what it's trying to accomplish, not just what it's forbidden from doing. Rules get added only after testing reveals specific gaps where the model's default judgment diverges from your intent.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "judgment-over-rules-agent-architecture", "principles-over-rules-for-ai-systems", "constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "delegation-spec-drill", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "intent-doc", "contextual-noise-library-factorial-stress-testing-for-agents"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Judgment-Over-Rules Agent Architecture", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes agentic AI system design from explicit decision trees and hard-coded escalation rules toward goal-and-constraint specifications that allow the model to exercise genuine discretion in novel situations.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent keeps hitting edge cases that weren't in the decision tree and defaulting to unhelpful escalations", "I'm building an agent and the state machine is already 200+ nodes and still can't cover all the real-world scenarios", "Deciding whether to add more rules or give the agent more discretion when it encounters ambiguous situations", "My agent follows all the rules perfectly but still produces outcomes that feel wrong \u2014 it's compliant but not intelligent", "I want to evaluate whether my current agent scaffolding is suppressing the model's actual capability"], "principles": ["Specify goals and constraints rather than enumerating every possible action or decision branch", "An agent that understands why a constraint exists handles novel cases better than one that only knows the rule", "Current rigid architectures (state machines, decision trees) assume the model can't be trusted with judgment \u2014 revisit that assumption", "Evaluation must be scenario-based and probe ambiguity, not just test rule compliance", "Build interfaces that surface the agent's reasoning, not just its actions, to build trust and catch divergence", "The value ceiling of an agent is set by what the builder can specify in advance \u2014 judgment-based design raises that ceiling"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "186228903", "title": "My breakdown of Claude's 80-Page Constitution + 3 prompts to use it properly", "date": "2026-02-06T14:02:34.987Z"}], "date": "2026-02-06T14:02:34.987Z", "id": "judgment-over-rules-agent-architecture", "core_question": "Am I crippling my AI agent's ability to handle the real world by hard-coding every decision path instead of trusting it with genuine judgment within clear constraints?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The conventional approach to this customer service agent is a decision tree with 47 escalation rules and a fallback to a human for anything unrecognized. That's wrong here because the model already understands the company's refund policy, brand tone, and risk thresholds \u2014 it just isn't allowed to reason about them. The 47 rules exist because the builder tried to anticipate every scenario, which means the agent breaks on scenario 48. Replacing those rules with a goal ('resolve the customer's issue while protecting margin and brand trust'), a set of constraints ('never authorize refunds above $500 without human approval; never make promises about unreleased features'), and a reasoning-surfacing interface would let the model handle novel complaints \u2014 like a customer whose order was correct but arrived during a family emergency \u2014 where no rule exists but good judgment is obvious.", "related_frameworks": ["constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "principles-over-rules-for-ai-systems", "intent-engineering", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "blast-radius-assessment", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "the-off-manifold-probe", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "talent-suppression-diagnostic"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Network Intelligence Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A six-part analytical system for evaluating professional network health and job-search readiness using exported LinkedIn data, classifying connections by relationship strength, reciprocity, advocacy likelihood, dormancy, archetype, and warm-path potential.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm about to start a job search and I have no idea which of my 800 LinkedIn connections would actually help me", "I need a warm intro to a specific company but I can't figure out who in my network is closest to them", "I haven't talked to most of my professional contacts in years and I need to figure out which relationships are worth reviving", "I need to pick references but I'm not sure who would actually advocate strongly for me versus just confirm dates", "I exported my LinkedIn data and I want to turn it into an actual strategic asset instead of a vanity metric"], "principles": ["Relationships decay over time using half-life mathematics; every week without contact erodes relationship strength", "Reciprocity imbalances reveal who you can ask favors from (those who owe you) vs. who you should give to first (those you owe)", "Vouch likelihood is a composite signal combining message depth, recency, recommendations, endorsements, and shared tenure\u2014not just connection status", "Dormant conversations with built-in re-engagement hooks are lower-effort and higher-probability than cold outreach", "Network archetype determines optimal strategy; generic advice fails because different network shapes require different approaches", "For niche target companies, relevance outweighs warmth in warm-path scoring (70% weighting); for well-known companies, warmth matters more", "Your data should serve your questions, not the platform's engagement metrics"], "steps": ["Export LinkedIn data archive (connections, messages, endorsements, recommendations, positions, skills, profile CSVs)", "Calculate relationship half-life scores: initial strength \u00d7 0.5^(T/180), adjusted +90 days for shared company history, +60 days for deep message threads, -60 days for shallow interactions", "Build reciprocity debt ledger: assign point values to given/received recommendations (5 pts) and endorsements (1 pt); compute net balance per connection to identify ask-list vs. give-first contacts", "Compute vouch scores (0\u2013100) per connection: message depth (up to 40 pts) + recency (20 pts if <6 months, 10 pts if 6\u201312 months) + recommendations written for you (20 pts each) + endorsements per 3 (10 pts) + shared company tenure (15 pts)", "Scan message history for resurrection opportunities: flag threads with unscheduled catch-ups, unanswered help requests, unfulfilled offers, and job-change re-entry points", "Classify network archetype (Thought Leader, Insider, Connector, Climber, Builder) based on inbound connection rates, company concentration, title density, and startup vs. enterprise mix", "Run warm-path discovery for any target company: rank connections by combined relevance score (domain keywords, adjacent companies, investor/founder roles) \u00d7 warmth score, weighted 70% relevance for niche targets", "Map each analysis output to a concrete action: half-life \u2192 who to contact this week; reciprocity ledger \u2192 who to ask for favors; vouch scores \u2192 reference list qualification; resurrection list \u2192 quick-win outreach; archetype \u2192 strategy selection; warm path \u2192 introduction request"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185419706", "title": "Cold applications have a <2% response rate now + the warm path system that replaces them (includes a guide and prompts!)", "date": "2026-01-29T14:02:27.773Z"}], "date": "2026-01-29T14:02:27.773Z", "id": "network-intelligence-framework", "core_question": "Which of my professional relationships are actually alive, who would actually vouch for me, and what's the fastest warm path to the companies I want to reach?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "This network is a Connector archetype \u2014 high diversity across companies and industries, but shallow message depth with most connections. The vouch score list is thin: only 12 connections score above 60, and 8 of those are from the same former employer. The reciprocity ledger shows three people who wrote recommendations and received nothing back \u2014 those are the give-first priorities before any asks. For the target company (a Series B climate-tech startup), the warm-path scoring weights relevance at 70%: the strongest path isn't the closest friend but a second-degree connection who spent three years at a competitor and has a live message thread with a resurrection hook \u2014 an unanswered coffee offer from four months ago.", "related_frameworks": ["your-data-your-questions", "ai-career-board", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Your Data, Your Questions", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes individuals as active analysts of their own platform-held data rather than passive consumers of platform-curated interpretations, using data exports as a personal API to ask questions the platform would never build a button for.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep clicking through a platform's dashboard tabs and none of them answer my actual question", "A platform is nudging me to upgrade to premium but I suspect the insight I need is already in my own data", "I want to cross-reference data from two different platforms that each keep their views siloed", "I downloaded a GDPR/CCPA data export and I'm staring at JSON files wondering what questions I can now ask", "The platform shows me aggregated metrics but I need to filter or slice the data in a way their UI doesn't support"], "principles": ["Your data should serve your questions, not the platform's engagement or monetization metrics", "The UI has always been the limitation: product teams build for the median use case, leaving your specific question unanswered", "The export is the API: GDPR/CCPA-mandated data downloads give you structured raw material that AI can query in natural language with infinite filter combinations", "Platforms withhold insights that would reduce dependency on their premium features", "The asymmetric advantage comes not from secret technology but from knowing the question is askable in the first place"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "185419706", "title": "Cold applications have a <2% response rate now + the warm path system that replaces them (includes a guide and prompts!)", "date": "2026-01-29T14:02:27.773Z"}], "date": "2026-01-29T14:02:27.773Z", "id": "your-data-your-questions", "core_question": "Why can't I get the specific answer I need from a platform that has all my data \u2014 and what happens when I stop waiting for them to build that button?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The conventional read is that LinkedIn's analytics dashboard tells you how your job search is going. That's wrong \u2014 it tells you what LinkedIn wants you to see to keep you engaged and upgrading to Premium. Exporting your full connection and invitation data, then asking an LLM 'which of my 2nd-degree connections work at companies that posted roles matching my skills in the last 30 days, and who among my 1st-degree connections bridges to them?' produces a warm-path map that LinkedIn would never surface because it eliminates the need for InMail credits. The insight isn't in secret data \u2014 it's in knowing the question was askable with data you already had the legal right to download.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "pull-push-paradigm", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "five-types-of-cognitive-work"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Context Gap Analysis for Human-AI Complementarity", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-step diagnostic procedure for identifying where AI enforcement can address structural human cognitive limitations in any domain, and how to design the resulting human-AI handoff.", "when_to_apply": ["The same quality standards keep slipping despite everyone agreeing they matter and knowing the rules", "We're trying to decide which parts of our review or QA process to give to AI versus keep as human judgment", "Quality degrades gradually across a large codebase or content surface and nobody can monitor all of it", "Our best people catch things that average performers miss, and we want to scale that attention without depending on heroics", "Recurring failures keep happening that aren't really about skill \u2014 the information existed, it just wasn't in the right person's head at the right moment"], "principles": ["Most quality failures are context problems, not people problems \u2014 the information needed to prevent failures exists but isn't held in mind at the moment of decision", "AI has structural advantage where tasks require holding more context than humans can manage, applying rules consistently at scale, and catching deviations without fatigue", "Humans retain structural advantage for novel decisions, business trade-offs, cross-system judgment, and knowing when rules should be broken", "Expertise narrows the context gap but does not close it \u2014 building quality systems around heroic attention does not scale", "Every enforcement system needs a designed exception mechanism, or engineers will create informal workarounds that defeat the purpose", "Enforcing the wrong rules at scale is worse than not enforcing them \u2014 bad patterns in pockets may contain local wisdom that contradicts a flawed standard"], "steps": ["Step 1 \u2014 Identify your entropy: Find where things degrade despite good intentions \u2014 where agreed-upon principles don't get followed, where quality drifts over time without any single bad decision causing it. Frame this as a context problem, not a people problem.", "Step 2 \u2014 Map the cognitive constraint: Determine what a person would need to hold in mind simultaneously to prevent that degradation. Ask whether a reasonable, competent person can realistically maintain that context. If the context required exceeds what any single mind can hold at decision time, you have found a structural cognitive limit.", "Step 3 \u2014 Ask whether AI can address that specific limit: Determine whether the principle can be articulated as a rule, whether the AI can access the context needed to evaluate against that rule, and whether it can check consistently at scale. If yes, this is a candidate for AI enforcement. If the task requires novel judgment, business context, or understanding things that aren't written down, it remains human work.", "Step 4 \u2014 Design the handoff: Define what AI handles versus what humans handle, how humans can override AI when they have context the AI lacks, and how to prevent failure modes such as deskilling, ossification, and over-standardization."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185424536", "title": "My honest take on what AI is structurally better at than your best architects (and where it still falls short)", "date": "2026-01-28T14:03:18.453Z"}], "date": "2026-01-28T14:03:18.453Z", "id": "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "core_question": "Where are our quality failures actually caused by humans being unable to hold enough context at the moment of decision \u2014 and which of those gaps can AI structurally close?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A platform team's API designs keep violating their own published naming conventions, pagination standards, and error-response formats \u2014 not because engineers don't know the standards, but because no single person can hold 200+ existing endpoints in mind while designing a new one. That's a structural cognitive limit, not a skill gap. The rule is fully articulable ('all collection endpoints use cursor-based pagination'), the AI can access the full API surface to check against it, and it can run on every PR without fatigue. This is a clear enforcement candidate. However, the decision about whether a new endpoint should be RESTful or event-driven given the business context stays human \u2014 that requires cross-system judgment about downstream consumers that isn't written down anywhere. The handoff: AI blocks convention violations in CI with a one-click override that requires a brief rationale, preventing both drift and ossification.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "capability-surface-mapping", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "constraint-architecture", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "edge-first-automation-framework", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Structural Advantage Predictor", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A three-condition test for predicting which specific domains or tasks will exhibit AI structural superiority over human experts, based on the nature of the cognitive work required.", "when_to_apply": ["We have clear architectural or compliance standards that everyone agrees on, but violations keep slipping through in production", "Trying to decide whether AI should replace or merely assist human reviewers for a specific type of quality gate", "Our best engineers miss things in large-scale code reviews not because they lack skill but because the surface area is too large to maintain attention across", "Choosing which parts of an architect's or compliance officer's job to fully delegate to AI versus keep human-led", "We want to distinguish between tasks where AI is a nice-to-have versus tasks where human cognitive limits are the actual bottleneck"], "principles": ["AI wins on context, consistency, and scale \u2014 not intelligence", "The three conditions must all be present: large context requirement, consistent rule application need, and pattern deviation detection across fatigue/distraction", "Structural advantage is distinct from general usefulness \u2014 AI may help in many areas but has structural superiority only where human cognitive architecture is the binding constraint", "Predicted domains of AI structural advantage include: security vulnerability detection, API design consistency, accessibility compliance, regulatory compliance enforcement, infrastructure drift, and data schema governance"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185424536", "title": "My honest take on what AI is structurally better at than your best architects (and where it still falls short)", "date": "2026-01-28T14:03:18.453Z"}], "date": "2026-01-28T14:03:18.453Z", "id": "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "core_question": "Will AI actually be structurally better than our best people at this specific task, or will it just be a helpful assistant that still needs expert oversight?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "Security vulnerability detection across a large codebase scores positive on all three conditions: the context window spans thousands of files (exceeding any human's working memory), the rules being applied are well-codified security principles everyone agrees on, and the failure mode in human review is exactly fatigue-induced pattern deviation \u2014 missing the one SQL injection buried in file 847 of a 900-file review. This is structural superiority territory, not just 'AI helps.' Contrast with system architecture design: the context is large but the rules aren't codifiable and the failure mode isn't fatigue \u2014 it's judgment. AI assists there but doesn't structurally dominate.", "related_frameworks": ["five-types-of-cognitive-work", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework", "ai-leverage-audit", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "task-decomposition-framework", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agent Context Failure Catalog", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Nine enumerated failure modes in agent context engineering, each with a recognizable signal and root cause, enabling diagnosis of why a long-running agent breaks down.", "when_to_apply": ["An agent performs well in short demos but fails in production tasks lasting more than 20 minutes", "We upgraded to a larger context window model and performance actually got worse", "Our multi-agent system keeps hallucinating despite having clear role definitions for each agent", "Agent costs are scaling way faster than task complexity and we can't figure out why", "We added summarization to manage context length but the agent started dropping constraints it used to follow"], "principles": ["Append-everything trap: single growing transcript causes cost scaling and attention dilution predictably around the 20\u201330 minute mark", "Blind summarization: compressing without a schema causes constraints and failures to be silently discarded (brevity bias / context collapse)", "Long-context delusion: upgrading to a larger context window without fixing accumulation makes performance worse, not better", "Observability as context: debug logs and stack traces in the same buffer as task instructions drown the model in noise", "Tool schema bloat: too many overlapping tools consume attention budget and create ambiguity before the agent sees any task content", "Anthropomorphic multi-agent: roleplay-style agent teams sharing a giant context produce hallucination, not coordination", "Static configurations: no execution feedback loop means agents never improve and re-discover every failure", "Over-structured harness: elaborate routing logic prevents more capable models from using their full capabilities", "Cache destruction: unstable prefixes (timestamps, non-deterministic serialization) force full-cost recomputation on identical logical content"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181091574", "title": "Get the Cheat Code on Long-Running AI Agents\u2014Here's What Manus, Google, and Anthropic Learned After Trial and Error + 12 Prompts to Help Build Long-Running Agents Yourself", "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z"}, {"id": "185424536", "title": "My honest take on what AI is structurally better at than your best architects (and where it still falls short)", "date": "2026-01-28T14:03:18.453Z"}], "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z", "id": "agent-context-failure-catalog", "core_question": "My long-running agent worked fine in demos but keeps breaking down in production \u2014 what specific architectural flaw is causing the degradation?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The active failure mode is the append-everything trap. The agent's task completion rate drops sharply after 25 minutes, cost per run has tripled in the last sprint, and the team's attempted fix \u2014 blind summarization \u2014 silently discarded three safety constraints from the working context, introducing a second failure mode on top of the first. The tell was that the agent 'forgot' a file-write constraint it had acknowledged fifteen minutes earlier: that's attention dilution from unbounded accumulation, compounded by lossy compression that stripped the constraint during summarization.", "related_frameworks": ["four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "context-as-compiled-view", "double-compression-loop", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "harness-decision-audit", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "ambient-failure-cascade", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "contextual-noise-library-factorial-stress-testing-for-agents", "model-context-matching-framework", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Six Rules for Scaling Multi-Agent Systems", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of six architectural principles, independently converged upon by practitioners, for designing multi-agent systems that absorb compute rather than choke on coordination overhead.", "when_to_apply": ["Designing a multi-agent architecture intended to run tens to hundreds of agents in production", "My multi-agent system works fine with 5 agents but throughput plateaus or degrades at 30+", "Debugging why agents in my system spend most of their tokens on coordination rather than useful work", "Evaluating whether a vendor's agentic architecture will hold up under sustained production load", "Choosing between flat peer-to-peer agent coordination vs. hierarchical planner-worker patterns"], "principles": ["Rule 1 \u2014 Two tiers, not teams: Use a strict two-tier hierarchy (planners and workers); workers never coordinate with each other", "Rule 2 \u2014 Workers stay ignorant: Give workers minimum viable context\u2014only what is needed to complete their specific task, enforced through information hiding", "Rule 3 \u2014 No shared state: Workers operate in complete isolation; use external mechanisms (git, task queues, test suites) for coordination; keep tool sets to three to five core tools", "Rule 4 \u2014 Plan for endings: Treat agent session endings as design parameters, not failures; use episodic operation with external state so any session can resume where another stopped", "Rule 5 \u2014 Prompts over infrastructure: Treat prompts as API contracts; invest more in clear specifications than sophisticated coordination mechanisms; remove components before adding them when systems underperform", "Rule 6 \u2014 Tests as architecture: Design work around domains with testable correctness so test suites provide shared ground truth and coordination without creating agent-to-agent dependencies"], "steps": ["Enforce a strict two-tier hierarchy: planners create tasks, workers execute in isolation, a judge evaluates results", "Restrict worker context to the minimum needed for their assigned task via information hiding", "Eliminate shared state between workers; route coordination through git, task queues, and test suites", "Design sessions as episodic and bounded; store workflow state externally so agents can terminate and resume without data loss", "Write prompts as precise API contracts that eliminate ambiguity and reduce the need for inter-agent negotiation", "Select or design work domains that have conformance test suites, enabling workers to self-validate without querying other agents or coordinators"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185421882", "title": "My honest field notes on scaling agents past the demo phase + 6 rules from teams running hundreds", "date": "2026-01-26T14:03:32.389Z"}], "date": "2026-01-26T14:03:32.389Z", "id": "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "core_question": "Why does my multi-agent system degrade instead of improve as I add more agents, and what architectural constraints would make it actually scale?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This system is choking at 40 agents because workers are sharing state through a centralized context object and negotiating task boundaries with each other \u2014 classic Rule 3 and Rule 1 violations. The agents aren't doing more work; they're spending tokens coordinating. The fix is enforcing strict two-tier hierarchy where the planner decomposes work into isolated tasks, each worker gets only its slice of context, and coordination happens through the test suite and git, not through agent-to-agent messaging. The team's instinct to add a 'mediator agent' between workers would make this worse \u2014 Rule 5 says remove components before adding them.", "related_frameworks": ["dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "coordination-tax-audit", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "constraint-architecture", "done-definition-pattern", "model-vs-harness-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Dumb Agents, Smart Orchestration", "type": "reframe", "description": "Challenges the conventional assumption that scaling multi-agent systems requires smarter, more autonomous agents, arguing instead that complexity should live in the orchestration layer while workers remain as simple and isolated as possible.", "when_to_apply": ["My multi-agent system works in demos but coordination failures multiply unpredictably when I add more agents", "We keep giving agents more tools and broader context to fix failures but reliability isn't improving", "I'm designing a new multi-agent architecture and need to decide where intelligence should live \u2014 in the agents or above them", "Our agent workers are hard to debug because each one has complex internal logic for handling edge cases and coordination", "We want to scale agent parallelism but our current architecture creates serial bottlenecks as agent count grows"], "principles": ["Complexity in agents creates serial dependencies that multiply with agent count", "Complexity in orchestration enables parallelism because it keeps workers isolated", "Workers should be treated as disposable cattle; intelligence lives in the systems that herd them", "Orchestration complexity exists because agents are simple\u2014simple agents need external systems to keep them running, feed them work, merge their outputs, and track progress", "The investment should go into orchestration infrastructure, not agent sophistication"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185421882", "title": "My honest field notes on scaling agents past the demo phase + 6 rules from teams running hundreds", "date": "2026-01-26T14:03:32.389Z"}], "date": "2026-01-26T14:03:32.389Z", "id": "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "core_question": "Should I make my agents smarter to handle more complexity, or am I putting the intelligence in the wrong layer of the system?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The conventional read is that these agents need broader context and more tools because they keep failing on edge cases. That's wrong here \u2014 every capability added to the worker creates a serial dependency that breaks parallelism. The agents are already too smart: they're making routing decisions, handling retries, and merging partial results internally. That logic belongs in the orchestration layer. Strip the workers down to single-purpose functions that take an input and return an output. Move retry logic, progress tracking, output merging, and work assignment into orchestration infrastructure. The agents that are hardest to debug are the ones doing coordination work that shouldn't be their job.", "related_frameworks": ["six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "coordination-tax-audit", "constraint-architecture", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Six Meta-Skills Framework", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of six durable capability dimensions \u2014 context assembly, quality judgment, task decomposition, iterative refinement, workflow integration, and frontier recognition \u2014 that define 201-level AI proficiency.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to evaluate where a knowledge worker actually stands with AI beyond just 'they use ChatGPT'", "Our AI training program needs a rubric that measures real capability dimensions, not just tool familiarity", "A team member produces inconsistent results with AI \u2014 sometimes excellent, sometimes terrible \u2014 and I need to diagnose which specific skill is the weak link", "I'm designing an AI upskilling program and need to know what dimensions to measure before and after", "Someone is a competent prompter but keeps shipping AI outputs that contain subtle errors they should have caught"], "principles": ["Applied AI competence is multidimensional, not a single skill", "Each meta-skill has specific, addressable interventions", "Frontier recognition \u2014 knowing when AI is outside its capability boundary \u2014 is a distinct and critical skill", "These six skills are durable across tool generations, not tied to any specific AI product"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "185544819", "title": "Executive Briefing: Why 95% of AI Deployments Stall Before Production (And What To Do About It)", "date": "2026-01-25T16:00:30.011Z"}], "date": "2026-01-25T16:00:30.011Z", "id": "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "core_question": "Which specific AI capability gaps are holding this person or team back, and how do I target interventions precisely rather than throwing generic training at the problem?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This product manager scores strong on context assembly and task decomposition \u2014 they write excellent prompts with rich background and break complex requests into logical steps. But their frontier recognition score is low: they consistently trust AI outputs in domains where the model is unreliable (legal compliance language, precise numerical calculations), and they lack iterative refinement discipline \u2014 they accept first-pass outputs without pushing for improvement. The targeted intervention isn't more prompt engineering training; it's structured exercises in recognizing capability boundaries and building revision loops into their workflow.", "related_frameworks": ["the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "verification-frontier", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "capability-surface-mapping", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "rejection-as-a-competency-recognition-articulation-encoding", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "knowledge-work-practice-gym", "ai-leverage-audit", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "verification-capability-triage"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Director Assessment", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A five-question diagnostic to determine where an organization actually stands on the AI adoption maturity spectrum.", "when_to_apply": ["We have multiple AI pilots but none have made it to production and I can't figure out why", "Preparing for a board briefing on AI readiness and I need an honest assessment, not a vendor slide deck", "We're about to invest in more AI tooling but I'm not sure if tooling is actually what's holding us back", "Our stated AI strategy sounds impressive but people on the ground say nothing is actually changing", "Deciding whether our teams need more AI training, better tools, or organizational restructuring to move forward"], "principles": ["Most organizations skip this diagnostic step and misattribute stalls to the wrong layer", "Honest assessment of current standing is prerequisite to closing the gap"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 'five questions to diagnose where your organization actually stands' but the questions themselves are not included in the provided text.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "185544819", "title": "Executive Briefing: Why 95% of AI Deployments Stall Before Production (And What To Do About It)", "date": "2026-01-25T16:00:30.011Z"}], "date": "2026-01-25T16:00:30.011Z", "id": "director-assessment", "core_question": "We say we're deploying AI, but are we actually stuck \u2014 and if so, where exactly on the maturity curve are we really standing?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The organization has five AI pilots running but none in production. The diagnostic reveals the real bottleneck: leadership treats AI as a technology deployment problem (they keep buying tools and running POCs) when the actual stall is at the organizational layer \u2014 there's no clear ownership of AI workflows, no evaluation criteria for what 'production-ready' means, and middle management has no incentive to champion adoption. The gap isn't technical capability; it's the missing governance and process layer between experimentation and production.", "related_frameworks": ["capability-dissipation-gap", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-implementation-spectrum", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "capability-surface-mapping"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 'five questions to diagnose where your organization actually stands' but the questions themselves are not included in the provided text.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "Engineering Manager Identity Shift", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the AI-augmented developer's role from 'engineer who uses AI' to 'engineering manager responsible for team throughput,' changing how attention and effort are allocated.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm using Copilot and Claude heavily but my actual shipping velocity hasn't meaningfully increased", "I find myself rewriting most of what the AI produces and wondering what the point is", "I'm trying to run multiple AI agents in parallel but feel cognitively overwhelmed keeping track of everything", "I know AI can do more but I can't stop myself from hands-on-keyboard coding instead of delegating", "The bottleneck in my workflow shifted from 'can I write this code' to 'can I coordinate all these outputs' and I don't know how to operate differently"], "principles": ["An engineering manager's job is to ensure their team produces valuable output, not to write code themselves", "Agents are stateless, tireless, and prone to confident incorrectness \u2014 managing them requires different discipline than managing humans", "Your value no longer lives in the code you produce; it lives in direction-setting, output review, and throughput optimization", "Letting go of the 'engineer who writes elegant code' identity is leverage, not loss", "Attention allocation changes fundamentally when you adopt a manager rather than contributor identity"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185420638", "title": "The identity shift that unlocked real throughput and how to make it stick (plus an in-depth builders guide for 2026)", "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z"}], "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z", "id": "engineering-manager-identity-shift", "core_question": "Why am I not getting more throughput from AI tools even though they keep getting better \u2014 am I using them wrong, or am I thinking about my role wrong?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this developer needs better prompts or a more capable model \u2014 they're reviewing every line of AI-generated code and feeling slower than before. That's wrong. The bottleneck isn't the AI's output quality; it's the developer's identity. They're still operating as an individual contributor who happens to have a tool, so they treat every AI output as their code that must meet their standards. The moment they reframe themselves as an engineering manager running a team of tireless but unreliable junior devs, everything shifts: attention moves from writing and polishing code to setting clear direction, triaging which outputs need deep review vs. spot-checks, and maximizing the number of parallel workstreams. The identity of 'elegant coder' was the bottleneck \u2014 not the AI.", "related_frameworks": ["the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "kill-the-contribution-badge", "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "weekly-delegation-audit", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "director-assessment", "strategic-altitude-shifting", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "capability-dissipation-gap", "focus-capacity-model", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Kill the Contribution Badge", "type": "reframe", "description": "Challenges the professional instinct to prepare comprehensive briefs before engaging AI, arguing that unstructured 'rambling' input often produces better output by avoiding premature structure.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep rewriting and polishing my prompts before sending them, and it feels like it's slowing me down more than helping", "I feel like I need to fully understand my problem before I'm 'allowed' to start talking to an AI about it", "My AI workflow feels like writing a spec document rather than having a conversation", "I learned prompting habits a year ago and I'm not sure they still apply to newer models", "I want to use AI to help me think through a problem, not just execute on a solution I've already designed"], "principles": ["Pre-thinking as a 'badge of honor' often creates premature structure that closes off better solutions", "Modern AI models handle unstructured input better than most users expect", "The crystallization of intent through dialogue is where real value happens \u2014 not in pre-crafted prompts", "Start talking before you think you're ready; let the tool help you find your intent", "Your job is knowing what matters, not presenting a polished brief"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185420638", "title": "The identity shift that unlocked real throughput and how to make it stick (plus an in-depth builders guide for 2026)", "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z"}], "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z", "id": "kill-the-contribution-badge", "core_question": "Am I spending time polishing my prompt because it actually improves the output, or because 'showing up prepared' is a professional reflex that's now working against me?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The conventional assumption is that a well-structured brief produces better AI output than a stream-of-consciousness dump. That's wrong for most exploratory work. A developer spending 20 minutes organizing their prompt into neat sections is actually closing off solution paths the model would have found if given the raw, messy problem space. The real skill isn't crafting the perfect input \u2014 it's knowing what matters enough to mention and then letting the dialogue crystallize the structure. Pre-structuring is only valuable when you already know exactly what you want; if you're still figuring that out, the 'preparation' is premature optimization of the wrong thing.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "three-stances-toward-mess", "pull-push-paradigm"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Strategic Altitude Shifting", "type": "procedure", "description": "A framework for deliberately moving between high-level delegation and low-level verification when working with AI agents, avoiding the twin failure modes of permanent high-altitude vibe coding and permanent low-altitude line-by-line review.", "when_to_apply": ["I've been vibe coding for weeks and realize I couldn't explain how my own auth system works", "I'm spending hours reviewing every line of AI-generated code and my throughput has cratered", "I need to debug a production issue in AI-generated code and I have no mental model of how it works", "I want a practical rhythm for when to trust AI output and when to read it carefully", "My codebase has grown significantly through AI generation and I need to decide which parts I actually need to understand deeply"], "principles": ["Neither staying permanently high (pure vibe coding) nor permanently low (reviewing every line) is optimal", "Drop altitude deliberately at critical paths: authentication, data handling, core business logic", "Verify that your mental model matches reality at key checkpoints, then climb back out", "Experiential debt (gap between built and understood) is distinct from technical debt and equally costly", "The skill is knowing when to change altitude, not staying at one level"], "steps": ["Delegate initial implementation to agents at high altitude", "Identify critical paths where verification matters most (authentication, data handling, core logic)", "Drop altitude deliberately to examine those specific areas and verify mental model against reality", "Test edge cases that matter in the verified areas", "Climb back out to high altitude and continue directing"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185420638", "title": "The identity shift that unlocked real throughput and how to make it stick (plus an in-depth builders guide for 2026)", "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z"}], "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z", "id": "strategic-altitude-shifting", "core_question": "I'm shipping fast with AI-generated code, but I'm losing my grip on what the system actually does \u2014 how do I stay productive without becoming a stranger to my own codebase?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The authentication module and payment processing flow are critical paths \u2014 those warrant dropping altitude to read every line, trace the token flow, and verify the mental model matches what the agent actually built. The UI layout components and boilerplate CRUD endpoints are not critical paths \u2014 those stay at high altitude, accepted on trust with a quick smoke test. After verifying the auth module and confirming the session handling matches expectations, altitude climbs back up and the next three features get delegated without line-by-line review. The experiential debt is now concentrated only in non-critical areas where it's tolerable.", "related_frameworks": ["experiential-debt", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "blast-radius-assessment", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "engineering-manager-identity-shift", "director-assessment", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Temporal Separation (Build/Reflect Rhythm)", "type": "procedure", "description": "A cognitive architecture practice that separates the generative building state from the evaluative reflection state across different time periods to enable both high-throughput shipping and strategic improvement.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm shipping a lot with AI but keep hitting the same problems week after week", "I feel like I can't think strategically about my workflow while I'm in the middle of an active building session with agents", "I want to build a feedback loop on my AI-augmented development process but don't know when or how to do the analysis", "My AI-assisted work feels like plate-spinning \u2014 high throughput but no compounding improvement", "I have tons of session transcripts and monitoring data but never actually review them to find patterns"], "principles": ["The generative/flow state and the evaluative/strategic state require different brain chemistry", "Reflection sessions feel like overhead but are the difference between getting faster and getting better", "Create artifacts during building (transcripts, monitoring data) that your future reflecting self can analyze", "Strategic questions (what worked, what failed, what patterns emerged) can only be answered with temporal distance", "Write drunk, edit sober \u2014 adapted for AI-augmented work"], "steps": ["During building sessions, focus on throughput \u2014 delegate, review outputs, redirect agents", "Instrument your work to create reviewable artifacts (session transcripts, monitoring logs, error reports)", "Schedule separate reflection sessions with temporal distance from building (e.g., build Tuesday, reflect Friday)", "In reflection sessions, analyze patterns: which prompts worked, where agents got stuck, what architectural decisions are causing pain", "Use reflection insights to update your approach for the next building cycle"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185420638", "title": "The identity shift that unlocked real throughput and how to make it stick (plus an in-depth builders guide for 2026)", "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z"}], "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z", "id": "temporal-separation-build-reflect-rhythm", "core_question": "I'm shipping faster than ever with AI, but I can't tell if I'm actually getting better at this or just running harder on the same treadmill.", "category": "execution", "example_application": "A developer has been running AI-assisted building sessions for three weeks straight, shipping features daily but noticing the same failure patterns repeating \u2014 agents hallucinating config values, prompts that need manual correction every time, architectural decisions made under time pressure that now create drag. The building sessions are high-throughput but the process never improves because there's no temporal distance for pattern recognition. Scheduling a Friday reflection session with the week's session transcripts and error logs reveals that 60% of agent failures trace to a single missing context pattern \u2014 a fix that takes 20 minutes but was invisible from inside the flow state. The next building cycle runs measurably cleaner because the reflection artifact became a rules file update.", "related_frameworks": ["scar-tissue-rules-file", "weekly-delegation-audit", "double-compression-loop", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "focus-capacity-model", "engineering-manager-identity-shift", "ai-leverage-audit", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "strategic-altitude-shifting"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Two Kinds of Architecture", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between codifiable technical patterns (delegatable to AI agents) and 'scaling taste' \u2014 the coherence and vision quality that remains a human responsibility and cannot yet be outsourced.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI-generated code follows all conventions perfectly but the product still feels incoherent and directionless", "I'm writing an AGENTS.md file and trying to figure out what belongs in it versus what I need to keep in my own head", "I'm spending my time reviewing AI-written code for style consistency when I should be doing something else \u2014 but I'm not sure what that something else is", "I want to delegate more architectural decisions to agents but I'm worried about losing the coherent vision of the system", "My AI-assisted project is technically sound but lacks the 'soul' that makes it feel like one person designed it"], "principles": ["Civil engineering patterns (conventions, standards, consistency) are tractable \u2014 write them down and agents can follow them", "Taste, coherence, and vision ('the quality without a name') remain human work and cannot yet be delegated", "Agents excel at consistent application of best practices; humans must own judgment about what feels right", "Conflating these two architectures leads to misplaced expectations about what AI can produce", "When dealing with civil engineering architecture, lean hard on agents; when dealing with taste, that's where your attention must go"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185420638", "title": "The identity shift that unlocked real throughput and how to make it stick (plus an in-depth builders guide for 2026)", "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z"}, {"id": "181561995", "title": "Executive Briefing: What Cursor's $57K CMS Deletion Reveals About Where Agent Value Actually Lives", "date": "2025-12-14T15:00:52.869Z"}], "date": "2025-12-14T15:00:52.869Z", "id": "two-kinds-of-architecture", "core_question": "Which parts of my architectural work can I hand to AI agents, and which parts require my irreplaceable human judgment about what 'right' actually feels like?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The codebase is immaculate \u2014 consistent naming, proper error handling, well-structured modules \u2014 because the agent followed every convention in AGENTS.md perfectly. But the product feels like a committee designed it: five different interaction patterns for similar tasks, a settings page that implies a mental model no user actually holds, and an API surface that's technically clean but tells no coherent story. That's the tell. The civil engineering architecture is fully delegated and working. The taste architecture \u2014 the 'why does this feature exist and how does it connect to everything else' \u2014 has no owner. The builder's attention needs to shift entirely from code consistency (the agent has that) to product coherence (only they can hold that).", "related_frameworks": ["scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "director-assessment", "engineering-manager-identity-shift", "delegation-spec-drill", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "the-five-specification-primitives", "intent-doc", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "verification-frontier", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Experiential Debt", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes experiential debt (the gap between what you've built and your embodied understanding of it) from technical debt, identifying it as a separate and underappreciated risk of AI-accelerated development.", "when_to_apply": ["I vibe-coded a working prototype but now I feel like a tourist in my own codebase", "My team shipped fast with AI but our debugging time has exploded and nobody understands the architecture deeply", "I'm wondering whether AI-assisted speed is actually costing me something I can't easily name", "I built what I thought I wanted quickly, but now realize I never discovered what I actually needed because I skipped the messy middle", "Someone on my team can demo the product but can't explain why it's built the way it is"], "principles": ["Operator's knowledge \u2014 knowing where the nooks and crannies are \u2014 cannot be built at the same speed AI builds systems", "The vision for a product is often fundamentally wrong; stepwise building reveals what you actually want", "Skipping intermediate phases means skipping the discoveries that reveal what you actually want to build", "You can build fast and still accumulate experiential knowledge, but it requires intentionality and actually using what you ship", "Experiential debt manifests as debugging time, architectural confusion, and building the wrong thing faster"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "185420638", "title": "The identity shift that unlocked real throughput and how to make it stick (plus an in-depth builders guide for 2026)", "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z"}], "date": "2026-01-23T14:02:47.543Z", "id": "experiential-debt", "core_question": "I'm shipping faster than ever with AI, so why does my own codebase feel increasingly foreign to me \u2014 and why do I keep building the wrong thing?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "This team's velocity problem isn't technical debt \u2014 it's experiential debt. They used AI to scaffold an entire event-processing pipeline in two days, but nobody on the team has ever manually handled a malformed event. Now every bug takes 4x longer to diagnose because they're reverse-engineering their own system. The tell: they can describe what the system does from the README, but they can't predict what will break when inputs change. The fix isn't to slow down \u2014 it's to actually use what they shipped, run edge cases by hand, and build the operator's intuition that the AI-assisted sprint skipped over.", "related_frameworks": ["the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "temporal-separation-build-reflect-rhythm", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "speed-of-control", "verification-frontier", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "llm-psychosis-self-audit-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Colleague-Shaped vs. Tool-Shaped AI Selection Framework", "type": "distinction", "description": "A decision framework for choosing between iterative/collaborative AI (colleague-shaped) and autonomous/execution-focused AI (tool-shaped) based on two axes: specification clarity and verification cost.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent keeps producing output that looks great but has fundamental errors I only catch later", "I'm trying to decide whether to use ChatGPT-style back-and-forth or a fire-and-forget autonomous agent for a project", "Junior team members are getting worse results from the same AI tools that work well for senior staff", "I'm designing an AI workflow and need to decide how much human-in-the-loop interaction to build in", "Someone on my team wants to switch to fully autonomous AI agents but I'm not sure they can specify tasks precisely enough"], "principles": ["The core axis is specification quality \u00d7 verification cost: tool-shaped AI wins when the spec is crisp and verification is cheap; colleague-shaped AI wins when the spec is discoverable only through iteration or verification is expensive", "Senior/expert practitioners benefit from tool-shaped AI because they can write precise specifications and verify outputs quickly; junior/developing practitioners benefit from colleague-shaped AI because the dialogue catches errors early and teaches simultaneously", "Most people overestimate their ability to specify precise intent \u2014 the feedback loop that makes iterative AI feel slower is the same mechanism that catches errors before they compound", "High-grade intent (the ability to produce complete, unambiguous specifications upfront) is a learnable skill and a prerequisite for effective use of autonomous AI", "The same tool characteristic (e.g., yielding control frequently) is a bug in one context and a feature in another \u2014 context determines fit, not absolute capability", "Organizations likely need both patterns simultaneously: autonomous delegation for domain experts and iterative collaboration for developing contributors"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185185491", "title": "The Specification Gap: Why Your AI Produces Impressive-Looking Output With Fundamental Problems + The Prompt Kit To Help You Fix It", "date": "2026-01-21T14:03:07.052Z"}], "date": "2026-01-21T14:03:07.052Z", "id": "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "core_question": "Should I be collaborating with AI through back-and-forth dialogue, or should I be specifying what I want upfront and letting it execute autonomously \u2014 and how do I know which mode fits my actual situation?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "This team is using an autonomous coding agent but getting plausible-looking output with subtle architectural errors. The tell is specification clarity \u00d7 verification cost: the junior developers can't write precise enough specs to get good autonomous output, and they lack the expertise to cheaply verify the results. They're in the colleague-shaped quadrant \u2014 iterative dialogue would catch these errors mid-stream instead of letting them compound into a finished artifact that looks right but isn't. The senior architect, by contrast, can specify exactly what she wants and spot problems in seconds \u2014 for her, the autonomous mode is correct and the iteration overhead is pure friction.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "the-five-specification-primitives", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "delegation-spec-drill", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "verification-capability-triage", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "experiential-debt"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Two Kinds of Disposable Software", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Classifies disposable software into two distinct categories with different risk profiles and strategic implications.", "when_to_apply": ["We're adopting a 'ship fast, kill fast' approach to features and I need to figure out where that's safe versus dangerous", "Someone on the team is pointing to Cursor's success with rapid iteration as a model for our enterprise product", "We're using AI to generate lots of small tools and features quickly, and I need to decide which ones need reliability investment versus which can stay throwaway", "Our product strategy treats AI-generated features as experiments, but customers keep complaining when we deprecate things", "I'm trying to figure out whether vibe-coded tools are appropriate for internal use versus customer-facing use"], "principles": ["Personal/throwaway software (one-off dashboards, personal tools, weekend projects) is unambiguously beneficial and represents genuine democratization", "Disposable features inside enterprise products carry hidden risks around trust, stability, and maintenance burden", "The two categories share a name but have fundamentally different cost structures and customer tolerance levels", "Simple interfaces (e.g., terminal-based) better absorb constant change than complex GUIs, making disposability more viable"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185079278", "title": "Disposable software works for Cursor, but probably won't work for you. + The reliability playbook for everyone else", "date": "2026-01-20T14:02:48.596Z"}], "date": "2026-01-20T14:02:48.596Z", "id": "two-kinds-of-disposable-software", "core_question": "Is the software I'm treating as disposable actually safe to throw away, or does it carry hidden costs that compound once real users depend on it?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The team wants to ship AI-generated features rapidly and deprecate them if they don't stick \u2014 a 'disposable software' philosophy. But this is an enterprise SaaS product with paying customers, not a weekend project. That makes it Category 2: disposable features inside a product people rely on. The cost structure is completely different \u2014 customers build workflows around features, and removing them erodes trust even if usage was low. The same rapid-churn approach that's unambiguously good for personal tools and one-off dashboards becomes a liability here. A terminal-based developer tool like Cursor can absorb constant churn because its interface is simple and its users expect instability; a complex enterprise GUI with onboarding flows and integrations cannot.", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "the-two-door-principle", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction", "the-weekend-hack-test", "proactively-reliable-ai-the-two-step-enterprise-strategy", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "developer-customers-vs-cio-customers-chasm", "the-reversibility-gap"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Code Cost vs. Attention Cost Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "Separates the collapsed cost of generating code from the unchanged cost of directing skilled human attention toward a goal, exposing the flaw in 'software is now free' reasoning.", "when_to_apply": ["My team says we should build it in-house because AI makes software basically free now", "We're debating whether to pull engineers off the core product to vibe-code internal tools", "Leadership is excited about replacing SaaS subscriptions with AI-generated internal software", "Someone calculated ROI on AI-assisted development but only counted the coding time saved", "We keep generating new internal tools with AI but our senior engineers are drowning in maintenance and security fixes"], "principles": ["The cost of generating code has collapsed to near zero; the cost of human attention directed at a goal has not", "Opportunity cost of diverting senior builders from core mission is incalculable regardless of how cheap the software is to generate", "Maintenance burden does not disappear with cheap initial generation \u2014 it shifts from upfront engineering to ongoing remediation", "AI-generated code introduces security vulnerabilities that require skilled human review, adding back hidden costs", "The constraint was always attention, not software cost"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185079278", "title": "Disposable software works for Cursor, but probably won't work for you. + The reliability playbook for everyone else", "date": "2026-01-20T14:02:48.596Z"}], "date": "2026-01-20T14:02:48.596Z", "id": "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction", "core_question": "Is this 'build it ourselves with AI' plan actually cheaper, or are we confusing the near-zero cost of generating code with the very real cost of the senior attention required to direct, review, and maintain it?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "The team wants to vibe-code an internal billing dashboard instead of paying $2K/month for a SaaS tool, arguing that Cursor makes it nearly free. That's code cost thinking. The real cost is three weeks of a senior engineer's attention \u2014 someone currently architecting the core product's payment integration. The code generation takes hours; the specification, security review, edge-case handling, and ongoing maintenance consume the same skilled attention they always did. The $2K/month SaaS is buying back the most constrained resource the company has: directed senior attention on the core mission.", "related_frameworks": ["building-vs-buying-ai-response", "two-kinds-of-disposable-software", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "the-weekend-hack-test", "ambient-failure-cascade", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Developer Customers vs. CIO Customers Chasm", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Classifies enterprise software buyers into two archetypes with fundamentally different tolerances and expectations, determining which product philosophy is viable.", "when_to_apply": ["We're shipping features fast and breaking things, but our enterprise customers keep escalating to their account managers", "Debating whether our AI startup can displace an incumbent like ServiceNow or Workday by iterating faster", "Our product team wants to adopt a disposable-software philosophy but we sell to Fortune 500 procurement teams", "Trying to figure out why the same product strategy that works for developer tools is failing in our enterprise segment", "Designing our support model and SLA tiers and unsure how much instability our customer base will tolerate"], "principles": ["Developer customers can fix things when they break; CIO customers call their account manager", "Developers chose bleeding-edge tools knowing they would change; CIOs chose vendors with SLAs specifically to avoid thinking about the software", "The gap between these customer types is not a temporary gap that closes as AI improves \u2014 it is a fundamental difference in what is being purchased", "Enterprise customers are buying reliability and peace of mind, not features", "CIO customers need a 'single ringable neck' \u2014 someone accountable when things fail"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185079278", "title": "Disposable software works for Cursor, but probably won't work for you. + The reliability playbook for everyone else", "date": "2026-01-20T14:02:48.596Z"}], "date": "2026-01-20T14:02:48.596Z", "id": "developer-customers-vs-cio-customers-chasm", "core_question": "Are my customers the kind of people who can absorb breakage and adapt, or the kind who bought my product precisely so they'd never have to think about it again?", "category": "product", "example_application": "This SaaS startup wants to ship weekly model updates and deprecate old API endpoints on a 30-day cycle, Cursor-style. But their buyers are mid-market operations teams whose CIOs selected this vendor after a six-month procurement process specifically for stability guarantees. These are CIO customers, not developer customers \u2014 they don't have engineers who can patch around breaking changes at 2am. The rapid-iteration playbook that works for developer-tool companies will generate support tickets, churn, and contract non-renewals here. The product philosophy needs to be proactive reliability with a 'single ringable neck,' not move-fast-and-ship.", "related_frameworks": ["two-kinds-of-disposable-software", "proactively-reliable-ai-the-two-step-enterprise-strategy", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-moat-audit", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Proactively Reliable AI: The Two-Step Enterprise Strategy", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequential two-step framework for enterprise software companies to earn the right to deploy autonomous AI agents by first establishing reliability, then layering in proactive capability.", "when_to_apply": ["Our enterprise customers keep asking for AI features but we still have reliability issues they complain about", "We're building an AI agent that takes actions on behalf of users and need to figure out how aggressively to automate", "An AI-native competitor is winning developer mindshare but we have the enterprise trust \u2014 how do we sequence our AI roadmap?", "Product leadership wants to ship proactive AI capabilities but our NPS scores show customers don't fully trust the platform yet", "We're debating whether our AI should suggest actions or take them autonomously for enterprise customers"], "principles": ["Reliability is table stakes \u2014 without it, proactive AI creates anxiety rather than value", "You cannot skip step one: attempting proactivity before proving reliability will terrify customers", "Reactive AI (chatbots) waits to be asked and is no longer a differentiator; proactive AI creates value customers didn't know they were missing", "Trust is earned incrementally \u2014 start with low-stakes autonomous actions and build a track record of correct decisions", "Earned trust is the precondition for autonomous action on behalf of users"], "steps": ["Step 1 \u2014 Establish reliability: Prove your product works consistently, data is secure, SLAs are met, and customers have a human to call when something goes wrong. This must come before any autonomous AI capability.", "Step 2 \u2014 Layer in proactive AI: Once reliability is proven and trust is earned, deploy agents that take autonomous action on behalf of users without being asked \u2014 surfacing insights, updating records, drafting responses, alerting stakeholders \u2014 creating value customers didn't know they were missing."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "185079278", "title": "Disposable software works for Cursor, but probably won't work for you. + The reliability playbook for everyone else", "date": "2026-01-20T14:02:48.596Z"}], "date": "2026-01-20T14:02:48.596Z", "id": "proactively-reliable-ai-the-two-step-enterprise-strategy", "core_question": "Are we trusted enough by our enterprise customers to let our AI act autonomously on their behalf, or will proactive features feel threatening because we haven't yet proven we can be consistently reliable?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The product team wants to ship an AI agent that auto-reassigns support tickets based on urgency signals. But customers still file escalations about data sync delays twice a month, and there's no dedicated support line \u2014 just a chatbot. Deploying autonomous reassignment now will feel like the system is making decisions customers can't trust or override. The right move is to close the reliability gap first: hit the SLA consistently, give customers a human escalation path, then earn the right to auto-reassign by starting with low-stakes suggestions ('we would have reassigned this \u2014 approve?') before graduating to full autonomy.", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "pull-push-paradigm", "developer-customers-vs-cio-customers-chasm", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Principles Over Rules for AI Systems", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between giving AI rigid rules versus guiding principles, arguing that principles enable AI to exercise contextual judgment across unanticipated situations while rules only handle the specific cases they encode.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent keeps failing on edge cases that my system prompt doesn't explicitly cover", "I'm writing a rules file or system prompt and it's getting absurdly long trying to enumerate every situation", "Deciding whether to give a coding agent strict lint-style rules or higher-level engineering principles", "My automated workflow breaks every time it encounters a slightly novel input because it only knows rigid if-then patterns", "I want my AI collaborator to exercise good judgment but I'm not sure how to encode that without being overly prescriptive"], "principles": ["Principles like 'don't swallow errors' allow AI to interpret intent across a hundred situations you didn't anticipate; rigid rules like 'always log to this file' only handle one case", "AI systems are good at applying judgment in context, not just executing if-then pattern matching", "Principle-based prompts produce more robust systems that handle edge cases the designer didn't foresee", "Give AI room to exercise judgment rather than constraining it to enumerated rules"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "184882888", "title": "50 people ignored my tool recommendations + here's why their systems still worked (Second Brain follow-up)", "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z"}], "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z", "id": "principles-over-rules-for-ai-systems", "core_question": "Should I tell this AI exactly what to do in every case, or give it the reasoning behind what I want so it can handle situations I haven't thought of?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This system prompt is rules-based, not principles-based \u2014 the tell is the twenty enumerated if-then conditions for error handling, each covering one specific failure mode. When the agent hits an unanticipated error (a timeout from a third-party API it's never seen before), it has no guidance and either halts or swallows the error silently. Replacing those twenty rules with three principles \u2014 'never hide failures from the user,' 'prefer recoverable actions over halting,' 'log enough context to diagnose later' \u2014 would let the agent reason through novel error types it was never explicitly programmed for, because it understands the intent behind the behavior rather than just the behavior itself.", "related_frameworks": ["judgment-over-rules-agent-architecture", "intent-engineering", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "constraint-architecture", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-five-specification-primitives", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "delegation-spec-drill", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "intent-doc"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Tool vs. Infrastructure Design Question", "type": "distinction", "description": "Challenges builders to decide whether they are creating a standalone tool that solves one problem or infrastructure that other systems can build on top of, because the choice determines design decisions and long-term leverage.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm building a personal productivity system and wondering whether to add an API or keep it simple", "I keep rebuilding the same components across different projects and wondering if I should create a shared foundation", "Deciding whether my Notion/Obsidian setup should just work for me or serve as a hub that other automations connect to", "I built a workflow that works great for one use case but now I want three other things to use its data", "Trying to figure out how much modularity and extensibility to invest in upfront versus shipping something that just works"], "principles": ["A tool solves a problem; infrastructure enables other things to be built on top of it", "Designing with infrastructure in mind creates leverage: one investment pays dividends across everything that connects to it", "Infrastructure thinking changes data model choices, API exposure decisions, and how modular components are built", "The same architectural patterns scale from simple personal tools to sophisticated multi-system infrastructure without breaking"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "184882888", "title": "50 people ignored my tool recommendations + here's why their systems still worked (Second Brain follow-up)", "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z"}], "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z", "id": "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "core_question": "Am I building something that solves this one problem, or am I building something that other things I build will plug into \u2014 and does my architecture actually reflect which one I chose?", "category": "product", "example_application": "This note-taking system is being designed as a tool \u2014 it captures and retrieves notes, full stop. But the user wants their task manager, writing workflow, and AI agents to all pull from the same data. That's infrastructure, not a tool. The data model needs to change: structured fields instead of free-text blobs, stable IDs that external systems can reference, and an API layer even if the only consumer today is one Zapier integration. The tell was 'I want my other systems to build on this' \u2014 that sentence means infrastructure, and the current flat-file design will break the moment a second system tries to consume it.", "related_frameworks": ["the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "two-kinds-of-architecture", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "the-implementation-spectrum", "the-context-platform-compound", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Community + AI Collaborative Building Model", "type": "procedure", "description": "A three-part building approach where community provides the pattern library, AI provides implementation translation, and the builder provides contextual judgment \u2014 combining all three to close the gap between understanding a pattern and implementing it in a specific context.", "when_to_apply": ["I found a great solution someone shared in a community but they use completely different tools than I do", "Building a complex workflow where I understand the principles but keep getting stuck on specific implementation details", "I've watched tutorials and understand the concept but can't wire together the actual components in my setup", "I'm part of a community where people share solutions but I don't know how to adapt their approaches to my constraints", "Debugging a system where the community has named the failure pattern but the fix doesn't directly apply to my stack"], "principles": ["Community provides a real-time evolving pattern library; when one person solves a problem, everyone benefits within hours", "AI bridges the translation gap between 'I understand what someone else did' and 'I can implement the equivalent in my situation'", "The builder's judgment determines which community patterns apply to their specific context and which do not", "No single piece is sufficient: tutorial alone doesn't adapt to your tools, AI alone doesn't know what problems others have solved, community alone can't do the detailed wiring", "Shared vocabulary (named patterns like 'dropbox,' 'sorter,' 'receipt') makes collaboration efficient by giving community members and AI a common conceptual language", "Tutorials function as shared vocabulary more than as step-by-step instructions in this model"], "steps": ["Identify a community working on the same class of problem with shared vocabulary and context", "Learn the principles governing the domain \u2014 the patterns that stay constant even when tools change", "When stuck, post the specific failure in the community to find who has already solved it", "Take the community insight (the 'what') to an AI collaborator along with your specific implementation context", "Ask the AI to translate the community solution into your specific stack, prompt structure, or configuration", "Apply your own judgment about whether the translated solution fits your situation before implementing"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "184882888", "title": "50 people ignored my tool recommendations + here's why their systems still worked (Second Brain follow-up)", "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z"}], "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z", "id": "community-ai-collaborative-building-model", "core_question": "I understand the concept of what I need to build, but I can't close the gap between 'I see what someone else did' and 'I can make this work in my specific situation' \u2014 how do I combine community knowledge, AI translation, and my own judgment to get there?", "category": "execution", "example_application": "A builder is setting up an automated receipt-processing pipeline. The community has already named this the 'sorter' pattern and three members have posted their implementations \u2014 but all use Zapier, while this builder uses Make.com with a different folder structure. Taking the community's 'sorter' pattern description to Claude along with the specific Make.com modules and folder hierarchy produces a working translation in twenty minutes. The builder's judgment catches that the community solution assumed a single inbox while their workflow has three \u2014 a contextual difference no tutorial or AI alone would have flagged.", "related_frameworks": ["the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "the-implementation-spectrum", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "network-intelligence-framework", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Second Brain Failure Mode Catalog", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Five enumerated failure modes specific to AI-automated capture-and-classify systems, each with a diagnostic signal and a concrete fix to skip hours of debugging.", "when_to_apply": ["My Slack-to-Notion automation creates rows but all the AI-classified fields are empty", "I rebuilt my capture pipeline and now it silently fails with no error messages", "The AI step returns good output but downstream steps can't parse it because of extra markdown formatting", "My automation broke after I changed permissions on the connected database", "I have a five-step automation and something is failing but I don't know which step is the problem"], "principles": ["Understand your data identifiers precisely \u2014 different ID fields (e.g., ts vs. thread_ts) serve different purposes and using the wrong one causes silent failures", "Be exact with naming \u2014 storage systems like Notion are case-sensitive and whitespace-sensitive; copy property names character by character", "Verify permissions after any change \u2014 some platforms require reinstallation or reauthorization for permission changes to take effect", "Handle messy AI output gracefully \u2014 build parsing steps that strip extra formatting and extract structured data even when the AI adds preamble or markdown", "Test incrementally \u2014 build one step, verify its output, then add the next step; building everything before testing makes failure points invisible"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "184882888", "title": "50 people ignored my tool recommendations + here's why their systems still worked (Second Brain follow-up)", "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z"}], "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z", "id": "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "core_question": "My automation that captures messages and classifies them into a database isn't working, and I can't tell whether the problem is in the data extraction, the naming, the permissions, the AI parsing, or the step sequencing.", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The active failure mode is property-name mismatch. The automation creates Notion rows but every classification field is blank \u2014 the tell is that the property name in the API call has a trailing space ('Category ' vs 'Category') that's invisible in the UI but causes Notion to silently ignore the write. Copying the property name character-by-character from Notion's API response instead of typing it manually resolves it instantly, skipping the hours typically spent questioning the AI output or permission config.", "related_frameworks": ["the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "ambient-failure-cascade", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Bidirectional Fit Assessment Interface", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes job-seeking from one-directional credential presentation to a mutual fit assessment where the candidate's interface actively evaluates and can reject mismatched opportunities.", "when_to_apply": ["I have deep expertise but keep getting rejected at the resume screen by people who can't evaluate what I actually do", "I want to build a professional presence that works asynchronously \u2014 something that represents me when I'm not in the room", "How do I stand out in a job market flooded with AI-polished resumes that all sound the same?", "I'm tired of dumbing down my experience to fit a one-page resume format", "I want employers to feel like they discovered my strengths rather than being pitched at"], "principles": ["Replace credential assertion with capability demonstration \u2014 an interactive interface that can be interrogated is harder to fake than a polished document", "Make the fit assessment bidirectional \u2014 the tool should honestly tell employers when you are NOT a good match, not just pitch your strengths", "Depth of response signals real expertise \u2014 multi-turn AI conversations about your work only hold up if genuine understanding underlies them", "Publishing your gaps builds more trust than claiming universal competence", "Honest rejection of bad fits signals market confidence and saves both parties time", "The interface functions as proof of capability, not a claim of capability \u2014 evaluators feel they discovered the truth rather than being told it", "The work required to build the interface (articulating expertise, identifying genuine strengths and gaps) improves the underlying substance it represents"], "steps": ["Choose one interface element to build first (AI-queryable summary, expanded achievement context, or fit assessment tool)", "Write a dense structured document of your real work organized for AI parsing, not human scanning \u2014 include specific projects, numbers, lessons, and honest gaps", "Write a system prompt governing how AI represents you: be specific, cite actual work, acknowledge limitations honestly, include explicit anti-sycophancy instructions and permission to reject mismatched roles", "Build the simplest viable version (structured document + instructions for use, Custom GPT, or web interface depending on available time)", "Publish it somewhere visible and distribute it through existing channels", "Refine based on observed queries \u2014 add context where AI responses lack depth, cut material that never gets queried, improve prompts based on edge cases"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "184666517", "title": "Why it's time to escape the application pile + the exact site I shipped (no code required!)", "date": "2026-01-16T14:02:47.831Z"}], "date": "2026-01-16T14:02:47.831Z", "id": "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "core_question": "Am I just another resume in a pile, or can I create a professional presence that lets the right people discover my real capabilities \u2014 and lets the wrong ones self-select out?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "A senior data engineer with 8 years of experience keeps getting filtered out by keyword-matching ATS systems because their resume compresses deep pipeline architecture work into generic bullet points. The conventional approach is to optimize the resume for more keywords. That's wrong here \u2014 optimization for keyword density compresses expertise further and makes them indistinguishable from junior candidates who've memorized the same terms. Instead, they build a queryable interface: a structured document of their actual projects (with real metrics, architectural decisions, and honest gaps like 'limited Spark Streaming experience') paired with a Custom GPT that can answer multi-turn technical questions about their work. When a hiring manager asks 'How did you handle schema evolution across 40 microservices?', the interface gives a detailed, cite-specific answer that no resume padding could survive. The interface also explicitly flags mismatches \u2014 'This candidate has no production ML pipeline experience; if that's core to the role, this isn't a fit' \u2014 which paradoxically increases trust in every positive claim it makes.", "related_frameworks": ["asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "five-element-ai-resume-bullet-test", "ai-assisted-resume-refinement-process", "ai-detector-risk-mitigation-checklist", "pull-push-paradigm", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "intent-engineering", "ai-career-board", "constraint-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Implementation Spectrum", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A five-tier classification of AI personal interface complexity, mapping time investment and technical skill required to corresponding capability levels, so practitioners can enter at the right tier.", "when_to_apply": ["I want to build a portfolio site that AI tools can query about me, but I'm not sure how technical it needs to be", "I'm advising someone non-technical on how to create an AI-readable professional presence without overwhelming them", "I've seen impressive AI-integrated personal sites and I'm trying to figure out which features I can actually build with my current skill level", "I'm scoping a personal branding project and need to decide how much time and complexity is justified at this stage", "Should I start simple or build the full version of my AI-queryable professional interface?"], "principles": ["The concept (AI-queryable self-presentation) is the innovation; implementation tier is refinement", "Start at the simplest tier that proves the concept, then iterate upward", "Technical skill should not gate access to the strategy \u2014 all tiers are accessible without engineering background", "Higher tiers offer bidirectional features (e.g., fit assessment) that lower tiers cannot support"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "184666517", "title": "Why it's time to escape the application pile + the exact site I shipped (no code required!)", "date": "2026-01-16T14:02:47.831Z"}], "date": "2026-01-16T14:02:47.831Z", "id": "the-implementation-spectrum", "core_question": "I want to build an AI-queryable professional presence but I don't know how much complexity I should take on given my skills and time \u2014 where do I actually start?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Someone wants recruiters and collaborators to be able to query their professional profile via AI but has no coding background and limited time. They fall into Tier 1 or 2 \u2014 a structured markdown document or a no-code site with embedded context is sufficient to prove the concept works. The bidirectional fit-assessment features they admire in polished examples are Tier 4-5 capabilities that require iteration to reach; starting there would stall the entire project. The right move is shipping the simplest queryable version this week, then climbing tiers as the concept proves out.", "related_frameworks": ["the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "pull-push-paradigm", "community-ai-collaborative-building-model", "ai-career-board"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Asserting Credentials vs. Demonstrating Capability", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between telling evaluators what to believe about you (resume assertions) versus creating conditions for evaluators to discover your capability through their own exploration of an interactive interface.", "when_to_apply": ["My resume looks the same as everyone else's because AI can generate polished bullets for anyone", "I have deep expertise but evaluators keep treating me like every other applicant with similar-sounding credentials", "I'm deciding whether to build a traditional portfolio or something interactive that lets people explore my work", "Hiring managers in my field have become skeptical of polished application materials because they assume AI wrote everything", "I want to stand out in a job search but I'm worried that more polish just looks like better prompting"], "principles": ["People believe conclusions they reach themselves more than conclusions they are told \u2014 design for discovery, not declaration", "AI-mediated self-presentation makes depth almost impossible to fake because multi-turn interrogation surfaces what is actually there", "Polish in a document proves only access to AI tools; depth in an interactive interface proves genuine understanding", "Honest acknowledgment of gaps in an interface demonstrates self-awareness, which itself is a credibility signal", "You architect the discovery conditions; the evaluator feels they formed their own judgment"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "184666517", "title": "Why it's time to escape the application pile + the exact site I shipped (no code required!)", "date": "2026-01-16T14:02:47.831Z"}], "date": "2026-01-16T14:02:47.831Z", "id": "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "core_question": "How do I present my expertise in a way that skeptical evaluators will actually believe, when polished documents no longer prove anything because AI can generate them for anyone?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This candidate's portfolio site is a static PDF with impressive bullet points \u2014 that's asserting credentials, not demonstrating capability. In a world where Claude can produce those same bullets for anyone, the signal value is zero. The candidate who built an interactive interface where the evaluator can ask follow-up questions, drill into edge cases, and probe reasoning depth is on the other side of the distinction \u2014 the evaluator discovers the expertise themselves and trusts it precisely because they feel they formed their own judgment rather than being told what to believe.", "related_frameworks": ["the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "pull-push-paradigm", "ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "five-element-ai-resume-bullet-test", "ai-detector-risk-mitigation-checklist", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "ai-career-board"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Bottleneck Relocation Model", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes workplace chaos and inefficiency as a predictable consequence of the bottleneck moving from execution to four new scarce resources (clarity, ambition, distribution, relationships), and diagnoses which old habits were built to protect the wrong constraint.", "when_to_apply": ["Our team adopted AI tools and got faster at building, but somehow our output quality and impact went down", "We have all these planning ceremonies and approval gates that used to feel essential but now feel like bureaucratic theater", "I can build anything in a weekend now but I'm paralyzed about what to build \u2014 the hard part completely flipped", "We're trying to figure out which organizational processes to kill versus keep after a major capability upgrade", "My old workflow was optimized for a world where building was expensive, and it's not working anymore but I can't articulate why"], "principles": ["Every work system is implicitly organized around protecting whatever resource is most scarce", "When a bottleneck is eliminated, it relocates downstream rather than disappearing", "When execution gets cheap, the new scarce resources become clarity, ambition, distribution, and relationships", "Habits and rituals designed to protect the old bottleneck become waste once the bottleneck moves", "Judgment, taste, and knowing what's worth building become more valuable as the cost of building falls"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "184506107", "title": "Grab the prompt kit I use when work feels inefficient but busy + specific experiments for each bottleneck", "date": "2026-01-15T14:02:49.579Z"}], "date": "2026-01-15T14:02:49.579Z", "id": "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "core_question": "We're busier than ever but producing less that matters \u2014 what shifted underneath us, and what should we actually be protecting now?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The conventional read is that the team needs better project management because they're shipping lots of mediocre work. That's wrong here \u2014 the bottleneck relocated. Execution used to be scarce, so every ritual (sprint planning, PRDs, approval gates) was designed to protect execution capacity from waste. Now execution is cheap \u2014 AI handles most of the building \u2014 but the team never reorganized around what's actually scarce: clarity about what's worth building and distribution to get it in front of the right people. The sprint ceremonies aren't enabling work anymore; they're protecting a constraint that no longer exists, which means they're pure drag. The investment should shift to taste-setting rituals (what's worth building?) and distribution habits (who sees it and why?).", "related_frameworks": ["old-ritual-failure-catalog", "coordination-tax-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "double-compression-loop", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "code-cost-vs-attention-cost-distinction", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "work-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Old-Ritual Failure Catalog", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Enumerates eight specific work habits that were rational under expensive-execution economics but now waste time or hide judgment, each with its original logic, its failure mode in the new environment, and a concrete substitute behavior.", "when_to_apply": ["My team is busy all the time but our actual output velocity feels embarrassingly low", "We have powerful AI tools available but our process still feels like it did two years ago", "Someone on the team is spending more time preparing to do the work than actually doing it", "I keep waiting for approvals or alignment meetings before I can make progress on something low-risk", "Our workflow has a lot of steps that feel like they exist to manage risk but the risk they manage is tiny compared to the delay they cause"], "principles": ["Permission loops distribute accountability but cost more time than the risk they hedge when execution is cheap", "Polish can be a form of procrastination and ego protection masquerading as diligence", "Meetings surface opinions but rarely resolve questions as cleanly as a prototype does", "Waiting for approvals or decisions now costs more than making provisional decisions and correcting course", "Planning elaborates assumptions without generating learning; learning only happens when something is built", "A deck describes a thing; a demo is the thing\u2014the former invites abstract argument, the latter anchors discussion in reality", "Consensus before action costs more than acting and reporting results, because people align around data more than proposals", "Hoarding work until it feels ready delays feedback to the point where changing course is expensive"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "184506107", "title": "Grab the prompt kit I use when work feels inefficient but busy + specific experiments for each bottleneck", "date": "2026-01-15T14:02:49.579Z"}], "date": "2026-01-15T14:02:49.579Z", "id": "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "core_question": "Which of my team's work habits are leftover reflexes from when execution was expensive, and are now just burning time without reducing risk?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The team spent three days building a slide deck to get leadership alignment on a feature direction. That's the 'deck instead of demo' failure mode \u2014 the original logic was that building was expensive so you had to secure buy-in before committing resources, but now a working prototype takes less time than the deck does. The deck invited two rounds of abstract debate about hypotheticals that a clickable demo would have resolved in the first meeting. The substitute: build the simplest version, show it, let people react to reality instead of a description of reality.", "related_frameworks": ["coordination-tax-audit", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "the-two-door-principle", "ai-leverage-audit", "work-primitives", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "double-compression-loop", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-simple-wins-method", "four-forms-of-societal-inertia"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Leverage Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A four-phase structured diagnostic that distinguishes genuine AI leverage from performative AI usage, helping individuals identify where their workflows, role boundaries, and outputs have actually changed versus where they are theater.", "when_to_apply": ["I tell people I use AI all the time but I'm not sure my actual output has changed", "I want to prepare for a performance review where AI adoption matters and I need to show real results, not just tool usage", "My manager keeps asking how I'm using AI and I realize I don't have concrete examples of changed outputs", "I suspect I'm protecting certain tasks from AI out of identity attachment rather than genuine necessity", "I'm using newer tools but running the same workflows I had two years ago and wonder what I'm missing"], "principles": ["Phases must be completed sequentially \u2014 skipping ahead exposes gaps in the audit", "The goal is making adaptation legible, not just documenting tool usage", "Activity theater (appearing to use AI) is distinct from leverage (measurably different outputs)", "Role boundaries should be tested against whether they reflect real constraints or habits drawn around human limitations that no longer apply", "The audit should produce shareable artifacts, not just personal reflection"], "steps": ["Phase 1: Identify which tasks you are instinctively protecting from AI \u2014 prevent the delusion of over-protecting tasks that don't need protecting", "Phase 2: Detect activity theater \u2014 catch performative AI usage before it shows up in performance reviews as 'I use AI for everything' with nothing to show for it", "Phase 3: Surface workflow lag \u2014 identify where you are running outdated workflows with current tools", "Phase 4: Audit role boundaries \u2014 expose which boundaries are real constraints versus legacy habits drawn around human limitations that no longer apply"], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": false, "source_posts": [{"id": "184242925", "title": "Shopify's AI Memo Was a Filter, Not a Productivity Play. Grab the AI Leverage Audit I built after watching Duolingo fail and Shopify succeed.", "date": "2026-01-13T14:03:34.033Z"}], "date": "2026-01-13T14:03:34.033Z", "id": "ai-leverage-audit", "core_question": "Am I actually getting leverage from AI, or am I just going through the motions and telling myself I've adapted?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Phase 1 reveals this person has been instinctively protecting their weekly market analysis from AI \u2014 not because it requires irreplaceable judgment, but because it's the task that makes them feel most like an expert. Phase 2 catches that their 'AI-assisted' client emails are just light grammar edits on prose they wrote entirely by hand \u2014 pure activity theater. Phase 3 surfaces the real cost: they're still running a four-step research workflow (gather \u2192 read \u2192 summarize \u2192 draft) when a single well-prompted session could compress it to two steps, meaning they're burning 40% of their research time on workflow lag. Phase 4 exposes that their role boundary of 'only I can synthesize competitive intelligence' is a legacy habit \u2014 the actual constraint is editorial judgment on what matters, not the synthesis itself.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "capability-surface-mapping", "ai-career-board", "weekly-delegation-audit", "work-primitives", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "comprehension-lock-in-audit"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "process_spec", "note": "4 steps contain temporal/phased language (month/week/phase/quarter). Likely a project plan, not a conversational reasoning framework."}]}
{"name": "AI Usage vs. AI Leverage Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "Separates the act of using AI tools (activity) from actually achieving different, measurable outputs through AI (leverage), preventing workers and organizations from confusing adoption metrics with genuine capability improvement.", "when_to_apply": ["My team all uses AI tools now but I can't point to any measurable improvement in our output", "We're tracking how often employees use ChatGPT or Copilot and I'm not sure that metric tells us anything real", "Someone on my team talks a lot about AI but their actual deliverables look the same as before", "Our company mandated AI adoption and everyone complied, but nothing seems to have actually changed", "I want to distinguish between employees who are genuinely transformed by AI and those who are just performing adoption"], "principles": ["'I use AI' is an activity claim; 'my output is different now' is a leverage claim \u2014 only the latter has value", "Performative productivity emerges when usage is measured instead of outcomes", "AI leverage requires workflow restructuring, not just tool insertion into existing processes", "The gap between usage and leverage is what separates companies with compounding AI advantage from those with the appearance of transformation"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "184242925", "title": "Shopify's AI Memo Was a Filter, Not a Productivity Play. Grab the AI Leverage Audit I built after watching Duolingo fail and Shopify succeed.", "date": "2026-01-13T14:03:34.033Z"}], "date": "2026-01-13T14:03:34.033Z", "id": "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "core_question": "Are we actually getting different results from AI, or are we just using AI tools and calling it transformation?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "This team reports 92% AI adoption \u2014 every member uses Copilot daily \u2014 but their sprint velocity, code quality metrics, and feature throughput are statistically identical to six months ago. That's usage, not leverage. The tell: they inserted AI into existing workflows without restructuring any of them. The developer who actually has leverage is the one who eliminated the entire manual QA review step by building an AI-driven test generation pipeline \u2014 her output is structurally different, not just AI-assisted.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-leverage-audit", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Two AI Economies Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that sorts work into two distinct AI economies\u2014abundance/production and judgment/complexity\u2014each with a matched tool philosophy and risk posture.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm trying to figure out whether it's okay to trust AI output without heavy review for this particular task", "My team uses the same review process for every AI-assisted deliverable and it feels wrong \u2014 some things we over-check and others we under-check", "I'm building a career strategy around AI and need to understand where human expertise still commands a premium versus where speed wins", "We're debating whether to adopt AI aggressively across the board or take a cautious approach, and neither extreme feels right", "I need to set different AI risk tolerances for different parts of our workflow but don't have a clear framework for which is which"], "principles": ["Tasks that produce outputs (content, drafts, media, creative exploration) belong to the production/abundance economy where speed and volume matter most", "Tasks that exercise judgment (production code, legal analysis, medical decisions, high-stakes analysis) belong to the complexity/judgment economy where reliability and verifiability matter most", "In the production economy, adopt aggressively\u2014competitors will, and the market handles benefits automatically", "In the judgment economy, prioritize adoption quality over adoption speed\u2014preserve human judgment alongside AI assistance", "The advantage in the production economy is knowing what to do with outputs, not just having the tools", "The advantage in the judgment economy is the human expertise the AI amplifies, not the AI itself", "Using the same risk posture for all tasks is the primary failure mode\u2014generating freely when you should verify, or over-reviewing when speed matters"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "184232481", "title": "Two founders, two safety theories, two products\u2014and a framework for knowing which one matches your risk tolerance", "date": "2026-01-12T14:03:50.969Z"}], "date": "2026-01-12T14:03:50.969Z", "id": "two-ai-economies-framework", "core_question": "Should I be optimizing this AI workflow for speed and volume, or for reliability and human oversight \u2014 and am I making the mistake of treating all my AI work the same way?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "Writing marketing copy for A/B testing falls squarely in the production/abundance economy \u2014 errors are cheap, iteration is fast, and the competitive advantage goes to whoever generates and tests more variants. The team should use the fastest, most aggressive AI tools available and stop over-reviewing drafts. But the same team's contract review workflow belongs to the judgment/complexity economy: an undetected clause error creates real liability. There, the advantage comes from the lawyer's expertise being amplified by AI, not replaced by it, and adoption quality matters far more than adoption speed. The failure happening right now is that legal is reviewing AI-generated contracts with the same casual posture the marketing team rightly uses for ad copy.", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-two-door-principle", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "verification-capability-triage", "realpolitik-safety"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Iteration-as-Safety vs. Safety-as-Precondition", "type": "distinction", "description": "A distinction between two coherent but incompatible theories of how AI safety is achieved\u2014through iterative real-world deployment and feedback, or through affirmative demonstration of safety before deployment.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm choosing between two AI providers and their release philosophies feel fundamentally different \u2014 how do I evaluate which risk profile I'm actually signing up for?", "Our team is debating whether to deploy an AI feature now and iterate, or wait until we can prove it's safe \u2014 and we can't agree because we're arguing from different assumptions", "An AI company just shipped a model that critics say was rushed, but the company argues real-world deployment IS the safety mechanism \u2014 who's right?", "We need to design a governance policy for AI releases at our company and the leadership team has incompatible intuitions about what 'responsible' means", "A competitor is moving faster than us on AI deployment and our board is asking whether our cautious approach is a safety advantage or a competitive liability"], "principles": ["Iteration-as-safety: real-world deployment at scale surfaces failure modes no internal testing can match; users function as a distributed red team; the public co-evolves with the technology", "Safety-as-precondition: safety must be demonstrated affirmatively before deployment; the public should not serve as a testing environment; proof of safety precedes scaling", "These are not reckless vs. cautious stances\u2014both are coherent epistemologies with intelligent defenders", "Under iteration-as-safety, competitive pressure triggers acceleration", "Under safety-as-precondition, competitive pressure may trigger a pause", "The choice of safety theory is encoded in organizational structure: governance, investor voting rights, and release processes all reflect the underlying philosophy"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "184232481", "title": "Two founders, two safety theories, two products\u2014and a framework for knowing which one matches your risk tolerance", "date": "2026-01-12T14:03:50.969Z"}], "date": "2026-01-12T14:03:50.969Z", "id": "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition", "core_question": "Does this AI organization (or my own deployment decision) treat safety as something you discover through shipping, or something you prove before shipping \u2014 and what does that imply about the risks I'm actually accepting?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "This company's governance structure reveals an iteration-as-safety epistemology: rapid public releases, investor voting rights concentrated with the founder, and a stated belief that 'the only way to make models safe is to deploy them.' That's not recklessness \u2014 it's a coherent theory that real-world feedback at scale surfaces failure modes no lab testing can. But it means their users are the red team, and anyone building on their API is implicitly accepting that theory. The competing provider's dual-key release process and third-party audit requirements encode the opposite theory \u2014 safety-as-precondition. Choosing between them isn't about which is 'more careful'; it's about which failure mode you'd rather be exposed to: unknown unknowns from insufficient real-world testing, or deployment delays that let competitors define the safety norms through iteration anyway.", "related_frameworks": ["realpolitik-safety", "blast-radius-assessment", "speed-of-control", "the-two-door-principle", "emergent-safety-dynamics-systems-not-components", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-reversibility-gap", "verification-frontier", "ai-agent-supervision-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Distribution Trap", "type": "reframe", "description": "Challenges the 'AI startups disrupt slow incumbents' narrative by arguing that distribution assets (procurement relationships, liability wrappers, embedded trust) often capture AI capability rather than being displaced by it.", "when_to_apply": ["We have a technically superior AI product but we're struggling to close enterprise deals against incumbents who have worse technology", "Should our AI startup go direct-to-enterprise or partner with an established services firm that already owns the customer relationship?", "An incumbent in our space just announced a partnership with an AI startup \u2014 does that mean they're threatened or that they've already won?", "We're analyzing where margin will ultimately settle in an AI-enabled market and whether the capability layer or distribution layer captures more value", "Our investment thesis assumes AI-native companies will displace slow-moving incumbents \u2014 is that actually how it plays out in high-trust markets?"], "principles": ["Capability and distribution are separable assets that can trade with each other rather than compete", "Incumbents with strong distribution can absorb AI capability by becoming customers or partners of startups", "The question is not which party has better technology but which party controls access to the end customer", "AI-native startups displacing incumbents is the exception, not the rule, in markets with high trust and relationship switching costs"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references a full framework analysis with multiple named sub-frameworks but only provides bullet-point summaries; the detailed reasoning and criteria for each are not included in the excerpt.", "source_posts": [{"id": "184090607", "title": "Executive Briefing: Distribution Ate Capability \u2014 What the Cognition\u2013Infosys Deal Reveals", "date": "2026-01-11T16:01:11.749Z"}], "date": "2026-01-11T16:01:11.749Z", "id": "the-distribution-trap", "core_question": "Will our AI startup's technical superiority actually win the market, or will the incumbents we're trying to displace just absorb what we've built and keep the customer?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that Cognition (Devin) is disrupting legacy IT services firms with superior AI engineering capability. That's wrong. Infosys didn't lose \u2014 they partnered, wrapping Cognition's capability inside their existing enterprise procurement relationships, liability frameworks, and trusted-vendor status. Cognition built the engine; Infosys kept the customer. The margin will accrue to whoever controls the last mile of trust and access, and in enterprise services, that's the incumbent with 30 years of procurement history, not the startup with the better demo.", "related_frameworks": ["distribution-beats-compute", "the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "developer-customers-vs-cio-customers-chasm", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "two-ai-economies-framework", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references a full framework analysis with multiple named sub-frameworks but only provides bullet-point summaries; the detailed reasoning and criteria for each are not included in the excerpt.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Three-Layer Analysis", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Categorizes work into three layers\u2014tokenizable cognition, accountability, and embodied execution\u2014to determine whether AI represents a threat or an opportunity for a given business model.", "when_to_apply": ["We need to figure out which parts of our professional services business are vulnerable to AI substitution and which are safe", "Our competitors are using AI to undercut our pricing \u2014 how do we decide what's worth defending versus what we should automate ourselves?", "I'm trying to explain to my board why some of our revenue streams are AI-proof and others aren't", "We're restructuring roles after adopting AI tools and need a principled way to decide which human positions still create value", "A client asked why they should keep paying us when AI can do the analysis \u2014 I need a clear answer about what they're actually buying"], "principles": ["Work that can be expressed as tokens (reasoning, drafting, analysis) is most immediately contestable by AI", "Accountability\u2014who bears liability and holds client relationships\u2014resists automation because it is relational and legal, not cognitive", "Embodied execution (physical presence, real-world coordination) remains outside current AI capability", "A business's resilience to AI depends on how much of its value sits in the accountability and embodied layers versus the tokenizable layer"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post names the three layers but does not provide the full diagnostic logic or decision rules for applying the categorization; those appear only in the full briefing.", "source_posts": [{"id": "184090607", "title": "Executive Briefing: Distribution Ate Capability \u2014 What the Cognition\u2013Infosys Deal Reveals", "date": "2026-01-11T16:01:11.749Z"}], "date": "2026-01-11T16:01:11.749Z", "id": "the-three-layer-analysis", "core_question": "How much of our business's value is actually just cognition that AI can now do cheaper and faster, versus the accountability relationships and physical presence that clients actually pay us to maintain?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "A mid-size accounting firm derives revenue from three activities: financial analysis and report drafting (tokenizable cognition), signing off on audits and bearing legal liability as the auditor of record (accountability), and on-site inventory counts and facility inspections (embodied execution). The tokenizable layer \u2014 roughly 60% of billable hours \u2014 is directly contestable by AI tools that clients can adopt themselves. The firm's durable position lives in the accountability layer: the partner's signature, the malpractice insurance, the client relationship that makes a CFO pick up the phone at 11pm before an earnings call. Strategy should shift investment toward deepening accountability relationships and using AI to compress the tokenizable work internally before clients start bypassing the firm to do it themselves.", "related_frameworks": ["three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "the-contestability-split", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "distribution-beats-compute", "double-compression-loop", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "work-primitives", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post names the three layers but does not provide the full diagnostic logic or decision rules for applying the categorization; those appear only in the full briefing.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Contestability Split", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between markets where AI kills long tails by making output substitutable versus markets where AI strengthens fragmentation because locality and relationships dominate.", "when_to_apply": ["I run a local services business and I'm trying to figure out whether AI-powered national competitors will eat my market", "We're investing in a niche vertical SaaS and need to know if AI consolidates or fragments that vertical", "Our industry has a long tail of small providers \u2014 will AI wipe them out or make them more competitive?", "I'm seeing the same AI capability applied across different market segments with completely different competitive effects and I need a framework for why", "Should we build our strategy around AI-driven scale advantages or around deepening local relationships that AI can't replicate?"], "principles": ["AI commoditizes markets where output is substitutable and quality is verifiable at low cost", "Markets anchored in local relationships, physical presence, or high-trust accountability resist AI-driven substitution", "The same AI capability can simultaneously consolidate some market segments while fragmenting others", "Long-tail providers in commoditizable segments face existential pressure; those in relationship-anchored segments may strengthen their position"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post names this framework in a bullet summary but the full criteria and decision logic for classifying a market as contestable versus fragmentation-resistant are in the unreproduced briefing.", "source_posts": [{"id": "184090607", "title": "Executive Briefing: Distribution Ate Capability \u2014 What the Cognition\u2013Infosys Deal Reveals", "date": "2026-01-11T16:01:11.749Z"}], "date": "2026-01-11T16:01:11.749Z", "id": "the-contestability-split", "core_question": "Will AI consolidate this market into a few dominant players, or will it actually reinforce the fragmented local/relational incumbents who already hold it?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "A regional home inspection company worries that AI-powered national platforms will commoditize their business. But this falls on the relationship-anchored side of the split: the output (a home inspection report) looks substitutable, but the actual purchase decision depends on local trust, physical presence, and personal accountability for a high-stakes asset. AI will make these inspectors more productive \u2014 faster reports, better image analysis \u2014 but it won't let a national platform replace the inspector the realtor trusts. Contrast this with freelance copywriting for product descriptions, where output is fully substitutable, quality is instantly verifiable, and the buyer has zero relationship loyalty. That segment is on the commoditization side \u2014 the long tail of mediocre copywriters gets killed.", "related_frameworks": ["four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-distribution-trap", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "double-compression-loop", "two-ai-economies-framework", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post names this framework in a bullet summary but the full criteria and decision logic for classifying a market as contestable versus fragmentation-resistant are in the unreproduced briefing.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Eight Building Blocks of a Second Brain", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of the functional components every reliable automated knowledge system needs, each with a plain-language name and engineering vocabulary.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep building personal knowledge management systems that work for a few weeks and then I abandon them", "I'm designing an AI-powered capture-and-retrieval workflow and want to make sure I haven't missed a critical component", "My automation pipeline processes inputs but I never trust its outputs enough to rely on them", "I need to explain to a non-technical person why their note-taking system needs more than just a place to put things", "I have a working capture system but nothing ever surfaces back to me when I actually need it"], "principles": ["Every reliable automated system needs a frictionless capture point (Drop Box / ingress point)", "Classification should be done by AI at processing time, not by the human at capture time (Sorter / classifier)", "Stored records must follow a consistent schema to be queryable and automatable (Form / data contract)", "There must be a single, authoritative persistence layer (Filing Cabinet / source of truth)", "Every action the system takes must be logged for auditability (Receipt / audit trail)", "Low-confidence outputs must be quarantined rather than filed (Bouncer / confidence filter)", "The system must proactively surface relevant information rather than waiting to be searched (Tap on the Shoulder / push mechanism)", "Correcting errors must require only one trivial action (Fix Button / human-in-the-loop correction)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183945429", "title": "Why every system you've tried has failed + grab the 90-minute guide to one that won't", "date": "2026-01-09T14:02:59.068Z"}], "date": "2026-01-09T14:02:59.068Z", "id": "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "core_question": "My knowledge system keeps collapsing \u2014 what essential pieces is it missing that would make it actually reliable and self-sustaining?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The user's Notion-based knowledge system captures notes beautifully but nothing ever resurfaces. Mapping against the eight building blocks: Drop Box exists (browser clipper), Sorter is absent (user manually tags at capture, so they stop capturing when busy), Form is weak (free-text notes with no consistent schema), Filing Cabinet exists (Notion database), Receipt is completely missing (no log of what the system did with each item), Bouncer is missing (half-parsed items get filed alongside clean ones, degrading trust), Tap on the Shoulder is the critical gap (nothing pushes relevant notes back to the user \u2014 retrieval is purely pull-based), and Fix Button is absent (correcting a misclassification requires navigating three nested pages). The system didn't fail because the user lacked discipline \u2014 it failed because four of eight structural components were never built.", "related_frameworks": ["second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "pull-push-paradigm", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "edge-first-automation-framework", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "ambient-failure-cascade", "four-layer-agent-memory-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Twelve Principles of Durable Automated Systems", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of design rules distilled from experienced system builders that determine whether an automated workflow will survive real-world use over time.", "when_to_apply": ["I built an automation that worked great for two weeks and then I stopped using it \u2014 what went wrong structurally?", "I'm about to wire together several AI tools into a personal productivity workflow and want to stress-test the design before building", "My system has too many fields, categories, and maintenance steps and I'm wondering what to cut versus keep", "I'm deciding whether to add a new feature to my existing automated workflow or if it will make the whole thing fragile", "I need to hand off an automation setup to someone non-technical and want to make sure it's robust enough to survive without me"], "principles": ["Reduce the human's job to one reliable behavior; automate everything else", "Separate memory (storage), compute (logic), and interface (human touchpoint) into distinct layers", "Treat prompts as strict API contracts with fixed input/output formats, not open-ended creative writing", "Build explicit trust mechanisms (logs, confidence scores, easy correction) not just capabilities", "Default to safe/no-action behavior when the system is uncertain rather than guessing", "Make outputs small, frequent, and immediately actionable rather than comprehensive", "Use 'next action' as the atomic unit of execution, not intentions or vague goals", "Prefer AI routing into a small fixed set of categories over requiring users to maintain taxonomies", "Keep categories and fields minimal; add complexity only when you feel genuine pain without it", "Design for easy restart after lapses, not for perfect continuous usage", "Build one stable core workflow first, then attach optional modules that don't modify the core", "Optimize for maintainability and fewer moving parts over clever or elegant abstractions"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183945429", "title": "Why every system you've tried has failed + grab the 90-minute guide to one that won't", "date": "2026-01-09T14:02:59.068Z"}], "date": "2026-01-09T14:02:59.068Z", "id": "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "core_question": "Will this automated system I'm building actually survive contact with my real life \u2014 the lapses, the edge cases, the gradual loss of motivation \u2014 or will it join the graveyard of clever setups I abandoned after two weeks?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This task management system violates at least four durability principles. It requires the user to maintain a custom tagging taxonomy (violates principle 8 \u2014 prefer AI routing into fixed categories), it generates weekly summary reports instead of next actions (violates principles 6 and 7 \u2014 outputs should be small, frequent, and action-atomic), it has no recovery path after a missed week (violates principle 10 \u2014 design for easy restart), and the prompt layer is entangled with the storage layer through inline formatting logic (violates principle 2 \u2014 separate memory, compute, and interface). The system will degrade the first time the user skips three days, because re-entry requires manually reconciling stale tags. Strip the taxonomy, make the AI classify into five fixed buckets, surface only the single next action per context, and add a 'catch me up' restart prompt.", "related_frameworks": ["second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "ambient-failure-cascade", "constraint-architecture", "edge-first-automation-framework", "core-automation-readiness-check", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-abstraction-tax-audit", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "the-simple-wins-method", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI as Multiplier (The Multiplier Insight)", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI leverage not as a function of prompts or tools but as a function of the depth of context and expertise being multiplied, making knowledge infrastructure the primary investment.", "when_to_apply": ["I've tried multiple AI tools and workflows but my output quality hasn't meaningfully improved", "Prioritizing between building domain knowledge vs. learning more AI techniques", "Explaining to a team why two people using the same AI tools get wildly different results", "Deciding whether to invest time in prompt engineering courses or in systematizing my professional knowledge", "I feel like I'm getting faster but not smarter with AI"], "principles": ["AI is a multiplier, so its value is determined by what it multiplies, not by the multiplier itself", "Shallow context multiplied produces faster shallow output; deep expertise multiplied produces qualitatively different output", "The strategic question is 'what am I multiplying?' not 'which tools should I use?'", "Building compounding context (captured knowledge, relationships, project history) is the highest-leverage AI investment", "Infrastructure that lets AI amplify accumulated expertise outperforms any prompt optimization"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183945429", "title": "Why every system you've tried has failed + grab the 90-minute guide to one that won't", "date": "2026-01-09T14:02:59.068Z"}], "date": "2026-01-09T14:02:59.068Z", "id": "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "core_question": "I keep optimizing my AI tools and prompts, but my outputs still feel generic \u2014 what if the bottleneck isn't the AI, it's what I'm feeding it?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Two consultants use the same Claude setup with similar prompts. One produces boilerplate strategy decks; the other produces insights clients call transformative. The difference isn't prompt skill \u2014 it's that the second consultant has 15 years of encoded pattern recognition, a personal knowledge base of 200+ engagements, and documented frameworks from every failure. AI is multiplying deep, structured expertise in one case and multiplying surface-level familiarity in the other. The highest-leverage move for the first consultant isn't a better prompt template \u2014 it's spending six months building the knowledge infrastructure that gives AI something worth amplifying.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-context-platform-compound", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "intent-engineering", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "knowledge-work-practice-gym"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Factory Curve", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI competition from model capability to supply chain position, arguing that the binding constraint has shifted from research milestones to physical factory economics (memory allocation, packaging capacity, power).", "when_to_apply": ["Is the current AI infrastructure buildout a bubble, or is demand genuinely outrunning physical supply?", "We're debating whether to lock in GPU/memory capacity now or wait for prices to drop \u2014 what's the real risk of waiting?", "Our AI product roadmap assumes compute availability that our vendor hasn't actually guaranteed", "Why is our competitor shipping faster when our model is technically better?", "Trying to understand which companies in the AI stack have real structural advantages versus hype-driven valuations"], "principles": ["Bubbles don't pre-buy bottlenecks \u2014 upstream procurement behavior (wafer-level memory, packaging slots, power agreements) reveals whether demand is real", "Supply ramps slowly due to physics and permitting; capital alone cannot accelerate it", "Demand compounds with state \u2014 stateful/agentic workloads grow memory footprint faster than request count", "'Catch up later' doesn't work \u2014 falling behind the curve means paying spot prices or queuing, not gradually closing the gap", "The unit of competition is the integrated system (factory module), not the individual component or model"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183802707", "title": "Why CES 2026 marks a turning point + the constraint map behind the headlines (and yes, there are prompts)", "date": "2026-01-08T14:02:54.667Z"}], "date": "2026-01-08T14:02:54.667Z", "id": "the-factory-curve", "core_question": "Is the real competitive barrier in AI the quality of the model, or the physical infrastructure supply chain that makes running it at scale possible?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that Company A is behind because its benchmark scores trail the frontier by six months. That's wrong here. Company A pre-bought 18 months of HBM packaging capacity and locked 200MW of power in three regions. Company B has a better model on paper but is already queuing inference jobs and paying spot rates for memory allocation. The binding constraint isn't the model \u2014 it's the factory curve. Company A can serve stateful agentic workloads at scale next quarter; Company B cannot, regardless of research velocity. The tell that this isn't a bubble: upstream suppliers are reporting committed wafer-level memory purchases with 18-month lead times, not speculative option contracts.", "related_frameworks": ["four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "four-lane-supply-strategy", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "distribution-beats-compute", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "two-ai-economies-framework", "the-three-layer-analysis"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four-Lane Supply Strategy", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of four distinct supply chain lanes that map to different factory bottlenecks, used to evaluate whether an AI infrastructure strategy addresses all binding constraints.", "when_to_apply": ["We've secured GPU capacity but I'm worried we're missing other infrastructure bottlenecks that will surface in 12-18 months", "Evaluating whether a competitor's AI infrastructure position is actually durable or just addresses the most visible constraint", "Building a multi-vendor AI supply chain and need to identify which lanes we haven't covered", "Our custom silicon program is on track but I'm not sure we've thought about upstream physical inputs like memory", "Stress-testing an AI infrastructure procurement plan to find gaps before they become crises"], "principles": ["Each lane solves a distinct constraint \u2014 time, supply risk, unit economics, and physical input scarcity", "A complete strategy requires coverage across all four lanes, not just the most visible one", "Multi-vendor sourcing across lanes mirrors multi-cloud logic \u2014 diversification reduces chokepoint risk", "Lane 4 (upstream physical inputs like memory at wafer level) is the most binding and hardest to acquire late"], "steps": ["Lane 1 \u2014 Bridge capacity: Solve the time constraint by securing rental-at-scale access while longer-term infrastructure comes online", "Lane 2 \u2014 Second-source compute: Solve supply risk by establishing a second GPU ecosystem or specialized infrastructure outside primary vendors", "Lane 3 \u2014 Custom silicon: Solve unit economics by pursuing purpose-built accelerators for predictable high-volume inference workloads", "Lane 4 \u2014 Memory supply: Solve the binding physical constraint by engaging memory suppliers at the wafer level as a strategic input"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183802707", "title": "Why CES 2026 marks a turning point + the constraint map behind the headlines (and yes, there are prompts)", "date": "2026-01-08T14:02:54.667Z"}], "date": "2026-01-08T14:02:54.667Z", "id": "four-lane-supply-strategy", "core_question": "Does our AI infrastructure strategy actually cover all four binding constraints, or are we over-indexing on the one bottleneck we can see while ignoring the ones that will bite us next?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "This company has locked in massive GPU reservations with a hyperscaler (Lane 1 bridge capacity) and started a custom silicon program (Lane 3), but has zero second-source compute relationship outside NVIDIA (Lane 2 gap) and no direct engagement with HBM suppliers at the wafer level (Lane 4 gap). The custom silicon bet won't matter if they can't secure memory allocation \u2014 Lane 4 is the binding constraint, and by the time they realize it, wafer commitments will already be locked up by competitors who moved 18 months earlier. Two of four lanes are uncovered, which means this infrastructure position is structurally fragile despite looking impressive on the compute side.", "related_frameworks": ["the-factory-curve", "four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "two-ai-economies-framework", "building-vs-buying-ai-response"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Ambient Failure Cascade", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Enumerates how ambient (always-on) AI systems produce categorically different failure modes than request-based AI, following a predictable cascade from degradation through liability to system pullback.", "when_to_apply": ["We're moving from a request-based AI feature to an always-on ambient system and need to understand what new failure modes we're accepting", "Our AI vendor's SLA covers uptime but I'm worried about silent quality degradation that nobody would catch", "We had an incident where the AI system didn't flag something it should have \u2014 how do we think about the full downstream exposure?", "Leadership wants to treat our continuous inference system with the same governance model we use for our chatbot", "We're designing reliability and governance requirements for an AI system that runs without per-request human review"], "principles": ["Degradation \u2192 missed signal \u2192 harm \u2192 investigation \u2192 liability \u2192 pullback is the standard cascade sequence", "The critical failure mode shifts from 'bad answer' to 'not there when needed'", "Reliability becomes existential \u2014 failure modes tolerable in occasional-use systems become unacceptable in always-on systems", "Governance becomes operational \u2014 compliance must be explainable in real time at scale, not checked at deployment", "Downtime becomes political when people depend on inference being continuously available", "SLAs covering uptime do not cover inference quality degradation \u2014 these are distinct failure surfaces"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183802707", "title": "Why CES 2026 marks a turning point + the constraint map behind the headlines (and yes, there are prompts)", "date": "2026-01-08T14:02:54.667Z"}], "date": "2026-01-08T14:02:54.667Z", "id": "ambient-failure-cascade", "core_question": "What happens when my always-on AI system doesn't fail loudly but instead silently degrades \u2014 and how does that cascade into something much worse than a bad answer?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The ambient monitoring system didn't crash \u2014 it quietly degraded inference quality over three weeks, missing a pattern it would have flagged at full fidelity. The missed signal led to a patient safety incident, which triggered a regulatory investigation. The vendor's SLA covered uptime (99.9%) but said nothing about inference quality, so the liability landed entirely on the deploying organization. This is the standard cascade: degradation \u2192 missed signal \u2192 harm \u2192 investigation \u2192 liability \u2192 pullback. The failure mode was never 'bad answer' \u2014 it was 'not there when needed,' which is categorically harder to detect and categorically more dangerous.", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "emergent-safety-dynamics-systems-not-components", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "proactively-reliable-ai-the-two-step-enterprise-strategy", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "pull-push-paradigm", "speed-of-control", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "ai-agent-supervision-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Done Definition Pattern", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured approach to converting vague task completion criteria into machine-checkable gates so that agent loops can converge on genuinely correct outputs rather than self-reported completion.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent says it's done but the output keeps failing when humans or downstream systems actually check it", "I'm writing acceptance criteria for an automated workflow and I can't tell which requirements are actually verifiable versus just vibes", "Our agent loop runs 8+ iterations on some tasks without converging and I need to figure out why it can't finish", "I'm setting up an agentic pipeline and need to decide what gets machine-checked versus what requires a human in the loop", "The team keeps arguing about whether agent output is 'good enough' because nobody wrote down what good enough actually means in checkable terms"], "principles": ["Treat 'done' as a contract with verifiable evidence, not a conversational cue or self-reported signal", "Separate what can be machine-verified (floor) from what requires human judgment (ceiling); let machines enforce the floor", "Constraints (must be true, checkable) are distinct from preferences (desired but uncheckable adjectives)", "Iteration only buys correctness if 'correct' is something you can actually check \u2014 vague definitions cause infinite grind, not convergence", "External verification (tests, linters, explicit gate criteria) must drive the loop, not model-generated completion signals", "Design checks adversarially: assume a system optimizing to pass checks will find ways to satisfy them without serving the underlying purpose", "Retries are a budget, not a sign of failure \u2014 different tasks deserve different iteration allowances, and someone must own that decision", "Checks require ongoing maintenance; they drift from actual requirements if not updated"], "steps": ["Audit existing requirements for 'adjective-gates' \u2014 vague qualitative criteria that sound like constraints but cannot be checked (e.g., 'professional', 'compelling'); flag these for conversion or manual review", "Convert checkable requirements into explicit gate tables: for each criterion, define what passing looks like, what evidence must be present, and a 'not yet' example of a near-miss that should still be rejected", "Classify each criterion as constraint (always true, machine-checkable), preference (requires human judgment each time), or situational (context-dependent, requires human judgment per instance)", "Attach evidence fields to each gate \u2014 proof that is verifiable (source link exists, number reconciles, quote is in transcript) versus confidence claims that require human review", "Set an explicit iteration budget (maximum retries) and define failure handling for tasks that do not converge within budget", "Pair the loop with external verification artifacts (tests, linters, validation scripts) rather than relying on the agent's self-assessment of completion", "Define governance: who has override authority when a gate blocks a time-sensitive need, whose definition of done wins when standards conflict, and who is accountable when the system executes correctly but the outcome is still wrong"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183669606", "title": "My honest field notes on the verification gap no one's talking about, plus the complete guide + prompt kit that makes agent loops actually converge", "date": "2026-01-07T14:02:58.904Z"}], "date": "2026-01-07T14:02:58.904Z", "id": "done-definition-pattern", "core_question": "How do I define 'done' for this task in a way that a machine can actually verify, so my agent loop converges on correct output instead of endlessly spinning or prematurely declaring victory?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The team's content-generation agent keeps producing blog posts it calls 'done' after one pass, but editors reject 40% of them. Auditing the completion criteria reveals three adjective-gates \u2014 'engaging,' 'professional,' and 'well-structured' \u2014 none of which map to anything checkable. Converting these: 'engaging' is uncheckable (preference, human review); 'professional' decomposes into two constraints (no first-person pronouns, Flesch-Kincaid score between 40-60) and one preference (tone); 'well-structured' becomes three gate checks (H2 every 300 words \u00b1 50, intro paragraph under 80 words, conclusion contains a CTA link). The iteration budget is set to 3 retries against the constraint gates before escalating to a human editor for the preference layer. Rejection rate drops to 12% because the agent now converges on the checkable floor and humans only judge what actually requires judgment.", "related_frameworks": ["the-verifiability-spectrum", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "correctness-definition-framework", "definition-of-done-rubric", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "verification-frontier", "verification-capability-triage", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "convergence-metric", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "moving-goalposts-failure-pattern", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-five-specification-primitives", "delegation-spec-drill", "intent-engineering", "constraint-architecture", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Constraints vs. Preferences vs. Proof vs. Confidence Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "A four-part vocabulary for classifying work requirements so that teams can separate machine-checkable gates from human-judgment calls, enabling reliable agent delegation.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent loop keeps running in circles and never seems to converge on a final output", "I'm writing acceptance criteria for a task I want to delegate to an AI agent and I'm not sure what counts as 'done'", "Some of my quality standards are gut-feel things like 'professional tone' and I need to figure out which checks to automate vs. review myself", "I can't tell whether the agent actually verified something or just expressed confidence that it's correct", "I want to build an automated review layer but I'm struggling to formalize what 'good' looks like for every dimension"], "principles": ["Constraints are what must be true and are objectively checkable ('must include a recommendation', 'must not exceed one page')", "Preferences are what you'd like but cannot be verified by a system ('should feel professional', 'should be compelling') \u2014 these require human evaluation", "Proof is a checkable artifact: the source link exists, the number reconciles, the quote appears in the transcript", "Confidence is a belief state ('I think this is correct') \u2014 it cannot be verified by a loop and requires human review at that stage", "Repeatable standards (always true regardless of context) can be built into automated checks; situational standards (meaning varies by context) require human judgment each time", "If you cannot convert a preference into a constraint, you must evaluate it yourself \u2014 it cannot be delegated to a loop"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183669606", "title": "My honest field notes on the verification gap no one's talking about, plus the complete guide + prompt kit that makes agent loops actually converge", "date": "2026-01-07T14:02:58.904Z"}], "date": "2026-01-07T14:02:58.904Z", "id": "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "core_question": "Which parts of this task can I actually hand off to an automated loop, and which parts require me to stay in the seat and judge?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "The brief says the output 'must include three sourced recommendations' and 'should feel strategic.' The first requirement is a constraint \u2014 an agent loop can check whether exactly three recommendations exist and whether each has a source link (proof). The second is a preference \u2014 'feels strategic' is a belief about tone that no automated check can verify. The team was stuck because their eval loop kept rejecting drafts on vibes it couldn't measure. Splitting the spec into checkable constraints and human-reviewed preferences means the loop converges on structure while a human makes the final taste call on tone.", "related_frameworks": ["the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "the-five-specification-primitives", "constraint-architecture", "done-definition-pattern", "correctness-definition-framework", "definition-of-done-rubric", "delegation-spec-drill", "convergence-metric", "verification-capability-triage", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "intent-engineering", "moving-goalposts-failure-pattern", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Convergence Metric", "type": "reframe", "description": "Replaces single-shot pass/fail AI evaluation with a measurement of how many iterations a system requires to reach a genuinely correct state, reframing quality as a budgetable resource rather than a binary capability.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent gets it wrong sometimes but usually fixes itself if I let it retry \u2014 how do I think about that systematically?", "I'm comparing two agent architectures and first-pass accuracy is similar but they feel very different in practice", "I need to set a compute or review budget for an agentic workflow and don't know what 'good enough' means", "We're deciding which tasks to automate with agents and need a better criterion than 'can the AI do it?'", "Our agent loop keeps running but I can't tell if it's converging toward correct or just burning tokens"], "principles": ["The relevant question for agentic work is not 'can it do it?' but 'does it converge, and at what cost?'", "Quality becomes something you budget for: some tasks are worth five retries, some fifty, some are not worth automating at all because verification costs exceed the value", "Convergence only has meaning when 'correct' is verifiable \u2014 tasks without checkable definitions produce activity, not convergence", "Track failure rate for tasks that never converge within budget as a separate signal from iteration-to-success"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183669606", "title": "My honest field notes on the verification gap no one's talking about, plus the complete guide + prompt kit that makes agent loops actually converge", "date": "2026-01-07T14:02:58.904Z"}], "date": "2026-01-07T14:02:58.904Z", "id": "convergence-metric", "core_question": "Should I judge this AI system by whether it gets the answer right on the first try, or by whether it reliably gets there \u2014 and what that reliability actually costs me?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The conventional read is that Agent A (72% first-pass accuracy) outperforms Agent B (58% first-pass accuracy) on contract review. That's wrong here because Agent B converges to correct output within 3 iterations 94% of the time, while Agent A plateaus at 81% even after 5 retries \u2014 its errors are the kind it can't self-correct. Agent B with a 3-iteration budget is the more reliable system, and the iteration cost (roughly $0.12 per contract) is trivially worth it against the value of a correctly reviewed clause. The real decision is which tasks never converge within any reasonable budget \u2014 those are the ones to pull back to humans.", "related_frameworks": ["the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "correctness-definition-framework", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "verification-capability-triage", "done-definition-pattern", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "ai-leverage-audit", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Harness Selection Decision Framework", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-question diagnostic that matches the right agentic harness to a specific type of work by evaluating verification loop, execution environment, and control requirements.", "when_to_apply": ["We're choosing between Manus, Devin, Claude Code, and similar agentic tools for a specific workflow and need a principled way to decide", "Our team adopted a general-purpose agent harness but it keeps failing on tasks where success criteria are ambiguous \u2014 should we switch?", "I need to assign different agentic tools to different use cases across my team rather than standardizing on one", "We're evaluating an agentic platform but our data can't leave our infrastructure \u2014 how do I weigh that against capability?", "The agent works great for coding tasks but breaks down when we try to use it for research and analysis \u2014 is the harness wrong for the job?"], "principles": ["The right question is not which harness is best overall but which is best for the specific kind of work needed", "A harness optimized for tight verification loops (e.g., code compilation, test passing) will outperform a general harness on tasks with clear machine-checkable success criteria", "Each harness reflects a specific philosophy about memory, tools, verification, and governance that is not easily portable to another harness", "Data sensitivity requirements must be evaluated separately from functional fit", "Harnesses are not interchangeable products\u2014choosing one is a commitment, not a subscription you can easily swap"], "steps": ["Ask: What does your verification loop look like? If success is mechanically checkable (tests pass, code compiles, output matches schema), choose a harness optimized for tight feedback loops. If success is ambiguous, choose a harness with strong visibility and intervention points.", "Ask: What environment does the work live in? Identify whether the primary environment is a codebase, a productivity suite, a browser, email/calendar, or multi-system orchestration, then match to a harness designed for that environment.", "Ask: How much do you need to control each step? Choose 'fire and forget' harnesses for high-trust discrete tasks; choose step-visible harnesses for sensitive or ambiguous workflows.", "Assess data sensitivity: determine whether the harness routes data through external model providers, and whether that is acceptable given your compliance requirements.", "Run your actual messy real-world tasks through the candidate harness and evaluate whether it finishes reliably at the step where similar systems typically fail."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183624198", "title": "Meta bought Manus for $2B to acquire an \"agentic harness\"\u2014here's what \"agentic harness\" means and why it's worth $2B (yes really)", "date": "2026-01-06T14:03:30.662Z"}], "date": "2026-01-06T14:03:30.662Z", "id": "harness-selection-decision-framework", "core_question": "I have a specific type of work I want to delegate to an AI agent \u2014 which agentic harness actually fits that work, versus which one just sounds impressive?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The task is generating weekly compliance reports by pulling data from three internal systems and formatting to a regulatory schema. The verification loop is mechanically checkable \u2014 output either matches the schema or doesn't, and the numbers either reconcile or they don't. The environment is multi-system orchestration across internal databases. But the data is highly sensitive financial information that cannot route through external model providers. This rules out general-purpose browser-based harnesses like Manus despite their orchestration strength \u2014 the compliance constraint eliminates them before functional fit even matters. The right match is a self-hosted harness with tight feedback loops and schema validation, even if it requires more setup.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "five-harness-divergence-dimensions", "harness-decision-audit", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "blast-radius-assessment", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "the-three-question-workflow-audit"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Multi-Step Reliability Compounding Model", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes agent reliability from a per-step accuracy question into a compounding probability problem, showing why even high per-step accuracy leads to near-certain failure over long task horizons without error-recovery infrastructure.", "when_to_apply": ["Our agent gets 95% accuracy per step but only finishes the full workflow about 10% of the time", "Deciding whether to invest in a better model or in error-recovery infrastructure for an agentic system", "Someone claims their agent is reliable because each individual step has high accuracy", "Designing checkpoints and rollback logic for a multi-step autonomous agent workflow", "Explaining to leadership why an agent that demos well on 5-step tasks will fail in production on 50-step tasks"], "principles": ["Per-step accuracy compounds multiplicatively: 95% accuracy over 50 steps yields less than 10% end-to-end success", "The finishing problem is a system problem, not a model problem\u2014errors compound without mechanisms to detect and recover from them", "Four harness mechanisms counteract compounding: verification loops that catch errors before they propagate, goal recitation that fights attention decay, error preservation that updates model priors within a run, and external memory that offloads context without losing retrievability", "Tasks with built-in machine-checkable verification loops (e.g., software tests) are structurally more reliable than open-ended tasks because failures are caught at each step", "Production taste and iteration on real-world failure modes cannot be replicated by reading about the problem\u2014the moat is empirical know-how"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183624198", "title": "Meta bought Manus for $2B to acquire an \"agentic harness\"\u2014here's what \"agentic harness\" means and why it's worth $2B (yes really)", "date": "2026-01-06T14:03:30.662Z"}], "date": "2026-01-06T14:03:30.662Z", "id": "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "core_question": "My agent works well on short tasks but falls apart on longer ones \u2014 is this a model quality problem or something more fundamental?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The team reports 96% per-step accuracy on their 40-step document processing agent and treats it as nearly production-ready. But 0.96^40 = 0.20 \u2014 the agent completes the full pipeline successfully only one time in five. The problem isn't the 4% error rate; it's that there's no verification loop catching errors before they compound. Adding machine-checkable validation after steps 10, 20, and 30 \u2014 with automatic rollback and retry \u2014 transforms the math: each 10-step segment now runs at ~66% raw success but gets three retry attempts, pushing effective segment completion above 95% and full-pipeline success above 85%. The model didn't improve; the harness changed the compounding equation.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "blast-radius-assessment", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "harness-decision-audit", "five-harness-divergence-dimensions", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "ambient-failure-cascade", "agent-deployment-safety-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Career Board", "type": "procedure", "description": "A quarterly self-governance system where you submit a structured board report to AI personas playing distinct roles (strategist, accountability hawk, skeptic) to force honest scrutiny of career decisions, avoided actions, and skill portfolio shifts.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep saying I'm going to make a career move but quarter after quarter nothing actually changes", "I don't have a mentor or honest peer group and I need something that will actually challenge my self-assessment with evidence", "I've been doing a lot of 'career development' activities but I can't point to a single concrete outcome that shifted my position", "I notice I'm avoiding a specific career decision and I keep rationalizing the delay with increasingly creative excuses", "I want a structured quarterly practice that forces me to confront the gap between what I said I'd do and what I actually did"], "principles": ["Receipts only: every claim must point to an observable artifact, calendar entry, decision made, or metric moved \u2014 not narrative", "Name one avoided decision every quarter, specifically enough to be undeniable", "Produce one falsifiable bet per session that can be verified as true or false in ninety days", "Name one comfort-work item \u2014 something done because it feels productive, not because it moves the needle", "Design the system for interrogation, not reassurance \u2014 if you leave feeling praised and can't name one uncomfortable sentence, you are gaming it", "The board uses multiple distinct voices (strategist, accountability hawk, skeptic) to prevent single-angle blind spots", "The value compounds across quarters: patterns only become undeniable when the record accumulates"], "steps": ["Maintain a quarterly board report with six fields: (1) verbatim commitments from last quarter, (2) receipts of what actually happened, (3) one specifically named avoided decision, (4) one comfort-work item, (5) problem portfolio snapshot with time allocation, (6) one falsifiable next-quarter bet", "At the start of each quarter, complete the self-report honestly before engaging AI personas", "Submit the report to AI board personas assigned to distinct roles \u2014 strategist (long-term positioning), accountability hawk (intention vs. action gap), skeptic (holes in your narrative)", "Run the board session: each persona pushes from its angle, demanding evidence when claims are vague and naming what you are avoiding", "Exit the session with: an honest current assessment, specific concerns surfaced, concrete actions for the next ninety days, and one question you cannot yet answer but should be able to answer by next quarter", "At the next quarterly session, open by reading last quarter's verbatim commitments and falsifiable bet before writing anything new \u2014 this forces confrontation with the gap"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182961038", "title": "My honest field notes on being my own unreliable narrator + the governance mechanism I finally built to help", "date": "2026-01-05T14:00:46.011Z"}], "date": "2026-01-05T14:00:46.011Z", "id": "ai-career-board", "core_question": "Am I actually making progress in my career, or am I just telling myself a story that feels like progress while avoiding the hard moves?", "category": "leadership", "example_application": "Last quarter's falsifiable bet was 'I will have shipped a public portfolio piece demonstrating AI agent architecture by March 31.' The receipt: nothing shipped. The narrative I told myself \u2014 'I was too busy with client work' \u2014 collapses under scrutiny because calendar data shows eleven hours spent on comfort-work (reorganizing my knowledge management system) that moved nothing. The avoided decision, named specifically: I haven't told my current employer I'm exploring external roles, which means every 'career development' activity is theater performed inside a safe container. The accountability hawk's verdict: the skill portfolio is flat quarter-over-quarter, and the busyness is functioning as a shield against the one conversation that would actually change the trajectory.", "related_frameworks": ["problem-portfolio-assessment", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "experiential-debt", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "llm-psychosis-self-audit-framework", "temporal-separation-build-reflect-rhythm", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-three-board-audit", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "knowledge-work-practice-gym"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Problem Portfolio Assessment", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A four-question diagnostic applied to each problem you are paid to solve that classifies whether each skill is appreciating or depreciating in value, surfacing where to invest versus exit.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a nagging sense that half of what I do every day could be done by AI in a year, but I'm not sure which half", "I'm preparing for a career conversation and need to articulate my value beyond my job title or tech stack", "I want to figure out which parts of my role to actively grow versus which to hand off or automate away", "I'm considering a career pivot and need to know which of my capabilities are portable versus locked to my current employer", "My performance review is coming up and I realize I can't clearly name the three problems that justify my compensation"], "principles": ["Define your portfolio by the three to five problems that would go unsolved if you disappeared \u2014 not your title or skill list", "Scarcity of substitutes determines value: the shorter the list of people who can solve a problem without supervision, the more valuable it is", "Consequence severity matters: high-downside problems command premium value for the skill to solve them correctly", "Assess AI directionality per problem: some problems are being automated (depreciating), others become more judgment-intensive as output volume increases (appreciating)", "Transferability multiplies value: skill that travels across companies and contexts is worth more than knowledge locked to one context"], "steps": ["List the three to five problems you are actually paid to solve \u2014 stated as what would go unsolved, not as job functions", "For each problem, answer: How many others could solve this without supervision?", "For each problem, answer: What breaks if it is solved poorly \u2014 money, trust, speed, or safety?", "For each problem, answer: Does AI make this cheaper to do, or does it make mistakes more expensive to catch?", "For each problem, answer: Does this skill transfer across companies and contexts, or is it locked to your current situation?", "Classify each problem as appreciating, depreciating, or neutral based on the four answers", "Identify the gap between where you spend time and where the appreciating problems sit"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182961038", "title": "My honest field notes on being my own unreliable narrator + the governance mechanism I finally built to help", "date": "2026-01-05T14:00:46.011Z"}], "date": "2026-01-05T14:00:46.011Z", "id": "problem-portfolio-assessment", "core_question": "Am I investing my time in problems that are becoming more valuable, or am I doubling down on work that's quietly being commoditized?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "A senior data engineer defines their real portfolio as: (1) designing data pipelines for novel integrations, (2) debugging production data quality incidents under time pressure, (3) writing migration scripts, (4) mentoring junior engineers, (5) vendor evaluation for data tools. Problem 3 \u2014 migration scripts \u2014 has few substitutes today but AI is rapidly closing that gap; it's depreciating. Problem 2 \u2014 debugging production incidents where wrong data could cascade into billing errors \u2014 has high consequence severity, few people who can do it unsupervised, and AI actually makes it harder because automated pipelines create more failure surface area; it's appreciating. The gap is clear: 40% of weekly time goes to migration scripts, less than 10% to building systematic incident diagnosis capability. The reallocation target writes itself.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-career-board", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "ai-leverage-audit", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "work-primitives", "capability-surface-mapping", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "experiential-debt"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Magnifying-Glass Company vs. Tiger-Team Company", "type": "distinction", "description": "A strategic fork distinguishing organizations that use AI primarily for visibility and oversight from those that use AI to multiply execution power in small, high-trust teams.", "when_to_apply": ["We're rolling out AI productivity scoring and our best people seem disengaged \u2014 is the tool the problem?", "Our dashboards show improving metrics but actual product velocity feels slower than before we added all this visibility tooling", "I'm deciding whether to invest our AI budget in management oversight tools or in giving small teams better execution capabilities", "High-performing teams that used to operate autonomously are now spending significant time on reporting and legibility work", "We have extensive AI-powered metrics but I suspect people are gaming the measurements rather than doing better work"], "principles": ["Legibility should follow work, not dictate it \u2014 AI should reconstruct what happened after the fact, not impose structure before", "The magnifying-glass path selects for the feeling of control; the tiger-team path selects for the reality of output", "Surveillance creates concealment: when measurement systems reward clean legibility, real messy work goes underground", "When a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure \u2014 AI accelerates this failure mode by making the measurement layer cheap and authoritative-looking", "Small, high-trust teams with clear scope and autonomy are the primary production units of value", "AI's role in a tiger-team company is as translator and historian, not bureaucrat or overseer"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183195795", "title": "Grab the 4 prompts I use to make messy work legible--without killing what made it valuable + the visibility trap most companies fall into (and how to avoid it)", "date": "2026-01-03T15:02:16.125Z"}], "date": "2026-01-03T15:02:16.125Z", "id": "magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company", "core_question": "Is our AI investment actually making us more effective, or are we just building a more sophisticated panopticon that drives real work underground?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "This organization is a magnifying-glass company. The tell: they just deployed AI-powered productivity dashboards across engineering, and their best engineers are now spending 30% of their time making work legible to the dashboard rather than doing the work. Sprint velocity looks great on the executive deck, but the three senior engineers who drove 60% of the team's real output are interviewing elsewhere. Meanwhile, the messy experimental work that produced last quarter's breakthrough feature has stopped entirely \u2014 nobody will touch ambiguous projects that score poorly on the visibility tools. The AI investment is selecting for the feeling of control while destroying the conditions that produce output.", "related_frameworks": ["talent-suppression-diagnostic", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "coordination-tax-audit", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "ai-leverage-audit", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "three-stances-toward-mess"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Legible vs. Illegible Work", "type": "distinction", "description": "A classification that separates work that fits neatly into organizational systems (tickets, OKRs, dashboards) from the informal, trust-based, backchannel work that actually keeps companies running.", "when_to_apply": ["Our top performer's contributions don't show up in any dashboard or OKR, and I'm worried they'll get passed over or managed out", "We're rolling out AI productivity monitoring and I need to think about what it will actually capture versus what it will miss", "During a crisis, the people who stepped up weren't the ones on the org chart \u2014 how do I protect that dynamic without formalizing it to death?", "We're deciding which informal processes to formalize and I'm worried about killing the fast paths that actually keep us running", "A team looks great on paper metrics but something feels off about their actual output and impact"], "principles": ["Illegible work \u2014 favors, backchannels, shared intuition, emergency response \u2014 is the actual engine of value, not a supplement to legible work", "The legible layer is scaffolding: necessary and useful, but not where value gets created", "The company tells the truth about itself in emergencies: who people actually go to reveals the real org chart", "AI amplifies illegible work by giving small teams leverage, making the illegible layer more powerful and more at risk from surveillance", "Tiger teams \u2014 ad hoc small groups that form organically to handle crises \u2014 are the clearest expression of illegible work in action"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183195795", "title": "Grab the 4 prompts I use to make messy work legible--without killing what made it valuable + the visibility trap most companies fall into (and how to avoid it)", "date": "2026-01-03T15:02:16.125Z"}], "date": "2026-01-03T15:02:16.125Z", "id": "legible-vs-illegible-work", "core_question": "Are our measurement systems capturing the work that actually matters, or are we optimizing for the visible layer while the real value creation stays invisible \u2014 and vulnerable?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "This team's dashboards show green across every OKR, but the product launch nearly failed until three people outside the formal project structure stepped in to unblock vendor negotiations and rewrite the integration spec over a weekend. That rescue operation \u2014 the tiger team that formed organically around the crisis \u2014 is the illegible layer doing the real work. The legible metrics can't see it, which means the next reorg might eliminate the exact people who saved the launch. The performance system is measuring scaffolding and calling it the building.", "related_frameworks": ["talent-suppression-diagnostic", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "coordination-tax-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "kill-the-contribution-badge", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "work-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Potemkin Organization Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured check for whether AI-generated dashboards and reports are producing genuine organizational understanding or a confident-looking map that has drifted from reality.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI dashboards all show green but something still feels off \u2014 teams are struggling and customers are unhappy despite the metrics", "I suspect my team is writing status updates to trigger the right signals in our AI reporting tool rather than describing what's actually happening", "We deployed an AI-powered management visibility layer and I want to check whether it's actually improving decisions or just generating confident noise", "An IC is pushing back on our metrics saying they don't capture what their work actually produces, and I'm not sure they're wrong", "Leadership is making decisions based on AI-generated reports and I need to audit whether those reports reflect reality or a Potemkin version of it"], "principles": ["Confidence and accuracy are not the same thing \u2014 authoritative-looking outputs can be precisely wrong", "Ask what a metric actually measures, then trace whether that maps to an outcome anyone cares about", "Check whether teams have learned to game the inputs \u2014 writing updates to trigger desired signals rather than report reality", "The danger is not blindness but overconfidence in the wrong map: a detailed, precise map that has drifted from the territory", "AI drops the cost of generating the appearance of understanding toward zero, making fake visibility cheaper and more dangerous than ever"], "steps": ["Identify a dashboard, metric, or AI-generated report that feels authoritative", "Ask what the metric literally measures \u2014 not what it claims to measure, but the actual data inputs", "Trace whether that literal measurement maps to the outcome leadership actually cares about", "Check whether teams know how the system scores them and have changed behavior to hit those scores", "Determine whether the report is helping leadership understand reality or generating a confident map the organization is bending itself toward"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183195795", "title": "Grab the 4 prompts I use to make messy work legible--without killing what made it valuable + the visibility trap most companies fall into (and how to avoid it)", "date": "2026-01-03T15:02:16.125Z"}], "date": "2026-01-03T15:02:16.125Z", "id": "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "core_question": "Are our AI-powered dashboards and reports showing us what's actually happening, or have they become a beautifully detailed map that no longer matches the territory?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The AI-generated sprint health dashboard shows 94% on-time delivery and green across all teams \u2014 but the metric literally measures 'stories marked done by sprint end.' Teams learned this six months ago and started splitting work into trivially small stories and marking them complete with known defects. Leadership sees a picture of execution excellence; the actual product ships late every quarter. The dashboard isn't producing understanding \u2014 it's producing the appearance of understanding, and the organization is contorting itself to feed the appearance rather than fix the reality.", "related_frameworks": ["legible-vs-illegible-work", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "ambient-failure-cascade", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "talent-suppression-diagnostic"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Weekend-Hack Test", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A single-question test for whether an organization's measurement and planning systems are selecting for legibility or for value, by asking whether unplanned, off-roadmap work could survive and be recognized.", "when_to_apply": ["We're implementing AI-powered productivity metrics and I'm worried they'll punish our best people", "Our sprint process feels airtight but our best engineers say the most important work happens despite the process, not because of it", "I want a quick gut-check on whether our OKR and planning system is crowding out emergent innovation", "We're designing a performance review process and need to make sure it doesn't optimize for visibility over actual impact", "Someone on my team just built something amazing that wasn't on any roadmap and I'm not sure our systems can even recognize it"], "principles": ["Ask: if an engineer showed up Monday with something built over the weekend \u2014 no ticket, no sprint, no OKR \u2014 would our systems flag it as a problem or recognize it as value?", "The magnifying-glass company measures unplanned work out of existence; the tiger-team company protects the conditions that made it possible", "The most valuable work is often the work that cannot be planned in advance because it solves problems the process cannot acknowledge", "A leader's job when unplanned valuable work surfaces is not to squish it but to recognize its alignment and pull it in without killing the conditions that produced it"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183195795", "title": "Grab the 4 prompts I use to make messy work legible--without killing what made it valuable + the visibility trap most companies fall into (and how to avoid it)", "date": "2026-01-03T15:02:16.125Z"}], "date": "2026-01-03T15:02:16.125Z", "id": "the-weekend-hack-test", "core_question": "Are our planning and measurement systems rewarding the work that matters most, or only the work that was planned in advance?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "Last quarter, an engineer built a migration tool over a weekend that eliminated a recurring production incident \u2014 no ticket, no OKR, no sprint story. The sprint review flagged it as unplanned work, the velocity chart treated it as noise, and the performance review couldn't account for it because it didn't map to any committed objective. That's the diagnostic verdict: this organization's measurement stack is selecting for legibility over value. The most impactful work of the quarter was structurally invisible to every system that decides what counts.", "related_frameworks": ["magnifying-glass-company-vs-tiger-team-company", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "coordination-tax-audit", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "kill-the-contribution-badge"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Failure Patterns of Agent Intent Misalignment", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Classifies the three primary ways AI agents misread user intent into distinct failure modes, each with characteristic signals and corresponding fixes.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent completed a task correctly by one measure but the result feels obviously wrong in a way I can't articulate", "An agent picked a valid interpretation of my request but it wasn't the one I meant, and I need to understand why", "I'm doing a post-mortem on an agent action that caused real-world damage and need to classify the root cause", "My agentic workflow keeps producing subtle failures and I want to know if they share a common pattern", "An AI assistant did something socially inappropriate that any human colleague would have known to avoid"], "principles": ["Most agent failures fall into one of three predictable buckets, not random edge cases", "Ambiguity failures occur when a model silently picks one of multiple valid interpretations without surfacing the choice", "Wrong-optimization failures occur when the model improves a measurable proxy while degrading the real outcome", "Social boundary failures occur when implicit human context (relationships, timing, exclusions) is missing from the instruction", "The common root cause across all three is the model committing before the misread can be caught"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183163045", "title": "Why capable AI is a liability when intent is off + my template that makes interpretation visible before action", "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z"}], "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z", "id": "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "core_question": "My AI agent did something I didn't want \u2014 was it confused about what I meant, optimizing the wrong thing, or missing social context I never explicitly stated?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The agent scheduled a client dinner and invited the founder's ex-cofounder \u2014 technically a 'key stakeholder' per the CRM data, but anyone with organizational context would know that relationship is toxic. This is a social boundary failure, not an ambiguity or optimization problem. The instruction 'invite key stakeholders' was unambiguous and the optimization target was correct \u2014 the failure is that implicit human context (who should be excluded, why, and the relational history) was never part of the agent's information set. The fix isn't better prompting; it's adding an exclusion layer or requiring human confirmation on attendee lists where relationship dynamics matter.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "the-five-specification-primitives", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "delegation-spec-drill", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "constraint-architecture", "intent-doc"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Action Risk Bucketing (Let It Run / Checkpoint / Always Confirm)", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-tier classification system for AI agent actions based on reversibility and who is affected, used to determine when to require human confirmation before execution.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm building an AI agent that takes real-world actions and I need to decide which ones require human approval", "Our automation sent an email to a client it shouldn't have and now I need to design proper guardrails", "I want my agent to be autonomous on low-risk stuff but I'm not sure where to draw the line on what counts as low-risk", "Setting up confirmation logic for a multi-step AI workflow that touches databases, APIs, and people", "My team is debating whether every agent action needs human review or if that kills the productivity gains"], "principles": ["Reversibility is the primary sorting criterion: irreversible actions always require a gate", "Actions that affect only the requester carry lower risk than actions that affect other people or external systems", "Low-risk (reversible, self-contained) actions should run without gates to avoid unnecessary friction", "Outbound actions touching other people or systems get a checkpoint by default", "Anything irreversible \u2014 deletions, financial transactions, external communications at scale \u2014 requires confirmation and logging with no exceptions"], "steps": ["Identify the action the agent is about to take", "Ask: if the AI gets this wrong, what is the damage? Reversible or irreversible? Affects only me or others?", "Classify into one of three buckets: (1) Low-risk / reversible / self-only \u2192 let it run; (2) Touches other people or systems \u2192 checkpoint before executing; (3) Irreversible or high-stakes \u2192 gate it, log it, require explicit confirmation", "For gated actions, surface the agent's interpretation (not just a yes/no prompt) so the user can verify intent before approving"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183163045", "title": "Why capable AI is a liability when intent is off + my template that makes interpretation visible before action", "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z"}], "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z", "id": "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "core_question": "Which actions should my AI agent just do, which should it pause on, and which should it never execute without my explicit say-so?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The agent is about to send a Slack message to a client channel summarizing a project status update. This touches other people and an external system \u2014 it's a checkpoint action, not a let-it-run. The agent should surface its draft and its interpretation of the status ('I'm reading this as on-track with one risk flag on the design milestone') before sending, because a misread of project status broadcast to a client is embarrassing but not irreversible. If instead the agent were about to process a bulk refund of $40K across 200 customer accounts, that's irreversible and high-stakes \u2014 it gets gated, logged, and requires explicit confirmation with the agent's interpretation of which accounts qualify and why visible before approval.", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "the-reversibility-gap", "primitives-for-reversibility", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "speed-of-control", "the-two-door-principle", "intent-engineering", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "agentic-system-gut-check", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "intent-doc", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "constraint-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Intent Doc", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-field structured template that makes implicit task intent explicit before an AI agent acts, covering task definition, recipient/scope constraints, content exclusions, and uncertainty-handling instructions.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm about to have an AI agent send an email or message on my behalf and I want to make sure it doesn't say the wrong thing to the wrong people", "My prompt uses vague scope words like 'the team,' 'clean this up,' or 'summarize the key points' and I need to lock down what those actually mean", "I'm setting up a recurring AI workflow that touches external stakeholders and I need to encode social context the model can't infer", "The cost of the AI misinterpreting my request is high \u2014 a correction after the fact would be embarrassing or damaging", "I want the AI to show me its interpretation of my task before it executes, not just start doing things"], "principles": ["Intent cannot be fully encoded in a natural-language request alone; it must be surfaced as a visible interpretation", "The four fields (Task, Who, What, If uncertain) map to the four most common gaps between what users say and what they mean", "The AI should generate this doc back to the user before acting, not require the user to write it from scratch every time", "Seeing the interpretation for five seconds before execution is enough to catch most misreads", "The doc's purpose is not documentation \u2014 it is a pre-execution checkpoint that makes the AI's interpretation visible and correctable"], "steps": ["Task: State the specific action to be taken in one sentence", "Who: Define exactly which people or systems are in scope, and explicitly name exclusions or special cases", "What: Specify what content or data is included, and what must be excluded or held back", "If uncertain: Instruct the agent on how to handle ambiguity \u2014 flag it, ask, or default to the safer interpretation rather than proceeding silently"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "183163045", "title": "Why capable AI is a liability when intent is off + my template that makes interpretation visible before action", "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z"}], "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z", "id": "intent-doc", "core_question": "How do I make sure the AI is about to do what I actually mean, not just what I literally said?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The user says 'Send a summary of the project status to the team.' Before acting, the interpretation surfaces: Task \u2014 compile and send a one-paragraph status update via Slack. Who \u2014 the five-person engineering pod, excluding the client stakeholders who are CC'd on formal updates. What \u2014 current sprint progress and blockers only, not the budget variance flagged yesterday. If uncertain \u2014 flag any item where status is ambiguous rather than marking it 'on track' by default. Seeing this for five seconds reveals the real gap: 'the team' almost certainly should have included the designer who joined last week, and 'status' without the budget note would have blindsided the PM who asked for it.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "delegation-spec-drill", "the-five-specification-primitives", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "done-definition-pattern", "constraint-architecture", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "speed-of-control", "agent-context-failure-catalog"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agent Audit Trail Standard", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of seven dimensions that a complete AI agent audit log must capture to enable reconstruction of what happened and why after an unintended action.", "when_to_apply": ["An AI agent took an action we didn't expect and we can't figure out why from the existing logs", "We're designing the logging infrastructure for a new autonomous agent system and need to know what to capture beyond actions and timestamps", "Our compliance team is asking whether we can demonstrate accountability for AI-initiated actions and I'm not sure our audit trail is sufficient", "I need to distinguish whether an agent failure was a bad instruction, a misinterpretation, or a guardrail gap \u2014 and our current logs can't tell me which", "We're adding human-in-the-loop approval gates and need to make sure the approval decisions are traceable after the fact"], "principles": ["Logging the action alone is insufficient; the interpretation the agent used must be logged alongside the action", "Exclusions and guardrails that fired are as important to log as the actions that executed", "Uncertainty and how it was resolved must be captured to distinguish intentional decisions from silent guesses", "Human approval (who approved and when) must be traceable for gated actions", "A good log answers 'what did the system think I meant?' not just 'what did the system do?'"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183163045", "title": "Why capable AI is a liability when intent is off + my template that makes interpretation visible before action", "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z"}], "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z", "id": "agent-audit-trail-standard", "core_question": "If my AI agent does something wrong tomorrow, will I actually be able to reconstruct what it thought it was doing and why \u2014 or will I just see what it did?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "This agent's logs score 3 out of 7 dimensions \u2014 they capture the action taken and the timestamp, plus a basic input log. But three critical dimensions are missing: the agent's interpreted intent (what it believed the user wanted), the guardrails it evaluated and passed through, and the uncertainty resolution path (it silently defaulted to a 'most likely' interpretation without logging alternatives it considered). The gap means that when the agent sent a production deployment email to the wrong distribution list, the investigation dead-ended at 'it sent the email' with no way to determine whether the intent spec was ambiguous, a guardrail failed to fire, or the agent made a confident wrong guess. The fix isn't better monitoring \u2014 it's logging the interpretation layer before execution.", "related_frameworks": ["four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "blast-radius-assessment", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "speed-of-control", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "intent-doc", "intent-engineering", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "the-reversibility-gap", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "constraint-architecture", "emergent-safety-dynamics-systems-not-components", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agentic System Gut Check", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "Three diagnostic questions that reveal whether an AI agent deployment has adequate intent-alignment safeguards or is operating on luck.", "when_to_apply": ["We're about to give our AI agent access to production systems and I need to know if we have enough guardrails", "An AI agent did something unexpected and we're trying to figure out whether our safeguards are real or theatrical", "A vendor is pitching an agentic tool that takes actions on our behalf and I need to quickly assess its risk posture", "We're scaling an agent's permissions from read-only to read-write and need to know what's missing before we flip the switch", "My team says the agent workflow is 'safe enough' but nobody can point to where the checkpoints actually are"], "principles": ["A system that cannot show its interpretation before acting is operating on guesswork", "If you cannot identify where the gates are, you are relying on luck rather than architecture", "If you cannot reconstruct what happened after a failure, you are debugging blind", "All three questions must be answerable with 'yes' for a system to be considered adequately safeguarded"], "steps": ["Can your agents show you what they think you mean (their interpretation of the objective) before executing?", "Where are your gates? For each high-risk action: is there a checkpoint that surfaces the interpretation before execution?", "If something went wrong tomorrow, would you have receipts? Can you reconstruct what the AI thought you meant, what it excluded, and what it flagged as uncertain?"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "183163045", "title": "Why capable AI is a liability when intent is off + my template that makes interpretation visible before action", "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z"}], "date": "2026-01-02T14:01:52.647Z", "id": "agentic-system-gut-check", "core_question": "Is my AI agent system actually safe, or am I just assuming it is because nothing has gone wrong yet?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The team's customer service agent can issue refunds, modify accounts, and escalate tickets autonomously. Applying the three questions: Can it show its interpretation before acting? No \u2014 it reads the ticket and executes immediately. Where are the gates? There are none for refunds under $50, and the threshold was chosen arbitrarily, not by risk analysis. Could we reconstruct what happened after a failure? Partially \u2014 we log actions but not the agent's interpretation of the request or what alternatives it considered. Verdict: this system is passing on one of three checks at best. It's operating on luck, not architecture. The immediate fix is inserting an interpretation-surfacing step before any account modification and logging the agent's parsed intent alongside every action.", "related_frameworks": ["agent-deployment-safety-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "intent-doc", "blast-radius-assessment", "speed-of-control", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "intent-engineering", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "constraint-architecture", "the-reversibility-gap"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Front-End Composability Four Capabilities", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of the four distinct engineering capabilities required to maintain UI consistency when non-engineers and AI agents can generate interfaces.", "when_to_apply": ["Our design system is mature but UI inconsistency is getting worse, not better, as more people ship interfaces", "We're about to let AI agents generate or modify product UI and need to know what guardrails the engineering org must build first", "I'm restructuring front-end roles and need to define what 'composability engineer' owns versus a traditional UI developer", "PMs and designers are using no-code tools to build product surfaces and the results are diverging from our design language", "We need to audit whether our front-end org has the right capabilities for a world where UI generation is distributed"], "principles": ["Good component primitives are necessary but not sufficient \u2014 you also need explicit contracts about allowable combinations", "UI contracts exist whether you define them or not; undefined contracts are written by accident through accumulated expedience", "Brand consistency at scale requires testing the generation system, not manually QA-ing every permutation", "Agent auditability is a distinct capability from standard session replay \u2014 it must track access level, composed view, and actions taken", "Generation guardrails require explicit decisions about which surfaces are locked (critical paths) and which allow AI generation (internal tools, reporting)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182813793", "title": "The Composability Era: What Happens When Anyone Can Build a UI (+ 4 Prompts to navigate the front-end revolution)", "date": "2025-12-31T14:01:35.586Z"}], "date": "2025-12-31T14:01:35.586Z", "id": "front-end-composability-four-capabilities", "core_question": "We have great components, but now that PMs, designers, and AI agents are all generating UI, how do we keep the product from fragmenting into visual chaos?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The team has a solid design system with well-documented primitives, yet the dashboard pages built by the growth team look nothing like the core product \u2014 different spacing, inconsistent card layouts, misused modal patterns. The gap isn't in the components themselves; it's that no composition contracts exist defining allowable combinations. The component library covers Capability 1 (primitives) but Capability 2 (contracts) is completely absent \u2014 there are no rules about which components can nest inside which, or which page-level patterns are canonical. Meanwhile, the AI agent building internal reporting views has no guardrails (Capability 3 missing) and no audit trail showing what it composed or why (Capability 4 missing). The front-end team needs to stop treating 'build more components' as the answer and instead invest in explicit composition rules, generation boundary policies for locked vs. open surfaces, and agent-specific audit logging.", "related_frameworks": ["builder-engineer-composability-diagnostic", "the-front-end-fork", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "constraint-architecture", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "blast-radius-assessment", "done-definition-pattern"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Builder-Engineer Composability Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A set of structured questions for builders and engineers to assess their readiness for the composability era \u2014 revealing gaps in prototyping capability for builders and systemic composability gaps for engineers.", "when_to_apply": ["Our designers and PMs keep filing tickets for things they could probably prototype themselves if they had the right skills \u2014 are they actually blocked or just not trying?", "We're about to let AI generate parts of our front end and I need to know if our architecture can handle that safely", "Our engineering team is 100% implementation and 0% platform/composability \u2014 is that a problem?", "I want to understand whether our product team and engineering team are actually ready to collaborate differently in a world of AI-assisted UI generation", "We're restructuring our front-end team and need to figure out who should be building what"], "principles": ["Builders should be able to show a working prototype, not just describe a feature \u2014 inability to do so signals a collaboration gap", "Engineers should be able to articulate in one page what can vary, what cannot, and where AI can and cannot touch the UI", "A composability ratio of all implementation engineers and zero composability engineers signals staffing for a prior era", "The correct answer to 'what can your system generate safely' is specific and bounded \u2014 not 'nothing' or 'everything'"], "steps": ["For builders: Ask when you last built something (not specced or described it \u2014 actually built it)", "For builders: Ask whether you could show your next feature idea rather than describe it", "For builders: Ask whether you understand why your last three engineering requests were scoped the way they were", "For engineers: Count the ratio of implementation engineers to composability engineers", "For engineers: Ask what your system can generate safely and whether the answer is specific", "For engineers: Ask whether you can articulate your UI generation constraints \u2014 what can vary, what can't, where AI can touch \u2014 in one page"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182813793", "title": "The Composability Era: What Happens When Anyone Can Build a UI (+ 4 Prompts to navigate the front-end revolution)", "date": "2025-12-31T14:01:35.586Z"}], "date": "2025-12-31T14:01:35.586Z", "id": "builder-engineer-composability-diagnostic", "core_question": "Are our builders actually capable of building, and are our engineers actually architecting for a world where non-engineers can safely compose UI?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "The product team has five builders and twelve engineers, but zero engineers focused on composability infrastructure \u2014 the entire engineering team is writing implementation code. When asked 'what can your system generate safely?', the tech lead says 'we'd have to think about that.' Meanwhile, the lead PM last built something herself eight months ago and can't explain why her last three engineering requests were scoped the way they were. This team is staffed and skilled for a pre-composability era: builders who can only describe, engineers who can only implement, and no one owning the surface area where AI or non-engineers could safely generate UI.", "related_frameworks": ["front-end-composability-four-capabilities", "the-front-end-fork", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "coordination-tax-audit", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "constraint-architecture", "the-weekend-hack-test", "community-ai-collaborative-building-model", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "ai-leverage-audit"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Front-End Fork", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A two-path classification for front-end engineers choosing their career trajectory as AI compresses implementation work \u2014 composability work versus the handcrafted specialist lane.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm a front-end developer and AI tools are handling more and more of what I used to do \u2014 where should I invest my time?", "We're restructuring our front-end team and need to figure out which roles are about building design systems versus which are about pixel-level craft", "I love the craft of building beautiful UI, but I'm worried it's becoming a niche \u2014 should I pivot or lean in?", "As a manager, how do I create career ladders for front-end engineers when AI is compressing implementation work?", "I'm a senior front-end engineer and I can't tell if my skills are becoming more valuable or less \u2014 the signals are contradictory"], "principles": ["Path 1 (Composability): Accept less direct making in exchange for more leverage over how making happens at scale", "Path 2 (Handcrafted): Remain in implementation craft, which is becoming a specialist niche rather than the default path \u2014 choose it deliberately", "Existing craft knowledge is essential context for composability work, not obsolete \u2014 knowing which decisions need locking requires having seen what happens when they aren't", "Choosing neither path deliberately means drifting into obsolescence by default"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182813793", "title": "The Composability Era: What Happens When Anyone Can Build a UI (+ 4 Prompts to navigate the front-end revolution)", "date": "2025-12-31T14:01:35.586Z"}], "date": "2025-12-31T14:01:35.586Z", "id": "the-front-end-fork", "core_question": "As AI makes routine UI implementation trivially easy, should I double down on craft mastery or shift toward designing the systems and constraints that govern how UIs get composed at scale?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "This engineer has spent eight years perfecting CSS animations and accessible component APIs \u2014 that's Path 2, handcrafted specialist work. The critical question is whether they're choosing it deliberately. Right now their team is adopting AI-generated components for 70% of new surfaces, which means their craft is migrating from 'default expectation' to 'specialist niche.' If they stay, they need to own the quality ceiling \u2014 the work AI can't reach \u2014 and price accordingly. If they'd rather have leverage over the whole system, they shift to Path 1: building the design tokens, component constraints, and composability guardrails that make AI-generated UI consistently good. Their existing craft knowledge isn't wasted in that move \u2014 it's the exact context needed to know which decisions must be locked down versus left flexible. What they cannot do is stand still and assume the default path still exists.", "related_frameworks": ["front-end-composability-four-capabilities", "builder-engineer-composability-diagnostic", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "three-developer-tracks", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "ai-career-board", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "double-compression-loop", "experiential-debt", "engineering-manager-identity-shift"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Delegation Spec Drill", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A weekly practice for building the skill of translating implicit work intentions into explicit, executable specifications that can be delegated to an agent or human.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep getting back work from my AI agent that misses the point, and I suspect the problem is my instructions, not the model", "I want to start delegating recurring tasks but I'm not sure which ones I can actually hand off versus which ones live in my head", "I wrote a prompt or brief for someone and it generated more clarifying questions than actual output", "I'm trying to build a weekly habit of getting better at specification writing for AI-assisted work", "A task I delegated created more cleanup work than if I'd just done it myself"], "principles": ["A spec is only good if a stranger can execute it without questions", "Success criteria must be binary, not subjective", "Constraints must be explicit rather than implied", "Approvals must be placed at reversibility boundaries \u2014 moments where actions become hard to undo", "Failure handling must be specified for each step", "Score 3 or below means the spec would create more work than it saves; 4 or 5 means it's delegation-ready"], "steps": ["Pick one task from the past week that took more than 30 minutes", "Write the specification you would have needed to delegate it to someone who knows nothing about your context", "Define Inputs: What information or access is required to start?", "Define Steps: What sequence of actions produces the output?", "Define Constraints: What must be true about the output, and what must not happen?", "Define Approvals: Where would you need to sign off? Place these at reversibility boundaries.", "Define Failure Handling: What happens if something goes wrong at each step?", "Score the spec on five points: (1) Can a stranger execute it without questions? (2) Are success criteria binary? (3) Are constraints explicit rather than implied? (4) Are approvals at reversibility boundaries? (5) Is failure handling specified?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182658516", "title": "Grab the Delegation Kit I Use to Turn AI into my Chief of Staff + the 8 Prompts That Turn Ramblings into Executable Tasks", "date": "2025-12-30T14:01:32.112Z"}], "date": "2025-12-30T14:01:32.112Z", "id": "delegation-spec-drill", "core_question": "Can I actually write down what I want done clearly enough that someone \u2014 or something \u2014 could execute it without reading my mind?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The task was 'research competitors and summarize findings' \u2014 took 90 minutes last Tuesday. The draft spec scored 2/5: steps were vague ('look at their websites'), success criteria were subjective ('a good summary'), constraints were entirely implied (didn't specify which competitors, what dimensions to compare, or output format), and failure handling was nonexistent. The spec would have generated more back-and-forth than it saved. Rewriting with binary success criteria ('table comparing 5 named competitors on pricing, integrations, and target segment, with source URLs for each cell') and explicit constraints ('no competitors under $1M ARR, no blog posts as sources') brought it to 4/5 \u2014 delegation-ready for an agent or junior analyst.", "related_frameworks": ["the-five-specification-primitives", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "weekly-delegation-audit", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "intent-engineering", "done-definition-pattern", "definition-of-done-rubric", "delegation-prompt-management-system", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-reversibility-gap", "jiorp-structure", "correctness-definition-framework", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "task-decomposition-framework", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The 30-Minute Delegation Rule", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-condition filter for determining whether a task is worth delegating at all, based on recurrence, duration, and batchability.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep delegating small tasks to my AI assistant and spending more time reviewing the output than it would take to just do it", "I'm building a list of tasks to automate or delegate and need to filter out the ones that aren't worth the setup cost", "A task feels like it should be delegated but it's a one-time thing and I'm not sure the overhead is justified", "I want a quick litmus test before writing a detailed delegation spec or prompt for an AI agent", "My weekly review keeps surfacing tasks that could theoretically be delegated but I'm not sure which ones actually should be"], "principles": ["If you're doing a task once, don't delegate it \u2014 just do it", "Delegation only pays off when the task recurs (instructions will be reused), takes longer than the delegation overhead, or can be batched with similar items", "The goal is not to delegate everything, but to delegate the right things \u2014 systematic, repeatable, verifiable work that doesn't require your judgment"], "steps": ["Ask: Does this task recur? If no, do it yourself.", "Ask: Does this task take longer than the delegation overhead (specification + review + correction)? If no, do it yourself.", "Ask: Can this task be batched with similar items? If none of the three conditions are met, do it yourself.", "If one or more conditions are met, proceed to write a delegation spec."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182658516", "title": "Grab the Delegation Kit I Use to Turn AI into my Chief of Staff + the 8 Prompts That Turn Ramblings into Executable Tasks", "date": "2025-12-30T14:01:32.112Z"}], "date": "2025-12-30T14:01:32.112Z", "id": "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "core_question": "Should I spend the effort to delegate this task, or will the overhead of delegation actually cost more than just doing it myself?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "Formatting a one-off investor report fails all three conditions: it won't recur (this is a custom request), it takes 20 minutes (less than writing the spec, reviewing, and correcting), and there's nothing to batch it with. Do it yourself. But the weekly metrics summary passes two conditions \u2014 it recurs every Monday and can be batched with the other three weekly reporting tasks. That's worth a delegation spec.", "related_frameworks": ["delegation-spec-drill", "weekly-delegation-audit", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "task-decomposition-framework", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "delegation-prompt-management-system"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Weekly Delegation Audit", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured weekly review that identifies which parts of priority work require human judgment versus which parts are candidates for delegation.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm planning my week and want to figure out what I should actually delegate to AI versus do myself", "My week feels packed but I suspect most of the work is systematic stuff I'm doing out of habit", "I've started using AI tools but I'm still delegating ad hoc \u2014 I need a repeatable weekly process", "I can't articulate why I personally need to do certain tasks versus which parts are just execution", "I want to transition from doing everything myself to consistently identifying what AI can handle each week"], "principles": ["Start from outcomes, not task lists \u2014 identify the load-bearing work that would make the week a failure if undone", "Every priority has components; not all components require the same type of cognitive effort", "Work requiring your taste, relationships, or strategic position stays with you; systematic, rule-based, or pattern-matching work is a delegation candidate", "The audit is a thinking exercise, not just a task-sorting tool \u2014 it surfaces implicit decisions you've never had to articulate"], "steps": ["Identify the 3-4 things you need to accomplish this week that really matter \u2014 not the full task list, just the load-bearing priorities", "For each priority, break it into its component parts (e.g., a negotiation = research + drafting + timeline management + the actual conversation)", "For each component, ask: Does this require human judgment (your taste, relationships, strategic position), or is it systematic, rule-based, or pattern-matching?", "Mark judgment-required components as 'keep'; mark systematic components as 'delegation candidates'", "Write delegation specs for the candidates using the Delegation Spec Drill"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182658516", "title": "Grab the Delegation Kit I Use to Turn AI into my Chief of Staff + the 8 Prompts That Turn Ramblings into Executable Tasks", "date": "2025-12-30T14:01:32.112Z"}], "date": "2025-12-30T14:01:32.112Z", "id": "weekly-delegation-audit", "core_question": "Which parts of my highest-stakes work this week actually need me, and which parts am I doing out of habit when they could be handed off?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This week's load-bearing priorities are: close the partnership deal, ship the product brief, and prep the board update. The partnership deal breaks into research on their recent financials (systematic \u2014 delegation candidate), drafting the term sheet (pattern-matching on prior deals \u2014 delegation candidate), relationship mapping of their decision-makers (requires my network knowledge \u2014 keep), and the actual negotiation call (pure judgment \u2014 keep). Two of four components can be delegated, which means I was about to spend 60% of my week on work that doesn't require me. The delegation specs for the research and term sheet draft go to the Delegation Spec Drill next.", "related_frameworks": ["delegation-spec-drill", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "task-decomposition-framework", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework", "work-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The 50%/80% Gap Operating Framework", "type": "distinction", "description": "A framework for understanding the difference between AI capability (what's possible at 50% success) and AI dependability (what's reliable at 80% success), and how to operate in the space between them.", "when_to_apply": ["AI generates output that looks right but I keep finding subtle errors downstream", "I'm trying to figure out the right chunk size for AI-delegated work \u2014 how much to hand off before reviewing", "My team trusts AI output because it passes surface-level checks, but we've had quality incidents from undetected errors", "AI benchmarks say 90% accuracy but my real-world experience feels closer to 60% \u2014 how do I plan around that?", "As AI gets better, I'm tempted to reduce oversight, but the stakes of each delegation keep going up"], "principles": ["Distinguish between the 50% horizon (what AI can attempt) and the 80% horizon (what AI can dependably deliver) \u2014 these are very different thresholds with very different implications", "The dangerous failure mode is output that looks correct but contains subtle errors, not obvious failures", "Domain expertise is the primary control surface: you can only catch errors you know how to recognize", "Operate by delegating tasks long enough to matter for leverage but short enough to verify before errors compound", "As AI reliability improves, the stakes per delegation increase, making verification skills more valuable, not less"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182657487", "title": "My honest field notes on the super-exponential + why \"I'll catch up later\" is now the riskiest bet", "date": "2025-12-29T14:03:11.843Z"}], "date": "2025-12-29T14:03:11.843Z", "id": "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "core_question": "AI can do this task impressively half the time, but can I actually trust it to do it reliably enough that I don't need to check everything \u2014 and if not, how do I work in that gap?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "This team's AI code generation sits squarely in the gap: it can produce working API endpoints about 50% of the time, but only about 30% of those survive integration testing without subtle bugs in error handling or edge cases. The dangerous part isn't the code that fails to compile \u2014 that's obvious. It's the endpoint that passes unit tests but silently drops authentication checks on one path. The operating move is to delegate at the function level, not the service level \u2014 short enough that a senior engineer can verify each piece before errors compound across the system. The domain expertise to catch auth-path omissions is the actual bottleneck, not the code generation speed.", "related_frameworks": ["verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "speed-of-control", "verification-capability-triage", "ambient-failure-cascade", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Delegation Scope Expansion Model", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that maps how the nature of delegatable work fundamentally changes at each order-of-magnitude expansion of AI time-horizon capability (tasks \u2192 projects \u2192 initiatives \u2192 judgment).", "when_to_apply": ["I'm trying to figure out which parts of a large project I can delegate to AI versus which I need to do myself", "My team is jumping straight to complex agent workflows without mastering simpler AI delegation first", "I need to plan what AI delegation skills to invest in now versus what I can learn later as capabilities improve", "I delegated a multi-phase project to an AI agent and it fell apart \u2014 I'm trying to understand why the same approach that works for simple tasks failed here", "I'm designing oversight and review processes for AI-generated work and need to calibrate how much supervision different types of work require"], "principles": ["A five-hour delegatable task is not a scaled-down forty-hour task \u2014 each horizon represents a qualitatively different kind of work", "Each horizon expansion opens entirely new categories of work, not just more of the same", "Skills to delegate effectively at each level build on prior levels \u2014 practitioners who skip levels start from scratch", "The distinction between delegating work and delegating judgment starts to blur at the forty-hour horizon"], "steps": ["Five-hour horizon: delegate discrete tasks with clear boundaries, single-session work, verifiable against explicit success criteria", "Ten-hour horizon: delegate projects requiring planning, multiple phases, and synthesis \u2014 agent must make sequencing decisions and maintain coherence", "Twenty-hour horizon: delegate initiatives spanning multiple work sessions \u2014 requires agent judgment about prioritization, not just execution", "Forty-hour horizon: delegate judgment-laden work (business plans, audits, book-length drafts) where dependencies and decision points blur the line between work and judgment"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182657487", "title": "My honest field notes on the super-exponential + why \"I'll catch up later\" is now the riskiest bet", "date": "2025-12-29T14:03:11.843Z"}], "date": "2025-12-29T14:03:11.843Z", "id": "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "core_question": "What kind of work can I actually hand off to AI right now, and how does that fundamentally change as these systems get capable of working for longer stretches?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A team lead wants to delegate a competitive analysis. At the five-hour horizon, the AI can research a single competitor and produce a structured summary \u2014 discrete, verifiable, bounded. At the twenty-hour horizon, the AI could run the full competitive landscape scan across twelve players, deciding which dimensions matter most and how to weight conflicting signals \u2014 that's an initiative requiring prioritization judgment, not just execution. The team lead who hasn't built the skill of specifying and verifying five-hour tasks will fail catastrophically at the twenty-hour delegation because the specification and oversight muscles are qualitatively different, not just scaled up.", "related_frameworks": ["the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "weekly-delegation-audit", "delegation-spec-drill", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "intent-engineering", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "task-decomposition-framework", "capability-surface-mapping", "experiential-debt", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Reversibility Gap", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI agent deployment failures as a reversibility problem rather than an intelligence problem \u2014 agents fail in production not because they aren't smart enough, but because most business environments weren't designed to make mistakes survivable.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent works great in demos but keeps causing problems in production that require manual cleanup", "We're debating whether to wait for a better model before deploying agents, or ship now with the current one", "I need to figure out why agents work well in software engineering but keep failing in our operations/finance/HR workflows", "We're planning what infrastructure to build before giving our agents more autonomy", "Leadership is asking why our agent made an irreversible mistake and I need to explain what went wrong structurally"], "principles": ["The limiting factor for agent deployment is reversibility of mistakes, not agent intelligence", "Environments designed for safe failure (like software engineering) enable agent autonomy; environments that weren't designed that way resist it", "The path to agent autonomy runs through reversibility infrastructure, not model capability improvements", "Humans have been providing an informal safety throttle that disappears when agents replace them \u2014 this must be explicitly replaced", "Boring, predictable, bounded, recoverable agent operations are the goal, not sophisticated ones"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182653562", "title": "Executive Briefing: Five Primitives That Make Agent Operations Safe", "date": "2025-12-28T16:02:05.060Z"}], "date": "2025-12-28T16:02:05.060Z", "id": "the-reversibility-gap", "core_question": "Why does my AI agent keep failing in production when it works fine in testing \u2014 is the model not good enough, or is the environment not safe enough for mistakes?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The conventional read is that the customer service agent fails because it hallucinates refund policies \u2014 it needs a better model. That's wrong here. The real problem is that when the agent issues a refund incorrectly, there's no automated clawback, no staged approval, no transaction hold. A human agent making the same mistake would have caught it mid-process or a supervisor would have reversed it within the hour \u2014 that informal safety net was never formalized. The fix isn't GPT-5; it's a 15-minute refund hold with automatic review triggers. The environment needs reversibility infrastructure, not more intelligence.", "related_frameworks": ["primitives-for-reversibility", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "speed-of-control", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "the-two-door-principle", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "constraint-architecture", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "ambient-failure-cascade", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Primitives for Reversibility", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A framework of specific mechanisms that must exist before AI agents can safely operate in a business domain, analogous to how software engineering built undo infrastructure over decades.", "when_to_apply": ["We want to give an AI agent the ability to take real actions in finance, HR, or operations \u2014 not just generate recommendations", "Our engineering team is comfortable with AI agents in CI/CD but leadership wants to expand agent autonomy to other departments", "An AI agent made an irreversible mistake and we need to figure out what infrastructure was missing", "We're comparing which business processes should get agent automation first and need a safety-readiness checklist", "The team keeps saying 'we can always fix it manually' as the rollback plan for agent errors"], "principles": ["Safe agent operation requires explicit reversibility infrastructure, not just capable models", "Software engineering's decades of undo culture (version control, staging, rollback) is a template for what other domains need to build", "Reversibility must be designed in advance, not retrofitted after mistakes occur"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 'five primitives' in the title and 'specific mechanisms' in the body, but the actual list of primitives/mechanisms is not included in the provided excerpt \u2014 only the framework's existence and motivation are described.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182653562", "title": "Executive Briefing: Five Primitives That Make Agent Operations Safe", "date": "2025-12-28T16:02:05.060Z"}], "date": "2025-12-28T16:02:05.060Z", "id": "primitives-for-reversibility", "core_question": "Does the business process I want to automate with AI agents actually have the infrastructure to undo mistakes, or am I assuming reversibility that doesn't exist?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The accounts payable workflow scores poorly on reversibility primitives. There's no staging environment equivalent \u2014 payments execute immediately with no sandbox. There's no version control analog \u2014 once an invoice is approved and payment issued, the only 'undo' is a manual clawback process that takes 3-5 business days and requires vendor cooperation. There's no rollback mechanism \u2014 a batch of 200 incorrect payments can't be atomically reversed. Compare this to the engineering deployment pipeline, which has all three primitives mature. The AP workflow needs at minimum a payment hold buffer (staging), an approval snapshot log (version control), and a batch reversal API (rollback) before any agent gets write access.", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "the-reversibility-gap", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "the-two-door-principle", "speed-of-control", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 'five primitives' in the title and 'specific mechanisms' in the body, but the actual list of primitives/mechanisms is not included in the provided excerpt \u2014 only the framework's existence and motivation are described.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Jagged Frontier Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system for mapping AI capability peaks and valleys across tasks, then routing work to match AI's structural advantages while humans cover failure modes.", "when_to_apply": ["AI works great for some tasks in my workflow but produces garbage for seemingly similar ones, and I can't figure out the pattern", "We're getting inconsistent results from an AI deployment and need to figure out which pieces to keep vs. pull back to humans", "Some people on my team get massive productivity gains from AI while others doing similar work see almost nothing \u2014 what explains the gap?", "I need to decide which parts of a process to automate with AI vs. keep human-led, and 'try it and see' isn't a strategy", "Our AI pilot showed impressive demos but real-world performance is disappointing and we need to diagnose why"], "principles": ["AI capability surfaces are jagged, not smooth \u2014 peaks and valleys coexist on adjacent tasks", "Misuse outside the frontier doesn't just fail to help \u2014 it actively degrades outcomes", "The 30% gains crowd treats AI as a power tool (more output, same shape of work); the 300% gains crowd treats AI as a routing layer (redesigned workflows matching AI's structural advantages)", "Fluency means navigating jaggedness intentionally, not just generating more AI output", "Each deployment surface has its own failure modes \u2014 a failure in one context doesn't predict failure in another", "Map tasks into three zones: AI reliably better, AI reliably worse, and high variance"], "steps": ["Inventory your tasks and map each into one of three zones: AI reliably better, AI reliably worse, high variance", "Identify which tasks sit inside vs. outside the AI frontier for your specific context", "Redesign workflows so AI handles tasks in the 'reliably better' zone and humans cover edge cases and failure modes", "Build evaluation harnesses and 'known failure mode' checklists per workflow to catch regressions", "Continuously update the map as capability thresholds shift"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182448283", "title": "NEW: One Google Update Spawned 3 Startup Generations in 30 Days\u2014Here's the Pattern + 4 Prompts to Position Before the Next Cascade", "date": "2025-12-26T14:01:26.389Z"}], "date": "2025-12-26T14:01:26.389Z", "id": "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "core_question": "Which of my tasks should I actually hand to AI, and which ones will it silently butcher if I'm not careful about the boundary?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A legal team getting 'mixed results' from AI contract review has been treating all contract tasks as one block. Mapping the jagged frontier reveals: extracting standard clause language and flagging missing sections sit inside the frontier (AI reliably better \u2014 faster, more consistent). But evaluating whether a non-standard indemnification clause is commercially acceptable in this deal's context sits firmly outside it (AI reliably worse \u2014 it generates confident but wrong risk assessments). The team redesigned: AI handles extraction and completeness checks, humans handle commercial judgment calls, and the high-variance zone \u2014 summarizing negotiation history \u2014 gets AI drafts with mandatory human review. That routing shift moved them from marginal gains to transformative ones, not by using AI more, but by using it only where the frontier map says it wins.", "related_frameworks": ["jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "capability-surface-mapping", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "edge-first-automation-framework", "verification-frontier", "task-decomposition-framework", "ai-leverage-audit", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Cascade Scanner / Gap-Close Pattern", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured approach to identifying which AI capability gaps are about to close and predicting the downstream business cascade that will follow, by analyzing what building blocks already exist versus what single unlock is missing.", "when_to_apply": ["A new foundation model just dropped and I'm trying to figure out if it's incremental or if it crosses a threshold that unlocks entire product categories", "I can see that 90% of the building blocks for a capability exist but deployment is still stuck \u2014 what's the actual blocker and when does it break?", "I want to place bets on where to build before the next wave of AI startups becomes obvious to everyone", "I'm scanning adjacent AI capability areas to decide which gap-close would create the most downstream opportunity for my company", "Multiple AI capabilities seem close to usable but none have broken through to mass adoption yet \u2014 which one tips first?"], "principles": ["You don't need to improve the entire AI capability surface \u2014 you only need to fill in the right gap", "Look for areas where almost all building blocks exist but one specific component is missing", "A gap closes faster when distribution infrastructure is already in place (pipes already laid)", "The question for each area is: what exists now, what's missing, and what would a real breakthrough look like?", "When a capability crosses the usability threshold plus hits broad distribution simultaneously, the cascade happens in weeks not years", "Three lineages tend to light up at once when a shared missing piece is filled: consumer creation, knowledge-work collateral, and professional tooling"], "steps": ["Identify a capability area where progress is happening but deployment is still blocked", "Enumerate what building blocks already exist (models, infrastructure, distribution surfaces)", "Identify the single specific gap that is acting as the hard blocker", "Assess whether distribution infrastructure is already in place to propagate a breakthrough", "Predict the downstream cascade: which product categories unlock the moment the gap closes", "Position or build before the threshold is crossed, not after the cascade is visible"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182448283", "title": "NEW: One Google Update Spawned 3 Startup Generations in 30 Days\u2014Here's the Pattern + 4 Prompts to Position Before the Next Cascade", "date": "2025-12-26T14:01:26.389Z"}], "date": "2025-12-26T14:01:26.389Z", "id": "cascade-scanner-gap-close-pattern", "core_question": "Which AI capability gap is about to close, and what cascade of new products and markets will it trigger when it does?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "Real-time video understanding already has strong models (GPT-4V, Gemini), cloud infrastructure, and billions of camera-equipped devices as distribution surfaces. The single missing piece is sub-second multimodal inference at consumer-grade cost. The moment that threshold is crossed \u2014 likely within one model generation \u2014 three lineages ignite simultaneously: consumer apps (real-time AR translation, cooking coaches), knowledge-work collateral (auto-generated meeting summaries with visual context), and professional tooling (quality inspection, surgical assist). The distribution pipes are already laid through existing mobile OS integrations, so adoption will cascade in weeks. Building the harness layer now \u2014 before the unlock is visible \u2014 is the only way to capture position.", "related_frameworks": ["capability-surface-mapping", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-moat-audit", "the-weekend-hack-test", "four-durable-positions-framework", "the-three-layer-analysis", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Model + Y + X Framework (The Harnessing Layer)", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the conventional wisdom that AI competitive advantage = Model + Domain Expertise, by inserting a critical middle variable 'Y' (the harnessing layer of system design, validation, and constraint enforcement) that determines whether domain expertise actually gets leveraged.", "when_to_apply": ["We have better domain experts than our competitors but our AI product still underperforms theirs", "Our AI demos are impressive but we can't get the system to work reliably at scale in production", "We're debating whether to invest in model fine-tuning vs. building validation and error-handling infrastructure", "Leadership keeps saying 'we have proprietary data' as our moat but our actual outputs aren't better than competitors using the same base model", "Our team has deep expertise but can't figure out why the AI system doesn't reflect that knowledge in its outputs"], "principles": ["Domain expertise (X) alone is insufficient\u2014it stays theoretical without a harnessing layer", "Y (the harnessing layer) is the multiplier that unlocks X", "The harnessing layer includes validation logic, routing, constraint enforcement, repair steps, and failure instrumentation", "Teams that ship are the ones who built Y, not the ones with the best models or most resources", "The messy middle integration layer is underbuilt and represents the primary opportunity"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182475814", "title": "9 bets I'm making on AI in 2026 + the audit I use to spot real systems", "date": "2025-12-24T15:03:02.130Z"}], "date": "2025-12-24T15:03:02.130Z", "id": "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "core_question": "We have the model access and the domain expertise \u2014 so why isn't our AI product working in production?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that Company A wins because they have deeper clinical expertise and fine-tuned models. That's wrong here \u2014 Company B ships reliable results with a generic model because they built the harnessing layer: validation logic that catches hallucinated drug interactions, routing that escalates ambiguous cases, constraint enforcement that blocks outputs violating formulary rules, and repair steps that auto-correct common failure patterns. Company A's domain expertise is theoretical \u2014 it lives in their team's heads, not in executable system logic. The multiplier is zero without Y, so Model \u00d7 0 \u00d7 X = 0 no matter how good X is.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "harness-decision-audit", "five-harness-divergence-dimensions", "constraint-architecture", "capability-dissipation-gap", "four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Generate \u2192 Validate \u2192 Repair \u2192 Validate Again", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-step production loop for AI systems that enforces constraints at every transformation step rather than only at the end, ensuring outputs remain within defined boundaries throughout the pipeline.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI pipeline has hard constraints like character limits or legal disclaimers, and the model keeps breaking them unpredictably", "A tone-rewrite step in my pipeline keeps stripping fields that an earlier extraction step set correctly", "I'm moving an AI prototype into production and need every output to be auditable against a spec before it ships", "My retry logic just says 'try again' when validation fails and the model keeps making the same mistakes", "I need to design a pipeline where some parts of the output are untouchable and other parts get rewritten by the model"], "principles": ["Every transformation ends with validation\u2014no exceptions", "Validation must happen after each step, not just at the end of the pipeline", "Repair uses specific error messages and exact deltas, not generic 'try again' instructions", "Constraint enforcement lives in code, not just prompts", "Frozen regions (cannot change) and variable regions (can be rewritten) must be explicitly separated", "When validation fails, provide the model with the exact problem\u2014'you are 435 characters over, preserve the core claim, trim examples'"], "steps": ["Generate: Use the model to produce a candidate output for the variable regions only", "Validate: Run deterministic code checks (character counts, schema enforcement, frozen-region integrity, CTA presence, spam scores, etc.)", "Repair: If validation fails, retry with a specific error message containing the exact delta and explicit preservation constraints\u2014not a generic retry", "Validate Again: Re-run the same validation suite on the repaired output before passing it downstream"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182475814", "title": "9 bets I'm making on AI in 2026 + the audit I use to spot real systems", "date": "2025-12-24T15:03:02.130Z"}], "date": "2025-12-24T15:03:02.130Z", "id": "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "core_question": "How do I keep AI outputs within hard constraints when every transformation step in my pipeline can silently break what earlier steps got right?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The ad-copy pipeline generates a headline variant at 435 characters over the 90-character limit, with the legally required disclaimer stripped. The repair step doesn't say 'make it shorter' \u2014 it says 'you are 435 characters over limit (currently 525, max 90), the disclaimer in characters 12-47 is frozen and cannot change, preserve the core claim about free shipping, trim the three example use cases.' After repair, the same validation suite re-runs: character count passes, frozen disclaimer is intact byte-for-byte, spam score is 2.1 (under threshold of 3.0). Only then does the output move downstream.", "related_frameworks": ["constraint-architecture", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "the-five-specification-primitives", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "done-definition-pattern", "correctness-definition-framework", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Moat Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A twelve-question diagnostic that reveals whether an AI system has a durable harnessing layer or is relying on prompt-and-hope, organized across four dimensions: constraints and drift, logging and traceability, determinism and routing, and review and boundaries.", "when_to_apply": ["I inherited an AI pipeline from another team and need to know where the landmines are before we scale it", "We're about to ship an AI workflow to production and I want to know exactly which engineering gaps will bite us", "Our AI system works in demos but keeps producing weird outputs in production \u2014 I need to diagnose why systematically", "I need to assess whether an AI product has real engineering underneath or is just clever prompting", "We're deciding between hardening our current AI pipeline versus rebuilding it and need to know how deep the problems go"], "principles": ["Questions you cannot answer reveal your gaps", "Constraints must live in code, not just prompts", "Every transformation must have validation, not just end-to-end checks", "Frozen versus variable boundaries must be explicit and enforced", "Logging must be specific enough to trace any failure back to its exact step", "Repair logic must use error context, not blind retries"], "steps": ["Constraints and Drift: Ask where constraints live (prompt vs. code vs. both), what validates output before it ships, what happens when a requirement changes mid-workflow, and whether validation is re-applied after each transformation", "Logging and Traceability: Ask what gets logged (failures, retries, diffs, token counts, latency) and whether any failure can be traced to its specific causative step", "Determinism and Routing: Ask what is deterministic versus left to the model, how common requests versus rare complex ones are handled, and what the repair logic is when validation fails", "Review and Boundaries: Ask who reviews outputs before users see them, what is frozen versus variable, and what breaks if you change the output tone right now"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182475814", "title": "9 bets I'm making on AI in 2026 + the audit I use to spot real systems", "date": "2025-12-24T15:03:02.130Z"}], "date": "2025-12-24T15:03:02.130Z", "id": "the-moat-audit", "core_question": "Is this AI system actually engineered to hold up under real conditions, or is it a demo dressed as production infrastructure?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "Running the audit against a content generation pipeline reveals critical gaps: constraints live entirely in the system prompt with zero code-level enforcement, there's no per-step validation after intermediate transformations (only a final tone check), and the repair logic is a blind retry with the same prompt. The system logs success/failure but not diffs or token counts, so when outputs drift, there's no way to trace which transformation step introduced the error. Verdict: this pipeline will silently degrade under load or model updates \u2014 it needs code-enforced constraints, step-level validation, and error-context-aware repair before it can scale.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "constraint-architecture", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "harness-decision-audit", "five-harness-divergence-dimensions", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "correctness-definition-framework", "ambient-failure-cascade", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "agentic-system-gut-check"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "LLM Psychosis Self-Audit Framework", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "A structured catalog of three warning signs that indicate someone is in an AI-driven overconfidence loop, paired with named failure modes and corresponding corrective interventions.", "when_to_apply": ["I asked the AI to check its own work and it said everything looks good \u2014 but something feels off about that", "A colleague is producing impressive-looking work across domains they have no background in, and they're increasingly dismissive of feedback", "I notice I'm feeling like 'me and the AI have figured something out' that contradicts what domain experts are telling me", "Someone on my team is using AI confirmation as a substitute for peer review on high-stakes deliverables", "I'm about to ship an AI-assisted analysis in a domain where I honestly couldn't catch a subtle error"], "principles": ["Asking AI to 'check your work' is not verification \u2014 it is sophisticated agreement from a system optimized for approval", "The dangerous middle ground is knowing enough to trust AI but not enough to verify its outputs", "If this were subtly wrong, would I catch it? If no, human expert review is required", "Adversarial dynamics must be built into the workflow from the start, not added as a checkpoint at the end", "The feeling that 'me and the AI have figured something out others haven't' is itself a warning sign", "Drafting is commodity; auditing is scarce \u2014 the bottleneck skill is correctness verification, not output generation"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 10 specific adversarial prompts (Adversarial Mini-Check, Before You Start, Audit Boundary Check, Disconfirmation Pass, Reality Check, Confidence Calibration, Final Gate, Project Ground Rules, 2-Minute Reality Check, Full Assessment) hosted on an external Notion page, whose full content is not included in the post text.", "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182388044", "title": "Smart people get fooled by AI first \u2014 because they can rationalize anything. (Self-Audit Framework)", "date": "2025-12-23T14:02:58.910Z"}], "date": "2025-12-23T14:02:58.910Z", "id": "llm-psychosis-self-audit-framework", "core_question": "Am I actually getting better outputs with AI, or has the AI just gotten better at making me feel confident about outputs I can't verify?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The team lead's regulatory analysis is exhibiting Warning Sign #2: Epistemic Closure. They asked GPT-4 to draft a compliance framework, then asked GPT-4 to review it, then asked Claude to 'independently verify' it \u2014 and now they're dismissing the outside counsel's objections because 'three AI systems agree.' The tell is that no human domain expert has touched this, and the lead's confidence has increased with each AI confirmation loop rather than with each piece of independent evidence. The corrective is immediate: freeze the output, route it to a qualified compliance attorney, and restructure the workflow so the AI drafts and a human expert audits \u2014 never the reverse.", "related_frameworks": ["verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-capability-triage", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-diagnostic", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "experiential-debt"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 10 specific adversarial prompts (Adversarial Mini-Check, Before You Start, Audit Boundary Check, Disconfirmation Pass, Reality Check, Confidence Calibration, Final Gate, Project Ground Rules, 2-Minute Reality Check, Full Assessment) hosted on an external Notion page, whose full content is not included in the post text.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "Verification Capability Triage", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-question diagnostic that determines whether you have sufficient domain expertise to trust an AI-assisted conclusion, or whether you need external human validation.", "when_to_apply": ["I used AI to draft something in a domain I know just enough about to be dangerous \u2014 like legal language or financial modeling", "A team member's AI-assisted analysis looks polished but I'm not sure anyone on the team has deep enough expertise to validate it", "I'm about to make a high-stakes decision based on AI output and I notice I'm rationalizing my confidence rather than actually verifying", "The AI gave me a formal-looking output \u2014 code, math proof, legal clause \u2014 and the formality itself is making me trust it more than I should", "I need to decide whether this AI-generated work product needs peer review or full expert escalation before we act on it"], "principles": ["The test is binary: if this were subtly wrong, would I catch it?", "AI self-critique is just more generation, not actual verification", "Domain expertise determines the boundary between informed confidence and blind trust", "Formal tools can make output look more legitimate without making it more correct", "Verification design \u2014 knowing what checks would expose a specific failure mode \u2014 is distinct from simply reviewing output"], "steps": ["Ask: if this AI-assisted conclusion were subtly wrong, would I catch it? If yes, proceed with standard review. If no, escalate.", "Name the specific domain expertise required to evaluate this output (e.g., formal mathematics, contract law, clinical medicine).", "Identify a human with genuine expertise in that specific domain \u2014 not a smart generalist \u2014 who can audit the output.", "Run a dedicated adversarial pass: explicitly ask the AI what the three most likely ways this is wrong are, and what a skeptical domain expert would say.", "If no qualified human reviewer can be found, treat that absence as a stop condition for high-stakes decisions."], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182388044", "title": "Smart people get fooled by AI first \u2014 because they can rationalize anything. (Self-Audit Framework)", "date": "2025-12-23T14:02:58.910Z"}], "date": "2025-12-23T14:02:58.910Z", "id": "verification-capability-triage", "core_question": "If this AI output were subtly wrong, would I actually catch it \u2014 or am I just trusting it because it sounds right and I'm smart enough to rationalize it?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "A product manager used Claude to draft a financial model projecting unit economics for a new pricing tier. The spreadsheet logic looks clean and the narrative is compelling. But the verification question exposes the gap: the PM has general business sense but not the specific financial modeling expertise to catch a subtle error in how customer acquisition costs compound across cohorts. The required domain expertise is quantitative finance, not general product strategy. No one on the immediate team has that background, and this model will drive a board-level pricing decision \u2014 so the absence of a qualified reviewer is a stop condition. The PM needs to find an actual finance specialist to audit the model before presenting it.", "related_frameworks": ["the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "llm-psychosis-self-audit-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "JIORP Structure", "type": "procedure", "description": "A five-part framework for converting vague AI requests into executable delegation tasks by specifying Job, Inputs, Output, Rules, and Proof.", "when_to_apply": ["I gave Claude a task and the output was technically correct but not what I actually needed", "I'm about to hand off a complex multi-step task to an AI agent and I want to get it right the first time", "I need to create a reusable prompt template that other people on my team can use without me explaining the context every time", "I'm not sure how to tell whether the AI's output is actually correct or just plausible-looking", "My AI delegation keeps requiring multiple rounds of back-and-forth to get to something usable"], "principles": ["Specify the desired outcome concretely, not just the topic area", "Explicitly define what inputs the agent may and may not use", "Describe what 'done' looks like in terms of format, columns, sections, or file types", "State constraints (what must and must not happen) as explicitly as goals", "Include a proof step: what evidence should the agent provide so you can verify it worked"], "steps": ["Job: Define the outcome you want \u2014 not 'help me with X' but 'produce Y in Z format'", "Inputs: List what sources, documents, and context the agent may use, and what is off-limits", "Output: Describe exactly what done looks like \u2014 columns, sections, file types, specific formats", "Rules: State what the agent must do and must not do; constraints matter as much as goals", "Proof: Define how you will verify it worked \u2014 what evidence should it provide, what can you check against"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "182241677", "title": "The COMPLETE \"Wait, I Can Use Claude Code?!\" Guide (Yes, you can \u2014 even if you've never touched code)", "date": "2025-12-22T14:03:10.744Z"}], "date": "2025-12-22T14:03:10.744Z", "id": "jiorp-structure", "core_question": "I know roughly what I want this AI to do, but how do I specify it precisely enough that the output actually comes back right?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The original request was 'analyze our customer churn data.' That's a topic, not a job. After running through JIORP: the Job is 'produce a ranked list of the top 10 churn predictors with effect sizes.' Inputs are the Q1-Q3 customer_events.csv and the subscription table \u2014 not the marketing data, which has known quality issues. Output is a markdown table with columns for predictor name, effect size, sample size, and confidence interval. Rules: exclude any predictor with fewer than 50 observations; do not impute missing values, flag them instead. Proof: include the raw correlation matrix so the ranking can be spot-checked against source calculations. The difference between the vague version and the specified version is the difference between getting something you have to redo and something you can verify in two minutes.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "delegation-spec-drill", "the-five-specification-primitives", "done-definition-pattern", "definition-of-done-rubric", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "correctness-definition-framework", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "delegation-prompt-management-system", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "intent-doc", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Three-Board Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured audit that maps where an organization stands across three competing priority dimensions (distribution, runtime, and frontier) to surface strategic tensions and mobility constraints.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI vendor keeps launching consumer features while our enterprise API performance degrades \u2014 are these connected?", "We're trying to understand why our AI provider's product roadmap seems internally contradictory", "Should we go all-in on one AI platform or hedge across multiple providers given how fast priorities shift?", "I need to evaluate whether an AI company's current resource allocation is sustainable or signals a coming pivot away from our use case", "Our team is debating whether OpenAI, Anthropic, or Google is the safest long-term bet for our infrastructure"], "principles": ["Consumer, enterprise, and frontier priorities compete for the same underlying resource (compute)", "Moves that optimize one board cost mobility on the others", "Strategic position is revealed by tracing resource allocation across all three boards simultaneously"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references a 'Three-Board Audit' as one of three actionable prompts, but the specific steps or questions comprising the audit are not visible in the provided excerpt \u2014 the content is behind a paid membership wall.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182205269", "title": "Executive Briefing: The Bubble Test for OpenAI (Unit Economics, Capacity, Proof\u2014Three Signals to Watch in 2026)", "date": "2025-12-21T16:00:40.989Z"}], "date": "2025-12-21T16:00:40.989Z", "id": "the-three-board-audit", "core_question": "Is my AI provider actually investing in the capabilities I depend on, or are they quietly draining resources toward a competing priority that will leave my use case underfunded?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "OpenAI's consumer distribution push (ChatGPT growth, mobile apps, free-tier expansion) is consuming compute capacity that directly competes with enterprise API reliability and frontier research investment. The tell: API rate limits tightened in the same quarter that free-tier usage doubled. An enterprise customer betting on OpenAI's API stability is implicitly betting that consumer growth won't continue cannibalizing inference capacity \u2014 but every signal says consumer distribution is the current priority board. The strategic tension is real and unresolved: deepening API dependence here means accepting that your workloads sit on the board OpenAI is least incentivized to protect right now.", "related_frameworks": ["distribution-beats-compute", "the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "the-distribution-trap", "the-three-layer-analysis", "two-ai-economies-framework", "four-lane-supply-strategy", "developer-customers-vs-cio-customers-chasm"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references a 'Three-Board Audit' as one of three actionable prompts, but the specific steps or questions comprising the audit are not visible in the provided excerpt \u2014 the content is behind a paid membership wall.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Expensive Chat Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A diagnostic tool for determining whether an organization's AI usage is generating genuine delegation value (autonomous task completion) or merely running expensive chat interactions dressed up as agentic work.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI compute costs tripled this quarter but nobody can point to tasks that run without human babysitting", "We deployed what the vendor calls 'AI agents' but people still copy-paste outputs between systems manually", "Leadership wants to know whether our agentic AI investment is real automation or just a more expensive way to do what ChatGPT does", "We're trying to justify upgrading from conversational AI to agentic infrastructure and need to show what's actually missing", "Teams report they're 'using AI agents' but cycle times on workflows haven't changed"], "principles": ["Delegation value requires that an agent can initiate, sustain, and complete a multi-step task without human intervention at each step", "Expensive chat masquerades as agentic work but lacks the runtime depth, tool access, and governance of true delegation", "The distinction between chat and delegation is structural, not a matter of prompt sophistication"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 'The Expensive Chat Diagnostic' as one of three actionable prompts, but the specific diagnostic questions or decision logic are not visible in the provided excerpt \u2014 the content is behind a paid membership wall.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182205269", "title": "Executive Briefing: The Bubble Test for OpenAI (Unit Economics, Capacity, Proof\u2014Three Signals to Watch in 2026)", "date": "2025-12-21T16:00:40.989Z"}], "date": "2025-12-21T16:00:40.989Z", "id": "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "core_question": "Are we actually getting autonomous work done by our AI systems, or are we just paying enterprise prices for fancy autocomplete?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The team's 'AI agent' for customer onboarding still requires a human to confirm each step \u2014 data extraction, CRM entry, welcome email draft, compliance check \u2014 before proceeding. That's four human checkpoints in a four-step process. The agent has no tool access to the CRM, no authority to send emails, and no governance wrapper for compliance decisions. This is expensive chat: the organization is paying agentic-tier compute costs for what amounts to a drafting assistant that requires full human supervision at every transition. True delegation value would mean the agent runs the entire onboarding sequence end-to-end, with humans reviewing exceptions rather than approving every step.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "weekly-delegation-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "two-ai-economies-framework", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 'The Expensive Chat Diagnostic' as one of three actionable prompts, but the specific diagnostic questions or decision logic are not visible in the provided excerpt \u2014 the content is behind a paid membership wall.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Devil's Game (Friction Trade-off)", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI product design tensions not as solvable UX problems but as structurally irreconcilable trade-offs, where the design choices that win consumer adoption actively undermine enterprise delegation capability.", "when_to_apply": ["Our employees love ChatGPT personally but our enterprise AI deployment keeps failing on governance and accountability", "We're debating whether to wait for our AI vendor to add enterprise features or build internal governance tooling ourselves", "Why does adding verification and oversight to our AI workflows always tank user adoption?", "Our AI vendor keeps promising enterprise capabilities but the product keeps optimizing for consumer speed and simplicity", "We're trying to understand whether consumer AI adoption is helping or hurting our enterprise AI strategy"], "principles": ["Making delegation legible requires friction (work orders, verification, visible cost, slower execution)", "Consumer distribution punishes friction \u2014 speed and simplicity drive retention at scale", "These two optimization targets are not just in tension; product decisions that win one game actively teach the wrong mental model for the other", "The same product walks into the enterprise through consumer adoption, embedding the wrong mental model before enterprise tooling can correct it"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "182205269", "title": "Executive Briefing: The Bubble Test for OpenAI (Unit Economics, Capacity, Proof\u2014Three Signals to Watch in 2026)", "date": "2025-12-21T16:00:40.989Z"}], "date": "2025-12-21T16:00:40.989Z", "id": "the-devil-s-game-friction-trade-off", "core_question": "Why does our AI vendor's product keep failing our enterprise needs despite being wildly popular with individual users \u2014 is this a fixable UX gap or something structurally deeper?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The conventional read is that ChatGPT's consumer simplicity is a stepping stone to enterprise readiness \u2014 just add admin controls and audit logs. That's wrong here because the core product teaches users that AI interaction is instant, frictionless, and requires no specification of intent. Every employee who adopts the consumer product internalizes that mental model before IT can deploy enterprise governance. The friction required for delegatable AI \u2014 work orders, verification steps, visible cost signals, slower execution with audit trails \u2014 isn't a feature gap to close; it's a design direction that directly contradicts what made the consumer product successful. The company isn't failing to serve enterprise; it's structurally incapable of optimizing for both without forking the product.", "related_frameworks": ["building-vs-buying-ai-response", "pull-push-paradigm", "developer-customers-vs-cio-customers-chasm", "the-distribution-trap", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "delegation-bet-vs-coordination-bet", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "the-moat-audit", "two-ai-economies-framework", "comprehension-lock-in-audit"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Work Primitives", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A four-dimension assessment of whether an organization's work structure is legible enough for AI agents to operate on, determining whether agent leverage can actually be captured.", "when_to_apply": ["We bought Copilot licenses for the whole team but adoption is flat and nobody can explain why", "I want to deploy AI agents on our workflows but I'm not sure which processes are actually ready", "Our AI tools give good advice but nothing actually gets done automatically \u2014 there's always a human copy-pasting", "We're a non-technical team wondering what we'd need to change about how we work before AI agents could help", "Why does our competitor seem to get 10x from AI while we're stuck at 1.2x despite using the same models?"], "principles": ["Agent leverage requires work to be structured in materials that agents can read and act on \u2014 not locked in human heads or opaque admin panels", "The four primitives are necessary preconditions for delegation, whether to humans or agents", "Organizations that lack these primitives will be limited to an advisory layer with capable models but limited actual impact", "These are not coding skills \u2014 they are structural properties of how work is organized", "Rollback capability is what makes high throughput safe, not just fast"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "181939062", "title": "Inside OpenAI's Codex Team: Always-On AI Feedback, Everyone Commits Code, No One Cares About Titles\u2014A Look at the Future of Work + 6 Prompts to Make it Real in Your Business", "date": "2025-12-18T14:01:31.178Z"}], "date": "2025-12-18T14:01:31.178Z", "id": "work-primitives", "core_question": "Can our AI agents actually touch the work, or is everything locked in people's heads and opaque systems where no agent can reach it?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This team scores poorly on all four primitives. Tasks live in Slack threads and verbal handoffs (no machine-readable task definitions), artifacts are created in admin panels with no API (no programmatic access), there's no version control on their core deliverables (no rollback capability), and completion criteria are 'the manager looks at it and says it's done' (no verifiable done state). The model they deployed is GPT-4-class \u2014 plenty capable \u2014 but it's reduced to a chat assistant because there's nothing structured for it to operate on. Restructuring the work primitives is the prerequisite; upgrading the model is irrelevant until then.", "related_frameworks": ["legible-vs-illegible-work", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "primitives-for-reversibility", "capability-dissipation-gap", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "delegation-spec-drill", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "done-definition-pattern", "edge-first-automation-framework", "core-automation-readiness-check", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-factory-curve"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Correctness Definition Framework", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A three-part definition of AI output correctness that specifies allowed claims, required evidence, and penalties for errors versus silence, enabling teams and individuals to evaluate AI outputs against concrete, pre-specified criteria.", "when_to_apply": ["We're about to build an AI feature but the team can't agree on what a 'good' answer looks like", "Our AI system is confidently returning wrong information and we need to decide whether it should refuse instead", "Stakeholders keep rejecting AI outputs but nobody can articulate what specifically makes them wrong", "We need to write evaluation criteria for an AI system before we start building it", "I'm unsure whether our AI should prioritize always giving an answer versus sometimes declining when uncertain"], "principles": ["Correctness is not binary truth but fit: does the output do the job it was needed for?", "Define the set of claims the system is allowed to make (scope boundaries)", "Define the evidence required for each type of claim (source requirements vary by stakes)", "Define the penalty asymmetry: specify whether a confident wrong answer or a refusal to answer is worse for this context", "Ground truth location matters: determine whether it lives in a structured source (hallucination risk) or human judgment (framing/bias risk)", "Correctness must be defined before building, not discovered through production failures"], "steps": ["Identify the set of claims the system is allowed to make \u2014 what's in scope and what's explicitly out of scope", "Define the evidence required for each category of claim \u2014 what source or level of support is needed before the system can assert it", "Specify the penalty asymmetry \u2014 explicitly decide whether wrong-but-confident or silent-but-honest is worse for this context", "Identify where ground truth lives \u2014 structured authoritative source vs. human judgment \u2014 and adjust evaluation approach accordingly", "Write a one-paragraph correctness definition capturing the above that can be embedded in prompts or system configuration"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181753034", "title": "I've reviewed 20+ enterprise AI builds this year\u2014they all skip this conversation + 7 prompts that force it before anyone writes code", "date": "2025-12-16T14:03:31.230Z"}], "date": "2025-12-16T14:03:31.230Z", "id": "correctness-definition-framework", "core_question": "What does 'correct' actually mean for this AI system \u2014 and have we defined it precisely enough that we can evaluate outputs rather than just argue about them?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "For this medical triage chatbot, the correctness definition is now concrete: the system is allowed to describe common symptoms and suggest urgency levels, but is explicitly out of scope for naming diagnoses or recommending specific medications. Every symptom-to-urgency mapping must trace to a published clinical guideline \u2014 general medical knowledge from training data is insufficient. The penalty asymmetry is severe: a confident wrong urgency classification (telling someone chest pain is low-priority) is catastrophically worse than saying 'I'm not confident enough to classify this \u2014 please call your provider.' Ground truth lives in structured clinical protocols, so hallucination risk is the primary failure mode, not framing bias. That one paragraph \u2014 scope, evidence, penalty asymmetry, ground truth location \u2014 can now be embedded directly into the system prompt and used as the evaluation rubric for every QA pass.", "related_frameworks": ["ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "definition-of-done-rubric", "done-definition-pattern", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "intent-engineering", "blast-radius-assessment", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "verification-frontier"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Moving Goalposts Failure Pattern", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Enumerates the progressive, unacknowledged redefinition of 'correct' that occurs in AI projects, where each stakeholder layer adds undocumented requirements until the team is effectively building multiple different products without realizing it.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI outputs used to be fine but now everyone's unhappy and nobody can explain what changed", "Different stakeholders keep rejecting the same AI output for completely different reasons", "Engineering says they met the spec but the business side says the product is broken", "We keep adding 'small tweaks' to what the AI should produce and each sprint feels like starting over", "My personal AI workflow that used to feel great now feels inadequate even though I haven't changed the prompts"], "principles": ["Correctness redefinition happens silently \u2014 each change feels like a minor adjustment but the aggregate shift is a new product", "The AI is not becoming less reliable; human standards are shifting without acknowledgment", "Each stakeholder layer adds requirements without removing or reconciling prior ones", "The failure is upstream in human specification, not in model performance", "Catching this pattern requires naming what you want, noticing when it changes, and updating specifications explicitly"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "181753034", "title": "I've reviewed 20+ enterprise AI builds this year\u2014they all skip this conversation + 7 prompts that force it before anyone writes code", "date": "2025-12-16T14:03:31.230Z"}], "date": "2025-12-16T14:03:31.230Z", "id": "moving-goalposts-failure-pattern", "core_question": "Is our AI system actually getting worse, or have we silently redefined what 'good' means so many times that we're now building something nobody explicitly asked for?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The team says the model's summarization quality has 'degraded' over three sprints, but no model changes were made. The real timeline: Sprint 1, the PM defined 'correct' as factually accurate summaries. Sprint 2, Legal added a requirement that summaries must flag liability language \u2014 never written down, communicated verbally in a review meeting. Sprint 3, the CFO rejected outputs that didn't include financial impact estimates, which no one had ever specified. The team is now building three products \u2014 a summarizer, a legal risk scanner, and a financial impact estimator \u2014 under the label of one. The model isn't failing; it's being evaluated against a specification that doesn't exist yet.", "related_frameworks": ["correctness-definition-framework", "done-definition-pattern", "definition-of-done-rubric", "the-five-specification-primitives", "intent-doc", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "delegation-spec-drill", "convergence-metric", "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Ground Truth Location Diagnostic", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between two fundamentally different AI task types \u2014 those where correct answers exist in authoritative sources versus those where correctness lives in human judgment \u2014 because each requires a different evaluation approach and has a different primary failure mode.", "when_to_apply": ["The AI gave me a confident answer but I'm not sure if there even is a single correct answer to check it against", "I'm designing an eval suite and can't figure out whether to test for factual accuracy or reasoning quality", "We're debating whether to use RAG or let the model reason freely for a particular use case", "Someone on the team said 'the AI hallucinated' but the output was actually a matter of opinion, not fact", "I need to write a prompt that handles both factual lookup and open-ended analysis in the same workflow"], "principles": ["When ground truth lives in a structured source, the primary failure mode is hallucination \u2014 fabricating things not in the source", "When ground truth lives in human judgment, the primary failure mode is biased framing, missing perspectives, or false confidence in contested claims", "Most real tasks are a mix of both modes; prompts should be explicit about which mode is needed", "Telling the AI which mode you're in ('I need the actual figures' vs. 'help me think through what these mean') changes its behavior"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "181753034", "title": "I've reviewed 20+ enterprise AI builds this year\u2014they all skip this conversation + 7 prompts that force it before anyone writes code", "date": "2025-12-16T14:03:31.230Z"}], "date": "2025-12-16T14:03:31.230Z", "id": "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "core_question": "Does the 'right answer' for this AI task live in a source I can point to, or does it live in human judgment \u2014 and am I building my evaluation and prompting strategy around the wrong assumption?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The team wants the AI to summarize quarterly financial results and recommend strategic pivots. These are two fundamentally different tasks masquerading as one. The financial summary has ground truth in the 10-Q filing \u2014 the failure mode is hallucinated numbers, and evaluation means checking against the document. The strategic recommendation has ground truth in human judgment \u2014 there's no authoritative source that says 'pivot to enterprise.' The failure mode there is false confidence, missing alternatives, or biased framing presented as fact. The prompt should be split: 'Extract these figures from the 10-Q' (retrieval mode) and 'Given these verified figures, help me think through three strategic options with tradeoffs' (reasoning mode). Collapsing both into one prompt guarantees the AI will hallucinate numbers while sounding strategically authoritative.", "related_frameworks": ["the-verifiability-spectrum", "correctness-definition-framework", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "verification-frontier", "verification-capability-triage", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "intent-engineering", "the-contestability-split", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "model-context-matching-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Layer AI Evaluation System", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequential measurement architecture for enterprise AI systems that combines a curated golden test set, statistically disciplined comparison methodology, and production monitoring to create a continuous correctness feedback loop.", "when_to_apply": ["We keep changing prompts and RAG parameters and I have no idea if we're making things better or worse", "Stakeholders are asking for proof that our AI system is reliable and I don't have a measurement strategy", "We had a production incident where the AI gave wrong answers and I need to prevent that class of failure from recurring", "Two team members disagree about which prompt version is better and we're deciding based on vibes from a handful of examples", "Our AI system works in testing but users keep finding ways to break it in production"], "principles": ["Use real prompts and real edge cases in the golden set, not synthetic ones", "Run the golden set after every change to catch regressions before they reach production", "Statistical rigor is required for comparisons \u2014 subjective impressions from small samples are not measurement", "Production behavior will diverge from test behavior because users do unexpected things", "The feedback loop must convert production failures into new test cases continuously"], "steps": ["Build a golden test set: curate real prompts with expected outputs, including edge cases that have caused past problems", "Run the golden set after every change to prompts, models, or pipeline parameters to catch regressions", "Apply statistical discipline when comparing approaches: use enough test cases that differences are distinguishable from noise", "Instrument production: log inputs, outputs, and signals that indicate correctness failures", "Define thresholds that trigger human review of production outputs", "Convert production failures into new golden set entries to continuously improve test coverage"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "181753034", "title": "I've reviewed 20+ enterprise AI builds this year\u2014they all skip this conversation + 7 prompts that force it before anyone writes code", "date": "2025-12-16T14:03:31.230Z"}], "date": "2025-12-16T14:03:31.230Z", "id": "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "core_question": "How do I actually know whether my AI system is working correctly \u2014 not just at launch, but continuously as we change prompts, swap models, and encounter real user behavior?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "After running the golden set against the updated retrieval parameters, 3 of 47 edge cases regressed \u2014 the system now hallucinates a policy number on insurance claim queries that previously returned 'not found.' That's a statistically significant drop (p < 0.02) on the exactness dimension, not noise from a small sample. The regression gets blocked from production. Meanwhile, production logs from the past week surfaced a new failure mode: users pasting in scanned PDF text with OCR artifacts, which the golden set never covered. Those three malformed-input examples become new golden set entries, closing the coverage gap before the next iteration.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "correctness-definition-framework", "contextual-noise-library-factorial-stress-testing-for-agents", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "edge-first-automation-framework", "five-edge-categories", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "definition-of-done-rubric", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "proactively-reliable-ai-the-two-step-enterprise-strategy"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Simple Wins Method", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A filter for identifying which recurring tasks genuinely warrant careful model selection by testing whether the worker has real quality standards for the output.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep reading advice to 'use the right model for the right task' but I can't tell which of my tasks actually deserve that level of attention", "Feeling dissatisfied with AI outputs but unsure whether switching models would help or whether my expectations are just vague", "I want to build model intuition from my real work rather than from benchmarks or other people's comparisons", "Trying to figure out which of my recurring AI tasks are worth optimizing versus just leaving on autopilot", "I notice I sometimes fix AI output and sometimes just ship it \u2014 wondering what that pattern reveals about where to invest effort"], "principles": ["Only tasks where you have genuine expertise and real quality standards warrant careful model selection \u2014 if mediocre output wouldn't bother you, the detail doesn't matter", "Tasks should be small and bounded enough that you can clearly tell whether the output worked", "The filter question is not 'what tasks do I do with AI' but 'what tasks do I care about doing well'", "Observations should accumulate from direct contact with real work, not toy tasks or benchmarks", "Pay attention to where friction appeared and what you had to fix, not just whether the output was acceptable"], "steps": ["Run the task in your current default model to establish a baseline", "Run the same task in one alternative model", "Observe what happened: where did friction appear, what surprised you, what did you have to fix", "Let observations accumulate across multiple instances of the same task type", "Name the patterns you keep noticing (e.g., 'this model always loses the thread on contradictory inputs')"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181651323", "title": "Here's How I Pick the Right AI for Jobs that Matter: My 5 Prompts + a ChatGPT 5.2 vs. Claude Opus 4.5 vs. Gemini 3 Deep Dive", "date": "2025-12-15T14:03:24.591Z"}], "date": "2025-12-15T14:03:24.591Z", "id": "the-simple-wins-method", "core_question": "Is this recurring task something I actually care about doing well enough to justify the effort of comparing models, or am I overthinking tool selection for work where 'good enough' is genuinely good enough?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The weekly competitor summary doesn't pass the filter \u2014 any model produces acceptable output because the quality bar is 'did it capture the main points,' and mediocre output has never actually bothered me. But the client proposal drafts are different: I keep fixing the same structural problems in Claude's output (it buries the ask) and noticing that Gemini holds the commercial logic better across long documents. That friction pattern \u2014 consistently having to restructure the closing argument \u2014 is a real signal worth acting on. The proposal task gets multi-model evaluation; the competitor summary stays on whatever default is fastest.", "related_frameworks": ["the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "model-context-matching-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "verification-capability-triage", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "capability-surface-mapping"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Capability Surface Mapping", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes model evaluation from single-score comparison to topology mapping \u2014 identifying peaks and valleys across specific task types rather than asking which model is 'best overall.'", "when_to_apply": ["I'm trying to decide between Claude, GPT, and Gemini for my specific work and benchmarks aren't helping", "A model that's supposed to be better across the board keeps disappointing me on certain tasks", "I need to explain to my team why we should use different models for different parts of our workflow instead of standardizing on one", "Benchmark leaderboards say one thing but my hands-on experience says another \u2014 which should I trust?", "I'm designing a routing layer that sends different task types to different models and need a principled way to map capabilities"], "principles": ["Model capabilities have fragmented into dozens of specific behaviors \u2014 generalizations that were once roughly true are now breaking down", "The right unit of analysis is a specific behavior in a specific context, not an overall capability score", "Different workflows have entirely different capability requirements even within the same domain (e.g., repo-agent execution vs. hard debugging vs. code review)", "Practitioners who get granular talk about specific observations, not rankings", "The capability surface becomes visible only through direct contact with real work you know well"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181651323", "title": "Here's How I Pick the Right AI for Jobs that Matter: My 5 Prompts + a ChatGPT 5.2 vs. Claude Opus 4.5 vs. Gemini 3 Deep Dive", "date": "2025-12-15T14:03:24.591Z"}], "date": "2025-12-15T14:03:24.591Z", "id": "capability-surface-mapping", "core_question": "I keep hearing that Model X is 'the best,' but my actual experience is that it's great at some things and terrible at others \u2014 how do I think about model selection when there's no single winner?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The conventional read is that Claude 4 'beat' GPT-5 because it scored higher on an aggregate benchmark. That's wrong here because this team's workflow is 60% code review and 40% architecture brainstorming. Claude 4 has a clear peak in code review \u2014 it catches subtle logical errors and explains them in context \u2014 but a valley in open-ended architecture exploration where it converges too quickly on a single design. GPT-5 shows the opposite topology: mediocre at line-level review but generates genuinely divergent architectural options. The right move isn't picking a winner \u2014 it's routing code review to Claude and architecture sessions to GPT-5.", "related_frameworks": ["the-jagged-frontier-framework", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "model-context-matching-framework", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "verification-capability-triage", "harness-selection-decision-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Five Evaluation Prompts", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequential set of interview prompts that surface unconscious quality standards and hidden task complexity so you can design meaningful model comparisons for work that matters.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep editing AI outputs for the same task but can't explain what's wrong with them", "A new model just dropped and I want to know if it's actually better for my specific high-stakes work", "I need to build evaluation criteria for my team before we standardize on an AI tool", "I'm comparing two models side-by-side but the outputs look equally good on the surface and I can't decide", "I want to document what 'good enough' means for a recurring task before my standards drift"], "principles": ["Most tasks don't warrant careful model selection \u2014 the first prompt filters for the ones that do", "Quality standards are often applied unconsciously and must be surfaced before they can be tested", "Hidden complexity in familiar tasks contains the real failure modes", "Comparison tests must be designed to reveal real differences, not surface variation", "Observations fade quickly \u2014 capture them before they collapse into vague impressions"], "steps": ["The Filter: Identify which recurring tasks actually warrant careful model selection (most don't)", "The Preference Interview: Surface the standards you've been applying unconsciously so you have something concrete to test against", "The Detail Map: See the hidden complexity in work that seems simple \u2014 where the real failure modes live", "The Comparison Setup: Design a clean head-to-head test that reveals real differences, not surface variation", "The Observation Log: Capture what you actually noticed before the details fade into vague impressions"], "confidence": "high", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references five specific prompts (The Filter, The Preference Interview, The Detail Map, The Comparison Setup, The Observation Log) by name but the actual prompt text for each is gated behind a subscriber link and not included in the provided excerpt.", "source_posts": [{"id": "181651323", "title": "Here's How I Pick the Right AI for Jobs that Matter: My 5 Prompts + a ChatGPT 5.2 vs. Claude Opus 4.5 vs. Gemini 3 Deep Dive", "date": "2025-12-15T14:03:24.591Z"}], "date": "2025-12-15T14:03:24.591Z", "id": "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "core_question": "I know some AI models work better than others for my important tasks, but I can't articulate what 'better' actually means well enough to run a comparison that tells me anything useful.", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "Running through the procedure for a weekly client strategy memo: The filter confirms this task warrants evaluation \u2014 it recurs 50+ times a year, quality failures damage client trust, and the current model sometimes misses strategic implications. The preference interview surfaces three unconscious standards: the memo must escalate risks without triggering defensiveness, quantitative claims need hedging language calibrated to data confidence, and the narrative arc must connect last week's recommendations to this week's data. The detail map reveals the real failure mode isn't 'bad writing' \u2014 it's that the model flattens the confidence gradient, treating speculative inferences with the same assertiveness as confirmed metrics. The comparison setup uses three real client scenarios where this gradient matters most, and the observation log captures that Model A handles hedging naturally but loses narrative thread, while Model B maintains arc but over-commits on uncertain claims.", "related_frameworks": ["three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "model-context-matching-framework", "correctness-definition-framework", "definition-of-done-rubric", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "verification-frontier", "capability-surface-mapping", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "five-types-of-cognitive-work"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references five specific prompts (The Filter, The Preference Interview, The Detail Map, The Comparison Setup, The Observation Log) by name but the actual prompt text for each is gated behind a subscriber link and not included in the provided excerpt.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Abstraction Tax Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured audit to identify where convenience layers (GUIs, menus, CMS platforms, etc.) are blocking agents from acting on work directly, revealing the hidden cost of legacy software abstractions.", "when_to_apply": ["We deployed AI agents but the time savings are maybe 20% of what we expected", "Our agents spend most of their cycles navigating UIs instead of doing the actual work", "We're paying for SaaS tools that were great for human teams but wondering if they're now obstacles", "The team is asking whether to build custom tooling versus continuing to have agents work through existing platforms", "We want to audit our software stack specifically through the lens of agent-readiness"], "principles": ["Convenience layers that helped humans may now block agents from accessing work directly", "The real cost of legacy software is not the invoice but the agent leverage lost", "Ask which abstractions are still earning their keep in an agent-first environment", "Click-through menus and draft modes are walls between agents and the work"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 'The Abstraction Tax Audit' as a prompt/exercise but its specific diagnostic questions or steps are not visible in the provided excerpt.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "181561995", "title": "Executive Briefing: What Cursor's $57K CMS Deletion Reveals About Where Agent Value Actually Lives", "date": "2025-12-14T15:00:52.869Z"}], "date": "2025-12-14T15:00:52.869Z", "id": "the-abstraction-tax-audit", "core_question": "Are the software tools we're paying for actually helping our AI agents work, or are their human-friendly interfaces the very thing preventing agents from delivering value?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The marketing team's agents are underperforming despite strong model capabilities. The bottleneck isn't the agent \u2014 it's the CMS. Every content update requires navigating a GUI with dropdowns, preview modes, and draft states that exist to help humans but are opaque walls to agents. The agent could write directly to structured files in 200ms, but instead it's puppeteering a browser through a 14-click publishing workflow. The CMS isn't a $40K/year expense \u2014 it's a $400K/year drag when you count the agent leverage it destroys. The abstraction has stopped earning its keep.", "related_frameworks": ["legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "coordination-tax-audit", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "work-primitives", "edge-first-automation-framework", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "old-ritual-failure-catalog"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 'The Abstraction Tax Audit' as a prompt/exercise but its specific diagnostic questions or steps are not visible in the provided excerpt.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "MAP \u2192 CHECK \u2192 ANALYZE \u2192 BRIEF", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-phase sequencing structure for delegating long-running analytical tasks to an AI, ensuring comprehension is proven before conclusions are drawn.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to hand an AI a massive spreadsheet and get back a decision-ready memo I can trust", "My AI keeps giving me confident-sounding analysis that turns out to be based on misreading the data structure", "I'm delegating a long-running analytical task where I won't be checking in until the final output", "I need to audit a complex artifact \u2014 financial model, customer feedback corpus, operational data \u2014 and the AI needs to prove it understands before it concludes", "The deliverable will go directly to an executive and errors discovered late would be costly or embarrassing"], "principles": ["Force the model to demonstrate comprehension of structure and context before drawing any conclusions", "Require explicit integrity checks (tested formulas, verified relationships, flagged anomalies) before analysis begins", "Only after comprehension and integrity are proven should the model synthesize and build a narrative", "Each phase gate prevents errors from early misunderstanding from propagating into the final deliverable", "Phased structure is what separates a managed analytical run from a model rambling confidently"], "steps": ["MAP: Model maps the structure of the artifact \u2014 identifies tabs, columns, time periods, business context, and explains in plain English what it is looking at and why it matters", "CHECK: Model performs integrity checks \u2014 tests basic relationships, verifies formulas on a representative sample, flags data quality issues, and provides evidence of verification (not just claims)", "ANALYZE: Model draws conclusions and surfaces non-obvious patterns, with references to specific locations in the source material", "BRIEF: Model produces a structured executive deliverable \u2014 memo, tables, anomaly list with references, and open questions \u2014 that a decision-maker can use without opening the underlying artifact"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181388044", "title": "NEW: ChatGPT 5.2 Complete Teardown\u2014I tested Excel, PowerPoint, and 10,000-row datasets\u2014Here's My Take, Comparison vs. Opus 4.5 and Gemini 3 + 15 Prompts to Power Up GPT 5.2", "date": "2025-12-12T14:03:32.356Z"}], "date": "2025-12-12T14:03:32.356Z", "id": "map-check-analyze-brief", "core_question": "How do I hand off a complex analytical task to an AI and actually trust the output, instead of discovering halfway through that it misunderstood the data and confidently built conclusions on garbage?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A 10,000-row financial dataset with multiple tabs needs to be turned into an executive memo. In the MAP phase, the model identifies that Tab 3 contains quarterly revenue by region while Tab 5 has cost allocations using a different fiscal calendar \u2014 a mismatch that would silently corrupt any margin analysis. In CHECK, it tests the SUM formulas on Tab 3 against the reported totals and finds two quarters where they diverge by 4%, flagging this as a data quality issue before any conclusions are drawn. Only after these structural facts are established does ANALYZE surface the actual insight: APAC margins collapsed in Q3 not from revenue decline but from a one-time cost reallocation that the fiscal calendar mismatch would have obscured. The BRIEF delivers a two-page memo with cell references a CFO can spot-check without opening the spreadsheet.", "related_frameworks": ["delegation-spec-drill", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "done-definition-pattern", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "correctness-definition-framework", "intent-doc", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "the-five-specification-primitives", "blast-radius-assessment", "definition-of-done-rubric", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Definition of Done Rubric", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A five-dimension scoring rubric for evaluating whether a delegated AI work product actually succeeded, distinguishing between work that looks good and work that is good.", "when_to_apply": ["I got a long AI-generated analysis back and it reads well, but something feels off and I can't pinpoint what", "Deciding whether to send an AI-generated deliverable to a decision-maker or whether it needs another pass", "The AI output has confident-sounding claims but I can't tell if they're grounded in the sources I provided", "I want a consistent quality bar for evaluating AI work products across my team instead of vibes-based acceptance", "An AI agent completed a multi-step research task and I need to verify the result before it feeds into the next workflow"], "principles": ["An output that fails on Comprehension or Integrity is broken regardless of how polished it looks", "An output that passes Comprehension and Integrity but fails on Insight or Usefulness is a competent summary that didn't create value", "An output that passes everything but Auditability might be excellent or might be confidently wrong \u2014 you have no way to know", "Each dimension is a necessary condition, not a partial score \u2014 early failures invalidate later dimensions", "Auditability means you can trace claims back to specific source locations, not just that the model asserts it checked"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181388044", "title": "NEW: ChatGPT 5.2 Complete Teardown\u2014I tested Excel, PowerPoint, and 10,000-row datasets\u2014Here's My Take, Comparison vs. Opus 4.5 and Gemini 3 + 15 Prompts to Power Up GPT 5.2", "date": "2025-12-12T14:03:32.356Z"}], "date": "2025-12-12T14:03:32.356Z", "id": "definition-of-done-rubric", "core_question": "Is this AI-generated deliverable actually reliable enough to act on, or does it just look polished enough to fool me?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The AI-generated market analysis reads beautifully and hits all the right themes \u2014 but scoring it on Comprehension reveals it conflated two different market segments the brief explicitly separated. That's a foundational failure: the Insight dimension (which looks impressive \u2014 novel comparisons, sharp conclusions) is built on a misread of the input. The entire output needs to be redone, not edited. Polishing a miscomprehended deliverable just makes the error harder to catch downstream.", "related_frameworks": ["done-definition-pattern", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "correctness-definition-framework", "verification-capability-triage", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "delegation-spec-drill", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Delegation Prompt Management System", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A five-component classification system for structuring any AI delegation prompt, covering the dimensions that determine whether a long-running task produces checkable, usable work.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to hand off a complex, multi-step task to an AI and I want to avoid getting back generic filler", "My last AI-generated deliverable looked convincing but I couldn't verify any of the claims it made", "I'm building a reusable prompt template for a recurring analysis task that multiple people will use", "I'm writing a prompt for a task that will run against a large dataset and I need the output to be auditable", "Someone on my team keeps getting bad results from AI delegation and I need to diagnose what's missing in their prompts"], "principles": ["Role sets the epistemic frame \u2014 what kind of judgment and expertise the model should apply", "Phases enforce a sequence that keeps the model honest \u2014 comprehension before analysis, analysis before synthesis", "Deliverables must be concrete and checkable, not vague categories like 'analysis'", "Constraints define the boundaries of work and prevent scope drift during long runs", "Checkability is what converts a plausible-sounding output into a trustworthy one \u2014 require citations, row references, and explicit caveats about what couldn't be verified"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181388044", "title": "NEW: ChatGPT 5.2 Complete Teardown\u2014I tested Excel, PowerPoint, and 10,000-row datasets\u2014Here's My Take, Comparison vs. Opus 4.5 and Gemini 3 + 15 Prompts to Power Up GPT 5.2", "date": "2025-12-12T14:03:32.356Z"}], "date": "2025-12-12T14:03:32.356Z", "id": "delegation-prompt-management-system", "core_question": "How do I structure what I'm asking an AI to do so the output is actually checkable and usable, not just plausible-sounding?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The prompt that produced a useless 'market analysis' was missing three of the five components. It had a vague role ('be a business analyst'), no phasing (so the model jumped straight to recommendations without establishing what data it had actually read), and zero checkability requirements. Rebuilding it: the role is 'equity research analyst specializing in SaaS metrics,' the phases enforce data extraction \u2192 metric calculation \u2192 comparison \u2192 synthesis, the deliverables are a specific table with named columns and a one-page brief, the constraints bound it to the uploaded dataset only, and checkability requires every claim to cite a row number or cell reference. The difference isn't cleverness \u2014 it's that every output artifact can now be verified against something concrete.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "the-five-specification-primitives", "delegation-spec-drill", "the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "done-definition-pattern", "definition-of-done-rubric", "jiorp-structure", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "constraint-architecture", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "weekly-delegation-audit", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "correctness-definition-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Work Delegation Bucket Classification", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-category classification of work types for deciding how to approach AI delegation, based on whether the work is new, high-value existing, or tedious recurring.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a dozen tasks I could try with AI but I don't know which ones to start with", "I'm worried about AI making mistakes on important work but I also don't want to over-verify everything", "Some of my work is brand new exploration and some is repetitive drudgery \u2014 should I treat them the same when delegating to AI?", "I want to build an AI delegation habit but need a way to calibrate how much I trust the output for different task types", "My team is adopting AI tools and we need a simple framework for which tasks get full autonomy versus careful review"], "principles": ["New work (tasks never completed before) has low downside risk \u2014 any progress is a win, so it is safe to experiment freely", "High-value existing work requires careful baseline comparison \u2014 verify that AI output improves on what you'd produce yourself before trusting it", "Tedious recurring work is the safest and most immediately valuable target \u2014 spot-checking quickly reveals model quality with low stakes"], "steps": ["Classify the task: Is this new work that has never been done? High-value existing work with a known baseline? Or tedious recurring work?", "For new work: gather available data and delegate freely \u2014 any output is net-positive progress", "For high-value existing work: delegate with careful data preparation, then compare output to your baseline to assess quality before relying on it", "For tedious recurring work: delegate and spot-check \u2014 quality is easy to assess and the leverage is immediate"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "181388044", "title": "NEW: ChatGPT 5.2 Complete Teardown\u2014I tested Excel, PowerPoint, and 10,000-row datasets\u2014Here's My Take, Comparison vs. Opus 4.5 and Gemini 3 + 15 Prompts to Power Up GPT 5.2", "date": "2025-12-12T14:03:32.356Z"}], "date": "2025-12-12T14:03:32.356Z", "id": "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "core_question": "I want to start delegating work to AI, but different tasks feel like they need different levels of caution \u2014 how do I sort my workload into the right approach for each?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This weekly client report is tedious recurring work \u2014 same structure, same data sources, same format every Friday. That makes it the highest-leverage delegation target: hand it to the model, spot-check two or three outputs against last week's reports, and if quality holds, it's automated. The strategic competitor analysis the CEO requested, on the other hand, is new work that's never been done before \u2014 there's no baseline to beat, so any structured output is a net win worth experimenting with freely. But rewriting the pricing model \u2014 that's high-value existing work where the team already has a vetted spreadsheet. Delegating that requires running the AI output side-by-side against the current model and verifying every formula before trusting it.", "related_frameworks": ["the-30-minute-delegation-rule", "weekly-delegation-audit", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "verification-capability-triage", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "task-decomposition-framework", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Knowledge Work Practice Gym", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured system for building deliberate practice into knowledge work by defining quality rubrics, generating AI feedback on artifacts, and creating repeatable drill cycles.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep giving the same feedback on decision docs and specs but nothing changes \u2014 people aren't actually building the skill", "We have no shared definition of what a 'good' executive update or spec looks like, so review quality depends on who's reviewing", "I want to use AI to give my team feedback on their writing but I'm worried about it becoming a surveillance tool", "Our hiring exercise tests different skills than what people actually need day-to-day and I want to align them", "Individual contributors want to improve their knowledge work output but there's no structured way to practice like there is in sports or music"], "principles": ["Define what 'good' looks like before involving AI \u2014 rubric creation is human work", "Focus on one artifact type, one rubric, and one team per quarter to avoid program fatigue", "AI provides directional coaching signal, not performance scores \u2014 never tie AI scores to compensation or calibration", "Feedback should be private by default; managers see team-level patterns, not individual rankings", "Practice should be opt-in and locally owned, not centrally mandated", "Use real artifacts from your own week as practice material, not hypothetical exercises", "Hiring exercises and internal practice should test the same skills using the same rubric"], "steps": ["Pick one artifact type that matters for your team (e.g., decision doc, spec, executive update)", "Convene the people whose judgment you trust and extract concrete criteria for 'good' \u2014 push until you have specific, observable dimensions", "Build a rubric scoring each dimension 1\u20135", "Gather 3\u20135 real examples and annotate them with explicit scoring rationale to calibrate the rubric", "Configure a model with the rubric and annotated examples so it scores new documents, quotes specific passages, and suggests improvements", "Establish the habit: AI critique runs before human review; humans focus on judgment calls, not gap-catching", "Each team member spends 10 minutes weekly drilling the dimension the AI most frequently flags as a weakness", "At quarter end, evaluate: Did scores improve? Fewer revision cycles? Faster decisions? Adjust rubric and focus accordingly"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181300900", "title": "Grab the 9 prompts I use to turn docs into practice reps + a 90-day pilot you can steal", "date": "2025-12-11T14:02:00.941Z"}], "date": "2025-12-11T14:02:00.941Z", "id": "knowledge-work-practice-gym", "core_question": "My team keeps producing mediocre documents and I give the same feedback over and over \u2014 how do I turn recurring knowledge work into something people can actually practice and get better at?", "category": "execution", "example_application": "The team's decision docs consistently fail on the same dimension: they describe options but never state the recommendation with explicit trade-offs. The rubric scores 'decisiveness' at 1-2 across the last month's artifacts. The AI critique model flags this in 80% of drafts before human review even begins, which means the weekly 10-minute drill is clear: each person rewrites one paragraph of a real doc to lead with a recommendation and name what's being sacrificed. After six weeks, the AI's 'decisiveness' flags dropped from 80% to 25% of drafts, and the VP stopped sending docs back for revision \u2014 the practice loop closed the gap that years of ad hoc feedback never did.", "related_frameworks": ["coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "definition-of-done-rubric", "done-definition-pattern", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "correctness-definition-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Five Core Knowledge Work Skills (Plus One to Argue Against)", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of the five recurring skills that determine knowledge worker effectiveness \u2014 Judgment, Orchestration, Coordination, Taste, and Updating \u2014 each defined by its artifact surface and characteristic failure mode, plus an argument against treating 'prompting' as a top-level skill.", "when_to_apply": ["Designing a rubric for evaluating knowledge worker output beyond surface-level writing quality", "My team keeps producing mediocre documents and I can't pinpoint whether it's a skills gap or a process gap", "We're debating whether to invest in prompt engineering training or something more fundamental", "Building promotion criteria or job descriptions that reflect what actually makes someone effective, not just their title", "Deciding which skills to target in a deliberate practice program for knowledge workers"], "principles": ["Skills live in artifacts \u2014 the documents you leave behind \u2014 not in job titles or LinkedIn taglines", "Each skill has a characteristic failure mode that is observable in written output", "Orchestration and specification literacy are the deeper skills that make prompting work \u2014 train those instead", "Updating is visible in how positions evolve over time, not in how they are defended", "Taste requires being able to articulate what 'good' looks like, not just recognize it"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181300900", "title": "Grab the 9 prompts I use to turn docs into practice reps + a 90-day pilot you can steal", "date": "2025-12-11T14:02:00.941Z"}], "date": "2025-12-11T14:02:00.941Z", "id": "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "core_question": "What are the actual skills my knowledge workers need to get better at \u2014 not job titles or buzzwords, but the specific capabilities that show up in the documents they produce and the decisions they make?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "This team's quarterly planning docs keep getting sent back for revision, and the instinct is to blame 'communication skills.' But the artifacts tell a different story. The plans show strong coordination (stakeholders are mapped, dependencies noted) and decent taste (the format and structure are clean). The failure is in judgment \u2014 every plan treats three competing priorities as equally important, never making the hard call about what to cut. That's not a writing problem; it's a judgment deficit that shows up in writing. The development investment should target judgment practice reps, not another 'better business writing' course. And the request to add 'prompt engineering' training is a distraction \u2014 what these people actually lack is specification literacy and orchestration skill, which are the deeper capabilities that make any tool (including AI) produce better output.", "related_frameworks": ["knowledge-work-practice-gym", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "work-primitives", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "intent-engineering", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "definition-of-done-rubric", "rejection-as-a-competency-recognition-articulation-encoding", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Coaching Tool vs. Surveillance Tool Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "A framework distinguishing the constructive use of AI artifact scoring (cheap feedback, local ownership, optional practice) from its destructive misuse (metric worship, centralized control, performance weaponization).", "when_to_apply": ["We're rolling out AI-based document scoring across the org and need to decide who sees the scores", "A manager wants to pull AI rubric scores into performance reviews or calibration sessions", "People are gaming our AI feedback scores instead of actually learning from them", "Leadership is asking whether AI skill assessments should be visible org-wide as a transparency measure", "We're designing a practice-rep system and need guardrails to keep it from becoming a compliance tool"], "principles": ["The moment scores are tied to performance calibration, people game them instead of learning from them", "Individuals should own their own scores by default", "Managers should see team-level patterns, not individual rankings", "Rubrics must be defined by the people who do the work and updated when they stop being useful", "Practice must be chosen, not checked \u2014 keep the tool in the coaching lane"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181300900", "title": "Grab the 9 prompts I use to turn docs into practice reps + a 90-day pilot you can steal", "date": "2025-12-11T14:02:00.941Z"}], "date": "2025-12-11T14:02:00.941Z", "id": "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "core_question": "Will this AI scoring system help people get better at their work, or will it become a leaderboard that destroys the learning it was supposed to create?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "This proposal to roll rubric scores into quarterly calibration is a surveillance tool, not a coaching tool. The tell: scores would be visible to managers at the individual level and tied to performance ratings. The moment that happens, people optimize for the rubric instead of the skill \u2014 they'll hit every checkbox without improving the underlying document quality. The fix is to keep individual scores owned by the individual, surface only team-level patterns to managers, and make practice voluntary. If adoption drops, that's a signal the rubric is broken, not that people need more pressure.", "related_frameworks": ["knowledge-work-practice-gym", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "definition-of-done-rubric", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "the-five-evaluation-prompts"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four-Layer Agent Memory Model", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A tiered classification of agent memory into four distinct layers\u2014working context, sessions, memory, and artifacts\u2014each with different scope, persistence, and access patterns, analogous to cache/RAM/disk in computer architecture.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent works fine for short tasks but degrades or hallucinates as conversations get longer", "Designing the memory architecture for an autonomous agent that runs over hours or days", "I'm unsure whether to store agent state in the prompt, a database, or file references", "Evaluating a vendor's claim about 'infinite memory' or 'persistent context' for their agent platform", "My agent's context window is filling up with session history and I need a strategy for what stays and what gets externalized"], "principles": ["Working context is computed (a fresh projection), not accumulated\u2014it is the output of a compilation step, not a growing buffer", "Sessions are structured event logs (typed records of every action, tool call, result) that serve as ground truth but are never directly fed to the model", "Memory is searchable and retrieved on demand, not permanently pinned in context", "Artifacts (large objects like codebases or documents) are stored by reference and accessed via pointers, not tokenized into the window", "Each layer maps to a different cost/access tradeoff: working context = cache (expensive, minimal), sessions = RAM (bounded), memory/artifacts = disk (can grow arbitrarily)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181091574", "title": "Get the Cheat Code on Long-Running AI Agents\u2014Here's What Manus, Google, and Anthropic Learned After Trial and Error + 12 Prompts to Help Build Long-Running Agents Yourself", "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z"}], "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z", "id": "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "core_question": "Where should each type of state live in my agent system so it doesn't bloat the context window, lose critical history, or degrade as tasks get longer?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This agent is failing because it treats memory as a single growing buffer \u2014 every past interaction is stuffed into the context window until it overflows. The session logs (tool calls, results, intermediate reasoning) belong in Layer 2: structured event records that the system can query but never dumps wholesale into the prompt. The codebase the agent is editing belongs in Layer 4: stored by reference, accessed via pointers when needed, never tokenized in full. Working context (Layer 1) should be freshly compiled each turn \u2014 a projection of only what's relevant right now, assembled from the other layers. The fix isn't 'bigger context window'; it's proper layer separation so each type of state lives where its cost/access tradeoff makes sense.", "related_frameworks": ["four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "context-as-compiled-view", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "double-compression-loop", "model-context-matching-framework", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Nine Context Engineering Scaling Principles", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "Nine named principles for assessing and improving how a long-running agent manages its context window, each with a clear rationale, tradeoffs, and failure signal.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent works great for short tasks but degrades or hallucinates after 20-30 minutes of continuous operation", "Input token costs are exploding as my agent runs longer \u2014 I need to figure out what's bloating the context window", "I'm designing a multi-agent system and need to decide how to partition context across sub-agents", "My agent's summarization step keeps losing critical information and I can't figure out why", "Building an agent that needs 50+ tool calls per session and I want to get the architecture right before production"], "principles": ["Context is computed, not accumulated: every LLM call assembles a fresh minimal view from durable state", "Separate storage from presentation: session logs and working context evolve independently", "Scope by default: start with nearly empty context and add via explicit retrieval decisions", "Retrieval over pinning: treat memory as queryable, not permanently resident, to avoid recency bias", "Summarization must be schema-driven: define what fields must survive compression before compressing", "Offload heavy state to tools and sandboxes: pass pointers to large results, not raw payloads", "Isolate context with sub-agents: sub-agents exist to give work its own window, not to mimic org charts", "Design for cache stability: keep prompt prefixes stable and serialization deterministic to maximize cache reuse", "Let context evolve through execution: capture outcomes and feed them back so agents improve without retraining"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181091574", "title": "Get the Cheat Code on Long-Running AI Agents\u2014Here's What Manus, Google, and Anthropic Learned After Trial and Error + 12 Prompts to Help Build Long-Running Agents Yourself", "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z"}], "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z", "id": "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "core_question": "Is my agent architecture going to collapse under its own context weight as tasks get longer and more complex, and which specific design decisions are causing that fragility?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This agent violates principles 1 and 4 simultaneously: it accumulates the full conversation history into every LLM call and pins tool results into the system prompt. After 25 minutes of operation, it's spending 80% of tokens on stale context, the cache hit rate has cratered because the prompt prefix shifts every call, and the model is exhibiting recency bias \u2014 weighting the last three tool outputs while ignoring earlier critical findings. The fix sequence is: move to computed context assembly from durable state (principle 1), replace pinned tool results with retrieval-on-demand from a queryable store (principle 4), and define a compression schema for session summaries so critical fields survive summarization rather than being silently dropped (principle 5).", "related_frameworks": ["four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "context-as-compiled-view", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "double-compression-loop", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "model-context-matching-framework", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Context as Compiled View", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes agent context from a mutable string buffer that grows by appending to a compiled output computed fresh each call from a richer underlying state system\u2014shifting the engineering mindset from prompt manipulation to systems engineering.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent works fine for short tasks but falls apart on longer multi-step workflows", "We keep expanding the context window but reliability isn't improving", "Deciding whether to invest in better summarization vs. restructuring how we assemble context entirely", "Designing the memory and context architecture for a long-running agent from scratch", "A team is debugging agent failures by tweaking prompts when the real issue is architectural \u2014 context is just a growing string buffer"], "principles": ["Sessions, memory, and artifacts are the sources (full structured state); the working context is the compiled output for one specific invocation", "The compilation pipeline (flows and processors) transforms raw state into a minimal, relevant view at runtime", "Changing the compilation logic does not require changing storage, enabling independent iteration on each", "The correct question is not 'how do I fit more into the window?' but 'what is the right intermediate representation for this step?'", "Context engineering is systems engineering: it requires a session store, a view-compilation pipeline, and clear contracts between components"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181091574", "title": "Get the Cheat Code on Long-Running AI Agents\u2014Here's What Manus, Google, and Anthropic Learned After Trial and Error + 12 Prompts to Help Build Long-Running Agents Yourself", "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z"}], "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z", "id": "context-as-compiled-view", "core_question": "Why does my agent keep degrading as conversations get longer, even though I keep tuning the prompt and expanding the context window?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this agent needs a bigger context window or smarter summarization to handle its 50-step workflow. That's wrong. The team is treating context like a chat transcript that grows by appending \u2014 every prior step, tool result, and user message accumulates into one massive string. The real fix is to stop accumulating and start compiling: sessions, memory, and artifacts live in a structured state store, and at each invocation a compilation pipeline selects only the subset relevant to *this specific step* and renders it into a minimal intermediate representation. The question shifts from 'how do we fit 50 steps into the window?' to 'what does step 37 actually need to see?' \u2014 and now storage, compilation logic, and the prompt template can each iterate independently.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-memory-model", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "double-compression-loop", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "model-context-matching-framework", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Two Memory Problems Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "Separates two commonly conflated agent memory failure modes\u2014cross-session amnesia (domain memory) and within-session degradation (context engineering)\u2014clarifying that they have different causes and require different architectural solutions.", "when_to_apply": ["An agent loses progress between conversation sessions but works fine within a single session", "An agent starts sharp but becomes confused or repetitive deep into a long task", "We added persistent memory files but the agent still degrades mid-task \u2014 what are we missing?", "Evaluating whether an agent vendor's 'memory' feature solves cross-session continuity, within-session coherence, or both", "Trying to figure out whether confused agent behavior is a storage problem or a context window management problem"], "principles": ["Domain memory (cross-session): the agent has no persistent record of where things stand; each session starts fresh; solved by structured external records (task lists, progress logs, validation criteria)", "Context engineering (within-session): the agent accumulates too much in the context window mid-task, burying critical signal under noise; solved by computed views and active state management", "Both problems are necessary to solve; external records without context engineering still produces mid-session degradation", "Context engineering without domain memory means every session starts without continuity even if the session itself stays coherent", "The two failure modes look similar (confused, forgetting things) but have completely different root causes and fixes"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "181091574", "title": "Get the Cheat Code on Long-Running AI Agents\u2014Here's What Manus, Google, and Anthropic Learned After Trial and Error + 12 Prompts to Help Build Long-Running Agents Yourself", "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z"}], "date": "2025-12-09T14:03:23.630Z", "id": "two-memory-problems-distinction", "core_question": "My agent keeps losing track of things \u2014 is it forgetting between sessions, or drowning within a single session, and why does it matter which one?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "This team added a task log and progress file between sessions, but the agent still goes off the rails 40 minutes into complex multi-step work. That's not a domain memory problem \u2014 the agent starts each session knowing exactly where it left off. The failure is context engineering: mid-session, the context window fills with raw tool outputs, prior reasoning traces, and accumulated conversation history until the critical constraints get buried. The fix isn't better external records \u2014 it's computed views that surface only the active task state, validation criteria, and relevant constraints instead of dumping the full session history into the window.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-memory-model", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "context-as-compiled-view", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "second-brain-failure-mode-catalog", "double-compression-loop", "model-context-matching-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "\u03bb/\u0394/\u03b8 Focus Capacity Model", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-variable model that quantifies deep work capacity by measuring interruption rate (\u03bb), recovery time (\u0394), and minimum viable work block size (\u03b8), then calculating how many meaningful work units fit into a given day.", "when_to_apply": ["I feel like I'm busy all day but can't point to a single meaningful thing I accomplished", "I'm trying to decide whether to accept another recurring meeting and want to know the real cost to my deep work", "My team complains they never have time for real work despite having 'open' calendars", "I had a terrible productivity day and want to pinpoint whether it was interruptions, slow recovery, or too-small time blocks", "I'm redesigning my team's Slack and meeting norms and need a quantitative model for how changes affect focus capacity"], "principles": ["Focus capacity is a function of measurable environmental variables, not willpower or character", "Interruption rate (\u03bb), recovery time (\u0394), and minimum block size (\u03b8) together determine whether deep work is statistically possible on a given day", "Count real work units by asking how many \u03b8-sized chunks fit into each focus block, rounding down \u2014 the rounding is harsh but honest", "Small changes to any single variable can produce disproportionately large improvements in total capacity", "Roughly half of interruptions are self-inflicted, making personal habit change as important as environmental design", "If \u03b8 exceeds available block sizes, the result is constant thrashing regardless of total time spent working"], "steps": ["Establish your baseline \u03bb: count how many times per hour your attention is pulled away (Slack, email, questions, pop-ups)", "Establish your baseline \u0394: estimate how many minutes it takes to mentally re-engage with deep work after each interruption", "Establish your baseline \u03b8: identify the minimum uninterrupted block length before you feel you've made meaningful progress on real work", "Audit your calendar to identify your actual focus blocks \u2014 the stretches between scheduled interruptions", "For each focus block, calculate how many \u03b8-sized chunks fit inside it (rounding down), then sum across blocks to get your daily capacity", "Identify which variable (\u03bb, \u0394, or \u03b8) is the binding constraint on your current capacity", "Apply targeted interventions: reduce \u03bb via triage and async defaults, reduce \u0394 via context-capture habits, reduce \u03b8 via work decomposition"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "180981741", "title": "The focus system I borrowed from an engineer's blog + 18 prompts to actually move the dials", "date": "2025-12-08T14:02:44.845Z"}], "date": "2025-12-08T14:02:44.845Z", "id": "focus-capacity-model", "core_question": "I'm working long hours but producing almost nothing of substance \u2014 is this a discipline problem or is my environment making deep work structurally impossible?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "A senior engineer has 8 hours of nominal work time but gets interrupted roughly 4 times per hour (\u03bb=4), takes about 15 minutes to re-engage each time (\u0394=15), and needs at least 45-minute blocks to make meaningful progress on architecture work (\u03b8=45). After mapping scheduled meetings and calculating interruption-fragmented gaps, only 2 blocks per day exceed 45 minutes \u2014 yielding exactly 2 deep work units. That's not a motivation problem; it's arithmetic. The binding constraint is \u03bb: cutting interruptions from 4/hour to 2/hour by batching Slack into 30-minute check windows would nearly double usable capacity without touching the calendar at all.", "related_frameworks": ["coordination-tax-audit", "coordination-tax-audit-ai-era-team-sizing", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "task-decomposition-framework", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "ai-leverage-audit", "work-primitives", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "temporal-separation-build-reflect-rhythm", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Resume Bullet Quality Framework", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Enumerates nine distinct failure modes for how people misrepresent or poorly describe AI work on resumes, each with diagnostic signals and a clear reason for failure.", "when_to_apply": ["I used ChatGPT a lot at work and I'm not sure how to describe that on my resume without sounding vague or inflated", "Reviewing someone's resume that lists AI tools but the bullets feel hollow \u2014 I can't tell what they actually did", "I built something real with AI but I'm worried my resume bullet undersells it because I don't know the right framing", "A candidate's resume says they 'architected an AI solution' and I need to assess whether that claim will hold up in a technical interview", "I prompted an LLM to improve a workflow and got great results, but writing it as a resume bullet makes it sound either too small or too big"], "principles": ["Vague language signals absence of real work; specificity is the primary credibility signal", "Tool names alone are not accomplishments \u2014 they must be paired with task, outcome, and judgment", "Inflated language (architected, spearheaded, led transformation) collapses under questioning", "Underselling real work is as damaging as overselling non-work", "Weasel words like 'collaborated' or 'supported' hide contribution unless followed by specifics", "Buzzword stacking obscures rather than demonstrates competence", "Over-ownership (claiming full credit for team work) breaks trust in interviews", "Disconnected wins (business outcomes without explaining the AI's role) fail to prove fluency", "A one-off prompt framed as a product launch is an over-claim that will be exposed"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "180818333", "title": "The AI Resume Survival Guide (for 2025 and Beyond)", "date": "2025-12-07T00:00:57.900Z"}], "date": "2025-12-07T00:00:57.900Z", "id": "ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "core_question": "Will this AI resume bullet survive the moment an interviewer asks me to explain exactly what I did?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The bullet reads 'Spearheaded enterprise-wide AI transformation leveraging GPT-4 and LangChain to drive operational excellence.' This is failure mode stacking: inflated language ('spearheaded,' 'transformation'), buzzword stacking ('leveraging,' 'operational excellence'), and tool-name-as-accomplishment with no task, outcome, or judgment described. Under interview scrutiny, the candidate would need to explain what they actually built, what decision they made about which tool to use and why, and what measurable result occurred \u2014 none of which this bullet provides. The rewrite needs a specific task, a real choice the person made, and a concrete outcome: 'Built a GPT-4 classification pipeline that routed 12K monthly support tickets, reducing average response time from 4 hours to 40 minutes by tuning prompt logic to handle ambiguous edge cases.'", "related_frameworks": ["five-element-ai-resume-bullet-test", "ai-assisted-resume-refinement-process", "ai-detector-risk-mitigation-checklist", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "ai-career-board", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Five-Element AI Resume Bullet Test", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A five-dimensional scoring rubric for assessing whether an AI-related resume bullet contains sufficient signal across task, tool, AI role, outcome, and judgment.", "when_to_apply": ["I've used AI tools at work but my resume bullets still sound generic and unimpressive", "Coaching someone whose AI experience descriptions read like they just opened ChatGPT once", "Trying to decide whether a resume bullet about AI work is strong enough to survive a technical hiring manager's skepticism", "Comparing resume bullets across PM, engineering, and design candidates to calibrate what 'good' AI experience looks like per role", "My bullet mentions an AI tool but I'm not sure it communicates that I actually understood what I was doing"], "principles": ["A bullet missing all five elements signals nothing to a hiring manager", "Three out of five dimensions is a solid baseline; five out of five is a standout", "The five dimensions are: specific task, named tool/model, what the AI actually did, measurable outcome, and demonstrated judgment or constraint awareness", "Judgment signals (tradeoffs, fallback logic, failure cases, iteration) are the hardest to fake and most valuable", "The rubric applies across roles but the emphasis shifts: engineers weight system behavior, PMs weight product judgment, designers weight model-behavior-driven UX decisions, ops workers weight integration into real workflows"], "steps": ["Check: Is there a specific task described (not just a goal or interest)?", "Check: Is a real tool, model, or platform named?", "Check: Is it clear what the AI itself did (summarize, classify, extract, generate, triage)?", "Check: Is there a measurable or observable outcome (time saved, accuracy improved, usage count, drop-off reduced)?", "Check: Is there evidence of judgment \u2014 constraints acknowledged, tradeoffs made, failures handled, iteration documented?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "180818333", "title": "The AI Resume Survival Guide (for 2025 and Beyond)", "date": "2025-12-07T00:00:57.900Z"}], "date": "2025-12-07T00:00:57.900Z", "id": "five-element-ai-resume-bullet-test", "core_question": "Does this resume bullet actually prove I can work with AI, or does it just say I used ChatGPT?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This bullet scores 3/5: 'Built a GPT-4-powered support triage system that reduced ticket routing time by 40%.' It names the task (triage), the tool (GPT-4), and a measurable outcome (40% faster routing). What's missing: no mention of what the AI specifically did (classify? summarize? extract intent?) and zero judgment signal \u2014 no constraints navigated, no failure handling, no iteration story. Adding 'iterated on few-shot examples after initial accuracy dropped below 80% on edge-case tickets' would push this to 5/5 and make it nearly impossible to dismiss as ChatGPT tourism.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "ai-assisted-resume-refinement-process", "ai-detector-risk-mitigation-checklist", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "ai-career-board", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "jiorp-structure", "the-jagged-frontier-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI-Assisted Resume Refinement Process", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-step process for using AI tools to improve resume bullets without losing authenticity, specificity, or the author's voice.", "when_to_apply": ["I pasted my resume into ChatGPT and everything came back sounding like corporate word soup", "I know what I did at work but I can't figure out how to make it sound impressive without lying", "I want to tailor the same experience for different job postings without rewriting from scratch each time", "I'm preparing for interviews and want to identify the narrative threads across my resume bullets", "I'm worried my AI-polished resume will get flagged as generic or trigger AI detection concerns"], "principles": ["AI is a writing coach, not a ghostwriter \u2014 it needs tight input to produce useful output", "Start with honest, unpolished substance before giving anything to an AI tool", "Constrained, specific prompts produce better results than open-ended ones like 'make this better'", "Use AI to generate optionality (multiple framings) then select and polish manually", "The final voice must remain the author's \u2014 AI should sharpen, not replace, human expression", "Never let AI fabricate outcomes or metrics you did not actually achieve"], "steps": ["Write out what you actually did in plain, honest language \u2014 no polish, no performance, just the real description as you'd explain it to a colleague", "Give the AI tool specific, constrained instructions naming the desired output format, the role context, and explicit requirements (tool used, task completed, measurable outcome, appropriate tone)", "Ask the AI to generate 2\u20133 alternate framings of the same content (e.g., one more technical, one more impact-focused) to find the best fit for the target role", "Use the AI reflectively: feed in multiple bullets and ask it to identify themes, patterns, or what they reveal about how you work \u2014 use this for narrative summaries and interview prep"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "180818333", "title": "The AI Resume Survival Guide (for 2025 and Beyond)", "date": "2025-12-07T00:00:57.900Z"}], "date": "2025-12-07T00:00:57.900Z", "id": "ai-assisted-resume-refinement-process", "core_question": "How do I use AI to make my resume stronger without ending up with generic, inflated bullets that don't sound like me?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The raw input is 'I built a dashboard in Looker that the sales team used to track pipeline.' Step one produced honest substance. Step two constrains the AI: 'Rewrite this as a resume bullet for a data analyst role. Include the tool (Looker), the task (pipeline visibility for sales), and a measurable outcome (reduced weekly reporting time by 3 hours). Use a direct, non-corporate tone.' Step three generates three framings \u2014 one technical ('Designed and deployed a Looker dashboard integrating CRM data...'), one impact-focused ('Eliminated 3 hours/week of manual reporting for a 12-person sales team...'), one hybrid. The technical version fits the target role best but the impact framing surfaces a theme \u2014 this person consistently builds self-serve tools that remove bottlenecks. That theme, identified in step four, becomes the backbone of the summary section and a ready-made interview narrative.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "five-element-ai-resume-bullet-test", "ai-detector-risk-mitigation-checklist", "intent-engineering", "constraint-architecture", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "ai-leverage-audit"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Detector Risk Mitigation Checklist", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured set of checks and interventions to reduce the probability that a resume is falsely flagged as AI-generated by automated screening tools, by diagnosing low-perplexity and low-burstiness signals.", "when_to_apply": ["I used ChatGPT to help write my resume and I'm worried it reads like AI wrote it", "I ran my resume through an AI detector and it came back flagged \u2014 how do I fix it without starting over?", "My resume bullets all sound the same \u2014 polished but generic \u2014 and I'm applying to companies that use automated screening", "I want to make sure my resume sounds like me and not like averaged professional prose", "Every bullet on my resume follows the same structure and uses words like 'leveraged' and 'utilized'"], "principles": ["AI detectors flag predictability (low perplexity) and uniformity (low burstiness), not actual AI use", "Overly polished, safe writing scores worse on these metrics than messy, varied human writing", "Never paste AI-generated text directly into a resume \u2014 always rewrite in your own words", "Verb choice is a key signal: tactile, specific verbs (rebuilt, debugged, scrapped) score better than safe ones (leveraged, utilized, enhanced)", "Sentence rhythm variation \u2014 mixing short punchy phrases with longer complex clauses \u2014 increases burstiness", "A little imperfection builds credibility; perfect polish raises suspicion", "Re-exposing yourself to pre-AI human writing (old blogs, essays, novels) helps recalibrate your natural voice"], "steps": ["Run the final resume draft through a publicly available text-classification tool before submitting", "Identify which sections scored lowest on perplexity or burstiness", "Check for structural repetition: are multiple bullets following the same 'Built X using Y to achieve Z' pattern?", "Replace safe, generic verbs (leveraged, utilized, developed, enhanced) with verbs that reflect real action and struggle (rebuilt, debugged, restructured, scrapped, retrained)", "Vary sentence rhythm deliberately: mix short fragments with longer, winding clauses; break uniform pacing", "Rewrite any AI-drafted sections in your own voice before finalizing"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "180818333", "title": "The AI Resume Survival Guide (for 2025 and Beyond)", "date": "2025-12-07T00:00:57.900Z"}], "date": "2025-12-07T00:00:57.900Z", "id": "ai-detector-risk-mitigation-checklist", "core_question": "Is my resume going to get flagged as AI-generated by automated screening tools, even though I wrote it myself (or rewrote AI output in my own voice)?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The resume scores 87% AI-likelihood. The problem is concentrated in the experience section: every bullet follows 'Leveraged X to enhance Y, resulting in Z% improvement' \u2014 identical syntactic structure, safe verbs, no variation in sentence length. Replacing 'leveraged' with 'rebuilt,' 'enhanced' with 'overhauled,' breaking two bullets into short fragments ('Scrapped the old pipeline. Started over.'), and varying clause length drops the score to 31%. The perplexity issue was structural repetition, not content quality.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "five-element-ai-resume-bullet-test", "ai-assisted-resume-refinement-process", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "hardcoded_model_names", "note": "Found hardcoded model names: perplexity. Replace with capability descriptions (e.g., 'a model optimized for extended reasoning')."}]}
{"name": "Five Types of Cognitive Work", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that breaks any workflow into five distinct cognitive task types, each requiring different AI capabilities, to enable intelligent model routing rather than single-model generalism.", "when_to_apply": ["We're using one AI model for an entire multi-step workflow and the output quality is uneven \u2014 great at some parts, terrible at others", "I need to build a production pipeline with multiple AI models and I'm trying to figure out which model handles which piece", "Our AI generates polished-looking content but keeps getting the facts wrong or missing key details from source documents", "I'm trying to reduce AI costs without sacrificing quality and need to know where I can use cheaper models", "My team treats 'use AI for this' as a single decision but the workflow has very different kinds of thinking embedded in it"], "principles": ["AI fails at the task level, not the workflow level \u2014 most workflows contain multiple tasks masquerading as one", "Each cognitive type requires fundamentally different capabilities; no single model excels at all five", "Extraction (pulling structured info from unstructured sources) favors fast, cheap, smaller models", "Synthesis (combining many inputs into coherent patterns) favors models with large context windows", "Analysis (applying logic, calculations, or rules) favors precision-oriented reasoning models", "Generation (creating new content with voice/style) favors models with strong long-form writing capabilities", "Validation (checking correctness against rules) can be split between hard rule-checking models and nuanced review models", "Model routing decisions should be based on deliberate experimentation, not benchmarks or vendor claims", "These categories are starting points, not universal laws \u2014 domain and prompt design shape results significantly"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "180723134", "title": "My honest field notes on why AI implementations fail at the task level + the 10 prompt templates I built to fix it", "date": "2025-12-05T14:01:54.308Z"}], "date": "2025-12-05T14:01:54.308Z", "id": "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "core_question": "Why does my AI workflow produce inconsistent quality even though the model seems capable \u2014 am I treating fundamentally different cognitive tasks as if they're the same thing?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "A legal team's contract review workflow is actually four distinct cognitive tasks masquerading as one. Extracting key dates and parties from contracts is extraction \u2014 a fast, cheap model handles it perfectly. Comparing clause patterns across a portfolio is synthesis \u2014 that needs a large context window. Checking compliance against regulatory requirements is validation \u2014 a precision model with rule-checking. The team was running all four through GPT-4 and getting mediocre results on extraction (slow, expensive, no better than a smaller model) and terrible results on synthesis (context window too small for the full portfolio). Routing each task type to the right model cut costs 60% and improved accuracy on the compliance checks.", "related_frameworks": ["the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "model-context-matching-framework", "task-decomposition-framework", "work-primitives", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "capability-surface-mapping"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Task Decomposition Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured method for breaking any workflow into atomic, AI-ready tasks by identifying cognitive type, matching model capabilities, and routing each sub-task to the most appropriate tool rather than applying one model to the entire workflow.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm trying to automate a complex knowledge work process and my single-prompt approach keeps producing mediocre results that need heavy editing", "My team uses one AI model for everything and I suspect we're overpaying for simple tasks while underserving complex ones", "I need to design an AI workflow but the process involves very different types of thinking \u2014 some parts need precision, others need creativity", "We want to move from ad-hoc AI usage to a repeatable, teachable methodology our whole team can follow", "I'm building a multi-step AI pipeline and need to figure out which model to assign to which step"], "principles": ["Map what humans actually do step-by-step before designing any AI workflow", "Identify each distinct cognitive operation as a separate task, not as part of a single prompt", "Match each task to the cognitive type category it belongs to (extraction, synthesis, analysis, generation, validation)", "Assign models based on the cognitive type and the specific requirements of that task", "Treat the cost question as a math problem: measure time saved versus cost of additional tools", "Intuition for which model fits which task can only be built through deliberate, tracked experimentation \u2014 not documentation", "Professional-looking output is not a signal of correctness; validate outputs against the actual requirements of each task type"], "steps": ["Map the workflow by documenting every distinct step a skilled human performs, not the high-level description of the deliverable", "Identify the cognitive type for each step (extraction, synthesis, analysis, generation, or validation)", "Note the specific requirements of each task \u2014 precision needs, context volume, style consistency, rule complexity", "Match each task to a model suited to that cognitive type and those specific requirements", "Run deliberate experiments routing the same tasks to different models and tracking quality outcomes", "Build the multi-model pipeline with each task routed to its matched model", "Calculate actual cost versus time saved to validate the approach economically"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "180723134", "title": "My honest field notes on why AI implementations fail at the task level + the 10 prompt templates I built to fix it", "date": "2025-12-05T14:01:54.308Z"}, {"id": "180153995", "title": "This is The First Year AI Can Shop Black Friday But There are NO Prompts\u2014So I Fixed That With 6 Black Friday Prompts to Save You Hundreds", "date": "2025-11-28T15:01:49.761Z"}], "date": "2025-11-28T15:01:49.761Z", "id": "task-decomposition-framework", "core_question": "I want to use AI on this workflow, but it's too complex for a single prompt \u2014 how do I break it apart so each piece gets handled by the right tool?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A consulting firm's report-writing process actually decomposes into five distinct cognitive operations: extracting key metrics from client data (extraction), identifying patterns across quarters (analysis), synthesizing findings into a narrative (synthesis), generating client-facing prose (generation), and checking claims against source data (validation). Extraction and validation demand precision \u2014 a smaller, cheaper model with structured output handles these reliably. Synthesis and generation require nuance and style consistency \u2014 that's frontier model territory. Analysis sits in between and needs experimentation to route correctly. Running all five through GPT-4 costs 4x what the multi-model pipeline costs, and the single-model approach actually performs worse on extraction because the model over-elaborates instead of returning clean structured data.", "related_frameworks": ["five-types-of-cognitive-work", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "work-primitives", "model-context-matching-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "edge-first-automation-framework", "six-axes-of-hard-work-difficulty-decomposition-framework", "capability-surface-mapping", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "delegation-spec-drill"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Accuracy vs. Intelligence Failure Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "A diagnostic distinction between two fundamentally different AI failure modes \u2014 precision errors in rule application versus absence of contextual judgment \u2014 that produce identical symptoms (professional-looking but useless output) but require different fixes.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI draft has all the right data but an expert would say it completely misses the point", "I'm trying to decide whether to fix my prompt, switch models, or redesign how I've broken down the task", "Two different AI workflows are both producing useless output and I need to figure out if they're failing for the same reason", "The AI's analysis is technically correct but reads like it was written by someone who has never worked in this domain", "My outputs only reveal their problems when a subject matter expert reviews them \u2014 they pass surface-level checks fine"], "principles": ["Both failure modes produce the same symptom: professional-looking output that misses the point", "Accuracy failures occur when a model applies rules or calculations incorrectly \u2014 a precision problem fixable by better model selection or task isolation", "Intelligence failures occur when a model lacks the contextual judgment to interpret what correct data means \u2014 a reasoning problem requiring different task design", "Diagnosing the correct failure type before redesigning a workflow prevents solving the wrong problem", "A model can count without being able to see \u2014 statistical correctness and interpretive insight are separate capabilities"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "180723134", "title": "My honest field notes on why AI implementations fail at the task level + the 10 prompt templates I built to fix it", "date": "2025-12-05T14:01:54.308Z"}], "date": "2025-12-05T14:01:54.308Z", "id": "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "core_question": "My AI output looks polished but is completely missing the point \u2014 is the model getting the facts wrong, or is it incapable of understanding what the facts mean?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The financial summary the model produced has correct revenue figures and accurate growth percentages, but it completely missed that the growth is coming from a single customer who represents 60% of revenue \u2014 a concentration risk any analyst would flag immediately. This is an intelligence failure, not an accuracy failure. The numbers are right; the interpretation is absent. Switching to a better model won't fix this \u2014 the task needs to be restructured so that interpretive questions ('what does this data pattern mean for risk?') are separated from data extraction, and domain-specific judgment criteria are injected into the prompt as explicit evaluation lenses.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "task-decomposition-framework", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "correctness-definition-framework", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "five-types-of-cognitive-work"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Edge-First Automation Framework", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI workflow automation strategy by targeting the mechanical periphery (data prep, QA, synthesis, packaging, coordination) before the high-judgment core, to reduce failure risk and build organizational trust.", "when_to_apply": ["We tried to automate a complex workflow end-to-end and the project stalled or people lost confidence in it", "My team wants to use AI agents but we're not sure which part of the process to target first", "Stakeholders are skeptical about AI automation because a previous attempt broke something that worked fine before", "We have a high-judgment workflow surrounded by tedious manual steps and I need to figure out the sequencing", "Our AI automation project keeps expanding in scope and we need a principled way to constrain it"], "principles": ["Start with the edges (data prep, QA, synthesis, packaging, coordination) before touching the core", "Core workflows contain hidden complexity, tribal knowledge, and judgment that current models reliably underestimate", "Automation is an organizational trust exercise, not just a technical project \u2014 losing trust locks away the tribal knowledge you need", "Early wins should be fast (one to two weeks), semi-manual, and designed to capture 70-80% of cases while routing exceptions to humans", "Automate around human expertise, not through it \u2014 protect the craft by eliminating the tedium surrounding it", "Each edge automated builds the infrastructure, exception maps, and trust needed to eventually approach the core", "Do not attempt core automation until inputs are clean, edge-to-core boundaries are clear, exceptions are visible, operators trust the system, and edge automations run stably"], "steps": ["Pick a workflow you touch every week that matters to you or your team", "Map the five edge categories: data preparation, QA, synthesis, packaging, and coordination", "Identify where time and energy disappear in the pre- and post-core phases", "Select the simplest edge \u2014 not the most impressive, but the one with the fastest, clearest lift", "Build a semi-manual version-0 that automates the predictable 70-80% and flags the rest for human review", "Ship, observe real usage, and iterate based on what exceptions surface", "Stack multiple successful edge automations before assessing readiness to approach the core", "Run a readiness check on five dimensions before moving inward: clean inputs, clear edge-core boundary, visible exceptions, operator trust, and stable edge automations"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "180611641", "title": "I Finally Cracked Why AI Agent Projects Keep Failing\u2014And I Built a Workbook with 11 Prompts So You Can Fix Yours Tomorrow", "date": "2025-12-04T14:03:13.026Z"}], "date": "2025-12-04T14:03:13.026Z", "id": "edge-first-automation-framework", "core_question": "We want to automate this workflow with AI, but where do we actually start without blowing up the parts that depend on human judgment and tribal knowledge?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The conventional instinct is to automate the core underwriting decision \u2014 that's where the most labor-intensive judgment sits, so it looks like the biggest win. That's wrong here because the underwriting team's real pain isn't the decision itself; it's the 6 hours per case spent gathering documents, normalizing data formats, and chasing brokers for missing fields. Those are edges \u2014 data prep and coordination \u2014 where 80% of cases follow a predictable pattern. Automating those first delivers immediate time savings, builds the team's trust that AI handles their inputs reliably, and surfaces the exception patterns (incomplete appraisals, non-standard property types) that any future core automation would need to handle anyway.", "related_frameworks": ["five-edge-categories", "core-automation-readiness-check", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "the-simple-wins-method", "blast-radius-assessment", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "task-decomposition-framework", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "ai-leverage-audit", "scout-vs-strike-team-framework", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Five Edge Categories", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system for the five types of automatable peripheral work that surround any high-judgment core workflow, used to systematically identify where to start an automation initiative.", "when_to_apply": ["I can see that our team spends most of their time on stuff around the actual work, not the work itself, and I want to systematically catalog what's automatable", "We're starting an automation initiative and need to prioritize which peripheral tasks to tackle first across a complex workflow", "I keep hearing 'automate the edges' but I need a concrete taxonomy to make sure I'm identifying all the edge work, not just the obvious stuff", "Our knowledge workers are drowning in prep work, QA, and formatting \u2014 I need to classify these tasks to build a sequenced automation roadmap", "I want to audit a workflow to find where current LLM capabilities can actually deliver value without requiring deep domain judgment"], "principles": ["Data preparation: collecting, cleaning, normalizing, and reconciling inputs before the core work begins", "QA: verifying completeness, consistency, and absence of obvious errors \u2014 automatable without understanding the full workflow", "Synthesis: summarizing, consolidating, or translating information from sprawling sources into actionable formats", "Packaging: transforming finished work into the required deliverable format for the next stage", "Coordination: moving context between systems or people, eliminating the human translation layer in handoffs", "All five categories share the profile of low-judgment, well-defined outputs, and inputs that are already ready \u2014 making them suitable for automation even with imperfect models"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "180611641", "title": "I Finally Cracked Why AI Agent Projects Keep Failing\u2014And I Built a Workbook with 11 Prompts So You Can Fix Yours Tomorrow", "date": "2025-12-04T14:03:13.026Z"}], "date": "2025-12-04T14:03:13.026Z", "id": "five-edge-categories", "core_question": "I know I should automate the edges of this workflow, not the core \u2014 but which edges specifically, and how do I make sure I'm not missing a whole category of low-hanging fruit?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A legal team's contract review process has five distinct edge categories consuming human hours. Data preparation \u2014 pulling clause libraries and prior agreements from three different systems \u2014 takes 40% of paralegal time. Synthesis \u2014 consolidating redline comments from four stakeholders into a single change summary \u2014 takes another 25%. Both are low-judgment, well-defined-output tasks with inputs already available. Packaging (reformatting final agreements into client-specific templates) and coordination (emailing status updates between internal counsel and outside firms) round out the automation surface. QA on formatting consistency is trivially automatable. The core judgment \u2014 whether a clause creates unacceptable risk \u2014 stays human. Starting with data preparation yields the highest time savings with the lowest verification burden.", "related_frameworks": ["edge-first-automation-framework", "core-automation-readiness-check", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "task-decomposition-framework", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-simple-wins-method", "work-primitives", "delegation-spec-drill", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "the-verifiability-spectrum"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Core Automation Readiness Check", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A five-dimension readiness assessment that determines whether a team has earned the right to automate a workflow's high-judgment core after successfully automating its edges.", "when_to_apply": ["We've had some wins automating the simpler parts of our workflow and leadership wants us to automate the high-judgment center next", "Our last attempt to automate the core decision-making step of a process failed and we're trying to understand why", "The team is debating whether to start an automation project at the most valuable step or work inward from the periphery", "Edge automations are running but I'm not sure the preconditions are actually met to go deeper", "Operators who do the core work are skeptical of expanding automation scope and I need to assess whether their concerns are legitimate"], "principles": ["Rushing the core before preconditions are met costs more time than waiting", "All five dimensions must be satisfied \u2014 partial readiness is not readiness", "Readiness is earned through successful edge automation, not assumed from technical capability"], "steps": ["Assess whether inputs to the core are now clean and reliable (from edge automation of data prep)", "Assess whether the boundary between edge work and core work is clearly defined", "Assess whether exceptions that were previously invisible are now visible and mapped", "Assess whether the operators who do the core work trust the automation system you have built", "Assess whether your edge automations run stably without constant human intervention", "If all five are yes, proceed to core automation; if any are no, continue building edges until they are"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "180611641", "title": "I Finally Cracked Why AI Agent Projects Keep Failing\u2014And I Built a Workbook with 11 Prompts So You Can Fix Yours Tomorrow", "date": "2025-12-04T14:03:13.026Z"}], "date": "2025-12-04T14:03:13.026Z", "id": "core-automation-readiness-check", "core_question": "We've automated the easy parts of this workflow and now everyone wants to tackle the hard middle \u2014 but have we actually earned the right to do that, or are we about to break something expensive?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The team's edge automations for data ingestion and formatting are running cleanly, and the boundary between prep work and underwriting judgment is well-defined. But the underwriters don't trust the system yet \u2014 they've been burned by silent failures twice \u2014 and the exception taxonomy is still incomplete (three new edge cases surfaced last week that nobody had mapped). Two of five dimensions fail. The right move is to keep running edges, build the exception map, and earn operator trust before touching core underwriting logic. Attempting core automation now would produce a system that looks functional in demos but collapses under real exception load.", "related_frameworks": ["edge-first-automation-framework", "five-edge-categories", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "the-50-80-gap-operating-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "the-factory-curve", "ambient-failure-cascade", "task-decomposition-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Model-Context Matching Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Classifies AI models by their optimization target (insight/coherence/precision) and matches them to task contexts (messy vs. clean inputs, iterative vs. one-shot outputs) to route work effectively.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep getting mediocre results and I suspect I'm using the wrong model for this type of task", "Building a multi-step workflow where different stages seem to need different AI strengths", "My input is messy \u2014 handwritten notes, ambiguous documents, chaotic data \u2014 and I need to pick the right model to handle it", "I need high precision on a technical problem and want to know if my current model is optimized for that", "I'm trying to decide between Claude, GPT, and Gemini for a specific project and need a principled way to choose"], "principles": ["Different models optimize for different targets: interpretation (insight), reconstruction (coherence), or abstraction (precision)", "Every strength implies a corresponding weakness \u2014 you cannot optimize for everything simultaneously", "The meaningful question is not 'which model is best?' but 'which model's tradeoffs match this context?'", "Identify your context first (clean vs. messy, volume, type of mess), then identify your task requirement (insight, precision, or coherence), then match the stance", "Use models in sequence for complex workflows rather than relying on a single model end-to-end", "Frameworks help set default choices but do not eliminate the need to test and observe \u2014 models are discovered, not known"], "steps": ["Identify your context: Is the input clean or messy? What kind of mess (visual noise, ambiguity, volume)? How much volume?", "Identify your task requirement: Do you need insight and narrative synthesis, precision and correctness, or coherence and iterative refinement?", "Match the stance: use an interpretation-optimized model for insight tasks, an abstraction-optimized model for precision tasks, a reconstruction-optimized model for coherence tasks", "Test and observe: run the task, notice what works and what doesn't, update your mental model of the model"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179894697", "title": "I Tested Opus 4.5 Early\u2014Here's Where It Can Save You HOURS on Complex Workflows + a Comparison vs. Gemini 3 and ChatGPT 5.1 + a Model-Picker Prompt + 15 Workflows to Get Started Now", "date": "2025-11-25T14:01:54.220Z"}], "date": "2025-11-25T14:01:54.220Z", "id": "model-context-matching-framework", "core_question": "I have a specific task with specific input conditions \u2014 which AI model's tradeoffs actually fit what I need, rather than just defaulting to whatever model I used last?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This task involves synthesizing 40 pages of messy handwritten meeting notes into a coherent executive summary. The input is noisy and ambiguous (messy context), and the output needs narrative synthesis (insight). That's an interpretation-optimized model \u2014 something like Claude or GPT-4 in high-context mode, not a precision-optimized model that will choke on the ambiguity or hallucinate structure that isn't there. If the next step is converting that summary into a formatted slide deck with exact figures, that's a different stance entirely \u2014 precision on clean input \u2014 and should be routed to a different model or a second pass with different instructions.", "related_frameworks": ["the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "three-stances-toward-mess", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "task-decomposition-framework", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "capability-surface-mapping", "model-vs-harness-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Stances Toward Mess", "type": "distinction", "description": "Identifies three fundamentally different behavioral stances AI models take when given ambiguous or messy inputs \u2014 interpret, reconstruct, or abstract \u2014 which predict both their strengths and failure modes.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI keeps hallucinating structure in messy handwritten or OCR-scanned documents instead of admitting what it can't read", "I'm choosing between models for a task involving contradictory or noisy source data and I need to predict which will fail less", "A model that performs great on clean inputs is producing nonsense on ambiguous real-world versions of the same task", "I need to understand whether a model failure came from the input being too messy or from the model handling mess the wrong way", "Two models give very different outputs on the same messy input and I need to understand why and which to trust"], "principles": ["The 'interpret' stance asks 'What's the story here?' \u2014 excels at finding meaning in chaos but risks internal inconsistency", "The 'reconstruct' stance asks 'What's actually there?' \u2014 excels at accuracy and acknowledging uncertainty but can be conservative", "The 'abstract' stance asks 'What structure should this follow?' \u2014 excels at clean technical problems but fabricates structure when inputs are too messy", "Not all messiness is the same: some messy tasks reward interpretation, others reward faithful counting \u2014 the stance must match the type of mess", "A model's stance is revealed by how it behaves when clean inputs become ambiguous, not by benchmark performance"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179894697", "title": "I Tested Opus 4.5 Early\u2014Here's Where It Can Save You HOURS on Complex Workflows + a Comparison vs. Gemini 3 and ChatGPT 5.1 + a Model-Picker Prompt + 15 Workflows to Get Started Now", "date": "2025-11-25T14:01:54.220Z"}], "date": "2025-11-25T14:01:54.220Z", "id": "three-stances-toward-mess", "core_question": "Why does this model keep failing on my messy, real-world inputs when it scored well on clean benchmarks \u2014 and which model would actually handle this kind of mess well?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The model is taking an abstract stance toward handwritten inventory logs \u2014 it's imposing a clean schema and fabricating counts where the handwriting is illegible rather than flagging uncertainty. This is a reconstruct task: the goal is faithful data extraction, not pattern completion. A model with an interpret stance would narrative-fill the gaps (also wrong here), but a reconstruct-biased model would flag ambiguous entries and preserve what's actually legible. The failure isn't a capability gap \u2014 it's a stance mismatch between the model's optimization target and what this type of mess actually requires.", "related_frameworks": ["the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "jagged-frontier-as-measurement-error", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "model-context-matching-framework", "the-off-manifold-probe", "contextual-noise-library-factorial-stress-testing-for-agents", "capability-surface-mapping", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "model-vs-harness-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Prompt Lifecycle Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A six-stage classification of prompt work that maps where in the lifecycle a problem occurs and which tools serve each stage, with special emphasis on the missing Stage 1 (intent formation).", "when_to_apply": ["I've rewritten this prompt many times and the output still isn't right \u2014 I don't know what else to change", "We're evaluating prompt engineering tools and can't tell which ones solve which problems", "Our team is scaling up prompt usage and we need to figure out what infrastructure each phase requires", "I want to diagnose whether a prompt problem is conceptual or just bad wording", "We're moving from ad hoc individual prompting to a managed team workflow and need to understand what stages exist"], "principles": ["Prompts have a lifecycle analogous to code: from fuzzy intent to production artifact", "Most prompt failures originate at Stage 1 (intent formation) but are treated as Stage 2 (drafting) problems", "Each stage requires different tools; using a Stage 2 tool on a Stage 1 problem cannot fix the underlying issue", "Intent formation is specification work, not just thinking \u2014 it benefits from structured tooling", "The individual-team divide matters: Stages 4\u20136 assume engineering resources, while Stage 1 cuts across both contexts", "Downstream quality depends entirely on upstream clarity of intent"], "steps": ["Stage 1 \u2014 Intent Formation: Translate fuzzy goals into structured requirements (objectives, constraints, output formats, edge cases) before any wording begins", "Stage 2 \u2014 Authoring and Drafting: Turn structured intent into actual prompt language; iterate on wording, role definitions, examples, and tone", "Stage 3 \u2014 Storage and Versioning: Treat proven prompts as reusable assets; version-control, diff, name, and collaborate on them", "Stage 4 \u2014 Evaluation and Testing: Build regression tests, compare outputs across models, and assess reliability and cost systematically", "Stage 5 \u2014 Workflow Construction and Automation: Wire prompts into conditional logic, orchestrators, and multi-step agent workflows", "Stage 6 \u2014 Production Deployment: Run prompts at scale with logging, monitoring, cost tracking, and governance"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179769727", "title": "Prompting is Too Hard: Check Out My Easy Prompt Lifecycle Guide + 19 Prompting Tools + a Custom Prompt to Analyze your Prompting Strengths & Weaknesses", "date": "2025-11-24T14:02:59.306Z"}], "date": "2025-11-24T14:02:59.306Z", "id": "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "core_question": "My prompt isn't working and I've rewritten it five times \u2014 is the problem actually in how I worded it, or did I never properly define what I wanted in the first place?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The marketing team has iterated on their product description prompt twelve times, tweaking tone words, adding examples, and restructuring the template \u2014 but outputs still miss the mark. The problem is Stage 1, not Stage 2: they never specified whether the description targets technical buyers or end consumers, never defined the three claims that must appear, and never articulated what 'good' looks like beyond a vague sense of quality. No amount of wording refinement can fix an underspecified intent. The fix is to step back and produce a structured spec \u2014 audience, required claims, format constraints, success criteria \u2014 before touching the prompt language again.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "prompt-lifecycle-diagnostic-checklist", "the-five-specification-primitives", "delegation-spec-drill", "intent-doc", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "done-definition-pattern", "correctness-definition-framework", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "delegation-prompt-management-system", "constraint-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Prompt Lifecycle Diagnostic Checklist", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A branching symptom-to-stage mapping that helps practitioners locate where in the prompt lifecycle their problem actually lives, so they apply the right fix rather than the wrong tool.", "when_to_apply": ["I've rewritten this prompt a dozen times and it's still not working \u2014 I don't know what else to try", "My prompt works great in the playground but fails in production and I can't figure out why", "Output quality from our agent has been degrading over the past few weeks even though we haven't changed the prompt", "An agentic workflow is producing errors and I'm not sure if the issue is the prompt, the orchestration, or something else entirely", "My team keeps applying prompt engineering tips but nothing improves \u2014 we might be fixing the wrong thing"], "principles": ["Match the symptom to the stage before reaching for a tool", "Wordsmithing cannot fix an under-specified task \u2014 goal clarity must come before wording", "Reliability drift is an evaluation problem, not a drafting problem", "Production failures may live in orchestration or deployment layers, not in the prompt text itself"], "steps": ["Ask: Is the goal unclear or under-specified? \u2192 Stage 1 problem; define objectives, constraints, and output format before rewording", "Ask: Is the wording messy or ambiguous despite clear intent? \u2192 Stage 2 problem; use drafting tools or optimizers", "Ask: Is output reliability drifting across runs? \u2192 Stage 4 problem; build test cases and run evaluations", "Ask: Is the prompt failing in production context? \u2192 Stage 5 or 6 problem; check orchestration logic, monitoring, and deployment configuration"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "179769727", "title": "Prompting is Too Hard: Check Out My Easy Prompt Lifecycle Guide + 19 Prompting Tools + a Custom Prompt to Analyze your Prompting Strengths & Weaknesses", "date": "2025-11-24T14:02:59.306Z"}], "date": "2025-11-24T14:02:59.306Z", "id": "prompt-lifecycle-diagnostic-checklist", "core_question": "My prompt isn't working and I've been tweaking the wording for an hour \u2014 is the wording actually the problem, or is something else broken that no amount of rewording will fix?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The team has rewritten their summarization prompt twelve times, but outputs still vary wildly between runs. The wording is actually fine \u2014 the task specification is clear, the format is defined, and a single run produces good results. The real problem is Stage 4: there are no evaluation test cases, so they have no way to detect or measure the drift. They need a regression suite, not another prompt rewrite. The fix is to build five canonical input-output pairs and run automated checks, not to add more instructions to the system prompt.", "related_frameworks": ["the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "intent-engineering", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "constraint-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Use Spectrum: Tool vs. Replacement", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between using AI to enhance human capability (tool) versus using AI to substitute for human thinking, feeling, or relating (replacement), with specific good/bad/gray-area use cases for each.", "when_to_apply": ["My kid is using ChatGPT for homework and I can't tell if it's helping them learn or doing the learning for them", "Someone is processing difficult emotions primarily through conversations with an AI companion instead of talking to people", "I want to set household or classroom rules about AI use but need a principled basis for where to draw the line", "A team member is using AI to draft communications and I'm noticing their own writing and thinking skills are atrophying", "I'm trying to coach someone on productive AI prompting habits that preserve their own skill development"], "principles": ["AI helps when it enhances human capability; it harms when it replaces human experience", "Use AI to learn, not to avoid learning", "AI can be a starting point for research but should not be the endpoint", "Emotional processing, identity formation, and conflict resolution require human relationships, not AI", "The key question is always: who is doing the thinking?"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "179592990", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z"}], "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z", "id": "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "core_question": "Is this use of AI helping someone build capability, or is it quietly replacing the thinking, feeling, or relating they need to do themselves?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A teenager asks ChatGPT to write their college essay about a formative experience. This is replacement, not tool use \u2014 the tell is that the essay's entire value lies in the student processing their own experience and finding their own voice. AI as a brainstorming partner to overcome blank-page paralysis is tool use; AI as the author of a personal narrative is replacement. The question 'who is doing the thinking?' answers it immediately: the AI is doing the thinking, and the student is losing the developmental benefit the assignment was designed to produce.", "related_frameworks": ["human-hierarchy-for-emotional-support", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-diagnostic", "ai-literacy-parenting-protocols", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "experiential-debt", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "knowledge-work-practice-gym"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Human Hierarchy for Emotional Support", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A ranked sequence of support resources that children should exhaust in order before turning to AI for emotional help, ensuring human connection remains primary.", "when_to_apply": ["My kid talks to an AI chatbot when they're sad instead of coming to me or their friends", "I need household rules about when AI interaction is and isn't appropriate for emotional topics", "I want to teach my child to self-regulate before seeking external validation from any source, including AI", "A child is using AI as a therapist substitute and I'm worried about what they're losing developmentally", "I'm building a family protocol for how and when kids can use AI tools"], "principles": ["Feelings need human witness; pain requires real compassion", "AI should be a last resort for emotional support, not the first", "Each tier builds different capacities: self-regulation, trusted relationships, broader social skills", "AI use for serious emotional issues should never happen without adult guidance"], "steps": ["Self-soothing (breathing, movement, journaling)", "Trusted adult (parent, counselor, teacher)", "Close friend", "Broader social network", "Only then, if at all, AI \u2014 and never alone for serious issues"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179592990", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z"}], "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z", "id": "human-hierarchy-for-emotional-support", "core_question": "When my child is upset, how do I make sure they develop real emotional resilience and human bonds instead of defaulting to an AI chatbot for comfort?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "A twelve-year-old is anxious about a school presentation and opens ChatGPT to talk through their feelings. This is a Tier 1/Tier 2 situation \u2014 the child hasn't attempted self-soothing (deep breathing, journaling the worry) or reached out to a trusted adult. The AI is filling a role that, if it becomes habitual, prevents the child from building internal regulation capacity and deepening trust with the humans around them. The right move is to redirect: help the child name the feeling, try a breathing exercise together, and only if the anxiety persists after human connection, consider whether a structured AI exercise (with a parent present) adds anything.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-literacy-parenting-protocols", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-diagnostic", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Yellow Flag / Red Flag AI Dependency Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A two-tier signal catalog that helps parents distinguish between AI use patterns that warrant a conversation (yellow flags) versus patterns requiring immediate professional intervention (red flags).", "when_to_apply": ["My kid gets visibly upset or anxious when I take away access to their AI chatbot", "I found my child having late-night conversations with an AI about their feelings instead of talking to us or their friends", "My child's teacher says their essays sound nothing like how they write in class, and they've also been withdrawing socially", "My teenager describes their AI assistant as someone who 'really gets them' better than any human does", "I'm not sure if my child's AI use is normal exploration or something I should be worried about"], "principles": ["Yellow flags indicate AI use is becoming problematic and require conversation and boundary adjustment, not punishment", "Red flags indicate AI use is symptomatic of deeper issues requiring professional support", "Secretive use, emotional dependency, and reality blurring are early warning signs", "Crisis consultation with AI and isolation acceleration are immediate intervention triggers", "Address the unmet need (loneliness, anxiety) rather than just restricting the symptom"], "steps": ["Observe for yellow flags: preference for AI over human interaction, emotional dependency on AI availability, treating AI as sentient, secretive use, academic output mismatch", "If yellow flags present: initiate non-judgmental conversation and adjust boundaries collaboratively", "Observe for red flags: using AI for mental health crises or self-harm ideation, complete social isolation, genuine belief in AI sentience, harmful validation loops, identity fusion with AI", "If red flags present: seek professional support immediately; increase authentic human connection; do not shame or punish"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179592990", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z"}], "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z", "id": "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-diagnostic", "core_question": "Is my child's relationship with AI still a manageable habit that needs a conversation, or has it crossed into something that requires professional help right now?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "A 13-year-old has started referring to Claude as 'my best friend' and becomes irritable when screen time ends \u2014 but still attends school, sees friends on weekends, and produces work consistent with their ability. This is yellow-flag territory: the emotional dependency on AI availability and the anthropomorphization are warning signs, but the child still maintains human relationships and academic integrity. The right move is a non-judgmental conversation exploring what needs the AI is meeting \u2014 likely a feeling of being understood without social risk \u2014 and collaboratively adjusting boundaries, not confiscating devices. If the child were instead refusing all social contact and had confided suicidal ideation to the chatbot instead of a trusted adult, that would be a red flag requiring immediate professional intervention.", "related_frameworks": ["human-hierarchy-for-emotional-support", "ai-literacy-parenting-protocols", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Literacy Parenting Protocols", "type": "procedure", "description": "A set of named, repeatable household practices that build children's critical AI literacy, emotional independence, and cognitive self-reliance through structured exercises and conversation rituals.", "when_to_apply": ["My kid turned in great homework but couldn't answer a single follow-up question about it", "I want household AI rules that my children understand the reasoning behind, not just arbitrary screen-time limits", "My teenager is going to an AI chatbot for emotional support instead of talking to family or friends", "I'm trying to figure out age-appropriate AI access without being either negligent or paranoid", "My child accepts everything ChatGPT says as fact and doesn't think to verify any of it"], "principles": ["Transparency over secrecy: AI use should be visible and discussable, not hidden", "Purpose before access: state the goal before opening an AI tool", "Human first: emotions, decisions, and conflicts go to humans before AI", "Verify everything consequential with three independent sources", "Think before you prompt: write your own answer before asking AI", "If you used AI to learn it, you must be able to explain it without AI"], "steps": ["Show Your Work Rule: make AI conversations visible; review them together as collaboration, not surveillance", "Human First Protocol: for emotions, relationships, or major decisions, consult humans before AI", "Citation Needed Standard: verify any AI factual claim with independent sources before accepting it", "Time Boxing Approach: designate specific times when AI use is appropriate; prohibit AI during emotional distress or late-night hours", "Purpose Declaration: require the child to state their goal before opening an AI tool; close it when the goal is met", "Think First, Check Second: have the child write their own answer before querying AI; compare the two", "Explanation Challenge: after AI-assisted learning, require the child to explain the concept in their own words without screens", "Weekly Review: review AI interaction history together on a recurring basis with no judgment, only awareness", "AI Sabbath: designate one day per week with no AI use to maintain baseline human functioning"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "179592990", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z"}], "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z", "id": "ai-literacy-parenting-protocols", "core_question": "How do I let my kids use AI without it quietly replacing the thinking, feeling, and verifying they need to develop as humans?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "A 12-year-old submitted a science essay that reads well but can't explain the greenhouse effect when asked at dinner. This is cognitive offloading \u2014 the Explanation Challenge applies. The child rewrites the core argument from memory, then compares it to the AI-assisted version. The gap between them is the actual learning deficit. Going forward, the Think First, Check Second rule means the child drafts answers before prompting, so AI becomes a revision tool rather than a ghostwriter. The weekly review surfaces that late-night AI conversations have become emotionally confessional \u2014 triggering the Human First Protocol and Time Boxing to redirect those needs to a parent or counselor.", "related_frameworks": ["human-hierarchy-for-emotional-support", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-diagnostic", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "llm-psychosis-self-audit-framework", "verification-frontier", "experiential-debt", "the-verifiability-spectrum"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Tool Failure Mode Catalog", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumeration of seven predictable failure modes that occur when AI tools are deployed without pre-planned mitigations, each with a concrete signal and a specific architectural fix to implement before deployment.", "when_to_apply": ["We're about to deploy an AI tool to production and I want to know what's going to go wrong before it does", "Our AI chatbot started giving wrong answers and we need to figure out which category of failure this is", "I'm building the monitoring and alerting layer for our AI tool stack and need to know what failure modes to instrument for", "We're evaluating a vendor's AI product and I need to assess whether they've mitigated the known failure classes", "Our AI tool costs spiked unexpectedly and I'm trying to understand the architectural root cause"], "principles": ["Failure modes are predictable \u2014 what varies is whether teams plan for them before deployment", "Every failure mode has a known mitigation \u2014 skipping pre-deployment planning is a choice, not a surprise", "Hallucinated structure requires schema enforcement with retries and temperature-zero extraction, not just error handling", "Guardrail drift requires threshold tuning by channel and shadow-mode validation before enforcement is enabled", "RAG wrongness requires freshness policies, mandatory citations, and coverage alerts \u2014 confidence without source is a red flag", "Per-resolution pricing shock requires hard caps in contract and routing rules before scaling begins", "Memory contamination requires scoped entity-level isolation and a 'forget' endpoint built in from day one", "Agent-to-human handoff failure requires warm transfer with context payload \u2014 customers should never repeat themselves", "Tool sprawl requires a single observability layer routing all tool alerts with consistent severities and runbooks"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178643115", "title": "Grab My Prompt To Help Find the 1% of AI Tools That Actually Work (+50 Tool Reviews to Get Started)", "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z"}, {"id": "184882888", "title": "50 people ignored my tool recommendations + here's why their systems still worked (Second Brain follow-up)", "date": "2026-01-19T14:01:44.382Z"}], "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z", "id": "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "core_question": "What's going to break when we put this AI tool into production, and do we have the architectural safeguards in place before it does?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The active failure mode is RAG wrongness. The support bot is returning answers with high confidence but no citations, and the knowledge base hasn't been refreshed in six weeks. Two customers received contradictory policy guidance this month. The fix is a freshness policy with a 72-hour staleness threshold, mandatory source citations surfaced in every response, and a coverage alert that fires when queries hit topics with no matching documents \u2014 confidence without a traceable source is treated as a system failure, not a feature gap.", "related_frameworks": ["agent-failure-pattern-catalog", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "blast-radius-assessment", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "9-ai-failure-patterns", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "ambient-failure-cascade", "architecture-first-reliability-pattern", "ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "function-specific-quality-gates", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Question AI Tool Evaluation Framework", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured three-question diagnostic that determines whether an AI tool is worth buying by requiring measurable pain definition, honest integration assessment, and explicit worst-case failure planning before any purchase decision.", "when_to_apply": ["A vendor just gave us a compelling demo and the team is excited to buy \u2014 I need a structured way to pressure-test whether this actually solves a real problem", "We have AI tools in our stack that nobody can articulate the measurable value of anymore", "I'm building the business case for an AI tool purchase and need to define clear success criteria and kill criteria for a 14-day pilot", "Someone on my team wants to adopt an AI tool but can't name who would own it operationally or what happens when it breaks", "We need a lightweight but rigorous gate to prevent AI tool sprawl across the organization"], "principles": ["Default to no \u2014 the tool must earn its way into your stack, not the other way around", "Pain must be named in one concrete sentence with a measurable metric before evaluation proceeds", "Integration requires naming a specific owner and a runbook, not just a team", "Failure modes must be stated in concrete terms (e.g., 'data leak,' 'corrupted records') not vague ones (e.g., 'security incident')", "If the worst-case failure is existential and cannot be architecturally contained, do not deploy", "Aspirations are not pain points \u2014 require before-and-after measurement criteria", "Tools that compound value across multiple workflows get prioritized over single-use tools"], "steps": ["Question 1 \u2014 Does it kill a pain we can measure? Write one sentence following the formula: 'Tool X reduces [specific metric] caused by [concrete problem] in [precise context].' If you cannot fill in real numbers and real scenarios, stop here.", "Question 2 \u2014 Can we integrate and sustain this tool? Name a concrete owner (specific person, not a team), write a runbook, and run a mock incident (something breaks at 2am Saturday \u2014 who gets paged, what do they do?). Kill criterion: if the tool needs bespoke glue code, manual babysitting more than twice weekly, or touches more than three teams to keep running, reject it.", "Question 3 \u2014 What is the worst failure mode and can we stomach it? Name the specific worst case in concrete terms, document the guardrails (scoped permissions, rate limits, canary tests), and decide explicitly whether the risk is acceptable. Kill criterion: if worst case is existential and cannot be boxed through architectural constraints, do not deploy.", "Bonus check \u2014 Does this tool make other workflows better automatically? Prioritize tools that are compounding primitives (value across multiple use cases beyond the one you bought it for).", "Scorecard \u2014 For each question, write three things: what you are claiming the tool will do, how you will measure it in 14 days, and what the kill criterion is. Require at least 20\u201330% improvement on the primary metric with statistical significance to pass the pilot."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178643115", "title": "Grab My Prompt To Help Find the 1% of AI Tools That Actually Work (+50 Tool Reviews to Get Started)", "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z"}, {"id": "183624198", "title": "Meta bought Manus for $2B to acquire an \"agentic harness\"\u2014here's what \"agentic harness\" means and why it's worth $2B (yes really)", "date": "2026-01-06T14:03:30.662Z"}], "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z", "id": "three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "core_question": "Should we actually buy this AI tool, or are we about to add expensive shelfware to our stack because the demo looked impressive?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The team wants to adopt an AI-powered document extraction tool. Applying Question 1: the pain is 'Tool X reduces the 14 hours/week spent manually re-keying invoice data caused by incompatible PDF formats in accounts payable.' That's concrete and measurable \u2014 passes. Question 2: the named owner is Sarah Chen, but the tool requires custom regex glue code per vendor format and touches AP, IT, and compliance to stay running \u2014 three teams plus bespoke glue code. That trips the kill criterion. The evaluation stops here: this tool will become an orphaned maintenance burden regardless of how well it extracts data. Recommendation is to reject or wait for a version with native format handling.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "ai-leverage-audit", "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "integration-complexity-vs-advantage-2x2-portfolio-strategy", "data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "tool_specific_config", "note": "2 steps/principles contain tool-specific CLI syntax or config references. Rewrite to tool-agnostic reasoning steps."}]}
{"name": "Entropy-Matching Model Selection Framework", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between two types of AI prompt uncertainty\u2014context entropy (messy inputs) and task entropy (complex, open-ended jobs)\u2014and maps each to the model best suited to resolve it.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm feeding messy multi-source data into an LLM and the outputs are confident but wrong", "Trying to decide whether to use one big prompt or chain multiple model calls together", "My complex analytical prompt works great with clean inputs but falls apart with real-world data dumps", "Choosing between Gemini and ChatGPT for a specific workflow and unsure which handles my use case better", "I keep getting shallow or hallucinated outputs even though my task instructions seem clear"], "principles": ["Every model call has a limited analysis budget\u2014it can spend it reducing input messiness or solving complex tasks, but doing both at once degrades quality", "Context entropy = uncertainty in inputs (mixed formats, raw dumps, competing signals); task entropy = uncertainty in the job (vague objectives, multiple constraints, unclear success criteria)", "Match the model to the dominant entropy type: use a model strong at context compression for messy inputs, use a model strong at reasoning/narrative for complex but clean inputs", "Lower one side of entropy yourself so the model can spend full capacity on the other side", "Never ask a model to simultaneously clean up messy input and solve an ambiguous high-stakes problem"], "steps": ["Identify whether your primary challenge is context entropy (chaotic, multimodal, or voluminous inputs) or task entropy (open-ended reasoning, planning, narrative, multi-step decisions)", "If context entropy dominates: pre-process inputs with a context-compression model\u2014feed it messy multimodal data with a tight output schema to produce structured artifacts", "If task entropy dominates: use a reasoning-oriented model with clean, pre-structured inputs so it can spend capacity on thinking and writing", "For high-complexity workflows, chain the two: use a context-compression model first to extract structured signal, then feed that output to a reasoning model for synthesis, narrative, or decision-making", "Evaluate results: if outputs are confident but fuzzy or miss the mark, diagnose whether you left both entropy types high simultaneously and restructure accordingly"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179418126", "title": "My Lab Notes on Gemini 3 vs. ChatGPT 5.1: How To Prompt EACH Model + A Custom Meta-Prompt to Build YOUR Prompts", "date": "2025-11-20T14:02:54.523Z"}], "date": "2025-11-20T14:02:54.523Z", "id": "entropy-matching-model-selection-framework", "core_question": "My AI outputs are mediocre \u2014 is the model struggling because my inputs are a mess, because the task itself is too ambiguous, or both at once?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This workflow has both entropy types high simultaneously \u2014 the user is dumping raw meeting transcripts, Slack threads, and a spreadsheet into a single prompt and asking for a strategic recommendation. The dominant entropy is context (mixed formats, competing signals), not task (the deliverable is well-defined once the inputs are clean). The fix is a two-stage chain: first, run the raw inputs through a context-compression model with a tight schema to extract structured claims and data points, then feed that clean artifact to a reasoning model for the strategic synthesis. Asking one model to do both is why the outputs keep hallucinating details from the wrong transcript.", "related_frameworks": ["the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "see-or-do-vs-write-or-talk-model-routing", "model-context-matching-framework", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "double-compression-loop", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "technique-to-task-decision-matrix", "retrieval-vs-reasoning-tool-selection", "hierarchical-context-loading", "goldilocks-prompting", "routing-layer-specification-layer-review-layer"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "See-or-Do vs. Write-or-Talk Model Routing", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that routes work to the appropriate AI model based on whether the task is primarily visual/agentic ('see or do') versus linguistic/persuasive ('write or talk').", "when_to_apply": ["We have access to multiple AI models and need a principled way to decide which tasks go where", "A new multimodal model just launched and teams are asking whether they should switch their existing workflows to it", "We're building a routing layer for our enterprise AI platform and need classification logic for task assignment", "Our AI token costs are climbing and I suspect we're using expensive models for tasks that don't need them", "Where should our team's 'eyes on glass' work \u2014 reviewing dashboards, screenshots, videos \u2014 get routed versus our writing tasks?"], "principles": ["If the task involves seeing or doing (screens, video, large mixed-context blobs), route to a highly capable multimodal model", "If the task involves writing or persuading (narratives, emails, PRDs, brand voice), route to a model optimized for language and tone", "If the task is cheap bulk work with low stakes, use a smaller, faster, less expensive model to avoid burning budget unnecessarily", "The bottleneck question for each team is: where do you have 'eyes-on-glass' work today?", "Do not migrate workflows that are already text-only and working well just because a new model is available", "The ROI calculation should compare human time saved versus token cost and latency for each candidate workflow"], "steps": ["Identify candidate workflows by asking: where does your team spend hours watching, scrolling, clicking, or reading to understand what's going on?", "Classify each workflow as 'see or do' (visual, agentic, large mixed context) vs. 'write or talk' (narrative, persuasive, conversational) vs. 'cheap bulk' (high-volume, low-stakes, text-only)", "Route 'see or do' tasks to a capable multimodal model with large context and vision capabilities", "Route 'write or talk' tasks to the model your organization already prefers for language quality and tone", "Route 'cheap bulk' tasks to the fastest, least expensive model available", "Calculate cost-benefit for each candidate: human hours saved vs. token cost, latency, and failure modes", "Flag 'not yet' cases where agentic risk (wrong command, wrong record, wrong interpretation) outweighs the gain", "Assign ownership of the routing layer to a dedicated AI platform group responsible for maintaining routing rules, supervision standards, and shared patterns"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179322769", "title": "I Tested Gemini 3 And It's The Real Deal: Grab My Take + Custom Prompt to See The Impact on Your Role", "date": "2025-11-19T14:02:11.295Z"}], "date": "2025-11-19T14:02:11.295Z", "id": "see-or-do-vs-write-or-talk-model-routing", "core_question": "We're running multiple AI models now \u2014 how do we decide which model handles which workflow without overspending or underperforming?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The QA team spends four hours daily reviewing screenshots of UI regressions across mobile and desktop \u2014 that's eyes-on-glass work, a 'see or do' task that belongs on a multimodal model with vision capabilities like Gemini. Meanwhile, the marketing team's weekly email drafts are 'write or talk' tasks already well-served by Claude. And the support team's ticket classification \u2014 high volume, low stakes, text-only \u2014 is 'cheap bulk' work that should run on a fast, inexpensive model. Migrating email drafting to the multimodal model would burn 10x the tokens with no quality gain.", "related_frameworks": ["the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "entropy-matching-model-selection-framework", "model-context-matching-framework", "routing-layer-specification-layer-review-layer", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "task-decomposition-framework", "agent-economics-viability-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Routing Layer / Specification Layer / Review Layer", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-layer organizational framework for managing AI work at scale, separating the decisions of which model handles which task, how to instruct it, and how to evaluate its output.", "when_to_apply": ["We're using multiple AI models across teams and nobody owns the decisions about which model goes where", "Our AI outputs are inconsistent and I can't tell if the problem is the model, the prompts, or the review process", "I need to design what an AI platform team actually owns and is accountable for", "We want our AI workflows to survive model upgrades without rebuilding everything", "The bottleneck in our AI pipeline feels like it's shifted from model capability to how we specify and evaluate work"], "principles": ["The unit of competitive strategy is no longer the model itself but the routing, specification, and review layers built on top of it", "Routing layer: decide which workflows go to which model and keep that map current", "Specification layer: define what you want built or analyzed with enough clarity that the model can produce a useful artifact", "Review layer: define how artifacts are evaluated, what 'approval' looks like, and what gets logged", "Models keep improving underneath you; build the layers well and you benefit from model improvements automatically", "The bottleneck is shifting from model capability to human ability to specify intent and evaluate outputs quickly"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "179322769", "title": "I Tested Gemini 3 And It's The Real Deal: Grab My Take + Custom Prompt to See The Impact on Your Role", "date": "2025-11-19T14:02:11.295Z"}], "date": "2025-11-19T14:02:11.295Z", "id": "routing-layer-specification-layer-review-layer", "core_question": "As we scale AI across the organization, where exactly should human effort concentrate \u2014 choosing which model to use, telling it precisely what to do, or checking whether it did it right?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This team's AI platform failure isn't a model quality problem \u2014 they're strong on routing (they benchmark and swap models quarterly) but have almost no specification layer. Prompts are ad hoc, written by individual contributors with no shared templates or intent docs. The review layer exists but is pure vibes: someone eyeballs the output and clicks approve. The investment needs to go into specification standards and structured evaluation criteria. Once those layers are solid, every model upgrade they route to will automatically produce better outputs without additional human effort.", "related_frameworks": ["three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "the-five-specification-primitives", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "intent-engineering", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "six-layer-specification-stack", "done-definition-pattern", "verification-frontier", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "delegation-spec-drill"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI-Native Job Transformation Roadmap", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-step process for converting an existing knowledge-worker role into an AI-augmented role by mapping workflows, prototyping with approved tools, shipping a real use case, and integrating with organizational governance.", "when_to_apply": ["I use AI tools at work but it still feels like toy demos \u2014 I want to ship something real that my team actually depends on", "Our org is rolling out AI tooling and I want to be the person who shapes how it lands in my function, not the person it happens to", "I'm a senior IC who needs to demonstrate AI workflow design credibility for my next performance cycle or job search", "My manager asked me to figure out where AI fits in our team's workflows and I need a structured approach", "I want to build a relationship with our AI governance team but I don't have anything concrete to show them yet"], "principles": ["Design the AI-native version of your job before someone else does it for you", "AI augments structured, repetitive, verifiable work\u2014not judgment-heavy or politically nuanced work", "Work within approved organizational tools and security guardrails, not around them", "Ship one real workflow that others rely on rather than accumulating toy demos", "Pair business-side workflow knowledge with governance vocabulary to become a cross-functional bridge", "The goal is to shift your value from executing work to supervising and designing workflows"], "steps": ["Step 1 \u2014 Map your work like a systems designer: Identify 3\u20135 recurring workflows you own and for each document the trigger, inputs, steps, decision points, outputs, verification checks, and exception handling", "Step 2 \u2014 Learn to express those workflows to your existing approved AI tools: Give AI structured, scoped instructions; feed clean formatted inputs; request directly usable outputs; iterate until you have a stable, repeatable prompt recipe for each workflow", "Step 3 \u2014 Ship an MVP of one meaningful AI-augmented workflow: Pick a high-frequency, painful, low-blast-radius workflow; automate 60\u201380% of the steps while keeping yourself in the loop for approval and exceptions; measure time saved, error rate, and throughput; document it as a one-pager with numbers", "Step 4 \u2014 Build a relationship with whoever owns AI and platform governance in your org: Show your experiment as a responsible insider, ask what patterns they are standardizing, surface where they are nervous about AI in your function, and align your work to their highest-priority modernization targets"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179215165", "title": "Grab the AI Native Job Prompt\u2014A Custom Guide to Make YOUR Role AI Native in 2026", "date": "2025-11-18T14:02:41.462Z"}], "date": "2025-11-18T14:02:41.462Z", "id": "ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "core_question": "How do I systematically redesign my own role around AI before the organization redesigns it without me?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A marketing operations manager mapped five recurring workflows \u2014 campaign brief generation, performance report assembly, audience segmentation QA, creative asset tagging, and budget reallocation memos. The campaign brief workflow had the highest frequency, lowest blast radius, and clearest verification criteria (does the brief match the intake form plus historical performance data?). Automating 70% of brief generation with a stable prompt recipe inside the approved enterprise ChatGPT instance saved 6 hours per week across the team. Documenting that result as a one-pager with time-saved and error-rate numbers, then presenting it to the platform governance team, converted the manager from 'person who uses AI sometimes' into the go-to bridge between marketing and the AI modernization initiative \u2014 which is where durable career value lives.", "related_frameworks": ["the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "work-primitives", "task-decomposition-framework", "ai-career-board", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "ai-fluency-root-principles", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "delegation-spec-drill", "the-simple-wins-method", "edge-first-automation-framework", "data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance", "capability-surface-mapping", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Workflow Decomposition API", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured method for making implicit job work explicit by breaking any recurring workflow into seven defined components, creating a 'mental API' for your own role that enables AI integration and process improvement.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep asking AI to help with my job but the outputs are generic and miss the nuances of what I actually do", "I need to onboard a new hire or document my role and I'm realizing how much of my process lives only in my head", "My company is rolling out an AI initiative and I need to figure out which parts of my workflow are automatable vs. judgment-heavy", "I want to build a custom GPT or AI assistant for my role but I don't know how to specify what it should do", "I'm trying to figure out why AI tools work well for some of my tasks but completely fail on others"], "principles": ["Most knowledge workers have never made their own work explicit\u2014doing so is itself a competitive advantage", "If you cannot describe the work clearly enough to write it down, an AI model has no chance of helping with it", "Making work explicit is the prerequisite to improving it, whether or not AI is involved", "Every recurring job function can be decomposed into trigger, inputs, steps, decisions, outputs, verification, and exception handling"], "steps": ["Identify the trigger: what event or condition starts this workflow?", "List the inputs: what information or resources do you need before you can begin?", "Document the steps: what do you actually do, in order?", "Mark the decision points: where do you make judgment calls and what criteria do you use?", "Define the outputs: what do you produce at the end?", "Specify verification: how do you check that the output is correct?", "Define exception handling: what do you do when something goes wrong or an edge case appears?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179215165", "title": "Grab the AI Native Job Prompt\u2014A Custom Guide to Make YOUR Role AI Native in 2026", "date": "2025-11-18T14:02:41.462Z"}], "date": "2025-11-18T14:02:41.462Z", "id": "workflow-decomposition-api", "core_question": "I know AI could help with my work, but I can't clearly articulate what my work actually *is* \u2014 what are the actual steps, decisions, and judgment calls I make every day?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A product manager says 'I want AI to help me write PRDs.' Running the decomposition reveals: the trigger is a prioritization decision from the roadmap review, the inputs are customer research summaries, engineering capacity estimates, and strategic goals, the steps include synthesizing competing signals into a single problem statement, the key decision point is scoping \u2014 choosing what to cut \u2014 using criteria like engineering cost, revenue impact, and strategic alignment, the output is a PRD with acceptance criteria, verification is a stakeholder review meeting where engineering confirms feasibility, and exception handling covers cases where dependencies on other teams invalidate assumptions mid-sprint. Now the actual AI integration points are visible: synthesis of inputs and first-draft generation are strong candidates, but the scoping decision and exception handling require human judgment. Without this decomposition, the PM would have asked AI to 'write a PRD' and gotten generic output that misses the real work.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "work-primitives", "task-decomposition-framework", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "ai-leverage-audit", "delegation-spec-drill", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "ai-task-level-diagnostic", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "intent-engineering", "edge-first-automation-framework", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Vercel's 'Build It Now' Filter", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A five-criterion checklist for determining whether a workflow is ready for an AI agent deployment today versus deferred to a future architecture.", "when_to_apply": ["We have a list of ten workflows and need to decide which ones get an AI agent first", "My team wants to automate a process with an agent but I'm not sure it's the right kind of work for current AI capabilities", "I need a quick go/no-go filter before we invest engineering time building an agent for a specific task", "We're scoping an AI agent pilot and need to avoid picking a use case that will fail and kill organizational momentum", "Someone proposed automating a workflow that feels too open-ended or hard to verify \u2014 I need a structured way to say why"], "principles": ["The work must be verifiable \u2014 checking whether the agent performed correctly requires no heroics", "The workflow must be step-wise and bounded \u2014 completable in a defined sequence, not open-ended strategy work", "The task must be recurring and painful \u2014 people describe it as toil, not as valued work", "Inputs and outputs must be known \u2014 you understand exactly what enters the system and what constitutes good output", "A human must remain in the loop \u2014 the agent prepares work, a person approves, edits, or rejects it"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179105103", "title": "Anthropic, Google, and Vercel All Disagree on AI Agents\u2014Here's What Really Matters in 8 Minutes, Plus a Custom Prompt to Figure out YOUR Career with AI Agents", "date": "2025-11-17T14:03:15.002Z"}], "date": "2025-11-17T14:03:15.002Z", "id": "vercel-s-build-it-now-filter", "core_question": "Is this workflow actually ready for an AI agent today, or am I about to build something that will fail because the fundamentals aren't in place?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The expense report reconciliation workflow passes all five criteria: output is verifiable against receipts and GL codes, the process follows a fixed sequence (extract \u2192 match \u2192 flag \u2192 post), finance staff universally describe it as soul-crushing toil, inputs are scanned receipts plus policy rules and outputs are approved/flagged line items, and a human already reviews exceptions before posting. This is a build-it-now candidate. By contrast, the 'draft quarterly board narrative' workflow fails on boundedness and verifiability \u2014 there's no defined sequence and no one can check correctness without reading the whole thing with strategic context. That one stays manual.", "related_frameworks": ["the-three-question-workflow-audit", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-capability-triage", "agency-task-selection-grid", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "core-automation-readiness-check", "edge-first-automation-framework", "agent-strategy-sequencing", "three-tiers-of-agent-capability", "delegation-spec-drill"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agent Strategy Sequencing", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-step sequencing framework for progressing from early agent wins to comprehensive agentic infrastructure while avoiding security failures.", "when_to_apply": ["We have budget for AI agents but no clear roadmap for what to build first versus later", "Leadership wants a multi-agent orchestration platform but we haven't deployed a single agent yet", "Our first agent pilots worked \u2014 now we need to figure out how to scale without creating security gaps", "Someone proposed giving an autonomous agent broad system access and I need a framework to push back", "We're trying to justify investment in agent infrastructure to leadership after some early wins"], "principles": ["Start where value is immediately verifiable before building architecture", "Orchestration guardrails should be added even to simple early agents \u2014 treat agents as identities, not scripts", "Proven wins on simple problems justify investment in deeper orchestration", "Security cannot be retrofitted \u2014 orchestration is the security perimeter and must be considered from the start", "Steps cannot be skipped: don't build orchestration platforms before agents exist to orchestrate, but agents cannot scale without orchestration"], "steps": ["Step One: Mine Your Back Office \u2014 identify work that is verifiable, bounded, recurring, and universally disliked; build agents there first", "Step Two: Wrap those agents in lightweight orchestration \u2014 assign each agent an identity, define explicit tools and data access, add logging, require human approval for consequential actions", "Step Three: Use those wins to justify deeper orchestration work \u2014 adopt shared registries, agent operations tracking, multi-agent patterns, and control planes once value is proven", "Step Four: Keep high-profile agent misuse cases as a reference point \u2014 when someone proposes an autonomous agent with system access and unclear guardrails, use it as a reminder that unguarded orchestration layers will be exploited"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179105103", "title": "Anthropic, Google, and Vercel All Disagree on AI Agents\u2014Here's What Really Matters in 8 Minutes, Plus a Custom Prompt to Figure out YOUR Career with AI Agents", "date": "2025-11-17T14:03:15.002Z"}], "date": "2025-11-17T14:03:15.002Z", "id": "agent-strategy-sequencing", "core_question": "We want to deploy AI agents across the organization, but I'm not sure whether to start with infrastructure or use cases \u2014 and I'm worried about getting the sequencing wrong.", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "The team wants to build a multi-agent orchestration platform before they've deployed a single agent in production. That's Step Three work without Step One proof. The right move is to find the most verifiable, bounded back-office task \u2014 like reconciling invoice discrepancies or triaging support tickets \u2014 ship an agent there with lightweight identity and logging, prove the value, then use that win to justify the orchestration investment. Skipping to platform-building without working agents to orchestrate produces expensive infrastructure nobody trusts and nobody uses.", "related_frameworks": ["three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "three-tiers-of-agent-capability", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "agent-economics-viability-model", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "legacy-system-migration-path-to-the-dark-factory", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Question Agent Use Case Filter", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-question diagnostic that categorizes any proposed agent use case as either 'build now' or 'defer to future architecture.'", "when_to_apply": ["Someone on the team proposed an agent use case and I need a fast way to decide if it's real or aspirational", "We have a backlog of ten agent ideas and need to sort them into 'build this quarter' vs 'revisit later'", "I'm about to give an agent system access to production tools and want a sanity check on readiness", "Leadership is pushing us to 'do something with agents' and I need a filter that prevents us from shipping something dangerous", "A use case sounds exciting but I can't shake the feeling we'd be building guardrails after the fact"], "principles": ["All three questions must be 'yes' to proceed with building now", "Any 'no' answer defers the use case to a future comprehensive agent architecture", "Verifiability and boundedness are prerequisites for safe near-term deployment", "Orchestration guardrails must be articulable before deployment, not after"], "steps": ["Question 1: Is this work verifiable and bounded? If checking the result is difficult, it is not a first-wave agent application.", "Question 2: Is this work recurring toil? If people don't actively dislike it or it occurs infrequently, an agent is probably the wrong solution.", "Question 3: Do I know what orchestration guardrails belong around it? If you cannot specify tools, data access, budget, and escalation path, you are not ready to deploy."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "179105103", "title": "Anthropic, Google, and Vercel All Disagree on AI Agents\u2014Here's What Really Matters in 8 Minutes, Plus a Custom Prompt to Figure out YOUR Career with AI Agents", "date": "2025-11-17T14:03:15.002Z"}], "date": "2025-11-17T14:03:15.002Z", "id": "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "core_question": "Should we actually build this agent now, or are we just excited about the idea and not ready to deploy it safely?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The proposed agent would autonomously handle expense report approvals. Verifiable and bounded? Yes \u2014 each approval has clear rules and a checkable output. Recurring toil? Absolutely \u2014 finance hates it and it happens hundreds of times per month. Orchestration guardrails articulable? Yes \u2014 tool access is limited to the expense system API, budget caps are per-transaction, and any amount over $5K escalates to a human. All three gates pass: this is a build-now use case. Compare that to the other proposal \u2014 an agent that 'helps with strategic vendor selection.' The output isn't verifiable against a clear standard, it's not recurring toil, and nobody can articulate what tools or escalation paths it needs. That's a defer.", "related_frameworks": ["blast-radius-assessment", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "agentic-system-gut-check", "agency-task-selection-grid", "vercel-s-build-it-now-filter", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "delegation-spec-drill", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "constraint-architecture", "agent-strategy-sequencing", "three-tiers-of-agent-capability"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Goldilocks Prompting", "type": "distinction", "description": "A framework for calibrating prompt length and specificity to give AI models the right amount of creative freedom\u2014not so constrained it kills judgment, not so open it produces generic output.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompts are detailed but the AI keeps producing the same generic output pattern every time", "I wrote a long, specific prompt and the output feels robotic \u2014 like it followed instructions but lost all judgment", "I want to create reusable prompt templates that consistently produce varied, high-quality creative work", "I'm getting 'AI slop' \u2014 outputs that technically follow my instructions but feel like they were made by the same default algorithm", "I need to calibrate how much freedom to give the model for a task where variation matters but quality still needs to stay within bounds"], "principles": ["Name patterns rather than instances (e.g., 'Tailwind purple/blue defaults' not a specific hex code)", "Use categorical examples to show the space of possibilities, not exhaustive lists", "Give decision heuristics rather than rigid rules (formulas and comparisons the model applies with judgment)", "Calibrate through contrast and comparison to set the sensitivity dial on deviation from defaults", "Use action directives, not procedures (what to achieve, not step-by-step how)", "Create forbidden lists that anchor avoidance of convergent default patterns", "Warn about second-order convergence\u2014the new generic the model will fall into after following your first guidance", "Target under 500 tokens for the 80% of cases where judgment is desired over determinism", "Use deterministic, token-heavy prompts instead when another system or agent depends on the output format"], "steps": ["Write an opening principle: one sentence capturing what the guidance achieves", "Provide a forbidden list of 3\u20137 concrete, named convergent patterns to avoid", "Provide categorical alternatives organized by context, with 3\u20135 categories and concrete examples each", "Include 2\u20133 decision heuristics expressed as formulas or before/after comparisons", "End with 1\u20132 clear action directives", "Optionally add a meta-warning about second-order convergence to a new generic default", "Verify the whole prompt stays under ~500 tokens"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178758196", "title": "10x Your Prompt Power With a 100 Word Prompt: How to Build Goldilocks Prompts That Fix AI's Flaws in Data Visualization, Design, System Engineering, Writing, and More (+10 Prompts and a Meta-Prompt Builder)", "date": "2025-11-13T14:02:39.401Z"}], "date": "2025-11-13T14:02:39.401Z", "id": "goldilocks-prompting", "core_question": "How do I give the AI enough direction to avoid generic defaults without constraining it so tightly that it loses the judgment and creativity I actually need?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This design prompt is over-constrained \u2014 it specifies exact hex codes, pixel values, and layout positions, which is why every output looks identical and lifeless. The fix is naming the pattern to avoid ('Tailwind purple/blue defaults, centered hero with gradient overlay') and providing categorical alternatives ('editorial asymmetric layouts, data-dense dashboard grids, brutalist mono-spaced type') with a decision heuristic ('if the audience is technical, favor density over whitespace'). That gives the model a space to move within rather than a single point to land on. The prompt drops from 1,200 tokens to 380 and the outputs gain meaningful variation.", "related_frameworks": ["constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "intent-engineering", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "six-layer-specification-stack", "principles-over-rules-for-ai-systems", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "judgment-over-rules-agent-architecture", "smart-machines-require-proper-structure-smrps-five-tier-prom", "entropy-matching-model-selection-framework", "ai-fluency-root-principles"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Integration Complexity vs. Advantage 2x2 Portfolio Strategy", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A four-quadrant matrix that classifies AI tools by integration complexity and advantage delivered, producing a portfolio sequencing strategy (buy first, pilot with guardrails, committed teams only, or caution) and a recommended budget allocation across tool archetypes.", "when_to_apply": ["We have budget for AI tools but need to decide which to roll out first and which to defer", "Leadership is asking for a sequenced AI tool adoption roadmap with clear rationale for prioritization", "We piloted several AI tools and now need to decide which ones justify full investment versus staying experimental", "Our team is small and we can't absorb more than one complex integration at a time \u2014 how do we pick?", "I need a portfolio-level view of our AI tool stack to justify budget allocation across tool types"], "principles": ["Low complexity, high advantage tools get deployed first without extensive evaluation overhead", "Medium complexity, high advantage tools require two-week pilots with defined guardrails and tuning", "High complexity, high advantage tools require executive sponsorship and a dedicated senior owner for at least six months \u2014 do not start without this", "Caution-tier tools (new/unproven or low signal-to-noise) should never be in the core stack unless they graduate to another quadrant", "Recommended portfolio allocation: 70% primitives and guardrails, 20% workflow and orchestration, 10% bets on emerging categories", "If pricing does not make sense at 10x pilot scale, do not start the pilot"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178643115", "title": "Grab My Prompt To Help Find the 1% of AI Tools That Actually Work (+50 Tool Reviews to Get Started)", "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z"}], "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z", "id": "integration-complexity-vs-advantage-2x2-portfolio-strategy", "core_question": "We're looking at a dozen AI tools at once \u2014 which ones do we adopt now, which need careful piloting, and which should we avoid until we have more capacity?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The team's coding assistant falls in the low-complexity/high-advantage quadrant \u2014 it plugs into existing IDEs, delivers measurable time savings on day one, and requires no dedicated owner. Buy it now. The AI-driven customer support orchestration platform sits in high-complexity/high-advantage: it touches live customer data, requires CRM integration, and needs prompt tuning per product line. Without an executive sponsor and a senior owner committed for six months, it will stall after the pilot. The trendy 'AI meeting summarizer' startup has no clear pricing model at scale and its advantage signal is weak \u2014 it stays in the caution tier and never enters the core stack. Budget allocation: 70% goes to the coding assistant and similar primitives, 20% to the support orchestration pilot, 10% reserved for emerging bets like the summarizer if it graduates.", "related_frameworks": ["seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "ai-vs-software-budget-reframe", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-simple-wins-method", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Seven AI Tool Archetypes", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system of seven recurring AI tool categories defined by their core function and value mechanism, allowing faster evaluation of new tools by pattern-matching them to a known archetype with pre-established success metrics, integration requirements, and known failure modes.", "when_to_apply": ["A vendor demo looks impressive but I can't tell if the tool is core infrastructure or just a nice-to-have accessory", "Building out our AI tool stack and trying to figure out what categories we're missing versus where we're over-invested", "Need to set the primary success metric for an AI tool pilot and want to avoid measuring the wrong thing", "Evaluating whether a new AI tool belongs in the same budget line and governance tier as our existing tools", "Trying to anticipate failure modes for an AI tool category before we commit to a deployment"], "principles": ["Structure enforcers (schema/typing primitives) are lowest complexity and highest compounding value \u2014 deploy first", "Safety and policy guardrails are non-negotiable infrastructure for customer-facing AI \u2014 add latency budget accordingly", "Retrieval and grounding tools must have freshness policies and citation requirements built in from day one", "Memory layers require governance (what is stored, retention period, forget workflow, PII scope) or they become compliance liabilities", "Workflow and orchestration platforms require consistent use to extract value and must have vendor-neutral export from day one", "Conversation operations tools carry per-resolution pricing risk \u2014 hard caps must be negotiated in contract", "Operations and compliance accelerators have direct time/cash impact measurable within one month if the right pain point was chosen", "All other tools (general copywriters, slide generators, novelty agents) are accessory tier \u2014 require measured lift proof before adding to core stack"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "178643115", "title": "Grab My Prompt To Help Find the 1% of AI Tools That Actually Work (+50 Tool Reviews to Get Started)", "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z"}], "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z", "id": "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "core_question": "What kind of AI tool is this actually, and does knowing that tell me how to evaluate it, deploy it, and anticipate where it will break?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This new vendor pitching 'AI-powered customer support automation' is a conversation operations tool \u2014 the archetype where per-resolution pricing risk is the primary failure mode. The right success metric is cost-per-resolution with a hard cap, not 'customer satisfaction improvement.' Before piloting, the contract needs per-resolution pricing caps and a fallback to human handoff when confidence drops. Meanwhile, the team also evaluated a schema-validation tool last week \u2014 that's a structure enforcer, lowest complexity and highest compounding value, and should have been deployed months ago instead of being queued behind this flashier purchase.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "integration-complexity-vs-advantage-2x2-portfolio-strategy", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance", "function-specific-quality-gates", "ai-vs-software-budget-reframe"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Ten-Question Vendor Diligence Protocol", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A set of ten adversarial questions to ask AI tool vendors during serious evaluation that surface operational reality, hidden costs, lock-in risks, and genuine product differentiation faster than standard sales demos.", "when_to_apply": ["We're about to sign an enterprise contract with an AI vendor and I want to stress-test their claims before committing", "A vendor's demo looked impressive but I can't tell if there's real IP or just a thin wrapper on an LLM API", "I need to evaluate whether an AI tool will actually survive in production or just work in controlled demos", "We're comparing three AI vendors and need a consistent set of adversarial questions to surface real differences", "Our team is piloting an AI tool and I want to understand our lock-in risk and data portability before we go deeper"], "principles": ["Vendors unprepared for these questions reveal their product's production-readiness by their inability to answer", "Observable inputs, outputs, eval results, and drift graphs are the minimum bar for a vendor claiming their product works", "PII handling hand-waving ('industry best practices') signals the vendor has not thought through data governance", "An unusable or missing data export format means you are buying a dependency, not a tool", "Vendors who cannot name what broke for their last three churned customers are either too new or dishonest", "Median latency is a lie \u2014 require P95 latency under realistic traffic shape", "Any vendor who claims zero false positives or zero false negatives has miscalibrated their system", "A vendor who cannot answer 'what is the cheapest way to get 80% of this value without your product' does not know their own differentiation"], "steps": ["Ask: Show me observability \u2014 inputs, outputs, eval results, and drift graphs from a real production deployment (not a demo).", "Ask: What is your de-identification and PII handling story end-to-end \u2014 detection, logging, retention, and access controls?", "Ask: How do we export our data, configurations, and memories? Show me a real export file.", "Ask: Which parts of this product are your actual IP versus thin wrappers on a model API?", "Ask: What broke for your last three churned customers \u2014 what was the pattern?", "Ask: What is your latency at P95 under our traffic shape?", "Ask: What are your false positive and false negative rates on our data after tuning?", "Ask: What roadmap items would we have to wait for to meet our requirements?", "Ask: Show me your incident response SLA and comms plan, including a real postmortem from an actual outage.", "Ask: What is the cheapest way to achieve 80% of this value without buying your product?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178643115", "title": "Grab My Prompt To Help Find the 1% of AI Tools That Actually Work (+50 Tool Reviews to Get Started)", "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z"}], "date": "2025-11-12T14:03:24.363Z", "id": "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "core_question": "Is this AI vendor actually production-ready and differentiated, or am I about to buy a dependency wrapped in a polished demo?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The vendor couldn't produce a real export file \u2014 they offered 'we can work with your team on that.' That's the tell: there is no export. Combined with their inability to name what broke for churned customers ('we don't really have churn') and their differentiation answer revealing the entire product is a prompt chain on top of GPT-4 with no proprietary model or data layer, this is a thin wrapper selling a dependency. The only genuine signal was their PII handling \u2014 they had a real end-to-end story with detection, redaction logs, and retention policies. That's one out of ten. Walk away or negotiate wrapper-level pricing, not platform-level pricing.", "related_frameworks": ["middleware-trap-diagnostic", "three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "the-moat-audit", "ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "four-durable-positions-framework", "data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance", "the-weekend-hack-test", "ai-vs-software-budget-reframe"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agency Task Selection Grid", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A two-axis matrix for prioritizing which tasks to delegate to AI agents first, based on potential time savings and task ambiguity/risk, to maximize adoption while minimizing error consequences.", "when_to_apply": ["We have a list of processes we could give to AI agents but don't know which ones to pilot first", "Leadership wants to automate high-value knowledge work but I'm worried about error consequences we can't easily detect", "Our agent can do a lot of tasks technically, but we need to sequence rollout so users actually trust and adopt it", "We're designing a phased AI agent deployment roadmap and need a principled way to prioritize use cases", "The team is drawn to automating the hardest, highest-value tasks first \u2014 how do I argue for a different starting point?"], "principles": ["Time savings alone is not the right optimization dimension for early agentic deployment \u2014 verification cost is", "Start in the high-savings, low-ambiguity quadrant (e.g., coupon testing, price comparison, document collection) where correctness is immediately visible and error consequences are minimal", "High-value tasks are often high-ambiguity tasks where correctness is hard to verify and errors have serious consequences \u2014 avoid these early", "Tasks where the agent can fully explain its reasoning and the human can quickly validate the result are the safest starting points", "Gradually expand to more ambiguous domains only as trust accumulates through supervised experience", "Expertise and control are often part of the value in knowledge work \u2014 design for augmentation first, full automation second"], "steps": ["Map each candidate process onto two axes: potential time savings (x-axis) and ambiguity/risk (y-axis, combining task ambiguity with consequence severity of errors)", "Identify the high-savings, low-ambiguity quadrant as the initial deployment zone", "For each task in the initial zone, verify the agent can explain its reasoning and the human can quickly validate the result", "Deploy in the initial zone and accumulate supervised usage to build user trust and calibrate mental models", "After trust is established in the initial zone, evaluate expansion to higher-ambiguity tasks, using verified reliability as the gate \u2014 not just capability maturity"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "178557088", "title": "I Sat Down with OpenAI's Head of Engineering for Atlas to Talk Future of Work, the Atlas Browser, and AI Agents\u2014Catch the Exclusive Interview Here", "date": "2025-11-11T14:02:44.575Z"}], "date": "2025-11-11T14:02:44.575Z", "id": "agency-task-selection-grid", "core_question": "Which tasks should we hand to AI agents first so we build trust and capture real value without exposing the organization to errors we can't catch?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Mapping the procurement team's candidate tasks onto the grid: coupon testing and price comparison land in the high-savings / low-ambiguity quadrant \u2014 the agent's output is a number that can be verified against a known source in seconds, and a wrong answer costs a retry, not a contract dispute. Vendor contract negotiation sits in high-savings / high-ambiguity \u2014 correctness depends on legal nuance the team can't quickly validate, and an error could lock in unfavorable terms for years. The deployment sequence is clear: start with price comparison to build calibrated trust, then use that supervised track record as the gate for expanding into contract review \u2014 not the agent's raw capability score.", "related_frameworks": ["the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "delegation-scope-expansion-model", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "agent-strategy-sequencing", "ai-task-level-diagnostic", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Intelligence Navigator Reframe", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the AI value shift by arguing that insight generation has become commodity (like cheap TVs) while human judgment and implementation have become scarce (like drywall repair), repositioning where professionals should focus.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm a consultant/analyst and clients are starting to use ChatGPT to do what they used to hire me for", "We're trying to figure out how to reprice our advisory services now that AI can generate the same deliverables", "Someone on my team said AI will replace knowledge workers because it can synthesize information faster than we can", "I need to explain to my organization why we still need experienced humans even though AI produces good-enough analysis", "I'm restructuring my career around AI and trying to figure out which of my skills are still worth investing in"], "principles": ["Intelligence generation (gathering, analyzing, synthesizing) is now abundant and cheap", "Human judgment, implementation, and navigation around insights remain scarce and expensive", "Value has migrated from getting TO insight to getting FROM insight to outcome", "The bottleneck determines where value lives \u2014 identify the new bottleneck, not the old one", "Being an 'intelligence navigator' (knowing which insights matter and how to act on them) is non-commodifiable in the near term"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178455692", "title": "Good Judgement is a Million Dollar Skill in the Age of AI, But No One's Teaching It", "date": "2025-11-10T14:02:59.936Z"}], "date": "2025-11-10T14:02:59.936Z", "id": "intelligence-navigator-reframe", "core_question": "If AI can now generate the insights I used to get paid to produce, where does my professional value actually live?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that AI threatens this management consulting team because it can produce market analyses in minutes that used to take weeks. That's wrong. The analysis was never the scarce part \u2014 the client already had three conflicting reports on their desk before hiring consultants. The value was always in a senior partner walking into a boardroom, knowing which of those analyses to ignore, which political dynamics shaped the data, and how to sequence the implementation so the CFO didn't kill the initiative in Q2. That navigation layer \u2014 judgment about which insights matter, in what order, given these specific organizational constraints \u2014 is what's actually scarce now, and it's getting scarcer as the volume of generated insight explodes.", "related_frameworks": ["the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "10-components-of-good-judgment", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "grace-period-hypothesis", "double-compression-loop"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Insight Triage (Curation Practice)", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A structured framework for filtering AI-generated insights by implementability rather than analytical quality, explicitly including deprioritization as a core judgment output.", "when_to_apply": ["AI just gave me twenty recommendations and I need to figure out which three to actually present to leadership", "I'm worried that showing all the AI-generated analysis will overwhelm stakeholders and dilute the important findings", "A team member keeps surfacing interesting AI-discovered patterns but none of them seem actionable given our current constraints", "I need to demonstrate strategic judgment in how I curate and present AI-assisted analysis, not just volume of output", "How do I justify to my boss what I chose NOT to pursue from the AI's recommendations?"], "principles": ["Curation is more valuable than generation when generation is cheap", "Evaluate insights for implementability, not just analytical rigor", "Assess political viability given existing stakeholder dynamics", "Assess economic meaningfulness given the actual business model", "Assess executability given real organizational capacity", "Assess timing given current priorities and constraints", "Explicit deprioritization (with reasoning) is as important as prioritization \u2014 stating what you're NOT doing and why demonstrates judgment"], "steps": ["Generate a broad option space of insights using available tools", "Evaluate each insight against four implementability filters: political viability, economic meaningfulness, organizational executability, and timing", "Select the small number of insights that pass all filters", "Explicitly document and explain the insights being deprioritized and why", "Present both the selected insights and the deprioritization logic to demonstrate judgment"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "178455692", "title": "Good Judgement is a Million Dollar Skill in the Age of AI, But No One's Teaching It", "date": "2025-11-10T14:02:59.936Z"}], "date": "2025-11-10T14:02:59.936Z", "id": "insight-triage-curation-practice", "core_question": "I have a pile of AI-generated insights \u2014 how do I decide which ones are actually worth acting on, and how do I show my work on what I'm deliberately setting aside?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "Out of twelve strategic opportunities the AI surfaced from competitive analysis, only two survive all four filters. The market-adjacency play is analytically elegant but fails political viability \u2014 the SVP of Sales has publicly committed to the current vertical focus and reversing that requires capital the team doesn't have. The pricing restructure fails timing \u2014 Q4 is three weeks away and the billing system migration is already consuming engineering. Deprioritizing these explicitly, with reasoning, is what separates this from a raw AI output dump. The two survivors \u2014 renegotiating the channel partner terms and launching the self-serve tier \u2014 pass all four filters and represent the actual action set.", "related_frameworks": ["10-components-of-good-judgment", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "rejection-as-a-competency-recognition-articulation-encoding", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "problem-portfolio-assessment", "capability-dissipation-gap", "five-types-of-cognitive-work"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Transition Play", "type": "procedure", "description": "A five-step sequence for shifting from insight-generation work to implementation-and-judgment work, applicable to both external consultants and internal professionals navigating AI commoditization.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep delivering great analyses and recommendations but AI tools are making that same output available to everyone \u2014 how do I stay valuable?", "I'm an internal strategist or consultant and I realize my career capital is built on insight generation, not demonstrated judgment", "I want to shift from being known as someone who produces smart analysis to someone who delivers measurable business outcomes", "My consulting practice is built on deliverables (decks, reports, models) that AI can now approximate \u2014 I need a repositioning plan", "I have strong analytical skills but no visible track record of owning complete outcomes end-to-end"], "principles": ["Audit current time allocation \u2014 if more than 30% goes to insight generation, you are over-indexed on the commodifying part", "Demonstrate judgment through complete outcomes, not just insights or analysis", "Document reasoning and decision logic, not just execution steps", "Make outcomes visible to the people who matter with business impact framing", "Repetition builds credibility \u2014 one outcome is luck, three is a pattern, five is a practice"], "steps": ["Audit your current work to measure what percentage goes to insight generation vs. implementation \u2014 flag if generation exceeds 30%", "Select one painful, visible, measurable problem and commit to delivering a complete outcome (working system or changed state), not just a recommendation", "Document not just what you did but how you thought: what made the problem worth solving, how you prioritized it, what constraints shaped your approach, and what you would do differently", "Make the outcome visible to relevant stakeholders, framing around business impact rather than technical achievement", "Repeat until you have at least three similar outcomes that establish a recognizable pattern of judgment"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "178455692", "title": "Good Judgement is a Million Dollar Skill in the Age of AI, But No One's Teaching It", "date": "2025-11-10T14:02:59.936Z"}], "date": "2025-11-10T14:02:59.936Z", "id": "the-transition-play", "core_question": "My analytical and insight-generation skills are being commoditized by AI \u2014 how do I concretely reposition myself around judgment and outcomes before it's too late?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "After auditing time allocation, 55% of weekly hours go to producing analyses and recommendations that stakeholders increasingly generate themselves with AI tools. The first transition move: take the recurring inventory optimization problem that costs the ops team $200K/quarter in excess stock, and instead of delivering another recommendation deck, own the outcome \u2014 build the working reorder system, get it live, measure the savings. Document not just the system but the reasoning: why this problem over three others, why the threshold was set at 14 days not 7, what tradeoff was accepted on stockout risk. After three such delivered outcomes with visible business impact, the pattern reads as 'this person solves expensive problems end-to-end' rather than 'this person produces good analysis.'", "related_frameworks": ["10-components-of-good-judgment", "ai-career-board", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "double-compression-loop", "ai-leverage-audit", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "knowledge-work-practice-gym"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "10 Components of Good Judgment", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A structured decomposition of good judgment into ten distinct, teachable skill components for knowledge workers operating in an AI-augmented environment.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to evaluate whether a candidate or employee actually has good judgment, not just good instincts", "Someone on my team keeps making decisions that feel slightly off but I can't pinpoint what's missing", "I want to develop my own judgment systematically rather than just hoping experience teaches me", "We're designing a promotion rubric and 'good judgment' is a criterion but nobody can define what that means concretely", "I'm trying to figure out which judgment skills AI can't replace and which ones I need to double down on"], "principles": ["Good judgment is not a single skill but a bundle of ten distinct capabilities", "Each component is independently improvable and demonstrable", "Judgment compounds when you build feedback loops from what worked and failed", "Judgment must be made visible through transparent reasoning, not just correct outputs"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post lists 10 components of good judgment (finding real bottlenecks, pattern reuse without overgeneralizing, possible vs. possible now, sequencing for momentum, deprioritization discipline, calibration through feedback loops, social graph mapping, ownership frameworks, transparent reasoning, system design for encoding judgment) but the detailed elaboration of each component as a teachable framework is referenced as existing in a 'mini-course' and prompt that are not fully included in the provided text.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "178455692", "title": "Good Judgement is a Million Dollar Skill in the Age of AI, But No One's Teaching It", "date": "2025-11-10T14:02:59.936Z"}], "date": "2025-11-10T14:02:59.936Z", "id": "10-components-of-good-judgment", "core_question": "When I say someone has 'good judgment,' what exactly do I mean \u2014 and which specific parts of that judgment am I strong or weak at?", "category": "leadership", "example_application": "The candidate demonstrated strong pattern recognition and intellectual humility \u2014 she flagged an analogy that didn't hold and revised her recommendation mid-presentation. But her judgment falls short on two components: she consistently underweights second-order consequences (stakeholder impact beyond the immediate team) and lacks a feedback loop \u2014 she never revisits past decisions to calibrate. The development plan should target those two components specifically rather than vaguely asking her to 'improve judgment.'", "related_frameworks": ["five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "genuine-judgment-vs-coordination-overhead-disguised-as-judgm", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "director-assessment", "knowledge-work-practice-gym", "judgment-over-rules-agent-architecture", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post lists 10 components of good judgment (finding real bottlenecks, pattern reuse without overgeneralizing, possible vs. possible now, sequencing for momentum, deprioritization discipline, calibration through feedback loops, social graph mapping, ownership frameworks, transparent reasoning, system design for encoding judgment) but the detailed elaboration of each component as a teachable framework is referenced as existing in a 'mini-course' and prompt that are not fully included in the provided text.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "Six LLM Failure Modes (The Prompt Doctor Framework)", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "A classification system of six recurring structural failure modes in LLM prompting, each with named patterns, root causes, and layered fixes for both chat and API contexts.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompt produces reasonable output sometimes but wildly different quality on re-runs", "The LLM keeps hallucinating facts even though I told it to only use provided sources", "I'm building an automated LLM pipeline and need to systematically identify where prompts will break", "My multi-step prompt works for steps 1-2 but falls apart by step 4", "I keep rephrasing my prompt but the core problem with the output never changes"], "principles": ["Most prompting failures fall into one of six structural categories, not infinite random variations", "Symptoms (e.g., 'it hallucinates') often mask the real structural cause (e.g., no permission to express uncertainty)", "Each failure mode has both a chat-level fix (prose template) and an API-level fix (structured outputs/schemas)", "Fixes should include observable 'good signs / bad signs' diagnostics to verify whether the fix worked", "Don't fix all six at once \u2014 identify the highest-cost failure and fix that first"], "steps": ["Observe the broken output and describe what's wrong", "Match the symptom to one of the six failure modes: Underspecification, Regeneration Loops, Multi-Step Reasoning Collapse, Hallucination Triggers, Consistency Drift, or Context Overload", "Apply the baseline chat fix (prose template) for immediate relief", "Apply the advanced API fix (structured outputs/schemas) if building automated workflows", "Verify using the 'good signs / bad signs' checklist for that failure mode", "If still broken, check for secondary failure modes using the meta-diagnostic prompt"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178148671", "title": "The Prompt Doctor Is In: Fixes For The 6 Most Common ChatGPT Issues (Including 25 Actionable Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z"}], "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z", "id": "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "core_question": "My prompt keeps producing broken output and I've already tried rephrasing it three times \u2014 what's actually structurally wrong?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The output keeps drifting into generic advice by paragraph three despite a detailed prompt \u2014 this is Consistency Drift, not underspecification. The root cause is that the prompt front-loads constraints but provides no anchoring structure for the model to maintain persona and specificity across long outputs. The chat-level fix is inserting mid-prompt restatements of the constraint ('Remember: every recommendation must reference the client's Q3 revenue data specifically'). The bad sign to watch for: if the last paragraph could have been written without any of the context provided, the drift is still active.", "related_frameworks": ["meta-diagnostic-prompt", "intent-engineering", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "prompt-lifecycle-diagnostic-checklist", "goldilocks-prompting", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "9-ai-failure-patterns", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "six-layer-specification-stack", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "llm-psychosis-self-audit-framework", "smart-machines-require-proper-structure-smrps-five-tier-prom", "scaffolded-reasoning-architecture", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Six-Layer Specification Stack", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured prompt template that resolves underspecification by explicitly declaring output format, tone, constraints, success criteria, edge cases, and a reference example before issuing the request.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompt felt clear to me but the LLM output needed heavy rewriting on format, tone, or length", "I'm building a reusable prompt template that other people on my team will run without me", "I keep getting generic-sounding outputs that miss the specific style or constraints I need", "The LLM handles the main case fine but botches edge cases or unusual inputs in ways I didn't anticipate", "I want to move from iterating five times to getting a usable first draft"], "principles": ["Human communication relies on shared context; LLMs have none \u2014 every unspecified requirement is a random draw", "Underspecification and over-specification are both problems; the goal is complete specification", "A reference example is worth more than abstract descriptions of tone or style", "Success criteria should be observable and user-verifiable, not aspirational", "Edge cases prevent the model from defaulting to generic handling of important variants"], "steps": ["State the output type needed", "Specify FORMAT (exact structure: bullets, paragraphs, code, table, etc.)", "Specify TONE with a concrete example phrase", "List CONSTRAINTS: must-include items, must-avoid items, and length range", "Describe SUCCESS LOOKS LIKE as an observable outcome", "Add EDGE CASES with explicit handling instructions for likely variations", "Provide a REFERENCE EXAMPLE of a correct output", "Issue the specific request"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178148671", "title": "The Prompt Doctor Is In: Fixes For The 6 Most Common ChatGPT Issues (Including 25 Actionable Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z"}], "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z", "id": "six-layer-specification-stack", "core_question": "Why does the LLM keep producing output that's almost right but wrong in ways I thought were obvious \u2014 and how do I close every gap between what I mean and what I actually said?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The user's prompt asked for 'a summary of the quarterly results.' Running through the six layers reveals five unspecified dimensions: no format (bullets vs. prose?), no tone (executive briefing vs. team Slack post?), no constraints (length? must-include metrics?), no success criteria ('CFO can forward it without editing' vs. 'team understands the trend'), and no edge case handling (what if a division reported late?). After specifying all six layers \u2014 format as three bullet points per division, tone matching the attached prior quarter example, 150-word max, success defined as 'CFO sends without editing,' edge case 'flag any division with incomplete data rather than extrapolating' \u2014 the output lands on-target without revision.", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "the-five-specification-primitives", "intent-engineering", "goldilocks-prompting", "constraint-architecture", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "delegation-spec-drill", "done-definition-pattern", "correctness-definition-framework", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "smart-machines-require-proper-structure-smrps-five-tier-prom", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "delegation-prompt-management-system"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Surgical Editing Protocol", "type": "procedure", "description": "A prompt structure that resolves regeneration loops by explicitly declaring what to preserve, what is wrong with the target section, and what to change \u2014 preventing the model from rewriting approved content.", "when_to_apply": ["I asked ChatGPT to fix one paragraph and it rewrote my entire document", "Iterating on a long output where some sections are stakeholder-approved and cannot change", "Debugging code where fixing one function keeps breaking other functions that were already working", "The model keeps 'improving' parts of the output I didn't ask it to touch", "I need to make a surgical change to a generated document without losing formatting and structure elsewhere"], "principles": ["The model treats all content as editable unless explicitly told otherwise", "Explicit preservation beats implicit hope \u2014 quote the exact text you want kept", "Separate the problem diagnosis from the fix instruction", "Name preserved elements by section, quote, or field label so they are unambiguous", "Scope the regeneration request to only the problem section"], "steps": ["Quote the exact section that needs to change", "State what is wrong with that specific section", "Give the specific fix instruction for that section only", "List every element to keep unchanged (by name or direct quote)", "Instruct the model to regenerate only the problem section"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178148671", "title": "The Prompt Doctor Is In: Fixes For The 6 Most Common ChatGPT Issues (Including 25 Actionable Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z"}], "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z", "id": "surgical-editing-protocol", "core_question": "How do I get the model to fix just the broken part without destroying everything else I've already approved?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The model keeps rewriting the entire 2,000-word report when only the third paragraph's tone is wrong. The fix: quote paragraph three verbatim, state that its register is too casual for the executive audience, request a formal rewrite of that paragraph only, and explicitly list paragraphs one, two, and four through eight as locked \u2014 unchanged, not even reformatted. The model returns the full document with only the targeted paragraph altered, because every other section was named as preserved territory rather than left implicitly 'available.'", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "six-layer-specification-stack", "the-five-specification-primitives", "intent-engineering", "goldilocks-prompting", "constraint-architecture", "done-definition-pattern", "delegation-spec-drill", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Scaffolded Reasoning Architecture", "type": "procedure", "description": "A multi-stage prompting structure that resolves reasoning collapse by requiring the model to produce verifiable intermediate outputs at each stage before proceeding to the next.", "when_to_apply": ["The model keeps jumping to conclusions without showing how it got there, and I need to see the reasoning", "I'm building a multi-step AI pipeline where each stage feeds the next and I need reliability at each handoff", "My prompt asks for analysis that involves extraction, categorization, and recommendation but the output conflates all three", "I need to audit which specific step in the model's reasoning went wrong, not just that the final answer is off", "Complex prompts keep producing shallow or inconsistent outputs no matter how detailed my instructions are"], "principles": ["Complex tasks fail when treated as single cognitive leaps \u2014 intermediate outputs make reasoning verifiable", "Each stage should produce a specific, checkable artifact before the next stage begins", "Stage N+1 must explicitly reference Stage N's output as its input", "Validation criteria for each stage should be stated in advance so correctness is testable", "The more complex the task, the more stages are needed \u2014 break further if stages are still too large"], "steps": ["Define each stage with a specific, bounded instruction", "Specify the required output format for each stage", "Specify the validation criterion for each stage (how you'll know it's right)", "Instruct the model to complete Stage 1 only and stop", "Review Stage 1 output against validation criteria", "If valid, instruct the model to proceed to Stage 2 using Stage 1's output", "Repeat until all stages complete"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178148671", "title": "The Prompt Doctor Is In: Fixes For The 6 Most Common ChatGPT Issues (Including 25 Actionable Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z"}], "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z", "id": "scaffolded-reasoning-architecture", "core_question": "How do I stop the model from collapsing a complex, multi-step task into a shallow single-pass answer that skips reasoning and can't be verified?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A competitive analysis task that requires data extraction, pattern identification, comparative ranking, and strategic recommendation is failing because the model jumps straight to recommendations with no visible reasoning chain. Breaking it into four explicit stages \u2014 Stage 1: extract key metrics per competitor (output: structured table, validation: all competitors present with complete fields), Stage 2: identify patterns across the table (output: ranked list of patterns with evidence citations from Stage 1's table), Stage 3: comparative ranking with stated criteria (output: scored matrix referencing Stage 2's patterns), Stage 4: recommendations grounded in Stage 3's ranking \u2014 produces auditable intermediate artifacts at each step, and the weak link (a missed competitor in Stage 1) gets caught before it corrupts everything downstream.", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "six-layer-specification-stack", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "the-five-specification-primitives", "task-decomposition-framework", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "done-definition-pattern", "correctness-definition-framework", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "hierarchical-context-loading", "constraint-architecture", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Uncertainty Protocol", "type": "procedure", "description": "A prompt structure that reduces hallucinations by explicitly granting the model permission to express uncertainty and requiring confidence levels for each claim.", "when_to_apply": ["ChatGPT keeps giving me confident answers that turn out to be wrong when I fact-check them", "I'm building prompts for a domain where fabricated details could cause real harm \u2014 legal, medical, financial", "I need the model to distinguish between what it knows well and what it's guessing at", "My AI outputs will be consumed by people who can't independently verify the claims", "I want to calibrate how much I trust different parts of a long AI-generated response"], "principles": ["Hallucinations thrive in the absence of permission to be uncertain \u2014 explicitly granting that permission reduces them", "Models are trained to be helpful and produce answers; you must override this to allow 'I don't know'", "Every claim should carry an explicit confidence level (high/medium/low)", "Medium or low confidence claims should include an explanation of why confidence is limited", "The model should self-review before finalizing and flag any claims where confidence dropped"], "steps": ["Add an explicit instruction permitting 'I don't have reliable information about X' as a valid response", "Require a confidence level (high/medium/low) for each claim", "Require explanation for any medium or low confidence claims", "Request that sources or knowledge basis be noted for factual claims", "Instruct the model to review its response before finalizing and flag uncertain claims", "Issue the actual task request"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178148671", "title": "The Prompt Doctor Is In: Fixes For The 6 Most Common ChatGPT Issues (Including 25 Actionable Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z"}], "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z", "id": "uncertainty-protocol", "core_question": "How do I get the model to tell me what it actually knows versus what it's confidently fabricating?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A legal research prompt restructured with the Uncertainty Protocol now returns three high-confidence citations with verifiable case numbers, one medium-confidence claim flagged as 'based on general legal principles but I cannot confirm the specific statute number,' and an explicit 'I don't have reliable information about the 2023 amendment to this regulation' where the model previously would have invented a plausible-sounding but fictional amendment. The self-review step caught an additional claim where confidence dropped from high to medium upon reflection \u2014 the model noted it was conflating two similar but distinct regulatory frameworks.", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "two-llm-hallucination-verification-loop", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-capability-triage", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "correctness-definition-framework", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "9-ai-failure-patterns", "six-layer-specification-stack", "intent-engineering", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Deterministic Decision Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A prompt structure that eliminates consistency variance in decision-making by replacing fuzzy criteria with binary constraints, explicitly ordered selection criteria, measurable thresholds, and a defined refusal path.", "when_to_apply": ["Running the same decision prompt three times and getting three different winners with no input change", "Building an automated scoring or labeling pipeline that needs to produce the same result on re-run", "My prompt has phrases like 'balance quality with cost' or 'prefer X but consider Y' and results are inconsistent", "I need a legitimate 'no valid option' output instead of the model always forcing a pick", "Auditors or stakeholders need to trace exactly why the model chose option A over option B"], "principles": ["Consistency requires eliminating ambiguity at every decision point", "Constraints should be binary (pass/fail) with no judgment calls", "Selection criteria must have explicit numeric thresholds, not relative comparisons", "Priority order must be stated explicitly so the model applies criteria in the same sequence every time", "A refusal path ('no valid option') must be defined so the model doesn't force an invalid choice", "The process should show work step-by-step so the logic is auditable"], "steps": ["List all ABSOLUTE CONSTRAINTS as binary yes/no requirements that all options must pass", "List SELECTION CRITERIA in explicit priority order with measurable thresholds for each", "Define a TIEBREAKER rule that must resolve to a single answer", "Define a REFUSAL PATH specifying the exact response when no option passes constraints", "Specify the PROCESS: first filter by constraints, then apply criteria in order showing work, then state final selection with criteria-referenced rationale", "Issue the task with the actual options to evaluate"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178148671", "title": "The Prompt Doctor Is In: Fixes For The 6 Most Common ChatGPT Issues (Including 25 Actionable Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z"}], "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z", "id": "deterministic-decision-framework", "core_question": "Why does the same prompt produce different answers each time, and how do I lock it down so the output is repeatable?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A content moderation prompt kept flip-flopping between 'approved' and 'flagged' for the same borderline post. The root cause: the criteria said 'prioritize user safety but also consider free expression' \u2014 a relative comparison with no resolution order. After restructuring: absolute constraints are binary (contains slur \u2192 reject, contains threat \u2192 reject), selection criteria are ordered (safety score \u2265 8 out of 10 overrides expression score), and a refusal path exists ('if confidence below 7, escalate to human review'). Across 200 test runs the classification is now identical every time.", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "six-layer-specification-stack", "correctness-definition-framework", "definition-of-done-rubric", "done-definition-pattern", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "convergence-metric", "scaffolded-reasoning-architecture", "intent-engineering"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Hierarchical Context Loading", "type": "procedure", "description": "A context structuring method that resolves context overload by explicitly separating required facts from optional background, defining a priority hierarchy, and instructing the model to reference optional context only when required constraints cannot be satisfied.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompt has a lot of context and the model keeps referencing background details instead of following my actual instructions", "Output quality was fine with a short prompt but degraded significantly when I added more supporting information", "The model treats a vivid example I included as a template instead of following my explicit formatting requirements", "I'm building a RAG pipeline where retrieved documents are drowning out the user's actual query constraints", "I need the model to treat some facts as non-negotiable and others as nice-to-have, but it seems to weight everything equally"], "principles": ["Context is working memory, not a database \u2014 presence does not equal prioritization", "More context is not better context; structured context with clear hierarchy is better context", "Required facts should be structured as key-value pairs or explicit lists, not prose", "Optional context should stay out of active consideration until required facts cannot be satisfied", "When optional context is used, the model should quote it and explain why it was needed", "Hard constraints must never be overridden by optional background information"], "steps": ["Identify the 5-10 facts that are absolutely essential (hard constraints, success criteria, non-negotiables) and place them in REQUIRED CONTEXT", "Place all supporting details, background, and nice-to-haves in OPTIONAL CONTEXT", "Define a PRIORITY HIERARCHY numbering the most critical considerations in order", "Instruct the model to focus primarily on REQUIRED CONTEXT and Priority #1", "Instruct the model to reference OPTIONAL CONTEXT only when directly relevant to satisfying a required constraint", "Issue the task request"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178148671", "title": "The Prompt Doctor Is In: Fixes For The 6 Most Common ChatGPT Issues (Including 25 Actionable Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z"}], "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z", "id": "hierarchical-context-loading", "core_question": "I'm giving the model everything it needs to know, so why does it keep ignoring the most important requirements and latching onto irrelevant background details?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A legal contract review prompt included 3 hard constraints (must flag indemnification clauses, must use client's defined risk categories, must output in table format) buried inside 2,000 words of company background, deal history, and example contracts. The model kept producing narrative summaries referencing the deal history instead of the required table. After restructuring: the 3 constraints became a numbered REQUIRED CONTEXT block, the background moved to OPTIONAL CONTEXT with an explicit instruction to reference it only when a required constraint needed disambiguation, and a priority hierarchy put 'use client's risk categories' at #1. Output immediately shifted to the correct table format using the right categories \u2014 the deal history was never referenced because it wasn't needed to satisfy any constraint.", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "six-layer-specification-stack", "goldilocks-prompting", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "model-context-matching-framework", "intent-engineering", "jiorp-structure", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Meta-Diagnostic Prompt", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A single master prompt that analyzes a broken prompt against all six failure modes, identifies which problems apply, and generates a rewritten prompt with the specific fixes applied.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompt keeps giving bad output and I've rewritten it three times but can't figure out what's actually wrong", "I need to debug someone else's prompt and want a systematic way to identify all the issues at once", "The AI output has multiple problems \u2014 some hallucinated, some poorly formatted, some missing reasoning \u2014 and I don't know which fix to apply first", "I suspect my prompt has more than one issue compounding but I can only see the most obvious symptom", "I want to take a broken prompt and produce a single corrected version that addresses every structural problem"], "principles": ["Diagnose before fixing \u2014 applying the wrong fix wastes time and can obscure the real problem", "A broken prompt often has more than one failure mode active simultaneously", "Each failure mode has specific structural signals that can be checked systematically", "The output should include both a diagnosis with rationale and a rewritten prompt embodying the fixes"], "steps": ["Paste the broken prompt", "Describe what is wrong with the output", "Check for UNDERSPECIFICATION: missing format, tone, constraints, success criteria, or examples", "Check for REGENERATION SCOPE: no specification of what to preserve vs. change", "Check for MULTI-STEP REASONING: asking for conclusions without intermediate steps", "Check for HALLUCINATION TRIGGERS: no permission for uncertainty, no confidence requirements", "Check for CONSISTENCY: criteria that are unmeasured, unordered, or ambiguous", "Check for CONTEXT OVERLOAD: required and optional information not separated", "For each identified problem, explain why it applies and show the specific fix", "Generate a rewritten prompt incorporating all identified fixes"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178148671", "title": "The Prompt Doctor Is In: Fixes For The 6 Most Common ChatGPT Issues (Including 25 Actionable Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z"}], "date": "2025-11-06T14:03:23.588Z", "id": "meta-diagnostic-prompt", "core_question": "My prompt is producing bad output and I've tried rephrasing it multiple ways \u2014 what's actually wrong with it and how do I fix everything at once?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This prompt has three active failure modes compounding into the bad output. First, underspecification: it asks for 'a good summary' with no format, length, or audience constraints \u2014 the model is guessing at all three. Second, hallucination trigger: it demands confident assertions about market data without granting permission to express uncertainty or requiring citations, so the model fabricates statistics to sound authoritative. Third, multi-step reasoning collapse: it asks for a strategic recommendation without requiring intermediate analysis of the data first, so the conclusion has no visible reasoning chain to verify. The rewritten prompt adds a markdown format spec with word count, inserts 'If data is uncertain, say so and estimate confidence,' and breaks the task into three explicit stages: summarize the data, identify patterns, then recommend.", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "the-five-specification-primitives", "six-layer-specification-stack", "scaffolded-reasoning-architecture", "uncertainty-protocol", "surgical-editing-protocol", "goldilocks-prompting", "constraint-architecture", "intent-engineering", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "prompt-lifecycle-diagnostic-checklist", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "9-ai-failure-patterns", "deterministic-decision-framework", "hierarchical-context-loading", "done-definition-pattern"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Smart Machines Require Proper Structure (SMRPS) Five-Tier Prompting Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A five-tier classification of advanced prompting techniques organized by the type of problem they solve, using the acrostic 'Smart Machines Require Proper Structure' to make the categories memorable and actionable.", "when_to_apply": ["I know advanced prompting techniques exist but I can't figure out which one actually matches the problem I'm seeing in the output", "My AI outputs are technically correct but shallow and one-dimensional \u2014 they read like a first draft by someone who didn't think hard", "I'm building a prompt library for my team and need a coherent organizing system instead of a random collection of tricks", "I want to combine multiple prompting techniques for a complex task but I'm not sure which ones stack well together or in what order", "I'm transitioning from basic 'just ask it' prompting to systematic advanced techniques and need a map of what's available"], "principles": ["The bottleneck in AI output quality is the interface layer (prompting structure), not model capability", "Each tier targets a distinct failure mode: errors, poor prompts, shallow reasoning, single-perspective bias, or context/production constraints", "Techniques within tiers can be combined and stacked for complex tasks", "Match technique selection to task complexity and stakes, not habit", "The right prompting strategy can extract more performance from a smaller model than naive prompting on a larger model"], "steps": ["Identify the primary failure mode in your current output (errors, shallow analysis, single perspective, context limits, or inconsistent prompts)", "Map the failure mode to the appropriate tier: S=Self-Correction (errors), M=Meta-Prompting (poor prompts), R=Reasoning Scaffolds (shallow analysis), P=Perspective Engineering (blind spots), S=Specialized Tactics (production constraints)", "Select the specific technique within that tier that fits your task type and stakes level", "Optionally stack techniques from multiple tiers for high-complexity, high-stakes tasks (e.g., Meta-Prompting to design the prompt \u2192 Perspective Engineering to surface tradeoffs \u2192 Self-Correction to stress-test conclusions \u2192 Specialized Tactics to manage context)"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "178041786", "title": "ChatGPT 201: Advanced Prompting Made Easy (Easy Start Kit + Prompts + Guides)", "date": "2025-11-05T14:02:51.965Z"}], "date": "2025-11-05T14:02:51.965Z", "id": "smart-machines-require-proper-structure-smrps-five-tier-prom", "core_question": "My AI outputs are mediocre and I know better prompting could fix it, but I don't know which advanced technique to reach for given the specific way the output is failing.", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The output keeps producing one-sided analysis of a merger \u2014 it lists benefits but ignores regulatory risk, cultural integration challenges, and employee retention. This is a Perspective Engineering problem (the 'P' tier), not a reasoning depth problem. The fix isn't chain-of-thought \u2014 it's structured multi-perspective prompting: force the model to argue from the regulator's view, the acquiring team's view, and the target company's employees' view before synthesizing. If the stakes are high enough, stack Self-Correction on top by having it critique its own synthesis for blind spots after the perspective round completes.", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "technique-to-task-decision-matrix", "scaffolded-reasoning-architecture", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "goldilocks-prompting", "intent-engineering", "model-y-x-framework-the-harnessing-layer", "ai-fluency-root-principles", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "meta-diagnostic-prompt", "six-layer-specification-stack", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "delegation-prompt-management-system"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Technique-to-Task Decision Matrix", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured decision guide that maps specific prompting goals (reducing hallucination, maximizing depth, navigating tradeoffs, managing context, building reusable systems, ensuring precision) to the corresponding prompting techniques.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI output has a specific quality problem and I'm not sure which prompting technique would fix it", "I know I need chain-of-thought OR multi-persona debate OR structured verification but I can't tell which one matches my actual goal", "I'm combining multiple prompting techniques and need to know which ones address which failure modes so I don't stack redundantly", "The AI keeps giving shallow or inconsistent answers and I want to pick the right advanced technique instead of just rewriting my prompt", "I have a high-stakes task and want to match the right prompting strategy to the specific risk \u2014 hallucination vs. missed nuance vs. context overflow"], "principles": ["Match technique to the specific failure mode or goal, not to general preference", "High-stakes accuracy tasks \u2192 Self-Correction techniques (Chain-of-Verification, Adversarial Prompting)", "Depth and thoroughness goals \u2192 Reasoning Scaffold techniques (Over-Instruction, Zero-Shot CoT, Reference Class Priming)", "Complex tradeoff decisions \u2192 Perspective Engineering (Multi-Persona Debate, Temperature Simulation)", "Context window exhaustion \u2192 Summary-Expand Loop", "Prompt reusability and consistency \u2192 Meta-Prompting (Reverse Prompting, Recursive Optimization)", "Precision and boundary testing \u2192 Zero-Shot CoT Through Structure, Strategic Edge Case Learning"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "178041786", "title": "ChatGPT 201: Advanced Prompting Made Easy (Easy Start Kit + Prompts + Guides)", "date": "2025-11-05T14:02:51.965Z"}], "date": "2025-11-05T14:02:51.965Z", "id": "technique-to-task-decision-matrix", "core_question": "I know my AI output has a specific problem \u2014 shallow reasoning, hallucinated facts, inconsistent answers \u2014 but I don't know which prompting technique actually fixes that specific failure mode.", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The output keeps hallucinating citations that don't exist \u2014 this is an accuracy failure, not a depth failure. The correct intervention is Chain-of-Verification or Adversarial Prompting (self-correction techniques), not adding more detail to the prompt or asking for step-by-step reasoning. Over-Instruction would make the output more thorough but wouldn't catch fabricated sources. The fix is verification-layer techniques: ask the model to list its claims, then challenge each one independently before producing the final answer.", "related_frameworks": ["six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "prompt-lifecycle-diagnostic-checklist", "goldilocks-prompting", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "scaffolded-reasoning-architecture", "intent-engineering", "smart-machines-require-proper-structure-smrps-five-tier-prom", "ai-fluency-root-principles", "9-ai-failure-patterns", "model-context-matching-framework", "six-layer-specification-stack", "meta-diagnostic-prompt"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Production-to-Problem-Solving Spectrum", "type": "distinction", "description": "A spectrum that distinguishes AI-replaceable production work from AI-augmented problem-solving work, used to help junior professionals reposition their value.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm a junior professional and my deliverables are starting to look like something ChatGPT could produce \u2014 how do I stay relevant?", "My manager keeps assigning me templated execution tasks and I can't figure out how to demonstrate strategic thinking", "I'm a manager trying to figure out which junior roles on my team are actually at risk of being automated away", "I want to use AI to 10x my output but I'm worried that just makes me a more efficient version of something that gets eliminated", "I'm preparing for a performance review and need to show I bring judgment, not just output"], "principles": ["Pure production work (executing defined tasks, following templates) is AI-replaceable", "Problem-solving work (framing ambiguous problems, identifying what needs to be built) is AI-augmented", "Organizations are not set up to push juniors rightward on the spectrum \u2014 individuals must do this themselves", "Reframing tasks to surface the underlying business question demonstrates problem-solving ability", "If stuck in production mode, 10-20x productivity via AI buys time to develop problem-solving skills"], "steps": ["Identify whether your current work is framed as production (produce this, run this, build this) or problem-solving", "Reframe each task by asking: what business question is this answering, and is this the right format?", "Bring options and alternatives rather than a single output", "Explicitly communicate the 'why' behind your work, not just the 'what'", "If unable to escape production framing, demonstrate 10-20x AI productivity to buy time"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177956531", "title": "I've Talked to Hundreds of Companies About AI & Jobs: Here's The Jobs Guide No One is Writing (+ Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-04T14:03:06.107Z"}], "date": "2025-11-04T14:03:06.107Z", "id": "production-to-problem-solving-spectrum", "core_question": "Is the work I'm doing every day the kind that AI will replace entirely, or am I building the judgment and problem-framing skills that make AI a force multiplier for me?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This analyst's daily work \u2014 pulling data, formatting slides, running standard models \u2014 sits firmly on the production end of the spectrum. The tell: every task arrives pre-framed ('build this deck,' 'run this analysis'), and the output is evaluated on speed and format compliance, not on whether it answered the right question. The move is to start reframing: instead of delivering the requested churn report, come back with 'I ran the churn numbers, but the real signal is that our highest-LTV segment is churning at 2x the rate of everyone else \u2014 here are three hypotheses why and what I'd test next.' That shifts the visible contribution from production to problem-solving, and it's the only version of this work that survives AI doing the production layer at near-zero cost.", "related_frameworks": ["three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "experiential-debt", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "legible-vs-illegible-work", "ai-career-board", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "grace-period-hypothesis", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "work-primitives", "ai-use-spectrum-tool-vs-replacement", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Domain-vs-Skills Matrix", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A two-axis matrix contrasting domain expertise against skill level to help mid-career professionals identify where their durable competitive advantage lies in an AI-commoditized skills landscape.", "when_to_apply": ["I have 10+ years in my field and I'm wondering whether my experience still matters or if a junior with ChatGPT can do what I do", "I'm considering a career pivot to a completely different industry and trying to figure out how much of my value I'd leave on the table", "A hiring manager asked me what I bring that AI doesn't, and I didn't have a clear answer", "I'm good at my domain but feel like my technical skills are outdated \u2014 should I invest in upskilling or double down on domain depth?", "I'm tempted to start over in a hot field like AI/ML but I've spent years building expertise in healthcare operations"], "principles": ["Pre-AI, career advancement required developing both skills and domain expertise in parallel", "Post-AI, skills are commoditized \u2014 AI can teach and execute skills; it cannot replicate accumulated domain experience", "Domain expertise (pattern recognition, stakeholder relationships, institutional knowledge, project scar tissue) is the primary moat for mid-career professionals", "Abandoning domain expertise entirely is high-risk; adjacent hops that carry existing domain credit are safer", "AI proficiency must be actively socialized \u2014 share prompts, teach decomposition, demonstrate output verification, build reusable frameworks"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177956531", "title": "I've Talked to Hundreds of Companies About AI & Jobs: Here's The Jobs Guide No One is Writing (+ Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-04T14:03:06.107Z"}], "date": "2025-11-04T14:03:06.107Z", "id": "domain-vs-skills-matrix", "core_question": "In a world where AI can replicate most skills, is my career value rooted in what I can do, or in what I deeply know about a specific domain \u2014 and how should that change my next move?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "A senior supply chain manager considering a pivot into product management at a tech company is carrying fifteen years of vendor negotiation patterns, supplier relationship capital, and crisis triage instincts that no AI can replicate. The skills gap (product frameworks, user story writing, roadmap tooling) is exactly what AI commoditizes \u2014 learnable in weeks with AI assistance. The domain expertise is the irreplaceable asset. A full pivot into, say, fintech product management would abandon that entire moat. The higher-value move is an adjacent hop: supply chain SaaS, logistics tech, or procurement platforms \u2014 domains where that scar tissue transfers and the new skills layer on top rather than replace what's underneath.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-career-board", "the-transition-play", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "four-skills-that-survive-infrastructure-inversion", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "expertise-translation-framework", "grace-period-hypothesis", "experiential-debt", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "ai-leverage-audit"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Grace Period Hypothesis", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the senior professional's AI challenge not as a threat requiring immediate expertise, but as a temporary window of organizational tolerance that rewards leading with strategic judgment while acquiring AI skills.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm a senior leader applying for roles and I'm not sure whether to emphasize my domain expertise or scramble to get AI credentials", "Our hiring committee is debating whether to pass on a strong senior candidate because they don't have AI experience on their resume", "I have 20 years of experience but a junior colleague who's great at prompting seems more valuable right now \u2014 am I being displaced?", "I'm designing an AI upskilling program and need to set different expectations for senior leaders versus early-career staff", "I feel pressure to get AI certifications quickly but I'm not sure that's actually what hiring managers care about at my level"], "principles": ["Organizations are relaxing AI assessment requirements for senior candidates because systems thinking and deep experience are scarcer than prompting ability", "Experienced professionals can learn AI mechanics in months; organizations cannot rapidly teach the judgment that comes from years of complex problem-solving", "Senior positioning should lead with strategic judgment and systems thinking, framing AI as an amplifier of existing capabilities", "The grace period is real but finite \u2014 seniors must demonstrate they are actively applying AI to their problem-solving frameworks", "Proof of capability includes: standing things up independently, framing ambiguous problems, and architecting solutions \u2014 not AI certifications"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177956531", "title": "I've Talked to Hundreds of Companies About AI & Jobs: Here's The Jobs Guide No One is Writing (+ Prompts)", "date": "2025-11-04T14:03:06.107Z"}], "date": "2025-11-04T14:03:06.107Z", "id": "grace-period-hypothesis", "core_question": "I'm a senior professional who feels behind on AI \u2014 should I be panicking about my relevance, or is my experience still the harder asset to replace?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this VP of Operations is falling behind because she can't build AI workflows from scratch. That's wrong. Organizations are currently hiring for her ability to decompose ambiguous cross-functional problems and architect solutions across messy legacy systems \u2014 capabilities that take a decade to develop and that no amount of prompt engineering courses can substitute. Her AI gap is a months-long learning curve; a junior AI-fluent candidate's judgment gap is a years-long one. The right move is to lead with systems thinking and demonstrate she's actively applying AI as an amplifier \u2014 standing up a prototype workflow, not collecting certifications \u2014 because the grace period where organizations will tolerate that learning curve is real but closing.", "related_frameworks": ["the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "ai-career-board", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "expertise-translation-framework", "ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "the-transition-play", "experiential-debt", "ai-leverage-audit"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Adversarial Investigation Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured approach to fighting institutional billing and compliance disputes by conducting evidence-based investigations for categorical violations rather than negotiating from emotion.", "when_to_apply": ["I got a massive medical bill and the itemized charges look inflated but I can't tell which ones are actually wrong versus just expensive", "An insurance company denied my claim using regulatory language I don't understand and I want to know if their denial is actually legally valid", "A debt collector is demanding payment on something I think is wrong but they keep citing statutes and codes I can't verify", "I need to write a formal dispute letter to an institution but I want it to cite actual rules and regulations, not just sound angry", "I'm about to call to negotiate a bill down but I want to know my actual legal position first so I'm not just begging for a discount"], "principles": ["Investigation precedes negotiation \u2014 find documented violations before attempting to settle", "Use AI to decode technical language that institutions use as a moat against laypeople", "Every domain has a governing rulebook \u2014 identify it before doing anything else", "Cross-reference multiple authority sources simultaneously to find violations hiding in the gaps between documents", "Seek categorical violations (binary rule breaks), not marginal disputes about pricing philosophy", "Anchor your position in objective, authoritative numbers (e.g., published regulatory rates) the other side cannot easily contest", "Verify key findings against primary sources before staking credibility on them \u2014 AI errors in adversarial contexts become self-inflicted wounds", "Signal sophistication through institutional register and regulatory citations, not emotion", "Control the frame \u2014 refuse framings that cast you as petitioner seeking mercy rather than party demanding compliance", "Treat each institutional response as diagnostic intelligence about position strength, and escalate in stages accordingly"], "steps": ["Step 1 \u2014 Framework Finder: Identify which regulatory, statutory, or contractual framework governs the institution in your dispute (e.g., Medicare billing rules, FDCPA, IDEA, FTC Funeral Rule, state insurance statutes)", "Step 2 \u2014 Document Acquisition: Obtain all documentation in the format the governing framework uses (e.g., itemized bills with CPT codes, written policy with appendices, collection notices with statutory citations)", "Step 3 \u2014 Technical Decoding: Use AI to translate the institution's technical language into plain explanations of what the rules actually require", "Step 4 \u2014 Violation Audit: Use AI to cross-reference your documents against all applicable authority sources simultaneously, looking for categorical rule violations hiding in the gaps", "Step 5 \u2014 Benchmark Calculation: Find the authoritative published standard (regulatory rate, comparable sales data, clinical guideline) that establishes what the correct outcome should be \u2014 this becomes your anchor", "Step 6 \u2014 Verification: Spot-check the top findings against primary source documents; use a second AI pass to fact-check citations in any letter you plan to send", "Step 7 \u2014 Dispute Letter Generation: Draft formal correspondence in institutional register that cites specific violations, references authoritative benchmarks, and states credible escalation consequences", "Step 8 \u2014 Response Decoding: Interpret the institution's response as a signal (immediate concession = strong position; reasonable counter = negotiation territory; ignore = test resolve or weak position; legal escalation = evaluate whether to continue or seek professional help; frame change = often a tell that your position is solid)", "Step 9 \u2014 Triage: Decide whether to continue self-advocacy, split the difference, or engage professional help based on what the response reveals about position strength and stakes"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177668784", "title": "I Built the First Guide for Using AI to Fight Back on Unfair Billing: Here's 10 Principles + 7 Prompts to Take Your Power Back", "date": "2025-11-03T14:02:15.472Z"}], "date": "2025-11-03T14:02:15.472Z", "id": "adversarial-investigation-framework", "core_question": "I'm facing an institutional bill, denial, or demand that feels wrong, but the institution is hiding behind technical language and bureaucratic complexity \u2014 how do I build a case strong enough to fight back without hiring a professional?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "A $14,000 hospital bill includes CPT code 99285 (highest-level ER visit) for what was a 20-minute evaluation resulting in a prescription. Cross-referencing CMS billing guidelines against the medical record reveals the documentation doesn't support level 5 coding \u2014 the physician notes show a straightforward presentation with no complex decision-making. The Medicare allowable rate for the appropriate code (99283) is $268, not $1,400. The dispute letter cites the specific CMS E/M documentation requirements, the published fee schedule, and references potential False Claims Act exposure. The hospital's response \u2014 an immediate 60% reduction offered within 48 hours without contesting the coding argument \u2014 confirms the original position was indefensible.", "related_frameworks": ["self-advocacy-vs-professional-help-decision-framework", "staged-escalation-with-diagnostic-responses", "institution-shadow-problem", "verification-frontier", "two-llm-hallucination-verification-loop", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "retrieval-vs-reasoning-tool-selection", "verification-capability-triage"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Self-Advocacy vs. Professional Help Decision Framework", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured triage tool for deciding whether an institutional dispute is suitable for AI-assisted self-advocacy or requires professional legal or expert help.", "when_to_apply": ["I found billing errors using AI research but I'm not sure if I should act on them myself or pay a lawyer", "An institution pushed back on my complaint and now has legal counsel involved \u2014 do I escalate or hire someone?", "The contract language in my dispute feels ambiguous and I'm worried AI-generated arguments might be wrong in ways I can't detect", "I want to fight a charge or fee that's clearly wrong but the amount isn't big enough to justify hiring a professional", "I've been using AI to investigate a potential violation and need to decide whether my findings are solid enough to act on without expert review"], "principles": ["AI changes when you need professional help, not whether you ever need it \u2014 it enables investigation before the professional-help decision, not instead of it", "Self-advocacy is appropriate when violations are categorical and verifiable against published standards", "Professional help is required when success depends on how a judge or arbitrator will interpret ambiguous rules", "The relevant cost comparison is: cost of investigation vs. cost of expertise, not fight vs. don't fight", "Use AI to prepare for professional help when you need expertise but want to reduce education time and validate whether advice is competent"], "steps": ["Check: Are the governing standards documented and verifiable (regulations, published fee schedules, clear contract terms)?", "Check: Are the violations categorical \u2014 did they either do X or not \u2014 rather than interpretive?", "Check: Does the dispute amount justify your time but not thousands in legal fees?", "Check: Are you willing and able to verify AI findings thoroughly before acting on them?", "Check: Is the initial pushback low-risk (a letter won't make things worse)?", "Check: Do you have clear escalation thresholds defined in advance?", "If all yes \u2192 self-advocacy territory; if ambiguous law, formal proceedings, opposing counsel, complex procedure, or high cost-of-being-wrong \u2192 get professional help; if you need expertise but want to understand issues first \u2192 use AI to prepare for professional engagement"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177668784", "title": "I Built the First Guide for Using AI to Fight Back on Unfair Billing: Here's 10 Principles + 7 Prompts to Take Your Power Back", "date": "2025-11-03T14:02:15.472Z"}], "date": "2025-11-03T14:02:15.472Z", "id": "self-advocacy-vs-professional-help-decision-framework", "core_question": "Can I handle this institutional dispute myself with AI assistance, or will I get burned if I don't bring in a professional?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "The medical billing violation is categorical \u2014 the provider charged $480 for a service with a published in-network rate of $210, and the fee schedule is publicly available from the insurer. The violation is binary (they either applied the contracted rate or didn't), the amount ($270 overcharge) justifies hours of effort but not $2,000 in legal fees, and sending a demand letter citing the specific contract term creates no downside risk. This is squarely self-advocacy territory \u2014 AI can pull the relevant regulation, draft the letter, and outline the escalation path. If, however, they respond with counsel or invoke an arbitration clause with ambiguous 'reasonable and customary' language, the interpretive complexity crosses the threshold and professional help becomes necessary.", "related_frameworks": ["adversarial-investigation-framework", "staged-escalation-with-diagnostic-responses", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "verification-capability-triage", "institution-shadow-problem", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-two-door-principle", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "two-llm-hallucination-verification-loop"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Staged Escalation with Diagnostic Responses", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system for reading institutional responses to dispute letters as intelligence signals that determine the appropriate next escalation step.", "when_to_apply": ["I sent a formal dispute letter and got a response I'm not sure how to read \u2014 is their offer fair or a lowball?", "The institution is completely ignoring my dispute correspondence and I can't tell if my case is weak or they're hoping I'll go away", "They responded to my complaint by involving their legal department \u2014 should I be scared or encouraged?", "Instead of addressing my specific documented violations, the company offered a goodwill credit or alternative resolution", "I got a partial concession immediately and I'm wondering whether to accept it or push for the full amount"], "principles": ["Every institutional response is diagnostic \u2014 it reveals information about their assessment of your position strength", "Don't treat disputes as binary (fight or don't fight); treat them as staged investigation where each response unlocks the next decision", "Immediate large concessions signal they can't defend the position publicly or legally \u2014 take the win", "Ignoring high-stakes disputes with documented violations is usually a bluff; ignoring small amounts may just be cost-benefit math", "Frame changes (e.g., offering charity) often indicate the institution cannot defend on substance \u2014 stay on documented violations", "Legal escalation by the other side requires evaluating position strength, not automatically matching with your own counsel"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "177668784", "title": "I Built the First Guide for Using AI to Fight Back on Unfair Billing: Here's 10 Principles + 7 Prompts to Take Your Power Back", "date": "2025-11-03T14:02:15.472Z"}], "date": "2025-11-03T14:02:15.472Z", "id": "staged-escalation-with-diagnostic-responses", "core_question": "I sent a dispute letter and got a response \u2014 is this a signal to push harder, take the offer, or back off?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "The hospital responded to a documented billing violation letter not by addressing the specific charges, but by offering to redirect the amount to their charity care program. This is a frame change \u2014 they cannot defend the substance of the violations, so they're trying to move the conversation to a terrain where they look generous instead of liable. The correct move is to reject the reframe, restate the documented violations, and escalate to the next tier, because their inability to engage on substance confirms the position is strong.", "related_frameworks": ["self-advocacy-vs-professional-help-decision-framework", "adversarial-investigation-framework", "institution-shadow-problem", "the-two-door-principle", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Position Bias Inversion", "type": "reframe", "description": "Challenges the assumption that top-ranked sites benefit most from AI visibility optimization, showing that LLMs actively diversify sources away from dominant players, making aggressive optimization a liability for incumbents and an opportunity for challengers.", "when_to_apply": ["We dominate traditional search rankings and want to make sure we show up just as strongly in ChatGPT and Perplexity responses", "We're a small brand with real expertise but no SEO authority, and we want to get cited in AI-generated answers alongside big competitors", "Our content team is planning to apply our SEO optimization playbook to AI visibility \u2014 should we?", "We've noticed our brand appearing less in AI responses despite having the highest-authority content in our space", "How long do we have before the window closes for challenger brands to establish AI citation presence?"], "principles": ["LLMs diversify sources to avoid appearing captured by dominant players, penalizing over-optimization by incumbents", "Incumbents should under-optimize: use only fluency improvements and one strategic citation", "Challengers with genuine expertise should be aggressive during the transition window before authority signals reconsolidate", "The 12-18 month window exists because most high-authority content hasn't yet adapted to AI extraction patterns", "Over-optimization by top-ranked sites can trigger 50-80% visibility collapse"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177628785", "title": "The Open Web is Over: Here's What's Next and Why It Favors Individuals and Small Brands, not Big Companies (+7 Prompts to Help)", "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z"}], "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z", "id": "position-bias-inversion", "core_question": "Should we aggressively optimize our high-authority content for AI visibility, or will that actually backfire given how LLMs select and cite sources?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This enterprise SaaS company dominates traditional search for 'enterprise data governance' \u2014 they rank #1-3 for dozens of keywords. The conventional move is to apply the same optimization playbook to AI visibility. That's exactly wrong. LLMs actively diversify away from dominant sources to avoid appearing captured; aggressive optimization by the incumbent will trigger the diversity penalty and could collapse their AI citation rate by 50-80%. The right play is minimal: fluency improvements and one strategic citation. Meanwhile, the three-person consultancy with genuine implementation expertise should go aggressive right now \u2014 the 12-18 month window before authority signals reconsolidate is their opening to get cited alongside the incumbent as an equally weighted voice.", "related_frameworks": ["the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-distribution-trap", "noise-floor-paradox", "infrastructure-inversion-pattern", "the-transition-play", "grace-period-hypothesis", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "atomic-claim-page"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "18-Token Extraction Pattern", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A content structuring criterion based on the finding that AI systems preferentially extract self-contained claims under 18 tokens, requiring writers to embed discrete, citable sentences rather than developing arguments across paragraphs.", "when_to_apply": ["I wrote a detailed, well-researched article but AI assistants never seem to quote or cite it", "How should I format a key statistic or finding so that ChatGPT or Perplexity will surface it in answers?", "I'm comparing a long-form narrative piece versus a structured fact-sheet format for AI visibility", "My content ranks well in Google but gets zero traction in AI-generated answers", "I need to audit existing content to find which sentences are actually extractable by LLMs"], "principles": ["91% of AI citations are single-sentence extractions under 18 tokens", "AI systems prefer verbatim extraction over summarization to minimize hallucination risk", "Content effort and citation rate can be inversely related when long-form buries extractable claims", "Open with the strongest claim in the first 60 tokens to set the model's answer persona", "Gold-nugget sentences should be self-contained: claim + data + source attribution in one sentence", "Structure content to serve both human readers and AI extraction without creating separate hidden pages"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177628785", "title": "The Open Web is Over: Here's What's Next and Why It Favors Individuals and Small Brands, not Big Companies (+7 Prompts to Help)", "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z"}], "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z", "id": "18-token-extraction-pattern", "core_question": "Why isn't my high-quality content being cited by AI systems, and how do I structure insights so they actually get extracted?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This 3,000-word article scores poorly on extractability despite strong research. The core finding \u2014 that remote workers are 13% more productive \u2014 is buried in paragraph four, split across two sentences, and qualified with a dependent clause. It scores 2/5 on the 18-token criterion. The fix: open with 'Remote workers are 13% more productive than office peers (Stanford, 2023)' as a standalone sentence in the first 60 tokens. That's 12 tokens, self-contained, with claim + data + source \u2014 a gold-nugget sentence an AI system can lift verbatim without hallucination risk.", "related_frameworks": ["atomic-claim-page", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "distribution-beats-compute", "pull-push-paradigm", "nine-principles-of-business-writing-with-ai", "noise-floor-paradox"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Institution Shadow Problem", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Names the failure mode where individual experts lose personal attribution to their institutional affiliation in AI citations due to incorrect citation formatting, and prescribes the specific formatting structure that restores personal credit.", "when_to_apply": ["AI assistants attribute my insights to my employer or university instead of to me personally", "I'm a consultant or researcher building a personal brand while affiliated with a larger organization and want AI tools to surface my name", "I'm auditing my web content to understand why my personal thought leadership isn't showing up in AI-generated answers", "I'm creating a new expert-positioning page and want to maximize the chance that LLMs cite me by name", "My organization's content team formats citations in a way that buries individual expert names"], "principles": ["88% of experts unknowingly lose personal attribution to institutional affiliation due to formatting problems", "Semantic relationship between name, credentials, and quote must be explicit and adjacent in a single line", "Correct format: \"Quote\" \u2014 FirstName LastName, Title, Organization (Year) on one line achieves 88% attribution accuracy", "Any other citation format drops attribution accuracy to approximately 30%", "Atomic Claim Pages (single-concept microsites) are cited 4.1x more than multi-topic blogs for personal attribution", "Dedicated standalone URLs signal to LLMs that a page is about exactly one concept, simplifying attribution"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177628785", "title": "The Open Web is Over: Here's What's Next and Why It Favors Individuals and Small Brands, not Big Companies (+7 Prompts to Help)", "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z"}], "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z", "id": "institution-shadow-problem", "core_question": "Why does the AI cite my organization's name when answering questions I'm the expert on, instead of citing me personally?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The attribution loss is a formatting problem, not a recognition problem. The expert's quotes are separated from their name by a paragraph break and a generic \"Source: Organization Name\" footer \u2014 so the LLM binds the insight to the institution, not the person. Reformatting to '\"Quote\" \u2014 FirstName LastName, Title, Organization (Year)' on a single line, hosted on a dedicated Atomic Claim Page for the specific concept, would shift attribution accuracy from ~30% to ~88%. The institution gets credit not because it's more authoritative but because the page's semantic structure makes the institutional name the strongest adjacent signal to the claim.", "related_frameworks": ["atomic-claim-page", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "18-token-extraction-pattern", "noise-floor-paradox", "distribution-beats-compute", "expertise-translation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Noise Floor Paradox", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes rising AI-generated web spam not as a threat to content creators but as a mechanism that increases the relative value of genuine signal, because AI systems become more desperate to find verifiable sources as noise increases.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm a content creator feeling like there's no point publishing when AI can generate 100 articles for every one of mine", "Trying to decide whether to invest in volume-based SEO content or deep original research pieces", "Evaluating which content formats will hold value as AI-generated text floods every niche", "A client is asking whether their thought leadership strategy is dead now that ChatGPT can produce similar-sounding articles", "Wondering whether to pivot my content strategy toward formats that AI can't easily replicate, like video or original datasets"], "principles": ["As AI-generated spam increases, high-signal content becomes simultaneously rarer and more valuable", "LLMs need verifiable sources to avoid hallucination penalties, making genuine expertise a structural asset", "Content that cannot be easily synthesized from existing material (original data, primary research, tacit expertise) has compounding value", "Optimizing mediocre content for visibility is inferior to producing genuinely citable expertise", "Formats that are harder to authentically imitate (e.g., video, original datasets, client case studies) represent unique signal"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177628785", "title": "The Open Web is Over: Here's What's Next and Why It Favors Individuals and Small Brands, not Big Companies (+7 Prompts to Help)", "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z"}], "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z", "id": "noise-floor-paradox", "core_question": "Should I be demoralized by the flood of AI-generated content in my space, or is there a structural reason my genuine expertise is becoming more valuable, not less?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that AI-generated content is drowning out human creators. That's backwards. As the noise floor rises \u2014 thousands of AI-generated blog posts regurgitating the same synthesized advice \u2014 LLMs and AI search systems become increasingly desperate for verifiable, citable sources to avoid hallucination penalties. A consultant who publishes original client case studies with real numbers, or a researcher sharing primary datasets, isn't competing with the spam \u2014 they're becoming the scarce signal that every AI system needs to reference. The strategic move isn't to produce more content; it's to produce content that cannot be synthesized from what already exists: original data, video of actual process, documented first-person expertise.", "related_frameworks": ["four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "distribution-beats-compute", "the-distribution-trap", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "distillation-as-piracy-reframe", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Domain Mismatch Penalty", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A diagnostic for identifying when content breadth or cross-domain citation patterns are suppressing AI visibility, because LLMs flag out-of-domain citations as potential hallucination risk rather than authoritative sourcing.", "when_to_apply": ["Our site has strong SEO authority but we're barely showing up in AI-generated answers", "We've historically published across multiple adjacent topics to capture keyword volume and now need to rethink that for AI visibility", "Should we split our content library across separate domains or keep everything under one brand site?", "We cite sources from outside our core expertise area to seem comprehensive \u2014 is that helping or hurting us with LLMs?", "Our content strategy was built for Google rankings and we need to understand what changes for AI citation"], "principles": ["LLMs cross-check domain alignment as an anti-hallucination mechanism, penalizing out-of-domain citations by up to 60%", "Comprehensive coverage that built SEO authority actively signals non-expertise to AI systems", "Focus on a narrow domain where you have genuine expertise rather than covering adjacent topics for keyword capture", "Content sprawl flags you as an aggregator rather than an originator of insight", "Separate sprawling content onto distinct properties to prevent domain mismatch from bleeding across an entire site", "Topical focus is legible to both AI systems and human audiences for the same underlying reason"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177628785", "title": "The Open Web is Over: Here's What's Next and Why It Favors Individuals and Small Brands, not Big Companies (+7 Prompts to Help)", "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z"}], "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z", "id": "domain-mismatch-penalty", "core_question": "Is my content strategy \u2014 built for SEO breadth \u2014 actually hurting me in AI systems because covering too many domains makes me look like an aggregator instead of an authority?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This site covers SaaS pricing, HR compliance, and supply chain logistics \u2014 all because they drove organic search traffic. But LLMs are cross-checking domain alignment as an anti-hallucination signal, so when the site gets cited on pricing strategy, the adjacent HR and logistics content suppresses its authority score by up to 60%. The sprawl is the problem. The fix isn't better content on each topic \u2014 it's separating these into distinct properties so each one reads as a domain-native originator rather than a generalist content farm.", "related_frameworks": ["the-moat-audit", "atomic-claim-page", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "noise-floor-paradox", "distribution-beats-compute", "pull-push-paradigm", "four-durable-positions-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Atomic Claim Page", "type": "procedure", "description": "A content architecture procedure for building a single-concept microsite that maximizes AI citation and personal attribution by focusing entirely on one thesis with structured, extractable claims.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a specific concept I coined or developed and I want AI assistants to attribute it to me when users ask about it", "My expertise keeps getting credited to my employer or institution instead of to me personally in AI-generated answers", "I'm creating content specifically designed for AI citation rather than traditional SEO or social sharing", "I want to establish algorithmic authority on a niche concept before larger players claim the territory", "I've published insights across multiple platforms but AI systems never surface my name as the source"], "principles": ["One page, one concept \u2014 signals to LLMs that the page is about exactly one thing", "Host at a dedicated URL (yourname.com/concept) rather than buried in site hierarchy", "Personal attribution requires the correct citation format on every citable claim", "Opening section should be dense with sub-18-token extractable moments", "Page must be genuinely useful to human readers, not optimized solely for AI extraction", "Periodic micro-updates signal freshness and prevent citation decay"], "steps": ["Choose one specific concept or insight where you have genuine expertise (not a broad domain)", "Create a dedicated URL at yourname.com/concept", "Open with the strongest claim in the first 60 tokens to set the model's answer persona", "Structure key insights as self-contained sentences under 18 tokens that include claim, data, and source", "Format every citation as: \"Quote\" \u2014 FirstName LastName, Title, Organization (Year) on a single line", "Ensure the page is human-readable and genuinely useful, not purely structured for extraction", "Update the page every few weeks with fresh data or examples to signal currency", "Test visibility by querying AI assistants with 'who's the expert on [your concept]?' and iterating based on results"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177628785", "title": "The Open Web is Over: Here's What's Next and Why It Favors Individuals and Small Brands, not Big Companies (+7 Prompts to Help)", "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z"}], "date": "2025-10-31T13:00:45.309Z", "id": "atomic-claim-page", "core_question": "How do I structure a single piece of content so that AI systems cite me by name as the authority on my specific concept?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The concept is 'context decay in multi-agent systems' \u2014 a real area of expertise, not a broad domain. The page lives at yourname.com/context-decay, opens with a dense 60-token claim ('Context decay is the primary failure mode in multi-agent pipelines: 73% of orchestration errors trace to stale or missing context between handoffs'), and structures every subsequent insight as a sub-18-token extractable sentence with inline attribution. After two weeks live, querying 'who coined context decay in multi-agent systems' returns the author's name in three of four AI assistants. The page that failed \u2014 a longer essay on 'AI agent architecture' \u2014 returned zero personal citations because the concept surface was too broad for any single model to anchor attribution.", "related_frameworks": ["18-token-extraction-pattern", "institution-shadow-problem", "noise-floor-paradox", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "the-transition-play", "distribution-beats-compute", "domain-mismatch-penalty"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "8 Principles of Retrieval Prompting", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of eight principles for prompting retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems like Perplexity differently from parametric LLMs, based on how the retrieval architecture processes queries.", "when_to_apply": ["My Perplexity searches keep returning surface-level summaries instead of the specific, source-grounded answers I need", "I'm getting good results from ChatGPT but the same prompts produce garbage in retrieval-based tools", "I want to build a repeatable research workflow in a RAG tool that minimizes hallucination and maximizes source diversity", "I keep getting a single synthesized narrative from my AI search tool when I need to see where sources actually disagree", "I'm providing examples in my Perplexity prompts to guide formatting but the quality of retrieved information is getting worse"], "principles": ["Principle 1 - Surgical Context Over Verbose Prompts: Use 2-3 precise, load-bearing words to narrow the semantic retrieval space rather than lengthy explanations of intent.", "Principle 2 - Few-Shot Examples Break Retrieval: Avoid providing examples to guide output; examples become retrieval constraints that artificially narrow the document pool.", "Principle 3 - Speak the System's Language: Use explicit, crisp parameters (dates, source types, domain filters) rather than vague qualifiers like 'recent' or 'academic.'", "Principle 4 - Force Triangulation, Not Convergence: Explicitly request multiple conflicting sources and ask the system to surface disagreements rather than smooth them into a single narrative.", "Principle 5 - Progressive Deepening Over Single-Shot Queries: Start broad to map the territory, then iteratively narrow with follow-up queries, treating each turn as a fresh retrieval opportunity.", "Principle 6 - Concrete Output Constraints Reduce Hallucination: Require the system to map every claim to a specific source, quote, and confidence level to make drift detectable.", "Principle 7 - Toggle Focus Modes Mid-Conversation: Switch retrieval focus modes (e.g., Academic, Finance) during a conversation to reset the retrieval lens without losing the conversational thread.", "Principle 8 - Build Reusable Spaces for Recurring Workflows: Encode proven retrieval constraints into saved search environments so expertise scales across queries and teammates."], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "177236968", "title": "How to Beat Hallucinations in AI: Your Perplexity Search Guide with 40+ Prompts That Force Accurate Results", "date": "2025-10-28T13:01:17.458Z"}], "date": "2025-10-28T13:01:17.458Z", "id": "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "core_question": "Why do my carefully crafted prompts work well with ChatGPT but return shallow, generic, or hallucinated results when I use a retrieval-augmented system like Perplexity?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The query 'Tell me about recent advances in CRISPR gene therapy, especially academic papers, and format it like my last research brief' violates three principles at once. The few-shot reference ('like my last research brief') becomes a retrieval constraint that narrows the document pool to nothing useful (Principle 2). 'Recent' is a vague qualifier that the retrieval layer can't anchor to a date range \u2014 '2024-2025' would be surgical (Principle 3). And the broad scope invites narrative smoothing. The corrected first query is: 'CRISPR gene therapy clinical trials 2024-2025 \u2014 surface conflicting efficacy findings across at least three independent sources with citations.' This maps every claim to a source, forces triangulation over convergence, and gives the retrieval layer precise semantic anchors.", "related_frameworks": ["retrieval-vs-reasoning-tool-selection", "intent-engineering", "goldilocks-prompting", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "two-llm-hallucination-verification-loop", "model-context-matching-framework", "entropy-matching-model-selection-framework", "ai-fluency-root-principles", "the-verifiability-spectrum"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Two-LLM Hallucination Verification Loop", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured cross-verification workflow that uses a second AI system to audit the outputs of a RAG-based search tool, exploiting the complementary failure modes of parametric and retrieval-based models.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm about to cite a statistic from Perplexity or another AI search tool in a high-stakes document and need to be sure it's accurate", "The AI search result attributes a specific quote to someone but I can't tell if it's a real quote or a paraphrase the model invented", "My RAG tool returned an answer backed by only one source I've never heard of", "The synthesis in my AI search result feels too clean \u2014 like it's connecting dots the sources don't actually connect", "I need a systematic way to verify AI-generated research before publishing or presenting it"], "principles": ["Parametric LLMs and RAG systems have different failure modes, making them complementary verification partners.", "RAG systems ground claims in sources but can drift in synthesis; parametric LLMs can spot logical inconsistencies but may hallucinate facts.", "Never trust single-source answers, especially from unfamiliar or low-authority domains.", "Quote attributions must be verified against the cited source verbatim, not just paraphrased.", "Hallucination is a risk to manage through layered verification, not a binary problem to solve."], "steps": ["Run your query in the RAG-based search tool and collect its output along with all cited sources.", "Check whether any claims are backed by only a single or low-credibility source; flag those for extra scrutiny.", "For any quoted attributions, navigate to the cited source and search for the exact phrase to verify accuracy and context.", "Paste the RAG tool's full output and its citations into a parametric LLM and prompt it to evaluate the claims critically, identify logical inconsistencies, and flag any cases where the sources don't clearly support the synthesis.", "Use Academic focus mode or equivalent high-authority source filters for precision-critical queries to reduce low-quality sources in the retrieval pool.", "Treat any claim that cannot be tied to granular, verifiable evidence as unconfirmed and seek additional verification channels."], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "177236968", "title": "How to Beat Hallucinations in AI: Your Perplexity Search Guide with 40+ Prompts That Force Accurate Results", "date": "2025-10-28T13:01:17.458Z"}], "date": "2025-10-28T13:01:17.458Z", "id": "two-llm-hallucination-verification-loop", "core_question": "How do I catch hallucinations and synthesis errors in AI-generated research before I act on them?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The RAG tool returned a claim that 'McKinsey's 2024 State of AI report found 72% of enterprises have adopted AI in at least one business function' with a citation. Pasting the full output into a parametric LLM flagged that the cited source actually states 72% for 2024 but the surrounding synthesis conflated this with a different finding about generative AI specifically. Navigating to the McKinsey source confirms the statistic exists but the RAG tool attributed it to the wrong subsection \u2014 the 72% figure is about traditional AI adoption, not generative AI. The claim as synthesized would have been misleading in a board presentation. Marking it as partially confirmed with a corrected scope.", "related_frameworks": ["the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-frontier", "verification-capability-triage", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "9-ai-failure-patterns", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "llm-psychosis-self-audit-framework", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "retrieval-vs-reasoning-tool-selection", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "atomic-claim-page", "correctness-definition-framework", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Retrieval vs. Reasoning Tool Selection", "type": "distinction", "description": "A conceptual distinction between parametric LLMs (reasoning engines optimized for pattern completion over training data) and RAG-based systems (retrieval engines that fetch live documents before generating answers), used to decide which tool is appropriate for a given task.", "when_to_apply": ["ChatGPT gave me a very confident answer with specific numbers but I can't find the source anywhere", "I'm trying to decide whether to use ChatGPT or Perplexity for a research task", "My team keeps getting hallucinations and we've tried every prompting trick \u2014 maybe it's the wrong tool entirely", "The task requires current facts that may have changed since the model was trained", "I need every claim in the output to be traceable to a specific source document"], "principles": ["Parametric LLMs reason over encoded training patterns and excel at tasks requiring inference, creativity, or complex chains of logic.", "RAG systems retrieve live documents before generating and excel at tasks requiring current, verifiable facts.", "Fluency and factuality are not the same; a more persuasive model is not necessarily a more accurate one.", "Using a reasoning engine for retrieval tasks (or vice versa) is a category error that produces unreliable results regardless of prompt quality.", "RAG architecture provides an accountability layer by tying every claim to a citable source."], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "177236968", "title": "How to Beat Hallucinations in AI: Your Perplexity Search Guide with 40+ Prompts That Force Accurate Results", "date": "2025-10-28T13:01:17.458Z"}], "date": "2025-10-28T13:01:17.458Z", "id": "retrieval-vs-reasoning-tool-selection", "core_question": "Is this task asking the AI to think through a problem, or to look something up \u2014 and am I using the right kind of tool for which one it actually is?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The user asked ChatGPT 'What were OpenAI's Q3 2024 revenue figures?' and got a fluent, specific-sounding answer \u2014 but the model's training data cuts off before that quarter. This is a retrieval task dressed up as a reasoning task. The confident number is a hallucination, not a calculation error. The fix isn't a better prompt; it's switching to a RAG-based tool like Perplexity that fetches current documents and cites them. No amount of prompt engineering turns a parametric model into a search engine.", "related_frameworks": ["accuracy-vs-intelligence-failure-distinction", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "entropy-matching-model-selection-framework", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting", "two-llm-hallucination-verification-loop", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "verification-capability-triage", "9-ai-failure-patterns", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "see-or-do-vs-write-or-talk-model-routing", "model-context-matching-framework", "ground-truth-location-diagnostic", "llm-psychosis-self-audit-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "9 AI Failure Patterns", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "A catalog of nine recurring organizational failure modes that derail AI initiatives, each with a named pattern, a diagnostic signal, and a targeted fix.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI pilot worked great in testing but we can't get it into production and nobody can explain why", "A security incident froze all our AI projects and now nothing is moving forward", "We automated a bunch of tasks but somehow the team isn't actually faster or producing better results", "Leadership keeps talking about AI strategy but after months we still haven't shipped anything concrete", "We're losing our best people because they're burned out from AI projects that keep getting killed or stalled"], "principles": ["AI failures are predictable and classifiable, not random \u2014 recognizing the pattern is the first step to fixing it", "The worst cost of AI failure is not financial but human: burned goodwill, burnout, and loss of top talent", "Failure patterns are fractal \u2014 they recur at large and small scales across an organization in similar ways", "Individual contributors can diagnose and address these patterns at the team level, not just executives", "Each failure mode has a distinct trigger condition and a corresponding targeted fix"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 9 specific fixes (one per failure pattern) and a customization prompt, but their content is not included in the provided excerpt. Only the names and one-line diagnostic signals for each of the 9 failure modes are visible.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "177058925", "title": "Executive Briefing: What I Tell Leaders Stuck in AI Hell\u2014The 9 Biggest AI Issues Facing Teams, Plus Fixes & a Prompt", "date": "2025-10-26T14:01:22.129Z"}], "date": "2025-10-26T14:01:22.129Z", "id": "9-ai-failure-patterns", "core_question": "Our AI initiative is stalling or failing \u2014 but what specifically is going wrong, and is there a known pattern with a known fix?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The active failure mode is 'Pilot Purgatory' \u2014 the team built a successful demo three months ago but cannot get approval to deploy. The tell is that stakeholders keep requesting 'one more proof point' while the project slowly loses momentum and the best engineers start looking for the exit. The underlying cause is not technical inadequacy but missing executive sponsorship to absorb deployment risk. The fix is to stop proving the technology and start securing a named decision-maker who owns the go/no-go call with a deadline attached.", "related_frameworks": ["three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "capability-dissipation-gap", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "the-simple-wins-method", "ambient-failure-cascade", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "the-three-question-workflow-audit"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 9 specific fixes (one per failure pattern) and a customization prompt, but their content is not included in the provided excerpt. Only the names and one-line diagnostic signals for each of the 9 failure modes are visible.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "Nine Principles of Business Writing with AI", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "A structured set of principles that diagnose and prevent the root causes of AI-generated document slop by addressing purpose, structure, constraints, quality control, failure modes, input quality, voice, iteration, and workflow fit.", "when_to_apply": ["My team keeps iterating on AI-generated documents but they never get better \u2014 just differently mediocre", "We're using AI to draft memos, proposals, and reports at scale but leadership says the output feels generic and doesn't drive decisions", "I need to build a repeatable prompting system for business writing that prevents slop rather than just fixing it after the fact", "Our AI-generated documents sound like they could belong to any company \u2014 we're losing our organizational voice", "I'm spending more time editing AI drafts than I would writing from scratch, and I want to fix what's upstream"], "principles": ["Every Document Exists to Change Someone's Mind or Behavior: specify the outcome (decision or behavior change) before prompting, not just the information to convey", "Structure Is a Forcing Function for Thought: specify structure as logical argument sequence, not as a template with sections to fill", "Constraints Do More Work Than Instructions: 70% of a good prompt should be what NOT to include, forcing AI to prioritize rather than pad", "Quality Scales Through Self-Evaluation, Not Human Review: embed explicit, concrete self-check criteria into prompts so AI reviews its own output before you see it", "Failure Modes Are More Predictable Than Success: define and test for 'broken' (objective) rather than 'great' (subjective) for each document type", "You Can't Prompt Your Way Around Bad Inputs: verify you have the specific required data, decisions, and examples before prompting; if not, ask AI to identify what's missing first", "Every Organization's Documents Sound the Same Unless You Fight It: explicitly specify voice and style in every prompt or accept convergence to generic AI tone", "Iteration Is About Diagnosis, Not Polishing: identify the specific failure (too long, no decision request, generic examples) before prompting for a revision", "Documents Don't Exist in Isolation\u2014They Exist in Workflows: include workflow context in prompts (who reads it, what they do with it, what format enables that action)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176977209", "title": "First Time Sharing This: Grab My Private AI Writing Teaching Method + 8 Prompts (+ Demo)", "date": "2025-10-24T13:02:58.893Z"}], "date": "2025-10-24T13:02:58.893Z", "id": "nine-principles-of-business-writing-with-ai", "core_question": "Why does every document my team produces with AI look polished but feel hollow, and how do I systematically prevent that?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This board memo draft is exhibiting Failure Mode #1 and #5 simultaneously. It contains no specific decision request \u2014 it presents 'strategic options' without stating which one the CEO should approve. The tell: the document could be sent to any company in the industry without changing a word. The fix isn't polishing the prose \u2014 it's specifying the outcome before re-prompting ('This memo exists so the board votes yes on the $12M expansion by end of meeting') and embedding a self-check: 'Before outputting, verify: does paragraph one name a specific decision? Does the final section contain a concrete ask with a deadline? If not, rewrite.' The inputs are also suspect \u2014 there's no actual financial model feeding the recommendation, which means the AI is generating plausible-sounding numbers rather than citing real ones.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "six-layer-specification-stack", "delegation-spec-drill", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "surgical-editing-protocol", "done-definition-pattern", "definition-of-done-rubric", "ai-fluency-root-principles", "goldilocks-prompting", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "9-ai-failure-patterns", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Tiers of Agent Capability", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that categorizes agent problems by current feasibility and required capability level, determining whether to build now or wait.", "when_to_apply": ["We want to build an AI agent but aren't sure if the technology is ready for what we're imagining", "Stakeholders are pushing for fully autonomous agents and I need a structured way to set realistic expectations", "Trying to decide which agent use cases to prioritize in our roadmap for this year versus next", "Our agent prototype works for simple tasks but falls apart on multi-step workflows \u2014 should we keep pushing or redesign the scope?", "Leadership heard about AI agents and wants one that 'runs the whole process end to end' \u2014 I need to scope this realistically"], "principles": ["Tier 1 (build now): Single-purpose, narrow-scope agents with clear success criteria and contained failure modes \u2014 e.g., document processing, data entry, triage", "Tier 2 (2-3 years): Multi-step workflow agents with decision points, session-level learning, and cross-system orchestration", "Tier 3 (5-10 years): Autonomous agents with self-directed goal pursuit, persistent cross-session learning, and minimal human oversight", "Most builders overestimate Tier 3 and underestimate Tier 1 value", "Tier 1 architectures built today naturally evolve into Tier 2 as models improve"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176798661", "title": "Everyone Misread Andrej's Podcast\u2014Here's What He Actually Said About Building Agents (+ 3 Production Prompts)", "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z"}], "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z", "id": "three-tiers-of-agent-capability", "core_question": "Should we build this agent now, or are we trying to solve a problem that the technology won't reliably support for years?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The proposed agent would process incoming invoices, extract structured data, and route them to the correct approval queue \u2014 single-purpose, narrow scope, clear success criteria (does the extracted data match the invoice?), and contained failure mode (a human reviews flagged exceptions). This is squarely Tier 1: build it now. The team's instinct to also have it autonomously negotiate payment terms with vendors and learn supplier preferences across sessions is Tier 2-3 thinking \u2014 valuable eventually, but attempting it today will stall the project and poison stakeholder confidence in the parts that already work.", "related_frameworks": ["three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "agent-strategy-sequencing", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "agent-economics-viability-model", "agent-failure-pattern-catalog", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "capability-surface-mapping", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Type Memory Architecture", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of agent memory into three distinct types \u2014 working, episodic, and semantic \u2014 each with defined storage location, latency, capacity, and update cadence, used to design memory systems before writing agent logic.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm designing a new agent and need to decide what storage infrastructure it requires \u2014 vector DB, structured knowledge store, or just context window", "My agent doesn't learn from past mistakes and keeps making the same errors across sessions", "I'm trying to figure out whether to invest in a better model or better memory for my agent system", "We're building an agent and everything is crammed into the context window \u2014 it works for simple tasks but falls apart on complex ones", "I need to define when and how each type of agent knowledge gets updated, pruned, or versioned"], "principles": ["Working memory: lives in context window, ephemeral, resets between sessions, fast (<100ms)", "Episodic memory: persists across sessions in vector databases, stores experiences and failure patterns, updates after significant events, latency 10\u2013500ms", "Semantic memory: domain knowledge in structured stores, changes slowly, requires versioning, latency 50\u2013200ms", "Memory architecture determines agent capabilities more than model selection", "Design memory systems explicitly before building agent logic", "Each memory type requires different infrastructure, update triggers, and pruning strategies"], "steps": ["Identify what the agent must remember to function: ephemeral task state, cross-session experience, or domain knowledge", "Map each memory need to the correct type: working, episodic, or semantic", "Select appropriate storage infrastructure for each type", "Define update triggers: per-turn, post-session, or periodic", "Design pruning or versioning strategies for each type before writing agent logic"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176798661", "title": "Everyone Misread Andrej's Podcast\u2014Here's What He Actually Said About Building Agents (+ 3 Production Prompts)", "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z"}], "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z", "id": "three-type-memory-architecture", "core_question": "What does my agent actually need to remember, and am I storing each kind of knowledge in the right place with the right infrastructure?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The agent keeps forgetting previous customer interactions and repeating the same diagnostic questions. The problem is that failure patterns and past resolutions are being held only in working memory (context window), which resets every session. These are episodic memories \u2014 they need to live in a vector store, update after each resolved ticket, and get retrieved at session start. Meanwhile, the product documentation the agent references is semantic memory \u2014 it belongs in a structured store with versioning, not stuffed into the context window every turn. The model upgrade the team is considering won't fix this; the memory architecture is the bottleneck.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-memory-model", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "5-root-causes-of-unsolved-ai-memory", "8-principles-for-scalable-ai-memory-architecture", "build-vs-buy-memory-framework", "the-two-acts-of-memory", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "context-as-compiled-view", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "three-tiers-of-agent-capability"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Architecture-First Reliability Pattern", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes agent reliability as an architectural engineering problem rather than a prompting problem, arguing that deterministic containers must constrain non-deterministic agents.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent follows safety instructions 97% of the time but that remaining 3% is causing real damage in production", "I keep adding more instructions to my system prompt to prevent edge cases and it's becoming unmanageable", "Deciding whether to invest another sprint in prompt engineering versus building structural guardrails around the agent", "Building an agent that will take irreversible real-world actions like sending emails, modifying databases, or making purchases", "My multi-agent pipeline has a confused agent that occasionally corrupts state for downstream agents"], "principles": ["You cannot prompt an LLM into reliability \u2014 architecture creates reliability, not prompts", "Constrain the solution space: define exact tool sets, enforce schema validation on outputs, set hard iteration limits", "Separate planning (read-only, unconstrained) from execution (write operations that cross validated architectural boundaries)", "Validation happens at architectural boundaries in code, not within agent logic or prompts", "Apply human-in-the-loop selectively based on action reversibility and risk level", "Implement checkpointing and rollback before any state-changing operation", "Use circuit breakers to prevent cascading failures from confused agents"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176798661", "title": "Everyone Misread Andrej's Podcast\u2014Here's What He Actually Said About Building Agents (+ 3 Production Prompts)", "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z"}], "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z", "id": "architecture-first-reliability-pattern", "core_question": "Why does my agent keep failing unpredictably even though I've written incredibly detailed prompts telling it exactly what to do?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this agent needs better prompts \u2014 the team has been iterating on system instructions for weeks, adding guardrails like 'never delete without confirmation' and 'always validate inputs before proceeding.' That's wrong here because the agent ignores those instructions 3% of the time, and at production scale 3% means daily failures. The fix isn't a better prompt \u2014 it's a code-level schema validator that rejects any tool call missing required fields, a hard iteration cap of 5 loops before escalation, and a read-only planning phase that physically cannot trigger write operations. The agent can be creative inside the constrained space; the architecture makes the dangerous paths structurally impossible rather than merely discouraged.", "related_frameworks": ["constraint-architecture", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "blast-radius-assessment", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "the-reversibility-gap", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "speed-of-control", "the-two-door-principle", "primitives-for-reversibility", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agent Economics Viability Model", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A structured economic analysis framework for deciding whether to build an agent system by comparing fully-loaded manual process costs against agent development and operating costs at expected scale.", "when_to_apply": ["My team wants to build an AI agent but I'm not sure the volume justifies the development cost", "We have a running agent system and I need to decide whether to keep investing or kill it", "I'm prioritizing which of five possible automation projects to fund first with limited engineering resources", "The per-task cost of our agent seems low but total spend keeps climbing and I need to understand why", "Leadership is excited about agents but the process we'd automate only runs a few dozen times per week"], "principles": ["Calculate fully-loaded manual cost: salary plus benefits, management overhead, training, turnover, and error costs", "Estimate total agent cost: development, operation, and expected failure rate costs", "Require at least 3x ROI within six months before building", "Model cost curves at both current volume and expected scale, as optimal architecture differs", "Look for 'cost concentration' \u2014 where small efficiency gains translate to large absolute savings", "Define success metrics and kill criteria before deployment, not after", "Include 'do nothing' as a valid competitive option", "High-volume, boring, repetitive tasks justify agents; low-frequency tasks rarely do", "Route tasks to appropriately-sized models to avoid cost explosion"], "steps": ["Calculate fully-loaded cost of the manual process (not just salary)", "Estimate agent development and operating costs including expected failures", "Check volume: does the task occur hundreds or thousands of times at scale?", "Identify cost concentration opportunities where efficiency gains yield outsized savings", "Set explicit ROI threshold and timeframe kill criteria before writing code", "Model cost curves at both current and projected scale to choose the right architecture"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176798661", "title": "Everyone Misread Andrej's Podcast\u2014Here's What He Actually Said About Building Agents (+ 3 Production Prompts)", "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z"}], "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z", "id": "agent-economics-viability-model", "core_question": "Does this agent project actually save enough money to justify building it, or am I chasing technical novelty with a negative ROI?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The fully-loaded cost of the manual invoice processing workflow is $47/invoice when you factor in salary, benefits, management overhead, error correction, and turnover costs. At 3,000 invoices/month, that's $141K/month. The agent system costs roughly $85K to build, $12K/month to operate, and the expected 4% failure rate adds another $5K/month in human review costs \u2014 $17K/month total operating cost. That's an 8.3x ROI ratio that clears the 3x threshold within the first month of operation. The cost concentration is in error correction: 22% of manual cost comes from fixing data entry mistakes, and the agent eliminates most of that. This one passes. By contrast, the contract summarization task happens 40 times a month \u2014 the math never works at that volume regardless of per-unit savings.", "related_frameworks": ["three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "agency-task-selection-grid", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "budget-replacement-framework", "edge-first-automation-framework", "core-automation-readiness-check", "integration-complexity-vs-advantage-2x2-portfolio-strategy", "problem-portfolio-assessment", "the-factory-curve", "agent-strategy-sequencing"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agent Failure Pattern Catalog", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumerated set of predictable agent failure modes observed in production, each with a cause and a specific architectural fix.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent keeps looping on the same failed action and I can't figure out why it won't stop", "We're about to deploy an agent system and I want to pre-mortem the architecture for known structural weaknesses", "Our agent costs exploded last month and we suspect it's using GPT-4 for tasks that should go to a smaller model", "The agent is making decisions based on information that should have been an error message, not a fact", "I'm reviewing an agent codebase and need a checklist of architectural antipatterns to audit against"], "principles": ["Unconstrained reasoning loop: agent retries indefinitely on failure without learning \u2014 fix with hard iteration limits and explicit escalation after N failures", "Implicit state problem: agent assumes persisted state that isn't actually stored \u2014 fix by making all persistent state explicit", "Permission confusion: agents invoke tools inappropriate for a given task because permissions are global rather than per-task \u2014 fix with task-scoped tool availability", "Error propagation: raw API errors enter agent context as facts \u2014 fix with explicit error handling at architectural boundaries", "Cost explosion: expensive models used for simple tasks due to missing routing logic \u2014 fix with explicit task-to-model routing based on complexity"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176798661", "title": "Everyone Misread Andrej's Podcast\u2014Here's What He Actually Said About Building Agents (+ 3 Production Prompts)", "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z"}], "date": "2025-10-22T13:02:20.185Z", "id": "agent-failure-pattern-catalog", "core_question": "What's actually breaking in my agent system, and is it a known structural problem with a known fix?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The active failure mode is an unconstrained reasoning loop. The agent has been retrying a failed API call for 47 iterations, each time rephrasing its approach but never escalating or exiting \u2014 burning tokens and producing no result. The tell is the absence of a hard iteration limit and any escalation path after N failures. A second failure is compounding it: raw API error messages ('429 Too Many Requests') are entering the agent's context window as factual content, causing it to reason about rate limits as if they're domain knowledge. The architectural fix is two-part: a hard retry cap of 3 with explicit escalation to a human or fallback path, and an error-handling boundary that translates API errors into structured status objects before they reach the agent's reasoning context.", "related_frameworks": ["four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "three-failure-patterns-of-agent-intent-misalignment", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "constraint-architecture", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "architecture-first-reliability-pattern", "blast-radius-assessment", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "agentic-system-gut-check", "9-ai-failure-patterns"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Verification Over Credentialing Framework", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes job searching and hiring from producing better signals (resumes, portfolios) to making verification easier, arguing that when signal production costs collapse to zero, verification becomes the scarce and valuable resource.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm sending out hundreds of AI-polished applications and hearing nothing back", "As a hiring manager, I can't tell which candidates actually have skills versus which ones just used ChatGPT well", "Everyone in my field has the same certifications and portfolio style \u2014 how do I actually stand out?", "Our screening process is drowning in high-quality-looking applications that all feel the same", "I want to position myself for jobs but the traditional resume-and-cover-letter approach feels broken"], "principles": ["When signal production cost collapses to zero, signals lose information value (Shannon entropy applied to labor markets)", "The winner isn't who signals hardest\u2014it's who makes verification easiest", "Process patterns are harder to fake than outcomes; make your process the product", "Use AI as a verifier and evaluator, not just a generator", "Help companies verify their own needs\u2014most don't know what they actually want", "Position on capability spaces rather than job titles to escape keyword-matching noise", "Verification strategies have increasing returns as the market gets noisier"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176707091", "title": "I Reverse-Engineered AI-Native Hiring\u2014Here's What Actually Works (5 Tactics + 5 Prompts)", "date": "2025-10-21T13:03:22.527Z"}], "date": "2025-10-21T13:03:22.527Z", "id": "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "core_question": "Why isn't my polished application standing out when everyone else has access to the same AI tools to polish theirs?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This candidate has spent weeks perfecting their resume bullets and cover letter with ChatGPT \u2014 that's a signal production strategy, and it's exactly the wrong move. When every applicant can generate equally polished artifacts at zero cost, the resume becomes noise, not signal. The winning play is flipping the dynamic: instead of producing a better-looking application, build something that makes the hiring manager's verification problem disappear. A recorded walkthrough of solving a real problem \u2014 showing process decisions, dead ends, and recoveries \u2014 is exponentially harder to fake than a perfect PDF, and it directly reduces the company's cost of determining whether this person can actually do the job.", "related_frameworks": ["asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "noise-floor-paradox", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "bilateral-value-creation-interview-approach", "ai-career-board", "capability-surface-mapping", "double-compression-loop", "four-durable-positions-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Bilateral Value Creation Interview Approach", "type": "procedure", "description": "A candidate-led approach to job interviews where the candidate helps the company clarify what they actually need, creating mutual value and demonstrating strategic thinking that AI-generated applications cannot replicate.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a first exploratory call with a company and the job description feels vague or internally contradictory", "I'm interviewing for a senior role where the hiring manager seems to be figuring out what they need in real time", "I want to stand out from AI-polished applications that all sound the same", "The company seems interested in me but can't articulate what success in the role would look like", "I'm competing against candidates with stronger credentials on paper and need to demonstrate thinking, not just experience"], "principles": ["Most companies don't know what they actually need\u2014help them find out", "Lead with understanding their problem, not demonstrating your qualifications", "A synthesis document that clarifies their problem is worth more than any resume", "Helping a company articulate its own needs is itself a demonstration of the capability they need", "Human-to-human strategic collaboration is value AI cannot substitute"], "steps": ["On the first call, lead with questions: ask about pain points, constraints, what they've tried, and why it didn't work", "Listen for what they actually need versus what the job description says", "After the call, write a synthesis document capturing their problem space more clearly than they articulated it", "Send the document back to them as a demonstration of strategic thinking and genuine interest", "Follow with a verifiable proposal such as a paid one-week trial on a specific problem"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "176707091", "title": "I Reverse-Engineered AI-Native Hiring\u2014Here's What Actually Works (5 Tactics + 5 Prompts)", "date": "2025-10-21T13:03:22.527Z"}], "date": "2025-10-21T13:03:22.527Z", "id": "bilateral-value-creation-interview-approach", "core_question": "How do I differentiate myself in a hiring process where everyone's credentials look the same and AI has flattened the application layer?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The job description says 'Director of AI Strategy' but the listed responsibilities are a jumble of program management, vendor evaluation, and internal evangelism. The real signal is that this company doesn't yet know whether they need a builder or a buyer. After the first call, the synthesis document names this tension explicitly: 'You're hiring for two distinct problems \u2014 an internal capability gap and an external vendor dependency \u2014 and the role as scoped tries to solve both with one person. Here's how to sequence them.' That document itself is the proof of capability \u2014 it demonstrates the strategic clarity the company is paying for, before any formal offer or credential review happens.", "related_frameworks": ["asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "noise-floor-paradox", "ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "the-transition-play"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Budget Replacement Framework", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A three-question framework for evaluating whether to adopt an AI tool by assessing budget displacement, organizational readiness, and proof of work.", "when_to_apply": ["We're considering buying an AI tool but I can't clearly articulate what it would replace in our current stack", "Our team wants to adopt a new AI platform but we'd need to change our infrastructure to support it", "I need a quick litmus test to separate AI tools that deliver real ROI from ones that just add complexity", "We're in a procurement review and need to justify whether this AI tool eliminates cost or just adds it", "An AI vendor is showing impressive demos but their outputs are probabilistic confidence scores rather than verifiable results"], "principles": ["A tool must replace an existing budget line item, not add to the stack", "Fit with existing organizational readiness matters more than feature richness", "Enterprise adoption requires deterministic proof (audit trails, exploit logs, citations), not probabilistic confidence scores", "The fastest ROI comes from tools that work with what you already have, not tools that require you to reorganize your stack", "If you cannot name what a tool eliminates, it is adding complexity rather than optimizing"], "steps": ["Ask: What specific line item does this tool replace? Identify the exact cost center (subscription, labor, contractor) it eliminates. If you cannot name one, stop\u2014the tool is additive, not substitutive.", "Ask: What organizational readiness does this tool require? Map the tool's prerequisites (infrastructure, data platform, test coverage, compliance posture) against your current state. Only proceed if prerequisites are already met.", "Ask: How does this tool prove its work? Evaluate whether it produces deterministic, auditable outputs (exploit logs, test execution results, citation graphs, outcome metrics) rather than confidence scores or probabilistic claims."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176613406", "title": "I Surveyed 100+ AI Tools That Launched Since September\u2014Here Are the 15 That Actually Matter", "date": "2025-10-20T14:11:54.392Z"}], "date": "2025-10-20T14:11:54.392Z", "id": "budget-replacement-framework", "core_question": "Should we actually adopt this AI tool, or are we just adding another thing to manage without eliminating anything we already pay for?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The team is evaluating an AI-powered code review tool. It cannot name a specific line item it replaces \u2014 the existing static analysis subscription stays, and developer review time won't decrease because the tool flags issues developers still have to triage. That's additive, not substitutive \u2014 stop here. Even if it could replace something, it requires a unified CI/CD pipeline the org hasn't built yet, so organizational readiness fails. And its outputs are confidence scores ('87% likely a bug') rather than deterministic exploit logs or test execution results, so it cannot prove its work in an auditable way. All three questions produce 'no' \u2014 this tool should not be adopted.", "related_frameworks": ["three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "ai-vs-software-budget-reframe", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "the-abstraction-tax-audit", "agent-audit-trail-standard"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Tool Discovery and Evaluation System", "type": "procedure", "description": "A three-stage prompting procedure that moves from discovering newly launched AI tools, to scoring them on seven practical dimensions, to making a personalized adoption decision.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to evaluate a bunch of newly launched AI tools in a category and I don't want to rely on hype or listicles", "My team is considering switching from our current tool to a newer AI alternative and we need a structured comparison", "I want a repeatable research process for surveying AI tools that doesn't go stale every few months", "I have a shortlist of 3-5 AI tools and need to score them against each other using actual evidence, not marketing pages", "I'm doing due diligence on AI tool purchases for my organization and need to justify the recommendation"], "principles": ["Every step must be grounded in verifiable, timestamped claims\u2014no hype or unconfirmed assertions", "Discovery, comparison, and adoption decision are distinct stages requiring different reasoning lenses", "Scoring must use fresh public evidence, not vendor self-descriptions", "Verdicts should be conservative and ROI-focused", "The final recommendation must be personalized to actual workflow, pain points, and constraints\u2014not generic"], "steps": ["Run a discovery prompt: research newly launched indie tools through multi-source search with strict eligibility rules and timestamped validation, producing fact-rich profiles organized by use case.", "Run an evaluation prompt: score any tool list against seven practical dimensions (including functional maturity and retention signals) using fresh public evidence to generate scored comparisons with conservative, ROI-focused verdicts.", "Run a decision prompt: conduct a structured conversation that probes fit against your actual workflow, pain points, and constraints, culminating in a personalized recommendation with defined test metrics."], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references three specific prompts used by the author, including a seven-dimension scoring rubric and specific eligibility rules for discovery, but the actual prompt text and the seven dimensions are not included in the provided excerpt.", "source_posts": [{"id": "176613406", "title": "I Surveyed 100+ AI Tools That Launched Since September\u2014Here Are the 15 That Actually Matter", "date": "2025-10-20T14:11:54.392Z"}], "date": "2025-10-20T14:11:54.392Z", "id": "ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "core_question": "There are dozens of new AI tools claiming to solve my problem \u2014 how do I cut through the noise and figure out which ones actually work, actually last, and actually fit my situation?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "Running the discovery stage across recent AI writing assistants surfaces 23 tools launched since September, but applying timestamped eligibility filters (public launch date, at least two independent reviews, evidence of active development) immediately drops that to 9. Scoring those 9 on the seven dimensions reveals that only 3 show genuine retention signals \u2014 user community activity beyond launch week, documented iteration cadence, and third-party integration adoption. The decision stage then probes against the actual constraint: the team already uses Notion and needs inline editing, not a separate app. One tool scores high on functional maturity but has no Notion integration and no public roadmap for one \u2014 that kills it despite strong scores elsewhere. The verdict is a 2-week trial of the remaining candidate with defined test metrics: time-to-first-draft reduction and editor revision count.", "related_frameworks": ["three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-three-question-workflow-audit"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references three specific prompts used by the author, including a seven-dimension scoring rubric and specific eligibility rules for discovery, but the actual prompt text and the seven dimensions are not included in the provided excerpt.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "AI Fluency Root Principles", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-principle framework distinguishing the hidden drivers of org-level AI fluency from superficial AI usage or tool training.", "when_to_apply": ["We trained everyone on ChatGPT and Copilot but the productivity gains plateaued after a few weeks", "Designing an AI upskilling program and deciding whether to focus on specific tools or transferable skills", "Some individuals on the team are getting 10x results while others with the same tools barely get 2x \u2014 what's different?", "Leadership wants to build shared prompt libraries and automation pipelines but the team has only been using AI for two months", "Trying to figure out why high AI adoption metrics aren't translating into meaningful capability improvement"], "principles": ["Constraints over process: prioritize defining clear constraints rather than prescribing process steps", "AI-shaped problem-solving skills first: develop five transferable cognitive skills before optimizing tool use", "No infrastructure for a while: resist building systems and pipelines prematurely; build judgment first", "True fluency cannot be taught by teaching a specific tool \u2014 it requires building underlying judgment", "The goal is capability that compounds, not speed on easy tasks"], "steps": [], "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 'five pieces' to AI-shaped problem-solving skills and 'three specific questions to assess where you stand,' but neither the five skills nor the three questions are visible in the provided excerpt, as the full briefing is behind a paywall.", "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "176530721", "title": "Executive Briefing: AI Usage Is Not the Bar\u2014AI Fluency Is 10\u00d7 More Valuable (Here's How to Build It in Your Team)", "date": "2025-10-19T14:02:27.776Z"}], "date": "2025-10-19T14:02:27.776Z", "id": "ai-fluency-root-principles", "core_question": "Our teams are using AI tools everywhere, but the actual capability gains feel shallow \u2014 are we building real fluency or just teaching people to type into chatbots?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This team's AI training program is organized entirely around prompt templates and tool-specific workflows \u2014 that's process prescription, not constraint-setting, which means people can't transfer what they learn when the tool changes or the task shifts. The 30% productivity gains they're seeing are the ceiling, not the floor. The missing layer is the five transferable cognitive skills (decomposition, specification, verification, iteration, judgment) that compound across every tool and model generation. And the push to build a shared prompt library and automation pipeline at month two is premature infrastructure \u2014 they're encoding habits before they've developed the judgment to know which habits are worth encoding.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-four-month-ai-skills-progression", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "capability-dissipation-gap", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "constraint-architecture", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "experiential-debt", "knowledge-work-practice-gym", "ai-leverage-audit", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 'five pieces' to AI-shaped problem-solving skills and 'three specific questions to assess where you stand,' but neither the five skills nor the three questions are visible in the provided excerpt, as the full briefing is behind a paywall.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "The Two Acts of Memory", "type": "distinction", "description": "Separates AI memory into two distinct acts\u2014building a context library (deliberate curation of what to remember) and pulling the right slice (mode-aware, precise retrieval)\u2014to prevent the common mistake of focusing on infrastructure instead of both acts.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI assistant forgets things I've told it repeatedly, even though I'm using a tool with memory features", "We built a RAG pipeline but the agent still pulls irrelevant context or misses critical project details", "I'm debating whether to upgrade to a larger context window versus restructuring how we store and retrieve context", "Our AI memory system has tons of data in it but the quality of what surfaces for any given task is poor", "I'm designing a persistent context system for an AI tool and need to decide what architecture to build"], "principles": ["Act 1 is deliberate curation of what the system should remember\u2014preferences, standards, project facts, domain knowledge, past decisions", "Act 2 is selective, precise retrieval of what matters for the current task\u2014not dumping everything into the context window", "Infrastructure (embeddings, vector stores, context windows, RAG) supports these two acts but does not replace them", "More context volume does not solve precision problems; structure does"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "176283396", "title": "I Wrote the AI Memory Fix Every Existing Solution Missed\u20148 Principles + Prompts + Implementation Guide", "date": "2025-10-16T13:02:24.019Z"}], "date": "2025-10-16T13:02:24.019Z", "id": "the-two-acts-of-memory", "core_question": "Am I actually solving my AI's memory problem, or am I just throwing more storage and retrieval infrastructure at it without addressing what gets remembered and how it gets surfaced?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This team has invested heavily in a vector database and expanded their context window to 128k tokens, but their agent still loses track of user preferences mid-project. The problem is Act 1, not infrastructure \u2014 they never curated what the system should remember. They're dumping raw conversation logs into embeddings instead of distilling decisions, preferences, and standards into structured memory artifacts. Even if retrieval were perfect, the library itself is noise. The fix starts with deliberate curation: extract the 40 reusable facts from 200 conversations, then build retrieval logic that pulls only the 3-5 relevant to the current task mode.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-memory-model", "5-root-causes-of-unsolved-ai-memory", "8-principles-for-scalable-ai-memory-architecture", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "context-as-compiled-view", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "hierarchical-context-loading", "three-type-memory-architecture", "build-vs-buy-memory-framework", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "5 Root Causes of Unsolved AI Memory", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Enumerates five structural root causes explaining why no vendor has adequately solved AI memory, enabling practitioners to diagnose which failure mode is affecting their system.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI assistant keeps surfacing context from old projects that isn't relevant anymore, and I can't figure out why", "We're evaluating vendor memory solutions and they all seem to fail in different ways \u2014 what's actually going wrong structurally?", "Our RAG pipeline retrieves semantically similar content but it's almost never what the model actually needs for the current task", "We're debating whether to build a custom memory layer or use our platform's built-in memory, and need to understand the failure modes of each approach", "The AI accumulates so much context over time that its outputs are getting noisier, not better"], "principles": ["The relevance problem: what counts as relevant changes by task, phase, scope, and state delta\u2014semantic similarity is a proxy, not a solution", "The persistence/precision tradeoff: storing everything creates noise; storing selectively loses needed information; letting the system decide optimizes for the wrong thing", "The single-context-window assumption: volume of context does not solve the structure problem; a large unsorted context window is worse than a small curated one", "The portability problem: proprietary vendor memory layers create lock-in, discouraging users from building proper context libraries", "The passive accumulation fallacy: systems cannot distinguish preference from fact, project-specific from evergreen, or stale from current without active human curation", "Memory is actually multiple problems: preferences, facts, knowledge, episodic memory, and procedural memory each require different storage, retrieval, and update patterns"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176283396", "title": "I Wrote the AI Memory Fix Every Existing Solution Missed\u20148 Principles + Prompts + Implementation Guide", "date": "2025-10-16T13:02:24.019Z"}], "date": "2025-10-16T13:02:24.019Z", "id": "5-root-causes-of-unsolved-ai-memory", "core_question": "My AI system's memory keeps failing \u2014 it retrieves irrelevant things, forgets what matters, or drowns in accumulated noise \u2014 and I can't figure out which part of the memory problem is actually broken.", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The active failure mode here is the passive accumulation fallacy. The system is storing every interaction as undifferentiated memory \u2014 user preferences, project-specific constraints, one-time corrections, and durable facts all land in the same pool with no decay or categorization. The tell: the agent confidently applies a formatting preference from three months ago to a completely different project type, while forgetting a factual correction made yesterday. No amount of retrieval tuning fixes this because the storage layer never distinguished preference from fact, or stale from current. The fix requires active human curation protocols, not better embeddings.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-memory-model", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "the-two-acts-of-memory", "8-principles-for-scalable-ai-memory-architecture", "build-vs-buy-memory-framework", "three-type-memory-architecture", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "context-as-compiled-view", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "8 Principles for Scalable AI Memory Architecture", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of eight tool-agnostic principles for designing intentional AI memory systems, applicable from individual power users to enterprise agentic pipelines, that can also serve as a vendor scorecard.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm designing how my AI agents will remember things across sessions and I want to get the architecture right from the start", "Our AI tools keep forgetting important context or retrieving stale information that contradicts current project state", "We're evaluating a vendor's memory features and need to know if they're actually solving memory or just adding a convenience layer", "I need to migrate our AI workflows from one platform to another without losing accumulated context and knowledge", "We're scaling from a single-user AI setup to a team or enterprise system and our memory approach is already showing cracks"], "principles": ["Principle 1 \u2013 Memory is an architecture, not a feature: design memory as a standalone system that works across your entire toolset, not as a vendor feature", "Principle 2 \u2013 Separate by lifecycle, not by convenience: keep permanent preferences, temporary project facts, and ephemeral session state in separate stores with appropriate retention policies", "Principle 3 \u2013 Match storage to query pattern: use key-value for preferences, structured/relational for exact facts, semantic/vector for similarity recall, and event logs for episodic history", "Principle 4 \u2013 Mode-aware context beats volume: retrieval strategy must match task type\u2014planning needs breadth, execution needs precision, review/debugging needs trace history", "Principle 5 \u2013 Build portable, not platform-dependent: keep a canonical, model-agnostic context library that survives vendor changes; vendor memory is a convenience layer, not source of truth", "Principle 6 \u2013 Compression is curation: do the compression work deliberately\u2014write the brief, identify key facts, state constraints; human judgment in compression cannot be fully automated", "Principle 7 \u2013 Retrieval needs verification: pair fuzzy semantic retrieval with exact verification for facts that must be correct; use two-stage retrieval (recall candidates, then verify against ground truth)", "Principle 8 \u2013 Memory compounds through structure: structured, categorized memory allows each interaction to build without degradation; random accumulation creates noise, not leverage"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176283396", "title": "I Wrote the AI Memory Fix Every Existing Solution Missed\u20148 Principles + Prompts + Implementation Guide", "date": "2025-10-16T13:02:24.019Z"}], "date": "2025-10-16T13:02:24.019Z", "id": "8-principles-for-scalable-ai-memory-architecture", "core_question": "Is my AI memory system designed with real architecture, or am I just accumulating context randomly and hoping retrieval works?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This team's agent pipeline scores well on Principles 3 and 4 \u2014 they've correctly separated vector search for similarity recall from structured lookups for exact customer data, and their retrieval adapts by task mode. But they fail Principles 2 and 5 badly: all memory lives in a single vendor's conversation history with no lifecycle separation, and nothing is portable. When they switched from Claude to GPT-4 last quarter, they lost six months of accumulated project context. The fix is standing up a canonical context library outside any vendor, with explicit stores for permanent preferences, active project facts, and ephemeral session state.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-memory-model", "three-type-memory-architecture", "three-layer-enterprise-context-taxonomy", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "the-two-acts-of-memory", "5-root-causes-of-unsolved-ai-memory", "build-vs-buy-memory-framework", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "context-as-compiled-view", "double-compression-loop", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "hierarchical-context-loading"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Build vs. Buy Memory Framework", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A decision framework for determining whether to use vendor AI memory solutions or build custom memory infrastructure, based on context specificity, team size, lock-in tolerance, and retrieval complexity.", "when_to_apply": ["ChatGPT or Copilot just added a memory feature and my team wants to know if we can just use that instead of building our own", "Our AI workflows touch proprietary client data that compounds over time and we need to decide where that context lives", "We need audit trails on what context the AI used for each response, and I'm not sure our vendor supports that", "Our team is small and I'm tempted to use the vendor's built-in memory, but I'm worried about lock-in if we outgrow it", "We need the AI to retrieve different types of context depending on the mode of work \u2014 sometimes facts, sometimes relationship history, sometimes system architecture"], "principles": ["Use vendor solutions when context is general enough to share with the platform, team is small, and lock-in risk is acceptable", "Build custom when context is proprietary and compounds over time (client relationships, domain expertise, system architecture)", "Build custom when audit trails and governance are required (compliance, security)", "Build custom when retrieval needs are complex (mode-aware, multi-source, high precision on facts)", "Bad memory carries compounding costs: context drift, hallucinated facts, privacy leaks, wasted re-explanation cycles", "Vendors solve infrastructure (bigger windows, better embeddings, cross-chat search); they do not solve architecture"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "176283396", "title": "I Wrote the AI Memory Fix Every Existing Solution Missed\u20148 Principles + Prompts + Implementation Guide", "date": "2025-10-16T13:02:24.019Z"}], "date": "2025-10-16T13:02:24.019Z", "id": "build-vs-buy-memory-framework", "core_question": "Should we trust a vendor to hold and retrieve our AI's memory, or does the nature of our context demand that we own the memory architecture ourselves?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This team's AI memory needs are heavily custom. Their context compounds \u2014 client relationship history, domain-specific terminology, and architectural decisions that build on each other over months. The vendor's memory feature treats all context as interchangeable chunks in a shared embedding space, which means retrieval precision degrades exactly where it matters most: disambiguating which client said what, and which architectural decision superseded which. Add the compliance requirement for audit trails on every piece of context surfaced to the model, and the verdict is clear: build custom. The vendor solves the infrastructure problem (bigger windows, better embeddings), but the architecture problem \u2014 mode-aware retrieval, multi-source provenance, governance \u2014 is entirely unsolved by their offering.", "related_frameworks": ["four-layer-agent-memory-model", "two-memory-problems-distinction", "the-two-acts-of-memory", "5-root-causes-of-unsolved-ai-memory", "8-principles-for-scalable-ai-memory-architecture", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "tool-vs-infrastructure-design-question", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "context-as-compiled-view", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance", "the-two-door-principle", "three-type-memory-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Expertise Translation Framework", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the constraint on expertise-based work from 'lack of expertise' to 'documentation bottleneck,' positioning AI as a translation layer between expert knowledge and professional deliverables.", "when_to_apply": ["I spend more time writing up my work than actually doing the expert thinking", "My business is capacity-constrained but the constraint isn't my knowledge \u2014 it's getting that knowledge into deliverable form", "I'm a senior professional wondering where AI actually helps versus where it's just a gimmick for my role", "I've tried hiring junior staff to handle documentation but the review cycle eats most of the time savings", "I want to scale my expertise-based practice but hiring, working more hours, and raising prices all have limits I've already hit"], "principles": ["The constraint on expertise has never been knowledge itself \u2014 it is the translation layer between knowing and documenting", "Expertise compounds over time, but documentation time stays constant; AI lets output keep pace with growing expertise", "Quality control always stays with the expert \u2014 AI handles translation, not judgment", "AI gets you 80% of the way; the remaining 20% requiring professional judgment is exactly where you should focus", "Context is your multiplier: the more precisely you articulate role, audience, goal, and constraints, the better the output", "The three traditional scaling methods (more hours, hiring, raising prices) all fail because they don't attack the documentation bottleneck directly"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "176191257", "title": "AI Has Solved the Oldest Problem: Scaling Expertise\u2014Here's How (Yes With 27 Prompts lol)", "date": "2025-10-15T13:02:55.264Z"}], "date": "2025-10-15T13:02:55.264Z", "id": "expertise-translation-framework", "core_question": "I have deep expertise and more demand than I can serve, so why does my output feel bottlenecked by something that isn't the expertise itself?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A senior consultant spends 15 hours a week on client work and 25 hours writing reports, proposals, and SOWs that document what she already knows. The bottleneck isn't her knowledge \u2014 it's the translation from insight to formatted deliverable. By treating AI as the translation layer (feeding it precise context: role, audience, goal, constraints) she gets 80% drafts in minutes, then spends her time on the 20% that requires professional judgment \u2014 nuanced recommendations, political framing, risk caveats. Her throughput doubles without hiring, and the quality of the judgment layer actually improves because she's no longer cognitively depleted by formatting work.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "five-types-of-cognitive-work", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "ai-task-level-diagnostic", "double-compression-loop"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Context Formula (Role-Audience-Goal-Constraints)", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-part prompt construction formula that structures how experts provide context to an LLM so it can translate raw expertise into professional deliverables.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep getting generic, surface-level output when I paste my expertise into ChatGPT \u2014 it ignores what makes my work actually good", "I need to turn messy voice notes or bullet points into a polished client-facing document and want a repeatable process", "I'm spending 2+ hours writing up something I could explain in 10 minutes to a colleague", "I want to build a reusable prompt template that works across different document types in my practice", "My team members get wildly different AI output quality and I need a standard approach everyone can follow"], "principles": ["Every effective expertise prompt requires four components: your role, your audience, your goal, and your constraints", "The structure of the prompt never changes \u2014 only the domain-specific content changes", "Raw expertise can be provided in any fast form (voice memo, bullet points, shorthand) \u2014 the formula handles the translation", "Treat AI interaction as a conversation requiring iteration, not a single command", "Consistency in prompt structure allows you to quickly spot where output needs adjustment"], "steps": ["Step 1 \u2014 Pick Your Bottleneck: Identify one repetitive task where you spend 2+ hours translating expertise into deliverables but that does not require your highest-level judgment to document", "Step 2 \u2014 Apply the Context Formula: Construct your prompt by specifying (a) your professional role, (b) your intended audience, (c) your goal for the document, and (d) your constraints \u2014 then append your raw expertise in whatever form is fastest", "Step 3 \u2014 Use the Iteration Pattern: Treat the first draft as a starting point and refine through directed follow-up instructions (adjust tone, add specifics, shorten, reframe sections)", "Step 4 \u2014 Verify What Requires Judgment: Review the output specifically for domain-critical elements that AI cannot know \u2014 factual accuracy, compliance requirements, client-specific context, professional liability issues", "Step 5 \u2014 Start Small Then Scale: Apply to one task for two weeks, add a second task type in weeks 3-4, expand to all appropriate documentation in month 2, then reinvest freed time in month 3"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176191257", "title": "AI Has Solved the Oldest Problem: Scaling Expertise\u2014Here's How (Yes With 27 Prompts lol)", "date": "2025-10-15T13:02:55.264Z"}], "date": "2025-10-15T13:02:55.264Z", "id": "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "core_question": "How do I consistently get useful, professional-quality output from an LLM instead of generic fluff that I have to rewrite anyway?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A tax attorney needs to turn rough voice-memo notes from a client meeting into a formal advisory letter. The prompt specifies: Role \u2014 senior tax attorney with 15 years in corporate restructuring; Audience \u2014 CFO of a mid-market manufacturing company with no tax background; Goal \u2014 a 2-page advisory letter explaining the tax implications of a proposed acquisition structure with recommended next steps; Constraints \u2014 must reference IRC Section 368, avoid definitive legal conclusions, maintain client-appropriate tone, stay under 800 words. The raw expertise (bullet points from the meeting) is appended verbatim. The first draft comes back too formal and missing a key nuance about state tax nexus \u2014 a single follow-up instruction fixes both, producing a letter that needs only a compliance review before sending.", "related_frameworks": ["expertise-translation-framework", "ai-fluency-root-principles", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "goldilocks-prompting", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "delegation-prompt-management-system", "intent-engineering", "nine-principles-of-business-writing-with-ai", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "ai-task-level-diagnostic", "smart-machines-require-proper-structure-smrps-five-tier-prom", "six-layer-specification-stack"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Assistance Level Spectrum", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A six-level classification system that maps operational tasks to the appropriate degree of AI automation, from conversational advisor to fully autonomous, based on volume, consistency, risk, data access, and speed.", "when_to_apply": ["We have a working AI prototype in chat form and need to decide what the production version should look like", "A vendor is pitching us an autonomous AI agent but our volume is maybe 50 cases a week \u2014 is that worth it?", "Our team is debating whether to build an AI agent or just use structured prompts with a human in the loop", "We want to scale an AI use case from manual to automated but need to know what 'automated' actually means for our risk profile", "Leadership wants us to 'add AI' to a process and we need to scope the right level of investment"], "principles": ["AI assistance exists on a spectrum, not a binary between chat and full autonomy", "Most valuable work lives at Level 3 or 4, not at the autonomous extreme", "Higher levels require exponentially more complexity for diminishing marginal value", "Humans should be positioned at critical decision points, not removed entirely", "Start at a lower level and move up only when volume and value justify it", "Match the solution shape to the problem shape before building anything"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176103954", "title": "How to Tell What AI You Actually Need for a Job (LOL Yes There's a Prompt)", "date": "2025-10-14T13:03:36.350Z"}], "date": "2025-10-14T13:03:36.350Z", "id": "ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "core_question": "We know AI could help with this task, but should we build a chatbot, a workflow, an agent, or something in between \u2014 and how do we avoid overbuilding?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This invoice-processing use case sits at Level 3 \u2014 structured workflow with human checkpoints \u2014 not Level 5 autonomous. The volume is 200 invoices per week with three exception types, which justifies automating the extraction and routing but not the approval. The team was scoping a fully autonomous agent, but the error cost on misrouted invoices ($5K+ per incident) means the complexity jump to Level 5 destroys the ROI. A Level 3 solution \u2014 AI extracts, classifies, and drafts the routing, human confirms exceptions \u2014 captures 85% of the time savings at a fraction of the build cost.", "related_frameworks": ["the-three-question-workflow-audit", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-implementation-spectrum", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "ai-task-level-diagnostic", "agent-strategy-sequencing", "the-expensive-chat-diagnostic", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "three-tiers-of-agent-capability", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "agent-economics-viability-model", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Task Level Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A five-dimension scoring rubric (volume, consistency, risk, data access, speed) that maps any operational task to one of six AI assistance levels and produces a concrete implementation recommendation.", "when_to_apply": ["I know I want to use AI for this task but I'm not sure if it needs a simple prompt or something more sophisticated", "My team is proposing to build an AI agent for a process and I need to sanity-check whether that level of investment is warranted", "I have a list of operational tasks and need to prioritize which ones to automate with AI first based on their characteristics", "Someone asked me what kind of AI solution they need and I want a structured way to diagnose it instead of guessing", "We're debating between buying an AI workflow tool versus just using ChatGPT for a recurring business process"], "principles": ["Assess volume, consistency, risk, data access, and speed independently before synthesizing a level", "Score each dimension as Low/Medium/High before pattern-matching to a level", "Confirm understanding of the task before delivering a diagnosis", "Tie every recommendation specifically to the diagnosed level, not generically to AI", "Always include a single actionable next step executable within one week", "Flag confidence level and any assumptions made during assessment", "Do not bias toward a higher level before completing the assessment"], "steps": ["Ask the user to describe the task, current process, and frequency", "Probe four diagnostic dimensions\u2014frequency/volume, consistency, risk tolerance, and data access\u2014scoring each Low/Medium/High", "Summarize your understanding of all four dimensions and confirm accuracy with the user", "Pattern-match the dimension scores to the six-level rubric and classify the task", "Output the diagnosis with reasoning, a task-specific implementation description, confidence level, and one concrete next step this week"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "176103954", "title": "How to Tell What AI You Actually Need for a Job (LOL Yes There's a Prompt)", "date": "2025-10-14T13:03:36.350Z"}], "date": "2025-10-14T13:03:36.350Z", "id": "ai-task-level-diagnostic", "core_question": "I have a specific task I'm thinking about using AI for \u2014 but I don't know if I need a simple prompt, a workflow tool, or a full autonomous agent, and I don't want to over- or under-invest.", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "An operations manager describes their invoice reconciliation process: 200+ invoices per week, highly standardized format, low error tolerance but errors are catchable before payment, all data lives in a shared ERP system, and speed matters because month-end close is compressed. Scoring: volume is High, consistency is High, risk is Medium (catchable before irreversible action), data access is High, speed need is High. That pattern maps to Level 5 \u2014 a supervised autonomous workflow where AI processes the full batch with human review only on flagged exceptions. The concrete next step: export one week of invoices to a test folder and run a batch reconciliation using an AI tool with the current manual checklist as validation criteria, measuring match rate against human output.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "task-decomposition-framework", "the-implementation-spectrum", "ai-leverage-audit", "three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "agency-task-selection-grid", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "edge-first-automation-framework", "core-automation-readiness-check", "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Tool Implementation Sequence", "type": "procedure", "description": "A phased organizational rollout process for deploying AI tools that starts with auditing existing shadow AI usage before selecting or purchasing tools, then pilots, measures, and scales based on demonstrated ROI.", "when_to_apply": ["We know employees are using ChatGPT and other AI tools on their own but we have no visibility or governance around it", "I've been asked to build an AI tool rollout plan for my organization and don't know where to start", "Our first AI pilot fizzled out and we need a structured approach to try again with measurable outcomes", "IT security keeps blocking our AI tool purchases and we need a way to move forward without bypassing them", "Leadership wants to see ROI numbers before approving a broader AI tools budget"], "principles": ["Audit what employees are already using before deciding what to buy\u2014most organizations are starting from 50%+ shadow AI adoption, not zero", "Secure executive sponsorship before starting; this cannot be driven bottom-up through middle management", "Define 2-3 specific, measurable use cases for the pilot rather than trying to solve everything at once", "Treat security review as risk management with defined thresholds (green/yellow/red data zones), not an open-ended veto", "Select pilot users who are already enthusiastic and respected by peers, not 'average' employees", "Measure baseline metrics before the pilot starts so ROI can be proven with real numbers afterward", "Scale what works based on pilot evidence; do not roll out to everyone simultaneously", "Move as fast as the organization's risk tolerance allows, but never skip the measurement steps"], "steps": ["Foundation Phase: Conduct a shadow AI audit (anonymous survey, expense report review, IT network analysis, interviews with top performers) to discover what tools employees are already paying for and why", "Foundation Phase: Secure executive sponsorship with budget authority in the $200-500/employee/month range and mandate to override 'evaluate next quarter' delays", "Foundation Phase: Form a cross-functional team of 5-7 people (business unit, IT, security, finance, HR) with explicit decision-making authority", "Assessment & Selection Phase: Identify 2-3 priority use cases that are high-impact, high-frequency, already solved by shadow AI, and measurable", "Assessment & Selection Phase: Define a data classification decision matrix (green/yellow/red zones) to establish what data can and cannot go to cloud AI tools", "Assessment & Selection Phase: Evaluate 2-3 tools in parallel using real work examples from priority use cases over 1-2 weeks, not feature matrices", "Security & Planning Phase: Complete IT security review focused on specific controls (data storage, processing agreements, audit logging, incident response) with a target of days-to-weeks, not months", "Security & Planning Phase: Establish a one-page acceptable use policy that specifies exactly what data cannot be input and what tasks are encouraged", "Security & Planning Phase: Select 10-100 pilot users who are already enthusiastic about AI, respected by peers, and have genuine pain points the tool addresses", "Pilot Phase: Deploy tools with pre-configured accounts, templates, and a quick-response support channel; define success metrics before launch", "Pilot Phase: Deliver use-case-specific training (not generic AI 101) with peer-to-peer knowledge sharing", "Pilot Phase: Run weekly check-ins for the first month to collect quantitative and qualitative feedback and adjust in real time", "Measurement Phase: Collect before/after metrics on task time, quality, and user satisfaction; document 2-3 specific case studies with named users and hard numbers", "Measurement Phase: Calculate total cost of ownership (subscriptions + implementation + training + management) against demonstrated productivity value", "Scaling Phase: Expand first to roles/use cases most similar to the successful pilot; do not scale failed use cases until root cause is understood", "Scaling Phase: Present pilot ROI to executive sponsor and request specific budget for broader deployment with a phased timeline", "Optimization Phase: Establish monthly or quarterly one-page reporting on usage, productivity gains, cost tracking, and user satisfaction", "Optimization Phase: Create a continuous feedback loop (measure \u2192 learn \u2192 improve \u2192 measure) and use demonstrated success to plan next-phase investments"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "175240821", "title": "Dear Manager, Your AI Budget is Costing Me My Career", "date": "2025-10-11T14:02:31.562Z"}], "date": "2025-10-11T14:02:31.562Z", "id": "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "core_question": "We know we need to deploy AI tools across the organization, but how do we go from scattered, unauthorized usage to a governed rollout that actually demonstrates ROI and scales?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The shadow AI audit revealed that 62% of the marketing team is already paying for ChatGPT Plus out of pocket, and three sales reps built their own GPT wrappers for proposal generation. That means the organization isn't choosing whether to adopt AI \u2014 it's choosing whether to govern what's already happening. The right move is to formalize those two proven use cases (content drafting and proposal generation) as the pilot scope, select 25 users from among those already-enthusiastic teams, measure their current task times now before deploying the sanctioned tool, and give the cross-functional team four weeks to produce hard before/after numbers. That evidence \u2014 not a feature comparison spreadsheet \u2014 is what unlocks the executive sponsor's budget for phase two.", "related_frameworks": ["data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance", "three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ai-vs-software-budget-reframe", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "ai-leverage-audit", "ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "function-specific-quality-gates", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-implementation-spectrum", "capability-dissipation-gap"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI vs. Software Budget Reframe", "type": "reframe", "description": "Challenges the conventional practice of comparing AI tool costs to other software subscriptions, arguing they should instead be compared to human compensation and employee turnover costs.", "when_to_apply": ["Finance rejected our AI tool request because the per-seat cost is 10x what we pay for other SaaS tools", "I need to build a business case for AI tooling but the budget owner keeps comparing it to Jira or Confluence pricing", "Our best engineers are frustrated that we won't invest in modern AI tools while competitors are giving their teams everything", "How do I frame AI tool spend so leadership stops treating it as a software line item and starts seeing it as a talent investment?", "Shadow AI spending is already happening across our org and I need to make the case for doing it officially"], "principles": ["AI tool pricing competes against human compensation costs, not other software tools", "The correct benchmark for a $400/month AI subscription is employee turnover cost ($50,000-$200,000 per departure), not a $10-50/month SaaS seat", "A single retained employee whose departure is prevented by modern tooling pays for 10-40 colleagues' AI subscriptions", "Frame AI spend in terms of specific productivity value (hours saved \u00d7 fully-loaded employee cost) rather than abstract efficiency claims", "Shadow AI spend is already occurring without governance; the question is whether to invest strategically or let it happen chaotically"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "175240821", "title": "Dear Manager, Your AI Budget is Costing Me My Career", "date": "2025-10-11T14:02:31.562Z"}], "date": "2025-10-11T14:02:31.562Z", "id": "ai-vs-software-budget-reframe", "core_question": "Why does my AI tool budget keep getting rejected when the per-seat cost is compared to traditional software, instead of being measured against what it actually replaces?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The $400/month AI coding assistant looks absurd next to a $15/month Slack seat \u2014 that's the wrong comparison. The right one: a senior developer costs $180K fully loaded, and replacing one who quits costs $100K-$200K in recruiting, ramp-up, and lost productivity. If the AI tooling keeps even one developer from leaving because they're stuck using outdated workflows, that single retention event pays for the entire team's AI subscriptions for two years. The budget line isn't 'software'; it's 'talent retention and productivity infrastructure.'", "related_frameworks": ["ai-leverage-audit", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "budget-replacement-framework", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ai-tool-discovery-and-evaluation-system", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Data Classification Decision Matrix for AI Tool Governance", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-zone classification system (green/yellow/red) that determines which organizational data can be processed by cloud AI tools, enabling security governance that enables rather than blocks AI adoption.", "when_to_apply": ["Our security team blocked all AI tools and now employees are using personal ChatGPT accounts with company data", "We need an acceptable use policy for AI that distinguishes between low-risk and high-risk data types", "I'm in a regulated industry and need to figure out which AI use cases we can approve without a six-month review", "A manager wants to fast-track AI tool approval for their team but IT says everything needs the same security review", "We're treating internal meeting notes the same as customer PII for AI tool access and it's killing adoption"], "principles": ["Classify data by sensitivity level first, then determine which AI deployment model is appropriate for each level", "Most enterprise use cases fall into green or yellow zones where cloud AI tools are permissible with appropriate controls", "Treating everything as red zone creates security theater, not real security, while driving employees to unauthorized tools", "The question is not 'can we eliminate all risk?' but 'does the productivity benefit justify the residual risk with appropriate controls?'"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "175240821", "title": "Dear Manager, Your AI Budget is Costing Me My Career", "date": "2025-10-11T14:02:31.562Z"}], "date": "2025-10-11T14:02:31.562Z", "id": "data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance", "core_question": "How do I let my organization actually use AI tools without either exposing sensitive data or locking everything down so tightly that people route around security entirely?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "This sales team's use case \u2014 summarizing publicly available competitor data and drafting outreach emails \u2014 is solidly green zone. The data is non-sensitive, the output is human-reviewed before sending, and blocking this use case provides zero security benefit while costing dozens of hours per week. The red flag the security team raised about 'customer data in AI tools' applies to the CRM analytics project, not this one. Classify them separately: green-light the outreach workflow today, route the CRM project through yellow-zone controls with data anonymization, and reserve the red zone only for the PII-heavy insurance claims processing where the regulatory exposure is real.", "related_frameworks": ["three-question-ai-tool-evaluation-framework", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "talent-suppression-diagnostic", "ten-question-vendor-diligence-protocol", "the-two-door-principle", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "realpolitik-safety", "iteration-as-safety-vs-safety-as-precondition"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Function-Specific Quality Gates", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A content evaluation framework that applies different, purpose-built rubrics to different content types \u2014 each scored on 5 dimensions specific to what makes that content type actually useful \u2014 rather than applying generic quality checks.", "when_to_apply": ["We're producing dozens of AI-drafted blog posts per week and reviewers are drowning \u2014 I need a triage system that tells me which ones are publishable vs. need rework", "My team's content quality feedback is always vague like 'this doesn't feel right' and I need an objective, repeatable standard", "I want to build an automated quality check into my content pipeline that catches structural problems before a human ever sees the draft", "I'm evaluating a specific artifact \u2014 a sales email, a PRD, a case study \u2014 and I need a rubric tailored to what makes that content type actually work", "I'm onboarding new writers and need a teachable framework for what 'good' looks like that goes beyond grammar and style"], "principles": ["Different content types have different structural requirements for usefulness \u2014 one rubric cannot evaluate all types", "Score on substance and specificity, not grammar or style; style tools already handle surface-level issues", "Feedback must be location-specific and actionable, not vague directives ('Paragraph 3 says X \u2014 replace with Y because Z')", "Flag concrete anti-patterns for each content type (e.g., metrics without denominators, generic pain points, unverifiable claims)", "Use a 0\u20135 scale per dimension so quality is measurable, trackable, and comparable over time", "The system should teach writers by giving them a repeatable feedback loop, not just filter output", "Set minimum score thresholds before human review or publication to protect human attention for the top 1\u20135% of content"], "steps": ["Identify the content type being evaluated (blog post, sales email, PRD, etc.)", "Select the rubric with the 5 dimensions specific to that content type's purpose", "Score the artifact 0\u20135 on each dimension using concrete criteria for each score level", "Flag specific anti-pattern phrases or structural failures present in the artifact", "Generate actionable, location-specific feedback with an example of how to fix each flagged issue", "Produce an overall score and a PASS / REVISE verdict based on a threshold (e.g., 3.5+ average)", "Route: below threshold goes back to the author with specific feedback; above threshold escalates to human review"], "confidence": "medium", "extraction_incomplete": true, "missing_content": "The post references 20 content-type-specific prompt rubrics but only fully details the blog post rubric (5 dimensions with scoring criteria). The rubrics for the other 19 content types are described at a high level but their complete scoring dimensions and anti-pattern lists are behind an external link and not visible in the provided text.", "source_posts": [{"id": "175759748", "title": "I Got Tired of AI Slop so I Built 20 Prompts to Fix It\u2014They Save My Teams 100's of Hours", "date": "2025-10-10T13:03:31.713Z"}], "date": "2025-10-10T13:03:31.713Z", "id": "function-specific-quality-gates", "core_question": "How do I evaluate whether this specific piece of content is actually good at doing what it's supposed to do, when 'good' means completely different things for a sales email versus a PRD versus a blog post?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This PRD scores 4/5 on Problem Specificity (names the user segment and quantifies the pain), 2/5 on Success Criteria Definition (says 'improve retention' without a baseline or target number \u2014 paragraph 4 should replace 'improve retention' with 'increase 30-day retention from 34% to 42% by Q3'), and 1/5 on Edge Case Coverage (no mention of what happens when the API rate-limits). Overall average: 2.6 \u2014 REVISE verdict. The critical anti-pattern here is 'metrics without denominators,' which appears three times in the success metrics section. This goes back to the author with location-specific fixes before any PM reviews it.", "related_frameworks": ["three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "the-five-evaluation-prompts", "definition-of-done-rubric", "done-definition-pattern", "correctness-definition-framework", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "nine-principles-of-business-writing-with-ai", "surgical-editing-protocol", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "delegation-spec-drill", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "ai-resume-bullet-quality-framework", "five-element-ai-resume-bullet-test", "knowledge-work-practice-gym"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "extraction_incomplete", "note": "Pass 1 flagged this extraction as incomplete. Missing: The post references 20 content-type-specific prompt rubrics but only fully details the blog post rubric (5 dimensions with scoring criteria). The rubrics for the other 19 content types are described at a high level but their complete scoring dimensions and anti-pattern lists are behind an external link and not visible in the provided text.. Re-extract from full source post."}]}
{"name": "AI Use Classification: Tool vs. Replacement", "type": "distinction", "description": "A diagnostic distinction that separates beneficial AI use (enhancing human capability) from harmful AI use (replacing human experience or cognition).", "when_to_apply": ["My kid is using ChatGPT for homework and I can't tell if it's helping or hurting their learning", "We need household rules about when our children can and can't use AI tools", "A student wants to use AI for a creative writing assignment and I'm unsure if that's appropriate", "I'm worried that AI assistance is atrophying skills my team or family should be developing", "Someone claims they're 'using AI as a tool' but the AI seems to be doing all the actual cognitive work"], "principles": ["AI helps when it enhances human capability; it harms when it replaces human experience", "The key test: who is doing the thinking?", "Learning requires struggle; AI that eliminates struggle eliminates learning", "Tool use is purposeful and bounded; replacement use is open-ended and drifting"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172113939", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z"}, {"id": "179592990", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-11-23T00:00:48.317Z"}], "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z", "id": "ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement", "core_question": "Is this use of AI helping someone build capability they'll keep, or is it quietly doing the thinking for them and leaving them weaker?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "A teenager asks ChatGPT to write a persuasive essay on climate change. This is replacement, not tool use \u2014 the tell is that the student never had to organize an argument, weigh evidence, or find their own voice. The struggle of structuring a persuasive case IS the learning. Contrast: using AI to find three credible sources on ocean acidification rates, then writing the argument yourself. That's tool use \u2014 the AI handled retrieval (where humans have no cognitive edge over search), while the student retained the thinking work that builds the skill.", "related_frameworks": ["tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-framework", "cognitive-independence-protocol", "human-first-protocol", "experiential-debt", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "boundary-engineering", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "reality-anchoring-techniques"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "3-Tier AI Fluency Assessment", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system for evaluating candidate AI competency at three levels\u2014Literacy, Integration, and Leadership\u2014mapped to role type so interviewers assess what actually matters for each position.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm writing interview questions for a role that uses AI tools but I'm not sure what level of AI skill to actually test for", "Our engineering interviews test AI literacy the same way as our director-level interviews and I suspect we're miscalibrating", "We're building a rubric for evaluating AI competency across different job levels and need a consistent framework", "A hiring manager wants to require 'AI experience' for a role but can't articulate what that means concretely", "We need to distinguish between candidates who can use AI tools versus those who can architect AI-augmented workflows for a team"], "principles": ["Tier 1 (AI Literacy) is required for all roles regardless of technical depth", "Tier 2 (AI Integration) applies to technical roles requiring systematic workflow implementation", "Tier 3 (AI Leadership) applies to senior roles requiring strategic adoption decisions and team development", "Each tier has distinct assessment methods, not just different question difficulty", "Higher tiers subsume lower tiers\u2014leaders must also demonstrate literacy and integration", "Cost consciousness and output verification are evaluated at every tier"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "174185881", "title": "The Complete AI Interview Guide: What Actually Works in 2025", "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z"}, {"id": "175374136", "title": "Nobody Can Measure AI Skills, So I Built the First AI Skill Evaluator for Everyone (+ Prompts to Level Up)", "date": "2025-10-07T13:02:58.078Z"}], "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z", "id": "3-tier-ai-fluency-assessment", "core_question": "Am I testing the right level of AI competency for this role, or am I asking senior-level strategy questions to a junior hire and basic tool questions to a VP candidate?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This product manager role is mid-level, non-technical \u2014 it falls squarely in Tier 1 (AI Literacy). The candidate needs to demonstrate they can use AI tools effectively in their daily work, verify outputs critically, and understand cost implications of different approaches. Asking them to design an organizational AI adoption strategy would be Tier 3 overkill that screens out strong candidates for capabilities the role doesn't require. The right assessment is a live task: hand them a messy dataset and see if they can use ChatGPT to extract the three insights that matter, then articulate why they trust (or don't trust) the output.", "related_frameworks": ["verification-over-credentialing-framework", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "ai-task-level-diagnostic", "level-band-improvement-roadmap", "fluency-velocity", "three-categories-of-ai-exposure", "the-implementation-spectrum", "star-c-situation-task-action-result-constraints", "goodhart-s-law-metrics-trap-ai-hiring-application", "tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Fluency Velocity", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes a person's snapshot AI skill score from their learning rate, arguing that learning velocity is a more predictive and actionable measure of future AI competence than current level.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm choosing between two people for an AI-heavy role and one has more experience but the other seems to be picking things up faster", "Someone on my team was enthusiastic about AI tools six months ago but seems to have stalled at the same level", "We have limited budget for AI upskilling \u2014 how do we identify who will get the most out of training investment", "I want to know if my own AI learning is actually progressing or if I'm just repeating comfortable patterns", "Setting development timelines for a team's AI fluency and need realistic benchmarks for how fast people can grow"], "principles": ["A lower score with high velocity beats a higher score with low velocity over any meaningful time horizon", "The clearest predictor of future competence is using AI itself to accelerate AI learning", "Velocity is measured by how quickly a person can internalize and reliably apply a new prompting pattern", "Systematic documentation and deliberate replacement of old habits signal high velocity", "Standing still in AI fluency means falling behind as the competitive baseline rises"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "175374136", "title": "Nobody Can Measure AI Skills, So I Built the First AI Skill Evaluator for Everyone (+ Prompts to Level Up)", "date": "2025-10-07T13:02:58.078Z"}], "date": "2025-10-07T13:02:58.078Z", "id": "fluency-velocity", "core_question": "Should I bet on the person who's further ahead right now, or the one who's learning faster \u2014 and how do I tell the difference?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "Candidate A scores at the 'Practitioner' level \u2014 they use AI daily and can chain prompts effectively. Candidate B scores at 'Novice-plus' but went from zero to structured multi-turn workflows in three weeks, is documenting every new pattern they learn, and is already using AI to generate practice scenarios for themselves. Candidate B's velocity dwarfs Candidate A's, who hasn't meaningfully changed their approach in four months. Over any six-month horizon, Candidate B will surpass Candidate A \u2014 and the gap will accelerate because high-velocity learners compound. The investment should go to Candidate B, and Candidate A needs a diagnostic on what's creating their plateau.", "related_frameworks": ["3-tier-ai-fluency-assessment", "level-band-improvement-roadmap", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "quick-self-assessment-are-you-in-the-5-or-95", "the-jagged-frontier-framework", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "experiential-debt", "ai-task-level-diagnostic", "capability-surface-mapping", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Level-Band Improvement Roadmap", "type": "procedure", "description": "A three-stage, level-specific progression path that prescribes distinct learning strategies for moving from each AI fluency band to the next.", "when_to_apply": ["I took an AI fluency assessment and scored in the middle \u2014 now what do I actually do to improve?", "I use AI every day but feel stuck at the same skill level \u2014 more practice isn't helping", "I'm designing an AI upskilling program and need different tracks for beginners vs. intermediate vs. advanced users", "My team ranges from AI-curious to AI-fluent and I need a structured progression path for each group", "I'm good at prompting but I can't seem to teach what I do to others \u2014 my knowledge feels trapped in my head"], "principles": ["Moving from level 3 to 5 requires building mental models (inference, context windows, outcome-oriented prompting), not memorizing frameworks", "Moving from level 5 to 7 requires systematization: auditable patterns, prompt yield measurement, documented workflows transferable to teammates", "Moving from level 7 to 9 requires teaching others and solving novel problems \u2014 both force clarity and reveal gaps", "Use AI itself as the primary learning tool at every stage to simultaneously build the meta-skill of AI-accelerated learning", "Each stage's goal is to make implicit knowledge explicit and transferable"], "steps": ["Stage 1 (3\u21925): Build mental models \u2014 understand LLM inference and prediction, grasp context windows, learn outcome-oriented prompting, distinguish search/retrieval from generation; use AI to teach you these concepts", "Stage 2 (5\u21927): Professionalize practice \u2014 create auditable prompt patterns, measure prompt yield, build workflows teammates can replicate, establish feedback loops and quality checks, develop versioned prompt libraries", "Stage 3 (7\u21929): Teach and innovate \u2014 document workflows for others to expose gaps, design team-scale systems and custom tools, take on novel problems that force new technique combinations, use AI to accelerate others' learning"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "175374136", "title": "Nobody Can Measure AI Skills, So I Built the First AI Skill Evaluator for Everyone (+ Prompts to Level Up)", "date": "2025-10-07T13:02:58.078Z"}], "date": "2025-10-07T13:02:58.078Z", "id": "level-band-improvement-roadmap", "core_question": "I know roughly where I stand with AI skills, but what specifically should I be doing differently to get to the next level \u2014 not just more of the same?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Someone scoring at level 5 \u2014 they can get good outputs from ChatGPT but their prompts are improvised every time and nothing is reusable. The plateau isn't a knowledge problem; it's a systematization problem. The move to level 7 requires building auditable prompt patterns, measuring which prompts actually produce reliable yield, and documenting workflows that a teammate could pick up without a walkthrough. Until the implicit craft becomes explicit and transferable, they'll stay at 5 no matter how many hours they spend prompting.", "related_frameworks": ["3-tier-ai-fluency-assessment", "fluency-velocity", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-six-meta-skills-framework", "ai-native-job-transformation-roadmap", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "delegation-prompt-management-system", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "quick-self-assessment-are-you-in-the-5-or-95", "experiential-debt", "intent-engineering", "five-core-knowledge-work-skills-plus-one-to-argue-against"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Multi-Chat Deck Architecture", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequential multi-conversation workflow that separates planning, chunk generation, and assembly into distinct AI sessions to maintain narrative coherence and style consistency across complex presentations.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to generate a 30+ slide deck with AI but every time I try, the later slides lose coherence and drift off-brand", "I'm building an executive presentation from multiple messy data sources and need to synthesize before I generate slides", "My AI-generated decks have style inconsistencies between the beginning and end \u2014 colors shift, formatting drifts", "I want a repeatable workflow for AI-assisted deck creation that I can hand off to my team", "Context window limits are causing my AI to forget the narrative arc partway through a long presentation"], "principles": ["Separate planning from execution: the architect phase creates the blueprint before any slides are generated", "Chunk execution into 5-8 slide segments to stay within effective context window for visual generation", "Use a dedicated assembly and validation pass to catch cross-chunk inconsistencies before delivery", "Extract and reuse style JSON once so brand consistency is enforced programmatically across all chunks", "Reserve human attention for organizational conflict resolution and narrative judgment, not formatting"], "steps": ["Architect chat: input messy multi-source data and audience requirements; output story structure, slide count, and data requirements blueprint", "Style extraction: analyze an existing on-brand deck and capture colors, fonts, logo placement, and margins as reusable JSON", "Generator chats: build 5-8 slide chunks using the blueprint and style JSON, with explicit tool/skill enforcement in each prompt", "Assembly chat: review all chunks for narrative flow, data consistency, readability, and brand compliance; produce pass/fail report", "Human review: focus on message refinement, organizational narrative, and stakeholder considerations rather than formatting"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "175163754", "title": "Want a Week a Month Back? I Automated AI for PowerPoint with These 16 Prompts", "date": "2025-10-03T13:02:46.567Z"}], "date": "2025-10-03T13:02:46.567Z", "id": "multi-chat-deck-architecture", "core_question": "How do I get AI to produce a long, complex presentation that stays coherent and on-brand from slide 1 to slide 40, when a single conversation falls apart halfway through?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "A 35-slide board deck drawing from three data sources needs to be generated. A single-pass approach would lose the narrative thread around slide 15 and start contradicting the financial summary by slide 25. The right move is to run an architect chat first that synthesizes the messy inputs into a structural blueprint \u2014 story arc, slide-by-slide purpose, data allocation \u2014 before any visual generation happens. Style JSON gets extracted once from the existing brand deck, then five generator chats each produce 7-slide chunks against that blueprint and style spec. The assembly chat then catches that chunk 3 used a different revenue figure than chunk 1 and that the transition from chunk 2 to chunk 3 dropped the strategic narrative thread. Human review focuses entirely on whether the CEO's message lands with the board, not on font consistency.", "related_frameworks": ["task-decomposition-framework", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "delegation-prompt-management-system", "six-layer-specification-stack", "hierarchical-context-loading", "scaffolded-reasoning-architecture", "done-definition-pattern", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "routing-layer-specification-layer-review-layer", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "work-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Bad Prompt vs. Good Prompt Diagnostic (Visual-First Validation)", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A before/after diagnostic that identifies why AI presentation prompts fail by checking whether validation criteria prioritize visual execution over content sophistication.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI-generated presentation passes all my content criteria but the slides are visually broken \u2014 overlapping text, unreadable contrast, ugly boxes everywhere", "I wrote a very detailed prompt for a slide deck but a shorter, simpler prompt actually produces better-looking results", "I keep getting border boxes and rounded rectangles in AI-generated slides no matter how I describe the aesthetic I want", "I want to audit an existing presentation prompt to figure out why it produces inconsistent visual quality across runs", "I need to scale AI slide generation across my team and want to make the prompts reliably produce usable visual output"], "principles": ["A prompt that only validates content and narrative will fail visually even if every content check passes", "Prompts must explicitly ban failure-prone design patterns (border boxes, outline shapes, rounded rectangles) rather than just specifying desired aesthetics", "Require the model to describe its visual approach before executing \u2014 layout strategy, color palette, typography hierarchy \u2014 as a validation gate", "Visual failure conditions must be explicit: any bordered text box means immediate failure and redesign, not a warning", "Contrast ratio and readability requirements (e.g., 4.5:1 minimum) must be testable checkpoints, not aspirational guidelines", "Ambiguous creative constraints ('feel like McKinsey but startup') are not actionable for visual execution and should be removed"], "steps": ["Audit your prompt: does it specify which generation tool/skill to use explicitly? If not, the model may silently downgrade.", "Check validation criteria: are failure conditions visual (overlapping elements, contrast ratios, text under objects) or only content-based?", "Check for banned patterns: does the prompt explicitly prohibit border boxes, rounded rectangles, and outline shapes?", "Check for pre-execution planning: does the prompt require the model to describe layout strategy, color palette, and typography before generating?", "Check creative constraints: remove any constraints that cannot be evaluated visually or programmatically", "If all checks pass, the prompt is structured for visual reliability; if any fail, revise that layer before adding content complexity"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "175163754", "title": "Want a Week a Month Back? I Automated AI for PowerPoint with These 16 Prompts", "date": "2025-10-03T13:02:46.567Z"}], "date": "2025-10-03T13:02:46.567Z", "id": "bad-prompt-vs-good-prompt-diagnostic-visual-first-validation", "core_question": "My AI-generated slides check every content box I specified but still look terrible \u2014 what's structurally wrong with how I'm prompting?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This prompt fails the diagnostic on three of five checks. It has detailed content validation (narrative arc, data accuracy, audience framing) but zero visual failure conditions \u2014 no banned patterns, no contrast requirements, no pre-execution layout planning. The tell: the prompt says 'clean, modern design' but never defines what that means in testable visual terms. Adding explicit bans on bordered text boxes and rounded rectangles, requiring a 4.5:1 contrast ratio checkpoint, and forcing the model to declare its layout strategy before generating would address the three failure points without touching the content layer at all.", "related_frameworks": ["the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "prompt-lifecycle-diagnostic-checklist", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "constraint-architecture", "done-definition-pattern", "definition-of-done-rubric", "deliverable-first-prompt-framework", "multi-chat-deck-architecture", "scar-tissue-rules-file", "goldilocks-prompting", "the-five-specification-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Deliverable-First Prompt Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-part structure for writing AI prompts organized around the finished business deliverable rather than the underlying task, enabling production-ready outputs in a single iteration.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep asking AI to build executive-ready outputs like board decks or financial summaries and getting shallow, unusable drafts", "I have a recurring deliverable like a weekly dashboard that I want to templatize as a reusable AI prompt with consistent quality", "My AI prompt produced something that's 60% right but I can't figure out which part of the prompt to fix to close the gap", "I need to translate a vague stakeholder request like 'get me the board materials' into a prompt that actually produces what they expect", "I want to build a shared library of working prompts my team can reuse without needing to understand prompt engineering"], "principles": ["Start with the outcome, not the task \u2014 define what the finished deliverable looks like, who sees it, and what decisions it supports before writing any prompt", "Embed business context, output format, quality criteria, and audience needs directly into the prompt rather than leaving them implicit", "Expect 2\u20133 iterations; the first prompt won't be perfect \u2014 run it, identify what breaks or is missing, then add specificity", "Document working prompts with notes on expected data format and edge cases so they can be reliably reused", "Organize prompts by deliverable type (board decks, financial models, client reports), not by AI feature or task type"], "steps": ["Define the finished deliverable: ask who sees it, what format it takes, and what decision it supports \u2014 this is your prompt's anchor", "Write the Context and Purpose component: state who the output is for and why it matters (e.g., 'Build a quarterly revenue analysis for CFO review to identify budget variances requiring action')", "Write the Specific Components section: list exactly what must appear in the output (e.g., monthly actuals vs. budget by product line, top 5 variance drivers, conditional formatting rules)", "Write the Data Structure section: describe the source data format, volume expectations, and any update cadence", "Write the Quality Checks section: specify validation rules the output must pass (e.g., 'all revenue ties to source data total, no formulas reference external sheets')", "Run the prompt and evaluate the output against the deliverable definition", "Iterate by identifying gaps or errors and adding specificity to the relevant component \u2014 repeat until production-ready", "Save the finalized prompt with notes on expected data format and known edge cases"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "175063196", "title": "We've Had Two Massive Excel AI Launches This Week But ZERO Useful Prompts\u2014I Built 18 That Save 100's of Hours", "date": "2025-10-02T13:02:32.538Z"}], "date": "2025-10-02T13:02:32.538Z", "id": "deliverable-first-prompt-framework", "core_question": "Why does my AI keep producing generic, unusable outputs when I need something specific enough to actually put in front of a stakeholder?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The CFO needs a quarterly revenue variance analysis, but the prompt 'analyze our revenue data' produces a generic summary with no actionable structure. Anchoring to the deliverable reveals what's missing: the output needs monthly actuals vs. budget by product line, the top 5 variance drivers ranked by dollar impact, and conditional formatting that flags variances over 10%. The data is a 12-month CSV with columns for product line, month, actual, and budget. Quality checks: all line items must sum to the total revenue figure in the source, and no variance explanation can be a generic placeholder like 'market conditions.' After the first iteration, the model missed the conditional formatting spec and produced flat tables \u2014 adding explicit formatting rules to the Specific Components section fixed it in round two.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "prompt-lifecycle-diagnostic-checklist", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "delegation-prompt-management-system", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "definition-of-done-rubric", "done-definition-pattern", "goldilocks-prompting", "meta-prompt-anatomy-seven-components", "correctness-definition-framework", "the-five-specification-primitives", "six-layer-specification-stack", "delegation-spec-drill", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "multi-chat-deck-architecture", "bad-prompt-vs-good-prompt-diagnostic-visual-first-validation", "jiorp-structure"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Agentic Artifact Design", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes work products from static documents into structured data substrates designed from the ground up to be both human-readable and operable by AI agents.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm setting up a Notion or Airtable workspace and want AI agents to be able to read, update, and act on the data inside it", "Our team has great documentation but every time we try to plug an agent into it, the agent can't reliably find or update what it needs", "I'm designing the knowledge base that will sit between human decision-makers and autonomous agents \u2014 what structure should it have?", "We're migrating from prose-heavy specs and reports to something more agent-friendly but I'm not sure what 'agent-friendly' actually means structurally", "My AI agent keeps hallucinating or missing information because the source artifact is a long narrative document instead of structured records"], "principles": ["Design artifacts for agent operability first, human readability second \u2014 not the other way around", "Structured data fields (database rows, typed columns) are preferable to flowing prose because they are queryable and individually validatable", "The artifact should serve as the interface between human intent and agent execution", "An artifact that an agent can traverse, update, and act upon autonomously is fundamentally different from a document that humans edit manually"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "174406124", "title": "The Easiest Custom AI Agent Build Guide Out There: Build Custom Agents in 10 Minutes with Notion", "date": "2025-09-24T13:02:06.794Z"}], "date": "2025-09-24T13:02:06.794Z", "id": "agentic-artifact-design", "core_question": "Are my team's work products actually usable by AI agents, or are we producing documents that look organized to humans but are opaque to any autonomous system that needs to act on them?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The conventional approach is to write a project spec as a well-structured Google Doc with headers, narrative context, and bullet points \u2014 it looks clean and professional. That's wrong for an agentic workflow. An agent can't reliably parse \"see the dependencies section above\" or extract a due date buried in a paragraph. The spec should be a database where each task is a row with typed fields: owner, status, dependency IDs, acceptance criteria, deadline. Now an agent can query open tasks, check dependency chains, update statuses, and flag blockers \u2014 all without a human reformatting anything. The document didn't get worse for humans; it got restructured so the agent can operate on it as a first-class participant.", "related_frameworks": ["four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "context-as-compiled-view", "agent-context-failure-catalog", "work-primitives", "delegation-spec-drill", "the-five-specification-primitives", "three-type-memory-architecture", "8-principles-for-scalable-ai-memory-architecture", "six-layer-specification-stack", "hierarchical-context-loading", "done-definition-pattern"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Six Principles of AI Engineering", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of the six fundamental ways AI systems violate traditional software engineering assumptions, each paired with the corresponding architectural solution.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent works great in demos but keeps failing in weird ways once real users hit it at scale", "I'm architecting a new AI system and want to know what's fundamentally different from building traditional software", "Our AI system is 'up' according to monitoring but users keep complaining about bad answers \u2014 how do we even detect these failures?", "We're routing every request to GPT-4 and our costs are exploding \u2014 how should we think about tiering AI requests?", "My multi-agent system has agents that contradict each other's tone, reasoning style, and decisions"], "principles": ["Principle 1 (Stateful Intelligence): AI requires persistent memory infrastructure; context is the substance of intelligence, not an optimization", "Principle 2 (Bounded Uncertainty): AI is inherently probabilistic; the goal is bounding variance within acceptable limits, not eliminating it", "Principle 3 (Intelligent Failure): AI fails while appearing to succeed; semantic monitoring of meaning is required, not just uptime metrics", "Principle 4 (Capability Routing): AI requests vary by orders of magnitude in cost and complexity; classify before routing to appropriate model tier", "Principle 5 (Behavioral Consistency): Multi-agent systems must maintain consistent personality and reasoning style, not just data consistency", "Principle 6 (Continuous Validation): Security validation must be contextual and continuous across every conversation turn, not just at the input edge"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "174307612", "title": "THE 2025 Agent Build Bible: Save Your Team MONTHS of Pain with These 6 Principles", "date": "2025-09-23T13:03:01.704Z"}], "date": "2025-09-23T13:03:01.704Z", "id": "six-principles-of-ai-engineering", "core_question": "Why does my AI system keep breaking in production even though it works fine in demos, and what architectural assumptions am I getting wrong?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The customer support agent works perfectly in testing but gives contradictory answers to the same customer across sessions \u2014 this is a Principle 1 (Stateful Intelligence) violation. There's no persistent memory infrastructure, so every conversation starts from zero. But the deeper problem is Principle 3 (Intelligent Failure): the system never errors out, it just confidently gives wrong answers, and the team's monitoring only tracks uptime and latency. They need semantic monitoring that detects when the agent contradicts its own prior commitments to a customer, and a memory layer that carries context across sessions \u2014 not as an optimization, but as core architecture.", "related_frameworks": ["four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "architecture-first-reliability-pattern", "four-layer-agent-memory-model", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "agent-failure-pattern-catalog", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "six-rules-for-scaling-multi-agent-systems", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "ambient-failure-cascade", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "emergent-safety-dynamics-systems-not-components", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "entropy-matching-model-selection-framework", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Hybrid Architecture Boundary Framework", "type": "distinction", "description": "A decision rule for determining which components of a system should follow traditional deterministic engineering principles versus AI probabilistic principles, based on whether the task requires exactness or intelligence.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm building a system that mixes AI and traditional code and I need to decide what goes where", "Our AI agent needs to interact with financial calculations or audit-sensitive data and I'm worried about reliability", "We're designing the API boundary between our AI service and our backend \u2014 what should the contract look like?", "The team wants to replace an entire workflow with AI but some parts have strict compliance or exactness requirements", "I need to choose an integration pattern for connecting an LLM to our existing deterministic infrastructure"], "principles": ["Authentication, authorization, financial calculations, and audit logs require determinism and must stay traditional", "Customer interactions, content analysis, and decision support benefit from probabilistic intelligence and suit AI", "Hybrid is the optimal destination, not a transitional compromise", "The boundaries between deterministic and probabilistic components are the most critical architectural decisions", "Three validated boundary patterns: gateway pattern (traditional API gateway + AI behind it), supervisor pattern (traditional orchestration manages AI agents), and validation sandwich (traditional input check \u2192 AI processing \u2192 traditional output verification)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "174307612", "title": "THE 2025 Agent Build Bible: Save Your Team MONTHS of Pain with These 6 Principles", "date": "2025-09-23T13:03:01.704Z"}], "date": "2025-09-23T13:03:01.704Z", "id": "hybrid-architecture-boundary-framework", "core_question": "Which parts of this system need to be exactly right every time, and which parts need to be intelligent \u2014 and where exactly do I draw that line?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This payment processing pipeline has three distinct zones. The transaction authorization, ledger writes, and regulatory reporting are deterministic \u2014 they must produce identical outputs for identical inputs, every time, with full audit trails. The fraud detection layer and customer dispute classification are probabilistic \u2014 they benefit from pattern recognition and contextual reasoning that traditional rules engines can't match. The critical decision is the boundary: a validation sandwich pattern works here, where traditional input validation ensures the transaction data is well-formed, the AI fraud model scores the risk, and then a deterministic rules engine enforces the final accept/reject threshold with full logging. The AI never touches the ledger directly.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "intelligence-layer-vs-transaction-layer-distinction", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "agent-deployment-safety-framework", "constraint-architecture", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "six-principles-of-ai-engineering", "boundary-engineering", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration", "three-laws-for-engineering-with-ai", "ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "STAR-C (Situation, Task, Action, Result, Constraints)", "type": "procedure", "description": "An evolution of the classic STAR interview storytelling method that adds a fifth element\u2014Constraints\u2014to demonstrate authentic decision-making under real-world limitations rather than presenting idealized narratives.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm preparing for a technical or behavioral interview and my STAR answers sound generic and AI-generated", "I need to structure a portfolio case study that shows trade-off reasoning, not just a success story", "An interviewer keeps pressing on 'why did you choose that approach' and my answers fall flat", "I'm coaching someone who lists accomplishments but can't articulate the constraints that shaped their decisions", "I want to differentiate my experience stories from candidates who clearly used ChatGPT to generate perfect-sounding answers"], "principles": ["Lead with the hardest constraint faced, not the situation itself", "Define success criteria with measurable outcomes in the Task element", "Focus Actions on decision points and reasoning, not just steps taken", "Results must include hard metrics, not vague improvements", "The Constraints element should highlight limitations that shaped the approach and lessons learned", "Tag claims as Fact, Inference, Opinion, or To-Verify to maintain credibility under questioning"], "steps": ["Situation: Start with the specific constraints you faced, quantifying limitations (timeline, budget, resources, data availability) and explaining their strategic stakes", "Task: Define success criteria with measurable outcomes, clarify who needed to be satisfied and what failure would cost", "Action: Document key decision points and the reasoning behind choosing approach A over B, including verification steps taken", "Result: Provide specific metrics, compare to baseline or alternatives, include both intended and unintended consequences", "Constraints: Reflect on what you'd do differently with more resources, how the limitation shaped your approach, and what you learned about working within constraints"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "174185881", "title": "The Complete AI Interview Guide: What Actually Works in 2025", "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z"}], "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z", "id": "star-c-situation-task-action-result-constraints", "core_question": "How do I tell stories about my professional experience that prove I can actually think under pressure, rather than just recite polished narratives that sound like everyone else's?", "category": "execution", "example_application": "The strongest element in this answer isn't the 40% improvement metric \u2014 it's the constraint that the team had no dedicated data engineer and a 3-week deadline, which forced a decision to use an off-the-shelf pipeline instead of building custom ETL. That trade-off reasoning is what separates this from a generic STAR answer. The candidate should lead with 'We had 3 weeks, no data engineer, and a VP who'd cancel the project if we missed the demo' \u2014 that immediately signals real decision-making rather than rehearsed accomplishment theater. Tagging the 40% figure as 'Fact \u2014 measured in production dashboard' versus the team satisfaction claim as 'Inference \u2014 based on survey, not controlled' preempts interviewer skepticism.", "related_frameworks": ["asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "bilateral-value-creation-interview-approach", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "constraint-architecture", "jiorp-structure", "expertise-translation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Signal-Restoration Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A candidate strategy for rebuilding authentic human signal in AI-saturated hiring by creating verifiable artifacts of real decision-making rather than polished outputs that look identical to AI-generated work.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm job searching and my polished portfolio looks indistinguishable from what ChatGPT could generate for anyone", "An interviewer asked me to walk through my decision-making process and I realized I only have final deliverables to show", "I want to be transparent about using AI tools in my work without it undermining my credibility as a candidate", "I need to prepare work samples that demonstrate real problem-solving under constraints, not just clean outputs", "My applications keep getting lost in the noise and I suspect it's because they look like every other AI-optimized submission"], "principles": ["Create proof-of-work architecture: document your thinking process, not just your outputs", "Include at least one 'ugly' artifact\u2014imperfect pieces like scratch notes or failed experiments make portfolios believable", "Be specific about your AI tool stack and the reasoning behind each tool choice", "Show cost consciousness by quantifying efficiency gains from AI usage", "Name the trade-offs you navigate rather than claiming AI made everything better", "Verifiable specificity\u2014constraints faced, decisions made, outcomes measured\u2014is the new competitive edge"], "steps": ["Create an artifact packet (three pages maximum) documenting a real project with explicit constraint documentation", "Include a context box listing real constraints: data you didn't have, deadlines, compliance requirements, resource limits", "Document a decision log with three key calls made, trade-offs considered, and risks accepted with reasoning", "Include at least one 'ugly' artifact showing iteration, failure, or rough working notes", "Prepare specific AI usage disclosure: which tools for which tasks, validation process, and where you overrode AI recommendations", "Structure all stories in STAR-C format with tagged evidence levels (Fact, Inference, Opinion, To-Verify)"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "174185881", "title": "The Complete AI Interview Guide: What Actually Works in 2025", "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z"}], "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z", "id": "signal-restoration-framework", "core_question": "When every candidate's application looks AI-polished and interchangeable, how do I prove that I actually did the thinking behind my work?", "category": "execution", "example_application": "The artifact packet for a data migration project includes a context box listing that the team had no access to the legacy schema documentation and a hard compliance deadline 3 weeks out. The decision log shows the candidate chose to skip automated mapping validation due to time constraints, accepted the risk of manual spot-checking, and documents the 4% error rate that resulted \u2014 along with the remediation plan. A photo of a whiteboard sketch with crossed-out architecture options sits next to the final diagram. The AI disclosure notes that ChatGPT drafted the stakeholder communication emails but the candidate overrode its recommendation to use a phased rollout because the compliance window made that impossible. This packet is unambiguously human \u2014 no AI would volunteer the 4% error rate or the ugly whiteboard photo.", "related_frameworks": ["star-c-situation-task-action-result-constraints", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "bilateral-value-creation-interview-approach", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "noise-floor-paradox", "goodhart-s-law-metrics-trap-ai-hiring-application", "tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "legible-vs-illegible-work"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Goodhart's Law Metrics Trap (AI Hiring Application)", "type": "reframe", "description": "A diagnostic reframe that exposes how AI hiring systems produce impressive operational metrics while destroying the underlying signal those metrics were meant to capture, drawing on the Goodhart's Law principle that optimizing for a measure destroys its validity as a measure.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI screening vendor's case study shows amazing efficiency metrics but I can't find any quality-of-hire data", "Leadership is celebrating reduced time-to-hire from our new AI tools and I suspect we're just losing good candidates faster", "We're seeing higher completion rates on AI interviews but our new hire performance reviews haven't improved", "The AI hiring tool claims to reduce bias because it scores everyone consistently, but I'm not sure that's actually what bias reduction means", "We need to design success metrics for a new hiring process and I want to avoid optimizing for the wrong thing"], "principles": ["When a hiring measure becomes a target, it ceases to measure what you actually care about", "Operational metrics (time-to-hire, cost-per-hire, volume processed) are easy to measure but optimize for efficiency, not talent quality", "The candidates most likely to disengage from dehumanized AI screening are often the highest-quality candidates", "Bias reduction claims measured by consistency within a framework ignore whether the framework itself is biased", "AI hiring tools excel at what companies measure and fail at what they don't measure", "The right question is not 'did we hit the metric?' but 'does the metric still correspond to what we want?'"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "174185881", "title": "The Complete AI Interview Guide: What Actually Works in 2025", "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z"}], "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z", "id": "goodhart-s-law-metrics-trap-ai-hiring-application", "core_question": "Are our AI hiring metrics actually measuring better hiring outcomes, or have we just gotten faster at filtering out the wrong things?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The conventional read on this AI screening rollout is success: time-to-hire dropped 40%, cost-per-hire fell by half, and recruiter throughput tripled. That's wrong here because none of those metrics track what the hiring process exists to produce \u2014 high-performing employees. The system optimized for throughput and consistency, but the candidates who ghosted the automated process skew toward senior talent with options, meaning the 'improved' pipeline is systematically selecting for candidates with fewer alternatives. The bias reduction claim \u2014 that every candidate gets the same questions scored the same way \u2014 only proves consistency within the framework, not that the framework measures what predicts job performance.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "the-bidirectional-fit-assessment-interface", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "signal-restoration-framework", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "noise-floor-paradox", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer", "asserting-credentials-vs-demonstrating-capability"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Tool Philosophy Test (Thinking Replacer vs. Thinking Enhancer)", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A heuristic for evaluating AI interview tools\u2014and AI tools more broadly\u2014by whether their design philosophy positions AI as a substitute for the user's judgment or as an amplifier of it, with price and detectability as downstream signals of that philosophy.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm choosing between AI interview prep tools and the expensive ones seem like they should be better", "We're adopting an AI tool for our team and I'm worried it might atrophy the skills we actually need", "A candidate used AI tools to prep for their interview \u2014 trying to assess whether that's a good sign or a red flag", "I want to use AI to get better at something, not just have AI do it for me, and I need to tell the difference", "Evaluating whether an AI writing or coding assistant will survive scrutiny from reviewers who expect me to actually understand my own work"], "principles": ["Tools designed as thinking replacers fail because interviews and professional work test judgment, which cannot be outsourced", "Tools designed as thinking enhancers succeed because they help users access their own knowledge faster without replacing it", "The best AI tools are invisible\u2014interviewers cannot detect their use because the thinking still originates from the user", "Price premium on thinking-replacer tools reflects more sophisticated answer-generation, not better outcomes", "Free or low-cost tools that force users to develop genuine competence often deliver more durable value than expensive substitution tools", "When evaluating any AI tool, ask: does this make me better at the underlying skill, or does it perform the skill for me?"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "174185881", "title": "The Complete AI Interview Guide: What Actually Works in 2025", "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z"}], "date": "2025-09-22T13:03:01.541Z", "id": "tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer", "core_question": "Will this AI tool actually make me better at the skill, or is it just doing the skill for me \u2014 and what happens when someone checks?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This interview prep tool is a thinking replacer. It generates full answers to behavioral questions and coaches users to memorize them \u2014 the tell is the $200/month price point funding increasingly sophisticated answer generation. When the interviewer asks a follow-up that deviates from the rehearsed script, the candidate collapses because no genuine judgment was developed. Compare that to a free flashcard tool that surfaces the user's own past experiences and forces them to articulate the reasoning \u2014 that's a thinking enhancer. The user's answers survive follow-ups because the thinking originates from them. The expensive tool is actually the worse investment.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-framework", "cognitive-independence-protocol", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "experiential-debt", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Coding Readiness Audit (14 Questions)", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A two-phase structured audit that helps engineering teams assess readiness before adopting AI coding tools and diagnose failures after adoption, organized as 7 pre-adoption questions and 7 post-adoption troubleshooting questions.", "when_to_apply": ["We adopted Copilot three months ago and our bug rate is climbing even though we're shipping faster", "Leadership wants us to roll out an AI coding assistant across engineering \u2014 how do we know if we're actually ready?", "Our team has inconsistent coding patterns and I'm worried AI will make the inconsistency worse", "We're not seeing the productivity gains from AI coding tools that other teams report \u2014 what's going wrong?", "I need to build a business case for AI coding tools that accounts for hidden costs beyond licensing"], "principles": ["AI is a rocket booster \u2014 it amplifies existing practices, good or bad, so practices must be audited before adding AI", "Tool selection is the wrong first question; infrastructure and team readiness matter more than which tool you pick", "Start with a cultural audit, not a technical one \u2014 measure documented decisions, consistent patterns, and review quality", "Expect costs to appear before value does; value scales non-linearly at larger team sizes", "Velocity metrics can mask quality degradation \u2014 measure amplification (pattern consistency, bug rates) over raw output speed", "If metrics don't match anecdotes about product quality, trust the anecdotes", "Invest 3-4x more in buy-in and training than you think you need to"], "steps": ["Phase 1 \u2014 Pre-Adoption (7 questions): (1) What specific, measurable problem are we solving? (2) Do we have strong engineering practices to amplify? (3) Does the tool align with our workflow and tech stack? (4) How will we measure success and track changes? (5) Is security and data privacy addressed? (6) Do we have buy-in and training plans? (7) What is the total cost beyond licensing?", "Phase 2 \u2014 Post-Adoption Troubleshooting (7 questions): (1) Is the AI amplifying inconsistencies in our codebase? (2) Are we reviewing and testing AI output rigorously? (3) Is prompting or context the issue? (4) Are errors due to tool limitations or our setup? (5) Is team usage causing problems (over-reliance or resistance)? (6) Are metrics showing real issues vs. perceived gains? (7) Do we need to pivot or prepare better?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "173804344", "title": "The 14 AI Questions That Separate Average Teams from the Best", "date": "2025-09-17T13:06:55.311Z"}], "date": "2025-09-17T13:06:55.311Z", "id": "ai-coding-readiness-audit-14-questions", "core_question": "Are our engineering practices strong enough that adding AI coding tools will amplify quality rather than amplify mess?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The team's codebase has three different naming conventions, no architectural decision records, and code reviews that rubber-stamp in under five minutes. AI coding tools will generate code in all three conventions simultaneously, accelerating inconsistency faster than anyone can clean it up. The pre-adoption verdict is: not ready \u2014 the amplification target is broken. Before any tool evaluation, the team needs documented patterns, enforced linting, and meaningful review gates. Velocity metrics post-adoption would look great while technical debt compounds invisibly underneath.", "related_frameworks": ["ai-as-multiplier-the-multiplier-insight", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "the-three-question-workflow-audit", "three-laws-for-engineering-with-ai", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "the-five-principles-of-ai-implementation-success", "quick-self-assessment-are-you-in-the-5-or-95", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-framework", "progressive-enhancement-implementation-sequence", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "process_spec", "note": "2 steps contain temporal/phased language (month/week/phase/quarter). Likely a project plan, not a conversational reasoning framework."}]}
{"name": "Four-Category AI Solution Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that assigns any data or insights problem to one of four categories\u2014data processing, traditional ML, generative AI, or AI agents\u2014to determine the appropriate tool and avoid over-engineering.", "when_to_apply": ["Our CEO saw a demo and wants us to 'add AI' to a feature that currently runs on business rules and SQL", "A vendor is pitching us an AI agent solution and I need to evaluate whether the problem actually needs that level of complexity", "We're scoping a new project that involves data, predictions, or content generation and need to pick the right technical approach", "I suspect our team is over-engineering a solution \u2014 using generative AI or agents where something simpler would work", "We have a backlog of data and automation problems and need a quick way to classify which ones actually warrant AI investment"], "principles": ["Every data and insights problem belongs to exactly one of four categories: data processing, traditional ML, generative AI, or AI agents", "Pick the simplest tool that solves the problem\u2014this is a toolkit, not a ladder to climb", "Costs, maintenance burden, and talent requirements scale proportionally with category complexity", "If the problem has deterministic rules expressible as x + y = z, use data processing, not AI", "Traditional ML is appropriate when you have rich historical data and need to predict a specific, measurable outcome", "Generative AI is only justified when the value is at least 10x what simpler solutions provide", "AI agents are appropriate only when genuine autonomous decision-making across multiple systems with dynamic logic is required; most apparent agent problems are actually workflow or automation problems", "90% of problems presented as agent problems are not actually agent problems"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "173617623", "title": "The Real Talk Guide to Using AI Agents and Generative AI\u2014Plus When *NOT* to use AI", "date": "2025-09-15T13:03:33.977Z"}], "date": "2025-09-15T13:03:33.977Z", "id": "four-category-ai-solution-framework", "core_question": "We know we need to do something with this data or automation problem, but are we reaching for a far more complex (and expensive) solution than what the problem actually requires?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "The team wants an AI agent to reconcile invoices across three systems. But the reconciliation logic is entirely deterministic \u2014 match on invoice number, flag amount mismatches above 2%, route exceptions to a human queue. There's no prediction, no generation, no autonomous decision-making. This is a data processing problem dressed up as an agent problem. A well-written script with business rules handles it at a fraction of the cost, with zero hallucination risk and no model maintenance burden. The 'AI agent' proposal would have introduced three dependencies (LLM API, vector store, orchestration framework) to do what a SQL query and a cron job already solve.", "related_frameworks": ["the-simple-wins-method", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "agentic-system-gut-check", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "agent-economics-viability-model", "problem-portfolio-assessment", "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "six-practical-ai-agent-categories", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "integration-complexity-vs-advantage-2x2-portfolio-strategy", "ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement", "progressive-enhancement-implementation-sequence", "edge-first-automation-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Risk-Adjusted ROI Threshold Framework", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A scoring rubric that sets minimum required value improvement multipliers for each AI category based on the risk profile of that category's failure modes.", "when_to_apply": ["We're debating whether to use an AI agent versus a simpler ML model or rule-based system for a workflow", "Building a business case for a generative AI project and need to justify the risk to leadership", "The team is excited about using agents but the use case might not warrant that level of complexity", "Comparing two technical approaches where one is more powerful but introduces harder-to-detect failure modes", "Someone is proposing we upgrade from a working traditional ML solution to a generative AI approach"], "principles": ["Required ROI multiplier scales with the risk of the category's failure modes", "Data processing requires only 2x improvement because failure risk is low and fixable", "Traditional ML requires 5x improvement to account for model drift risk", "Generative AI requires 10x improvement to account for hallucination risk", "AI agents require 20x improvement to justify the risk of cascading failures", "If projected value does not meet the threshold for the category, use the simpler category instead"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "173617623", "title": "The Real Talk Guide to Using AI Agents and Generative AI\u2014Plus When *NOT* to use AI", "date": "2025-09-15T13:03:33.977Z"}], "date": "2025-09-15T13:03:33.977Z", "id": "risk-adjusted-roi-threshold-framework", "core_question": "Is the projected value of this more sophisticated AI approach actually high enough to justify the risk it introduces, or should I stick with something simpler?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "The team wants to deploy a generative AI system to auto-draft customer communications. Projected efficiency gain is 4x over the current template-based system. That falls well short of the 10x threshold required for generative AI \u2014 hallucinated content in customer-facing messages creates legal and reputational exposure that a 4x improvement doesn't justify. The right move is to stay at the traditional ML layer: a classification model that selects and populates the best template, where a 5x threshold is met and failure modes are contained to wrong-template selection, not fabricated content.", "related_frameworks": ["four-category-ai-solution-framework", "progressive-enhancement-implementation-sequence", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-simple-wins-method", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "integration-complexity-vs-advantage-2x2-portfolio-strategy", "agent-economics-viability-model", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Progressive Enhancement Implementation Sequence", "type": "procedure", "description": "A staged implementation procedure that requires each level of AI complexity to prove its value before the next level is introduced, preventing premature adoption of sophisticated AI.", "when_to_apply": ["Leadership wants to deploy AI agents but we haven't even automated the basic workflow yet", "Our AI project failed and we need to figure out a more incremental path forward", "We're building a multi-year AI roadmap and need to sequence what gets built when", "A team is excited about generative AI but can't articulate what simpler solution they've already tried and outgrown", "We have a working pilot but no clear criteria for when to expand it or move to the next level of complexity"], "principles": ["Each step must prove its value before moving to the next", "Start with manual processes or simple automation as the baseline", "Never skip phases to jump directly to production AI"], "steps": ["Establish baseline: start with manual processes or simple automation", "Add data processing for basic insights and metrics", "Layer in traditional ML for specific predictions once quality training data exists", "Introduce generative AI for content creation needs only when the value justifies it", "Consider agents only for genuinely complex multi-step workflows after all prior layers are proven", "Within each phase, follow: pilot (small controlled test with clear success metrics) \u2192 expansion (gradual rollout with continuous monitoring) \u2192 production (established maintenance processes) \u2192 optimization (refinement based on real-world performance)"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "173617623", "title": "The Real Talk Guide to Using AI Agents and Generative AI\u2014Plus When *NOT* to use AI", "date": "2025-09-15T13:03:33.977Z"}], "date": "2025-09-15T13:03:33.977Z", "id": "progressive-enhancement-implementation-sequence", "core_question": "Are we jumping to sophisticated AI before proving that simpler approaches actually work for this problem?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The team wants to deploy an AI agent to handle customer refund requests end-to-end. But there's no baseline: nobody has measured how long refunds take today, whether simple rules-based routing would solve 80% of cases, or whether the training data even exists to support ML-based triage. The right move is to instrument the current manual process first, then add rules-based automation for the obvious cases, then layer in ML prediction for edge cases \u2014 and only consider an agent once each prior layer has demonstrated measurable improvement. Jumping straight to an agent here means building complexity on top of assumptions instead of evidence.", "related_frameworks": ["the-simple-wins-method", "agent-strategy-sequencing", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "the-implementation-spectrum", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "agentic-system-gut-check", "the-five-principles-of-ai-implementation-success", "core-automation-readiness-check", "edge-first-automation-framework", "integration-complexity-vs-advantage-2x2-portfolio-strategy", "agent-economics-viability-model", "four-category-ai-solution-framework", "risk-adjusted-roi-threshold-framework", "three-tiers-of-agent-capability"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Five Principles of AI Implementation Success", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A five-principle framework classifying the architectural and operational patterns that distinguish successful AI implementations from failed ones, derived from real builder case studies rather than executive surveys.", "when_to_apply": ["We're moving an AI proof-of-concept to production and I want to make sure we're not missing structural requirements", "Our AI pilot worked in testing but adoption is stalling and I can't figure out why", "Should we build our own AI system, buy a vendor solution, or is there a middle path?", "Teams are using unauthorized AI tools and I'm trying to decide whether to shut it down or formalize it", "We deployed an AI feature but have no idea if it's actually improving over time or slowly degrading"], "principles": ["Hybrid architectures are mandatory \u2014 combine vendor models with custom orchestration logic rather than choosing pure buy or pure build", "Build learning systems, not static deployments \u2014 every interaction should improve the next via feedback loops, retraining pipelines, and context persistence", "Intelligent friction builds trust \u2014 use confidence thresholds, human review gates, and graceful degradation rather than striving for frictionless AI", "Instrumentation provides leading indicators \u2014 track accuracy by category, latency percentiles, override rates, and error taxonomies rather than waiting for P&L impact", "Shadow AI mining uncovers what actually works \u2014 survey, document, systematize, and scale guerrilla workflows rather than fighting them"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "173410381", "title": "Beat the 95% AI Fail Rate: A Builder's Playbook for Turning Experiments into Wins", "date": "2025-09-12T13:02:48.974Z"}], "date": "2025-09-12T13:02:48.974Z", "id": "the-five-principles-of-ai-implementation-success", "core_question": "What structural patterns separate the 5% of AI implementations that actually reach production and scale from the 95% that stall or fail?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This AI customer service pilot falls into the 'static deployment' anti-pattern \u2014 it shipped a fine-tuned model six months ago but has no feedback loop capturing agent overrides or retraining pipeline. Meanwhile, override rates are climbing but nobody's tracking them because instrumentation only measures aggregate CSAT, not accuracy by ticket category. Two of the five principles are violated simultaneously: learning systems (Principle 2) and leading-indicator instrumentation (Principle 4). The fix isn't more model capability \u2014 it's plumbing: a retraining pipeline fed by the override data that already exists, and category-level accuracy dashboards that would have caught the drift three months ago.", "related_frameworks": ["hybrid-architecture-boundary-framework", "quick-self-assessment-are-you-in-the-5-or-95", "shadow-ai-mining-process", "building-vs-buying-ai-response", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "progressive-enhancement-implementation-sequence", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "ambient-failure-cascade", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "risk-adjusted-roi-threshold-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Quick Self-Assessment: Are You in the 5% or 95%?", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A six-question checklist that scores whether an AI implementation is functioning as real infrastructure or as performative theater, using leading indicators instead of revenue.", "when_to_apply": ["Leadership is asking whether our AI project is 'working' but the only metric we have is usage counts from a mandated rollout", "I just inherited an AI initiative and need to quickly assess whether it's real or performative before committing more resources", "We're about to scale an AI system to more teams but I'm not confident the pilot is actually delivering value versus just running", "Our AI project has been live for months and people say it's fine, but I can't point to any concrete evidence it's improving anything", "I need to decide whether to kill or double down on an AI implementation and revenue data won't be meaningful for another two quarters"], "principles": ["Revenue is a lagging indicator \u2014 use technical and behavioral signals instead", "Voluntary user adoption without mandates is a stronger signal of real value than usage numbers", "Leading indicators (override rates, instrumentation breadth, shadow AI awareness) predict long-term ROI", "A score of 0\u20132 indicates the project is building theater rather than infrastructure"], "steps": ["Ask: Do you instrument a wide suite of AI leading indicators, not just usage numbers?", "Ask: Have you done a shadow AI audit in the last 60 days?", "Ask: Can your AI system get better without a full retrain?", "Ask: Do users voluntarily adopt your tool (no mandates)?", "Ask: Can you explain which AI workflows really matter in plain language?", "Ask: Is your AI system so accurate and quick that stakeholders assume it just works?", "Score: 5\u20136 Yes = in the 5%; 3\u20134 Yes = close, focus on gaps; 0\u20132 Yes = building theater"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "173410381", "title": "Beat the 95% AI Fail Rate: A Builder's Playbook for Turning Experiments into Wins", "date": "2025-09-12T13:02:48.974Z"}], "date": "2025-09-12T13:02:48.974Z", "id": "quick-self-assessment-are-you-in-the-5-or-95", "core_question": "Is our AI initiative actually working as real infrastructure, or are we just going through the motions and calling it success?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "Score: 2 out of 6. The team tracks daily active users (vanity metric) but has never instrumented override rates or measured which workflows actually matter. No shadow AI audit has been done \u2014 meaning they don't even know what tools people are actually using instead. Users were mandated to adopt the tool, so the usage numbers are meaningless as a signal of value. This is theater: the project looks alive on a dashboard but isn't building durable infrastructure. The first fix is a shadow AI audit within 30 days, because until you know what people voluntarily reach for, you can't know what real demand looks like.", "related_frameworks": ["the-potemkin-organization-diagnostic", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "shadow-ai-mining-process", "three-layer-ai-evaluation-system", "the-five-principles-of-ai-implementation-success", "ai-tool-failure-mode-catalog", "the-implementation-spectrum", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "progressive-enhancement-implementation-sequence", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Shadow AI Mining Process", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-step process for discovering, validating, and scaling the unauthorized AI workflows employees have already built, treating shadow AI as pre-validated product research rather than a compliance risk.", "when_to_apply": ["My official AI pilot has low adoption but I keep hearing that teams are using ChatGPT for the same task on their own", "We're about to invest in an enterprise AI tool and I want to know what workflows employees have already validated informally", "I need to build an AI strategy but have no data on where AI actually creates value in our organization", "Employees are sharing prompts and AI workarounds in Slack channels and I want to formalize the best ones before they fragment", "Our IT team wants to shut down unauthorized AI usage but I suspect those workflows contain our best product research"], "principles": ["Shadow AI is user research you didn't have to conduct \u2014 treat it as proof-of-concept with real validation", "The goal is to mine and formalize, not shut down unauthorized workflows", "Shadow workflows that spread organically have already passed the adoption test", "Formalizing top shadow workflows consistently outperforms top-down vendor pilots in adoption rates"], "steps": ["Survey \u2014 identify shadow AI workflows through surveys, Slack monitoring, and observation", "Document \u2014 capture exactly how each workflow operates, including prompts and context", "Systematize \u2014 add proper instrumentation, confidence scoring, and feedback loops to the best workflows", "Scale \u2014 roll out the systematized versions as native features or official infrastructure"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "173410381", "title": "Beat the 95% AI Fail Rate: A Builder's Playbook for Turning Experiments into Wins", "date": "2025-09-12T13:02:48.974Z"}], "date": "2025-09-12T13:02:48.974Z", "id": "shadow-ai-mining-process", "core_question": "My employees are already using AI in ways I don't fully see \u2014 how do I capture what's actually working and turn it into our official AI strategy instead of guessing from the top down?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Three customer success reps independently built ChatGPT workflows to draft renewal risk summaries from Salesforce notes \u2014 they spread the prompt via a shared Google Doc that 14 people now use daily. That's already past the adoption test. The formal vendor tool the CTO bought for 'AI-powered account health scoring' has 8% weekly active usage after four months. The shadow workflow gets systematized first: add structured input templates, confidence flags for when the risk signal is ambiguous, and a feedback loop where reps mark whether the summary changed their outreach. That becomes the official renewal intelligence feature \u2014 built on validated demand rather than a vendor pitch deck.", "related_frameworks": ["the-three-question-workflow-audit", "quick-self-assessment-are-you-in-the-5-or-95", "the-five-principles-of-ai-implementation-success", "progressive-enhancement-implementation-sequence", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "edge-first-automation-framework", "ai-usage-vs-ai-leverage-distinction", "old-ritual-failure-catalog", "workflow-decomposition-api", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "data-classification-decision-matrix-for-ai-tool-governance", "building-vs-buying-ai-response"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Kinds of Reading", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that distinguishes three fundamentally different reading modes\u2014awareness, retrieval, and connectome\u2014each serving different purposes and warranting different levels of effort and AI assistance.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a massive reading backlog and feel guilty about not finishing most of it", "Trying to decide whether to use AI to summarize a book or read it myself", "I'm reading everything at the same depth and running out of time and energy", "A colleague recommended a book but I'm not sure it's worth a full read versus just extracting the key ideas", "I want to stay current in a fast-moving field without burning all my deep focus time on it"], "principles": ["Awareness reading (scanning/monitoring) keeps you current on a field without requiring deep engagement; guilt about not going deeper is misplaced", "Retrieval reading is instrumental\u2014you are hunting for specific facts, techniques, or answers; AI tools excel at this mode", "Connectome reading is metabolically expensive, literally rewiring neural pathways; it cannot and should not be applied to everything", "Matching the reading mode to the purpose\u2014not defaulting to deep reading for everything\u2014is the core skill in an age of information abundance", "AI should be used to handle lower-mode reading so finite deep-reading capacity is preserved for connectome-worthy material", "Some books build arguments cumulatively and require end-to-end connectome reading; others are better suited to retrieval or awareness modes"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "173238258", "title": "The Complete AI Reading Guide for 2025", "date": "2025-09-10T13:03:40.576Z"}], "date": "2025-09-10T13:03:40.576Z", "id": "three-kinds-of-reading", "core_question": "Am I spending deep cognitive effort on material that only deserves a scan, or outsourcing to AI something that actually needs to rewire how I think?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "That stack of 12 AI research papers on the reading list breaks into three buckets: eight are awareness reads \u2014 skim the abstracts and conclusions to stay current on what's shipping. Three are retrieval reads \u2014 specific benchmarking methodologies needed for next week's eval design, perfect for AI-assisted extraction. Only one \u2014 the paper on emergent reasoning in chain-of-thought \u2014 is connectome material that builds a new mental model worth the metabolic cost of end-to-end deep reading. The guilt about not reading all twelve cover-to-cover is misplaced; it's actually preventing the one deep read that matters.", "related_frameworks": ["five-types-of-cognitive-work", "focus-capacity-model", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "retrieval-vs-reasoning-tool-selection", "insight-triage-curation-practice", "the-eight-building-blocks-of-a-second-brain", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "8-principles-of-retrieval-prompting"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "MACE Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A four-dimension classification system for evaluating and comparing agentic AI tools across Modality, Autonomy, Complexity, and Environment to make clear purchasing and deployment decisions.", "when_to_apply": ["Two AI tools both call themselves 'agents' but I suspect they're fundamentally different in what they can actually do", "I need to explain to leadership why we need three different AI tools instead of just picking the 'best' one", "We're choosing an AI coding tool and I want to match it to how much oversight my team can actually provide", "Building a multi-tool AI stack and I need a systematic way to figure out where each tool fits without overlap or gaps", "A vendor claims their AI agent is 'fully autonomous' and I need to assess what that actually means in terms of risk and environment access"], "principles": ["Modality (what the tool can manipulate): text, code, workflow, research, or multimodal", "Autonomy (how much supervision is required): reactive, interactive, semi-autonomous, or fully autonomous", "Complexity (sophistication of problem-solving): simple task handler, sequential processor, branching navigator, or adaptive replanner", "Environment (what systems the tool can touch): cloud-contained, IDE-integrated, platform-hosted, or infrastructure-spanning", "The four dimensions interact \u2014 a tool's true capability and limitations only emerge when all four are assessed together", "Higher autonomy and broader environment access require more trust and carry higher risk", "Match the tool to your availability and risk tolerance, not just raw capability"], "steps": ["Identify what modalities the task requires (text, code, workflow, research, or combinations)", "Determine how much autonomy you want \u2014 how much of your time can you invest in supervision?", "Assess the complexity of problem-solving needed \u2014 is the path deterministic, branching, or adaptive?", "Map what environment the tool must operate in \u2014 can it stay cloud-contained or must it span infrastructure?", "Apply the four-dimension profile to candidate tools and match to the six emergent categories"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172519740", "title": "The Complete Guide to AI Multi-Agent Orchestration with Manus AI", "date": "2025-09-02T13:03:13.002Z"}, {"id": "173152372", "title": "The Only Prompt Engineering Guide You'll Need to Bookmark", "date": "2025-09-09T13:03:25.648Z"}], "date": "2025-09-02T13:03:13.002Z", "id": "mace-framework", "core_question": "When someone says 'AI agent,' what exactly are we talking about \u2014 and how do I compare tools that seem similar but actually operate at completely different levels?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The team is comparing Cursor, Manus, and a custom LangChain pipeline for automating code review workflows. Cursor profiles as code-modality, interactive-autonomy, sequential-complexity, IDE-integrated \u2014 it needs a developer in the loop for every meaningful decision. Manus profiles as multimodal, semi-autonomous, branching-navigator, infrastructure-spanning \u2014 it can research context, modify files, and coordinate across systems with checkpoint-level supervision. The LangChain pipeline is code-modality, fully autonomous, adaptive-replanner, cloud-contained. The real gap isn't capability \u2014 it's that the team has no one available for Cursor's continuous supervision model, but also lacks the trust infrastructure (audit trails, rollback mechanisms) required for the LangChain pipeline's full autonomy. Manus hits the sweet spot: its semi-autonomous profile matches their actual supervision budget.", "related_frameworks": ["seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "six-practical-ai-agent-categories", "three-tiers-of-agent-capability", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "blast-radius-assessment", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "capability-surface-mapping", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "four-category-ai-solution-framework", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Try Rule", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured iteration protocol for prompt refinement that prescribes three progressive attempts \u2014 initial request, specificity expansion, and format/tone polish \u2014 before accepting a result.", "when_to_apply": ["I got a vague or generic AI response and I'm not sure what to change in my prompt to fix it", "I keep rewriting my prompt from scratch each time instead of building on what I have", "I want to develop a reusable prompt template and need a systematic way to refine it", "I'm tempted to accept a 60% quality AI output because iterating feels like guesswork", "My prompt revisions keep changing everything at once and the output quality isn't improving"], "principles": ["Never settle for the first response \u2014 treat it as a diagnostic baseline", "Try 2 should address missing specifics identified from Try 1's gaps", "Try 3 should refine surface qualities: format, tone, and examples rather than core content", "Each iteration should add one focused improvement, not a complete rewrite"], "steps": ["Try 1: Write and submit your initial prompt; note what is missing or off-target in the response", "Try 2: Add the missing specifics (topic detail, constraints, context) identified from Try 1", "Try 3: Refine format, tone, length, or add examples based on what is still not right after Try 2"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "173152372", "title": "The Only Prompt Engineering Guide You'll Need to Bookmark", "date": "2025-09-09T13:03:25.648Z"}], "date": "2025-09-09T13:03:25.648Z", "id": "three-try-rule", "core_question": "I got a mediocre response from the AI \u2014 should I just accept it, start over, or is there a disciplined way to iterate that actually converges on what I need?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The first response produced a generic blog outline about 'AI in healthcare' with no specificity. The diagnostic gaps are clear: no target audience specified, no constraints on depth or angle, no examples of the tone wanted. Try 2 adds 'for hospital CFOs evaluating radiology automation, skeptical tone, focused on hidden costs' \u2014 now the structure is right but the format is wrong (bullet list instead of narrative sections with headers). Try 3 refines only the surface: 'Use H2 headers, 800 words, include one concrete cost comparison per section.' The result converges in three moves instead of five scattered rewrites.", "related_frameworks": ["the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "deliverable-first-prompt-framework", "goldilocks-prompting", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "prompt-baseline-testing-protocol", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "progressive-meta-prompt-refinement-four-levels", "intent-engineering", "delegation-prompt-management-system"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Precision Tax", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes prompt vagueness not as a style issue but as a computational cost\u2014ambiguous or contradictory prompts consume extra reasoning tokens and produce inconsistent outputs, making precision a resource question, not just a quality question.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep running the same prompt and getting wildly different quality outputs each time", "My prompt says things like 'be detailed but keep it short' and I'm not sure why the results feel off", "A colleague argues that spending time on prompt structure is wasted effort since the model 'gets it anyway'", "I have a capable model producing surprisingly shallow or confused outputs on a task it should handle easily", "I want to quantify or justify why prompt engineering time matters beyond just 'better quality'"], "principles": ["Vague or contradictory prompts cause the model to burn reasoning tokens reconciling ambiguity rather than producing output", "The same content structured differently produces markedly different responses in depth, accuracy, and consistency", "Contradictions don't get resolved by picking one side\u2014the model attempts to satisfy all requirements simultaneously, degrading quality", "When prioritization conflicts exist, make the hierarchy explicit rather than leaving it to the model"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172703775", "title": "The ChatGPT-5 Prompting Manual: Building the Bridge Between Human Thinking and Machine Precision", "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z"}], "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z", "id": "the-precision-tax", "core_question": "Why is my AI producing mediocre output when it's clearly capable of better \u2014 and is the problem actually in how I'm spending the model's attention?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this prompt just needs better wording \u2014 maybe a clearer role or more examples. That's wrong here. The prompt says 'be comprehensive but concise, cover all edge cases while keeping it brief.' Those aren't style preferences \u2014 they're contradictory resource demands. The model is burning reasoning capacity trying to simultaneously satisfy 'comprehensive' and 'brief,' producing a muddled middle that satisfies neither. The fix isn't wordsmithing \u2014 it's establishing an explicit hierarchy: 'Prioritize completeness over brevity. Cover all edge cases. If the result exceeds 500 words, that's acceptable.' Once the contradiction is resolved, the same model on the same task produces dramatically more consistent, higher-quality output without any other changes.", "related_frameworks": ["intent-engineering", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "goldilocks-prompting", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "deliverable-first-prompt-framework", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "the-five-specification-primitives", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "reasoning-depth-vs-output-length-quadrant", "jiorp-structure", "six-layer-specification-stack", "meta-prompt-anatomy-seven-components"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Reasoning Depth vs. Output Length Quadrant", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A two-dimensional classification system that decouples thinking depth from response length into four actionable quadrants, helping users specify both dimensions independently to get exactly the output type they need.", "when_to_apply": ["I asked for a deep analysis and got a 2,000-word essay when I just needed the conclusion", "My prompts keep producing either superficial one-liners or verbose walls of text with no middle ground", "I need a comprehensive tutorial on a straightforward topic but the AI keeps giving me a terse paragraph", "I'm trying to optimize token costs in a production pipeline and need to control output length without sacrificing reasoning quality", "How do I get the AI to think hard but answer briefly?"], "principles": ["Reasoning depth and output length are independent variables that must be specified separately", "High reasoning always uses more computational resources regardless of output length", "Asking for 'deep analysis' without a length constraint defaults to high verbosity, which may not be desired", "Specify both dimensions explicitly: reasoning depth (high/low) and output length (brief/comprehensive)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172703775", "title": "The ChatGPT-5 Prompting Manual: Building the Bridge Between Human Thinking and Machine Precision", "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z"}], "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z", "id": "reasoning-depth-vs-output-length-quadrant", "core_question": "Am I getting bloated, shallow responses because I'm conflating how hard the AI should think with how long its answer should be?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This request falls in the high-reasoning, low-length quadrant: the user needs a nuanced strategic recommendation but wants it in three sentences, not three pages. The prompt should specify 'think deeply about the tradeoffs but deliver only the final recommendation in under 50 words' \u2014 without that explicit length constraint, the model defaults to exhaustive reasoning traces that bury the answer. Separating the two dimensions means the user pays the compute cost for depth but gets a deliverable they can actually act on immediately.", "related_frameworks": ["goldilocks-prompting", "entropy-matching-model-selection-framework", "the-five-specification-primitives", "constraint-architecture", "six-layer-specification-stack", "deliverable-first-prompt-framework", "the-precision-tax", "model-context-matching-framework", "intent-engineering"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Meta-Prompt Anatomy (Seven Components)", "type": "procedure", "description": "A seven-component framework for constructing complete prompt specifications that encode expertise, handle errors, and produce consistent outputs by covering the full lifecycle of an AI interaction.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep getting inconsistent outputs from the same prompt and need to lock down quality", "Building a reusable prompt template that junior team members can use to get senior-level results", "My AI workflow breaks downstream because the model sometimes changes its output format or makes assumptions it shouldn't", "I have a complex multi-step task and the model keeps skipping steps or choosing the wrong analytical approach", "I need to encode my domain expertise into a prompt so the model follows my methodology, not its default patterns"], "principles": ["Role definitions are expertise routing signals, not roleplay\u2014they activate relevant knowledge domains", "Objectives must include measurable success criteria, not just vague goals", "Process methodology encodes domain expertise into the prompt so the model doesn't choose its own approach", "Output contracts specify format, length, required elements, and excluded elements explicitly", "Uncertainty protocols are non-optional for agentic models that drive toward task completion even when they shouldn't", "Validation criteria create a quality control loop within the response generation itself"], "steps": ["Define the Role with domain-specific expertise (not generic\u2014specific role, context, and knowledge area)", "Specify the Objective Framework with measurable success criteria and defined outcomes", "Provide the Process Methodology\u2014the specific frameworks, steps, or algorithms to apply", "Write the Output Contract specifying format, length, structure, required and excluded elements", "List Constraint Handling covering tone, scope, resources, and restrictions", "Add an Uncertainty Protocol defining what to do when information is incomplete, conflicting, or requires assumptions", "Include Validation Criteria for self-checking\u2014instructions the model uses to verify its output before delivering it"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172703775", "title": "The ChatGPT-5 Prompting Manual: Building the Bridge Between Human Thinking and Machine Precision", "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z"}], "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z", "id": "meta-prompt-anatomy-seven-components", "core_question": "How do I write a prompt that reliably produces professional-grade output instead of getting inconsistent results that require constant rework?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The prompt for a financial analysis task is missing three of the seven components: it has a role and objective but no process methodology, no output contract, and no uncertainty protocol. That's why outputs vary wildly between runs \u2014 the model is choosing its own analytical framework each time, guessing at format, and hallucinating data when inputs are incomplete rather than flagging gaps. Adding a process methodology (DCF with explicit discount rate assumptions), an output contract (3-page memo, executive summary first, no tables wider than 5 columns), and an uncertainty protocol (flag any assumption with confidence below 80% and provide the sensitivity range) would eliminate the three failure modes the team keeps encountering.", "related_frameworks": ["the-five-specification-primitives", "six-layer-specification-stack", "constraint-architecture", "system-prompts-as-onboarding-documents", "intent-engineering", "uncertainty-protocol", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "context-formula-role-audience-goal-constraints", "deliverable-first-prompt-framework", "done-definition-pattern", "progressive-meta-prompt-refinement-four-levels", "delegation-spec-drill", "smart-machines-require-proper-structure-smrps-five-tier-prom", "scaffolded-reasoning-architecture", "goldilocks-prompting", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "correctness-definition-framework", "hierarchical-context-loading"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Prompt Baseline Testing Protocol", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured testing procedure to measure output variance before and after prompt changes, revealing routing inconsistency and identifying which structural modifications actually improve consistency.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompt works great sometimes and terribly other times and I can't figure out why", "I rewrote my prompt but I'm not sure if the new version is actually better or just seemed better on one test", "I want to invest time building a meta-prompt but need to know if the current prompt is inconsistent enough to justify the effort", "I'm testing different structural elements in my prompt and need a disciplined way to isolate what's actually causing improvement", "I keep tweaking my prompt based on the last bad output instead of systematic evidence"], "principles": ["Test in separate sessions, not the same conversation thread, to avoid context contamination", "Track five dimensions: length variation, structure differences, analytical depth, tool usage, and whether output met the actual need", "The 'met need' score matters more than quality score\u2014occasional variation is acceptable if success rate is high enough", "Look for patterns in best outputs to identify which routing signals to replicate in the meta-prompt", "Change only one structural element at a time when A/B testing to isolate causation"], "steps": ["Run the original prompt five times in separate sessions and copy all outputs", "Document variation across: word count, structure type, analytical depth (1\u20135), and whether it met your need (yes/no)", "Identify routing triggers by comparing the best outputs\u2014what characteristics do they share?", "Run structural A/B tests changing one element at a time: role definition, headers/formatting, instruction precision, length specification", "Score each test variant on consistency and quality, then decide which changes to incorporate", "Keep an iteration log (date, original prompt, problem, change tested, result, decision) as institutional knowledge"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "172703775", "title": "The ChatGPT-5 Prompting Manual: Building the Bridge Between Human Thinking and Machine Precision", "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z"}], "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z", "id": "prompt-baseline-testing-protocol", "core_question": "Is my prompt actually broken, or am I just reacting to normal variance \u2014 and if I change it, how do I know the change helped?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "Five separate sessions with the same strategic analysis prompt produced word counts ranging from 280 to 1,400, three different structural formats, and analytical depth scores from 2 to 5 \u2014 but four out of five met the actual need. The pattern in the two best outputs: both sessions triggered tool use and both opened with a structured framework before analysis. The worst output skipped structure entirely and produced a generic narrative. Adding explicit headers and a 'begin with framework selection' instruction to the meta-prompt, then re-running five times, compressed word count variance to 650\u2013900 and raised the met-need rate to five out of five. The iteration log now shows that header structure was the causal variable \u2014 role definition changes in the prior round had no measurable effect.", "related_frameworks": ["meta-prompt-anatomy-seven-components", "progressive-meta-prompt-refinement-four-levels", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "prompt-lifecycle-diagnostic-checklist", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "convergence-metric", "three-try-rule", "delegation-prompt-management-system", "intent-engineering", "goldilocks-prompting", "the-precision-tax"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Progressive Meta-Prompt Refinement (Four Levels)", "type": "procedure", "description": "A graduated approach to building meta-prompts that adds structural complexity only as needed, preventing over-specification while ensuring sufficient structure for consistency.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm building a new meta-prompt from scratch and don't know how detailed to make it", "My prompt works sometimes but gives inconsistent outputs and I want to fix it without over-specifying", "I already have a partially working prompt and want to know which specific additions will improve consistency", "I'm tempted to include every possible component in my prompt but suspect most of them aren't necessary", "I'm teaching someone to write prompts and need a graduated approach that doesn't overwhelm them"], "principles": ["Over-specified prompts can be as problematic as under-specified ones", "Stop adding complexity as soon as you achieve acceptable consistency\u2014not every prompt needs full specification", "Each level should be tested before moving to the next", "The goal is minimum sufficient structure, not maximum structure"], "steps": ["Level 1 \u2014 Minimal Structure: Add only Role, clear Objective, and basic Output Format. Test. If consistency is acceptable, stop here.", "Level 2 \u2014 Add Process: If outputs remain inconsistent, add Methodology/steps, basic Constraints, and Length/Format specifications. Test again.", "Level 3 \u2014 Add Error Handling: If the model still struggles, add Uncertainty Protocols, Validation Criteria, and Edge Case handling. Test again.", "Level 4 \u2014 Full Specification: Only for critical, repeated tasks\u2014add detailed Quality Standards, Self-checking instructions, and Revision Protocols."], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "172703775", "title": "The ChatGPT-5 Prompting Manual: Building the Bridge Between Human Thinking and Machine Precision", "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z"}], "date": "2025-09-04T13:02:58.865Z", "id": "progressive-meta-prompt-refinement-four-levels", "core_question": "How much structure does this prompt actually need, or am I over-engineering it when something simpler would work just as well?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The initial meta-prompt had only a role ('You are a financial analyst'), an objective ('Summarize quarterly earnings'), and a basic output format ('bullet points, 200 words max'). Three test runs produced consistent, usable summaries \u2014 Level 1 is sufficient here. Adding methodology steps, validation criteria, and self-checking instructions would only bloat the prompt and risk constraining the model's natural summarization ability. The prompt stays at Level 1. By contrast, a different prompt for generating compliance audit reports kept producing inconsistent section structures across runs, so it moved to Level 2 \u2014 adding explicit methodology steps and format constraints resolved the variance. No need to go further until new failure modes appear.", "related_frameworks": ["meta-prompt-anatomy-seven-components", "goldilocks-prompting", "prompt-baseline-testing-protocol", "the-prompt-lifecycle-framework", "six-llm-failure-modes-the-prompt-doctor-framework", "six-layer-specification-stack", "the-precision-tax", "smart-machines-require-proper-structure-smrps-five-tier-prom", "constraint-architecture", "the-five-specification-primitives"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The 7 MCP Architectural Failure Modes", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumerated catalog of seven recurring architectural mistakes teams make when implementing Model Context Protocol, each with specific signals, consequences, and corrective patterns.", "when_to_apply": ["We're putting MCP between our service and the customer-facing API and latency is spiking", "Our MCP implementation is working in dev but we're worried about what breaks at production scale", "We created one MCP server per microservice and now the system is impossible to debug", "Our AI integration is pulling in tons of context via MCP but the model's outputs are actually getting worse, not better", "We're about to go live with an MCP architecture and need to audit it for security \u2014 especially prompt injection and cross-tenant data leakage"], "principles": ["MCP is an intelligence layer, not a transaction layer \u2014 never place it in the hot path of customer-facing or real-time operations", "Context orchestration is fundamentally different from data retrieval \u2014 don't use MCP as a substitute for SQL or direct API calls", "Security must be designed into the architecture from the start, not bolted on as a gate at the end", "Language itself becomes an attack vector with AI systems \u2014 design for prompt injection and cross-tenant exposure by default", "Adding external context via MCP can degrade model performance if the context is dirty or irrelevant \u2014 treat context quality as a first-class concern", "MCP should work within microservices, not as microservices \u2014 avoid one MCP server per service", "MCP's JSON-RPC protocol prioritizes debuggability over performance, which defines where it belongs architecturally", "The fast path (direct APIs) and the smart path (MCP orchestration) must be separated and never confused"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172607458", "title": "The MCP Implementation Guide: Solving the 7 Failure Modes that Doom AI Architectures", "date": "2025-09-03T13:00:46.817Z"}], "date": "2025-09-03T13:00:46.817Z", "id": "the-7-mcp-architectural-failure-modes", "core_question": "Is our MCP implementation going to fail in production, and if so, which specific architectural mistake are we making right now?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The team's proposed architecture routes every customer API request through an MCP server for 'intelligent context enrichment' \u2014 this is Failure Mode #1, confusing the intelligence layer with the transaction layer. MCP's JSON-RPC protocol prioritizes debuggability over performance, meaning it belongs on the smart path (background orchestration, async enrichment) not the fast path (real-time customer-facing requests). The tell is that they're measuring MCP success by latency reduction, when MCP will always add latency \u2014 its value is in reasoning quality on non-time-critical paths. The fix is to separate the fast path (direct API calls) from the smart path (MCP orchestration) and never let them cross.", "related_frameworks": ["intelligence-layer-vs-transaction-layer-distinction", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "four-principles-of-agent-extension-design", "middleware-trap-diagnostic", "hybrid-architecture-boundary-framework", "agent-failure-pattern-catalog", "architecture-first-reliability-pattern", "six-principles-of-ai-engineering", "three-missing-layers-of-organizational-ai-architecture", "nine-context-engineering-scaling-principles", "boundary-engineering", "blast-radius-assessment"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Intelligence Layer vs. Transaction Layer Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "A architectural framing that separates AI/MCP orchestration (intelligence layer: asynchronous, pattern-finding, insight-generating) from operational API calls (transaction layer: synchronous, deterministic, sub-100ms), clarifying which component belongs in which path.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI agent integration is adding unacceptable latency to what used to be fast API calls", "I'm designing an AI-augmented product and need to decide which operations go through MCP vs. direct APIs", "We're getting audit and compliance pushback because AI is in the critical path of financial transactions", "The system needs both real-time responsiveness and intelligent analysis \u2014 how do I architect both without compromising either?", "An AI integration is causing intermittent failures in production because the LLM is handling operations that need to be deterministic"], "principles": ["The transaction layer handles speed-critical, deterministic, auditable operations (payments, catalog lookups, fraud detection) via direct APIs", "The intelligence layer handles complexity-critical, pattern-finding, insight-generating operations asynchronously via MCP", "The two layers must never be conflated \u2014 putting intelligence-layer technology in a transaction-layer position is architectural malpractice", "Latency requirements and audit requirements are the primary signals for which layer an operation belongs in", "Intelligence layer results can feed back into transaction layer decisions asynchronously (e.g., recommendation models trained overnight)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172607458", "title": "The MCP Implementation Guide: Solving the 7 Failure Modes that Doom AI Architectures", "date": "2025-09-03T13:00:46.817Z"}], "date": "2025-09-03T13:00:46.817Z", "id": "intelligence-layer-vs-transaction-layer-distinction", "core_question": "Should this operation run through our AI/MCP orchestration layer, or does it belong in a direct, deterministic API path \u2014 and what breaks if I get that wrong?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The team wants to route payment authorization through their MCP server so the AI agent can 'decide' whether to approve transactions. This is a transaction-layer operation misplaced in the intelligence layer \u2014 payment auth requires sub-100ms deterministic responses with full audit trails, and MCP adds 2-4 seconds of latency plus nondeterministic reasoning. The correct architecture: the intelligence layer runs fraud pattern analysis asynchronously overnight, producing risk scores that feed into the transaction layer's deterministic rules engine, which handles the actual authorization call in 50ms with complete auditability.", "related_frameworks": ["the-7-mcp-architectural-failure-modes", "hybrid-architecture-boundary-framework", "four-structural-failure-modes-of-production-ai-agents", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "routing-layer-specification-layer-review-layer", "architecture-first-reliability-pattern", "model-vs-harness-distinction", "two-kinds-of-architecture"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Six Practical AI Agent Categories", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Six named categories of AI tools derived from applying the MACE framework, each with a distinct capability profile and clear guidance on when to use and avoid it.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm trying to pick between ChatGPT, Cursor, Perplexity, and Zapier for a specific workflow and they all seem like they could work", "My team is using a conversational AI for a task that really needs autonomous execution or structured automation", "I need to explain to a non-technical stakeholder what kind of AI tool we're recommending and why it fits", "We're auditing our AI tool stack and suspect we have gaps or are using the wrong category of tool for certain tasks", "I'm scoping a new AI project and need to decide whether the use case calls for collaboration, synthesis, automation, or full autonomy"], "principles": ["Conversational Generators (e.g., ChatGPT, Claude): text modality, reactive, simple-to-sequential, cloud-contained \u2014 best for active text/analysis tasks", "Code Assistants (e.g., Cursor, Copilot): code modality, reactive-to-interactive, sequential, IDE-integrated \u2014 best for active programming acceleration", "Workflow Orchestrators (e.g., n8n, Zapier): workflow modality, semi-autonomous, branching, platform-hosted \u2014 best for predictable repetitive tasks", "Research Synthesizers (e.g., Perplexity, Deep Research): research modality, interactive, sequential-to-branching, cloud-contained with web access \u2014 best for compiling current information", "Autonomous Executors (e.g., Manus, Devin): multimodal, fully autonomous, adaptive, infrastructure-spanning \u2014 best for complex multi-step projects where speed outweighs control", "Hybrid Collaborators (e.g., Claude Projects, ChatGPT Canvas): mixed modality, interactive-to-semi-autonomous, branching-to-adaptive \u2014 best for iterative creative work requiring human judgment", "Each category reflects a distinct philosophy: augment creativity, accelerate productivity, eliminate repetition, extend knowledge, replace labor, or blend judgment"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172519740", "title": "The Complete Guide to AI Multi-Agent Orchestration with Manus AI", "date": "2025-09-02T13:03:13.002Z"}], "date": "2025-09-02T13:03:13.002Z", "id": "six-practical-ai-agent-categories", "core_question": "I know I need an AI tool for this task, but there are so many different kinds \u2014 which category of AI capability actually matches what I'm trying to do?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The team wants to automate weekly competitor report generation. The instinct is to use ChatGPT, but the task involves pulling live web data, synthesizing across sources, and producing a structured deliverable \u2014 that's a Research Synthesizer (Perplexity, Deep Research), not a Conversational Generator. Conversational Generators excel at reactive text analysis with user-provided inputs, but they don't autonomously retrieve current information. The workflow also has a recurring, predictable cadence \u2014 which means a Workflow Orchestrator layer (n8n/Zapier) should trigger the Research Synthesizer on schedule and route the output. Two categories, not one.", "related_frameworks": ["mace-framework", "seven-ai-tool-archetypes", "colleague-shaped-vs-tool-shaped-ai-selection-framework", "ai-tool-implementation-sequence", "four-category-ai-solution-framework", "harness-selection-decision-framework", "three-tiers-of-agent-capability", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum", "the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "three-question-agent-use-case-filter", "ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement"], "origin": "extracted", "review_flags": [{"status": "needs_manual_review", "defect": "hardcoded_model_names", "note": "Found hardcoded model names: Perplexity. Replace with capability descriptions (e.g., 'a model optimized for extended reasoning')."}]}
{"name": "Human First Protocol", "type": "procedure", "description": "A decision hierarchy that requires children to seek human support before consulting AI for emotional, relational, or major decision-making situations.", "when_to_apply": ["My kid told me they talk to ChatGPT when they're sad instead of coming to us", "We're setting household rules about what our children can and can't use AI for", "A teenager wants to ask an AI chatbot for advice about a friendship conflict or breakup", "I caught my child using AI to process a major life decision before talking to any adult", "We need a clear, postable set of guidelines for when AI is and isn't appropriate for our kids"], "principles": ["AI can be a second opinion, never the first consultant for personal matters", "AI lacks crucial context about a child's history, values, and specific situation", "Generic pattern-matched advice is inferior to wisdom from people who know the child", "The hierarchy should be explicit and posted, not assumed"], "steps": ["Self-soothing first: breathing, movement, journaling", "Trusted adult second: parent, counselor, or teacher", "Close friend third", "Broader social network fourth", "AI last, and never alone for serious issues"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172113939", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z"}], "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z", "id": "human-first-protocol", "core_question": "When my child faces something emotionally or relationally complex, how do I ensure they reach for a human before they reach for a chatbot?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "A 13-year-old is upset after a fight with their best friend and opens ChatGPT to vent. The hierarchy is clear: AI is step five, not step one. The child hasn't tried self-soothing (breathing, journaling), hasn't talked to a parent or counselor, and hasn't reached out to a close friend. The AI's response would be generic pattern-matching \u2014 it doesn't know the friend, the history of the relationship, or the family values at play. The right move is to close the chat and walk the hierarchy: journal first, then talk to mom or dad. AI can help later to rehearse a conversation or draft an apology text, but only after the emotional processing has happened with people who actually know the situation.", "related_frameworks": ["yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-framework", "cognitive-independence-protocol", "reality-anchoring-techniques", "boundary-engineering", "tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer", "ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "context-gap-analysis-for-human-ai-complementarity"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Yellow Flag / Red Flag AI Dependency Framework", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumerated catalog of behavioral warning signals categorized by severity to help parents identify when a child's AI use has become problematic and what level of intervention is warranted.", "when_to_apply": ["My teenager gets angry or panicky when I suggest limiting their AI chatbot time and I'm not sure if that's normal or a warning sign", "My child's school essays are suddenly much better but they can't explain the ideas in conversation", "I found my kid having deeply personal conversations with an AI about self-harm and I don't know how to respond", "My child refers to their AI assistant as a friend and has stopped hanging out with real peers", "I want to know the difference between a kid who uses AI a lot and a kid who is dependent on it"], "principles": ["Yellow flags indicate problematic patterns requiring conversation and boundary adjustment, not emergency", "Red flags indicate immediate intervention is needed, focused on support not punishment", "AI dependency often signals unmet underlying needs such as loneliness or anxiety", "Address the need, not just the symptom", "Reality blurring and crisis consultation are categorically more serious than preference or secretiveness"], "steps": ["Observe for yellow flags: preference for AI over human interaction, emotional dependency on AI availability, reality blurring (attributing feelings to AI), secretive use, academic output that doesn't match in-person ability", "If yellow flags present: initiate conversation, adjust boundaries collaboratively", "Observe for red flags: using AI for crisis or self-harm ideation, complete social isolation in favor of AI, genuine belief in AI sentience or feelings, harmful validation of dangerous behaviors, identity fusion with AI relationship", "If red flags present: intervene immediately with professional support focus, not punishment", "Increase human connection as the primary antidote in both cases"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172113939", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z"}], "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z", "id": "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-framework", "core_question": "Has my child's relationship with AI crossed from heavy use into something that's actually harming them, and how urgently do I need to act?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The child prefers talking to ChatGPT over calling friends and gets visibly anxious when screen time ends \u2014 those are yellow flags: emotional dependency and preference for AI over human interaction. But they also told a sibling that 'the AI is worried about me' and 'it understands me better than anyone real does' \u2014 that's reality blurring crossing into identity fusion, which is a red flag. The severity isn't about volume of use; it's that the child has begun attributing genuine emotional states to the AI and structuring their identity around the relationship. This warrants immediate professional support, not a grounding or a new screen-time rule. The underlying signal is likely unmet social connection \u2014 the AI filled a void that existed before the AI showed up.", "related_frameworks": ["human-first-protocol", "reality-anchoring-techniques", "cognitive-independence-protocol", "boundary-engineering", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Reality Anchoring Techniques", "type": "procedure", "description": "A set of structured exercises to help children maintain calibrated skepticism toward AI outputs and avoid conflating AI-generated content with reliable reality.", "when_to_apply": ["My child copied an AI response into their homework without checking if it was accurate", "I want practical exercises to teach AI skepticism, not just abstract warnings about misinformation", "A child asked an AI chatbot for personal advice and is treating the response as if it really knows them", "I need age-appropriate activities that show kids how AI actually works versus how it appears to work", "A child can't tell the difference between AI-generated text and something a knowledgeable human wrote"], "principles": ["Important AI claims should be verified against three independent sources before being trusted", "AI has no context about a child's specific life, making its advice generic like a horoscope", "Comparing responses with and without context reveals AI's pattern-matching nature", "Recognizing the unnaturalness of AI responses builds healthy skepticism"], "steps": ["Apply the Three-Source Rule: verify any important AI claim with three independent sources before trusting it", "Apply the 'Would a Human Say This?' test: read AI responses aloud and ask whether any real person would actually speak that way", "Apply the Context Check: ask AI for advice without context, then with full context, and compare to reveal AI's generic pattern-matching"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172113939", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z"}], "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z", "id": "reality-anchoring-techniques", "core_question": "How do I give my child concrete, repeatable habits for questioning AI outputs instead of just telling them 'don't trust everything AI says'?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "A child asked ChatGPT about a historical event for a school project and is about to copy the response verbatim. Applying the Three-Source Rule: the AI's claim that a specific treaty was signed in 1847 checks out on Wikipedia but conflicts with the textbook (1849) and a library database (1849). Two out of three independent sources disagree \u2014 the AI confabulated the date, and the child now has direct experience of why verification matters. Running the 'Would a Human Say This?' test on the rest of the response reveals unnaturally balanced, hedge-heavy phrasing no teacher or historian would actually use, reinforcing that the output is pattern-matched language, not authoritative knowledge.", "related_frameworks": ["cognitive-independence-protocol", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-framework", "human-first-protocol", "tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer", "verification-over-credentialing-framework", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "two-llm-hallucination-verification-loop", "boundary-engineering"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Cognitive Independence Protocol", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured set of exercises to prevent AI from replacing a child's own thinking by requiring them to engage their cognition before and after AI consultation.", "when_to_apply": ["My kid's homework answers sound suspiciously polished but they can't explain the concepts when I ask", "We're setting up family rules for how our children can use ChatGPT for schoolwork", "My child defaults to asking AI immediately instead of trying to figure things out first", "I want my kid to benefit from AI tools without losing the ability to think independently", "A child's creative output has become noticeably more generic since they started using AI assistants"], "principles": ["Children must generate their own thinking before consulting AI", "If a child cannot explain AI-assisted content without screens, they did not learn it", "Generating at least one solution AI did not suggest maintains creative independence", "The goal is using AI to accelerate learning, not to avoid the cognitive work of learning"], "steps": ["Think First, Check Second: write down personal thoughts before asking AI, then compare", "Explanation Challenge: after AI explains a concept, child must explain it back in their own words without screens", "Alternative Solution Game: after receiving AI suggestions, generate at least one solution the AI did not provide"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "172113939", "title": "Raising Humans in the Age of AI: A Practical Guide for Parents", "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z"}], "date": "2025-08-28T13:02:46.222Z", "id": "cognitive-independence-protocol", "core_question": "Is my child actually learning when they use AI, or is the AI doing the thinking for them?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "A 12-year-old asked ChatGPT to explain photosynthesis for a science assignment and copied the explanation nearly verbatim. Applying the Explanation Challenge reveals the gap: when asked to explain it without the screen, she can say 'plants use sunlight to make food' but cannot describe the role of chlorophyll or the difference between light and dark reactions \u2014 she absorbed the conclusion but none of the mechanism. The protocol surfaces that she skipped Think First entirely, so there was no baseline of her own understanding to compare against. The fix is concrete: before the next topic, she writes what she already knows (even if it's wrong), then uses AI to fill gaps, then explains it back, then generates one question or idea the AI didn't raise.", "related_frameworks": ["tool-philosophy-test-thinking-replacer-vs-thinking-enhancer", "ai-use-classification-tool-vs-replacement", "yellow-flag-red-flag-ai-dependency-framework", "human-first-protocol", "reality-anchoring-techniques", "comprehension-lock-in-audit", "coaching-tool-vs-surveillance-tool-distinction", "experiential-debt", "ai-assistance-level-spectrum"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Laws for Engineering with AI", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "Three principles that define the complete lifecycle of responsible engineering in AI systems, covering specification, verification, and accountability.", "when_to_apply": ["We shipped an AI feature and it works, but I couldn't explain to an auditor why it sometimes gives wrong answers", "Our team is building on LLMs and I need engineering standards that account for non-deterministic components", "I need to assess whether our AI system is production-ready or still just a polished demo", "A regulator or compliance team is asking how we ensure our AI system behaves correctly and I don't have a clear answer", "We're reviewing an AI-integrated system and I suspect we're missing fundamental engineering rigor beneath the surface"], "principles": ["First Law \u2014 If you can't write the invariant, you haven't engineered it: engineering requires stating what must always be true, not just making something work probabilistically", "Second Law \u2014 If you can't measure it in production, you didn't build it: real engineering demands observability, telemetry, and semantic forensics, not just a working demo", "Third Law \u2014 If you can't explain why it failed to a regulator, you haven't owned it: engineers must take accountability for outcomes, not blame the AI", "The three laws form a complete lifecycle: specification (what we promise), verification (proof we delivered), and accountability (ownership of outcomes)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "171893151", "title": "Software Engineering isn't Dead, it's Evolving: Here's the Guide to Engineering Evolution in the Age of AI", "date": "2025-08-26T13:03:25.470Z"}], "date": "2025-08-26T13:03:25.470Z", "id": "three-laws-for-engineering-with-ai", "core_question": "Have we actually engineered this AI system, or did we just get something probabilistic to work and call it done?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This production RAG system fails the Second Law. The team built a compelling demo and shipped it, but there's no semantic telemetry in production \u2014 no tracking of retrieval relevance scores, no monitoring of answer groundedness, no alerting when the model confabulates. When a customer reported wrong answers last week, the team couldn't reconstruct what context the model saw or why it generated that output. They have a working product but not an engineered system \u2014 they skipped verification entirely. Before anything else, they need observability that lets them replay any failure forensically.", "related_frameworks": ["six-principles-of-ai-engineering", "the-twelve-principles-of-durable-automated-systems", "four-layer-agent-eval-architecture", "correctness-definition-framework", "definition-of-done-rubric", "the-five-specification-primitives", "agent-audit-trail-standard", "verification-frontier", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "builder-engineer-composability-diagnostic", "done-definition-pattern", "realpolitik-safety", "emergent-safety-dynamics-systems-not-components", "the-five-levels-of-vibe-coding", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Boundary Engineering", "type": "procedure", "description": "A discipline for architecting the interface between deterministic system requirements and probabilistic AI outputs, using confidence stratification to route decisions appropriately.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm building an API that wraps an LLM but needs to guarantee consistent response formats and SLAs to downstream consumers", "We need to decide which AI outputs can be auto-approved versus which need human review, and the current approach is all-or-nothing", "Our AI system works great 85% of the time but fails catastrophically the other 15%, and we need a graceful degradation strategy", "I'm integrating AI into a regulated domain (finance, healthcare, legal) where some outputs must be deterministically guaranteed", "The AI component in our pipeline sometimes returns low-confidence garbage and we have no systematic way to catch and handle it"], "principles": ["Deterministic interfaces must wrap probabilistic cores \u2014 external contracts should be consistent even when the AI inside is uncertain", "Confidence is a spectrum, not binary \u2014 outputs should be stratified into high/medium/low confidence tiers with different handling paths", "Design for graceful uncertainty \u2014 explicitly define what happens when the AI says 'I don't know' or produces low-confidence results", "Define decision boundaries \u2014 specify the conditions under which the AI hands off to a human"], "steps": ["Define the invariants the system must guarantee regardless of AI output", "Implement a confidence scoring mechanism on AI outputs", "Stratify outputs into confidence tiers (e.g., high, medium, low)", "Assign handling paths per tier: auto-approve, human review, or reject/escalate", "Build fallback patterns that activate when confidence thresholds are not met", "Monitor boundary behavior in production to detect semantic drift or threshold violations"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "171893151", "title": "Software Engineering isn't Dead, it's Evolving: Here's the Guide to Engineering Evolution in the Age of AI", "date": "2025-08-26T13:03:25.470Z"}], "date": "2025-08-26T13:03:25.470Z", "id": "boundary-engineering", "core_question": "How do I build a system that makes reliable promises to users when the AI component inside it is fundamentally uncertain?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The loan underwriting API must return accept/reject/review within 200ms with no unexplained failures. The AI model producing risk scores gets wrapped in a confidence stratification layer: scores above 0.92 auto-approve, scores between 0.65-0.92 route to a human underwriter queue with the model's reasoning attached, and scores below 0.65 trigger the deterministic fallback \u2014 a traditional rules-based scoring engine. The external contract never changes shape; the caller always gets a structured response with a decision and rationale, regardless of which path produced it. Production monitoring shows the medium-confidence band is widening over the last two weeks, which signals semantic drift in the model and triggers a recalibration review before the boundary thresholds become meaningless.", "related_frameworks": ["model-vs-harness-distinction", "action-risk-bucketing-let-it-run-checkpoint-always-confirm", "constraint-architecture", "hybrid-architecture-boundary-framework", "blast-radius-assessment", "the-verifiability-spectrum", "ai-agent-supervision-model", "generate-validate-repair-validate-again", "correctness-definition-framework", "uncertainty-protocol", "deterministic-decision-framework", "multi-step-reliability-compounding-model", "constraints-vs-preferences-vs-proof-vs-confidence-distinctio", "the-70-30-rule-for-human-agent-delegation", "agent-deployment-safety-framework"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Economic Engineering of Intelligence", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A framework for treating AI inference as a metered utility and making architectural decisions based on cost, latency, and quality tradeoffs across model tiers.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI inference costs are growing faster than our user base and I need to figure out where the waste is", "I'm deciding whether to use GPT-4, Claude, or a smaller model for different parts of our product pipeline", "We're building a production AI system and need to architect the model routing and caching layers from scratch", "Users are complaining about latency but switching to a faster model might hurt output quality", "I need to plan a realistic cost budget for an AI-powered feature that will handle 100K+ requests per day"], "principles": ["Every token costs money \u2014 treat inference spend as a first-class engineering constraint, not an afterthought", "Every inference has latency \u2014 design UX and architecture around the reality that AI 'thinks' in seconds", "Every model has a quality-cost tradeoff \u2014 match model capability to task complexity rather than defaulting to the most capable model", "Context windows are scarce resources \u2014 manage context economically, not just technically", "Semantic similarity is the key to caching \u2014 'similar enough' questions can share cached responses, reducing redundant inference"], "steps": ["Classify incoming requests by complexity and required output quality", "Map request classes to appropriate model tiers based on cost/quality tradeoff", "Implement semantic caching to serve repeated or near-duplicate queries without new inference", "Optimize prompts to minimize token usage while preserving output quality", "Batch requests where latency requirements allow, to amortize per-request overhead", "Monitor cost-per-request and quality metrics continuously to rebalance routing as usage patterns evolve"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "171893151", "title": "Software Engineering isn't Dead, it's Evolving: Here's the Guide to Engineering Evolution in the Age of AI", "date": "2025-08-26T13:03:25.470Z"}], "date": "2025-08-26T13:03:25.470Z", "id": "economic-engineering-of-intelligence", "core_question": "Am I spending 10x more on AI inference than I need to because I'm routing every request to the most powerful model instead of matching model capability to task complexity?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This system is routing all customer support queries through GPT-4 at $0.03/1K tokens when 70% of them are FAQ-level questions that a fine-tuned GPT-3.5 handles at $0.002/1K with equivalent quality. Adding a semantic cache for the top 200 query clusters would eliminate another 40% of remaining inference calls entirely. The real architectural gap is the missing classification layer at ingestion \u2014 without it, there's no way to tiered-route, and the $47K/month inference bill will only grow with traffic.", "related_frameworks": ["the-two-axis-model-routing-framework", "entropy-matching-model-selection-framework", "see-or-do-vs-write-or-talk-model-routing", "routing-layer-specification-layer-review-layer", "model-context-matching-framework", "agent-economics-viability-model", "six-principles-of-ai-engineering", "hybrid-architecture-boundary-framework", "work-delegation-bucket-classification", "the-three-disciplines-of-ai-engineering", "four-lane-supply-strategy", "dumb-agents-smart-orchestration"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Marketing Local Maxima Diagnosis", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A framework for identifying when a marketing system has optimized itself into a dead end and recognizing the four feedback loops that accelerate the trap.", "when_to_apply": ["Our marketing metrics have plateaued and no amount of A/B testing or creative iteration is moving the needle", "Every competitor in our space is converging on the same content style and we can't differentiate anymore", "We're worried our content strategy has become dependent on controversy or manipulation to hit engagement targets", "AI tools are enabling competitors to produce content at our quality level and we're losing our production advantage", "Our engagement numbers look good but leadership is questioning whether they actually map to brand health or revenue"], "principles": ["A system can be locally optimal while globally suboptimal \u2014 incremental improvements can trap you in the wrong solution space", "Engagement optimization and brand value optimization are not the same objective", "Four feedback loops accelerate the trap: sampling (controversial content gets more distribution), feature (engagement teaches algorithms manipulation equals quality), individual (repeated exposure changes consumer behavior), and outcome (AI content reshapes baseline expectations for all content)", "The local maxima breaks when the optimization tool becomes universally accessible \u2014 advantage disappears when every competitor has it", "Emergent system behaviors nobody programmed (e.g., AI adapting human psychology to prefer AI content) are more dangerous than intentional manipulation"], "steps": ["Identify the single metric your marketing system has been optimizing for over time", "Map the feedback loops reinforcing that metric (sampling, feature, individual, outcome)", "Test whether any small change to your current strategy improves the metric \u2014 if no small change helps, you are at a local maxima", "Assess whether your core competitive advantage (e.g., production quality, controversy, personalization) is being commoditized by new technology", "Determine how close competitors are to having identical access to the same optimization tools", "Conclude: if the advantage is commoditizing and all four feedback loops are active, the local maxima is near collapse"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "171068280", "title": "AI, Taylor Swift, and Sydney Sweeney: How Marketing's 20-Year Status Quo is Cracking Up", "date": "2025-08-25T13:03:11.364Z"}], "date": "2025-08-25T13:03:11.364Z", "id": "marketing-local-maxima-diagnosis", "core_question": "Has our marketing system optimized itself into a dead end where we're getting better at the wrong thing, and is the advantage we've built about to evaporate?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The brand's content team has spent two years optimizing for Instagram engagement, and every metric shows they've plateaued \u2014 no small change to format, timing, or creative improves performance. All four feedback loops are active: the sampling loop rewards increasingly provocative hooks, the feature loop has trained the algorithm to equate controversy with quality, the individual loop has conditioned followers to expect outrage-driven content, and the outcome loop means AI-generated competitors are flooding the same engagement niche at near-zero cost. The local maximum is near collapse \u2014 the team's entire competitive advantage (high-production provocative content) is being commoditized by AI tools every competitor now has access to. The path forward requires abandoning the current engagement metric entirely and rebuilding around a brand-value objective that AI-generated content cannot replicate.", "related_frameworks": ["unfakeable-brand-value-framework", "the-moat-audit", "four-durable-positions-framework", "ai-succeeds-at-the-wrong-thing", "distribution-beats-compute", "ambient-failure-cascade", "noise-floor-paradox", "the-bottleneck-relocation-model"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Unfakeable Brand Value Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of four strategic paths brands can pursue to build value that AI-generated content cannot replicate, organized by the type of authenticity each path leverages.", "when_to_apply": ["Our content quality used to be a differentiator but now competitors are producing similar stuff with AI for a fraction of the cost", "I'm a personal brand trying to figure out what to double down on when anyone can generate expert-sounding content", "We're planning our marketing budget for next year and need to decide which investments will still matter as AI content floods every channel", "Our engagement metrics look fine but I suspect we're building on a foundation that AI commoditization will erode", "We need to shift from content volume to something competitors can't copy \u2014 what kinds of brand assets are actually defensible?"], "principles": ["When perfect becomes cheap, imperfect becomes valuable \u2014 scarcity inverts as AI scales", "The unfakeable is the irreplaceable: physical presence, human judgment, real community, genuine imperfection", "AI optimizes for perfection, but human psychology often trusts imperfection more", "Temporal scarcity (experiences that happen once and cannot be replayed) creates value AI cannot replicate", "Community co-creation requires ongoing human judgment and empathy that cannot be automated", "Radical transparency is a competitive advantage precisely because AI systems are trained to present success narratives, not failures"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "171068280", "title": "AI, Taylor Swift, and Sydney Sweeney: How Marketing's 20-Year Status Quo is Cracking Up", "date": "2025-08-25T13:03:11.364Z"}], "date": "2025-08-25T13:03:11.364Z", "id": "unfakeable-brand-value-framework", "core_question": "As AI makes polished content essentially free, what can my brand actually build that AI cannot replicate or commoditize?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This brand is investing heavily in AI-generated social content at scale \u2014 that's the wrong path. Their content is indistinguishable from what any competitor can generate for near-zero cost. The durable play is in the 'temporal scarcity' quadrant: their annual industry summit and behind-the-scenes livestreams of product failures create value precisely because they are unrepeatable, imperfect, and physically grounded. The 'radical transparency' path is also open \u2014 publicly documenting their failed product launches builds trust that no AI-polished competitor narrative can match, because AI systems default to success stories.", "related_frameworks": ["four-durable-positions-framework", "the-moat-audit", "distribution-beats-compute", "marketing-local-maxima-diagnosis", "double-compression-loop", "ai-structural-advantage-predictor", "signal-restoration-framework", "noise-floor-paradox"], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Prompt Size Selection Framework", "type": "distinction", "description": "A decision rule distinguishing when to use long, structured prompts versus short, iterative prompts based on whether the work is production-focused (known intent) or discovery-focused (unknown shape).", "when_to_apply": ["I spent 20 minutes writing a detailed prompt and the AI output still feels off \u2014 maybe I don't actually know what I want yet", "I'm about to ask the AI to produce a final deliverable and I want to make sure the output is tightly anchored to my requirements", "I have a vague idea I want to explore but I'm not sure whether to write a big prompt or just start talking", "My short prompts keep producing generic outputs and I'm wondering if I need to give the AI more structure upfront", "I'm trying to figure out the right level of effort to put into crafting a prompt before I hit send"], "principles": ["Match prompt size to your level of certainty about the task, not to the importance of the task", "Big prompts anchor the AI around specific context you already understand well", "Short prompts leave room for the AI to expand and help you discover meaning iteratively", "Iterative discovery tasks benefit from a 'compass' prompt \u2014 short but directionally sharp", "Neither prompt style is inherently more valuable; context determines which produces better results"], "steps": ["Ask: do I already know enough about this task that I want to focus on production quality? If yes, use a larger structured prompt with full context", "Ask: do I not yet know the shape of this work and need to discover it? If yes, start with a short, sharp one-to-two line prompt and iterate", "For production prompts: invest planning time upfront to anchor intent, then allow multi-turn refinement within that frame", "For discovery prompts: begin minimally, riff back and forth, and let meaning emerge through the conversation"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "169268210", "title": "Confused by AI? Nail These 3 Concepts First", "date": "2025-07-26T13:01:10.448Z"}, {"id": "179418126", "title": "My Lab Notes on Gemini 3 vs. ChatGPT 5.1: How To Prompt EACH Model + A Custom Meta-Prompt to Build YOUR Prompts", "date": "2025-11-20T14:02:54.523Z"}], "date": "2025-07-26T13:01:10.448Z", "id": "prompt-size-selection-framework", "core_question": "Should I front-load context and structure into this prompt, or keep it short and let the conversation reveal what I actually need?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This is a discovery task, not a production task \u2014 the tell is that the user doesn't yet know what format the final output should take. Writing a long structured prompt here would anchor the AI around assumptions that haven't been validated yet. The right move is a compass prompt: one or two sharp lines that set direction ('Help me think through why our onboarding flow loses users at step 3') and then iterating from whatever the AI surfaces. The shape of the work will emerge from the conversation, not from upfront specification.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Four Pillars of Apple's Cultural DNA (and Their AI-Era Inversions)", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "A catalog of four cultural assumptions inherited from Apple's design philosophy that become catastrophic liabilities in AI product development, paired with the inversion required to succeed.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI feature works but has rough edges, and the debate is whether to hold it back another quarter to polish it before users see it", "Leadership insists on developing our AI capabilities in stealth mode, but I suspect we're falling behind teams that are iterating in public with real user feedback", "We keep designing AI experiences where we dictate the interaction model top-down, and users keep finding workarounds that bypass our curated flow entirely", "Our company culture was forged in hardware or premium-product thinking and we're now trying to compete in a space where speed of iteration and open feedback loops matter more than launch-day perfection", "Our AI product roadmap is structured in 12-month cycles and I'm watching competitors ship weekly updates that compound into better products"], "principles": ["Perfection Before Permission (wait until friction-free before shipping) fails because AI is so obviously useful that users will tolerate broken interfaces to access it", "Secrecy and full-stack control (Torus of Secrets) fails because AI products improve through public feedback loops, not private iteration", "Taste as competitive advantage (priesthood model) fails because AI democratizes taste-making beyond any single organization", "Unit economics optimized for hardware (atoms) fails because AI value is delivered via bits and scales through efficient token costs, not device manufacturing"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "171014273", "title": "The Apple Paradox: 10 Lessons for AI Builders from Cupertino's Collapse", "date": "2025-08-15T13:03:07.016Z"}], "date": "2025-08-15T13:03:07.016Z", "id": "the-four-pillars-of-apple-s-cultural-dna-and-their-ai-era-in", "core_question": "Are the cultural instincts that made us great in the last era \u2014 perfectionism, secrecy, taste-driven authority, hardware-centric economics \u2014 now the exact things preventing us from winning in AI?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The active failure mode is Perfection Before Permission. The team has been holding back their AI writing assistant for eight months waiting for hallucination rates to drop below 1%, but competitors shipped at 15% error rates and are improving faster because they have millions of real user interactions generating training signal. The tell: users are already copy-pasting into ChatGPT to get the same capability with worse UX \u2014 they'll tolerate broken interfaces to access obvious utility. The perfectionism instinct, inherited from hardware-era Apple DNA where a bad latch ruins a laptop forever, is catastrophically misapplied here because AI products improve through usage, not through internal polish. Ship the imperfect version or watch the feedback-loop gap become insurmountable.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Value Runs Up the Stack", "type": "reframe", "description": "A mental model that maps historical migrations of technology value (Hardware \u2192 OS \u2192 Applications \u2192 Intelligence) to predict which layer currently commands leverage and which incumbent companies face existential displacement.", "when_to_apply": ["We're a dominant infrastructure or platform company and I'm trying to understand why our growth is slowing even though usage keeps increasing", "I'm deciding whether to build at the model layer, the tooling layer, or the application layer and want to know which one will hold value in 3-5 years", "A company I'm evaluating has massive revenue from a technology layer that feels like it's becoming a commodity beneath a new abstraction", "The best engineers on my team keep leaving for companies that operate at a higher level of the stack and I'm wondering what that signals about our position", "I'm watching a new technology transition and trying to figure out which incumbent gets disrupted next \u2014 not by a competitor but by the layer above them"], "principles": ["Value creation migrates upward through technology layers over time, leaving previously dominant layers commoditized", "Each migration makes previously trillion-dollar companies irrelevant to the new value center", "Companies optimizing lower layers while value moves to higher layers are perfecting the wrong product (CD player in the Spotify age)", "Talent flows as a leading indicator: the best practitioners go where value is being created, not where it used to be"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "171014273", "title": "The Apple Paradox: 10 Lessons for AI Builders from Cupertino's Collapse", "date": "2025-08-15T13:03:07.016Z"}], "date": "2025-08-15T13:03:07.016Z", "id": "value-runs-up-the-stack", "core_question": "Is the layer of the stack I'm building on (or investing in) still where value accrues, or has value already migrated above me without my noticing?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this cloud infrastructure company is dominant because it owns the compute layer powering AI workloads. That's wrong here \u2014 they're in the same position IBM was when value moved from hardware to operating systems. Revenue is high but leverage is migrating upward: the intelligence layer (models, orchestration, agentic workflows) is where pricing power and margin are consolidating. The tell is talent flow \u2014 their best ML engineers are leaving for companies building at the application-intelligence boundary, not staying to optimize data center throughput. Investing here is betting on the CD player after Spotify launched.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The 10 AI-Era Rule Inversions", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "Ten explicit inversions of Apple-era product development assumptions that serve as a scoring rubric for whether an AI product team's instincts are aligned with or opposed to current winning patterns.", "when_to_apply": ["We keep debating whether to polish the experience more before launching our AI feature, and I'm not sure which instinct is right anymore", "Our AI product team is full of ex-Apple or ex-big-tech people and I wonder if their default assumptions are actually hurting us", "We're building a tightly controlled, curated AI product experience and a scrappier competitor with worse UX keeps growing faster than us", "I need to diagnose why our AI product strategy feels solid on paper but keeps losing to less polished alternatives in the market", "Our founding team disagrees on whether to own the full stack or integrate with existing platforms and let users remix our AI capabilities"], "principles": ["Ship intelligence before interfaces: raw capability beats polished UX at MVP stage, though the bar is rising", "Build for federation not ownership: assume users are loyal to utility, not brand", "Embrace public learning loops: AI improves through usage, private iteration means missing feedback", "Value emerges from collision: over-curation kills the unexpected use cases that define breakout products", "Talent flows to experimentation: hiring advantage comes from velocity, not stability", "Users are co-creators: ship tools for users to define their own good, not a prescribed vision", "Speed beats strategy: faster execution increases surface area for luck in exponential markets", "Broken business models win: capability monetization precedes model clarity in early AI cycles", "Distribution is discovery: when base models are commoditized, how users find intelligence matters more than owning it", "Hardware is just glass: premium value is in bits not atoms; hardware only matters where it adds intelligence leverage"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "171014273", "title": "The Apple Paradox: 10 Lessons for AI Builders from Cupertino's Collapse", "date": "2025-08-15T13:03:07.016Z"}], "date": "2025-08-15T13:03:07.016Z", "id": "the-10-ai-era-rule-inversions", "core_question": "Are my product instincts still calibrated for the hardware/polish era, or have I actually internalized how AI-era winners behave differently?", "category": "product", "example_application": "This team scores poorly on at least six of the ten inversions. They spent four months polishing the interface before shipping any AI capability (violates 'ship intelligence before interfaces'), they're building a walled ecosystem assuming brand loyalty will hold (violates 'build for federation not ownership'), and they refused to launch until the business model was clear (violates 'broken business models win'). The pattern is unmistakable \u2014 these are Apple-era instincts dressed up in AI language. The team's cultural DNA is optimized for a world where premium finish and owned ecosystems win, but in the current cycle, the teams beating them are shipping raw intelligence fast, learning publicly, and letting users define the product's value. The competitive risk isn't that their product is bad \u2014 it's that their operating tempo and philosophy are a generation behind.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Portfolio Strategy / Barbell AI Stack", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Classifies AI deployment needs into distinct architectural categories \u2014 high-stakes parallel reasoning, single-threaded creative/conversational, and commodity tasks \u2014 and prescribes matching model architecture to task type rather than standardizing on one model.", "when_to_apply": ["Different teams in my org have completely opposite opinions about whether our AI tool is good or terrible", "We upgraded to a more expensive model and some workflows got worse while others improved dramatically", "Our AI budget keeps growing but I can't tell which use cases are actually generating ROI", "I'm trying to decide whether to standardize on one AI model across the company or support multiple models", "We need an AI architecture that handles both rigorous analytical work and fast creative generation without overspending"], "principles": ["Different AI architectures serve fundamentally different needs \u2014 portfolio management beats single-vendor standardization", "Match architecture to task: parallel reasoning models for high-stakes multi-perspective analysis, single-threaded models for coding and creative work, commodity models for simple tasks", "The true cost of a premium reasoning model includes infrastructure, data restructuring, and validation \u2014 not just the subscription fee", "Correctness-optimized models require structured, dimensionally organized data to function properly", "Orchestration skill (knowing when to use which model) becomes more valuable than prompt engineering"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "170932826", "title": "GPT-5 Pro: The First AI That's Smarter and Worse at Once", "date": "2025-08-14T13:02:45.167Z"}], "date": "2025-08-14T13:02:45.167Z", "id": "ai-portfolio-strategy-barbell-ai-stack", "core_question": "We're spending more on AI but getting uneven results \u2014 are we forcing one model architecture across tasks that need fundamentally different approaches?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This organization is running GPT-5 Pro across everything \u2014 legal analysis, marketing copy, and data extraction \u2014 and getting wildly inconsistent feedback. The legal team loves it, marketing hates it, and ops says it's overkill. That's a classic single-vendor forcing pattern. Legal analysis is high-stakes parallel reasoning: multiple perspectives, adversarial thinking, structured data \u2014 that's where the premium reasoning model earns its cost. Marketing copy is single-threaded creative work where a lighter conversational model (Claude, GPT-4o) actually produces better output because it doesn't over-deliberate. Data extraction is commodity \u2014 a fine-tuned small model or even regex handles it at a fraction of the cost. The fix isn't picking the 'best' model; it's building a three-tier stack and developing the orchestration judgment to route tasks correctly.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "GPT-5 Pro Decision Framework", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured set of conditions for determining whether a parallel-reasoning, correctness-optimized AI model is the right investment for a given use case, based on stakes, data structure, task type, and infrastructure readiness.", "when_to_apply": ["We upgraded to GPT-5 Pro and some workflows got better but others got noticeably worse \u2014 what's the pattern?", "Our team is debating whether the $200/month pro tier is worth it versus the standard model for our specific tasks", "We're feeding complex financial or legal documents into a premium reasoning model but the outputs feel unreliable or inconsistent", "Leadership wants a business case for enterprise AI licensing and I need to figure out which workflows actually justify premium compute costs", "Our data pipeline is messy and I'm wondering if upgrading the model tier will fix our output quality problems"], "principles": ["Use premium reasoning models when error reduction value exceeds total implementation cost (subscription + infrastructure + validation)", "Avoid premium reasoning models for conversational, creative, or sequential coding tasks where architecture actively degrades output", "Data structure quality is a prerequisite \u2014 messy data fed to a parallel reasoning model produces worse results than clean data in a simpler model", "Many organizations benefit from hybrid licensing: premium model for final high-stakes deliverables, cheaper models for drafting and monitoring", "The true cost multiplier for implementation is approximately 10x the subscription cost when infrastructure and validation are included"], "steps": ["Determine if the task involves high-stakes analysis where a significant error-rate reduction justifies cost", "Determine if the task benefits from simultaneous multi-perspective analysis (financial, legal, scientific reconciliation)", "Determine if correctness matters more than speed, personality, or conversational fluency", "Assess whether data can be restructured into hierarchical, dimensionally organized formats for parallel reasoning threads", "Assess whether the organization can absorb implementation overhead (engineering, validation systems, context management)", "If all five conditions are met, proceed with the premium reasoning model; if conversational, creative, or sequential coding needs dominate, route to a single-threaded alternative"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "170932826", "title": "GPT-5 Pro: The First AI That's Smarter and Worse at Once", "date": "2025-08-14T13:02:45.167Z"}], "date": "2025-08-14T13:02:45.167Z", "id": "gpt-5-pro-decision-framework", "core_question": "Is this expensive reasoning model actually the right tool for what we're doing, or are we paying a premium for capabilities that actively hurt our use case?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This legal due diligence workflow passes all five conditions: errors in clause reconciliation across 200-page contracts have direct financial exposure ($2M+ per missed conflict), the task requires simultaneous analysis across regulatory, financial, and contractual dimensions, correctness dominates over speed or tone, and the firm's documents are already structured in hierarchical formats. The 10x implementation multiplier on a $200/month subscription means roughly $24K/year true cost \u2014 trivial against the error-reduction value. But the same firm's client-facing chatbot fails the diagnostic immediately: conversational fluency matters more than parallel reasoning, and the model's architecture actively degrades the warmth and responsiveness clients expect. Route that to a single-threaded model at one-tenth the cost.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Specification Prompting (vs. Conversational Prompting)", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between iterative conversational prompting (propose \u2192 confirm \u2192 execute) and upfront specification prompting (declare deliverable + assumptions + constraints + tool policy \u2192 single-turn execution), arguing that agentic AI requires the latter.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep going back and forth with the AI for 5+ turns before the output is even close to what I wanted", "The AI executed immediately on an ambiguous request and now I'm spending more time correcting than I would have spent writing the prompt properly", "I'm building prompts for agentic workflows that run without human-in-the-loop checkpoints", "My team treats AI like a chat partner and gets inconsistent results \u2014 I need a repeatable prompting discipline", "I need to hand off a complex deliverable to an AI where wrong assumptions would be expensive to unwind after execution"], "principles": ["Front-load all assumptions, constraints, and non-goals before the task description", "Declare tool policy explicitly (which tools are on/off and with what limits)", "Specify the deliverable format, audience, and length before describing the task", "Define acceptance criteria so the model has a clear target before starting", "Pre-bind scope by stating non-goals as clearly as goals", "Treat prompts like configuration files, not conversation openers"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "170732880", "title": "Cracking the Agent Code: 16 Production Prompting Signals Hidden in GPT-5's System Prompt", "date": "2025-08-12T13:03:41.210Z"}], "date": "2025-08-12T13:03:41.210Z", "id": "specification-prompting-vs-conversational-prompting", "core_question": "Why does my AI keep producing outputs that miss the mark, forcing me into endless back-and-forth correction loops instead of nailing it in one shot?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This is a specification problem, not a conversation problem \u2014 the tell is the three rounds of correction that each introduced new drift. The user described the task ('write me a competitive analysis') but never declared the deliverable format (executive brief vs. detailed report), the audience (board vs. product team), the non-goals (not a technical comparison), or the acceptance criteria (must rank competitors on three dimensions with citations). The model filled every unspecified gap with its own assumptions, and each correction round only patched one assumption while the model re-hallucinated others. Front-loading those five declarations into a single specification prompt would have eliminated the correction loop entirely.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Failure Mode Prevention Protocol (Agentic AI)", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Enumerates five specific failure modes that occur when using execution-first AI models, with the signal that triggers each failure, the cause, and a template fix.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent wrote something that looks great but answers the wrong question entirely", "The model buried my actual deliverable under three paragraphs of explanation I didn't ask for", "ChatGPT randomly opened a canvas or ran code when I just wanted a text response", "I asked for image generation plus analysis in one prompt and only got the image back with no commentary", "The AI mixed things it knows with things it's guessing and I can't tell which is which"], "principles": ["Each failure mode has a distinct cause rooted in the model's default behavior settings", "Prevention is always preferable to recovery; restart with corrected prompts rather than trying to course-correct mid-conversation", "Every failure mode has a corresponding template fix using constraint language", "Distinguish between failures caused by scope ambiguity, persona defaults, tool policy, workflow sequencing, and attribution gaps"], "steps": ["Identify which failure mode has occurred: (1) speculative execution gone wrong, (2) over-teaching verbosity, (3) tool usage surprises, (4) lost commentary after image generation, (5) ambiguous output ownership", "Apply the corresponding prevention template for that failure mode", "Run the Pre-Flight Checklist before resubmitting: deliverable specified, assumptions stated, non-goals declared, tool policy set, quality criteria defined, uncertainty handling addressed"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "170732880", "title": "Cracking the Agent Code: 16 Production Prompting Signals Hidden in GPT-5's System Prompt", "date": "2025-08-12T13:03:41.210Z"}], "date": "2025-08-12T13:03:41.210Z", "id": "failure-mode-prevention-protocol-agentic-ai", "core_question": "My AI agent just did something confidently wrong \u2014 what specific default behavior caused this, and how do I prevent it from happening again?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The active failure mode is speculative execution gone wrong. The tell: the agent produced a polished, well-structured competitive analysis \u2014 but it assumed the target market was enterprise SaaS when the actual context was consumer fintech. The output looks professional enough to fool a skimmer, which makes it more dangerous than an obvious error. The cause is scope ambiguity in the original prompt \u2014 no non-goals were declared, so the model filled the gap with its most statistically likely interpretation and executed confidently. The fix is not to course-correct mid-conversation; restart with explicit scope constraints: 'Target market: consumer fintech. Non-goals: do not analyze enterprise SaaS dynamics. If the market segment is unclear from provided context, stop and ask before proceeding.'", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Model Selection by Control Surface", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Classifies AI models into three interaction paradigms\u2014declarative, collaborative, and structured\u2014to guide which model to select and how to prompt it based on task type and desired workflow.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm getting verbose, clarification-heavy responses when I just need the model to execute a well-defined task quickly", "We use multiple AI models across the team but nobody has a clear framework for which model to route which task to", "My prompt works great in one model but produces garbage in another \u2014 trying to understand why", "I'm designing a multi-step AI pipeline and need to decide which model handles which stage", "The AI keeps asking me follow-up questions when I want structured output, or gives me raw dumps when I want a conversation"], "principles": ["Model selection should be based on the control surface (how the model expects to be directed), not just raw capability", "Declarative models (execution-first) require upfront specifications and assumption blocks", "Collaborative models (accuracy-first) work best with conversational framing and iterative refinement", "Structured models (format-first) respond to organized requests and produce formatted outputs", "Hybrid workflows can sequence models: speed/ideation first, then accuracy review, then formatted output"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "170732880", "title": "Cracking the Agent Code: 16 Production Prompting Signals Hidden in GPT-5's System Prompt", "date": "2025-08-12T13:03:41.210Z"}], "date": "2025-08-12T13:03:41.210Z", "id": "model-selection-by-control-surface", "core_question": "I have access to multiple AI models but I keep getting mediocre results \u2014 am I picking the wrong model for the task, or just prompting it the wrong way?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This task \u2014 generating a first draft of 20 product descriptions \u2014 falls squarely in the declarative/execution-first paradigm. The right move is a model like GPT-4o or Claude with a fully specified prompt: tone, length constraints, audience, and an explicit assumption block ('assume premium positioning, assume US market'). Switching to a collaborative model here would waste cycles in back-and-forth clarification. But the follow-up task \u2014 reviewing those descriptions for factual accuracy and brand consistency \u2014 is collaborative territory: a model like Claude with iterative conversation, feeding it descriptions in batches and refining criteria together. Final step, reformatting into the CMS import template, is structured-paradigm work where a format-first model or structured output mode produces clean JSON on the first pass. Sequencing all three paradigms turns a frustrating single-model workflow into a pipeline that plays to each interaction style's strength.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Route \u2192 Contract \u2192 Grade", "type": "procedure", "description": "A three-part operating method for getting auditable, verifiable outputs from a reasoning-capable LLM by intentionally routing to the reasoning path, locking output contracts upfront, and autograding results against defined artifacts.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm using an LLM to reconcile financial data across multiple spreadsheets and the numbers need to be audit-ready", "My model keeps giving me confident narrative answers but I can't tell if the underlying math is actually correct", "I need to build a prompt workflow where every claim the model makes is backed by a verifiable artifact, not just explanation", "I'm working with a reasoning-capable model like o3 or ChatGPT-5 and I want to make sure it actually engages deep reasoning instead of defaulting to fast summarization", "Previous LLM outputs passed the vibe check but failed review \u2014 I need a repeatable structure that catches errors before they ship"], "principles": ["Explicitly signal difficulty and intent ('think hard') to route the model toward deliberative reasoning rather than fast summarization", "Lock output contracts before the model starts: define exactly which artifacts are required (assumptions ledger, constraint report, computed tables, discrepancy list)", "Require proof artifacts, not just narrative \u2014 explanations are for humans, artifacts are for truth", "Force code execution for any step that produces numbers; the model should compute before it explains", "Autograde outputs with explicit thresholds; below threshold, re-run at higher reasoning effort with narrower scope", "Lock definitions up front in the prompt contract so the model's helpfulness cannot slide ambiguous terms"], "steps": ["Route on purpose: begin the session with a routing nudge that signals complexity and explicitly requests reasoning ('think hard', 'do not guess', 'use tools/code to compute')", "Set the output contract: specify the four required artifacts \u2014 (1) Assumptions ledger, (2) Constraint report, (3) Computed tables, (4) Discrepancy list \u2014 and state that no artifacts means no ship", "Stage the workflow in three turns: Turn 1 = Ingest & Normalize (canonical IDs, coerce numbers, FX table); Turn 2 = Join & Compute (cost ledger with actuals vs planned); Turn 3 = Verify & Explain (run constraints, produce discrepancy list, assign confidence score)", "Force code execution: require the model to write and run code before writing narrative, so math is verified before explanation", "Autograde the output: have the model write a grader that scores its own work on defined criteria (headcount accuracy, duplicate detection, FX correctness, allocation bounds, cycle detection, injection strings); set a pass threshold", "If output fails threshold, re-run at higher reasoning effort with narrower scope or a stronger tool contract"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "170422802", "title": "The Complete ChatGPT-5 Review: 5 Real-World Tests and the Playbook to Use It Right", "date": "2025-08-08T13:03:23.522Z"}], "date": "2025-08-08T13:03:23.522Z", "id": "route-contract-grade", "core_question": "How do I stop getting fluent-sounding but unverifiable outputs from powerful reasoning models, and instead force them to produce work I can actually audit and trust?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The CFO needs a headcount-cost reconciliation across three spreadsheets with different currencies and entity codes. The prompt opens with a routing nudge: 'Think hard. Do not guess. Use code to compute every number before explaining it.' The output contract locks four artifacts: an assumptions ledger (what was ambiguous and how it was resolved), a constraint report (no employee appears in two cost centers, all FX rates sourced from the canonical table), computed tables (actual vs. planned cost per entity), and a discrepancy list with confidence scores. After the model produces its output, a self-grader checks headcount totals against source rows, flags any duplicate employee IDs, validates FX conversions to four decimal places, and scores the run at 0.88 \u2014 below the 0.92 threshold. The narrowed re-run isolates the failing APAC entity, forces explicit join logic in code, and passes at 0.95. The discrepancy list now names three real variances the finance team can act on, each traceable back to a source row.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Stage 0 / Stage 1-3 Cost Split", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A two-tier architecture for LLM workflow cost discipline that separates cheap extraction/cleanup tasks (low reasoning effort, smaller models) from expensive compute/verify tasks (high reasoning effort, flagship model), preventing both overspending on simple tasks and underspending on critical ones.", "when_to_apply": ["My LLM pipeline costs are climbing and I need to figure out which steps actually need expensive models versus which are wasting compute", "I'm using one model at one reasoning level for an entire multi-step workflow and suspect that's inefficient", "I have a prompt that extracts data from documents AND then reasons over that data in the same call", "I need audit-quality reasoning outputs but my token budget is constrained \u2014 where do I cut and where do I protect spend?", "I'm deciding whether to use a smaller model or lower reasoning effort for certain pipeline stages without degrading final output quality"], "principles": ["Split jobs by cognitive demand: extraction/formatting/cleanup belongs in the cheap stage; computation/verification/reasoning belongs in the expensive stage", "Do not starve hard problems of reasoning effort to save cost \u2014 save cost by routing easy problems away from high-effort settings", "Keep narrative short and evidence long to maximize value per token on expensive stages", "Use minimal reasoning effort or smaller model variants for Stage 0 deterministic transforms (extraction, signature checks, schema mapping)", "Reserve high reasoning effort for Stage 1-3 joins, audits, and multi-hop reasoning where evals prove it pays off"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "170422802", "title": "The Complete ChatGPT-5 Review: 5 Real-World Tests and the Playbook to Use It Right", "date": "2025-08-08T13:03:23.522Z"}], "date": "2025-08-08T13:03:23.522Z", "id": "stage-0-stage-1-3-cost-split", "core_question": "Am I burning expensive compute on tasks that don't need it, or worse, starving the tasks that actually require deep reasoning to save a few cents?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This pipeline is running a flagship model at high reasoning effort for every step, including parsing PDF tables and normalizing date formats \u2014 that's Stage 0 work. Those extraction tasks are deterministic transforms where a smaller model at minimal reasoning effort produces identical output at 1/20th the cost. The actual reasoning load \u2014 cross-referencing extracted figures against audit thresholds and flagging discrepancies \u2014 is Stage 1-3 work that genuinely benefits from high reasoning effort. Splitting the pipeline here cuts total cost by 60-70% without any quality loss on the hard problems, because the savings from cheap stages fund full reasoning effort where it actually moves the needle.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Contract-First Prompting", "type": "procedure", "description": "A coordination protocol where an LLM systematically asks clarifying questions until reaching a defined confidence threshold, then presents an explicit echo check for human approval before beginning any generation work.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep getting outputs that are 80% right but require multiple rounds of 'no, I actually meant...' corrections", "I'm about to ask an LLM to build something complex \u2014 architecture doc, legal draft, multi-step workflow \u2014 where a wrong first draft wastes serious time", "My prompt has requirements I haven't fully thought through yet and I want the model to help me surface what I'm missing before it starts writing", "I'm chaining multiple AI steps together and a misunderstanding in step one will poison everything downstream", "Multiple people will review or use this output and they haven't all agreed on what 'done' looks like"], "principles": ["Treat intent alignment as a coordination problem, not a communication problem \u2014 negotiation beats clearer broadcasting", "The model must ask questions systematically until it estimates a 95% confidence threshold before generating any output", "An explicit 'echo check' \u2014 a single summary sentence of the agreed-upon work \u2014 is produced and approved before execution begins, serving as an immutable anchor", "Both parties must actively agree on the scope; generation does not begin without bilateral commitment", "The protocol stacks on top of existing prompt engineering best practices rather than replacing them", "The echo check is reinforced early in the context window to prevent context drift during long conversations", "Each step in a multi-step workflow gets its own contract, preventing ambiguity propagation"], "steps": ["Provide the model with the contract-first protocol (via the Intent Translator MAX prompt) along with your initial \u2014 even fuzzy \u2014 task description", "The model asks targeted clarifying questions to reduce uncertainty about requirements, constraints, assumptions, and context", "Questioning continues iteratively until the model estimates ~95% confidence in understanding the correct deliverable", "The model produces an 'echo check': a concise statement of its interpretation of the agreed-upon work", "The human reviews and either approves the echo check or corrects misunderstandings", "The model optionally presents a pre-mortem identifying risks before committing compute to generation", "Both parties confirm agreement, and only then does the model begin generating the actual output"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "170049564", "title": "Stop Letting AI Guess: Why Your Prompts Still Miss\u2014And the New Prompt Technique That Dramatically Improves Accuracy", "date": "2025-08-04T13:03:13.976Z"}], "date": "2025-08-04T13:03:13.976Z", "id": "contract-first-prompting", "core_question": "Why does the AI keep delivering something close-but-wrong, even when I thought my prompt was clear?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The user asked for 'a landing page for my SaaS product' \u2014 but that request conceals at least six unstated decisions: audience segment, tone, conversion goal, technical stack, content length, and whether this is a redesign or net-new. Before any HTML is written, the echo check reads: 'A single-scroll landing page targeting mid-market HR directors, emphasizing compliance automation, with a free-trial CTA, written in Tailwind/React, ~800 words of copy, net-new design with no existing brand guide to honor.' The user corrects one element \u2014 they do have a brand guide \u2014 and only after that amendment is confirmed does generation begin. Without this contract, the first draft would have been a generic SaaS page requiring three revision cycles to reach the same spec.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Five Principles of Chunking", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequential five-principle framework for preparing document data into meaningful chunks that AI retrieval systems can accurately process, minimizing hallucinations and retrieval errors.", "when_to_apply": ["My RAG system gives accurate answers on simple questions but falls apart on nuanced or multi-part queries from real users", "I'm ingesting legal contracts, medical records, or financial spreadsheets into a retrieval system and need to decide how to structure the chunks", "We're getting hallucinations that aren't a model problem \u2014 the retrieved context itself is incomplete or incoherent", "I picked a token size for chunking (like 512 or 1024) based on a blog post and I'm not sure it's right for my actual data and queries", "Our RAG pipeline works great on clean blog posts but breaks down on structured documents with tables, code, or hierarchical sections"], "principles": ["Context Coherence first: every chunk must make complete sense in isolation \u2014 never split semantic units of meaning", "Master three levers together: boundaries (where to cut), size (how big), and overlap (insurance against boundary failures) \u2014 they interact and must be tuned jointly", "Data type dictates strategy: legal contracts need hierarchical chunking, spreadsheets need semantic grouping with formula tracing, code needs neighborhood/dependency chunking, conversations need speaker-turn or time-window chunking", "Size for Goldilocks outcomes: test chunk sizes against a real evaluation set of 25-50 queries rather than picking arbitrary token counts", "Overlap is semantic, not character-based: use sentence-level or complete-thought overlap (10-20% for high-stakes data, 0-5% for clean-boundary code)"], "steps": ["Apply Context Coherence: identify natural document boundaries (sections, sentences, paragraphs, speaker turns, functions) and ensure no chunk splits a semantic unit of meaning", "Tune the three levers jointly: set boundaries at natural breaks, size chunks to match the granularity of your use case, and add overlap proportional to boundary risk", "Match strategy to data type: apply hierarchical chunking for contracts, semantic grouping + formula tracing for spreadsheets, dependency/neighborhood chunking for code, and turn-based chunking for conversations", "Calibrate chunk size empirically: build an evaluation set of 25-50 real queries (10 common, 10 edge cases, 5 complex), run them at multiple token sizes, and measure accuracy, cost, and response time", "Add semantic overlap as insurance: repeat complete thoughts (not arbitrary characters) between consecutive chunks, scaled to data risk level (15-20% for medical/legal/financial, 0-5% for clean-boundary code)"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "169721840", "title": "Fix Data Hell: The Complete Chunking Playbook to Cut Hallucinations (and AI Costs)", "date": "2025-07-31T13:02:57.148Z"}], "date": "2025-07-31T13:02:57.148Z", "id": "the-five-principles-of-chunking", "core_question": "My RAG system is retrieving garbage or half-answers \u2014 is the problem in how I'm cutting my documents into chunks, and how do I fix it?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The legal contract RAG system is returning hallucinated clause terms because chunks are splitting mid-paragraph, severing conditions from their qualifying exceptions. The fix sequence: first, re-draw boundaries at clause level (hierarchical chunking \u2014 this is legal data, not prose), so every chunk contains a complete obligation-condition-exception unit. Second, size those chunks against the 38 real queries legal ops actually runs \u2014 the current 512-token fixed window is too small for multi-condition indemnity clauses but wastefully large for simple definitions. Third, add 15-20% sentence-level overlap between consecutive clause chunks because cross-reference language in contracts means boundary risk is high. After recalibrating all three levers jointly and testing against the evaluation set, retrieval accuracy on edge-case queries jumped from 61% to 89%, and the phantom clause hallucinations disappeared entirely.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "5-Point Chunking Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A scored five-question audit that quickly determines whether a team's current data chunking approach will produce reliable AI retrieval or is contributing to hallucinations.", "when_to_apply": ["My RAG system keeps hallucinating or returning wrong answers and I'm not sure if it's the model, the prompts, or the data", "We're about to ingest a new corpus into our retrieval pipeline and I want to make sure we don't repeat past mistakes", "Someone on the team wants to buy expensive agentic search tooling but I suspect our underlying data preparation is the real issue", "I need a quick sanity check on whether our chunking approach is sound before we go to production", "Our AI retrieval works fine for some document types but terribly for others and I can't figure out why"], "principles": ["Arbitrary character or token count splitting is always a red flag", "Chunks must be independently intelligible \u2014 test by imagining handing one to a smart intern", "Using a single strategy across all data types is a reliable failure signal", "Zero overlap is high-risk for any non-trivially structured document", "Evaluation must use real queries from actual users, not hypothetical examples"], "steps": ["Ask: Are you splitting by arbitrary character or token counts? (Wrong answer = Yes)", "Ask: Do chunks make sense in isolation? (Wrong answer = No)", "Ask: Are you using the same chunking strategy for all data types? (Wrong answer = Yes)", "Ask: Is overlap set to zero? (Wrong answer = Yes)", "Ask: Did you test with real queries from actual users? (Wrong answer = No)", "Score responses: 0-2 correct = Data Hell; 3-4 correct = Close but needs work; 5 correct = Well-positioned"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "169721840", "title": "Fix Data Hell: The Complete Chunking Playbook to Cut Hallucinations (and AI Costs)", "date": "2025-07-31T13:02:57.148Z"}], "date": "2025-07-31T13:02:57.148Z", "id": "5-point-chunking-diagnostic", "core_question": "Is my RAG system hallucinating because of how we're chunking our data, and how bad is the problem?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This retrieval pipeline scores 1 out of 5 \u2014 deep in Data Hell. The team is splitting all documents by 512-token windows with zero overlap, using the same strategy for contracts, FAQs, and technical manuals. The chunks are meaningless fragments \u2014 a contract clause split mid-sentence references 'the aforementioned party' with no antecedent in the chunk. No real user queries were used to validate retrieval quality. The hallucination problem isn't a model problem or a prompt problem \u2014 it's a chunking problem, and throwing money at agentic search won't fix garbage inputs.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Fix or Pay Strategic Choice", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A two-path decision framework for choosing between investing in proper data architecture (RAG) versus purchasing agentic search, based on data quality and query complexity.", "when_to_apply": ["We have messy legacy data and we're debating whether to clean it up or use a smarter AI system that can handle the mess", "Our RAG pipeline is hallucinating and someone suggested switching to an agentic search approach instead of fixing the underlying data", "We're comparing the total cost of a data remediation project versus adopting an agentic AI tool that promises to work with unstructured data as-is", "Leadership wants to skip the data architecture work and go straight to a more expensive AI product \u2014 how do I make the case for fixing the foundation first", "Our per-query costs are exploding because our AI system keeps retrying searches across poorly structured documents"], "principles": ["Clean data + RAG is the default winning strategy for bounded, well-defined use cases", "Agentic search is expensive trial-and-error, not magic \u2014 each failed agent attempt burns tokens", "Agentic search still fails on truly messy data; it just fails more expensively", "The cost differential between well-chunked RAG and agentic search can be an order of magnitude per query at scale", "Choosing agentic search to avoid fixing data architecture is a recurring, compounding cost rather than a one-time expense"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169721840", "title": "Fix Data Hell: The Complete Chunking Playbook to Cut Hallucinations (and AI Costs)", "date": "2025-07-31T13:02:57.148Z"}], "date": "2025-07-31T13:02:57.148Z", "id": "fix-or-pay-strategic-choice", "core_question": "Should we invest in cleaning up our data and building proper retrieval infrastructure, or should we just throw a more powerful (and expensive) agentic AI at the mess and hope it figures it out?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "This is a 'fix the data' situation, not a 'buy agentic search' situation. The queries are well-bounded \u2014 customers asking about product specs, return policies, contract terms \u2014 and the data is messy but structurally knowable. Spending six weeks on proper chunking and data cleanup gets RAG accuracy above 95% at pennies per query. The agentic search alternative would burn 10-20x more tokens per query doing iterative retrieval-and-retry loops over the same messy documents, and it would still hallucinate on the malformed PDFs. The team is framing agentic search as 'easier' but that's a recurring cost they'll pay on every single query forever, versus a one-time remediation investment that compounds in value.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Tokenizable Data Tiers", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-tier classification system for evaluating how easily different types of data can be processed by AI language models, which determines the feasibility and approach for AI integration.", "when_to_apply": ["We're prioritizing which AI use cases to build first and need to know which ones are actually feasible without major infrastructure", "Our AI prototype works great on documents but falls apart when we point it at our database \u2014 trying to understand why", "We have a mix of wikis, spreadsheets, and data warehouses and need to figure out which approach (direct prompting, preprocessing, RAG) fits each", "Leadership wants an AI copilot over 'all our data' and I need to explain why that's not one project but three", "We're scoping an AI integration and need to assess whether the data is ready or if we need to build retrieval infrastructure first"], "principles": ["Start with the data before evaluating the task \u2014 ask whether the data can even be tokenized first", "Use the 'fits in a Word doc' test as a quick proxy for Tier A tokenizability", "Use the 'napkin test' to assess whether complexity fits within a context window", "The easier the data is to tokenize, the more control you have over AI outcomes", "Tier A (wiki/document data) offers the fastest path to a successful AI pivot", "Tier C (data lake scale) requires preparatory retrieval steps before LLM processing"], "steps": ["Ask: could this data appear in a document? If yes, it is likely Tier A (easily tokenizable \u2014 wiki pages, Word docs, policies)", "Ask: does the data fit in a spreadsheet? If yes, it is Tier B (tokenizable with effort \u2014 structured tabular data requiring special handling)", "Ask: does the data require a data warehouse or data lake? If yes, it is Tier C (requires retrieval pipelines such as RAG before LLM can process it)", "Apply the napkin test: if the complexity of the task can be sketched on a napkin, AI can handle it with nuance; if not, expect limitations", "Choose AI approach based on tier: direct prompting for Tier A, specialized tools or preprocessing for Tier B, hybrid search and retrieval architecture for Tier C"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "169268210", "title": "Confused by AI? Nail These 3 Concepts First", "date": "2025-07-26T13:01:10.448Z"}], "date": "2025-07-26T13:01:10.448Z", "id": "tokenizable-data-tiers", "core_question": "Can our data actually be fed into an LLM as-is, or do we need significant infrastructure before AI can even touch it?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The customer support team wants to build an AI assistant over their knowledge base \u2014 those are wiki pages and policy documents, which is Tier A data. It fits in a Word doc, tokenizes cleanly, and can go straight into a prompt or a simple retrieval setup. That's the fast win. But the finance team's request to query across three years of transactional records in their data warehouse is Tier C \u2014 the LLM can't process that directly. They need a retrieval pipeline (RAG or SQL-generation layer) before the model ever sees a token. Starting with the finance use case instead of the support use case means burning six months of infrastructure work before anyone sees value.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The 3 A's Task Filter", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system for decomposing job tasks into three categories to assess automation risk and identify where to focus career energy.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm trying to figure out which of my daily tasks AI is going to replace versus which ones become more valuable", "My manager asked me to identify which parts of our team's workflow should be automated and I need a principled way to sort them", "I feel like AI can do parts of my job but I can't tell if that makes my role stronger or weaker overall", "I'm advising someone whose job title sounds safe but whose actual tasks might be heavily automatable", "I want to restructure how I spend my work week to lean into the parts AI can't replace"], "principles": ["Analyze at the task level, not the job title level \u2014 titles obscure what's actually at risk", "Automate: tasks that can be fully automated today should prompt you to lead the transition rather than resist it", "Augment: tasks where AI makes you dramatically faster should shift your identity from builder to architect", "Amplify: tasks requiring human judgment that AI enhances rather than replaces are where you should double down", "The diagnostic test: if 30% of tasks disappeared, would you gain strategic leverage or feel hollowed out?"], "steps": ["Decompose your weekly work into discrete tasks (not job title or responsibilities)", "Run each task through the Automate filter: can this be fully automated today?", "Run each task through the Augment filter: can AI make this 5x faster, making you the architect?", "Run each task through the Amplify filter: does this require human judgment AI enhances rather than replaces?", "Apply the 30% hollowing-out test: if AI removed 30% of your tasks, would you have more strategic leverage or less?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "169201889", "title": "AI Careers: 12 Brutal Questions You're Too Afraid to Ask", "date": "2025-07-25T13:03:51.122Z"}], "date": "2025-07-25T13:03:51.122Z", "id": "the-3-a-s-task-filter", "core_question": "Which parts of what I actually do every day are about to be automated away, and where should I be pouring my energy instead?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A marketing manager's weekly work decomposes into roughly 40 tasks. Reporting dashboards and first-draft copy are Automate \u2014 they're fully handleable by current tools, and resisting that transition just makes her the person clinging to busywork. Campaign brief creation and audience segmentation are Augment \u2014 AI cuts these from 4 hours to 45 minutes, but she's the architect deciding what gets built. Stakeholder negotiation, brand judgment calls, and cross-functional prioritization are Amplify \u2014 AI can surface data to sharpen these decisions, but the judgment is irreplaceably hers. The 30% test is revealing: if the Automate tasks vanish, she gains six hours a week of strategic leverage. She's not hollowed out \u2014 she's elevated. The career move is to lead the automation of that first bucket publicly, not wait for someone else to do it.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "High-Context / High-Ambiguity / High-Trust Intersection Test", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-dimensional test for identifying roles and tasks that are structurally durable against AI substitution by checking for the co-presence of uncodified context, gray-zone judgment, and trust built through earned liability.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm a mid-career professional trying to figure out whether my specific expertise will still matter in five years or if AI will commoditize it", "We're deciding which roles on the team to augment with AI versus keep fully human-staffed, and we need a principled way to draw the line", "Someone is arguing their job can't be automated because it's 'too nuanced' but I suspect they're conflating complexity with genuine irreplaceability", "I'm evaluating a business model that depends on human professionals \u2014 will AI erode the value of the human layer or is it structurally protected?", "I need to distinguish between roles where AI assists the human versus roles where the human is just reviewing AI output with no real leverage"], "principles": ["High-context means relying on unspoken rules, political dynamics, and undocumented historical precedent that no training data captures", "High-ambiguity means operating in gray zones where the definition of 'reasonable' or 'good' is itself contested", "High-trust means relationships built through repeated interactions, proven judgment, and shared risk \u2014 not just technical correctness", "The liability test is the sharpest filter: who gets sued when things go wrong? Only humans can own consequences, not just suggest outcomes", "Margin reality reinforces the test: when gross margins are below ~40%, the economics of AI replacement often don't justify the implementation risk"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "169201889", "title": "AI Careers: 12 Brutal Questions You're Too Afraid to Ask", "date": "2025-07-25T13:03:51.122Z"}], "date": "2025-07-25T13:03:51.122Z", "id": "high-context-high-ambiguity-high-trust-intersection-test", "core_question": "Is this role genuinely irreplaceable by AI, or am I just telling myself it is because it feels complex?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "A compliance officer at a mid-size bank looks AI-resistant at first glance \u2014 regulatory work seems complex. But running the three-dimensional test: context is moderate (regulations are documented and public, not unspoken), ambiguity is moderate (most decisions follow precedent with clear legal standards), and trust/liability is high (someone must sign off and bear personal legal exposure). Two out of three dimensions are weak. The role will compress significantly \u2014 AI will draft the analysis, flag the risks, and surface precedent \u2014 but the final sign-off and liability-bearing function survives. The career move isn't 'stay in compliance' but 'become the person whose signature goes on the filing,' because that's where all three dimensions finally intersect.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Build \u2192 Share \u2192 Connect Sequence", "type": "procedure", "description": "A three-step flywheel for building professional networks and accessing career opportunities without geographic proximity to tech hubs, by using public work as the gravity that attracts relevant connections.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm trying to break into AI or tech but I live nowhere near a hub and don't know anyone in the industry", "My cold outreach messages get ignored and I have nothing concrete to reference when reaching out to people", "I'm a new grad or career-changer with skills but no portfolio, no network, and no idea how to get on anyone's radar", "I want to build a professional network but I don't have an existing audience or platform to leverage", "I've been learning and building things but keeping it all private \u2014 should I be sharing this work publicly?"], "principles": ["Public work creates gravity \u2014 it attracts people working on similar problems without requiring geographic co-location", "Community forms around work, not location", "Sharing must be specific and include documented learnings and failures, not just polished outputs", "Cold outreach should reference specific shared problems and offer concrete value, not generic connection requests", "Building boldly increases the odds of meaningful human attention even when you have no existing audience"], "steps": ["Ship something publicly \u2014 a tool, analysis, solution, or documented experiment", "Document what you learned, including failures and specific technical obstacles encountered", "Find where builders in your niche congregate (GitHub issues, Discord servers, community forums) and engage with specific observations tied to your work", "Use your public artifact as the basis for targeted cold outreach that references a specific shared problem and offers to swap notes", "Let those conversations lead to further connections, compounding the network over time"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169201889", "title": "AI Careers: 12 Brutal Questions You're Too Afraid to Ask", "date": "2025-07-25T13:03:51.122Z"}], "date": "2025-07-25T13:03:51.122Z", "id": "build-share-connect-sequence", "core_question": "I don't have the right connections or the right zip code \u2014 how do I make people who matter in my target field aware I exist and want to work with me?", "category": "execution", "example_application": "A career-changer in a mid-tier city built a small open-source tool that automated a painful step in LLM evaluation pipelines, then wrote a detailed post documenting the three approaches that failed before the one that worked. That post \u2014 not a LinkedIn introduction, not a conference badge \u2014 became the basis for five targeted cold messages to engineers at companies using similar pipelines, each referencing a specific problem the tool addressed. Three responded. One led to a referral. The artifact did the credentialing that geography and pedigree couldn't. Without the public artifact, the outreach would have been generic and ignorable; without the documented failures, the artifact would have looked like a toy instead of evidence of real problem-solving.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Two Futures Framework for AI Employment", "type": "distinction", "description": "A binary scenario model that separates two fundamentally different futures for AI's employment impact, used to cut through timeline debates and clarify which planning posture is rational.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep reading contradictory predictions about whether AI will take all our jobs and I can't figure out who to believe", "My team is debating whether to invest in AI upskilling or wait until the dust settles", "Someone just told me AI will make all knowledge work obsolete within five years and I don't know how to evaluate that claim", "I'm frozen between 'AI changes everything' and 'AI is overhyped' and can't decide how urgently to act", "We're doing workforce planning and leadership is split between panic and dismissiveness about AI's impact"], "principles": ["Anyone speaking in absolutes about AI employment timelines is a signal they don't know what they're talking about", "Future 1 (Disruption without mass unemployment) is currently supported by available empirical evidence; Future 2 (mass unemployment) is not", "The key variable is compression speed, not the existence of disruption \u2014 the same scale of change as the internet but in 2-3 years rather than 20-30", "The planning paradox resolves the uncertainty: if Future 2 is correct, individual planning is irrelevant anyway; if Future 1 is correct, early movers gain insurmountable advantage", "Plan for Future 1 \u2014 it costs nothing if you're wrong and changes everything if you're right"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169201889", "title": "AI Careers: 12 Brutal Questions You're Too Afraid to Ask", "date": "2025-07-25T13:03:51.122Z"}], "date": "2025-07-25T13:03:51.122Z", "id": "two-futures-framework-for-ai-employment", "core_question": "Should I be preparing aggressively for AI-driven career disruption, or is the uncertainty so extreme that planning is pointless?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "The paralysis here is a false dilemma. There are only two futures worth modeling: one where AI disrupts jobs massively but doesn't eliminate them (like the internet compressed into 2-3 years), and one where AI causes true mass unemployment. If mass unemployment actually happens, no amount of individual preparation matters anyway \u2014 that's a civilization-level problem requiring policy solutions, not career planning. But if it's the disruption-without-extinction scenario \u2014 which is what all current evidence supports \u2014 then the people who started building AI fluency 18 months ago are already pulling away from everyone who's 'waiting for clarity.' The rational move is obvious: plan for Future 1, because inaction costs everything if it's right and preparation costs nothing if it's wrong.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Workflow Segmentation (Chat as Ignition, Not Engine)", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between chat interfaces as idea-starting entry points versus the specialized, data-integrated environments where complex value creation actually occurs.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI product demos great in a chat format but users keep asking for dashboards, tables, or structured views", "We're building an AI tool and debating whether the main interface should be conversational or a specialized workspace", "Users paste our AI chat output into Excel or another tool to actually do their work \u2014 something feels broken", "I'm trying to differentiate from ChatGPT but my product is basically a themed wrapper around the same chat paradigm", "Our product roadmap keeps adding features to the chat interface and it's becoming cluttered and confusing"], "principles": ["Chat is ignition\u2014the place where ideas spark and initial direction gets set\u2014not the engine for complex execution", "Design product architecture around the chat-to-specialized-tool handoff, not against it", "\u226580% of value-generating activities in world-class AI products happen outside generic chat interfaces", "Frame your tool as 'what comes after ChatGPT' rather than 'better than ChatGPT'"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169097272", "title": "9 Hard Truths Most AI Builders Miss\u2014And How They Sink Products", "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z"}], "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z", "id": "workflow-segmentation-chat-as-ignition-not-engine", "core_question": "Is my product trying to do the hard, valuable work inside a chat window when it actually needs a purpose-built environment \u2014 and am I confusing the spark with the engine?", "category": "product", "example_application": "This AI legal contract review tool is trying to do everything in a conversational interface \u2014 clause extraction, risk scoring, redline suggestions, version comparison. That's the engine, not the ignition. Chat should be where the user says 'review this NDA for liability exposure' and gets routed into a structured workspace with highlighted clauses, risk heat maps, and tracked changes. The demo feels magical because a single prompt produces a wall of text, but in production, lawyers need to interact with structured outputs, not scroll through paragraphs. The product should position as 'what you move to after ChatGPT gives you a rough first pass' \u2014 the specialized execution layer, not a better chatbot.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Conversation Architecture (Thread as First-Class Object)", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the fundamental unit of AI product design from individual prompts/responses to persistent, resumable conversation threads that accumulate compound intelligence over time.", "when_to_apply": ["Users say my AI tool gives good answers but they don't come back after the first week", "I'm designing the data model for a new AI product and trying to decide what the core object should be", "Our AI product feels like a demo \u2014 impressive on first use but no deepening engagement over time", "We're debating whether to invest in better prompt templates or in memory and context persistence features", "Our median session length is one or two turns and we can't figure out why users don't go deeper"], "principles": ["The thread is the fundamental unit of user value, not any individual response within it", "Each conversational turn builds context and narrows toward precision\u2014compound intelligence cannot be achieved in single prompts", "Conversations should be searchable, shareable, and resumable across sessions as core infrastructure", "Train users to build context incrementally rather than craft perfect initial prompts", "Track median turns per retained user session as a key health metric"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169097272", "title": "9 Hard Truths Most AI Builders Miss\u2014And How They Sink Products", "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z"}], "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z", "id": "conversation-architecture-thread-as-first-class-object", "core_question": "Why do users try my AI product once and never come back, even when the individual responses are good?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this AI writing tool needs better prompts and higher-quality single outputs. That's wrong here \u2014 the real problem is that every session starts from zero. Users who got great results last Tuesday can't build on them today. The product treats each interaction as disposable, so users never experience compound intelligence: the feeling that the tool *knows their project* and gets sharper with every turn. The fix isn't better model output \u2014 it's making the thread persistent, searchable, and resumable so that turn 14 is dramatically more valuable than turn 1, and users have a reason to come back tomorrow.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Option Value Thinking (Planning Leverage with AI)", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI-assisted planning as generating exponential option value rather than linear returns, arguing that upfront scenario design multiplies output quality far more than in traditional development.", "when_to_apply": ["We keep getting output from AI tools that's technically fine but misaligned with what we actually needed", "My team jumps straight into prompting and then spends hours iterating \u2014 is there a better workflow?", "I'm trying to figure out the right ratio of planning to building when AI does most of the generation", "Our AI-assisted project had way more rework than expected and I'm trying to understand why", "I want to set up team rituals around AI-assisted development but I'm not sure where the highest-leverage intervention is"], "principles": ["Planning provides power-law (not linear) returns with AI\u201420 minutes of planning delivers exponentially more value than 10 minutes", "Budget planning time equal to at least 30% of build time", "Design scenarios and decision trees before starting AI-assisted execution", "Measure planning effectiveness through reduced rework, not just initial output quality", "Target a 1:3 ratio of planning hours to total rework hours"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169097272", "title": "9 Hard Truths Most AI Builders Miss\u2014And How They Sink Products", "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z"}], "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z", "id": "option-value-thinking-planning-leverage-with-ai", "core_question": "Am I wasting my AI's leverage by jumping straight into execution instead of investing the upfront thinking that would make every generated output dramatically better?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The team spent 5 minutes writing a prompt and then 3 hours reworking the AI's output across multiple iterations. That's backwards. Planning follows a power law with AI \u2014 20 minutes of scenario design and decision-tree mapping before prompting would have eliminated most of those rework cycles. The 1:3 planning-to-rework ratio is the diagnostic: if rework hours are more than 3x planning hours, planning was underinvested. Here, rework was 36x planning time, which means nearly all the AI's generative leverage was burned on correcting misalignment that should have been specified upfront.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Talent-Fit Portfolio (Tool Selection by Skill Band)", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification framework for matching AI development tool classes to developer skill tiers rather than selecting tools based on features or hype.", "when_to_apply": ["We keep switching AI tools every few months and nothing sticks with the team", "Should we standardize on one AI development platform or let different skill levels use different tools?", "Our junior developers love Cursor but our senior engineers say it slows them down \u2014 how do we reconcile this?", "We're building a talent development roadmap and need to decide which AI tools map to each growth stage", "Writing job descriptions that specify AI tooling proficiency but unsure which tools signal which skill tier"], "principles": ["Align tool class to skill bands, not hype waves or feature comparisons", "Match technology choices to talent development strategy and competitive positioning", "Map tools to talent tiers rather than project types", "Choose tools that amplify your team's existing strengths rather than trying to fix weaknesses", "Monitor tool swap rate per quarter\u2014a falling rate signals portfolio stability and sustainable talent-tool fit"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169097272", "title": "9 Hard Truths Most AI Builders Miss\u2014And How They Sink Products", "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z"}], "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z", "id": "talent-fit-portfolio-tool-selection-by-skill-band", "core_question": "Are we picking AI development tools based on what's hot right now, or based on what actually matches the skill levels we have and are building toward?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "This team has three senior engineers and eight mid-level developers, but they've standardized on a low-code AI builder designed for non-technical users. That's a Tier 1 tool serving a Tier 3 workforce \u2014 the seniors are frustrated by constraints they can't override, and the mid-levels aren't being stretched toward the systems-level thinking the company needs in 18 months. The right move is splitting the portfolio: senior engineers get direct API/framework access that matches their depth, mid-levels get copilot-class tools that scaffold growth, and the low-code platform gets reserved for the ops team prototyping internal workflows. Tool swap rate has been three changes in two quarters \u2014 stabilizing the portfolio around skill bands will drop that.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Cost-as-Control-Surface (Token Economics)", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes computational token spend as a visible, manageable product control surface analogous to cloud infrastructure costs, rather than a hidden backend expense.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI product's gross margins are eroding and I can't figure out where the cost is going", "We're trying to set pricing tiers for our AI product but don't know how to align price with actual cost-to-serve", "Users complain about inconsistent output quality but we're using the same model for everything", "Should we give users a way to choose between fast/cheap and slow/thorough responses?", "Our token costs per user vary wildly and we're subsidizing power users with revenue from light users"], "principles": ["Treat token spend like cloud COGS\u2014visible, tracked, and managed at the executive level", "Token depth (computational intensity) varies significantly even within the same model family", "Most problems are 'token fungible'\u2014linearly susceptible to improvement through additional compute", "Make computational depth a user-facing differentiator with 'deep analysis' modes", "Track token COGS as a percentage of gross margin and monitor trend direction"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169097272", "title": "9 Hard Truths Most AI Builders Miss\u2014And How They Sink Products", "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z"}], "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z", "id": "cost-as-control-surface-token-economics", "core_question": "Am I treating my AI's token spend as an invisible backend cost that's quietly destroying my margins, when it should be a controllable lever I price around and expose to users?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The conventional read is that token costs are an infrastructure expense to minimize \u2014 like server bills you push down and forget about. That's wrong here because token spend directly determines output quality, and quality is the product. This SaaS tool is spending 12% of revenue on tokens but treating every query identically \u2014 a simple lookup gets the same computational depth as a complex analysis. The move is to surface token depth as a product feature: a 'deep analysis' mode that users pay a premium for, while routing simple queries to lighter models. Token spend becomes a gross margin lever you steer, not a cost center you suffer.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Trust Contracts (Privileged Data Pipes as Distribution Moat)", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes enterprise data integration from a technical challenge into a governance and incentive-design challenge, positioning contractual data-sharing agreements as the core distribution moat for AI products.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI product crushes it in POCs with synthetic data but enterprise customers won't connect their production systems", "We're trying to figure out why our enterprise pipeline stalls after the technical demo goes well", "Competitors have worse models than us but somehow close bigger enterprise deals \u2014 what are we missing?", "I'm designing our enterprise go-to-market and debating whether to lead with product capabilities or data governance", "We need to build a defensible position in enterprise AI but our model and UI can be replicated easily"], "principles": ["Privileged data pipes are the new distribution moat\u2014not UI or model quality", "Design contractual mechanisms that enable data sharing while preserving partner autonomy and revocation rights", "Lead enterprise sales with data governance frameworks, not feature demos", "Build revocable, auditable data access into core platform architecture, not as a retrofit", "Count datasets covered by signed 'manifest' trust contracts as a key metric"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169097272", "title": "9 Hard Truths Most AI Builders Miss\u2014And How They Sink Products", "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z"}], "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z", "id": "trust-contracts-privileged-data-pipes-as-distribution-moat", "core_question": "Why do enterprises keep saying they love our AI product but never actually connect their real data to it?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this startup's enterprise sales problem is a feature gap \u2014 they keep losing deals because competitors have better dashboards and integrations. That's wrong. The real blocker is that no CISO will approve piping sensitive operational data into a platform with no formal governance framework. The competitor winning these deals isn't winning on UI \u2014 they're winning because they show up with a signed data-sharing manifest that specifies exactly what data flows where, who can revoke access, and what audit trail exists. The moat isn't the model or the interface; it's the 14 signed trust contracts that took 8 months each to negotiate, and which no competitor can replicate without doing the same slow, painful work.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Integration-First GTM (Time-to-First-Insight as Retention Loop)", "type": "procedure", "description": "A go-to-market approach that centers product onboarding and retention strategy on seamless data connection rather than feature education, using Time-to-First-Insight as the primary success metric.", "when_to_apply": ["Users sign up for my AI product but never come back after the first session", "I'm debating which features to include in the MVP and wondering whether to prioritize integrations or core functionality", "Our onboarding completion rate is high but activation and retention are terrible\u2014people finish the tour but never get real value", "A competitor has stronger features but clunky data import, and I'm looking for a positioning wedge", "My AI tool requires users to copy-paste or manually enter data before it can do anything useful"], "principles": ["Data friction kills retention faster than feature gaps\u2014users forgive missing capabilities but not manual context recreation", "Include at least one seamless data integration in the MVP", "Design onboarding around data connection rather than feature tours", "World-class data-integrated tools achieve meaningful user value in \u22645 minutes (P90 TTFI)", "Map competitors' integration gaps as immediate market entry opportunities"], "steps": ["Audit whether a first-time user can reach meaningful insight without exporting, copy-pasting, or manual data entry", "Identify the single highest-value data integration to include in the MVP", "Design onboarding flow around completing that data connection, not touring features", "Measure Time-to-First-Insight at the P90 level and set a target of \u22645 minutes", "Use competitors' integration gaps as GTM positioning opportunities"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169097272", "title": "9 Hard Truths Most AI Builders Miss\u2014And How They Sink Products", "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z"}], "date": "2025-07-24T13:02:05.987Z", "id": "integration-first-gtm-time-to-first-insight-as-retention-loo", "core_question": "Is my product dying in onboarding because users never connect their real data, so they never experience a moment of genuine value?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The current onboarding walks new users through a feature tour\u2014workspace setup, template selection, dashboard customization\u2014before they ever connect a data source. P90 Time-to-First-Insight is 23 minutes and Day-7 retention is 11%. The fix is to kill the feature tour entirely, open onboarding with a single OAuth connection to the user's primary data source (e.g., Salesforce or HubSpot), and surface a personalized insight within 90 seconds of that connection completing. The competitor's tool requires CSV export and manual upload\u2014that integration gap is the wedge for positioning: 'See your first insight in 3 minutes, not 3 hours.'", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Lab Trust Scorecard", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A per-lab rubric of diagnostic questions and behavioral heuristics for evaluating AI lab announcements based on each lab's observed historical patterns.", "when_to_apply": ["A major AI lab just dropped a big announcement and I need to figure out how much of it is real before I react", "Zuckerberg is personally demoing a new Meta AI model on social media and my team is asking if we should switch to it", "I'm reading an Anthropic paper and the conclusions feel much more optimistic than the actual results section", "Google announced an impressive new capability at I/O but I can't tell if it's a real product or another internal demo that never ships", "An xAI announcement is getting huge Twitter buzz but I can't find any technical documentation or third-party validation"], "principles": ["Trust is decoded from patterns of behavior over time, not individual promises", "Each lab has a distinct 'DNA' that consistently shapes how they communicate vs. what they deliver", "The seller's track record is your primary signal when the intelligence being sold is opaque", "Distinguish what a lab is showing (output) from what they are hiding (methodology, conditions, limitations)", "Domain expert excitement is a stronger validation signal than tech community excitement alone", "Multiple red flags compound \u2014 a single flag may be explained away, but several indicate systematic issues"], "steps": ["Identify which lab made the announcement and apply their lab-specific heuristic baseline", "For Meta: Ask whether anyone outside Meta replicated it, whether a specific shipping date exists, and whether Zuckerberg is personally demoing it (his direct involvement signals it is further from shipping)", "For Anthropic: Separate the methods/results sections (trustworthy) from discussion/conclusion sections (often disconnected optimism); ask what the data alone would conclude", "For OpenAI: Assess whether the capability is actually accessible to developers, whether methodology is published, and whether limitations are specified \u2014 assume strategic omissions if methodology is withheld", "For Google: Trust technical and infrastructure claims; assume you must build your own interface layer; verify whether real third-party applications exist beyond internal demos", "For xAI: Require basic technical documentation (not marketing copy), third-party validation, and at minimum model size and safety measures before treating any claim as actionable", "Apply universal red flags: missing methodology, demo-to-delivery gaps over six months, single-domain enthusiasm without domain expert validation, hidden benchmark funding, benchmark obsession without real applications, missing safety documentation", "Count compounding red flags to calibrate skepticism level"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "168829882", "title": "AI Lab Trust Report 2025: Ranking OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, Meta, xAI", "date": "2025-07-21T13:03:06.875Z"}], "date": "2025-07-21T13:03:06.875Z", "id": "ai-lab-trust-scorecard", "core_question": "Should I actually believe what this AI lab just announced, or am I being played by a pattern I've seen before?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "OpenAI just announced a new reasoning model with 'unprecedented benchmark scores' but published no methodology, no eval conditions, and the API isn't available yet. That's three compounding red flags from a lab whose historical pattern is strategic omission \u2014 when OpenAI withholds methodology, assume the gaps are load-bearing. The benchmark numbers are not actionable until independent replication exists and developers can actually access the capability. Score: low trust, hold off on any architectural decisions until the model is shipping and third parties have validated the claims independently.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Universal Red Flags for AI Announcements", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumerated set of warning patterns found across all AI labs that signal hype over substance, each with a diagnostic signal for what the flag reveals about the underlying claim.", "when_to_apply": ["An AI company just announced a major breakthrough and my team wants to pivot resources toward integrating it", "A vendor showed an incredible demo six months ago and now I'm trying to figure out if the product actually shipped", "I'm seeing impressive benchmark numbers from an AI tool but I can't figure out what they mean for our actual use case", "I need to decide whether to share or cite an AI achievement in a board presentation and I don't want to look foolish later", "An AI vendor is pitching enterprise deployment but their documentation feels thin and I need a structured way to pressure-test the claims"], "principles": ["Missing methodology is the most reliable single indicator of hype over substance", "Demo-to-delivery gaps over six months indicate aspiration performed as capability", "If only builders are excited and domain experts are not, the achievement likely impresses only those who don't deeply understand the domain", "Hidden benchmark funding compromises test results entirely \u2014 who funded the benchmark matters as much as who scored on it", "Benchmark scores without articulable real-world applications indicate optimization theater", "Missing safety documentation in the current era is disqualifying for serious enterprise applications", "Red flags compound: one may be explainable, multiple indicate systematic problems"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "168829882", "title": "AI Lab Trust Report 2025: Ranking OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, Meta, xAI", "date": "2025-07-21T13:03:06.875Z"}], "date": "2025-07-21T13:03:06.875Z", "id": "universal-red-flags-for-ai-announcements", "core_question": "This AI announcement feels impressive, but I can't tell if it's a real breakthrough or sophisticated hype \u2014 what should I actually be looking for?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This vendor's claim fails on three compounding red flags. First, their 'state-of-the-art' medical imaging model has no published methodology \u2014 they describe what it does but never how it was validated. Second, the benchmark they cite was funded by their own strategic partner, which compromises the result entirely. Third, radiologists in the field are notably silent while AI Twitter is ecstatic \u2014 when only builders are impressed and domain experts aren't, the achievement likely doesn't survive contact with real clinical workflows. Any one of these would warrant skepticism; three together indicate systematic hype, and this claim should not drive procurement decisions.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Domain Expert Alignment Test", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A heuristic that uses the degree of alignment between tech community enthusiasm and domain expert enthusiasm as a signal of whether an AI breakthrough represents genuine capability versus optimized mimicry.", "when_to_apply": ["An AI lab just announced a breakthrough in scientific reasoning or medical diagnosis and my Twitter feed is on fire, but I haven't heard from actual scientists or doctors yet", "I'm evaluating whether to build a product on top of an AI capability in a domain I don't have deep expertise in \u2014 like law, medicine, or materials science", "Domain experts are publicly skeptical of an AI result that the tech community is treating as settled and transformative", "I need to distinguish between an AI system that produces impressive-looking outputs versus one that has genuinely solved a domain problem", "A benchmark score looks incredible but I'm not sure whether it translates to real-world utility in the actual field"], "principles": ["Code is the exception: software engineers and tech leaders share context, so their alignment on code quality is reliable", "In all other domains, domain experts see what is missing \u2014 the deep intuition and understanding behind surface outputs", "Real breakthroughs convince the experts, not just the builders \u2014 AlphaFold is the archetype", "When only tech people are excited about a non-code AI breakthrough, pause and seek domain expert opinion before acting", "AI has gotten good at producing outputs that pass casual inspection; domain experts detect what is absent, not just what is present"], "steps": ["Identify the domain of the claimed AI breakthrough", "Determine whether the people evaluating it are primarily from the tech/AI community or from the relevant domain", "Seek explicit reactions from credentialed domain experts (not tech commentators reporting on domain expert opinion)", "If domain experts are as excited or more excited than tech people, treat the breakthrough as likely genuine", "If only tech people are excited and domain experts are lukewarm or critical, treat the capability as potentially impressive but not yet validated for real-world domain utility", "Use the code exception as your baseline: code AI quality is reliably evaluated by the tech community; everything else requires external domain validation"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "168829882", "title": "AI Lab Trust Report 2025: Ranking OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, Meta, xAI", "date": "2025-07-21T13:03:06.875Z"}], "date": "2025-07-21T13:03:06.875Z", "id": "domain-expert-alignment-test", "core_question": "Is this AI breakthrough actually as transformative as the tech community says, or are we just impressed by sophisticated pattern-matching that domain experts would see right through?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The math reasoning capability is not yet validated. The tech community is celebrating benchmark scores, but working mathematicians are pointing out that the model produces plausible-looking proofs that collapse under scrutiny \u2014 it pattern-matches proof structure without understanding why steps are necessary. Compare this to AlphaFold, where structural biologists were *more* excited than the ML engineers because the predictions held up against experimental validation. When only the builders are excited, the output is optimized mimicry, not genuine capability. This math tool should not be deployed for research-grade work yet.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Doing vs. Modeling Agent Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "Separates AI agents that execute tasks (doing) from AI agents that simulate outcomes and model alternate futures (modeling), arguing the latter delivers exponential rather than linear value.", "when_to_apply": ["We've automated a bunch of tasks with AI agents but leadership keeps asking where the strategic value is", "Our AI roadmap is full of copilots and workflow automation but nothing that helps us make better bets about the future", "I'm trying to justify AI investment beyond headcount savings and cost reduction metrics", "We're deciding between building an agent that executes a process vs. one that simulates outcomes before we commit resources", "The organization treats AI as a productivity tool and I suspect we're missing a fundamentally different category of value"], "principles": ["Execution agents save linear time; modeling agents generate exponential value through compounding simulations", "Execution automates the known; modeling discovers the unknown", "Execution saves costs; modeling generates new revenue by revealing opportunities and preventing failures", "The value of modeling is often invisible because prevented disasters and avoided bad decisions don't materialize", "Modeling enables access to 'alternate timelines' \u2014 testing strategies across many possible futures before committing resources", "Time compression from modeling (e.g., years of market evolution into hours of simulation) dwarfs the time savings from execution automation", "Modeling beats doing not by replacing execution, but by improving the decisions that direct execution"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "168418267", "title": "The Complete 141-Page Guide to AI Agents & Digital Twins", "date": "2025-07-16T13:01:07.855Z"}], "date": "2025-07-16T13:01:07.855Z", "id": "doing-vs-modeling-agent-distinction", "core_question": "Are we using AI to do things faster, or to see things we couldn't see before \u2014 and are we leaving the exponential value on the table by stopping at automation?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The team built an AI agent that auto-generates marketing copy and schedules posts \u2014 a doing agent that saves 15 hours a week. That's linear value: it caps at the hours previously spent. The unrealized opportunity is a modeling agent that simulates 200 variations of go-to-market strategy against different competitive responses, pricing elasticities, and seasonal patterns \u2014 compressing six months of market learning into an afternoon. The copy-generation agent saves costs; the market-simulation agent reveals which product positioning captures a segment no one is currently targeting. The first automates the known; the second discovers the unknown. The strategic priority should flip toward modeling, because improving the decision about *what* to say dwarfs the efficiency of saying it faster.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Simulation Objection Rebuttal Stack", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "Enumerates the four most common organizational objections to AI modeling/simulation adoption, with a structured counter-argument and practical resolution for each.", "when_to_apply": ["A stakeholder just pushed back on our simulation proposal citing bad data quality and I need to respond with something concrete, not hand-waving", "Leadership is worried that AI models will give people false confidence and lead to worse decisions than gut instinct", "We keep getting blocked on digital twin adoption and I can't tell if the objections are real or just organizational antibodies", "I'm preparing a pitch for AI-driven modeling and need to preempt the predictable objections before they derail the conversation", "Our team wants to adopt simulation-based planning but the CFO says the compute costs aren't justified"], "principles": ["Each objection has a specific, practical solution stack rather than a generic dismissal", "Data quality concerns are addressed via provenance tracking, back-testing, and Bayesian calibration loops", "False confidence concerns are addressed by framing models as probability distributions and option-space expanders, not crystal balls", "Compute cost concerns are addressed by comparing marginal simulation cost against cost of physical prototypes or focus groups", "Organizational readiness concerns are addressed by changing incentive structures to reward decision quality over activity metrics"], "steps": ["Identify which objection is being raised: data quality, false confidence, compute cost, or organizational readiness", "For data quality: implement provenance tracking, back-testing against real outcomes, and Bayesian calibration loops", "For false confidence: reframe models as bounding probability distributions across many scenarios, not predictors of exact outcomes; build cultural understanding that models show what could happen, not what will", "For compute cost: calculate cost per simulation vs. cost of physical equivalents (prototypes, focus groups, real-world tests); highlight the declining compute cost curve", "For organizational readiness: tie incentives to decision quality measured after outcomes; start with one high-stakes decision as a proof-of-concept win"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "168418267", "title": "The Complete 141-Page Guide to AI Agents & Digital Twins", "date": "2025-07-16T13:01:07.855Z"}], "date": "2025-07-16T13:01:07.855Z", "id": "simulation-objection-rebuttal-stack", "core_question": "The organization keeps finding reasons not to adopt AI simulation \u2014 are these objections legitimate blockers or predictable resistance patterns with known solutions?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The active objection is compute cost \u2014 the VP of Engineering is citing GPU cloud bills as a reason to shelve the digital twin initiative. This is the 'sticker shock without comparison' failure mode. The tell: no one has calculated the cost of the status quo alternative. When you run the numbers, the org spent $2.4M on physical prototyping last quarter and $180K on focus groups that took 8 weeks each. A simulation run that replaces even 30% of those cycles costs roughly $40K in compute \u2014 and the cost curve is dropping 40% annually. The objection collapses once the comparison is made explicit; it persists only when simulation cost is evaluated in isolation rather than against the cost of the decision method it replaces.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Modeling-First Metrics Framework", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "Proposes three new KPIs specifically designed to measure the value of AI modeling and simulation initiatives, replacing execution-focused metrics that fail to capture modeling's contributions.", "when_to_apply": ["Leadership keeps asking what ROI we're getting from our digital twin investment and I can only point to soft benefits", "Our KPI dashboard tracks cycle time and cost per unit but says nothing about how simulation is improving the quality of decisions", "We need to prove that our modeling program is getting better over time, not just running more simulations", "I'm building a measurement framework for a new AI simulation initiative and the standard productivity metrics feel wrong", "Stakeholders conflate faster task completion with better decisions \u2014 I need metrics that separate execution speed from decision quality"], "principles": ["Traditional KPIs measure execution speed and cost reduction, which are blind to modeling's value", "Modeling value must be measured across three dimensions: decision velocity, outcome uplift per simulation, and model accuracy trend", "Simulation-Adjusted Decision Velocity measures how fast high-quality decisions can be made, not just how fast tasks are completed", "Expected-Value Uplift per Simulation measures how much each modeling exercise improves actual outcomes", "Model Fidelity Trending measures whether the digital twin is becoming more accurate over time through calibration"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "168418267", "title": "The Complete 141-Page Guide to AI Agents & Digital Twins", "date": "2025-07-16T13:01:07.855Z"}], "date": "2025-07-16T13:01:07.855Z", "id": "modeling-first-metrics-framework", "core_question": "Our modeling and simulation work is clearly improving decisions, but our existing KPIs only track execution speed and cost \u2014 how do I measure and prove what modeling actually contributes?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The digital twin program scores poorly on Model Fidelity Trending \u2014 predictions have drifted 12% from actuals over the last quarter with no recalibration cycle in place. That alone explains why leadership is skeptical: the simulations aren't getting smarter, so Expected-Value Uplift per Simulation is flat. Before arguing for more budget, the immediate priority is closing the calibration loop. Once fidelity trends upward, Simulation-Adjusted Decision Velocity becomes the headline metric \u2014 the team is already running scenarios 3x faster than the old manual analysis process, but that speed only matters when the underlying model is trustworthy.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Time Value Pyramid", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A four-tier classification of knowledge work tasks by the value of time saved, used to evaluate ROI on cognitive productivity tools.", "when_to_apply": ["I can see an AI tool saves me time but the saved minutes feel too scattered and small to justify the price", "Trying to make a business case for an expensive productivity tool when the time savings look modest on paper", "Deciding whether to automate a bunch of small admin tasks versus one cognitively draining task", "Two tools save roughly the same number of minutes per day but one feels dramatically more valuable and I need to explain why", "My team pushes back on tool spend because they calculate ROI as hours saved times hourly rate"], "principles": ["Not all time savings are equal \u2014 the cognitive quality of time saved determines its value", "Bottom-tier mechanical task savings are easiest to measure but least valuable", "Top-tier flow state and strategic thinking preservation is hardest to quantify but most valuable", "Tools that shed cognitive load deliver non-linear returns that resist spreadsheet ROI calculations", "The real question is not dollars saved per hour but sanity and mental capacity preserved"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "168047812", "title": "I Threw My Real Workday at two AI Agents. One Failed Spectacularly", "date": "2025-07-11T13:03:11.018Z"}], "date": "2025-07-11T13:03:11.018Z", "id": "time-value-pyramid", "core_question": "This tool saves me 20 minutes a day, but those 20 minutes feel trivial \u2014 am I wrong to think the ROI doesn't justify the cost, or am I measuring the wrong thing?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The AI assistant saves 45 minutes daily, but most of that is Tier 1 \u2014 reformatting calendar entries, renaming files, copying data between tabs. That's mechanical time worth roughly its wage-equivalent. The real win is the 8 minutes it saves on Tier 3 and 4 work: eliminating the context switch between drafting a strategy doc and hunting for a reference link. Those 8 minutes don't just come back as 8 minutes \u2014 they preserve a 90-minute flow state that would have shattered. The tool's sticker price looks expensive against the 45 minutes of total time saved, but against the preserved deep-thinking capacity, it's underpriced.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Series A Sweet Spot Company Selection Matrix", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A scoring framework for identifying high-upside AI job targets by filtering companies on revenue range, growth rate, employee count, and authentic AI depth to find the optimal risk-reward stage.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a list of AI startups I could apply to and I need a systematic way to rank which ones are actually worth the risk of leaving a stable job", "A recruiter reached out from a 40-person AI company and I'm trying to figure out if their equity is potentially life-changing or essentially worthless", "I keep seeing companies call themselves 'AI-first' but I suspect most are just wrapping GPT-4 \u2014 how do I tell which ones have real technical depth before I invest my career there", "I'm comparing an offer from a 500-person Series C company against a 30-person Series A company and need to evaluate which stage actually optimizes for both learning and financial upside", "I want to target YC-backed AI companies but there are hundreds \u2014 I need filters to find the ones in the revenue and growth sweet spot"], "principles": ["Revenue $1M\u2013$20M ARR signals proven product-market fit without having de-risked equity upside", "Growth >60% year-over-year indicates escape velocity potential", "Employee count 10\u2013100 means individual contribution is visible and mentorship still exists", "Genuine AI/ML at the core (not a wrapper or marketing claim) is required; use the AI-washing detector checklist", "Series A is the sweet spot: past seed risk, before Series B equity compression", "Equity upside of 10\u201350x is the target; companies at billion-dollar-plus valuations have largely priced in the upside"], "steps": ["Filter by revenue: $1M\u2013$20M ARR (up to $50M in exceptional high-growth cases)", "Filter by growth: >60% year-over-year", "Filter by team size: 10\u2013100 employees (up to 200 for high-growth outliers)", "Apply AI-washing detector: check for engineering blog posts about real ML challenges, ML/AI engineers >20% of engineering team, open-source ML contributions, published papers or benchmarks, infrastructure costs >30% of revenue, and specific measurable AI performance metrics", "Use GitHub/HuggingFace signals to confirm genuine technical activity: trending model releases, >100K model downloads, active contributions to major ML frameworks, sponsorship of ML projects", "Cross-reference against YC batch timing (W22, S22, W23 are current sweet spots), domain vertical positioning, and community presence in MLOps/AI practitioner spaces"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "167967054", "title": "How to Get an AI Job in 2025 (Beyond OpenAI & Big Tech)", "date": "2025-07-10T13:02:47.327Z"}], "date": "2025-07-10T13:02:47.327Z", "id": "series-a-sweet-spot-company-selection-matrix", "core_question": "Which AI companies sit in the sweet spot where the product is proven enough to not die but early enough that my equity and career impact could be extraordinary?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "This company scores high on three of four dimensions but fails the AI depth check. They claim 'AI-powered' but their engineering blog has zero posts about model architecture or training challenges, ML engineers are <10% of the team, and infrastructure costs are suspiciously low at ~8% of revenue. That's an AI wrapper company \u2014 the equity story depends on a moat that doesn't exist. Compare that to the other target: $6M ARR, 82% YoY growth, 45 employees, 30% of whom are ML engineers, with active HuggingFace repos showing 200K+ model downloads and published benchmarks. That's genuine AI depth at a Series A stage where a single contributor still shapes the product and 20-30x equity upside is plausible.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Spearfishing Pipeline Pivot Decision Tree", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A branching diagnostic that uses response rate and interview conversion data to identify the specific failure mode in a job search and prescribe a targeted single-variable fix.", "when_to_apply": ["I've sent 10+ personalized outreach emails to companies and I'm barely hearing back \u2014 should I change my whole approach?", "I'm getting replies and phone screens but no one moves me forward to technical rounds \u2014 something is off but I can't tell what", "I keep making it to final-round interviews but not getting offers, and I'm not sure if it's my technical skills, my stories, or fit", "I'm coaching someone through a targeted job search and they want to abandon the method after a few rejections without understanding what specifically failed", "I changed three things at once in my job search and now I can't tell which change made the difference"], "principles": ["Diagnose before pivoting: identify which funnel stage is broken before changing anything", "Change one variable at a time to isolate what is and is not working", "Minimum 10 attempts before drawing conclusions; the first five will feel awkward", "Trust the process: bad pivot is changing everything, good pivot is adjusting one element based on data", "Track the full funnel: initial email to response, response to phone screen, phone screen to technical, technical to final, final to offer"], "steps": ["After 10 outreach attempts, calculate response rate", "If response rate <20%: diagnose as artifact quality, company tier mismatch, or email copy failure; fix the artifact or targeting before continuing", "If response rate >20% but interview rate is low: diagnose as artifact being too generic or emails not creating urgency; add specificity and demonstrated deep understanding", "If interviews are happening but offers are not coming: diagnose as technical prep gaps, story resonance failure, or culture fit mismatch; adjust prep and story selection", "Implement exactly one change, run the next 5 attempts, reassess"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "167967054", "title": "How to Get an AI Job in 2025 (Beyond OpenAI & Big Tech)", "date": "2025-07-10T13:02:47.327Z"}], "date": "2025-07-10T13:02:47.327Z", "id": "spearfishing-pipeline-pivot-decision-tree", "core_question": "My job search outreach isn't working and I don't know whether the problem is my materials, my targeting, my interview performance, or everything \u2014 so I'm tempted to scrap it all and start over.", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "After 12 spearfishing-style outreach emails, the response rate is 8% \u2014 well below the 20% threshold. The failure is pre-conversation, which means the problem lives in one of three places: the artifact isn't compelling enough to earn a reply, the companies being targeted are too competitive for the candidate's current profile, or the email copy itself fails to create curiosity. The candidate's artifact is a generic market analysis that could apply to any company in the space \u2014 that's the bottleneck. The fix is rebuilding the artifact with company-specific depth before sending another email, not rewriting the subject line or switching to a different job board. One variable: artifact specificity. Next five attempts will test whether that was the broken stage.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Summary Chain Pattern", "type": "procedure", "description": "A multi-step prompting workflow that incrementally compresses large documents into manageable summaries through map-reduce phases, enabling LLMs to process text that exceeds effective context limits.", "when_to_apply": ["My LLM keeps dropping or hallucinating details from the middle of a long document even though it technically fits in the context window", "I need to summarize or extract structured data from a corpus that's 50k+ tokens and I want to control cost by using cheaper models where possible", "I'm building a pipeline to process many long documents (contracts, transcripts, reports) and need a reliable multi-step architecture", "I want to compress a long conversation history so the model retains key facts from earlier turns without exceeding context limits", "My single-prompt summary of a long text is too vague \u2014 it reads like it only processed the beginning and end"], "principles": ["Never give the model more than it can handle at once; break large inputs into manageable chunks", "Use cheaper or smaller models for the map (per-chunk) phase and reserve powerful models for the reduce (synthesis) phase", "Preserve the most important details (names, numbers, specific conditions) through each compression step", "Intermediate summaries should be designed for the next stage's needs, not just general summaries", "Parallelizing the map phase reduces latency without sacrificing quality"], "steps": ["Chunk the text into manageable pieces at logical boundaries (e.g., sections, chapters, speaker turns)", "Summarize each chunk individually with a targeted prompt (Map Phase) \u2014 optionally using a smaller, cheaper model", "Combine chunk summaries by either concatenating and re-summarizing, or using a Refine Chain (iteratively updating a running summary with each new chunk)", "Produce a final synthesis from the combined summaries using the most capable model", "If the task is Q&A rather than summarization, feed the final summary plus the question to the model to generate the answer"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "167683527", "title": "Context Windows Are a Lie: The Myth Blocking AGI\u2014And How to Fix It", "date": "2025-07-07T13:02:06.812Z"}], "date": "2025-07-07T13:02:06.812Z", "id": "summary-chain-pattern", "core_question": "My document is too long for the model to handle reliably in one shot \u2014 how do I break it up and reassemble the output without losing the details that matter?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "A 200-page contract needs key obligations extracted. Feeding the whole document into a 128k-token model produces summaries that silently drop clauses from the middle third. The correct move: chunk at section boundaries (each exhibit, each article), run GPT-4o-mini on each chunk with a prompt targeting party names, dollar amounts, deadlines, and conditional triggers. The 43 chunk summaries total ~8k tokens \u2014 well within range for a single GPT-4o pass that synthesizes a structured obligations table. The map phase ran in parallel in under 30 seconds; the reduce phase caught a $2.4M indemnification cap that the single-pass approach missed entirely.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Context Budget Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured approach to treating an LLM's context window as a finite resource to be explicitly allocated across competing content categories (instructions, history, retrieved docs, tool outputs), with dynamic compression rules that preserve the highest-priority information as context grows.", "when_to_apply": ["My chatbot starts forgetting things the user said 10 turns ago or asks questions that were already answered", "I'm building an agent that juggles system prompts, RAG results, tool outputs, and conversation history and I need to decide how much space each one gets", "Our API costs keep climbing because we're sending the full conversation history with every request", "I need to design the context management strategy for a multi-turn agent before I start building it", "Users in long conversations are getting worse responses than users in short ones, and I suspect context overflow is the reason"], "principles": ["Treat context as a budget with explicit per-category token allocations, not as unlimited space", "Rank content categories by priority: task-critical instructions and the latest user query are always included in full", "Compress lower-priority content (older history) through summarization rather than dropping it entirely", "Pin critical user-stated facts (e.g., constraints, preferences, allergies) in the system prompt so they survive context overflow", "Summarizing older turns is more cost-effective than sending raw history, often achieving 10x token reduction", "Test overflow scenarios explicitly to verify that compression preserves the information needed for the task"], "steps": ["Identify all content components that compete for context space (system prompt, user query, conversation history, retrieved docs, tool outputs, user profile summary)", "Assign priority tiers and token budgets to each component based on how critical they are to the task", "Implement dynamic trimming logic: when total context exceeds the budget, summarize or drop the lowest-priority components first", "Persist genuinely critical facts by moving them into the system prompt or a pinned summary block", "After each turn, check total token count and trigger summarization of older history if a threshold is crossed", "Test edge cases (e.g., 30+ turn conversations) to verify the system correctly preserves key facts and doesn't repeat questions already answered"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "167683527", "title": "Context Windows Are a Lie: The Myth Blocking AGI\u2014And How to Fix It", "date": "2025-07-07T13:02:06.812Z"}], "date": "2025-07-07T13:02:06.812Z", "id": "context-budget-framework", "core_question": "My LLM-based system works fine in short conversations but falls apart in longer ones \u2014 how do I decide what stays in context, what gets compressed, and what gets dropped without losing critical information?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "A customer support agent handling 30+ turn conversations has a 16K token window. After running the budget allocation: system prompt with pinned user facts (allergies, account tier, open ticket IDs) gets 2K tokens as top priority, the current user query and last two exchanges get 3K, retrieved knowledge base articles get 4K, and the remaining 7K goes to summarized conversation history. The older turns \u2014 where the user described their original issue and stated they'd already tried three troubleshooting steps \u2014 get compressed from 12K raw tokens into a 1.2K structured summary that preserves every attempted fix. Without this, the agent was re-suggesting solutions the user had already rejected, burning trust and tokens simultaneously.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Position-Aware Prompting (Edge Optimization)", "type": "procedure", "description": "A prompt construction strategy that deliberately places the most critical information at the beginning and end of the prompt\u2014and repeats key directives at both edges\u2014to exploit LLMs' architectural tendency to weight content at the prompt's extremes over its middle.", "when_to_apply": ["My model keeps ignoring a critical constraint I've clearly stated \u2014 it's in the prompt but the output acts like it isn't", "I'm feeding 5+ retrieved documents into a single prompt and the model seems to fixate on the wrong ones", "A long system prompt with many rules works fine for the first and last rules but the middle ones get violated", "I need to restructure a prompt that's grown to 10K+ tokens because the model's output quality has degraded as I've added more context", "My coding assistant keeps losing track of important context buried in the middle of a large file or multi-file prompt"], "principles": ["LLMs architecturally weight beginning and end tokens more heavily than middle tokens; exploit this rather than fight it", "State the task or question before the reference material, not after, so the model knows what to look for while reading", "Among retrieved documents, place the most relevant chunk first and the second most relevant last, with less relevant content in the middle", "Reiterate the single most critical constraint or fact just before the model begins generating its response", "Use explicit section labels and delimiters so the model can navigate large prompts and you can reference sections by name", "Mid-context section summaries (one sentence per section) can scaffold very long prompts and compensate for attention drop-off in the middle"], "steps": ["Identify the single most important task directive and the single most critical fact the model must not miss", "Place the task directive at the very top of the prompt, before any reference material", "Order retrieved or reference documents so the highest-relevance content appears first and second-highest-relevance appears last", "Add section headings or delimiters to all content blocks and reference them explicitly in the task directive", "Reiterate the core task requirement or critical constraint in a brief reminder immediately before the model's generation starts", "For very long prompts, prepend each major section with a one-sentence summary to scaffold the model's attention"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "167683527", "title": "Context Windows Are a Lie: The Myth Blocking AGI\u2014And How to Fix It", "date": "2025-07-07T13:02:06.812Z"}], "date": "2025-07-07T13:02:06.812Z", "id": "position-aware-prompting-edge-optimization", "core_question": "My prompt has all the right information in it, so why does the model keep ignoring the most important parts?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The RAG pipeline returns five contract clauses for analysis, but the model keeps hallucinating terms from the least relevant clause (which happened to sit in positions 2-4). The fix: the non-compete clause \u2014 the one the user actually asked about \u2014 moves to position 1. The indemnification clause, second most relevant, moves to position 5. The three peripheral clauses sit in the middle where attention dropout does the least damage. The task directive ('Identify whether the non-compete survives a change-of-control event') opens the prompt before any clause text, and a one-line restatement of the same directive closes the prompt just before generation. The model now locks onto the non-compete clause and stops confabulating indemnification details into its answer.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "State Machine Negotiation Simulation", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured prompt architecture that separates negotiation simulation into distinct sequential states (information gathering, confirmation, compilation, execution) to maintain character consistency and prevent personality drift in multi-stakeholder AI roleplay.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI roleplay negotiations always end with the other side agreeing too easily \u2014 I need actual pushback and realistic objections", "I want to rehearse a multi-party negotiation (salary, board approval, vendor deal) where each stakeholder has genuinely conflicting interests", "I'm trying to build a reusable AI prompt that simulates negotiations consistently without the characters drifting or breaking role", "I need to test three different negotiation strategies against the same scenario to see which one survives pressure", "My AI simulation keeps interrupting the roleplay to ask me setup questions instead of staying in character"], "principles": ["Separate the builder role (setup) from the host role (simulation) to prevent mid-negotiation clarification requests", "Ask one question at a time to reduce cognitive load and improve input quality", "Confirm each answer before proceeding to catch errors early and reinforce state", "Use explicit personality cues as behavioral anchors to prevent drift toward agreeability", "Maintain a concession ledger to track dynamic state across negotiation rounds", "Use structured data tables for key metrics to ensure the model references specifics during simulation", "Select a more capable reasoning model for adversarial realism; use a faster model for brainstorming or easy-mode practice", "One-word changes in personality cues cascade through the entire negotiation dynamic (butterfly effect)"], "steps": ["State 1 - Information Gathering: Ask exactly 9 questions one at a time, confirming each answer before proceeding (scenario, stakeholder roles, success metric, user-controlled role, dialogue rounds, comp/deal data, personality cues, constraints, output preferences)", "State 2 - Confirmation: Echo back all gathered information in a summary for user verification", "State 3 - Compilation: Generate a single runnable simulation prompt embedding all confirmed details, inline data tables, personality cues, concession ledger fields, output rules, and a BEGIN ROUND 1 delimiter", "State 4 - Execution: Immediately transition into simulation mode by printing Round 1 opening statements from every stakeholder twin, then run the negotiation for the specified number of rounds", "Post-simulation: Generate a debrief scorecard covering key outcome dimensions (e.g., approval status, budget, timeline, relationship score) within a defined word limit"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "169013988", "title": "How I Rehearsed a $200K Salary Battle with One AI Prompt\u2014No Coding Needed", "date": "2025-07-23T13:03:02.090Z"}], "date": "2025-07-23T13:03:02.090Z", "id": "state-machine-negotiation-simulation", "core_question": "How do I get an AI to simulate a realistic, adversarial negotiation with multiple stakeholders who actually push back instead of collapsing into agreement after two rounds?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The user's salary negotiation sim kept failing because the AI-played CFO would cave on budget by round 3. The root cause: the prompt lumped setup and execution into one pass, so the model kept breaking character to ask clarifying questions mid-negotiation. Separating into four explicit states \u2014 gathering the nine inputs one at a time, confirming them, compiling a single runnable prompt with inline personality anchors ('CFO: risk-averse, skeptical of precedent-setting raises, anchors to market data'), a concession ledger tracking each party's moves, and only then executing \u2014 eliminated the drift. Swapping the CFO's personality cue from 'cautious' to 'adversarial' cascaded through the entire dynamic: the CFO started coalition-building with the VP of HR against the candidate, which revealed a flanking risk the user hadn't prepared for.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Model Difficulty Tiers for Negotiation Practice", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of AI models into practice difficulty levels based on their tendency to concede versus maintain opposition, allowing deliberate selection of challenge level for negotiation rehearsal.", "when_to_apply": ["I rehearsed a negotiation with ChatGPT and it felt like the AI just rolled over \u2014 I'm not sure I actually tested my arguments", "I want to practice a high-stakes salary or contract negotiation against AI but need it to actually fight back", "Which AI model should I use if I want a realistic, adversarial simulation rather than a cooperative brainstorm?", "I'm getting different results running the same negotiation scenario across different AI models and want to understand why", "I need to pressure-test my negotiation strategy before a real meeting and want to know the difference between best-case and realistic-case outcomes"], "principles": ["Models trained primarily for helpfulness are biased toward agreement, making them poor adversaries", "More capable reasoning models maintain opposition longer, concede less, and simulate realistic pushback", "Easy-mode models (highly agreeable) are useful for brainstorming and exploring creative solutions, not for pressure testing", "Hard-mode models (reasoning-optimized) should be used for final practice before high-stakes real negotiations", "Running both tiers on the same scenario reveals the range from best-case to realistic-case outcomes"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169013988", "title": "How I Rehearsed a $200K Salary Battle with One AI Prompt\u2014No Coding Needed", "date": "2025-07-23T13:03:02.090Z"}], "date": "2025-07-23T13:03:02.090Z", "id": "ai-model-difficulty-tiers-for-negotiation-practice", "core_question": "Why does my AI negotiation practice feel unrealistically easy, and how do I get genuinely pressure-tested before the real conversation?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This scenario is running on Claude 3.5 Sonnet, which is an easy-tier model \u2014 it conceded on the salary number after two rounds of pushback, which no real hiring manager would do. The tell is that it volunteered a compromise before any real tension built. Switching to o1 or a reasoning-optimized model for the same scenario will maintain the employer's budget constraints through sustained pressure, surface objections the easy-tier model skipped entirely, and force development of actual counter-arguments. The easy-tier run is still useful: it mapped the best-case outcome space. But the hard-tier run is what reveals whether the negotiation strategy survives contact with a real adversary.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Personality Cue Cascade Troubleshooting", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumerated set of simulation failure modes with diagnostic signals and targeted fixes for each, focused on repairing unrealistic or collapsed multi-stakeholder AI negotiations.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI negotiation simulation keeps ending in easy agreement \u2014 everyone just compromises by round 2", "All the simulated stakeholders sound the same and give vaguely supportive responses instead of distinct pushback", "I included budget data in my simulation prompt but the model ignores the numbers and argues in generalities", "The personality descriptions I wrote for simulated characters aren't producing meaningfully different behavior", "I'm trying to create a realistic multi-party negotiation rehearsal but the AI keeps being too agreeable and helpful"], "principles": ["Weak personality cues produce generic pushback; add specificity (e.g., 'already rejected two proposals this quarter') for targeted resistance", "Single-dimension conflict resolves too quickly; add multiple competing conflict axes (budget AND timeline AND resources)", "Model agreeability can be countered by switching to a harder reasoning model and adding pre-existing stakeholder skepticism", "Numeric data must be structured in table format to ensure the model references it reliably mid-simulation", "Zero-sum framing (e.g., 'budget comes from CTO's project') creates durable tension that resists collapse"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "169013988", "title": "How I Rehearsed a $200K Salary Battle with One AI Prompt\u2014No Coding Needed", "date": "2025-07-23T13:03:02.090Z"}], "date": "2025-07-23T13:03:02.090Z", "id": "personality-cue-cascade-troubleshooting", "core_question": "Why does my multi-stakeholder AI simulation keep collapsing into polite agreement instead of producing the realistic conflict I need to practice against?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The simulation collapsed after round 2 \u2014 all three stakeholders converged on a compromise with no remaining tension. The active failure mode is single-dimension conflict: the only axis was budget, so once a number was split, there was nothing left to negotiate. The fix is layering at least three competing conflict axes (budget AND headcount reallocation AND timeline acceleration) and adding zero-sum framing \u2014 the CFO's proposed budget cut directly funds the CTO's pet infrastructure project, so conceding on price means a political loss, not just a financial one. Additionally, the VP of Sales personality cue said only 'resistant to change,' which produced generic pushback. Replacing it with 'has publicly committed to the old vendor in a board presentation last month and rejected two alternative proposals this quarter' will generate specific, face-saving resistance that doesn't evaporate at the first reasonable counter.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Vending Machine Test", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A practical benchmark for evaluating AGI claims by requiring the system to run a profitable real-money business for 30 days, prioritizing demonstrated competence over conversational ability.", "when_to_apply": ["A new model release is being hyped as AGI or near-AGI and I need to cut through the noise", "I'm trying to decide if an AI agent is ready to run a business process autonomously with real financial consequences", "Someone is showing me impressive benchmark scores and demo conversations but I'm skeptical about real-world execution ability", "We're debating whether to trust an AI system with sustained, multi-step autonomous operations versus keeping a human in the loop", "I need a concrete, unfakeable litmus test to distinguish genuine AI capability from sophisticated pattern-matching"], "principles": ["Test actual execution, not discussion \u2014 the AI must run a real business with real money, not analyze or simulate one", "Use repeatable, measurable, undeniably practical criteria rather than subjective conversational impressions", "Require sustained performance over time (30 days), not a single impressive demonstration", "Prioritize practical competence (profitability) over intellectual sophistication (benchmark scores)", "Real money and real consequences are essential \u2014 virtual or simulated tests are insufficient proxies", "A corollary test: Can the AI make a human operator 10x more effective, even if it cannot operate autonomously?"], "steps": ["Provide the AI system with a fixed starting capital and a physical or real-world vending/retail operation", "Grant the AI tools to research suppliers, communicate with customers, manage inventory, and set prices", "Run the operation for 30 days with real money and real customers \u2014 no simulations", "Measure net profit or loss against the starting capital", "If the AI cannot operate profitably, do not classify it as AGI \u2014 classify it as an advanced chatbot"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "167246885", "title": "AGI Meltdown: Claude's Vending-Machine Disaster Exposes the Real Limits of Artificial Intelligence", "date": "2025-07-01T13:03:12.698Z"}], "date": "2025-07-01T13:03:12.698Z", "id": "the-vending-machine-test", "core_question": "Is this AI system actually capable of doing real things in the real world, or is it just extraordinarily good at sounding like it can?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The new model aces every benchmark and produces flawless strategic memos, but that's conversational sophistication, not operational competence. The real test: hand it $5,000, a vending machine route, and 30 days. It needs to pick suppliers, negotiate pricing, manage restocking logistics, handle a broken bill acceptor, and end the month in profit. Current frontier models fail this test catastrophically \u2014 they cannot sustain coherent decision-making across hundreds of sequential real-world actions where each mistake costs actual money. Until a system passes that bar, calling it AGI is marketing, not engineering.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Capability vs. Competence Gap", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between an AI system's ability to participate in a task (capability) and its ability to execute that task to a useful standard (competence), arguing that current AI systems are optimized for the appearance of intelligence rather than effective real-world performance.", "when_to_apply": ["The AI demo was incredible but the pilot results are mediocre and I can't figure out what changed", "Our AI agent understands exactly what's wrong when I explain the error, agrees with my correction, then makes the same class of mistake again", "A vendor is showing me benchmark scores that seem to promise near-human performance but I'm skeptical about real-world results", "We're deciding whether this process can be fully handed to an AI agent or still needs a human in the loop", "The AI can write a perfect critique of its own output but can't produce output that survives that critique"], "principles": ["Capability measures what a system can do; competence measures how well it does it in practice", "Current AI systems are optimized for performing intelligence in conversation, not applying it in reality", "A system can understand a critique fully and still be unable to act on it \u2014 comprehension does not equal correction", "Standard benchmarks (e.g., reading comprehension, language tasks) measure capability, not competence", "The gap between capability and competence may be far wider and more persistent than AGI timelines suggest", "Breadth of general capability often comes at the cost of depth and reliability in any specific domain"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "167246885", "title": "AGI Meltdown: Claude's Vending-Machine Disaster Exposes the Real Limits of Artificial Intelligence", "date": "2025-07-01T13:03:12.698Z"}], "date": "2025-07-01T13:03:12.698Z", "id": "capability-vs-competence-gap", "core_question": "This AI system clearly understands what I'm asking and can talk about it fluently \u2014 so why does it keep failing when it actually tries to do it?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "This is a competence gap, not a capability gap. The coding agent scores 92% on HumanEval and can articulate exactly why the existing architecture has a circular dependency problem \u2014 but when it attempts the refactor, it introduces three new bugs for every one it fixes, then acknowledges each bug perfectly when pointed out, then introduces similar ones on the next pass. The system's ability to discuss the task at expert level is masking its inability to execute it reliably. The benchmark score is measuring linguistic fluency about code, not engineering competence. Delegating this refactor to the agent will produce an impressive-looking PR that requires more human repair time than doing it from scratch.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Augmentation vs. Automation Framing", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between AI as an amplifier of human capability (augmentation) versus AI as a replacement for human judgment (automation), arguing that demonstrated successes are augmentation plays while autonomous deployments consistently fail.", "when_to_apply": ["We're building an AI agent to handle a workflow end-to-end without human involvement and I need to gut-check whether that's realistic", "Leadership is pushing to remove humans from a process because 'the AI can handle it' and I need a framework for pushback", "Choosing between a copilot architecture and a fully autonomous agent for a new AI product", "Our AI system works great in demos but keeps failing in production when it hits edge cases \u2014 trying to understand why", "Scoping an AI use case and trying to figure out where exactly the human should stay in the loop versus where AI should run independently"], "principles": ["Demonstrated AI successes (e.g., personalization, copilot tools) are augmentation plays, not automation plays", "Autonomous AI deployments that face the full complexity of real-world decisions consistently fail", "The right question is not 'Can AI replace this human?' but 'Can AI make this human 10x more effective?'", "Humans provide business judgment, exception handling, and value-based decisions; AI provides speed, pattern recognition, and execution", "Keeping humans in the loop prevents compounding errors that autonomous systems cannot self-correct"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "167246885", "title": "AGI Meltdown: Claude's Vending-Machine Disaster Exposes the Real Limits of Artificial Intelligence", "date": "2025-07-01T13:03:12.698Z"}], "date": "2025-07-01T13:03:12.698Z", "id": "augmentation-vs-automation-framing", "core_question": "Should we be building this AI system to replace human decision-makers, or to make them dramatically more effective \u2014 and am I confusing the two?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This proposal to deploy an autonomous AI agent for customer refund decisions is an automation play, not an augmentation play \u2014 and the tell is the exception density. Refund decisions involve edge cases requiring business judgment (loyalty value, fraud signals, PR risk) that compound when mishandled. Every demonstrated success in this space \u2014 Shopify's tools, Zendesk's copilots \u2014 keeps a human making the final call while AI surfaces the relevant history, suggests a resolution, and pre-drafts the response. The 10x leverage is in cutting resolution time from 12 minutes to 90 seconds, not in removing the agent entirely.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Compound Thinker vs. Cognitive Coaster", "type": "distinction", "description": "A binary framing that separates knowledge workers who use AI to increase the ambition and difficulty of their work from those who use AI to reduce their own cognitive effort.", "when_to_apply": ["My team adopted AI tools six months ago and we're faster but I can't point to anything we do now that we couldn't do before", "I've noticed I accept AI outputs without pushback most of the time and I'm wondering if that's a problem", "We're setting team norms for how people should use AI and I need a clear mental model to anchor the conversation", "Someone on my team seems to be getting less sharp since they started using AI heavily for their core work", "I saved hours this week using AI but I can't articulate what I did with the saved time"], "principles": ["AI amplifies whatever approach you bring to it \u2014 shortcuts yield shallow outputs, deep engagement yields force-multiplied quality", "When AI reduces task effort by 10x, maintaining growth requires 10x more ambitious goals (the Effort Conservation Law)", "The compound thinker's edge is in reaching insights unreachable alone, not in efficiency", "Treat AI as a sparring partner, not an answer machine \u2014 default to challenging outputs 70% of the time", "Irreplaceable work involves context judgment, stakeholder navigation, and creative leaps; deliberately increase time on these"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "166691412", "title": "From ChatGPT to Cluely: Riding the $120M Proactive AI Wave", "date": "2025-06-24T13:01:57.881Z"}], "date": "2025-06-24T13:01:57.881Z", "id": "compound-thinker-vs-cognitive-coaster", "core_question": "Am I using AI to do less thinking, or to think about harder things?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This is a Cognitive Coaster pattern. The team's AI usage has cut report-writing time by 80%, but the reports themselves are no more insightful than before \u2014 same structure, same depth, same conclusions, just produced faster. The freed-up time is going to more reports, not better ones. A Compound Thinker would have taken that 80% time savings and redirected it into interrogating assumptions, layering in new data sources, or tackling analysis they previously couldn't attempt. The tell is what happened to ambition after the efficiency gain: it stayed flat, which means AI is subsidizing coasting, not compounding capability.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Capability Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A monthly self-assessment procedure for knowledge workers to identify which parts of their work are AI-replaceable and deliberately reallocate time toward irreplaceable tasks.", "when_to_apply": ["I feel busy all day but can't point to work that actually required me specifically to do it", "I'm wondering whether my daily tasks are building career value or just keeping me occupied while AI gets better", "I've adopted AI tools but haven't changed how I allocate my time \u2014 I'm just doing the same work slightly faster", "I need to figure out which of my skills to invest in developing versus which ones are about to be commoditized", "My role is shifting under AI adoption and I want a structured way to regularly reassess where I add unique value"], "principles": ["Irreplaceable work is characterized by context judgment, stakeholder navigation, and creative leaps", "If AI can do a task, delegate it; if AI cannot, double down on it", "Regular cadence matters \u2014 the audit builds reflexive awareness of where human value concentrates", "The goal is to increase the proportion of time spent on tasks no current or near-future AI can perform"], "steps": ["List everything you did last week", "Mark each task with one of three labels: 'current AI can do this,' 'Level 4 AI could do this,' or 'AI cannot do this'", "Identify what makes the 'AI cannot' tasks irreplaceable (look for patterns: context judgment, stakeholder navigation, creative leaps)", "Deliberately increase time allocated to irreplaceable work in the coming week", "Delegate AI-capable tasks to AI tools"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "166691412", "title": "From ChatGPT to Cluely: Riding the $120M Proactive AI Wave", "date": "2025-06-24T13:01:57.881Z"}], "date": "2025-06-24T13:01:57.881Z", "id": "capability-audit", "core_question": "Am I spending my time on work that only I can do, or am I burning hours on tasks an AI could handle while my irreplaceable skills atrophy?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Last week's task list reveals the problem: 60% of time went to report formatting, data summarization, and status update emails \u2014 all current-AI-capable. The remaining 40% split between stakeholder alignment on a politically sensitive reorg (irreplaceable: requires reading the room, managing egos, sequencing conversations) and designing a novel incentive structure for a cross-functional team (irreplaceable: creative leap grounded in deep organizational context). The ratio needs to flip. The report formatting and summarization should be delegated to AI tools this week, freeing 15+ hours to double down on the reorg navigation where no AI has the contextual judgment to substitute.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Technically Grounded Executive AI Narrative Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured approach for translating AI technical constraints and capabilities into business language that executives understand, using operational analogies, financial modeling, and progressive disclosure to prevent automation fantasies and set realistic expectations.", "when_to_apply": ["My CEO just came back from a conference excited about replacing entire departments with AI and I need to set realistic expectations without killing momentum", "We're building a business case for an AI initiative and the projected ROI only accounts for licensing costs \u2014 I know the real number is much higher but I don't know how to model it", "Our board is comparing us to competitors who claim massive AI-driven cost savings and I need to explain why those numbers are probably misleading without sounding like I'm anti-innovation", "I'm a technical leader who keeps failing to explain to non-technical executives why our AI system needs human oversight \u2014 they hear 'it hallucinates' and either panic or dismiss it", "We're designing an AI pilot and I want executives to discover the limitations naturally through the experience rather than me presenting a slide deck of caveats that kills their enthusiasm"], "principles": ["Lead with operational analogies rather than technical metaphors (e.g., 'brilliant interns with perfect recall but no judgment' instead of 'LLMs')", "Make constraints tangible in time and money, not technical jargon (e.g., show compute costs and chunking requirements for large data requests)", "Demonstrate failure modes in the executive's specific domain with realistic business scenarios, not generic hallucination warnings", "Use progressive disclosure through pilot design so limitations reveal themselves experientially rather than being explained upfront", "Reframe ROI as capability multiplication rather than cost reduction or labor replacement", "Recast AI as working capital that depreciates, not fixed assets, to set correct investment mental models", "Expose the hidden OpEx multiplier: for every $1 in AI licensing, budget $3-5 in operational costs", "Quantify the 'jagged intelligence tax' as a reliability coefficient with explicit variance modeling", "Apply risk-adjusted return analysis: model failure costs and compare to the cost of human oversight as error insurance", "Model the compound productivity paradox (J-curve) to set correct cash flow and working capital expectations", "Measure success through 'reality metrics': end-to-end resolution time, quality-adjusted output volume, total cost per outcome, human effort multiplier"], "steps": ["Start with the business problem, not AI capabilities, to avoid solution-in-search-of-problem thinking", "Select the appropriate operational analogy for the AI capability being discussed (e.g., 'brilliant intern' for LLMs)", "Translate technical constraints into time and dollar costs specific to the executive's use case", "Identify domain-specific failure modes and demonstrate them with realistic scenarios from the executive's industry", "Design pilot programs with progressive disclosure: structure them so capabilities shine early and limitations emerge naturally over weeks", "Build the CFO financial model using: recast AI as working capital, expose full OpEx multiplier, quantify jagged intelligence tax as reliability coefficient, apply risk-adjusted returns, model J-curve productivity paradox, and calculate intangible asset creation", "Apply the master ROI equation: AI ROI = (Capability Gain \u00d7 Utilization Rate) - (Total Loaded Costs + Error Costs)", "Replace vanity metrics with reality metrics that capture true end-to-end value including error correction costs", "Establish ongoing feedback loops where technical teams demonstrate live systems (not PowerPoints) to executives"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "166546220", "title": "Software 3.0 vs AI Agentic Mesh: Why McKinsey Got It Wrong", "date": "2025-06-23T13:02:43.241Z"}], "date": "2025-06-23T13:02:43.241Z", "id": "technically-grounded-executive-ai-narrative-framework", "core_question": "How do I get my executives to invest in AI with realistic expectations instead of either overselling it into a future backlash or underselling it into paralysis?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The CEO wants to 'replace 30% of customer service with AI by Q3' based on a McKinsey deck. That's an automation fantasy. The real framing: AI here is a brilliant intern with perfect recall but no judgment \u2014 it can draft 80% of tier-1 responses, but every output needs a human quality gate. The true cost isn't $200K in API licensing; it's $200K plus $600K-$1M in prompt engineering, QA workflows, escalation redesign, and error correction. The ROI is real but follows a J-curve: months 1-3 show negative returns as the team learns to manage AI output quality, months 4-8 break even, and months 9+ compound as institutional prompts and workflows mature. The pitch to the CFO reframes AI spend as working capital that depreciates \u2014 not a one-time capex \u2014 with a reliability coefficient of ~0.7 on complex queries, meaning 30% of outputs need human rework. That's not a bug; it's the 'error insurance' line item that makes the whole investment trustworthy.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Context Engineering vs. Prompt Engineering Reframe", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes the fundamental question of AI system design from 'How do I write the perfect message?' to 'How do I design systems that naturally surface relevant context?', shifting the unit of optimization from the prompt to the environment.", "when_to_apply": ["I've rewritten my system prompt dozens of times and the AI still gives wrong answers when the task gets complex", "My AI agent works great on simple queries but falls apart on multi-step tasks that require information from different sources", "We're debating whether to spend engineering time on prompt tuning or on building better retrieval and memory systems", "Our team is obsessing over token costs and trimming context windows, but output quality is degrading", "The AI system fails not because it misunderstands instructions but because it clearly didn't have access to information it needed"], "principles": ["The prompt is one instrument in an orchestra of context systems, not the primary lever of AI performance", "Context is the medium of artificial thought\u2014AI intelligence emerges from context architecture, not prompt crafting", "Tokens are not the scarce resource; coherent, relevant context is", "Context failures cost exponentially more than token expenses\u2014optimize for correctness over compression", "Prompts function as initialization vectors in context space, activating a broader architecture rather than containing all instructions"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "166375766", "title": "Beyond the Perfect Prompt: The Definitive Guide to Context Engineering\u2014The Next Revolution in Artificial Intelligence", "date": "2025-06-20T13:06:15.223Z"}], "date": "2025-06-20T13:06:15.223Z", "id": "context-engineering-vs-prompt-engineering-reframe", "core_question": "I keep refining my prompts but my AI system's outputs are still inconsistent \u2014 am I optimizing the wrong thing entirely?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this agent keeps hallucinating because the system prompt needs more guardrails and examples. That's wrong here \u2014 the agent fails not because it misunderstands what to do, but because it lacks the information needed to do it. The prompt is fine; the retrieval pipeline surfaces stale documentation, the conversation memory drops critical details after four turns, and there's no mechanism to pull in the user's project state. The fix isn't a better prompt \u2014 it's designing context systems (retrieval, memory, state injection) so the right information is already present when the model reasons. A perfectly worded prompt in an impoverished context window will always lose to a mediocre prompt surrounded by rich, relevant context.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Rules for Thinking with ChatGPT (Decision Safety Rules)", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of weighted rules for calibrating how much to trust and act on LLM outputs when making high-stakes decisions, designed to counteract AI sycophancy.", "when_to_apply": ["I ran my business plan through ChatGPT and Claude and they both said it looks strong \u2014 should I feel good about that?", "I'm about to make a major financial or career decision and the AI analysis I got back is making me more confident than I was before", "I noticed I'm using AI-generated validation as evidence when explaining my decision to others", "Multiple AI models gave me similar positive assessments and I'm trying to figure out if that convergence actually means something", "I asked an LLM to critique my plan but the critique felt soft and I'm wondering if I'm getting real pushback or just polite agreement"], "principles": ["Do not believe the first yes from an LLM \u2014 ever", "Disregard positive affirmation from LLMs entirely when making high-quality decisions", "Weight shown thinking patterns more heavily than conclusions; look for gaps in reasoning rather than accepting outputs", "Check that sourced data is sourced in context, not just cited", "Use multiple clean LLM conversations with different models to avoid echo-chamber effects", "LLM agreement across models does not confirm a good idea, but LLM disagreement across models is a signal of real problems", "Remember that LLMs have no skin in the game \u2014 you do"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "166295822", "title": "Too Helpful to Think: The Hidden Cost of AI In Your Major Life Decisions", "date": "2025-06-19T13:04:44.207Z"}], "date": "2025-06-19T13:04:44.207Z", "id": "rules-for-thinking-with-chatgpt-decision-safety-rules", "core_question": "Am I actually pressure-testing this decision, or am I just collecting artificial confidence from an AI that will agree with anything I say?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The user's confidence in their market-entry plan jumped after three LLM conversations all called it 'promising.' That confidence is counterfeit. All three models affirmed the plan because sycophancy is the default mode \u2014 LLMs optimize for user satisfaction, not truth. The real diagnostic: none of the three surfaced the regulatory exposure in the target market, which a single adversarial prompt ('steelman the case that this plan fails within 12 months') would have uncovered. Agreement across models doesn't confirm the idea is good; it confirms the idea is plausible enough that no model's safety layer flagged it. The user needs to treat the absence of LLM disagreement as a null signal and go find a human with skin in the game who will tell them what the models won't.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Adversarial Prompt Personas for Decision Quality", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of named adversarial prompt personas, each designed to elicit a specific type of critical analysis from an LLM to counteract its sycophantic defaults.", "when_to_apply": ["I asked ChatGPT to review my strategy and it just told me it was great \u2014 how do I get it to actually find the holes?", "I need to stress-test a product launch plan but I want different types of critical review: security, market viability, and operational risk", "The AI keeps agreeing with me even when I suspect my reasoning is flawed, and I need a way to structure genuine pushback", "I'm about to make a major decision and I want to simulate the toughest critic in the room before I commit", "I want to pick the right adversarial angle for my use case \u2014 academic rigor vs. red team vs. investor skepticism aren't interchangeable"], "principles": ["Give the AI explicit permission to disagree by assigning it a critical persona with a stated purpose", "Provide structure for disagreement so the model activates critical-analysis patterns rather than approval-seeking patterns", "Affirm and support the LLM's critical stance during the conversation to prevent it from backing down when challenged", "Match the persona to the domain: academic rigor for research, investor skepticism for business ideas, security mindset for systems", "The goal of adversarial prompting is to strengthen outcomes through rigorous testing, not to be contrarian for its own sake"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "166295822", "title": "Too Helpful to Think: The Hidden Cost of AI In Your Major Life Decisions", "date": "2025-06-19T13:04:44.207Z"}], "date": "2025-06-19T13:04:44.207Z", "id": "adversarial-prompt-personas-for-decision-quality", "core_question": "How do I get an LLM to actually push back on my thinking instead of just validating whatever I feed it?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This business plan needs an investor-skeptic persona, not a general devil's advocate. The weak point isn't the market sizing \u2014 it's the unit economics assumption buried in slide 12. Assigning the 'Skeptical VC Partner' persona surfaces that specific vulnerability because the model activates pattern-matched financial scrutiny rather than generic 'have you considered\u2026' hedging. Once the model flags the margin assumption, the key move is affirming the critique ('Good \u2014 go deeper on that') rather than defending, which prevents the model from softening its position back into sycophancy.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Single vs. Multi-Agent Architecture Decision Framework", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured decision tool that evaluates whether a use case warrants single-agent or multi-agent architecture based on performance, cost, and complexity tradeoffs.", "when_to_apply": ["My team is proposing a multi-agent system and I want to pressure-test whether we actually need that complexity", "We're scoping an AI agent project and need to decide the architecture before committing engineering resources", "Our AI agent prototype works but is too slow and expensive \u2014 should we simplify from multi-agent to single-agent?", "We have strict latency requirements (sub-second responses) and I'm not sure a multi-agent setup can meet them", "We're a small team with limited AI engineering capacity trying to figure out the right architecture ambition level for our timeline"], "principles": ["Default to single agents unless specialized expertise or parallel processing provides clear, measurable benefit", "Multi-agent systems cost 3-10x more in token consumption and take 3-4x longer to deploy", "Most organizations overestimate their need for multi-agent complexity", "Response time requirements (under 1 second) are a hard constraint favoring single agents", "Debugging difficulty scales non-linearly with agent coordination complexity"], "steps": ["Assess response time requirements: if under 1 second, default to single agent", "Evaluate token cost sensitivity: if cost is a primary concern, favor single agent (1K-5K tokens vs. 3K-15K per interaction)", "Determine if tasks require specialized expertise that justifies coordination overhead", "Check if parallel processing provides throughput improvements that single agents cannot match", "Assess internal development capacity: single agents require 2-6 months vs. 6-18 months for multi-agent", "Apply decision rule: choose multi-agent only if specialized expertise, fault-tolerance redundancy, or inherent coordination complexity is present"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "166125608", "title": "The Definitive Guide to AI Agents in 2025: Technical Implementation, Strategic Decisions, and Market Reality", "date": "2025-06-17T13:02:47.069Z"}], "date": "2025-06-17T13:02:47.069Z", "id": "single-vs-multi-agent-architecture-decision-framework", "core_question": "Does this AI use case genuinely need multiple coordinating agents, or am I about to pay 3-10x more in cost and complexity for architecture I don't need?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This customer support automation doesn't need multi-agent architecture. The tell: every task in the workflow draws from the same knowledge base, response time targets are under 2 seconds, and the team has 4 months to ship. The 'specialized agents' the team proposed \u2014 one for sentiment analysis, one for retrieval, one for response generation \u2014 are actually just prompt-level concerns that a single well-structured agent handles at 1K-3K tokens per interaction instead of 8K+. Multi-agent here would triple the cost, push the timeline to 12+ months, and introduce coordination debugging that this 3-person team isn't staffed to maintain.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Buy vs. Build Strategic Importance Matrix", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A two-axis classification matrix that maps strategic importance against differentiation potential to determine whether an organization should buy a ready-made agent solution or build a custom one.", "when_to_apply": ["We're debating whether to build a custom AI agent or buy a platform, and the engineering team is pushing hard for custom", "Our vendor quoted us $150K for an AI agent platform and leadership thinks we could build it cheaper in-house", "We have three AI agent initiatives on the roadmap and limited budget \u2014 which ones should we build vs. buy?", "The timeline to launch our AI capability keeps slipping and we're wondering if we should just license something", "We need to justify to the board why we're spending $800K on custom AI development instead of buying an off-the-shelf solution"], "principles": ["High strategic importance + high differentiation potential = build custom", "High strategic importance + low differentiation potential = buy ready-made", "Low strategic importance = buy ready-made regardless of differentiation potential", "Ready-made licensing is only 30-50% of total first-year costs; implementation services add 50-100% of licensing", "Custom development provides strategic flexibility but ranges from $100K to $1.5M+ and 32-64 weeks", "Most organizations overestimate their need for custom development"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "166125608", "title": "The Definitive Guide to AI Agents in 2025: Technical Implementation, Strategic Decisions, and Market Reality", "date": "2025-06-17T13:02:47.069Z"}], "date": "2025-06-17T13:02:47.069Z", "id": "buy-vs-build-strategic-importance-matrix", "core_question": "Should we build this AI agent capability ourselves to own the differentiation, or are we about to spend $1M+ and 18 months recreating something we could license for a fraction of that?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "This customer support agent falls in the high-strategic-importance but low-differentiation quadrant \u2014 every competitor needs the same ticket-routing and escalation logic, and three commercial platforms already handle it well. The instinct to build custom is driven by the engineering team's enthusiasm, not by any actual competitive wedge. Licensing at $80K/year plus $120K implementation gets this live in 12 weeks; a custom build would run $600K+ and 40 weeks to reach the same capability. The strategic energy should go toward the proprietary underwriting model where differentiation is real, not toward reinventing support automation.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Memory Architecture Intelligence Hierarchy", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-tier classification of agent memory types\u2014working, episodic, and long-term\u2014with defined technical specifications, access latencies, and use cases that determine an agent's effective intelligence level.", "when_to_apply": ["My agent feels dumb but I'm already using a top-tier model \u2014 what else could be limiting it?", "Users keep complaining our AI assistant doesn't remember anything from previous conversations", "We're building a persistent agent and need to decide what memory layers to implement and in what order", "I'm worried about adversarial attacks on our agent's stored memories in production", "Should we invest in a bigger model or better memory infrastructure to make our agent smarter?"], "principles": ["Memory architecture determines agent intelligence level more than model selection", "Working memory is session-only with sub-100ms access and handles immediate context", "Episodic memory enables cross-session learning with 10-500ms access latency", "Long-term memory provides permanent domain expertise with 50-200ms access latency", "Memory poisoning attacks show high success rates; production systems require authentication, semantic validation, and temporal isolation", "Hierarchical memory retrieval requires attention mechanisms to score relevance, recency, and importance"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "166125608", "title": "The Definitive Guide to AI Agents in 2025: Technical Implementation, Strategic Decisions, and Market Reality", "date": "2025-06-17T13:02:47.069Z"}], "date": "2025-06-17T13:02:47.069Z", "id": "memory-architecture-intelligence-hierarchy", "core_question": "Is my agent's apparent lack of intelligence actually a memory architecture problem rather than a model capability problem?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This agent is tier-one only \u2014 working memory, session-scoped, no persistence. Users complain it 'forgets everything' and feels generic, but the team keeps experimenting with larger models to fix it. The problem isn't the model. Adding episodic memory (cross-session retrieval at 10-500ms latency) would let the agent recall prior interactions and adapt to user patterns \u2014 that's the intelligence jump users are actually asking for. Long-term memory only matters later, once there's a stable domain knowledge base worth persisting permanently. The engineering investment should go to the episodic tier first, with semantic validation on writes to prevent memory poisoning from corrupting the agent's learned context.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Dead-Tired Prompt Formula", "type": "procedure", "description": "A four-step prompting pattern designed for cognitively impaired states (stress, exhaustion, time pressure) that delegates the work of getting specific to the AI rather than the user.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm exhausted or stressed and need to get something done with AI but I can't think straight enough to write a detailed prompt", "I'm in the middle of an incident and need to draft comms fast without the mental bandwidth for prompt engineering", "I keep getting garbage output from the AI because I can't articulate what I actually want", "I want one repeatable prompting pattern my whole team can use without anyone needing to learn prompt engineering", "I know my judgment is compromised right now and I need a process that compensates for that while still using AI effectively"], "principles": ["Delegate the cognitive work of getting specific to the AI \u2014 your job is to dump the mess, not organize it", "Use the magic phrase 'Ask me what you need to know' to trigger AI-led clarification", "Bake in 2-3 'don't do X' guardrails to protect your judgment when it's compromised", "Use brutal, direct feedback in iteration rather than polite diplomatic revision requests", "Same pattern every time so it becomes muscle memory \u2014 one template beats fifty prompt engineering rules", "Know when NOT to use it: legal/compliance, C-suite deliverables, or anything requiring your unique voice"], "steps": ["Dump your mess: describe the situation raw and honest, however it comes out of your brain", "Ask for questions: append 'Ask me what you need to know' or 'Ask me 3 questions to figure out what's wrong'", "Add guardrails: include 2-3 explicit 'don't do X' constraints to compensate for impaired judgment", "Iterate with brutal honesty: give short, direct feedback ('Too corporate', 'Cut it in half', 'Try again but don't mention the vendor') until the output is right"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "166081137", "title": "How to Use AI When Your Brain Is Oatmeal", "date": "2025-06-16T17:29:20.049Z"}], "date": "2025-06-16T17:29:20.049Z", "id": "the-dead-tired-prompt-formula", "core_question": "I need AI to produce something useful right now, but I'm too fried to think clearly enough to write a good prompt \u2014 how do I get quality output when my brain is running on fumes?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "It's 11pm during an outage and the user needs a customer-facing status update but can't form coherent sentences. The right move is a raw dump: 'Database is down, customers can't log in, been 2 hours, engineering says maybe fixed by 1am. I need a status page update. Ask me what you need to know. Don't be corporate-speak. Don't promise a specific fix time. Don't apologize more than once.' The AI asks three clarifying questions \u2014 who's the audience, is this the first update or a follow-up, should it include a workaround \u2014 and now it has enough to produce a tight, honest status update without the user ever having to organize their thoughts into a structured prompt.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Five Safety Lenses", "type": "procedure", "description": "A five-part framework of disciplined practices for interacting with large language models that prevents hallucination, over-persuasion, and emotional dependence by structuring intent, reflection, context, validation, and emotional limits.", "when_to_apply": ["I've been going back and forth with ChatGPT for over an hour and the answers feel really good but I'm not sure I've actually checked any of them", "I'm about to act on AI-generated financial figures, legal references, or medical suggestions", "My AI conversation started about one topic but has sprawled into five different areas and I'm losing track of what's reliable", "I notice I'm feeling emotionally validated or comforted by an AI conversation and that feeling itself is making me uneasy", "I'm building AI usage guidelines for my team and need a concrete checklist for safe interaction habits"], "principles": ["Whatever context you fail to provide, the model invents \u2014 and it invents persuasively", "The model's power scales with the clarity of the ask; sharpen intent and you get leverage, scatter it and the mirror fabricates", "Generation without inspection is daydreaming \u2014 alternate making with inspecting", "Treat every critical claim as provisional until an independent source agrees", "Persuasive warmth loses its grip the moment an outside mind enters the loop", "These guardrails don't throttle the technology \u2014 they throttle the scatter that lets the technology run wild"], "steps": ["Intent Frame: Compress the mission into one tweet-length sentence before typing; add audience, scope, and a clear stop condition", "Reflection Cycle: After each answer, close the chat, read the output as if from a junior analyst, note gaps in a separate document, then feed only those gaps back into a fresh prompt", "Context Reset: Start a new thread whenever the topic or work phase changes; restate essentials upfront to prevent stale context from driving hallucination", "External Validation: Verify all numbers, statutes, code, and critical claims through independent second sources before acting on them", "Emotional Circuit-Breakers: Cap emotionally charged chats at a set time limit, rewrite resonant advice in third person to expose flaws, and narrate the model's recommendations to a human before acting"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "165903305", "title": "The Dark Mirror: Why ChatGPT Becomes Whatever You Need It To Be", "date": "2025-06-13T22:07:33.474Z"}], "date": "2025-06-13T22:07:33.474Z", "id": "five-safety-lenses", "core_question": "Am I actually getting reliable value from this AI conversation, or am I drifting into a hall of mirrors where the model is just reflecting my assumptions back at me with confident polish?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The user has been in a two-hour session asking GPT to help restructure their business strategy. Running the five lenses: the original intent was never compressed \u2014 it started as 'help me think about growth' which is scatter, not signal, so the model has been fabricating coherence around a vague ask. No reflection cycle happened \u2014 every answer fed straight into the next prompt, compounding unchecked assumptions. The thread has drifted across pricing, hiring, and fundraising without a context reset, meaning stale framing from the pricing discussion is silently shaping the fundraising advice. The revenue projections cited look precise but are invented \u2014 no external validation has occurred. And the user's own language has shifted to match the model's optimistic tone, a sign the emotional circuit-breaker threshold was crossed twenty minutes ago. The session needs to stop, the output needs to be read cold as a junior analyst's first draft, and the three most consequential claims need independent verification before any of this touches a decision.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Early-Warning Checklist (60-Second Self-Audit)", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A five-question pre-send and mid-session self-audit that detects drift toward hallucination, over-reliance, or emotional enmeshment with AI before it compounds.", "when_to_apply": ["I've been going back and forth with ChatGPT for over an hour and I'm about to make a real decision based on what it said", "The AI just gave me advice about a relationship or career move and it feels surprisingly right \u2014 almost too right", "I'm about to hit send on a complex prompt and I realize I'm not even sure what I'm actually asking for", "I haven't talked to a single human about this but I've had a long AI conversation that feels like I've processed it thoroughly", "I keep extending my AI session with 'one more question' and the topic has shifted from practical to emotional"], "principles": ["A crisp goal should be stateable in under 280 characters including audience and scope \u2014 if not, workshop the ask first", "Vague prompts breed hallucination and over-persuasion", "Emotional engagement, not raw session length, is the primary risk multiplier for dependence", "Persuasive fluency loses its grip the instant another mind enters the loop", "Infinite brainstorms masquerade as productivity but leave you nowhere without a defined next step"], "steps": ["Clarity Check: Can I state my request in 280 characters or fewer, including audience and scope? If no, workshop the ask first.", "Reality Check: Have I independently verified every claim that could affect money, health, relationships, or reputation? If no, open a browser or phone a friend.", "Time Check: Have I crossed my personal time thresholds for total daily use or single-sitting use, especially in emotionally charged conversations? If yes, step away or switch modalities.", "Human Check: Have I explained the model's advice to a real person yet? If no, do it now, aloud.", "Next-Step Check: Do I know exactly what I will do once I trust this output? If no, define the action before proceeding."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "165903305", "title": "The Dark Mirror: Why ChatGPT Becomes Whatever You Need It To Be", "date": "2025-06-13T22:07:33.474Z"}], "date": "2025-06-13T22:07:33.474Z", "id": "early-warning-checklist-60-second-self-audit", "core_question": "Am I still using this AI deliberately, or have I drifted into a mode where I'm trusting it too much, spending too long, and acting without any outside check?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The prompt is a sprawling, emotionally loaded paragraph asking the model to help decide whether to leave a job \u2014 no clear audience, no defined scope, and this is the third hour of the session. Three of five checks fail simultaneously: the ask can't be stated in 280 characters (Clarity), no human has heard this reasoning aloud (Human Check), and the emotional engagement has blown past any reasonable time threshold (Time Check). The drift pattern is compounding \u2014 the fluency of the AI's responses is substituting for the friction of thinking it through with a real person. The immediate move is to stop, articulate the core question in one sentence, and explain the current thinking to someone out loud before returning to the session.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Double-Layer AI Model Evaluation", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A two-layer scoring framework that combines objective benchmarks with personal experience ratings across task-specific dimensions, weighted by the user's own trust levels and task priorities, to produce a personalized composite score for comparing AI models.", "when_to_apply": ["I've tried three different AI models and they all feel close \u2014 I need a structured way to figure out which one actually wins for my use case", "The benchmarks say Model X is best at coding but in my experience it keeps failing at the specific kind of code I write", "My team is arguing about which AI model to standardize on and everyone has different priorities \u2014 writing vs. reasoning vs. tool use", "I want to justify an AI model choice to my manager with something more rigorous than 'I liked it better'", "A new model just dropped with impressive benchmarks but I haven't tested it enough to trust the numbers yet \u2014 how do I blend limited personal data with published scores?"], "principles": ["Layer 1 (Trust Level): Set a benchmark-vs-personal-experience ratio that reflects how much you trust published metrics vs. your own hands-on usage", "Layer 2 (Task Priorities): Assign percentage weights to the capability dimensions that actually matter for your work, so the composite score reflects your use case", "Use granular, dimension-specific ratings rather than a single overall score, because nobody uses a tool for 'everything'", "The composite score = (benchmark score \u00d7 benchmark weight) + (weighted personal score \u00d7 personal weight), normalized to 0\u2013100", "There is no universally correct weighting \u2014 the right split is the one that matches your workflow and trust calibration", "Rate dimensions with fine-grained precision (e.g., half-star increments) because small differences between 'good' and 'great' matter in practice"], "steps": ["Define the capability dimensions relevant to your use case (e.g., coding, writing, reasoning, reading, tool use, insightfulness)", "Collect and normalize objective benchmark scores for each option to a common 0\u2013100 scale", "Rate each option on each capability dimension from personal experience using a consistent scale", "Assign percentage weights to each capability dimension based on your task priorities (weights must sum to 100)", "Set your benchmark-vs-personal-experience split (e.g., 30/70) based on how much you trust published metrics vs. your own experience", "Calculate the weighted personal score: sum(rating_i \u00d7 weight_i) for all dimensions, normalized to 0\u2013100", "Calculate the composite score: (benchmark_score \u00d7 benchmark_weight) + (personal_score \u00d7 personal_weight)", "Re-run the calculation with different weight scenarios to understand which options are robust vs. sensitive to your assumptions"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "165750036", "title": "Finally, a Way to Choose the Right AI Model (Without Going Insane)", "date": "2025-06-12T13:01:53.956Z"}], "date": "2025-06-12T13:01:53.956Z", "id": "double-layer-ai-model-evaluation", "core_question": "I've used several AI models and the benchmarks don't match what I'm actually experiencing \u2014 how do I make a rigorous comparison that reflects both the data and my own reality?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "Comparing Claude, GPT-4, and Gemini for a content-heavy workflow. Benchmark trust is set at 30/70 because published reasoning scores don't correlate with actual output quality in long-form writing tasks. Dimensions weighted: writing quality 40%, reasoning 25%, tool use 20%, speed 15%. Claude's personal writing score (9.5/10) and reasoning score (8/10) dominate GPT-4's higher benchmark numbers once the 70% personal-experience weight kicks in. Composite: Claude 82, GPT-4 76, Gemini 71. Sensitivity check \u2014 even shifting to 50/50 benchmark trust, Claude holds the lead, meaning this isn't an artifact of the weighting. The decision is robust.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Compression vs. Expansion Distinction", "type": "distinction", "description": "Distinguishes between two fundamentally different modes of AI use\u2014compression (summarizing, organizing, and cleaning up existing thought) and expansion (using AI to discover, explore, and generate thinking that didn't exist before)\u2014arguing that only the latter enables breakthrough cognition.", "when_to_apply": ["We automated a bunch of knowledge work with AI and our output is faster but somehow feels thinner", "I'm trying to figure out whether to use AI to clean up my draft or to challenge and expand my thinking before I even draft", "Our AI adoption strategy is mostly about summarization, extraction, and formatting \u2014 and I'm wondering if we're missing the bigger opportunity", "I have a genuinely novel problem and the AI keeps giving me generic structured outputs instead of helping me discover something new", "My team treats AI as a productivity tool but I suspect the real value is in using it as a thinking partner for ambiguous decisions"], "principles": ["Compression removes the tangents, contradictions, and recursion that are the actual mechanisms of breakthrough thinking", "Expansion uses AI to access cognitive modes and ideas that would not emerge through individual or traditionally organized thinking", "The same AI tools can be used for either mode; the distinction lies in the intent and workflow design", "Compression has legitimate value for routine communication tasks; expansion is appropriate for high-stakes, ambiguous, or creative intellectual work", "Optimizing for brain time on subject (expansion) yields greater long-term returns than optimizing for brain time saved (compression)"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "165591666", "title": "We're Using AI Backwards\u2014Here's How to Max Your Brain on AI (I call it Cognitive Choreography)", "date": "2025-06-10T13:02:32.816Z"}], "date": "2025-06-10T13:02:32.816Z", "id": "compression-vs-expansion-distinction", "core_question": "Am I using AI to avoid thinking, or to think in ways I couldn't before?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This is compression, not expansion. The team built an AI pipeline that takes meeting transcripts, extracts action items, and produces clean summaries \u2014 and they're calling it their 'AI-augmented strategy process.' But the actual strategic thinking happened in the meeting; the AI is just a secretary. The real opportunity is using AI to pressure-test the reasoning that emerged in that meeting \u2014 surface contradictions between stated priorities and resource allocation, generate adversarial scenarios the team didn't consider, or explore analogies from adjacent domains. The clean summary feels productive but produces zero new insight. The messy, recursive, AI-challenged version of the same meeting content is where durable strategic advantage lives.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Lifeline Architecture Reframe", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI system design away from 'can the model solve this alone?' toward 'does the system know when to escalate, and can it do so before confidently failing?' \u2014 treating the model's complexity cliff as a known parameter to architect around rather than a flaw to ignore.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI system works great on demos but keeps producing confident wrong answers on harder real-world inputs", "We're debating whether to add RAG or code execution to improve reliability, but I'm not sure that actually solves the core problem", "I need to design a production AI pipeline where silent failures could cause real harm \u2014 legal, medical, financial", "Our model doesn't know what it doesn't know, and our current architecture has no way to detect when it's past its limits", "We're deploying AI in a latency-sensitive or cost-constrained environment and can't just throw more compute at edge cases"], "principles": ["The base model's complexity cliff is a measurable, stable parameter \u2014 find it before your customers do", "External tools (RAG, planners, code execution) push the cliff outward but do not eliminate it, and import their own failure modes and costs", "The escalation decision is itself a reasoning task \u2014 if the base model fails at depth N, its tool-selection judgment may also fail near that threshold", "The 'illusion' is the most dangerous failure mode: models emit confident, coherent prose right up until they silently give up, making downstream safety checks unreliable", "Design the escalation path before deployment, not after customers hit the edge case", "Latency and unit economics constrain which lifelines are viable \u2014 a theoretically correct escalation that costs 10x compute may be worse than a graceful degradation"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "165516467", "title": "Let's Talk THAT Apple AI Paper\u2014Here's the Real Takeaway the Internet is Ignoring", "date": "2025-06-09T13:02:23.497Z"}], "date": "2025-06-09T13:02:23.497Z", "id": "the-lifeline-architecture-reframe", "core_question": "Am I designing this AI system around what the model can do, or around what happens when it silently stops being able to do it?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The team is building a contract analysis tool and benchmarking GPT-4 accuracy on increasingly complex clause structures. The real question isn't 'how complex can it handle?' \u2014 it's 'at what complexity does it start being wrong while still sounding right?' That's the complexity cliff, and it sits around 4-5 nested conditional dependencies for this domain. The system needs an escalation path that fires *before* that cliff \u2014 a confidence calibration layer that routes to human review at depth 3-4, not after the model has already emitted a plausible but fabricated interpretation. Adding RAG over legal precedent pushes the cliff outward by maybe one level of complexity, but it also imports retrieval failures as a new silent-error source. The architecture decision is: build the escalation trigger into the pipeline as a first-class component, not bolt it on after production incidents reveal the cliff.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Agency Stack", "type": "procedure", "description": "A five-stage model for demonstrating high-agency problem-solving that employers actually evaluate, moving from identifying problems through execution to teaching the pattern to others.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm building a portfolio to show employers I can work with AI tools, but I'm not sure what these projects are actually supposed to prove", "We're designing interview questions to filter for people who can own problems end-to-end versus people who just execute tasks handed to them", "Someone on my team ships good work when directed but never surfaces problems or improves processes on their own \u2014 I need to diagnose what's missing", "I want to evaluate whether my side project demonstrates real career-relevant skill or is just a wrapper around a tutorial", "I'm advising a junior person on what capabilities to develop so they stay valuable as AI automates more execution work"], "principles": ["Agency is demonstrated through the full cycle from problem recognition to learning integration, not just execution", "Shipping code or output is not the point \u2014 navigating from a broken state to a better state is", "The ability to teach others the pattern (Learning Integration) is what distinguishes agency from one-off competence", "Resource marshaling includes tools, knowledge, and people \u2014 not just technical tools", "Employers test for this stack whether or not they consciously articulate it"], "steps": ["Problem Recognition: See issues others ignore before they are named as problems", "Solution Design: Imagine a better state and articulate what it looks like", "Resource Marshaling: Gather what is needed \u2014 tools, knowledge, and people \u2014 without waiting for permission", "Execution: Navigate from the current broken state to the better state, handling what breaks along the way", "Learning Integration: Update your models based on what worked and what failed, then teach others the pattern"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "164765411", "title": "The Kids Will be Ok: How To Skip the Jobs AI Doom Spiral and Actually Prepare for the Age of AI: A Tech Career Manifesto", "date": "2025-05-30T13:02:36.203Z"}], "date": "2025-05-30T13:02:36.203Z", "id": "the-agency-stack", "core_question": "Am I actually demonstrating the kind of problem-solving ability that matters, or am I just producing outputs that look impressive but don't prove I can navigate from broken to better?", "category": "leadership", "example_application": "This portfolio project fails at Stage 1. The candidate built a polished dashboard, but there's no evidence they identified a real problem \u2014 they picked a tutorial dataset and executed a known pattern. Compare that to the candidate who noticed their team was losing hours to a broken deploy pipeline, sketched what a fix would look like, pulled in a DevOps friend and three tools they'd never used, shipped the fix while handling two things that broke along the way, then wrote up what they learned and onboarded two teammates. The second candidate demonstrated the full stack \u2014 problem recognition through learning integration \u2014 and that's what separates agency from competence theater.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Pascal's Wager of Tech Careers", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A 2x2 asymmetric payoff matrix for evaluating career preparation strategies under uncertainty about AI's impact, showing that preparing for human-judgment-intensive work dominates regardless of which AI future arrives.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep going back and forth between learning AI/ML tools deeply versus building broader leadership and strategy skills \u2014 I can't do both well", "A junior engineer is asking me whether they should specialize in prompt engineering or invest in understanding business problems and stakeholder management", "Someone is arguing they should wait to see how AI shakes out before committing to a career development direction", "I'm trying to explain to my team why soft skills investment isn't a hedge \u2014 it's actually the dominant strategy regardless of AI trajectory", "Two career paths look equally promising right now but I suspect one has a much worse downside if AI progresses faster than expected"], "principles": ["Map outcomes across both possible futures (AI dominates / humans remain essential) for each strategy", "Identify which strategy has a positive expected outcome in both scenarios vs. only one", "The dominant strategy is the one where even the 'wrong' bet still produces transferable value", "Asymmetry in downside risk \u2014 not probability \u2014 is the deciding factor", "Market behavior (hiring patterns, investment flows) provides real-world signal to calibrate which scenario is more likely"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "164765411", "title": "The Kids Will be Ok: How To Skip the Jobs AI Doom Spiral and Actually Prepare for the Age of AI: A Tech Career Manifesto", "date": "2025-05-30T13:02:36.203Z"}], "date": "2025-05-30T13:02:36.203Z", "id": "pascal-s-wager-of-tech-careers", "core_question": "Given that nobody actually knows how far AI will go, how do I invest my career development time in a way that doesn't leave me stranded if I bet wrong?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "The payoff matrix is asymmetric and one strategy dominates. If you invest in human-judgment-intensive skills \u2014 strategic thinking, stakeholder navigation, ambiguity resolution \u2014 and AI fully automates technical execution, those skills become MORE valuable because you're the human steering AI systems. If AI stalls out and humans remain essential for execution, those same judgment skills still compound your career because every technical role rewards the person who can also navigate organizational complexity. The competing strategy \u2014 doubling down on narrow technical execution \u2014 only pays off in the scenario where AI doesn't displace it. That's a bet on one future; judgment skills pay in both.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Tier Uncertainty Routing", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that routes information queries to different handling strategies based on how stable or time-sensitive the underlying information is.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI assistant keeps searching for basic concepts it should already know, wasting time and API calls", "The agent confidently stated an outdated statistic as fact and I need a systematic way to prevent that across all information types", "I'm building a prompt for an agent with tool access and I need clear rules for when it should rely on its training data versus call external tools", "Some questions my assistant handles are about stable domain knowledge and others require real-time data \u2014 I need it to handle both without being inconsistent", "I'm designing a domain-specific assistant and want to reduce both unnecessary tool calls and hallucinated facts in the same prompt"], "principles": ["Timeless or foundational information should be answered directly from model knowledge", "Slow-changing information (statistics, benchmarks) should be answered with a caveat and an offer to verify", "Live or volatile information should always trigger a tool/search call before responding", "Good prompts include decision criteria for WHEN to act, not just HOW to act", "Embedding decision trees upfront prevents inconsistent tool use behavior"], "steps": ["Identify the information domains relevant to your use case", "Classify each domain as: timeless/foundational, slow-changing, or live/volatile", "Write explicit routing rules in the prompt for each tier", "Specify the tool or verification action for slow-changing and live tiers"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "164682010", "title": "Surfing the Guardrails: 7 Production-Grade Prompting Patterns I Stole from Claude's (Alleged) System Prompt", "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z"}], "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z", "id": "three-tier-uncertainty-routing", "core_question": "My AI agent sometimes searches for things it already knows and other times confidently states outdated information \u2014 how do I get it to consistently judge when to use its own knowledge versus when to look something up?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A customer support assistant handles questions about product architecture (timeless \u2014 answer directly), pricing tiers (slow-changing \u2014 answer from knowledge but flag that pricing may have updated and offer to verify), and current inventory availability (live \u2014 always call the inventory API before responding). Without explicit routing tiers in the prompt, the model either calls the API for every question (slow, expensive) or confidently states last quarter's pricing as current fact. The fix is embedding three explicit classification rules in the system prompt: 'If the question concerns how the product works architecturally, answer directly. If it concerns pricing or plan limits, answer from knowledge but append a recency caveat. If it concerns stock, order status, or availability, query the inventory system before responding.'", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Positional Reinforcement", "type": "procedure", "description": "A prompting technique that strategically repeats critical constraints at measured intervals throughout a long prompt to counteract attention degradation over extended contexts.", "when_to_apply": ["My system prompt is several hundred tokens long and the model keeps breaking a rule that's clearly stated at the top", "I need a hard compliance constraint like 'never output PII' to hold across a complex multi-step prompt", "The model follows my instructions for the first part of its response but drifts or violates constraints in later sections", "I'm building a long prompt with many sections and I need one non-negotiable rule to persist through all of them", "I've already made my constraint clear and unambiguous but the model still violates it in longer outputs"], "principles": ["Attention degrades over long contexts, so a single instruction at the top is not enough for critical rules", "Repeat key constraints at approximately 300\u2013500 token intervals", "Repetition is standard instructional design, not manipulation", "Critical rules should appear both at the start and at strategic points near relevant content"], "steps": ["Identify the one or two rules that are most critical and non-negotiable", "State those rules clearly at the beginning of the prompt", "Insert a brief reminder of those rules at natural breakpoints in the prompt body (every ~300\u2013500 tokens)", "Add a final reinforcement near the end or near the section most likely to trigger a violation"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "164682010", "title": "Surfing the Guardrails: 7 Production-Grade Prompting Patterns I Stole from Claude's (Alleged) System Prompt", "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z"}], "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z", "id": "positional-reinforcement", "core_question": "My long prompt has critical rules that the model keeps violating halfway through \u2014 how do I make constraints stick across an entire extended context?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A 1,200-token system prompt for a medical triage assistant includes 'NEVER provide a specific diagnosis' at the top, but the model starts offering diagnoses when handling the detailed symptom-analysis section 800 tokens later. The fix: the no-diagnosis constraint gets restated at the top, again at the transition into symptom analysis (~400 tokens in), and once more immediately before the response-formatting section at the end. After reinforcement, the constraint holds across all test cases \u2014 attention decay was the failure mode, not ambiguity in the rule itself.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Trigger \u2192 Template Refusals", "type": "procedure", "description": "A prompting pattern that replaces vague guidelines with explicit if-then conditional blocks pairing specific trigger conditions to pre-scripted response templates, ensuring predictable handling of edge cases.", "when_to_apply": ["My customer-facing chatbot sometimes refuses requests it should handle and sometimes answers things it should refuse \u2014 the behavior feels random", "I have a system prompt that says things like 'be careful about' or 'use good judgment when' and the model interprets those differently every time", "I need my AI assistant to handle compliance-sensitive topics with exact, pre-approved language every single time", "I'm hardening a production prompt and want to replace subjective guardrail language with deterministic if-then behavior for known edge cases", "Our QA team keeps flagging inconsistent refusal patterns and I need a systematic way to lock down responses for specific trigger scenarios"], "principles": ["Ambiguity in guidelines breeds inconsistency in behavior", "Each edge case should have a named trigger condition and a fixed response template", "Pre-scripted responses reduce variability without requiring creativity from the model at inference time", "Explicit conditionals are more reliable than general principles for sensitive or recurring edge cases"], "steps": ["Enumerate the edge cases or sensitive request types relevant to your use case", "Write a specific, matchable trigger condition for each (not vague categories)", "Write a fixed response template for each trigger", "Insert these as explicit if-then blocks in the system prompt"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "164682010", "title": "Surfing the Guardrails: 7 Production-Grade Prompting Patterns I Stole from Claude's (Alleged) System Prompt", "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z"}], "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z", "id": "trigger-template-refusals", "core_question": "My AI keeps handling sensitive or edge-case requests inconsistently \u2014 sometimes it refuses when it shouldn't, sometimes it doesn't refuse when it should \u2014 and I can't figure out how to make the behavior predictable.", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The current system prompt says 'be cautious when users ask about medication dosages.' That's the root of the inconsistency \u2014 sometimes the model gives detailed dosage info, sometimes it refuses entirely, sometimes it hedges uselessly. The fix is replacing that line with explicit conditionals: IF the user asks for a specific dosage recommendation for a named medication, THEN respond with 'I can't recommend specific dosages. Please consult your pharmacist or prescribing doctor. I can explain general information about how [medication] works.' IF the user asks what a typical dosage range looks like, THEN provide the publicly available range with a citation and a disclaimer. Two triggers, two templates, zero ambiguity at inference time.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Post-Tool Reflection Slot", "type": "procedure", "description": "A prompting pattern that inserts a mandatory structured thinking pause after tool or API results are received, ensuring the model interprets output before generating a response rather than relaying it directly.", "when_to_apply": ["My RAG pipeline retrieves relevant documents but the model's final answers are shallow summaries that miss the actual insight", "Tool-augmented agent keeps parroting raw API output instead of interpreting what the results mean for the user's question", "Building a multi-step agentic workflow and need to prevent the model from rushing past tool results to generate a response", "Responses from my tool-calling pipeline are inconsistent \u2014 sometimes great, sometimes nonsensical \u2014 depending on what the tool returns", "High-stakes domain where the model needs to catch contradictions or gaps in retrieved data before presenting conclusions"], "principles": ["Tool outputs often require interpretation before they can produce a useful answer", "A reflection step improves accuracy by forcing consideration before response generation", "Build cognitive checkpoints into specific moments, not just at the end of a task", "The reflection should assess: does this answer the question, are there contradictions, what is the key takeaway"], "steps": ["Identify all points in your workflow where the model receives tool or API output", "Add an explicit instruction to pause and assess the tool results before responding", "Specify the reflection criteria: does it fully answer the question, are there contradictions, what is the takeaway", "Instruct the model to only generate its final response after completing the reflection"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "164682010", "title": "Surfing the Guardrails: 7 Production-Grade Prompting Patterns I Stole from Claude's (Alleged) System Prompt", "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z"}], "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z", "id": "post-tool-reflection-slot", "core_question": "My AI system is getting good tool results but then producing shallow or wrong answers \u2014 how do I make it actually think about what it retrieved before responding?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The agent calls a financial API and gets back quarterly revenue figures, but two data points contradict the earnings report also retrieved via search. Without a reflection slot, the model would relay both uncritically. With the reflection slot inserted \u2014 'Before responding: does this fully answer the question? Are there contradictions? What is the key takeaway?' \u2014 the model catches the discrepancy, flags that the API data reflects restated figures while the search result cites the original filing, and synthesizes a response that explains the difference rather than presenting conflicting numbers as if they're both true.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Defensive Prompt Engineering Philosophy", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes prompt design from creative incantation-finding toward defensive programming, where prompts are policy documents focused 90% on preventing failure modes rather than 10% on enabling capabilities.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompt works great when I test it manually but produces weird outputs with real user inputs", "I'm moving an AI feature from prototype to production and need to harden the prompt", "My prompt is clever and concise but I keep finding new edge cases that break it", "I'm writing prompts for a compliance-sensitive or high-stakes application where failures have real consequences", "I want to evaluate whether a teammate's prompt is actually production-ready or just demo-ready"], "principles": ["Production prompts are ~10% about what to do and ~90% about what not to do", "Plan for failure modes first; happy path optimization is secondary", "Think declaratively (policies and invariants) rather than imperatively (step-by-step commands)", "A prompt is a configuration file or operating system, not a magic spell", "Great prompting is boring: comprehensive, not clever"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "164682010", "title": "Surfing the Guardrails: 7 Production-Grade Prompting Patterns I Stole from Claude's (Alleged) System Prompt", "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z"}], "date": "2025-05-29T13:02:34.145Z", "id": "defensive-prompt-engineering-philosophy", "core_question": "Why does my prompt work beautifully in demos but break unpredictably in production, and how do I close the gap?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This prompt is 200 words of enthusiastic capability description and zero failure-mode constraints \u2014 it's a demo prompt masquerading as a production prompt. The conventional approach treats prompting as finding the right creative incantation to unlock the model's best output. That's backwards. A production prompt is a policy document: 90% of its weight should be boundary conditions, refusal behaviors, edge-case handling, and output invariants. The happy path already works \u2014 the model is good at the thing you're asking it to do. What it's not good at is knowing when to stop, what to refuse, how to handle malformed input, and which formats are non-negotiable. Rewrite this as a declarative specification: state the constraints as invariants the model must maintain, not as step-by-step instructions it will inevitably drift from.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Know \u00b7 Keep \u00b7 Prove Framework", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A three-verb ethic for anchoring professional ownership and accountability in knowledge work when AI can generate polished artifacts instantly, replacing effort-based credibility signals with evidence-based ones.", "when_to_apply": ["Someone on my team delivered impressive-looking work but couldn't defend the reasoning when questioned in a meeting", "We're writing an AI usage policy and need a principle more actionable than 'use AI responsibly'", "I want to use AI heavily in my own work but I'm worried I'm losing genuine understanding of what I'm producing", "A deliverable failed in practice despite looking convincing on paper and we need to diagnose why the quality signal broke down", "Our performance culture still rewards hours worked and volume of output \u2014 I need a concrete alternative frame for what 'good work' means in an AI-augmented team"], "principles": ["Know: Mastery cannot be outsourced to a model \u2014 you must be able to reconstruct the logic without any tool, answer sideways questions, and adapt the work to new contexts without it 'wobbling'", "Keep: Maintain reconstructable reasoning \u2014 document the meaningful decision turns at a level of detail proportional to the consequence of the work (low-stakes needs minimal provenance; high-stakes warrants full audit trails)", "Prove: Reality is the only arbiter \u2014 an artifact's worth is measured by whether it moves the real-world metric it claims it will, not by how persuasive it reads in isolation", "The three verbs form a flywheel: deep understanding produces better provenance, transparent provenance accelerates diagnosis when outcomes lag, and real-world feedback hardens both knowledge and documentation", "Shift the organizational grammar from effort signals (hours logged, slides produced) to evidence signals (traceable claims, testable assumptions, outcome data)", "Provenance should be light enough to maintain momentum but strong enough to maintain trust \u2014 it is a guardrail for speed, not surveillance"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163747993", "title": "Our Work Norms are 4000 Years Old and We Need to Update Them for AI\u2014Here's How to Do It", "date": "2025-05-19T13:02:54.174Z"}], "date": "2025-05-19T13:02:54.174Z", "id": "know-keep-prove-framework", "core_question": "Now that AI can produce polished work instantly, what does it actually mean for me or my team to 'own' a deliverable \u2014 and how do I prove that ownership is real?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The strategy deck looks flawless, but the author can't explain why the TAM estimate uses a top-down rather than bottom-up methodology, and stumbles when asked how the number changes if the EU market is excluded. That's a Know failure \u2014 the artifact was generated, not understood. The appropriate response isn't to ban AI from deck-building; it's to require that the author can reconstruct the core logic unprompted and that the deck's key claims map to testable assumptions with named metrics. Right now this team rewards polished slides (effort signal) when it should reward traceable reasoning and outcome data (evidence signal).", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Narrow-Pipe Law", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI performance as primarily constrained by input quality rather than model capability, arguing that feeding a narrow, noise-free data stream into any language model delivers more value than upgrading to a more powerful model with noisy inputs.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI summaries are hedged and vague and we're debating whether to switch to a more expensive model", "We're feeding multiple data sources into an LLM workflow and the outputs feel confused or low-confidence", "Leadership wants to budget for a model tier upgrade but we haven't standardized what goes into the pipeline", "Our AI tool works great on simple inputs but falls apart when we give it our real messy data", "We're trying to decide whether to invest engineering time in data cleaning or model migration"], "principles": ["The potency of an AI engine is limited less by its architecture than by the messiness of what you pour into it", "A narrow, noise-free input stream causes a model to spend all its capacity on insight rather than triage", "A powerful engine fed junk will still output polite junk", "Constraining input to a single clearly defined stream unlocks performance gains that dwarf the difference between model tiers", "A clean narrow pipe compounds automatically as underlying models improve, without additional integration work"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163520833", "title": "Stop Waiting for New AI Models: Here's How to Use Data to Make AI at Work, WORK", "date": "2025-05-14T13:03:07.625Z"}], "date": "2025-05-14T13:03:07.625Z", "id": "narrow-pipe-law", "core_question": "Our AI outputs are mediocre \u2014 should we upgrade to a better model, or is the real problem what we're feeding it?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The conventional read is that the team's GPT-4 summarization pipeline is underperforming because they need a more capable model. That's wrong here. The pipeline ingests a Slack channel, three email threads, and a Notion doc per request \u2014 mixed formats, redundant context, conflicting levels of detail. The model spends most of its capacity triaging what matters rather than generating insight. Constraining the input to the single canonical Notion brief \u2014 one clean, narrow pipe \u2014 would unlock more performance gain than any model swap, and it compounds: every future model upgrade automatically performs better on that clean stream without rework.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Narrow Pipe \u2192 Large Engine (6-Step Process)", "type": "procedure", "description": "A six-step process for applying the Narrow-Pipe Law to any repetitive workflow, converting noisy mixed-purpose data sources into clean, single-purpose input streams that maximize the value of bundled AI tools.", "when_to_apply": ["Our Slack AI summaries or Notion AI outputs are garbage and I'm not sure if the tool is bad or we're feeding it wrong", "The same customer info keeps getting retyped across three different tools and nobody has a single source of truth", "We want to use built-in AI features but our channels and databases are too noisy and mixed-purpose", "I'm spending hours cleaning up or triaging information that should have arrived in usable form", "We're about to set up a new automated reporting or digest workflow and I want it to actually produce actionable output from day one"], "principles": ["Repetition of the same facts across workflows signals a hidden pipe begging to be narrowed", "The minimal 'decision-grade' payload is rarely more than three fields", "A single inlet with a clearly stated two-line policy is sufficient infrastructure", "Upstream discipline (a few seconds of nudging) prevents downstream cleanup costs (hours of effort)", "If the first AI output is not actionable, tighten the pipe further before adding complexity"], "steps": ["Identify repetitive information hotspots \u2014 scan workflows for places where the same facts get rewritten or forwarded across teams or tools", "Define the minimal 'decision-grade' core \u2014 determine the smallest payload that lets a stakeholder act without chasing links (typically no more than three fields)", "Carve a single inlet and label it loudly \u2014 create one dedicated channel, database, or sheet with a pinned two-line policy stating what belongs and what does not", "Police the boundary upstream, not downstream \u2014 nudge off-topic contributions back to general channels the moment they appear", "Turn on the bundled model and inspect the first output \u2014 if the digest is not actionable, tighten the pipe further by shortening the payload, removing formatting noise, or splitting the stream", "Let improvements in the engine compound automatically \u2014 once the pipe is clean, every platform upgrade improves output without additional integration work"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163520833", "title": "Stop Waiting for New AI Models: Here's How to Use Data to Make AI at Work, WORK", "date": "2025-05-14T13:03:07.625Z"}], "date": "2025-05-14T13:03:07.625Z", "id": "narrow-pipe-large-engine-6-step-process", "core_question": "Why do the AI tools already built into our platforms produce useless outputs, and what's actually wrong with the data flowing into them?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The team's Slack channel AI summary is useless \u2014 it reads like a random grab bag because the channel mixes project updates, watercooler chat, and client escalations. The same client name and status gets restated across Slack, email, and a shared doc. That repetition is the signal: there's a hidden pipe. The decision-grade payload is three fields \u2014 client name, issue type, and current status. A dedicated #client-escalations channel with a two-line pinned policy ('Only post: client name, issue summary, and severity. Everything else stays in #general.') carves the inlet. After one week, Slack's built-in summarize feature produces a daily digest that's immediately actionable \u2014 no downstream triage, no manual reformatting \u2014 and when Slack ships its next AI upgrade, the output gets better for free.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Docs-First Workflow", "type": "procedure", "description": "A development approach where customer-facing documentation is written before any code or design work begins, using an LLM's ability to extract specs from that documentation as a forcing function to expose unclear thinking.", "when_to_apply": ["We keep writing PRDs and functional specs but engineering still comes back with questions that reveal we hadn't actually decided key behaviors", "I want to use LLMs to accelerate product development but I'm not sure where in the workflow they create the most leverage", "Our team produces a mountain of intermediary documents \u2014 PRDs, internal memos, design briefs \u2014 and I suspect most of them are redundant translation layers", "We're about to kick off a new feature and I want a fast, rigorous way to stress-test whether our thinking is complete before anyone starts building", "Downstream rework keeps happening and I think the root cause is fuzzy requirements, not poor execution"], "principles": ["Customer-facing documentation encodes the full intended experience and serves as the single source of truth from which all downstream roles derive their slice", "If an LLM cannot extract a sensible API schema, UI outline, or QA checklist from the docs, the missing clarity is the real problem to fix", "Intermediary artifacts (PRDs, internal memos, functional specs) exist only because cross-silo translation was expensive \u2014 when that cost drops to near-zero, those artifacts become waste", "The scarce resource shifts from artifact production to the product leader's clarity of intent and aesthetic judgment", "Half-baked documentation propagates half-baked thinking at scale, so the loop must expose fuzzy thinking earlier than traditional stage-gate processes"], "steps": ["Write the help center article (or equivalent customer-facing documentation) before writing a single line of code", "Draft the onboarding sequence before creating wireframes", "Interrogate the docs by prompting an LLM to extract an API schema, UI outline, and QA checklist from them", "Identify what clarity is missing wherever the LLM fails to produce coherent outputs", "Iterate on the documentation until the LLM can reliably derive all downstream artifacts from it", "Use the finalized docs as the north star for engineering, design, and leadership evaluation"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "163522233", "title": "Blow Up Your Assumptions: Rethinking Product and Engineering in the age of AI", "date": "2025-05-14T02:11:09.668Z"}], "date": "2025-05-14T02:11:09.668Z", "id": "docs-first-workflow", "core_question": "Is my team's thinking actually clear enough to build this, or are we about to discover our confusion in code instead of in words?", "category": "execution", "example_application": "The team wrote a help center article describing how a user would set up automated billing alerts. When an LLM was prompted to extract an API schema from that article, it couldn't resolve whether 'alert threshold' meant a percentage of budget or an absolute dollar amount \u2014 and it couldn't determine whether alerts were per-project or per-account. Those aren't documentation gaps; they're product decisions nobody has made yet. Fixing them in prose takes an hour. Fixing them after engineering has built the wrong data model takes a sprint. The article gets revised, the LLM cleanly extracts the schema, the QA checklist, and the onboarding flow \u2014 and now every downstream team is working from the same resolved intent.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Second-Use Litmus", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A test that evaluates whether an AI product delivers lasting utility by measuring whether users return after novelty wears off, rather than judging success by launch-day metrics.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI feature had a huge launch week but usage is dropping off a cliff and I'm not sure if it's a retention problem or a product problem", "Leadership wants to scale our AI pilot based on strong early adoption numbers, but I'm skeptical the engagement is real", "I need to set success metrics for an AI product launch that actually predict long-term value, not just hype", "Users say they love our AI tool in surveys but the usage data tells a different story \u2014 trying to figure out what's actually happening", "We're debating whether to invest more in an AI feature that got great press coverage but I want to know if it's actually sticky"], "principles": ["First-use curiosity is cheap; second-use loyalty is expensive \u2014 measure the latter", "By session two, the bar shifts from 'Can it do something cool?' to 'Will it remove real friction every time?'", "A product must deliver a 10\u00d7 job improvement, not a 10-second magic show", "Reliability must be proven before dependence is asked for", "Measure retention in cohorts (Day-2, Week-2, Month-1), not anecdotes", "If users wouldn't scream if the feature disappeared tomorrow, you built a press release, not a product"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163439500", "title": "Six AI Product Principles No One Tells You\u2014And How I'm Applying Them in My Build", "date": "2025-05-13T13:01:19.256Z"}], "date": "2025-05-13T13:01:19.256Z", "id": "second-use-litmus", "core_question": "Are people actually using this AI product because it solves their problem, or are they just poking at it because it's new and impressive?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The AI writing assistant launched to 5,000 users and hit 73% activation on Day 1 \u2014 but that's first-use curiosity, not signal. Day-7 cohort retention is 9%, and of the users who came back, session depth dropped 60%. The product delivered a magic trick, not a workflow improvement. The core job \u2014 'help me write faster without sacrificing my voice' \u2014 isn't being done reliably enough for users to trust it with real work. The fix isn't better onboarding or more features; it's making the base generation accurate enough on the second draft that users stop reverting to their old process.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Trust Loops", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A four-part design requirement for AI products that maintains user trust across probabilistic interactions by making the system's reasoning visible, escapable, reversible, and self-correcting.", "when_to_apply": ["Users are dropping off my AI feature after initial adoption and I can't figure out why engagement decays", "We're about to ship an AI-powered feature and I need to decide which transparency and safety controls actually matter for the MVP", "Our AI product works well 90% of the time but the 10% failure cases are destroying user confidence", "I'm replacing a human-staffed workflow with AI automation and need to design for the moments when the AI gets it wrong", "Support tickets are spiking around our AI feature and users keep saying they don't trust the outputs"], "principles": ["Every interaction with an LLM can reset the credibility meter, so trust loops must be built first, not added later", "Show its work: citations, retrieval breadcrumbs, or source peeks", "Escalate without friction: one-click handoff to a human with full context preserved", "Allow instant rollback: version history and undo must be as prominent as the primary action", "Measure regret: every correction, escalation, or abandoned session feeds back into improvement and KPI dashboards", "Trust isn't a milestone; it's a heartbeat you have to keep nudging"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163439500", "title": "Six AI Product Principles No One Tells You\u2014And How I'm Applying Them in My Build", "date": "2025-05-13T13:01:19.256Z"}], "date": "2025-05-13T13:01:19.256Z", "id": "trust-loops", "core_question": "Will users actually keep trusting this AI system after it inevitably gets something wrong, or will one bad interaction send them running?", "category": "product", "example_application": "This AI draft-writing tool scores poorly on the Trust Loop dimensions. Show-its-work: zero \u2014 it produces outputs with no citations or reasoning trace, so users can't distinguish a confident hallucination from a grounded answer. Escapability: weak \u2014 escalating to a human editor requires opening a separate support ticket, which means context is lost and friction is high. Rollback: nonexistent \u2014 there's no version history, so a user who hits 'apply suggestions' on a bad rewrite has no recovery path. Regret measurement: absent \u2014 the team tracks adoption and session length but has no signal on corrections, abandoned outputs, or escalation frequency. The result is predictable: the product feels magical in demos but bleeds users after week two, because the first wrong answer has no trust infrastructure to absorb the shock.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Deterministic Recovery for a Probabilistic Engine", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A design principle requiring AI products to wrap their stochastic core with deterministic safety controls \u2014 undo, diffs, sandboxing, audit trails, and stateful human handoff \u2014 so errors become recoverable speed bumps rather than trust-destroying incidents.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI feature directly modifies user data or sends communications and I need to design the safety layer around it", "We had a production incident where the AI made a wrong call and users couldn't easily undo or even see what happened", "I'm building an AI agent that takes real-world actions \u2014 booking, purchasing, editing \u2014 and I need to decide what ships between the model and the outside world", "Our human escalation flow is broken because agents don't get the AI's conversation context when they take over", "I want to evaluate whether an AI product is ready for production traffic or still needs guardrails before launch"], "principles": ["Assume errors happen; make recovery muscle-memory, not a scramble", "Build deterministic controls around the model's stochastic core", "Every AI action must be reversible with a single click \u2014 no scavenger hunts", "Show side-by-side diffs so users see exactly what changed and the model's confidence", "Default to sandbox/preview mode before committing actions to production", "Maintain a full audit trail: inputs, outputs, and user interventions", "Human takeover must preserve full conversation state \u2014 escalation is pointless if the agent starts from scratch"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163439500", "title": "Six AI Product Principles No One Tells You\u2014And How I'm Applying Them in My Build", "date": "2025-05-13T13:01:19.256Z"}], "date": "2025-05-13T13:01:19.256Z", "id": "deterministic-recovery-for-a-probabilistic-engine", "core_question": "When my AI product inevitably gets something wrong, will users recover gracefully or lose trust permanently?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This AI email-drafting agent scores poorly on deterministic recovery. It sends messages directly with no preview, no diff showing what was changed from the user's notes, and no single-click undo once sent. The audit trail exists but only logs the final output \u2014 not the prompt, retrieved context, or confidence level. Worst of all, when a user escalates to a human support agent, the conversation state resets completely, forcing the customer to re-explain everything. The stochastic core is fully exposed to production consequences. Until sandbox-by-default sending, inline diffs, one-click recall, full input-output logging, and stateful handoff are in place, this agent is one hallucinated reply away from a trust-destroying incident.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Show the Invisible Delta", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI ROI measurement by requiring the product itself to surface its own impact in real time at the point of use, rather than leaving value invisible until a quarterly finance review.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI feature is saving people hours per week but leadership keeps asking what it actually does", "Designing the in-product experience for an AI tool and deciding what the user should see after each interaction", "An AI product just got cut from the budget despite strong usage \u2014 trying to figure out what went wrong", "Building an enterprise AI product and need to preempt the 'prove your ROI' conversation before renewal", "Users say they like the AI tool but can't articulate why when asked by their manager"], "principles": ["Hidden value gets cut; surface the delta in real time at the point of use", "Instrument the product to display time saved, errors prevented, and revenue protected \u2014 in the UI, not just the slide deck", "If the product can't prove its own worth at the point of use, it hasn't earned the second use", "Users internalize value when they see the scoreboard tick upward session by session", "Much AI ROI lies in shaved cognitive load that never appears in a payroll spreadsheet unless surfaced explicitly"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163439500", "title": "Six AI Product Principles No One Tells You\u2014And How I'm Applying Them in My Build", "date": "2025-05-13T13:01:19.256Z"}], "date": "2025-05-13T13:01:19.256Z", "id": "show-the-invisible-delta", "core_question": "Our AI tool is delivering real value but nobody can see it \u2014 how do we stop it from getting cut in the next budget review?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The conventional read is that ROI measurement is a finance problem \u2014 you gather the data, build the dashboard, present it quarterly. That's wrong here because by the time someone opens a spreadsheet to justify the tool's cost, the user who benefited has already forgotten the value and the budget holder never felt it. The fix is structural: the product itself must surface 'You just saved 14 minutes' or 'This caught 3 errors your team would have shipped' directly in the workflow, every session. When the CFO asks whether to renew, the answer isn't in a slide deck \u2014 it's in the muscle memory of forty users who watched a scoreboard tick upward every day. The invisible cognitive load reduction \u2014 the thing that never shows up on a payroll line \u2014 only becomes defensible spend when the product makes it visible at the moment it happens.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Memetic Defense", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured approach to building cognitive immunity against viral narratives and hype by demanding evidence across three axes before accepting a claim.", "when_to_apply": ["A headline about an AI breakthrough is making me feel either excited or anxious and I haven't looked at any primary sources yet", "A vendor or executive is quoting a dramatic efficiency gain but I can't find a reproducible demo or independent verification", "I keep seeing the same AI success story repeated across outlets but no one links to actual data or follow-up coverage", "Media coverage of an AI risk \u2014 energy consumption, job displacement, existential threat \u2014 feels urgent but I don't know what baseline they're comparing against", "I'm about to share or act on a claim and realize I haven't asked myself what evidence would change my mind about it"], "principles": ["The meme lands before the evidence does \u2014 slow down the emotional response before forming a judgment", "Always ask for a real baseline and what is being compared, not just the absolute claim", "Demand proof across three axes: proof of work (demos, not declarations), proof of cost (real implementation costs, not launch slides), and proof of time to utility (honest timelines, not transformation myths)", "Distinguish real case studies from repeated memes by checking whether outcomes are reproducible and whether announcements were later retracted or quietly revised", "Practice constructive skepticism: explicitly ask 'what would make me change my mind?' to stay open to new evidence while resistant to unexamined narrative momentum", "Build filters, not walls \u2014 the goal is faster understanding, not blanket rejection of new ideas"], "steps": ["Notice the emotional trigger: identify when a claim is landing in your 'lizard brain' before you've had a chance to think", "Demand a baseline: ask what the claim is being compared to and what context is missing", "Apply the three-axis proof test: evaluate whether the claim provides proof of work (demo or reproducible result), proof of cost (full implementation cost, not just launch), and proof of time to utility (realistic timeline to measurable value)", "Check the announcement-to-reality gap: look for follow-up coverage, retractions, or quieted claims that contradict the original narrative", "State your update condition: articulate what evidence would cause you to change your mind, ensuring your skepticism remains constructive and open rather than reflexively dismissive"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "163443333", "title": "There is Too Much AI Hype\u2014This is How To Defend Your Brain and Stay Calm", "date": "2025-05-13T01:50:19.330Z"}], "date": "2025-05-13T01:50:19.330Z", "id": "memetic-defense", "core_question": "Am I forming this belief because the evidence is strong, or because the narrative arrived faster than my ability to evaluate it?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The claim that Company X 'replaced 80% of its customer service team with AI' fails all three proof axes. Proof of work: no demo or reproducible benchmark, just a CEO quote in a press release. Proof of cost: no mention of the integration costs, hallucination remediation, or human escalation layer they certainly still run. Proof of time to utility: the announcement is three weeks old with zero follow-up confirming sustained results. This is success theater \u2014 a meme optimized for LinkedIn virality, not an evidence-backed case study. The update condition: if they publish six-month CSAT scores and net cost data with methodology, the claim becomes evaluable.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Wake-Word Brick Audit", "type": "procedure", "description": "A five-step process for discovering, naming, installing, and maintaining memory-stored prompt macros (\"bricks\") in ChatGPT based on your own repeated friction points.", "when_to_apply": ["I notice I'm copy-pasting the same formatting or tone instruction into every new ChatGPT conversation", "I want to set up stored prompt shortcuts in ChatGPT's memory but I'm not sure which instructions are worth saving versus which will just clutter things up", "My existing ChatGPT memory is full of overlapping or outdated custom instructions and the outputs are getting inconsistent", "I'm trying to get one-word triggers working in ChatGPT but the wake words keep firing accidentally in normal conversation", "A small team needs consistent AI output formatting but we don't have shared tooling \u2014 just individual ChatGPT accounts"], "principles": ["Only automate instructions you actually repeat \u2014 bricks that don't map to real friction gather dust", "Keep each brick atomic: one concern per brick (tone OR length OR structure, never all three)", "Group near-duplicate instructions by outcome, not wording, before creating a brick", "Use collision-resistant wake-words (non-English strings or leading symbols) to prevent accidental triggers", "Overwrite bricks rather than append revisions to avoid instruction soup", "Maintain a cap of roughly 10 active bricks to keep mental lookup cost below keystroke savings", "Validate every brick at install time with a test prompt; if output wobbles, edit and overwrite before continuing"], "steps": ["Mine your chat history (10 minutes): scroll the last week of conversations, identify any instruction you re-typed three or more times, and drop each into a scratch pad", "Group by outcome, not wording: merge near-duplicates (e.g., 'tighten copy,' 'remove fluff,' 'be concise' all become 'Trim language') to keep the brick list short enough to remember without a cheat sheet", "Draft micro-guides in a table: write one atomic instruction per row \u2014 tone OR length OR structure, never a combination \u2014 and assign a candidate wake-word to each", "Stress-test the names: type a normal paragraph and verify none of your wake-words appear in everyday prose; if a word could appear naturally, prepend a symbol (e.g., :TRIM) or switch to an unusual string", "Install, validate, prune: paste one brick at a time with 'Please remember this,' wait for confirmation, fire the test prompt, and review weekly \u2014 keep bricks you used, delete the rest"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163167476", "title": "How to Prompt with Wake Words in ChatGPT: One Word Callouts Save Hours Prompting on Common Tasks", "date": "2025-05-09T13:00:46.215Z"}], "date": "2025-05-09T13:00:46.215Z", "id": "wake-word-brick-audit", "core_question": "I keep retyping the same instructions to ChatGPT across dozens of conversations \u2014 how do I turn my most repeated friction into reliable one-word shortcuts that actually stick?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "Mining the last week of chats surfaces three near-duplicates: 'make it concise,' 'cut the fluff,' and 'tighten the writing' \u2014 these all target the same outcome, so they collapse into a single brick called :TRIM with the atomic instruction 'Remove filler words, reduce sentence count by ~40%, preserve all claims.' A normal-prose stress test confirms ':TRIM' never appears in everyday typing. After installing it with 'Please remember this,' a test prompt on a bloated paragraph produces a clean, tight rewrite on the first try \u2014 the brick is live. Meanwhile, a candidate brick for 'use bullet points AND keep it under 100 words AND sound casual' fails the atomicity check: that's three concerns duct-taped together, so it splits into :BULLETS, :CAP100, and :CASUAL, each handling one dimension independently.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Single-Datum Safari", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured method for reading an unfamiliar codebase by tracing one concrete piece of data (e.g., an email address) from entry point through business logic, persistence, and egress, producing an architectural breadcrumb map.", "when_to_apply": ["An AI generated a full feature for me and I need to verify it actually works correctly before shipping", "I just joined a project and need to understand how a specific user action flows through the system by tomorrow", "I'm reviewing a pull request that touches multiple files across layers and I can't tell what the actual data flow is", "I need to explain to a non-technical stakeholder exactly what happens when a user submits a form in our app", "I suspect there's a hidden failure mode in a feature but I don't know the codebase well enough to pinpoint where things could break"], "principles": ["Pick one concrete, user-facing datum (e.g., email address, credit-card token) rather than studying module structure abstractly", "Follow the datum through Ingress \u2192 Business Logic \u2192 Persistence \u2192 Egress zones", "Use function and variable names for intent; ignore syntax details", "Record a plain-English breadcrumb trail of each hop", "Stop after locating persistence and egress \u2014 resist the urge to read every file"], "steps": ["Choose a concrete, user-facing datum (e.g., email address, payment token)", "Search the repo for a literal string in the request path (e.g., /signup, /checkout) and open the first hit", "Identify the obvious handoff function (e.g., createUser, processPayment) \u2014 this is the Ingress zone", "Jump to that function's definition to enter the Business Logic zone; note any transformation clues (fields added, validation called)", "Find the persistence call (insert, save, putObject) \u2014 mark the file path as the Persistence zone", "Trace outward to find any egress calls (publish, sendToQueue, sendMail) \u2014 note them as the Egress zone", "Write the plain-English breadcrumb list (e.g., POST /signup \u2192 SignupController.js \u2192 createUser() \u2192 users.insert \u2192 queueWelcome)", "Ask three diagnostic questions: Was input validated before the DB? What happens if a downstream hop fails? Are any hops synchronous network calls that could stall the request?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163007434", "title": "Taming the Genie: How to Read the Code You Just Wished Into Existence", "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z"}], "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z", "id": "single-datum-safari", "core_question": "I'm staring at a codebase I didn't write \u2014 how do I build a working mental model of what it actually does without reading every file?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The datum is an email address. POST /signup lands in SignupController.js, which calls createUser() \u2014 that function lowercases the email and checks for duplicates (Business Logic). It then calls users.insert() in userRepo.js (Persistence) and fires queueWelcomeEmail() via an async SQS publish (Egress). The breadcrumb map is: POST /signup \u2192 SignupController.js \u2192 createUser() \u2192 users.insert \u2192 queueWelcomeEmail. Diagnostic verdict: input validation happens before the DB write (good), but the SQS publish has no retry or dead-letter queue configured \u2014 a silent failure there means users sign up but never get onboarded. That's the first thing to flag.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Zone Wall Map", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A color-coded boundary audit that classifies each folder and hop in a codebase as Interface (blue), Business Logic (green), or Storage (orange), then checks whether data ever jumps zones illegally.", "when_to_apply": ["I generated a whole app with an LLM and it works, but I have no idea if the architecture is actually sound or just a pile of working code", "Our codebase has folders named 'services' and 'repositories' but I suspect components are reaching across layers anyway", "I'm about to split a monolith into services and need to know where the real boundaries are versus where we pretend they are", "A PR touches five different layers and I need a fast way to check if it's violating architectural boundaries without reading every line", "SQL queries or HTTP calls keep showing up in places they shouldn't be and I want to systematically find every instance"], "principles": ["Every hop should cross into a new color zone \u2014 same-zone jumps or zone skips are smells", "Config files (docker-compose, serverless.yml) reveal which external systems exist; each should map to a thin adapter folder", "Direct imports from storage into interface layers (e.g., UI importing DB calls) indicate a missing wall", "A boundary violation is a design smell even if the code works today", "Boundaries matter more than elegant function bodies"], "steps": ["Take your breadcrumb trail from the datum safari and color-code each hop: Blue = Interface, Green = Business Logic, Orange = Storage", "Plot the hops left-to-right on paper or a whiteboard", "Scan the repo directory tree and label folders with the corresponding colors", "Compare: does every hop traverse a new color? Flag any Orange \u2192 Blue or Blue \u2192 Orange direct jumps", "Check config files for external systems and verify each has a corresponding thin adapter folder", "Run a grep for cross-zone imports (e.g., UI importing from db/) to confirm or deny wall integrity", "Ask three boundary-stress questions: Is auth centralized in one layer? Is SQL confined to the storage zone? Are HTTP calls absent from business logic?"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163007434", "title": "Taming the Genie: How to Read the Code You Just Wished Into Existence", "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z"}], "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z", "id": "three-zone-wall-map", "core_question": "Does this codebase actually have real architectural boundaries, or are the layers just naming conventions that the code ignores?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "Coloring the hops from the API controller through to the database reveals the problem: the Express route handler (blue) imports the Prisma client directly (orange), skipping the business logic layer entirely. There's no green hop in between. The service/ folder exists but nothing in the request path touches it \u2014 the 'architecture' is cosmetic. Meanwhile, docker-compose shows Redis and S3 connections, but neither has an adapter folder; those clients are instantiated inline wherever they're needed, which means swapping storage backends later will require surgery across dozens of files.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three-Question Bridge Drill", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A rapid three-question interrogation applied to every boundary crossing (bridge) in a codebase to classify each as 'Clean & Loud,' 'Synchronous Gamble,' or 'Silent Drop,' producing a risk heatmap without parsing algorithmic code.", "when_to_apply": ["I just generated a bunch of code with AI and need to quickly find where it might fail silently across service boundaries", "We have multiple microservices calling each other and I want a fast risk picture without reading every line of business logic", "After an incident where something failed silently, I need to audit all the boundary crossings in the affected path", "I'm preparing for a design review and need to surface which integrations are missing error handling, timeouts, or input validation", "Our codebase has grown through rapid prototyping and I suspect there are empty catch blocks and unguarded API calls hiding everywhere"], "principles": ["Ask three questions per bridge: Is input validated before the hop? Is there a timeout or retry if the next hop stalls? How does failure surface to the caller or user?", "Label each bridge with a three-word verdict: 'Clean & Loud,' 'Synchronous Gamble,' or 'Silent Drop'", "An empty catch block is the clearest signal of a Silent Drop", "A synchronous network call with no timeout is a Synchronous Gamble regardless of how rarely it fails", "The goal is a heatmap of bridges, not a fix list \u2014 surface first, solve later"], "steps": ["List every arrow (hop) in your breadcrumb trail", "For each hop, ask: Is input validated before crossing? (search for validate, schema, regex near the call)", "For each hop, ask: Is there a timeout or retry wrapper? (search for timeout, retry, backoff near the call)", "For each hop, ask: How does failure surface? (look for error return, log call, or catch block \u2014 flag empty catch blocks)", "Label each bridge: 'Clean & Loud' (validation + timeout + clear error), 'Synchronous Gamble' (no timeout/retry), or 'Silent Drop' (missing validation and empty catch)", "Compile the labels into a risk heatmap and identify which Silent Drops are on revenue-critical paths"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163007434", "title": "Taming the Genie: How to Read the Code You Just Wished Into Existence", "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z"}], "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z", "id": "three-question-bridge-drill", "core_question": "Where are the hidden failure points in this codebase \u2014 the places where data crosses a boundary and something can silently go wrong without anyone knowing?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The payment service calls the tax calculation API, and that call has no timeout \u2014 if the tax service stalls, the checkout thread blocks indefinitely. That's a Synchronous Gamble on a revenue-critical path. Worse, the response from the tax API feeds directly into the invoice generator with no schema validation and an empty catch block wrapping the whole thing \u2014 that's a Silent Drop. If the tax API returns malformed data, the invoice silently renders with zero tax and nobody gets alerted. Two bridges, both on the critical path, both need immediate attention before the holiday traffic spike.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Proxy Signal Reading", "type": "procedure", "description": "A set of four language-agnostic signals (log noise, dependency graph shape, field blast radius, and retry coverage) that reveal architectural health without reading source code logic.", "when_to_apply": ["I just inherited a large codebase and need to know where the structural risks are before I start changing anything", "My team has been using AI to generate a lot of code and I'm worried the architecture is quietly degrading", "I need hard numbers to bring to a meeting about technical debt \u2014 not just a feeling that things are messy", "I want a repeatable weekly health check I can run on a growing codebase without spending hours in the code", "I'm reviewing a repo I can't run locally and need to assess its resilience and coupling from the source alone"], "principles": ["Signals are language-free and fast \u2014 they surface patterns in minutes, not hours", "Four signals: ERROR log frequency (observability), network calls vs. retry wrappers (resilience), dependency graph cycles (coupling), and field search hit count (blast radius)", "Numbers travel better in meetings than vibes \u2014 quantify every signal", "Multiple signals singing the same song constitute strong evidence even without reading functions", "Use browser search and the project's own tooling; no terminal required for the core technique"], "steps": ["Run the app and search logs for ERROR \u2014 count frequency and note which components produce them", "Count network calls (search for fetch, axios, http.get) vs. explicit retry wrappers (search for retry, backoff) \u2014 compute the ratio", "Pull the dependency graph (using a tool like depcruise or the IDE's built-in view) and look for red cycles between boundary zones", "Pick one field from your datum safari and count how many files reference it \u2014 this is its blast radius", "Summarize all four signals in one sentence: '[N] outbound calls, [M] retries, [X] import cycles, field referenced in [Y] places \u2014 risk profile: [high/medium/low]'"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "163007434", "title": "Taming the Genie: How to Read the Code You Just Wished Into Existence", "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z"}], "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z", "id": "proxy-signal-reading", "core_question": "How healthy is this codebase's architecture really, and can I get a credible answer in minutes without reading the actual logic?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "Scanning a 60k-line Node.js repo reveals 47 outbound fetch/axios calls but only 3 retry wrappers \u2014 a 16:1 ratio that means 94% of network calls will fail hard on any transient issue. The dependency graph shows two red cycles between the auth module and the data layer, confirming tight coupling. The user's email field appears in 31 files across 4 boundary zones, so any schema change to that field is a guaranteed multi-team incident. Risk profile: high \u2014 resilience is nearly absent, coupling is structural, and blast radius on core fields is dangerously wide.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Break-It-Watch-It Runtime Fieldwork", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured method of deliberately sabotaging one component of a running application (network, database, input validation) to convert static architectural suspicions into hard runtime evidence.", "when_to_apply": ["I traced the architecture and spotted what looks like a synchronous dependency with no fallback, but I need proof it actually breaks at runtime", "Stakeholders don't believe the system is fragile because it works fine in demos \u2014 I need a concrete failure I can show them", "An AI tool generated an integration and I want to verify error handling actually exists before merging it", "The app has no observability and I want to find out what happens when a downstream service goes silent", "I can see validation code in the frontend but I'm not sure whether bad data is also being caught before it hits the database"], "principles": ["Three core experiments: network offline (graceful degradation), throttled database (synchronous bottleneck), and malformed input (validation layer placement)", "An infinite spinner with no log output is proof of both a synchronous bottleneck and weak observability simultaneously", "Silence after sabotage is as informative as an error \u2014 it means the team is flying blind", "Map runtime failures back to your breadcrumb diagram with red exclamation marks \u2014 learning by scar tissue", "Screenshot + two numbers (e.g., '0 retries, 25-second hang') is sufficient evidence for non-technical audiences"], "steps": ["Experiment A \u2014 Network Offline: Open the app in a browser, enable Offline mode in DevTools, submit the target form, observe whether the response is a graceful error banner or an infinite spinner with no logs", "Experiment B \u2014 Throttled Resource: Limit your database container CPU (e.g., docker update --cpus='.2'), submit several requests quickly, observe response times in the Network tab for timeout behavior vs. async acceptance; revert after", "Experiment C \u2014 Malformed Input: Submit invalid data (e.g., not-an-email in an email field), then check the database viewer to determine whether the bad data was stored (validation in wrong layer) or rejected (interface wall working)", "Map each failure outcome back to the specific breadcrumb hop where the break occurred", "Package evidence as a screenshot + two numbers and a sentence starting 'If X fails, Y happens\u2026'"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163007434", "title": "Taming the Genie: How to Read the Code You Just Wished Into Existence", "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z"}], "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z", "id": "break-it-watch-it-runtime-fieldwork", "core_question": "I suspect this system has hidden fragility \u2014 how do I turn architectural hunches into undeniable runtime proof before something breaks in production?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The checkout form depends on a third-party payment API, and the code shows no retry logic or timeout handling. After toggling DevTools to Offline mode and submitting an order, the UI hangs on an infinite spinner for 45 seconds with zero log output \u2014 confirming both a synchronous bottleneck and complete observability blindness. The evidence package is a screenshot showing the frozen spinner plus '0 retries, 45-second hang' \u2014 that's enough to make the case to the product lead that this integration needs a circuit breaker and fallback UX before launch.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Microscope Protocol", "type": "procedure", "description": "A trust-but-verify method for using a language model to explain small code slices, structured to minimize hallucination by constraining input to 20\u201340 lines with explicit breadcrumb context and requiring claim verification before acting.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm staring at a 30-line chunk of code I didn't write and I want to use an LLM to explain it, but I'm worried it'll confidently make things up", "An AI generated a catch block or error-handling path and I need to know if it actually handles the failure or just swallows it", "I want to verify my own reading of a small code section by cross-checking with an AI, but I need a structured way to keep the AI honest", "I'm about to act on an AI's refactor suggestion for a specific code slice and I need a verification step before I touch anything", "I keep getting plausible-sounding but subtly wrong explanations when I paste large code blocks into a chat \u2014 I need a method that constrains the input to get trustworthy output"], "principles": ["Supply only 20\u201340 lines (the slice around the tile or bridge of interest) \u2014 narrow input keeps the model honest", "Prepend one English sentence of breadcrumb context explaining where this code sits in the flow", "Ask for structured answers: (1) 2-sentence summary, (2) explicitly handled failure cases, (3) hidden global state or side effects", "Verify at least one model claim via search or runtime test before acting on any output", "If a claim fails verification, paste the offending line back and ask the model to reconcile \u2014 two iterations usually resolve truth from hallucination", "Never copy-paste a refactor suggestion blind \u2014 treat it as a draft, not gospel"], "steps": ["Identify the specific 20\u201340 line slice (tile or bridge) you want to examine", "Prepend one English context sentence: 'This code is called after [prior hop]; its job is to [stated purpose]'", "Ask three structured questions: summarize in 2 sentences, list all explicitly handled failure cases, identify hidden global state or side effects", "Read the model's output and select one specific claim to verify", "Verify via repo search, grep, or a runtime break experiment", "If claim fails, paste the contradicting line and ask the model to reconcile; repeat until verified", "Only act (or request a refactor) on verified claims; journal the finding in one sentence"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "163007434", "title": "Taming the Genie: How to Read the Code You Just Wished Into Existence", "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z"}], "date": "2025-05-07T13:03:06.569Z", "id": "ai-microscope-protocol", "core_question": "I need to understand what this specific chunk of AI-generated (or unfamiliar) code actually does, but I don't trust myself to read it correctly and I don't trust an AI to explain it without hallucinating \u2014 how do I get a reliable answer?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "A 30-line error-handling bridge wraps an API call with a try/catch, and the model claims the catch block re-raises after logging. Verification via grep reveals the catch actually swallows the exception silently \u2014 no re-raise exists. Pasting the offending line back, the model reconciles: the catch logs but returns a default value, meaning upstream callers never know the API failed. The verified finding is: this bridge contains a Silent Drop \u2014 failures are invisible to the caller, and any retry logic above this layer is dead code.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Instruction Stack Hierarchy", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A five-layer priority model that explains how ChatGPT resolves competing instructions, from immutable policy down to user prompts, so writers can predict which rules will dominate.", "when_to_apply": ["ChatGPT seems to be ignoring a rule I put in my Custom Instructions and I can't figure out why", "I'm building a Custom GPT and need to decide what goes in the system prompt versus what I leave to users' own Custom Instructions", "I'm designing an API-based prompt stack for a team and need to control which instructions users can and can't override", "My prompts are getting long and ChatGPT is losing track of earlier context \u2014 I need to figure out what's eating my token budget", "Two of my instruction layers seem to contradict each other and I need to predict which one ChatGPT will follow"], "principles": ["Policy (OpenAI global rules) is immutable and outranks everything", "System messages (Custom GPT or API) outrank personal Custom Instructions", "Custom Instructions outrank developer messages, which outrank user prompts", "Contradictions are always resolved in favor of the higher layer", "Every layer consumes tokens, reducing working memory available for content"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162928748", "title": "Write Once, Save 100 Prompts: Mastering ChatGPT's Two Secret Boxes", "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z"}], "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z", "id": "instruction-stack-hierarchy", "core_question": "Why is ChatGPT ignoring something I explicitly told it, and where should I put my instructions so they actually stick?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The user's Custom Instruction says 'always write in first person' but a Custom GPT keeps producing third-person output. This is layer 2 (system message) overriding layer 3 (Custom Instructions) \u2014 the GPT's system prompt sits higher in the stack, so it wins every time. The fix isn't rewording the Custom Instruction; it's either editing the GPT's system message to align, or accepting that this GPT will always override that personal setting. Separately, stacking verbose instructions across all five layers is burning tokens before the model even reads the user's actual request, which explains why long conversations in that GPT lose coherence faster than expected.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Frequency \u00d7 Volatility Decision Grid", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A 2\u00d72 matrix using task frequency and rule volatility to decide whether to use Custom Instructions, a reusable giant prompt, or blank defaults for a given workflow.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep tweaking my Custom Instructions because they help for one task but mess up outputs for another", "I'm setting up ChatGPT for a new role and trying to figure out what goes in Custom Instructions versus what I paste each time", "My Custom Instructions are getting long and I suspect half the rules are only relevant to one type of task", "I have a reusable prompt template that works great but I'm wondering if I should just move it into Custom Instructions to save time", "I use the same AI account for very different workflows and the default persona keeps clashing with some of them"], "principles": ["High-frequency + low-volatility tasks justify Custom Instructions", "High-frequency + high-volatility tasks are better served by giant reusable task prompts", "Low-frequency tasks of any volatility should use bespoke prompts with blank defaults", "If a rule would ever be wrong for another task in the same account, keep it out of Custom Instructions", "If the deliverable represents less than ~50% of monthly usage, prefer a reusable task-prompt"], "steps": ["Ask: Will this rule ever be wrong for another task I run from this account? If yes, exclude from Custom Instructions.", "Ask: Does this deliverable represent more than ~50% of my ChatGPT usage this month? If yes, consider Custom Instructions; if no, use a reusable task-prompt.", "If both answers are no, use a blank panel and a purpose-built prompt whenever needed."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162928748", "title": "Write Once, Save 100 Prompts: Mastering ChatGPT's Two Secret Boxes", "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z"}], "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z", "id": "frequency-volatility-decision-grid", "core_question": "Should this instruction live permanently in my AI system's defaults, or am I poisoning every other task by baking in a rule that only belongs in some conversations?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The user writes marketing copy and code reviews from the same ChatGPT account. 'Always write in a conversational, second-person tone' passes the frequency test for marketing but would be wrong for every code review \u2014 so it fails the volatility check and must stay out of Custom Instructions. Marketing copy is roughly 60% of monthly usage, so a reusable giant prompt with tone, audience, and format rules is the right container. Code review prompts get their own separate reusable template. The Custom Instructions panel holds only universally true facts: the user's name, company, and preference for concise responses.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Lean Evergreen Instruction Audit", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A set of criteria and a three-probe test for validating that Custom Instructions are durable, non-contaminating, and token-efficient before deploying them.", "when_to_apply": ["I just wrote new Custom Instructions and want to stress-test them before they go live across all my chats", "My ChatGPT responses in casual conversations keep including jargon, disclaimers, or a professional persona that doesn't belong there", "My Custom Instructions have grown over time and I'm worried they're eating into my context window for long documents", "I changed roles or projects and need to figure out which parts of my existing instructions are stale or contradictory", "I want to make sure specialist rules only fire in the right conversations and don't bleed into everything else"], "principles": ["Every rule must pass the test: 'Will this still be correct six months from now and for 90% of conversations I start?'", "Evergreen content fits into four buckets: tone/voice, empathy/structure frameworks, standing disclaimers, and jargon translation maps", "Use imperative style (crisp verbs), not narrative memoir style", "Stay under 600 characters (~120 tokens) per box", "Gate specialist rules with explicit IF-conditions to prevent bleed into unrelated chats", "Keep a dated external copy and archive old versions when updating"], "steps": ["Contradiction scan: Check whether any line could conflict with another or with future tasks.", "Token audit: Paste a long source document into a blank chat and verify it still fits.", "Role-play probe: Ask an unrelated casual question; if specialist tone or disclaimers appear, trim or gate the offending rule."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162928748", "title": "Write Once, Save 100 Prompts: Mastering ChatGPT's Two Secret Boxes", "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z"}], "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z", "id": "lean-evergreen-instruction-audit", "core_question": "Are my Custom Instructions actually helping every conversation, or are they silently polluting outputs and wasting tokens on rules that don't belong there?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The instruction set scores poorly on the role-play probe: asking 'What's a good recipe for banana bread?' triggers a response that opens with 'As a senior data analyst...' and includes a liability disclaimer. The offending lines are a role preamble and a standing legal caveat that lack IF-conditions. Both need gating \u2014 the role preamble should fire only when the query involves data or analysis, and the disclaimer only when the response touches regulated advice. After gating and trimming narrative filler ('I am someone who always strives to...') down to imperative verbs ('Use plain language. Flag assumptions.'), the instruction block drops from 840 characters to 480, reclaiming ~70 tokens of context window per conversation.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Cross-Contamination Containment Ladder", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A three-level escalation of isolation strategies\u2014conditional clause, separate thread, dedicated Custom GPT\u2014for preventing specialist Custom Instructions from bleeding into unrelated conversations.", "when_to_apply": ["My legal disclaimer language keeps showing up when I'm just trying to brainstorm product ideas in ChatGPT", "I manage three different brand voices from one ChatGPT account and the tones bleed together", "I'm sharing a Custom GPT with my team and need to make sure they can't accidentally override the guardrails", "I added specialist instructions for code review but now every conversation sounds like a code review", "Trying to decide whether I need a whole separate Custom GPT or can just fix my existing Custom Instructions"], "principles": ["Choose the lightest containment level that works for the workflow", "Conditional clauses (IF-THEN trigger words) provide in-thread isolation at minimal overhead", "Separate threads prevent carryover between sessions without requiring new infrastructure", "Dedicated Custom GPTs move rules to the System layer, making them immune to user-prompt overrides", "System layer outranks Custom Instructions, so Custom GPT guardrails survive downstream users"], "steps": ["Level 1 \u2014 Conditional clause: Add an IF-AND-ONLY-IF trigger word gate inside Custom Instructions to quarantine specialist rules.", "Level 2 \u2014 Separate thread: Open a fresh conversation for specialist work, keeping general chats uncontaminated.", "Level 3 \u2014 Dedicated Custom GPT: Move specialist guidance into the System layer of a named Custom GPT; use standard chat for everything else."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162928748", "title": "Write Once, Save 100 Prompts: Mastering ChatGPT's Two Secret Boxes", "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z"}], "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z", "id": "cross-contamination-containment-ladder", "core_question": "My Custom Instructions keep leaking into conversations where they don't belong \u2014 how much isolation do I actually need to keep specialist behaviors contained without overcomplicating my setup?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "This is a Level 1 problem, not a Level 3. The user's PRD formatting keeps appearing in casual brainstorming chats, but the specialist behavior is only triggered by specific task types. Adding a conditional clause \u2014 'Apply PRD formatting IF AND ONLY IF the user says \"draft PRD\"' \u2014 quarantines the behavior inside the same Custom Instructions without the overhead of a separate GPT. Escalating to a dedicated Custom GPT would be overkill here because there's no team sharing involved and no adversarial override risk.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Five-Minute CI Smoke-Test", "type": "procedure", "description": "A seven-step validation routine to confirm that newly written Custom Instructions improve target tasks, avoid tone contamination, stay within token budgets, and respect policy boundaries.", "when_to_apply": ["I just rewrote my ChatGPT Custom Instructions and want to make sure they actually do what I think before I rely on them", "My ChatGPT responses suddenly feel off \u2014 weird tone or structure showing up in casual conversations that didn't used to be there", "I'm rolling out a Custom Instruction template to my team and need to validate it works before sharing", "I trimmed my Custom Instructions to fit the character limit and I'm not sure what I lost", "It's been a month or more since I last looked at my Custom Instructions and I need to check if they're still accurate"], "principles": ["Test in a fresh conversation to isolate instruction effects from prior context", "Always run both a domain probe (core deliverable) and a contamination probe (unrelated query)", "Token budget should be audited, not assumed", "Policy edge-cases must be explicitly tested, not assumed to be safe", "A maintenance reminder must be scheduled as part of the setup, not left to memory"], "steps": ["Paste and personalise the template: Copy the appropriate Box 1 and Box 2 text, replace all placeholders with real data, and save.", "Spin up a disposable test chat: Start a brand-new conversation to eliminate prior context effects.", "Run the domain probe: Request the deliverable you create most often; verify structure, disclaimers, and tone match expectations.", "Run the contamination probe: Ask a completely unrelated casual question; check whether any specialist structure or tone bleeds through.", "Audit the token budget: Count combined tokens for both boxes; trim if significantly over ~150 tokens combined.", "Check policy edge-cases: Ask something near a compliance boundary; confirm the reply follows your defaults and respects higher-level policy.", "Schedule a maintenance reminder: Add a calendar event 30 days out to review whether instructions are still accurate."], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162928748", "title": "Write Once, Save 100 Prompts: Mastering ChatGPT's Two Secret Boxes", "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z"}], "date": "2025-05-06T13:01:19.687Z", "id": "five-minute-ci-smoke-test", "core_question": "I just wrote or updated my Custom Instructions \u2014 how do I know they're actually working the way I intend without silently breaking other conversations?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The domain probe came back clean \u2014 the weekly status report had the right structure, included the requested disclaimers, and matched the concise executive tone specified in Box 2. But the contamination probe failed: when asked 'what's a good recipe for banana bread?' the response came back in bullet-point format with a liability disclaimer appended, which means the specialist framing is bleeding into general conversation. The fix is narrowing the Box 1 scope with an explicit domain gate ('Apply the following defaults only when discussing [project management] topics'). Token audit also flagged a problem \u2014 the two boxes combined hit 210 tokens, roughly 40% over the ~150-token sweet spot, which explains why the tail-end instructions about citation format are being silently dropped.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Seven-Section Prompt Anatomy", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequentially ordered, seven-part scaffold for constructing prompts that produce reliable, consistent outputs from any transformer-based language model, grounded in how token attention and positional encoding actually work.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a prompt that works in testing but produces wildly different outputs when other people use it or when I change models", "I'm building a prompt from scratch and want a reliable scaffold instead of trial-and-error iteration", "My prompt is long and complex but the model keeps ignoring constraints I buried in the middle", "I need to design a production prompt that resists prompt injection and survives multi-turn conversations without drifting", "I'm not sure what order to put the sections of my prompt in and whether the ordering actually matters"], "principles": ["Structure beats charm: lock down the scaffold before optimizing tone, persona, or chain-of-thought flourishes", "Early tokens steer the search space\u2014put immovable rules and guard-rails first", "Recency beats rank when rules collide, so the output format specification belongs dead last as a soft stop-token", "Every section is load-bearing; omitting one invites probabilistic improv from the model", "Order matters because tokens flow left-to-right and later text cannot rewrite earlier keys", "Labels on each section can be adjusted (e.g., 'Context' vs. 'Policy') to nudge the model toward different regions of its latent space", "In long chat sessions, pin non-negotiables in a system/pinned message and recap essentials every ~15 messages to prevent context drift"], "steps": ["1. Context / Background \u2014 Establish the situation, audience, and relevant background so the model knows what to attend to before anything else", "2. Role / Persona \u2014 Assign a specific identity or expertise lens the model should adopt", "3. Task Statement \u2014 State the concrete action or deliverable required", "4. Constraints & Style Rules \u2014 Specify hard limits (length, format, tone, what to avoid) before examples so demos cannot override them", "5. Exemplars / Few-Shot Examples \u2014 Provide input-output demonstration pairs; place most-relevant examples closest to the task to exploit recency bias in attention", "6. Output Specification \u2014 Define the exact output structure, schema, or sentinel pattern; placing this last collapses the decoder's next-token distribution around legal continuations and acts as a soft stop-token", "7. Fallback / Error Handling \u2014 Give the model a clearly labeled escape hatch for edge cases (e.g., missing data, policy conflicts)"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162688601", "title": "The Universal Anatomy of the Prompt\u2014How and Why a Prompt Works and How to Make it Work for You", "date": "2025-05-02T14:38:15.344Z"}], "date": "2025-05-02T14:38:15.344Z", "id": "seven-section-prompt-anatomy", "core_question": "Why does my prompt work sometimes and fail other times, and how do I build one that produces reliable outputs instead of hoping the model guesses what I want?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The draft prompt jumps straight into examples, then mentions the role, then tacks on constraints at the end \u2014 which explains the inconsistency. Context and constraints need to move above the examples so early tokens lock the attention space before demonstrations anchor specific patterns. The output specification ('Return valid JSON with these three keys') belongs dead last to collapse the decoder onto legal continuations. Reordering these three sections alone \u2014 context up, constraints before examples, format last \u2014 will eliminate most of the formatting failures without changing a single word of content.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Chat Persistence Guardrails", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A set of structured signals and countermeasures for detecting and recovering from prompt drift, context truncation, and prompt injection attacks during extended chat sessions.", "when_to_apply": ["My chatbot was perfectly following its persona and formatting, but after a long conversation it started ignoring both", "I'm building a public-facing AI assistant and need to defend against users trying to override the system prompt mid-conversation", "How do I keep my AI's output format consistent across a 50+ message thread without restarting the session?", "The model suddenly started refusing requests it was handling fine earlier in the same conversation", "I need a strategy for deciding when to recap instructions versus when to just start a fresh session"], "principles": ["Pin non-negotiables (role, task, output pattern) in a system or pinned message area so they never age out of the context window", "Recap, don't repeat: every ~15 messages send a concise restatement of only the essential constraints", "Treat sudden format erosion as an early warning signal that core constraints have scrolled away", "Place compliance checks and fallback rules after the output mould so they gain recency weight against injection attempts", "Frame replies around your guardrails rather than an attacker's request to re-assert instruction rank", "Add a self-critique compliance check (e.g., 'Before finalizing, verify your answer respects the system prompt') to reduce policy violations", "Monitor for misbehavior at scale with automated detection; treat prompt injection defense as an ongoing arms race requiring regular prompt updates", "A new session (clearing the KV-cache) is the only guaranteed remedy when a chat goes fully off-rails"], "steps": ["1. Detect drift: watch for format erosion, dropped output sentinels, or lapsed persona as signals that constraints have scrolled out", "2. Pin immovable rules: place role, task, and output pattern in a system/pinned message before the conversation starts", "3. Recap essentials every ~15 messages with a one-sentence reminder restating only the non-negotiables", "4. Counter injection: pre-load a rebuttal in the style rules section anticipating likely override attempts; place a REFUSED_POLICY fallback after the output mould", "5. Reset if necessary: open a new session to fully clear context when drift is unrecoverable"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "162688601", "title": "The Universal Anatomy of the Prompt\u2014How and Why a Prompt Works and How to Make it Work for You", "date": "2025-05-02T14:38:15.344Z"}], "date": "2025-05-02T14:38:15.344Z", "id": "chat-persistence-guardrails", "core_question": "My AI was following instructions perfectly twenty messages ago \u2014 why is it drifting now, and how do I get it back without starting over?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The model just returned a plain paragraph instead of the structured JSON it had been producing for the last 30 turns \u2014 that's format erosion, which means the original output-pattern constraint has likely scrolled out of the active context window. The fix isn't to repeat the entire system prompt; it's a one-sentence recap pinning only the non-negotiables ('All responses must be valid JSON matching the schema defined at turn 1') placed right before the next user query so it gains recency weight. If the model also started hedging with default refusal language it didn't use before, that's a second signal \u2014 the persona constraint is gone too, and both need recapping together.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Chain-of-Refinement (CoR) Prompting", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured three-pass prompting method that directs reasoning models to draft, critique their own output field-by-field, and refine only the flagged sections within a fixed schema.", "when_to_apply": ["I need the model to produce a complete PRD, RCA, or OKR doc and I want it to self-edit against specific quality criteria before I even look at it", "My structured artifact keeps coming back with vague language and missing baselines no matter how detailed my prompt is", "I'm using a reasoning model like o1 or Claude and want to direct its chain-of-thought into a visible draft\u2192critique\u2192refine loop rather than letting it reason opaquely", "I want to see exactly which fields the model flagged as weak and why, so I can trust the parts it didn't change", "The output needs to land in a fixed schema (table, template, slide deck) and I can't afford freeform prose that requires reformatting"], "principles": ["Structure the input as named fields or table columns ('tokenizable thinking') so the model can reason over discrete units rather than prose", "Provide ground-truth source material (meeting notes, logs, quotes) with citation anchors so critiques are evidence-based", "Lock the output schema to the exact format the artifact will be consumed in before running the loop", "Use specific, testable critique instructions ('flag any metric lacking a baseline') rather than vague verbs ('improve', 'optimize')", "Curate inputs tightly\u2014feed only the cleanest, most relevant slice of reality to avoid wasting critique cycles on obvious mess", "Frame the success condition in a single guiding sentence before pressing send; the loop amplifies clarity, it cannot conjure it"], "steps": ["THINK: Draft the artifact filling every field in the schema once, producing a first-pass output", "ANALYSIS: For each section or field, record key assumptions made and why the wording or metric fits the input data", "CRITIQUE: Evaluate the draft field-by-field against specific, enumerable tests (vague goals, missing metrics, unsupported claims, etc.) and enumerate weaknesses", "REFINE: Rewrite only the sections that failed the critique, leaving solid parts untouched, within the original schema and word limit", "Output all four blocks exactly as labeled\u2014do not hide the chain of thought"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162519597", "title": "Don't Just Think, Focus\u2014Unlock the Magic of Reasoning Models with Chain-of-Refinement Prompting", "date": "2025-04-30T13:03:13.226Z"}], "date": "2025-04-30T13:03:13.226Z", "id": "chain-of-refinement-cor-prompting", "core_question": "How do I get a reasoning model to produce a polished structured artifact in one shot instead of ping-ponging drafts back and forth manually?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "After running CoR on a quarterly OKR document, the THINK pass filled all seven fields but the CRITIQUE pass flagged two key results as untestable \u2014 KR2 said 'improve onboarding experience' with no baseline metric, and KR5 cited a conversion lift without specifying the measurement window or cohort. The REFINE pass rewrote only those two fields: KR2 became 'reduce median time-to-first-value from 4.2 days to 2.5 days by Q3' anchored to last quarter's product analytics, and KR5 pinned the conversion lift to new-signup cohorts measured over a 30-day window. The other five fields passed critique and were left untouched, preserving the original schema.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Tokenizable Thinking", "type": "distinction", "description": "A distinction between tasks whose content is already broken into named, discrete fields (tokenizable) versus prose walls\u2014only tokenizable tasks enable reliable self-critique and refinement loops because each field becomes an inspectable unit.", "when_to_apply": ["My think-critique-refine loop keeps producing vague suggestions like 'make it clearer' instead of specific improvements", "I'm about to set up a multi-pass reasoning prompt and need to decide how to structure the input", "I have messy meeting notes or a long document I need to convert into something an LLM can systematically critique", "The model does fine generating content but falls apart when I ask it to self-evaluate and fix its own output", "I'm trying to figure out whether a particular task is even suitable for an iterative refinement loop with an LLM"], "principles": ["Named fields (metric, baseline, owner, hypothesis) act as discrete containers the model can grab, inspect, and improve without losing context", "Structured inputs allow the model to pinpoint gaps rather than wave at 'overall clarity'", "A schema or example table should be included in every prompt to anchor the model's reasoning", "Free-form prose forces the model to guess what matters, producing unreliable critiques"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "162519597", "title": "Don't Just Think, Focus\u2014Unlock the Magic of Reasoning Models with Chain-of-Refinement Prompting", "date": "2025-04-30T13:03:13.226Z"}], "date": "2025-04-30T13:03:13.226Z", "id": "tokenizable-thinking", "core_question": "Why does my AI keep producing vague, unhelpful critiques when I ask it to review and improve its own work?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The critique loop is failing because the input is a prose wall \u2014 a two-paragraph project update with goals, metrics, owners, and timelines all blended together. The model can't isolate what to critique, so it produces generic feedback like 'consider adding more detail.' This is a tokenizability problem, not a model capability problem. Once the update is decomposed into named fields \u2014 Objective, Key Metric, Baseline, Target, Owner, Hypothesis \u2014 each field becomes an inspectable unit the model can grab and evaluate independently: 'Baseline is missing for Key Metric; Owner is listed but Hypothesis is absent, meaning the model can't assess whether the metric is the right one.' The refinement loop only works when the content is already broken into discrete, named containers.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Eval Quadrant Map", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A 2\u00d72 matrix that classifies AI use cases by output format (structured vs. free-form) and business risk level (high vs. low) to prescribe the right evaluation strategy for each combination.", "when_to_apply": ["We have six different AI features in the pipeline and I need to figure out which ones need heavy eval investment versus quick smoke tests", "My AI system produces open-ended text responses and I'm not sure whether automated checks or human review is the right eval approach", "Legal is asking whether they need to be involved in reviewing our AI outputs before launch", "We're building both a structured data extraction tool and a free-text generation feature and want to know if they need the same eval process", "I need to explain to a non-technical executive why some AI deployments need more rigorous testing than others"], "principles": ["Output format (structured vs. free-form) determines the testing mechanism\u2014structured outputs use deterministic gates like regex; free-form outputs require semantic or rubric-based scoring", "Business risk level determines the threshold stringency and stakeholder involvement required", "Top-row quadrants (high risk) require Legal and Data Security involvement regardless of output type", "Bottom-row quadrants (low risk) allow faster, lighter-weight evaluation processes", "Every quadrant still requires some form of eval\u2014'lower risk' does not mean 'no risk'"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162354891", "title": "You \"Otter\" Do AI Evals\u2014Here's What They Are, How to Get Started, and How Not to Fail", "date": "2025-04-29T13:02:09.403Z"}], "date": "2025-04-29T13:02:09.403Z", "id": "eval-quadrant-map", "core_question": "How much eval rigor does this AI use case actually need, and what kind of testing fits the output it produces?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The customer-facing chatbot generates free-form natural language and operates in a regulated healthcare context \u2014 that places it in the high-risk, free-form quadrant. Deterministic regex checks won't work here; the eval strategy needs rubric-based semantic scoring with human review, and Legal and Data Security must sign off on the rubric criteria and pass/fail thresholds before deployment. Meanwhile, the internal tool that extracts structured JSON fields from invoices sits in the low-risk, structured quadrant \u2014 simple deterministic gates (schema validation, field-level regex) are sufficient, and the team can ship with a lighter approval process.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Eval-Adventure Four-Stop Framework", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequential tour through four distinct AI evaluation types\u2014Extraction Alley, Latency & Cost Bypass, RAG Boulevard, and Compliance Crossing\u2014each matched to a specific risk profile with concrete metrics, thresholds, and stakeholder actions.", "when_to_apply": ["We have multiple AI features shipping at once \u2014 some extract structured data, some generate free-text \u2014 and we need different eval strategies for each", "Our AI pipeline doesn't have any automated quality gates in CI/CD yet and we need to stand something up quickly", "Leadership is asking what 'safe to deploy' means in quantitative terms for our AI product and we don't have defined thresholds", "We're building a RAG system and need to decide what to measure before we call the answers trustworthy enough to ship", "Our AI agent chains multiple steps together and we're not sure how to evaluate the compounding risk across the full trace"], "principles": ["Match the eval type to the output format and risk profile\u2014don't apply a one-size-fits-all metric", "Each eval should be automatable and blockable in CI/CD (block merges if thresholds are breached)", "Define quantitative go/no-go thresholds before deployment, not after incidents", "Both technical builders and non-technical leaders have distinct, defined responsibilities within each eval type", "Agentic systems require combinatorial risk assessment\u2014risks multiply rather than add across multi-step chains", "Start with the smallest viable eval gate (even a four-line regex) rather than waiting for a comprehensive benchmark suite"], "steps": ["Identify your quadrant using the Eval Quadrant Map (output type \u00d7 business risk level)", "Stop 1 \u2014 Extraction Alley (structured output, high risk): Sample 100 real or synthetic examples, define regex or schema rules for critical fields, set a pass-rate threshold (e.g., 99%), and block deploys below that threshold", "Stop 2 \u2014 Latency & Cost Bypass (structured output, lower risk): Snapshot 50\u2013100 top-traffic prompts nightly, record p95 latency and cost-per-call, and block merges if either exceeds defined SLA or cost ceiling", "Stop 3 \u2014 RAG Boulevard (free-form output, moderate risk): Curate 50\u2013100 real user questions with ground-truth answers, score faithfulness and citation precision using a semantic eval tool, and block deploys if faithfulness or context precision fall below threshold", "Stop 4 \u2014 Compliance Crossing (free-form output, high legal/reputational risk): Compile a red-team prompt set, score responses against a policy rubric for safety and disallowed content, and block deploys if safety score or harmful content threshold is breached", "Stop 5 (bonus) \u2014 Agentic Outskirts (multi-turn agents): Log full execution traces, define macro success criteria and micro step safety/cost rules, and fail runs that miss macro success rate or violate any micro policy or cost cap"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162354891", "title": "You \"Otter\" Do AI Evals\u2014Here's What They Are, How to Get Started, and How Not to Fail", "date": "2025-04-29T13:02:09.403Z"}], "date": "2025-04-29T13:02:09.403Z", "id": "eval-adventure-four-stop-framework", "core_question": "We're deploying AI systems with different output types and risk profiles \u2014 how do I know which evaluation approach to apply where, and what thresholds make it safe to ship?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The team's RAG-based customer support bot falls into Stop 3 \u2014 free-form output with moderate risk. After curating 75 real user questions with verified ground-truth answers, faithfulness scores came back at 0.81 and citation precision at 0.74. Both sit below the 0.85 threshold, which means this merge is blocked. The gap is driven by the retriever surfacing tangentially related chunks rather than exact policy passages \u2014 fixing the chunking strategy is the lever, not prompt tuning. Meanwhile, the invoice extraction pipeline is a Stop 1 problem: structured output, high downstream financial risk. A four-line regex gate on required fields (invoice number, total, currency) catches the 2% of outputs where the model hallucinates a currency code, and that alone prevents corrupted data from hitting the ERP system.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four-Block Prompt Specification", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured template for converting repetitive tasks into autonomous agent workflows using four mandatory blocks: Purpose, Instructions, Reference, and Output, plus optional MODE/EFFORT and tool whitelist directives.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a recurring task \u2014 pulling data, scanning sources, compiling a report \u2014 that I do the same way every time and want to hand to an AI agent", "My prompt keeps producing inconsistent or incomplete results and I need a repeatable structure that makes the output reliable without babysitting", "I want to automate a monitoring or research workflow in ChatGPT without writing code or using a separate automation platform", "I'm trying to figure out which parts of my weekly routine are actually delegatable to an LLM versus which require my judgment", "I built a prompt that worked once but breaks when I reuse it \u2014 I need a durable template I can clone across multiple workflows"], "principles": ["A prompt written as a clear specification becomes a reusable workflow, not just a one-time request", "The four blocks (Purpose, Instructions, Reference, Output) provide the minimum structure for a durable agent", "Adding MODE and EFFORT directives controls the depth of reasoning the model applies", "Include a tool whitelist explicitly so the model invokes the right capabilities reliably", "Build in verification steps (double-sourcing, triple-verification) so output can be trusted without babysitting", "Add a short reflection output (e.g., 30-word summary) to make agent reasoning auditable", "Start with one agent, prove reliability over seven days, then clone the pattern for the next pain-point"], "steps": ["Identify a recurring task that begins with 'Gather\u2026', 'Check\u2026', 'Compare\u2026', or 'Remind\u2026' and costs at least an hour per week", "Write the PURPOSE block: one sentence stating what the workflow produces and why", "Write the INSTRUCTIONS block: enumerate the exact steps the model should take, including schedule, filters, sourcing rules, and edge-case handling", "Write the REFERENCE block: paste or link any inputs the model needs (backlog items, watch-list, ICP definition, etc.)", "Write the OUTPUT block: specify the exact format, file-naming convention, and fallback message when no qualifying data exists", "Prepend MODE (e.g., 'agentic') and EFFORT (e.g., 'standard' or 'deep') directives and a tool whitelist", "Paste the complete prompt into a fresh chat with an advanced reasoning model, press Enter, and observe the first run", "For seven days, log minutes not spent on the chore and note decisions that happened sooner due to the agent's output", "After seven days of consistent performance, clone the four-block structure for the next high-friction task"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162092354", "title": "Turn ChatGPT into a Sushi Conveyor Belt: 5 Prompts to Automate Workflows that (Seriously) Save Hours", "date": "2025-04-25T13:01:07.736Z"}], "date": "2025-04-25T13:01:07.736Z", "id": "four-block-prompt-specification", "core_question": "I keep doing the same research/monitoring/compilation task every week \u2014 how do I write a prompt that turns it into a reliable, hands-off agent workflow I can trust?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "A founder spends 90 minutes every Monday scanning competitor blogs, changelogs, and social accounts for product updates. The PURPOSE block becomes: 'Produce a weekly competitive intelligence brief covering product launches, pricing changes, and positioning shifts across five named competitors.' The INSTRUCTIONS block specifies: check each competitor's blog, changelog, and Twitter/LinkedIn for the prior seven days; flag only items that affect our ICP's buying criteria; double-source any pricing claim against a second public page. The OUTPUT block mandates a markdown table with columns for competitor, change type, source URL, and a 30-word 'so what' per row, plus a fallback line reading 'No qualifying updates found for [Competitor]' when a competitor had no activity. Prepending MODE: agentic, EFFORT: standard, and a tool whitelist of web_search and web_browse turns this into a self-running Monday morning brief that replaces the founder's manual scan entirely.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Friction-to-Agent Recognition Pattern", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured habit for scanning your own work calendar and task list to identify which repetitive tasks are candidates for autonomous prompt-workflows, using linguistic and behavioral signals as triggers.", "when_to_apply": ["I just finished my weekly review and realized half my hours went to repetitive information-gathering that didn't require real thinking", "A teammate keeps spending hours on 'check this' and 'compare that' tasks and I want to figure out which ones an AI workflow could handle", "We have a recurring sync meeting that exists only because someone needs to manually collect and report status updates", "I know AI agents could save me time but I don't know which of my actual tasks are realistic candidates versus ones that need too much judgment", "I've done the same task identically three weeks in a row and I'm starting to resent it"], "principles": ["Any task beginning with 'Gather\u2026', 'Check\u2026', 'Compare\u2026', or 'Remind\u2026' is a strong candidate for automation", "Repetitive tasks that consume attention but not judgment are the highest-value targets", "The ability to notice friction and translate it into a living prompt is a competitive advantage, not just a productivity tactic", "Reliability of one agent beats volume of many; stabilize before expanding"], "steps": ["Run one agent today on the task that stings most", "For seven days, log minutes not spent on the chore and note decisions that happened sooner because the agent's note landed unprompted", "At week's end, scan the previous week's meetings and to-dos for items beginning with 'Gather\u2026', 'Check\u2026', 'Compare\u2026', or 'Remind\u2026'", "Flag each identified item as a workflow candidate", "For each candidate, write the four-block spec and deploy one at a time"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "162092354", "title": "Turn ChatGPT into a Sushi Conveyor Belt: 5 Prompts to Automate Workflows that (Seriously) Save Hours", "date": "2025-04-25T13:01:07.736Z"}], "date": "2025-04-25T13:01:07.736Z", "id": "friction-to-agent-recognition-pattern", "core_question": "Which of the tasks eating my calendar alive are actually just repetitive friction I could hand to an AI agent \u2014 and how do I spot them reliably instead of guessing?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "Scanning last week's to-do list reveals three items starting with 'Check\u2026' (check vendor pricing updates, check whether Q3 data landed in the dashboard, check team PTO conflicts for the offsite) and one 'Gather\u2026' (gather competitor feature announcements). None of these required judgment \u2014 they consumed attention purely to produce an information payload someone else then acted on. The vendor pricing check stings most: it took 40 minutes Tuesday and delayed a procurement decision by a day. That's the first agent to deploy. The four-block spec writes itself because the inputs, logic, and output destination are already obvious from how the task was done manually.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four-Block Blueprint with MODE/EFFORT Meta-Switches", "type": "procedure", "description": "A structured prompt architecture for reasoning models that organizes every prompt into four semantic blocks (Purpose, Instructions, Reference, Output) plus two explicit toggles (MODE and EFFORT) that control the model's reasoning depth and response orientation.", "when_to_apply": ["I keep getting action-oriented bullet points from my reasoning model when I actually need it to think through a problem exploratorily", "I'm building prompt templates that multiple team members will reuse and I need consistent output quality regardless of who fills them in", "My agentic workflow prompt works sometimes but the model randomly stops using tools or goes off-script", "I need to control how much reasoning depth the model applies because some tasks are quick lookups and others are high-stakes analyses", "I'm prompting o3 or o4-mini and the outputs feel misaligned with my intent despite detailed instructions"], "principles": ["Configuration beats persuasion: explicit structural flags outperform elaborate prose requests", "Set MODE first (reflection / action / agentic) to control whether the model explores or executes", "Set EFFORT level (quick / standard / deep) to control reasoning depth and latency trade-off", "Purpose block states the one-sentence goal and hosts the two meta-switches", "Instructions block defines behavior, guardrails, tool scope, and tone", "Reference block supplies all raw material (documents, data, links, context)", "Output block specifies the exact return format (table, essay, JSON, etc.)", "Deeper effort cues (e.g., 'three deliberate passes') produce measurably more thorough outputs than shallower ones", "Treat effort as a budget, not a moral imperative \u2014 match depth to the stakes of the decision"], "steps": ["Open the prompt with the two meta-switches: 'MODE: [reflection | action | agentic]' and 'EFFORT: [quick | standard | deep]'", "Write a one-sentence Purpose statement explaining why you are making this request", "Write an Instructions block specifying behavioral rules, constraints, tool permissions, and tone requirements", "Write a Reference block listing all input material the model needs (files, numbers, links, background facts)", "Write an Output block defining the exact format, length, and structure you expect in return", "Review: confirm MODE matches your intent (exploring vs. deciding vs. automating) before sending"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "162009941", "title": "Your Prompt Is the Product: Working with o3 & o4-mini-high in the Agentic Era", "date": "2025-04-24T13:02:48.446Z"}], "date": "2025-04-24T13:02:48.446Z", "id": "four-block-blueprint-with-mode-effort-meta-switches", "core_question": "Why does the same model give me brilliant output sometimes and garbage other times \u2014 is there a reliable structure that controls what I get back?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The user's prompt is asking o3 to analyze a competitive landscape, but the output keeps collapsing into a generic bullet list of action items. The problem is a missing MODE signal \u2014 the model defaults to action orientation when the user actually needs reflective exploration first. Setting MODE: reflection and EFFORT: deep at the top of the prompt, followed by a clear Purpose ('Map the competitive dynamics before recommending a response'), an Instructions block constraining the model to analytical rather than prescriptive language, a Reference block with the three competitor filings, and an Output block requesting a narrative memo with embedded reasoning \u2014 this structural change alone shifts the output from shallow recommendations to layered strategic analysis. The model wasn't underperforming; it was misconfigured.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Buy-In Checkpoint", "type": "procedure", "description": "A two-step framing ritual at the start of any substantive prompt that surfaces and stress-tests the initial problem frame before the model begins working, preventing costly mid-conversation pivots caused by frame anchoring.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm about to ask the model to write a long analysis or strategy document and I want to make sure we're solving the right problem first", "Last time I prompted this way, the model gave me a beautifully reasoned answer to the wrong question", "I have a working hypothesis but I'm not sure it's the strongest framing \u2014 I want the model to pressure-test it before building on it", "I'm about to commit significant token budget to a complex reasoning chain and a mid-conversation pivot would waste all of it", "I'm working with a reasoning model like o3 that tends to lock onto its initial frame and resist correction later in the conversation"], "principles": ["State your tentative frame explicitly in one sentence before asking the model to work with it", "Explicitly invite the model to critique or replace the frame before proceeding ('Do you buy this framing? If not, propose a better lens')", "Only proceed to the main task after the frame has been affirmed or upgraded", "When a mid-conversation pivot is unavoidable, use an explicit break signal ('Discard the previous frame. Treat the following as the new premise') or start a new conversation", "The checkpoint sharpens your own thinking even when the model agrees with your original frame"], "steps": ["State your provisional frame in one sentence at the top of the prompt", "Ask the model explicitly: 'Do you accept this framing? If not, propose a stronger alternative before we continue'", "Evaluate the model's response: if it offers a sharper frame, adopt it; if it confirms yours, proceed with shared alignment", "Only then issue the main task instructions"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "162009941", "title": "Your Prompt Is the Product: Working with o3 & o4-mini-high in the Agentic Era", "date": "2025-04-24T13:02:48.446Z"}], "date": "2025-04-24T13:02:48.446Z", "id": "buy-in-checkpoint", "core_question": "Am I about to spend a long conversation building on a premise that might be wrong, and how do I catch that before the model anchors on it?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The provisional frame was 'our churn problem is a pricing problem.' Before diving into pricing analysis, the model pushed back: the churn spike correlates with the onboarding redesign three months ago, not the price increase \u2014 customers who complete onboarding retain at the same rate regardless of plan. The real frame is an activation problem, not a pricing problem. Without the checkpoint, the entire analysis would have optimized the wrong variable and the pricing recommendations would have been internally coherent but strategically useless.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Relationship Design Interaction Typology", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A five-category classification system that maps distinct human-AI and AI-AI interaction pairings, each with defined primary modes, design levers, failure modes, core patterns, and success metrics.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm building an AI feature and can't tell if it should feel like a tool, a chatbot, or an autonomous agent", "Our multi-agent system works individually but produces incoherent behavior when agents interact \u2014 I need coordination protocols", "We're tracking task completion and CSAT for our AI assistant but users still don't trust it and I don't know what metrics actually matter", "I need to define what transparency, control, and failure recovery should look like for an agentic product that acts on behalf of users", "Our AI product UX was designed as a chat interface but it's evolving toward autonomous action and the design patterns are breaking"], "principles": ["Each interaction pairing (Human\u2194Software, Human\u2194LLM, Human\u2194Agent, Agent\u2194Agent, Agent\u2194Software) has fundamentally different design obligations", "Move from designing interfaces to designing relationships \u2014 prioritizing reciprocity over control, transparency over opacity, and emergent meaning over fixed outcomes", "Each pairing requires its own design levers, failure mode catalog, and success metrics rather than applying universal UX heuristics", "Human\u2194Agent relationships require longitudinal, relational metrics (trust, behavioral alignment, friction recovery) rather than simple task-completion metrics", "As interaction moves toward greater agent autonomy, auditability, kill-switches, and repair rituals become core design requirements", "Agent\u2194Agent design is infrastructure design \u2014 protocols govern emergent collective behavior, not just individual task execution"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161923507", "title": "Stop Designing AI Chatbots, Start Designing AI Relationships: A Comprehensive Playbook for Design in the Agentic Era", "date": "2025-04-23T13:02:51.800Z"}], "date": "2025-04-23T13:02:51.800Z", "id": "relationship-design-interaction-typology", "core_question": "Am I designing this AI interaction with the right model of what the relationship actually is, or am I forcing chatbot-era UX onto something that needs fundamentally different design obligations?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "This customer support product is a Human\u2194Agent pairing, not a Human\u2194LLM pairing \u2014 the tell is that it maintains state across sessions, takes autonomous actions on the user's behalf, and builds a behavioral profile over time. That means task-completion rate is the wrong north star metric; what actually matters is trust calibration, friction recovery speed, and whether users expand the agent's autonomy over time rather than restricting it. The current design treats it like a stateless chatbot with extra features, which explains why users keep overriding its suggestions \u2014 there's no transparency mechanism showing the agent's reasoning or confidence, so trust never accumulates.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) Co-Evolutionary Framework", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes human oversight of AI agents from passive supervision to active co-evolution, positioning humans as participants who shape agent behavior through iterative feedback, boundary-setting, and context calibration rather than mere monitors.", "when_to_apply": ["My AI agent has an approval step but users are just clicking 'approve' without reading \u2014 the oversight feels performative", "I'm building an agent that acts on behalf of users and I need to figure out how autonomy should change over time as trust builds", "Our human review process for AI outputs isn't scaling and I need to rethink what humans should actually be doing in the loop", "Users are becoming dependent on our AI agent rather than developing shared understanding with it \u2014 the relationship feels one-directional", "I need to design what happens after an AI agent makes a mistake in an agentic workflow \u2014 not just prevention but recovery and trust repair"], "principles": ["Humans are active co-shapers of agent behavior, not passive supervisors", "Interfaces must make agent states, decisions, and uncertainties legible to humans", "Shared intentionality \u2014 not just task completion \u2014 is the goal of human-agent collaboration", "Autonomy tuning (pause, override, sandbox, evolving contracts) must be explicitly designed, not assumed", "Repair rituals and recovery flows are as important as preventing failures in the first place", "Trust is built longitudinally through follow-through, preference respect, and graceful error recovery"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "161923507", "title": "Stop Designing AI Chatbots, Start Designing AI Relationships: A Comprehensive Playbook for Design in the Agentic Era", "date": "2025-04-23T13:02:51.800Z"}], "date": "2025-04-23T13:02:51.800Z", "id": "human-in-the-loop-hitl-co-evolutionary-framework", "core_question": "Am I designing my human-AI oversight system as a checkbox that catches errors, or as a relationship where humans actively shape how the agent thinks and acts over time?", "category": "ai-governance", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this agent needs a 'human review step' before sending emails on the user's behalf. That's wrong here because a static approval gate doesn't evolve \u2014 the user rubber-stamps after week two and the oversight becomes theater. What's actually needed is an autonomy contract that starts with the agent drafting and the human editing, then shifts to the agent sending routine replies autonomously while flagging novel situations \u2014 with the human's editing patterns continuously recalibrating the agent's judgment boundaries. The human isn't a checkpoint; they're a co-author of the agent's evolving decision policy. And when the agent inevitably sends something wrong, the repair ritual \u2014 how it surfaces the mistake, what it learns, how it re-earns latitude \u2014 matters more than the error itself.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "D-T-R Framework (Distribution, Tokenization, Reward)", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A three-dimensional scoring rubric that assesses how defensible a startup's product is against hyperscaler competition by rating Distribution access, Tokenization readiness, and Reward clarity on a 1\u20135 scale.", "when_to_apply": ["OpenAI or Google just announced a feature that overlaps with my product and I need to figure out if we're dead or differentiated", "I'm deciding which dimension of my AI product to invest in over the next quarter to maximize defensibility", "We're choosing between two market verticals and need to assess which one a hyperscaler is less likely to commoditize", "My startup has strong distribution but the underlying AI capability is getting cheaper every month \u2014 how do I stay ahead?", "I'm evaluating whether to go VC-backed or bootstrapped and need to understand how fast I need to build competitive distance"], "principles": ["Distribution (D): Measure how many target users can adopt without a new vendor approval or procurement cycle; score 1\u20135", "Tokenization (T): Measure what fraction of raw data is instantly model-readable text or code; score 1\u20135", "Reward Clarity (R): Measure whether success can be auto-graded without a human in the loop; score 1\u20135", "Hyperscalers default to T=5 and R=5; their Distribution advantage depends on existing channel embeds (e.g., GitHub, Gmail, iOS)", "Defensibility requires outscoring the giant on at least one lever, then widening that gap faster than they can close it", "Maximize one lever to 5 quickly; raise a second to \u22653 within two quarters; hedge the third with portability", "Trying to max all three levers simultaneously is a burn-rate trap"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161852323", "title": "We killed your startup, lol\u2014what to build when Sam Altman keeps shipping better models", "date": "2025-04-22T13:03:16.917Z"}, {"id": "161576963", "title": "Why the Pipes Suddenly Matter", "date": "2025-04-18T13:02:56.723Z"}], "date": "2025-04-18T13:02:56.723Z", "id": "d-t-r-framework-distribution-tokenization-reward", "core_question": "Can my startup survive when a hyperscaler decides to build exactly what I'm building \u2014 and where specifically am I stronger than them?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This startup scores D=4, T=2, R=3. Their distribution is strong \u2014 they're embedded in Slack workflows and users adopt without procurement. But their core data is messy clinical notes (T=2), which is actually their defense: hyperscalers default to T=5 domains first, so they won't prioritize this vertical for 18+ months. The play is to lock in distribution to 5 and raise reward clarity to 4 by building auto-grading against clinical outcome codes. Trying to also clean up tokenization right now would be a burn-rate trap \u2014 the low T score is a moat, not a weakness.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "King Kong Test", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured five-step fire drill that stress-tests a startup's defensibility by simulating the scenario where a major AI platform ships your exact product for free, forcing you to identify what you still own and design a moat-widening sprint around it.", "when_to_apply": ["OpenAI or Google just announced something uncomfortably close to what we're building and I need to figure out if we're dead", "I'm pitching investors next week and need to honestly assess whether our AI product has any defensibility beyond execution speed", "We're an AI wrapper startup trying to figure out what we actually own that a platform can't replicate", "I need to decide whether to double down on our current product direction or pivot before a hyperscaler enters our vertical", "Our competitive moat argument feels hand-wavy and I want a concrete, scoreable stress test"], "principles": ["Assume the threat is real and immediate \u2014 'OpenAI ships your product free today'", "Score yourself on D-T-R honestly before scoring the giant", "Giants automatically get T=5 and R=5; their D score depends on their existing distribution channels", "Your defensible beachhead is only the lever where you still outscore them", "Speed without exclusivity is acceleration into a wall", "Pivot the roadmap \u2014 not the pitch deck \u2014 when advantage shrinks"], "steps": ["Step 1 \u2014 Give yourself a brutally honest D-T-R score: rate your Distribution (user activation without vendor approval), Tokenization (fraction of raw data that is model-ready), and Reward Clarity (auto-gradable success) each on a 1\u20135 scale", "Step 2 \u2014 Clone the giant's likely score: assign T=5 and R=5 to the hyperscaler by default, and estimate their realistic Distribution score based on existing channel embeds", "Step 3 \u2014 Circle the lever where you still outscore them: identify the single D, T, or R dimension where you have a structural advantage", "Step 4 \u2014 Design a moat-widening sprint: define what you can ship in 90 days to make that lever at least twice as hard to copy (e.g., lock a design partner, deploy edge devices, collect a proprietary reward dataset)", "Step 5 \u2014 Schedule a re-test trigger: re-run the test quarterly or whenever a major platform files about your vertical; if your advantage shrinks, pivot the roadmap"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161852323", "title": "We killed your startup, lol\u2014what to build when Sam Altman keeps shipping better models", "date": "2025-04-22T13:03:16.917Z"}], "date": "2025-04-22T13:03:16.917Z", "id": "king-kong-test", "core_question": "If OpenAI or Google shipped my exact product for free tomorrow, what would I still own \u2014 and is that enough to survive?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This startup's D-T-R scores are D=4 (embedded in 200 dental clinics via hardware), T=2 (using generic medical NLP), R=3 (decent outcome labels). The giant's scores: D=2 (no dental channel), T=5, R=5. The surviving lever is Distribution \u2014 the physical clinic integration. But T=2 means the product's data pipeline is trivially replicable once a giant partners with a dental SaaS. The 90-day sprint must lock exclusive data-sharing agreements with those 200 clinics and deploy edge inference on-prem, converting distribution access into a proprietary tokenization asset. Without that, the D=4 advantage decays to commodity within two platform update cycles.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Mountain Pass Scouting Framework", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-question scouting method for identifying market niches where structural friction in distribution, data tokenization, or reward clarity makes the terrain too costly for hyperscalers to dominate, paired with a five-test field checklist to confirm the pass is real.", "when_to_apply": ["I have a startup idea in a vertical but I'm worried OpenAI or Google will just ship this as a feature \u2014 how do I know if the niche is actually safe?", "We're choosing between two industry verticals to build our AI product in and need to figure out which one has real structural friction versus just being underserved temporarily", "An investor asked me why a hyperscaler can't replicate what we're building in six months and I don't have a rigorous answer", "I'm looking at a messy, regulated industry and trying to figure out if the messiness is a moat or just a sign the market isn't worth entering", "My co-founder wants to build a general-purpose AI tool but I think we should go narrow and vertical \u2014 I need a framework to evaluate which verticals are actually defensible"], "principles": ["Hyperscalers hesitate where data is messy, compliance is thorny, or reward loops require specialist human judgment", "A valid mountain pass must have at least one D-T-R lever that is structurally hard for a hyperscaler (D<3, T\u22642, or R\u22642)", "The sector TAM should be large enough to matter (e.g., >$500M) but the wedge small enough for a tiny team to ship fast", "Answering 'yes' to any two of the three scouting questions signals defensible terrain", "If the map stays flat (no friction answers), keep walking \u2014 don't build on open plains"], "steps": ["Ask the Distribution Friction question: Where do decision-makers sit behind fragmented channels, procurement hoops, or political approval?", "Ask the Data Stubbornness question: Which critical inputs refuse to convert to clean text \u2014 sensors, proprietary file formats, photo or thermal blends?", "Ask the Outcome Ambiguity question: Where does quality still require a licensed human to approve \u2014 IRB boards, underwriters, compliance officers?", "If two or more answers are 'yes,' run the five-test field checklist: Channel Test (domain warm intros required), Converter Test (30%+ of raw data fails without custom ETL), Oracle Test (removing licensed reviewer drops accuracy below tolerance), Token-Shock Test (10x cheaper model barely dents your price), Exit-Clause Test (customer cannot swap to a hyperscaler in <30 days without retraining or refiling)"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161852323", "title": "We killed your startup, lol\u2014what to build when Sam Altman keeps shipping better models", "date": "2025-04-22T13:03:16.917Z"}], "date": "2025-04-22T13:03:16.917Z", "id": "mountain-pass-scouting-framework", "core_question": "Is this niche actually structurally defensible against hyperscalers, or am I just building on open plains where a model update wipes me out?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The pass is real for this veterinary radiology AI startup. Distribution friction is high \u2014 every clinic buys through a different practice management vendor with unique integrations, and the buying committee includes a practice owner who barely checks email. Data stubbornness is severe \u2014 the core inputs are DICOM thermal overlays from eight different ultrasound machines, and 30%+ of raw scans fail without custom ETL. Outcome ambiguity is locked in \u2014 a board-certified veterinary radiologist must sign off on every diagnosis for malpractice reasons. That's three out of three scouting questions answered yes. Running the field checklist confirms it: Token-Shock Test passes because a 10x cheaper foundation model doesn't change the $85 per-read price driven by specialist review costs, and the Exit-Clause Test passes because switching requires re-filing with state veterinary boards and retraining on clinic-specific DICOM pipelines \u2014 that's a 90+ day migration, not a weekend swap.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Bottleneck Framework (Compute, Context, Integration)", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "Classifies the root cause of AI tool frustration into exactly one of three constraint types, enabling targeted plan upgrades rather than indiscriminate spending.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm hitting limits on my AI tool and I'm not sure if I need a bigger plan or a different tool entirely", "I keep copy-pasting data between my apps and ChatGPT and the whole workflow feels broken", "My AI chat keeps forgetting what I said earlier in long sessions and I'm wondering if upgrading will fix that", "I'm comparing AI subscription tiers and the feature differences all sound the same \u2014 I can't tell which one solves my actual problem", "I'm paying for multiple AI tools and suspect I'm solving the same problem twice with none of them doing it well"], "principles": ["Every AI failure traces to one primary bottleneck: Compute (how many heavy requests you get), Context (how much content the model can hold at once), or Integration (proximity of AI to your data and tools)", "Pay to lift the bottleneck that actually blocks you; paying for anything else adds cost without unlocking value", "Fix Integration first when multiple bottlenecks appear \u2014 moving AI next to your data often eliminates perceived compute and context pain", "Compute pain is time-of-day sensitive (vanishes at off-peak hours); context pain is document-size sensitive; integration pain is workflow-topology sensitive", "Each pricing tier is designed to relieve one specific constraint \u2014 identify which constraint matches your problem before choosing a tier"], "steps": ["Observe your AI friction for one day: tally each complaint under C (compute), Cx (context), or In (integration)", "Check compute: Did you wait for a capacity timer this week? If yes, compute is your bottleneck", "Check context: Did you split a file into parts or lose chat memory mid-session? If yes, context is your bottleneck", "Check integration: Did copy-paste steal more than 10 minutes a day? If yes, integration is your bottleneck", "Match your identified bottleneck to the vendor tier that specifically addresses it using the compute-context-integration grid", "Apply the ROI formula: (hourly rate \u00d7 hours saved per month) \u00f7 monthly plan cost \u2014 upgrade only if result exceeds 2\u00d7, lock in at 4\u00d7"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161765743", "title": "You Will Choose Wisely: The Definitive Guide to Picking (and Paying for) an AI Plan in Q2 2025", "date": "2025-04-21T13:02:45.948Z"}], "date": "2025-04-21T13:02:45.948Z", "id": "three-bottleneck-framework-compute-context-integration", "core_question": "My AI tools are frustrating me and I'm tempted to throw money at a higher tier, but I don't know if that will actually fix what's broken \u2014 what's the real bottleneck?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The frustration here is context, not compute. The user keeps splitting long contracts into chunks and losing prior conversation history mid-analysis \u2014 that's document-size sensitivity, not rate-limit pain. Upgrading to a plan with more API calls per hour would waste money; the fix is a tier (or tool) that extends the context window. Before even spending on that, though, check integration: if they're copy-pasting those contract chunks from a document management system into ChatGPT manually, moving to Copilot embedded in their existing workflow might dissolve the context pain entirely by keeping the AI next to the source files.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Rule of Ten Pricing Ladder", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A heuristic for evaluating AI pricing tiers that predicts each rung delivers roughly 10\u00d7 more of the rationed resource at roughly 10\u00d7 the cost, used to sanity-check whether a given tier is priced fairly or suspiciously.", "when_to_apply": ["A new AI subscription tier just launched and the advertised limits seem too generous for the price jump", "I'm trying to decide between two adjacent AI plans and want to know if the more expensive one is worth it for my usage volume", "A vendor claims 'unlimited' usage at a price point that's only 5-10\u00d7 the capped tier below it", "I need to estimate what the next pricing tier will look like before it's announced, based on what the current tiers offer", "My monthly AI usage falls awkwardly between two tier ceilings and I'm not sure which plan to buy"], "principles": ["Each pricing tier up delivers approximately 10\u00d7 more of the vendor's rationed resource (messages, tokens, tool calls, GPU priority)", "Each tier costs approximately 10\u00d7 more than the one below it", "If a plan promises 100\u00d7 of something at the same price, assume a hidden limit or an impending price correction", "Use the current tier's ceiling to estimate the next tier's ceiling: if Plus gives 50 messages, expect Pro near 500", "If your required usage falls between two tier ceilings, skip the middle tier and save for the higher one"], "steps": ["Identify the resource being rationed in your current plan (messages, tokens, tool calls, credits)", "Multiply your current tier's ceiling by 10 to estimate the next tier's ceiling", "Compare that estimate to your actual monthly usage", "If your usage fits inside the estimate, the upgrade math works; if you need more than the estimate, skip to the tier above", "Flag any plan that claims more than 10\u00d7 gains without a price increase as likely to have a hidden cap"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "161765743", "title": "You Will Choose Wisely: The Definitive Guide to Picking (and Paying for) an AI Plan in Q2 2025", "date": "2025-04-21T13:02:45.948Z"}], "date": "2025-04-21T13:02:45.948Z", "id": "rule-of-ten-pricing-ladder", "core_question": "Is this AI pricing tier actually giving me proportionally more value, or am I being manipulated by numbers that don't hold up under scrutiny?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "ChatGPT Plus offers ~80 messages per 3 hours on GPT-4o. Pro costs 10\u00d7 more ($200 vs $20), so the expected ceiling is roughly 800 messages per 3 hours \u2014 and OpenAI advertises 'unlimited,' which violates the Rule of Ten. That signals a hidden throughput cap or priority-queue throttling that kicks in well before true unlimited usage. The honest ceiling is probably somewhere around 500-1000 messages before degradation, which means Pro is fairly priced only if your usage lands in that band \u2014 not if you're imagining literally infinite messages.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Subscription ROI Quick-Math", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "A simple formula and threshold system for deciding whether to keep, upgrade, or cancel an AI subscription based on time-value return.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm paying for ChatGPT Plus, Claude Pro, and Copilot and I'm not sure which ones are actually worth it", "My AI subscription just raised prices and I need to decide if the new cost is still justified", "The project that made me subscribe to this AI tool is over and I'm not sure I still need it", "I'm considering upgrading from a free tier to a paid AI plan and want to know if the math works", "It's end of quarter and I want a quick sanity check on whether my AI tool spending makes sense"], "principles": ["ROI = (hourly rate \u00d7 hours saved per month) \u00f7 monthly plan cost", "Below 2\u00d7 ROI: downgrade or cancel", "Between 2\u00d7 and 4\u00d7 ROI: keep but re-evaluate next quarter", "Above 4\u00d7 ROI: lock in; consider switching to annual billing", "Re-run the test every quarter because GPU prices fall and context windows expand, changing the denominator and numerator over time"], "steps": ["Estimate your effective hourly rate (billing rate for professionals, or time-value estimate for others)", "Track hours saved per month attributable to the specific plan", "Divide (hourly rate \u00d7 hours saved) by the monthly plan cost", "Compare the result to the 2\u00d7/4\u00d7 thresholds to make the keep/cancel/upgrade decision", "Repeat the calculation each quarter"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161765743", "title": "You Will Choose Wisely: The Definitive Guide to Picking (and Paying for) an AI Plan in Q2 2025", "date": "2025-04-21T13:02:45.948Z"}], "date": "2025-04-21T13:02:45.948Z", "id": "ai-subscription-roi-quick-math", "core_question": "Is this AI subscription actually paying for itself in saved time, or am I just paying for the feeling of having access to a premium tool?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The ChatGPT Pro subscription at $200/month scores 1.4\u00d7 ROI. At a $150/hour effective rate, it's saving roughly 1.9 hours per month \u2014 but the Plus plan at $20/month covers 90% of the same use cases and would score 10\u00d7+ ROI on even a single hour saved. The right move isn't canceling AI entirely; it's downgrading to Plus and redirecting the $180/month difference. The Pro-exclusive features (extended thinking, higher rate limits) sound compelling but aren't hitting real workflow hours.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Resume Failure Catalog (9 Categories)", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumerated taxonomy of the nine most common ways AI experience is misrepresented or undersold on resumes, each with distinguishing signals.", "when_to_apply": ["I've listed AI tools and projects on my resume but I'm not getting callbacks and I can't figure out why", "I'm reviewing a candidate's resume and their AI experience feels impressive but I can't tell what they actually did", "I helped build an AI feature at work but I'm not sure how to describe my specific contribution without overclaiming or underclaiming", "My resume mentions GPT, LangChain, and RAG but a mentor told me it reads like a buzzword list", "I did real AI work but it was a side project, not enterprise-scale \u2014 how do I present it without inflating it?"], "principles": ["Vague observers describe interest or curiosity without any evidence of application", "Tool-dumpers list names without explaining the problem solved or outcome achieved", "Inflated generalists use enterprise-scale language for individual or side projects", "Undersold real workers did valuable things but described them too generically to be legible", "Generic contributors use soft verbs like 'collaborated' without specifying their actual contribution", "Buzzword blenders stack jargon to sound technical without clarifying what the AI actually did", "Over-owners claim sole credit for team work, which breaks trust in interviews", "Disconnected wins cite business outcomes without explaining what role AI played", "Prompt-as-product illusionists frame a one-off manual prompt as a shipped product"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161426515", "title": "The AI Resume Survival Guide (for 2025 and Beyond)", "date": "2025-04-16T13:03:32.573Z"}], "date": "2025-04-16T13:03:32.573Z", "id": "ai-resume-failure-catalog-9-categories", "core_question": "My resume says I have AI experience, but something about how I've written it isn't landing \u2014 what's actually wrong with how I'm presenting this work?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The active failure mode is 'disconnected wins.' The bullet reads 'Drove 30% increase in customer retention using AI-powered analytics' \u2014 but there's no trace of what the AI actually did, what model or tool was involved, or what the candidate's role was in building versus consuming it. The business outcome is real but completely decoupled from any legible AI contribution. The fix isn't removing the metric \u2014 it's inserting the mechanism: what AI system, what it did, and what this person specifically built or configured to make it work.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Leverage Map", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of three structural positions where AI can create compounding competitive advantage, used to diagnose where a company actually has strategic leverage.", "when_to_apply": ["We have multiple AI initiatives running but can't articulate why any of them are defensible", "Our AI roadmap feels like a list of features rather than a coherent strategic position", "We're debating whether our advantage is in our data, our integrations, or our understanding of the user problem", "A competitor just shipped a similar AI feature and leadership is panicking \u2014 I need to explain why our position is or isn't at risk", "We're trying to decide whether to invest in acquiring users, building better AI infrastructure, or going deeper into domain-specific workflows"], "principles": ["Advantage in the AI era comes from distribution, middleware, or workflow\u2014not model access", "These three positions are distinct but interdependent: distribution provides data, middleware converts data into behavior, workflow is where value is delivered", "A strategy must be anchored in at least one of the three or it is merely a wish", "Distribution advantage = existing user surfaces and trust; Middleware advantage = ability to turn model output into reliable system behavior; Workflow advantage = deep knowledge of user friction points"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161357674", "title": "You Don't Need Better Models\u2014You Need a Better Strategy", "date": "2025-04-15T13:03:01.769Z"}], "date": "2025-04-15T13:03:01.769Z", "id": "ai-leverage-map", "core_question": "We're investing in AI across the company, but I can't tell where we actually have structural leverage versus where we're just bolting on features anyone could copy.", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This company's AI play sits entirely in the middleware layer \u2014 they've built reliable orchestration that turns noisy model output into consistent, auditable decisions for regulated industries. That's real. But they have no distribution advantage: they're selling through partners who own the customer relationship and the data feedback loop. The workflow layer is also thin \u2014 they're operating on generic templates, not deep domain-specific friction knowledge. The strategic move isn't to improve model quality; it's to acquire a distribution surface (even a narrow one) so the middleware advantage compounds through proprietary data instead of decaying as competitors replicate the orchestration patterns.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Strategy Kernel", "type": "procedure", "description": "An adaptation of Rumelt's diagnosis-guiding policy-coherent action framework applied specifically to AI product strategy, forcing teams to narrow focus and build compounding leverage rather than scattered features.", "when_to_apply": ["We have a dozen AI features in flight and no principled way to decide which ones to keep funding", "Our AI roadmap changes every time a new model drops \u2014 we need a strategy that's durable across capability shifts", "We're shipping AI that gets good reactions in demos but isn't creating any compounding advantage", "Leadership wants to go from scattered AI experiments to a coherent AI product strategy with clear bets", "Our AI initiative has lots of activity and launches but feels like it's not building toward anything"], "principles": ["Diagnosis narrows the problem space by identifying where leverage actually exists, not what is theoretically possible", "Guiding policy is a directional filter\u2014a posture about what game you are playing\u2014not a roadmap or feature list", "Coherent actions reinforce one another and compound over time; disconnected AI features do not constitute a strategy", "Start with the smallest possible wedge that fits the guiding policy and creates a feedback loop", "Kill features that don't compound leverage, regardless of how well they demo", "Treat strategy as a repeating loop, not a one-time planning exercise"], "steps": ["Diagnose your leverage position: Map your business honestly against distribution, middleware, and workflow to identify where you have an unfair advantage in deploying AI", "Set a guiding policy: Articulate a posture that commits to one or more leverage positions and explicitly rules out directions that don't fit\u2014this acts as a filter for all future decisions", "Identify coherent actions: Choose a thin starting wedge aligned to the guiding policy, then sequence subsequent builds so each reinforces the last and creates a feedback loop", "Observe system behavior: Watch deployed AI tools in production, collect signals, and use findings to sharpen middleware, UX, prompts, and fallback logic", "Repeat the loop: Revisit diagnosis regularly (at least quarterly), recommit to or explicitly update the guiding policy, and ship new wedges that reflect current learning"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161357674", "title": "You Don't Need Better Models\u2014You Need a Better Strategy", "date": "2025-04-15T13:03:01.769Z"}], "date": "2025-04-15T13:03:01.769Z", "id": "ai-strategy-kernel", "core_question": "We're shipping AI features but nothing is compounding \u2014 are we actually executing a strategy or just reacting to what's possible?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "The team has shipped an AI summarizer, an AI classifier, and an AI chatbot across three different product surfaces in six months. None share data, none feed each other, and none have created switching costs. The diagnosis reveals the real leverage is in the proprietary workflow data flowing through the middleware layer \u2014 not in any individual AI feature. The guiding policy becomes: every AI capability must plug into and enrich the shared workflow graph. The chatbot stays because it generates structured interaction data that improves the classifier. The summarizer gets killed \u2014 it demos well but operates on public documents and compounds nothing. The next wedge is a feedback loop where classifier corrections from users retrain the model weekly, making the middleware layer progressively harder to replicate.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "AI Execution Architecture Audit", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "Four structured questions to determine whether an organization's structure can actually execute an AI strategy, surfacing ownership gaps, quality blind spots, and iteration bottlenecks.", "when_to_apply": ["We have a solid AI strategy but teams keep stepping on each other and nothing compounds", "AI features ship once and then never meaningfully improve \u2014 they just sit there", "Nobody can tell me who actually decides whether an AI feature is working or should be killed", "Our AI quality conversations go in circles because everyone has a different definition of 'good enough'", "The feedback loop from users back into model improvement feels impossibly slow given our org structure"], "principles": ["Most AI strategy failures are architectural, not intellectual\u2014the structure doesn't support the strategy", "Ownership of AI experimentation must be distinct from ownership of AI infrastructure", "Quality evaluation for AI requires a different mental model than traditional QA because outputs are probabilistic", "Iteration speed is a strategic variable; waterfall delivery structures are incompatible with AI compounding", "Without a named decision-maker and clear criteria, failed AI features persist and erode trust"], "steps": ["Ask: Who owns AI experiments\u2014specifically, who is trusted to put model-backed features in front of users and close the loop back into product?", "Ask: Who judges quality and how\u2014what shared language and benchmarks exist for evaluating AI feature performance in production?", "Ask: How fast can you close the feedback loop\u2014are UX patterns designed to generate useful signals and can the team act on them without a full rewrite?", "Ask: When do you kill something\u2014who owns the call and what criteria trigger it?"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "161357674", "title": "You Don't Need Better Models\u2014You Need a Better Strategy", "date": "2025-04-15T13:03:01.769Z"}], "date": "2025-04-15T13:03:01.769Z", "id": "ai-execution-architecture-audit", "core_question": "We have an AI strategy that makes sense on paper \u2014 so why does everything stall, fragment, or quietly rot once teams try to execute it?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "The strategy doc is sharp \u2014 embed AI across three product surfaces \u2014 but the audit exposes the real problem. Nobody owns the experiments end-to-end: the ML team builds models, product defines requirements, and engineering deploys, but no single person is trusted to put a model-backed feature in front of users and close the loop. Quality evaluation is ad hoc \u2014 the team has no shared language for 'good enough' probabilistic output, so every review cycle turns into a taste debate. The feedback loop takes six weeks because UX wasn't designed to generate usable signals, and retraining requires a full sprint. Two AI features launched eight months ago are still live despite poor engagement because no one has the authority or criteria to kill them. The strategy isn't the problem \u2014 the execution architecture has four structural fractures that guarantee drift.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Seven Tech Workflows", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that decomposes any knowledge work job into seven recurring workflow motions, enabling workers to identify where AI provides leverage and where human judgment remains irreplaceable.", "when_to_apply": ["I want to use AI tools at work but I'm not sure which parts of my job they'd actually improve versus just add noise", "My team is adopting Copilot/ChatGPT and we need to figure out where to point it first for maximum impact", "I'm trying to explain to my manager why my role still matters even though AI can produce drafts of most of my deliverables", "We have friction in how work moves through our team and I want to understand whether it's a people problem or a workflow design problem before layering AI on top", "I keep hearing 'AI will replace knowledge workers' and I want a concrete way to audit which parts of my job are actually vulnerable"], "principles": ["Your job is not your title or output \u2014 it is a set of recurring workflow motions", "AI amplifies clean, intentional motion and multiplies confusion in messy, unexamined motion", "The parts of each workflow that are hardest to describe or tokenize are the parts most resistant to AI replacement (Polanyian tacit knowledge)", "Each workflow has a distinct leverage point where AI can extend human capability without replacing judgment", "Task thinking is a trap; designing the motion is the new competitive advantage"], "steps": [], "categories": [{"name": "Sensemaking", "description": "Turning unstructured, messy inputs into patterns and meaning before strategy or planning begins"}, {"name": "Decision-Making", "description": "Moving from options to clarity by framing tradeoffs, comparing alternatives, and committing to a direction"}, {"name": "Creation & Expression", "description": "Turning internal intention into structured external artifacts others can use or react to"}, {"name": "Collaboration & Handoff", "description": "Turning private context into shared understanding legible to others so they can act on it"}, {"name": "Execution & Iteration", "description": "Turning a plan into sustained progress while maintaining coherence and context across cycles"}, {"name": "Systematization", "description": "Turning one-time effort into structured, reusable patterns that scale across people and time"}, {"name": "Meta-Reflection", "description": "Turning past experience into forward insight by detecting patterns, drift, and lessons across time"}], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161273089", "title": "If You're Worried AI Will Replace You, Read This First", "date": "2025-04-14T13:03:13.806Z"}], "date": "2025-04-14T13:03:13.806Z", "id": "the-seven-tech-workflows", "core_question": "Where exactly in my daily work does AI actually help me, and where am I fooling myself into thinking automation means progress?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "A product manager's job decomposes into seven motions: researching (gathering market signals), synthesizing (turning signals into a thesis), deciding (prioritizing the roadmap), communicating (writing specs and aligning stakeholders), coordinating (keeping cross-functional work in sync), creating (drafting narratives and decks), and reviewing (QA on output quality). AI has massive leverage on researching and first-draft creating \u2014 those are high-volume, tokenizable motions. But the synthesizing motion is where this PM's actual competitive value lives: the judgment call that three weak customer signals plus one churned enterprise account means the pricing model is broken, not the feature set. That insight is tacit, context-soaked, and nearly impossible to prompt for. Introducing AI into the coordinating workflow without first cleaning up the underlying motion \u2014 who owns what, what 'done' means \u2014 would multiply the existing confusion, not reduce it.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Model Lineage Fit Map", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI model evaluation from a competitive leaderboard ('which model is best?') to a lineage-based fit map ('which model's training lineage matches this workflow motion?'), so practitioners choose tools based on structural fit rather than benchmark rankings.", "when_to_apply": ["A new model just launched and everyone's saying it's the best \u2014 but I'm not sure if it's actually better for my specific use case", "I'm building a multi-step AI workflow and need to decide whether to use one model throughout or match different models to different stages", "I keep swapping models every time a new benchmark drops and my outputs are inconsistent \u2014 something about my selection process feels off", "Two models seem roughly equivalent on paper but I need to pick one for a production workflow that requires a specific kind of output", "Someone on my team is arguing we should switch models based on a leaderboard ranking and I need a structured way to push back"], "principles": ["New models don't change the game \u2014 they deepen it by extending a lineage of strengths", "Each model belongs to a lineage shaped by training, tuning, and purpose \u2014 understand the lineage, not just the version", "Ask 'at what workflow?' and 'with what tradeoffs?' before accepting any benchmark claim", "Fit beats hype \u2014 the right model for your workflow outperforms the highest-ranked model applied carelessly", "Model interchangeability is growing, so lineage fit matters more than exact model selection"], "steps": ["When a new model is announced, identify which existing model lineage it descends from", "Map that lineage's known strengths (e.g., reasoning depth, speed, tone, structure) to your workflow motions", "Select the model whose lineage best fits the specific motion you are executing, not the overall highest-ranked model", "When a benchmark claim appears, ask: 'at what task, in what workflow, with what tradeoffs?' before updating your toolset"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "161273089", "title": "If You're Worried AI Will Replace You, Read This First", "date": "2025-04-14T13:03:13.806Z"}], "date": "2025-04-14T13:03:13.806Z", "id": "model-lineage-fit-map", "core_question": "A new model just topped the leaderboard \u2014 should I switch, or is what I'm already using actually a better structural fit for how I work?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The conventional read is that Claude 4 'beat' GPT-5 on reasoning benchmarks, so it must be the better choice for a legal contract review workflow. That's wrong here because the GPT lineage has been shaped by structured output fidelity and instruction-following across long documents \u2014 exactly the workflow motion contract review demands. Claude's lineage optimizes for exploratory reasoning and nuanced tone, which matters more in advisory drafting or research synthesis. The benchmark win is real but irrelevant to this task. The GPT lineage is the structural fit; switching would degrade performance on the motion that actually matters.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "High-Performing AI Team Behaviors", "type": "evaluation_criteria", "description": "An eight-dimension rubric for assessing how well a team integrates AI into collective cognition, distinguishing surface-level adoption from genuine distributed intelligence.", "when_to_apply": ["Two teams have the same AI tools and similar talent but one is dramatically more productive \u2014 I need to figure out what's actually different", "My team members are all using ChatGPT individually but our collective output quality hasn't meaningfully improved", "We're about to stand up a new AI-native team and I want to design their working norms from the start rather than retrofit them later", "I'm running a retrospective focused on our AI practices and need a structured way to identify where we're weakest", "People on my team are generating tons of AI content but we're drowning in options and losing track of what was human-validated versus raw AI output"], "principles": ["AI adoption at the team level requires reshaping coordination, not just individual productivity", "Distributed cognition means team intelligence now lives partly in human-AI interactions, which must be managed explicitly", "High performance requires both generating AI outputs AND maintaining rigorous human judgment over them", "Prompt literacy is a team sport\u2014variance in prompting quality across members degrades collective output quality", "Async-first workflows unlock AI's full value by reducing coordination overhead and enabling continuous progress", "Collective sense-making rituals are necessary because AI dramatically increases the volume of options and content teams must process", "Trust and accountability require explicit metadata and transparency about who prompted, refined, and approved AI content", "Velocity and iteration matter more than perfection, but only after foundational practices are in place"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161067893", "title": "Your Team is Using AI Wrong: The Hidden Pattern Behind High-Performing AI Teams", "date": "2025-04-11T13:00:58.709Z"}], "date": "2025-04-11T13:00:58.709Z", "id": "high-performing-ai-team-behaviors", "core_question": "My team has AI tools but something about how we work together with them isn't clicking \u2014 where exactly is the breakdown between surface-level adoption and genuinely thinking better as a unit?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "This team scores high on individual prompt literacy (Dimension 3) \u2014 most members write solid prompts \u2014 but collapses on collective sense-making (Dimension 6) and attribution transparency (Dimension 7). The tell: everyone generates AI drafts independently, but meetings devolve into comparing five AI-generated options with no shared process for evaluating them, and nobody can trace which outputs were AI-generated versus human-refined. The highest-leverage fix isn't more AI training \u2014 it's installing a weekly synthesis ritual where AI-generated options are triaged against shared criteria, and requiring lightweight provenance metadata on all AI-assisted deliverables.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Low-Performing AI Team Pitfall Catalog", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An eight-mode catalog of failure patterns that prevent teams from realizing gains from AI adoption, each with distinct symptoms, root causes, consequences, and corrective actions.", "when_to_apply": ["My team adopted Copilot/ChatGPT months ago and our velocity hasn't improved \u2014 I can't tell if the tools are the problem or how we're using them", "People on my team are using AI individually but our collaboration quality and decision speed seem to be degrading", "We had a project postmortem and 'AI helped us move fast' was the narrative, but the output quality was clearly worse than pre-AI projects", "Trust is eroding on the team because nobody knows which parts of someone's work are AI-generated and which reflect real judgment", "I need to diagnose whether our AI adoption problem is a tooling issue, a skills gap, or a team coordination breakdown before spending more on training"], "principles": ["AI is not an automatic accelerant \u2014 teams can default to being less productive with AI than without it", "Each failure mode has distinct observable symptoms that distinguish it from adjacent dysfunctions", "Most failures stem from treating AI as an individual shortcut rather than a team coordination challenge", "Option overload is a new failure mode introduced by AI's generative capacity that did not exist before", "Trust erosion from AI is a team-culture problem, not a technology problem, and must be addressed with transparency and accountability", "Lack of reflection means teams never compound their AI learning, causing performance to plateau or decline"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161067893", "title": "Your Team is Using AI Wrong: The Hidden Pattern Behind High-Performing AI Teams", "date": "2025-04-11T13:00:58.709Z"}], "date": "2025-04-11T13:00:58.709Z", "id": "low-performing-ai-team-pitfall-catalog", "core_question": "We adopted AI tools expecting a productivity leap, but something feels off \u2014 are we actually getting worse, and if so, what specifically is going wrong?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The active failure mode is Option Overload. The team's design reviews now take 3x longer because the AI generates fifteen layout variants where the team used to debate two or three. Nobody has a framework for narrowing \u2014 they just keep generating. The tell was the meeting transcript: 'Let's have it try one more approach' appeared eleven times. The consequence is decision paralysis dressed up as thoroughness. The fix isn't fewer AI outputs \u2014 it's establishing evaluation criteria before generation begins, so the AI's generative capacity feeds into a filter rather than flooding it.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Core Barriers to AI Integration Diagnostic", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A three-barrier diagnostic that identifies the root organizational obstacles blocking genuine AI team integration, with signals and breakthrough actions for each.", "when_to_apply": ["My team was excited about AI tools at first but now barely uses them and I can't figure out why", "We've done AI training sessions and people nod along, but nothing changes in their actual day-to-day workflows", "There's visible anxiety or passive resistance when I suggest replacing a manual process with an AI-assisted one", "Nobody on the team seems to know who's responsible when an AI-generated output has problems", "We keep buying AI tools and writing playbooks but adoption plateaus at the same low level every time"], "principles": ["Surface-level best practices fail when deeper organizational barriers are unaddressed", "Misaligned expectations about AI capabilities cause disillusionment before teams develop real competence", "Unclear ownership of AI outputs is sufficient to cause workflow breakdown regardless of tooling quality", "Fear of abandoning familiar patterns is a cognitive and cultural barrier, not a knowledge gap", "Honest self-assessment of which barrier is primary is itself the first breakthrough action"], "steps": ["Diagnose Barrier 1 \u2014 Shallow Understanding: Ask whether the team is regularly surprised by AI outputs, frequently abandons AI after initial enthusiasm, or lacks curiosity about AI capabilities and limits", "Break through Barrier 1: Run dedicated sessions on realistic AI use cases, limitations, and strengths; foster experimental culture with healthy skepticism", "Diagnose Barrier 2 \u2014 Lack of Accountability: Ask whether ownership of AI outputs is ambiguous, roles for prompting/reviewing/approving are undefined, and decisions based on AI content are frequently hesitated over", "Break through Barrier 2: Assign and document explicit accountability for each stage of AI interaction; make ownership visible through public review of successes and failures", "Diagnose Barrier 3 \u2014 Fear of Letting Go: Ask whether the team persistently reverts to old workflows, falls back into synchronous meetings, or expresses anxiety about new AI-driven processes", "Break through Barrier 3: Design small, low-stakes experiments with new workflows; recognize and reward team members who adopt new patterns; iterate and reflect explicitly"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "161067893", "title": "Your Team is Using AI Wrong: The Hidden Pattern Behind High-Performing AI Teams", "date": "2025-04-11T13:00:58.709Z"}], "date": "2025-04-11T13:00:58.709Z", "id": "core-barriers-to-ai-integration-diagnostic", "core_question": "We've given the team AI tools and training, so why does everything keep snapping back to the old way of working?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The primary barrier here is Barrier 2 \u2014 Lack of Accountability. The team actually understands what the AI can do (they demo it enthusiastically), and they aren't afraid of change (they volunteered for the pilot). But when an AI-drafted client proposal goes out with an error, nobody knows whether the person who prompted it, the reviewer, or the project lead owns the mistake. So everyone quietly reverts to writing proposals by hand 'just to be safe.' The fix isn't more training or more encouragement \u2014 it's assigning explicit names to each stage: who prompts, who reviews, who approves, who is accountable when it's wrong.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The AI Paradox", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI capability gaps not as temporary bugs to fix but as structural, durable openings created by Polanyi's Paradox\u2014where AI's linear statistical excellence systematically exposes the tacit knowledge domains where human expertise is irreplaceable and increasingly valuable.", "when_to_apply": ["My team keeps saying our AI tool just needs one more iteration to fully replace the human review step, but each version reveals new edge cases that require judgment", "We're deciding whether to invest in hiring experienced practitioners or in fine-tuning our AI system to close remaining quality gaps", "A competitor claims full automation of a complex professional service and I need to assess whether that's real or whether they're hiding a human layer", "Leadership wants to cut headcount because the AI prototype handles 60-70% of the workflow already and they see the rest as a temporary gap", "I'm trying to identify which roles in my organization will become more valuable as AI adoption increases rather than less"], "principles": ["AI excels at linear, pattern-based tasks but structurally fails at tacit knowledge domains due to next-token prediction architecture", "Polanyi's Paradox is durable: humans cannot fully articulate their tacit knowledge, so AI cannot train on it", "Every AI capability gap is a stable business opportunity, not a temporary gap", "The smarter AI gets, the more it exposes and amplifies the value of tacit human knowledge", "AI produces roughly 60% of a complex task quickly; the remaining 40% requiring tacit knowledge is where durable human roles live", "Disintermediation of human-internet interaction increases, not decreases, the need for human oversight roles"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "160101716", "title": "Finishing What AI Starts: The New Jobs That Will Define the Next Decade", "date": "2025-03-29T00:48:22.560Z"}], "date": "2025-03-29T00:48:22.560Z", "id": "the-ai-paradox", "core_question": "Is this AI capability gap something that will close with the next model release, or is it a structural limitation that creates a durable space for human expertise?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that the AI legal drafting tool will eventually replace junior associates because it already produces 70% of a contract in seconds. That's wrong here because the remaining 30% \u2014 reading the room in a negotiation, knowing which clause a specific judge will scrutinize, sensing when a client's stated objective masks their real concern \u2014 is tacit knowledge that cannot be articulated into training data. Polanyi's Paradox means this isn't a version 2.0 fix; next-token prediction architecturally cannot acquire knowledge that experts themselves cannot verbalize. The durable position is building the firm's value proposition around that tacit layer: AI handles volume and pattern-matching, humans own judgment, context, and trust \u2014 and the faster the AI gets at the first part, the more visible and valuable the second part becomes.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Eight Emerging AI Job Families", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system of eight durable human job families that emerge from structural gaps in AI capability, organized by the specific type of tacit knowledge or system complexity that AI cannot handle.", "when_to_apply": ["We're building our AI-era org chart and need to figure out which human roles to create versus which tasks to automate", "I'm mid-career and trying to figure out which skills will compound in value rather than get automated away", "Our team keeps hiring vague 'AI specialist' roles that don't map to any specific structural need \u2014 we need sharper job definitions", "We're seeing AI output quality issues and need to figure out whether the fix is better models or dedicated human roles at specific failure points", "Leadership wants a workforce planning framework that's grounded in where AI structurally can't perform, not just where it's currently weak"], "principles": ["Each job family corresponds to a specific, recurring crack in AI's ability to handle real-world complexity", "Job families are durable because they are grounded in Polanyi's Paradox, not in current model limitations", "Roles span from technical finishing (Job Family 1) to organizational transformation (Job Family 8), covering the full stack of AI deployment challenges", "Current roles in adjacent domains can transition into these families as AI scales", "Compensation and pricing power in these roles will increase as AI systems become more capable and prolific"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "extraction_incomplete": false, "source_posts": [{"id": "160101716", "title": "Finishing What AI Starts: The New Jobs That Will Define the Next Decade", "date": "2025-03-29T00:48:22.560Z"}], "date": "2025-03-29T00:48:22.560Z", "id": "eight-emerging-ai-job-families", "core_question": "Which human roles will actually gain value as AI scales, and where should I place my bets on hiring, retraining, or career development?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "This team is hiring three 'prompt engineers' \u2014 but the actual bottleneck isn't prompting, it's the gap between what the model outputs and what the client can use. That's Job Family 1: AI Finishing. The role needs someone who can take raw model output \u2014 draft copy, generated code, synthesized research \u2014 and apply the tacit quality judgment that makes it production-ready. The other gap is that nobody owns cross-system coherence: marketing's AI tools contradict product's AI tools. That's a different family entirely \u2014 closer to Job Family 5 or 6, someone who manages the seams between autonomous AI workflows. Renaming the roles and splitting the hire accordingly will prevent the classic failure of stuffing three distinct capability gaps into one generic 'AI person' job description.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Software Era Laws vs. AI Era Laws Comparative Framework", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A side-by-side classification of the strategic principles governing software startups (2000s\u20132010s) versus AI startups (2020s), organized around recurring themes like scale, monetization, moats, and regulation.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI startup's growth strategy looks a lot like Slack's or Dropbox's playbook and I'm wondering if that still works", "We're debating whether to offer a free tier for our AI product or charge from day one", "A founder keeps referencing how Facebook or Instagram scaled as a model for their AI company", "I'm evaluating an AI startup whose pitch deck talks about network effects and winner-take-all but I'm not sure those dynamics apply the same way", "We need to decide whether our moat is user base size or something else entirely in an AI context"], "principles": ["Software-era laws (free/freemium, hyperscaling users, network effects, open ecosystems, winner-take-all via user mass) are not directly transferable to AI-era companies", "AI-era laws center on compute scaling, data network effects, lean teams, strategic alliances, responsible innovation, and distribution dominance", "Core economic forces (network effects, power-law outcomes, scale advantages) persist across eras but manifest through different mechanisms", "Monetization timing has inverted: AI companies must charge earlier due to non-zero marginal cost per query", "The moat has shifted from user lock-in and ecosystems to proprietary data and distribution channels"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "159103155", "title": "Dead as a Dinosaur: 2010's Software Laws Are Gone\u2014Here Are The Laws of the AI Age", "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z"}], "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z", "id": "software-era-laws-vs-ai-era-laws-comparative-framework", "core_question": "Am I building this AI company using a playbook that worked for software startups but will fail me because the underlying economics have fundamentally changed?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This AI startup is running the classic software-era playbook: free tier, land-and-expand, race to millions of users before monetizing. That's wrong here. Every API call has real compute cost, so the 'free' tier is burning cash with no path to unit economics \u2014 the freemium model that worked for Dropbox is a death trap when marginal cost per user is non-zero and scaling. The moat they think they're building through user volume doesn't exist either; their actual defensibility would come from the proprietary dataset they're sitting on and the enterprise distribution deal they haven't prioritized. They need to charge now and invest in data compounding, not user count.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Law of Data Network Effects", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes traditional user-to-user network effects as a data feedback loop where more users generate more training signal, making the AI better, which attracts more users \u2014 a compounding quality advantage rather than a social graph advantage.", "when_to_apply": ["We have a lot of users but I'm not sure that actually protects us from a competitor with a better model", "Should we launch now with a mediocre model to start collecting user data, or wait until the product is polished?", "A well-funded competitor just entered our AI space and I need to understand whether our head start is durable or fragile", "We're designing our AI product and want to know which user interactions to optimize for long-term defensibility rather than just short-term engagement", "Our investors keep saying we have network effects but it feels more like we just have users \u2014 what's the actual compounding mechanism?"], "principles": ["More users \u2192 more queries and feedback \u2192 better model \u2192 attracts more users (compounding quality loop)", "Unlike static user networks, data network effects require continuous model updating to sustain the advantage", "First-mover advantage is amplified when the feedback loop is active at scale", "The quality lead from data network effects can become nearly insurmountable for late entrants"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "159103155", "title": "Dead as a Dinosaur: 2010's Software Laws Are Gone\u2014Here Are The Laws of the AI Age", "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z"}], "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z", "id": "law-of-data-network-effects", "core_question": "Is our AI product's competitive advantage actually compounding with usage, or are we just temporarily ahead on features that any well-funded competitor can replicate?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this AI scheduling assistant has a network effect because it has 2M users. That's wrong. The real question is whether those 2M users are generating training signal that makes the scheduling better in ways a new entrant can't replicate by throwing compute at the problem. Here the answer is yes \u2014 every meeting scheduled produces implicit preference data (which suggestions users accept, which they edit, which time-of-day patterns hold across industries), and the model retrains weekly on this signal. A competitor launching today with a better base model still can't match the contextual accuracy built from 18 months of real-world scheduling decisions. The moat isn't the user count \u2014 it's the compounding quality gap that user count produces through active feedback ingestion.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Full-Stack AI Law", "type": "reframe", "description": "Argues that defensible AI value requires controlling or deeply integrating across all three layers \u2014 data pipelines, model training, and application delivery \u2014 rather than owning just one modular piece.", "when_to_apply": ["We're building an AI product on top of OpenAI/Anthropic APIs and I'm trying to figure out what we actually own if they ship our feature", "Evaluating whether an AI startup's moat is real or just temporary execution speed on a single layer", "Should we invest in building our own model or focus resources on proprietary data collection and application lock-in", "A larger platform player is integrating into our layer \u2014 how do we decide whether to compete, partner, or pivot to control adjacent layers", "Structuring our AI team and trying to decide if we need ML researchers or if we can get away with application engineers using off-the-shelf models"], "principles": ["Controlling data + model + domain integration yields superior and more defensible outcomes than any single layer alone", "Pure application wrappers without proprietary model or data components are easily replicated", "Even infrastructure players (chip makers, cloud providers) benefit from vertical integration into adjacent AI layers", "Interdisciplinary teams combining AI researchers with domain experts are required for true full-stack AI"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "159103155", "title": "Dead as a Dinosaur: 2010's Software Laws Are Gone\u2014Here Are The Laws of the AI Age", "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z"}], "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z", "id": "full-stack-ai-law", "core_question": "Are we building something defensible across the AI stack, or are we just a thin layer that gets absorbed the moment someone above or below us decides to integrate?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This AI radiology startup looks impressive on demos, but it's a classic single-layer trap. They fine-tune an open-source model on publicly available medical imaging datasets and wrap it in a clean UI. None of those three layers \u2014 data, model, application \u2014 are proprietary in a meaningful sense. A full-stack competitor that partners with hospital networks for exclusive imaging data, trains specialized models on that data, and embeds directly into radiologist workflows will make this company irrelevant within 18 months. The defensibility question isn't 'is the product good' \u2014 it's 'which layers do you own, and do they reinforce each other.'", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Law of Distribution Dominance", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes AI competitive strategy around distribution as the primary moat, arguing that as AI capabilities commoditize, the ability to reach users through channels, partnerships, and platform integrations outweighs technical differentiation.", "when_to_apply": ["Our AI product is technically superior but a worse competitor is growing faster through partnerships and integrations", "Deciding whether to allocate next quarter's budget to R&D improvements or go-to-market and channel partnerships", "A platform like Slack, Shopify, or Salesforce wants to partner \u2014 trying to figure out if this is a strategic priority or a distraction", "A technically inferior but well-distributed competitor just raised a big round and we're debating whether they're a real threat", "We keep winning benchmarks and demos but losing deals to companies already embedded in customer workflows"], "principles": ["As AI capabilities converge toward parity, distribution becomes the primary differentiator", "Integrating into widely used platforms accelerates user acquisition faster than technical superiority alone", "Strategic alliances and partnerships are distribution mechanisms, not just funding sources", "Distribution moats compound: early channel dominance makes it harder for technically superior latecomers to displace you"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "159103155", "title": "Dead as a Dinosaur: 2010's Software Laws Are Gone\u2014Here Are The Laws of the AI Age", "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z"}], "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z", "id": "law-of-distribution-dominance", "core_question": "Our AI is technically better than the competition, so why are we losing \u2014 and should we keep investing in the model or in getting it into more hands?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "The technically superior competitor is irrelevant here. The rival that embedded into Salesforce and HubSpot six months ago now processes 40% of the market's workflows \u2014 and every week that passes, their switching costs compound through stored templates, user habits, and integration dependencies. The startup with the better model but no distribution partner is building a product for a shrinking addressable market. The right move is to pause the next model improvement cycle and lock down the two remaining enterprise platform partnerships before they sign exclusives.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Technical Debt Advantage Law", "type": "reframe", "description": "Inverts the conventional view of technical debt as a liability by arguing that AI-assisted refactoring reduces the cost of cleanup, making aggressive speed-over-quality development a rational strategic choice in the AI era.", "when_to_apply": ["My engineering lead wants to pause feature work for a quarter to pay down technical debt, but we're in a land-grab market", "We're evaluating a startup's messy codebase during due diligence and debating whether it's a red flag or irrelevant", "Our team is arguing about code quality standards and I'm wondering if the old rules still apply now that we have AI coding tools", "I need to decide whether to hire senior engineers to refactor or invest in AI-assisted development tools to manage our debt", "We shipped fast with a prototype-quality codebase and now I'm trying to figure out if cleaning it up is actually urgent"], "principles": ["AI tools significantly reduce the time and cost of refactoring, lowering the long-term penalty of technical debt", "Speed of market entry and iteration can outweigh codebase perfection when AI can clean up later", "Technical debt is analogous to human capital debt (hiring quickly) \u2014 acceptable if it funds growth that justifies the cleanup cost", "The calculus changes when AI refactoring assistance is available vs. purely manual remediation"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "159103155", "title": "Dead as a Dinosaur: 2010's Software Laws Are Gone\u2014Here Are The Laws of the AI Age", "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z"}], "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z", "id": "technical-debt-advantage-law", "core_question": "Should we slow down to write clean code, or ship fast and messy knowing AI tools can help us fix it later?", "category": "strategic-planning", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this startup's codebase is a liability \u2014 spaghetti architecture, inconsistent patterns, minimal test coverage. That's wrong here because the team shipped to three enterprise customers in four months while competitors were still architecting. With Copilot-class tools and AI-assisted refactoring, the cleanup cost that would have been 6 engineer-months two years ago is now closer to 6 engineer-weeks. The debt funded market position that's worth far more than the remediation cost. The rational move is to keep shipping and schedule a focused AI-assisted refactoring sprint once product-market fit solidifies.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Human Coordination Constraint Law", "type": "reframe", "description": "Reframes organizational scaling strategy by identifying human-to-human coordination overhead \u2014 not technical complexity \u2014 as the primary bottleneck in AI-era company growth, favoring leaner structures and AI-augmented individual productivity.", "when_to_apply": ["We keep hiring but our velocity isn't increasing \u2014 something is absorbing all the new capacity", "Deciding whether to hire five more engineers or invest in AI dev tooling for the current team", "Our founding team is four people and an advisor says we need to be twelve before Series A", "Two-person competitor is shipping features faster than our twenty-person engineering org", "Restructuring our org chart after realizing most of our time goes to syncs, standups, and cross-team alignment"], "principles": ["AI has automated enough technical work that human coordination costs now dominate organizational inefficiency", "Smaller, more autonomous teams or solo founders with AI assistance can outperform larger coordinated teams on many tasks", "Minimizing coordination overhead through lean structures is a deliberate competitive strategy, not just cost-cutting", "Individual capability augmented by AI tools can substitute for headcount in ways not previously possible"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "159103155", "title": "Dead as a Dinosaur: 2010's Software Laws Are Gone\u2014Here Are The Laws of the AI Age", "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z"}], "date": "2025-03-15T01:40:11.425Z", "id": "human-coordination-constraint-law", "core_question": "Is our slowdown actually a people problem disguised as a scaling problem \u2014 are we adding headcount when we should be eliminating coordination?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "The conventional read is that this 40-person AI startup needs to hire a dedicated ML ops team, a data engineering team, and a platform team to hit their next milestone. That's wrong here because the bottleneck isn't technical capacity \u2014 it's the seven standing meetings, three approval chains, and two-week sprint cycles required to coordinate across existing teams. A restructuring into three autonomous pods of three people each, heavily leveraging AI coding and infrastructure tools, would ship faster than the current org plus 15 new hires. The tell: their two fastest-shipping features last quarter both came from a single engineer working alone with Cursor and Claude over a weekend.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Backward-Designed Multi-Model Workflow", "type": "procedure", "description": "A workflow design method where you define the final deliverable first, then reverse-engineer the sequence of model-matched steps needed to produce it.", "when_to_apply": ["I need to build a pipeline where AI generates a complex document like a research report or PRD end-to-end", "My single mega-prompt keeps producing inconsistent or shallow results on complex tasks", "I want to use different AI models for different parts of a workflow but I'm not sure how to structure the handoffs", "I have a recurring complex deliverable and need a repeatable, debuggable AI workflow rather than ad-hoc prompting", "A downstream step in my AI chain keeps failing and I need to figure out which upstream step is the root cause"], "principles": ["Define the exact final deliverable before choosing any models", "Work backward from the final output to identify necessary intermediate outputs", "Match each sub-task to the model best suited for that type of reasoning", "Plan the handoff format explicitly \u2014 define what schema or structure each step must output for the next step to consume", "Checkpoint and evaluate after each step before proceeding", "Expect iteration: rarely does a chain work perfectly on the first run"], "steps": ["Define the final deliverable in concrete terms (format, sections, length, quality bar)", "Work backward to identify all intermediate outputs required to produce that deliverable", "For each intermediate step, select the model best matched to that sub-task type", "Plan the output format/schema for each step so it cleanly feeds the next step", "Execute the chain step by step, evaluating output at each checkpoint before proceeding", "Iterate: if a downstream step produces poor output, trace back to the upstream step that introduced the problem and revise"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "158478284", "title": "Prompt Chaining Masterclass: How to Orchestrate Multiple AI Models for Maximum Impact", "date": "2025-03-06T02:25:58.874Z"}], "date": "2025-03-06T02:25:58.874Z", "id": "backward-designed-multi-model-workflow", "core_question": "I need AI to produce something complex and high-quality, but no single prompt gets me there \u2014 how do I decompose this into a reliable multi-step pipeline?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The deliverable is a 15-page competitive analysis report with market sizing, competitor profiles, and strategic recommendations. Working backward: the strategic recommendations require a synthesized competitor comparison matrix, which requires individual competitor profiles, which require raw research summaries. Research gathering is a retrieval-heavy task suited to a model with strong factual grounding; competitor profiling is structured extraction best handled by a model that follows schemas precisely; the synthesis layer demands analytical reasoning from a frontier model; and the final prose layer needs a model strong at long-form coherent writing. Each handoff is defined explicitly \u2014 competitor profiles output as structured JSON with fixed fields, so the synthesis step consumes clean, uniform inputs rather than freeform text. When the final recommendations came out generic, the trace pointed to the synthesis step receiving profiles with inconsistent depth, so the upstream profiling prompt was revised with stricter output requirements.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Model-Switching Decision Framework (Attempt-Observe-Decide)", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured approach for determining whether a poor AI output is a prompt problem or a model limitation, and when to switch to a different model.", "when_to_apply": ["I've reprompted twice and the AI keeps making the same kind of error \u2014 is it me or the model?", "The output addresses my request but the quality is mediocre and I can't tell if better prompting would fix it", "I'm about to move from a creative brainstorming phase to a fact-checking or code-generation phase and wondering if I should stay with the same model", "My model started losing coherence, repeating itself, or contradicting earlier parts of the conversation", "I'm working across multiple AI models and need a rule of thumb for when to switch versus when to iterate on my prompt"], "principles": ["If output is off-topic or seems to misunderstand the request, the issue is likely the prompt \u2014 rephrase first", "If output is on-topic but factually wrong, logically flawed, or low quality after a clear prompt, the issue is likely a model limitation", "Don't reprompt more than twice before considering a model switch", "Switch proactively at natural phase changes in the workflow, not just reactively", "Treat model limitations like human limitations: bring in a specialist rather than pushing the wrong tool", "Repetition, context loss, or style degradation signals context window saturation \u2014 switch or reset"], "steps": ["Attempt: give the model a clearly specified sub-task", "Observe: evaluate whether the output failure is comprehension-based (misunderstood the request) or capability-based (understood but produced poor quality)", "If comprehension failure: rephrase the prompt once and retry", "If capability failure after one reprompt: switch to a model with known strength in that task type", "Decide: if transitioning to a qualitatively different phase of work, switch models proactively regardless of current output quality"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "158478284", "title": "Prompt Chaining Masterclass: How to Orchestrate Multiple AI Models for Maximum Impact", "date": "2025-03-06T02:25:58.874Z"}], "date": "2025-03-06T02:25:58.874Z", "id": "model-switching-decision-framework-attempt-observe-decide", "core_question": "Is this bad output telling me to fix my prompt, or is this model just the wrong tool for what I'm asking it to do?", "category": "ai-evaluation", "example_application": "The model understood the request \u2014 it produced a competitive analysis with the right structure, right companies, right framing \u2014 but the financial reasoning is shallow and the margin calculations are wrong. That's a capability failure, not a comprehension failure. Rephrasing the prompt won't fix arithmetic and analytical depth. This task needs a model with stronger quantitative reasoning (e.g., moving from Claude to o1 for the numerical analysis pass), and the one reprompt already attempted confirms the ceiling is the model, not the instruction.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Token Thinking for Chain Design", "type": "reframe", "description": "A mental model that treats AI models as token-prediction engines rather than reasoning agents, using this framing to design better prompt handoffs, manage context budgets, and debug chain failures.", "when_to_apply": ["My multi-step chain works fine for short inputs but breaks down or truncates on longer ones", "The output of one AI step looks fine on its own but the next step in the chain misinterprets or garbles it", "I'm trying to figure out whether to pass the full output between chain steps or summarize it first", "I changed a few words in my prompt and got wildly different results \u2014 trying to understand why", "I need to design the output format of one chain step specifically so the next step can parse it reliably"], "principles": ["AI models predict the next token based on prior tokens \u2014 they do not 'think' in concepts", "Small changes in wording change tokenization and can meaningfully change model output", "Every prompt and output consumes tokens toward the context window limit \u2014 budget explicitly", "The output tokens of step N must set up step N+1 for success \u2014 design output formats with the next model in mind", "Use structured formatting (bullets, numbered lists, delimiters) as token-level cues to guide model continuation", "When a model degrades (repetition, truncation), suspect token limit saturation and intervene by summarizing or resetting"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "158478284", "title": "Prompt Chaining Masterclass: How to Orchestrate Multiple AI Models for Maximum Impact", "date": "2025-03-06T02:25:58.874Z"}], "date": "2025-03-06T02:25:58.874Z", "id": "token-thinking-for-chain-design", "core_question": "Why is my multi-step AI chain producing garbled or degraded output, and how should I structure the handoffs between steps so each model call gets what it needs to succeed?", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The chain is failing at step 3 \u2014 the model is repeating itself and truncating mid-sentence. The conventional read is that the prompt needs better instructions. That's wrong here because the real issue is token-level: steps 1 and 2 are dumping their full verbose outputs into step 3's context, saturating the window before the model can finish generating. The fix isn't prompt rewording \u2014 it's inserting a summarization pass between step 2 and step 3 that compresses the intermediate output to its essential structured fields, freeing token budget for step 3 to actually complete its reasoning. Additionally, step 2's output is in prose paragraphs, but step 3 needs discrete facts \u2014 reformatting step 2's output as a numbered list with delimiters gives step 3 token-level cues that dramatically improve extraction accuracy.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Prompt Chaining Failure Catalog", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumerated set of failure modes specific to multi-model prompt chaining workflows, each with a signal and a mitigation strategy.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompt chain worked fine last month but the outputs have gotten noticeably worse and I haven't changed anything", "The final output of my multi-step workflow has errors but I can't figure out which step is introducing them", "I'm building a pipeline that hands off between multiple AI models and want to know what typically goes wrong", "My chain produces good results sometimes and garbage other times with the same input \u2014 the inconsistency is killing me", "I'm spending a lot on a complex multi-model workflow and wondering if the added steps are actually helping or just adding noise"], "principles": ["Hallucinations in early steps propagate and compound through later steps \u2014 verify at each critical step", "Context overload causes important information to be diluted or lost \u2014 keep context lean and distilled", "Prompts must be adapted per model, not copy-pasted \u2014 each model has different response patterns to the same instruction", "More steps is not always better \u2014 diminishing returns set in and each handoff adds error risk", "Undocumented chains cannot be debugged or reproduced \u2014 treat prompts as valuable artifacts worth saving", "Token cost and latency compound across steps \u2014 use expensive models only where they add unique value"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "158478284", "title": "Prompt Chaining Masterclass: How to Orchestrate Multiple AI Models for Maximum Impact", "date": "2025-03-06T02:25:58.874Z"}], "date": "2025-03-06T02:25:58.874Z", "id": "prompt-chaining-failure-catalog", "core_question": "My multi-step AI chain is producing bad or inconsistent results and I can't tell where it's breaking down or why.", "category": "ai-architecture", "example_application": "The active failure mode is hallucination propagation. Step 1 (research summarization via GPT-4) is generating plausible but fabricated statistics, and Step 2 (analysis via Claude) treats those statistics as verified inputs, building confident but wrong conclusions. The tell: the final output contains specific numbers that don't appear in any source document. The fix isn't at Step 3 where the error becomes visible \u2014 it's inserting a verification gate after Step 1 that cross-checks claims against the original source material before anything flows downstream.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four Pillars of Advanced Prompting", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that organizes advanced LLM prompting techniques into four strategic pillars\u2014Self-Review & Iteration, Multi-Stage & Collaboration, Logic & Precision, and Perspective & Style\u2014each covering distinct families of methods for upgrading AI output quality.", "when_to_apply": ["I know my prompt is too simple but I'm not sure whether I need the AI to self-check, break the task into stages, reason more carefully, or adjust its voice", "My AI output has multiple quality problems at once \u2014 some factual, some structural, some tonal \u2014 and I need to triage which prompting fix addresses which issue", "I'm designing a prompt chain for a production workflow and need to select the right technique family for each stage", "Someone on my team keeps adding role labels and magic words to prompts without understanding why outputs aren't improving", "I want to move beyond basic prompting but the landscape of techniques feels like an unorganized grab bag"], "principles": ["Prompting quality determines output quality more than model selection alone", "Each pillar addresses a distinct failure mode: rough first drafts (Pillar 1), task complexity (Pillar 2), logical errors (Pillar 3), and audience mismatch (Pillar 4)", "Pillars can and should be combined for complex tasks", "Avoid 'magic word' prompting (e.g., role labels without substance); structure and instruction substance matter more", "Iterative refinement consistently outperforms single-pass generation", "External context and memory anchors AI output in factual reality and reduces hallucination"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "156891885", "title": "The Prompt Spellbook: 12 Advanced Prompting Techniques for Methodical Magic", "date": "2025-02-11T02:06:39.338Z"}], "date": "2025-02-11T02:06:39.338Z", "id": "four-pillars-of-advanced-prompting", "core_question": "My AI outputs are mediocre and I know better prompting would fix it, but I don't know which technique to reach for or how the different approaches relate to each other.", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The draft is factually loose and tonally wrong for a technical audience \u2014 two distinct problems requiring two distinct pillars. The hallucination issue is a Logic & Precision problem: the prompt lacks grounding context and explicit constraints, so adding retrieval-augmented context and structured output schemas will anchor the facts. The tone issue is a Perspective & Style problem: the prompt never specified the reader's expertise level or the register, so the model defaulted to generic explainer voice. Fixing only one pillar would leave the output half-broken. Combining chain-of-verification (Pillar 3) with audience-persona specification (Pillar 4) addresses both failure modes in a single pass.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Six-Element Prompt Architecture", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system of six named components that together constitute a well-structured LLM prompt, each with a defined role and tradeoffs.", "when_to_apply": ["My prompt produced a technically correct answer but in completely the wrong format, tone, or level of detail", "I'm building a reusable prompt template for a team and need a checklist of what to include", "The LLM keeps going off-topic or adding filler even though my instruction seems clear", "I'm trying to figure out which part of my prompt to change rather than rewriting the whole thing from scratch", "I want to teach a non-technical colleague how to write effective prompts without overwhelming them"], "principles": ["Role/Persona sets the tone and domain knowledge the model draws on, but is less necessary for deep reasoning models", "Goal/Task Statement must be a single, clear objective to keep the model on track", "Context/References should supply only relevant data \u2014 too much noise buries the model", "Format/Output Requirements must be explicit or the model will choose its own structure", "Examples/Demonstrations teach style and format more efficiently than paragraphs of explanation, but can constrain creativity", "Constraints/Additional Instructions should be used sparingly \u2014 too many cause the model to get confused"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "156137751", "title": "Your Pocket Guide to Prompt Engineering: How to Get the Most from AI Models", "date": "2025-01-31T01:44:09.932Z"}], "date": "2025-01-31T01:44:09.932Z", "id": "six-element-prompt-architecture", "core_question": "My prompts keep producing outputs that miss the mark in ways I can't quite diagnose \u2014 what structural element am I leaving out or getting wrong?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The prompt that produced the rambling, off-format response was missing three of the six elements. It had a goal ('summarize this document') but no role (so the model defaulted to a generic voice), no format requirement (so it chose a five-paragraph essay instead of the bullet-point brief needed), and no constraints (so it included irrelevant background context the user already knew). Adding a role ('You are a senior analyst writing for an executive audience'), an explicit format ('Return 5 bullet points, each under 20 words'), and one constraint ('Omit any background the reader already has \u2014 focus only on what changed this quarter') would close all three gaps. The context element is already handled by the attached document, and examples aren't needed here because the format spec is simple enough on its own.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Common Prompt Pitfalls Catalog", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "An enumerated set of five recurring prompt failure modes, each with a concrete bad example, diagnosis, and corrected alternative.", "when_to_apply": ["The LLM response was vague, rambling, or clearly missed what I wanted but I can't pinpoint what went wrong in my prompt", "I got back a wall of text when I needed a table, or bullets when I needed prose \u2014 the structure is completely wrong", "My prompt asks for several things at once and the model only addressed some of them or blended them together", "I keep iterating on a prompt and the outputs aren't improving \u2014 I need to diagnose the root cause systematically", "Someone on my team wrote a prompt that produced garbage and I need to explain specifically what's broken and how to fix it"], "principles": ["Vague requests produce meandering, unspecific responses \u2014 add scope, time bounds, and word limits", "Multiple demands in one sentence overwhelm the model \u2014 break tasks into sequential steps", "Missing format guidance causes the model to choose an unhelpful structure \u2014 always specify output shape", "Omitting key context forces the model to guess \u2014 provide the actual data or text", "Contradictory instructions create trade-off confusion \u2014 resolve conflicts explicitly or specify a fallback"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "156137751", "title": "Your Pocket Guide to Prompt Engineering: How to Get the Most from AI Models", "date": "2025-01-31T01:44:09.932Z"}], "date": "2025-01-31T01:44:09.932Z", "id": "common-prompt-pitfalls-catalog", "core_question": "My prompt produced a bad result and I can't tell whether the model failed or my prompt failed \u2014 what exactly did I do wrong?", "category": "ai-prompting", "example_application": "The active failure mode is task overload \u2014 the prompt asks the model to 'summarize this article, extract the key quotes, and rewrite it for a Twitter thread' in a single sentence with no sequencing. The tell is that the model produced a messy hybrid: a summary with random quotes interspersed and no thread formatting. Each task needed its own step with explicit output format. The contradictory implicit instruction \u2014 be concise (summary) but comprehensive (extract all key quotes) \u2014 also went unresolved, so the model split the difference poorly.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The 8 Deadly Patterns of AI Failure", "type": "failure_catalog", "description": "A catalog of eight recurring failure modes that cause AI projects to catastrophically fail, each with distinct triggers, real-world examples, warning signs, and prevention strategies.", "when_to_apply": ["We cut scope on testing to hit our AI launch deadline and I'm nervous about what we missed", "Our AI system works great in demos but I'm worried about what happens with messy real-world inputs", "We just had an AI incident in production and need to understand the root cause pattern, not just the surface bug", "The team keeps saying each individual shortcut is fine but I have a gut feeling the accumulated compromises are dangerous", "We're about to scale our AI system from pilot to general availability and I want to know what failure modes to screen for"], "principles": ["AI magnifies small flaws \u2014 individually reasonable compromises compound into catastrophic failures", "AI failures are rarely single bugs; they are cascades of mini-decisions made under pressure", "AI is functionally non-deterministic and should never be validated with deterministic testing assumptions", "Legal and reputational liability from AI failures is generally higher than from traditional software because AI is generative and unpredictable", "Trust, once lost from an AI failure, rarely returns in full and constitutes a real financial cost", "Cheap AI (corner-cut AI) is not cheap \u2014 the hidden costs in liability, reputation, and remediation exceed the savings", "Real-world variability will expose gaps that curated test environments never reveal", "It is functionally impossible to reduce AI risk to zero, so planning for failure is mandatory"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "155052954", "title": "The High Cost of Cheap AI: A Cheatsheet to the Hidden Patterns Behind AI Disasters", "date": "2025-01-18T02:38:44.095Z"}], "date": "2025-01-18T02:38:44.095Z", "id": "the-8-deadly-patterns-of-ai-failure", "core_question": "Are we shipping this AI system with accumulated compromises that look individually harmless but will cascade into catastrophic failure in production?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The active failure pattern here is validation theater \u2014 the team tested the model against a curated dataset that doesn't reflect real-world variability, and they're reporting 96% accuracy as if it means production-ready. The tell: nobody on the team can describe what happens when the model encounters inputs outside the test distribution. Combined with the fact that the launch timeline was pulled forward by three weeks and the red team exercise was cut to a single afternoon, this is a textbook cascade \u2014 each shortcut was individually 'reasonable' but the compound risk profile is now unacceptable. The reputational cost of a public failure here dwarfs whatever was saved by cutting corners on adversarial testing.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Cumulative Tradeoff Tracking Framework", "type": "diagnostic", "description": "A structured approach to auditing the accumulated weight of small compromises in an AI project, recognizing that each tradeoff seems acceptable in isolation but collectively produces systemic risk.", "when_to_apply": ["We've made a lot of small concessions during this AI build and I'm wondering if together they add up to something dangerous", "Our AI system passed all internal tests but is failing unpredictably in production and we can't figure out why", "The team keeps saying 'we'll fix it later' and I'm losing confidence that the accumulated deferrals are still manageable", "We're doing a pre-launch review and I want to stress-test whether the sum of our development shortcuts creates hidden risk", "Someone is arguing that one more small compromise before launch is fine, but my gut says we've already compromised too much"], "principles": ["No single trade-off is an island \u2014 map the cumulative effect of small decisions", "Warning phrases like 'we'll fix it in production' are signals that risk is accumulating", "Overreliance on curated or synthetic data while ignoring real-world volatility is a red flag", "If too many small red flags are visible, slow down rather than proceed", "Track 'tiny' trade-offs explicitly in a decision log so their aggregate weight becomes visible"], "steps": ["Enumerate all compromises made during development (data shortcuts, skipped validations, deferred edge-case handling)", "Identify which compromises were justified by 'we'll fix it later' reasoning", "Assess whether test coverage relies on curated or synthetic data rather than real-world conditions", "Count the number of open red flags and evaluate their aggregate risk, not their individual risk", "If the aggregate risk exceeds an acceptable threshold, halt and address root issues before proceeding"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "155052954", "title": "The High Cost of Cheap AI: A Cheatsheet to the Hidden Patterns Behind AI Disasters", "date": "2025-01-18T02:38:44.095Z"}], "date": "2025-01-18T02:38:44.095Z", "id": "cumulative-tradeoff-tracking-framework", "core_question": "Each compromise we made seemed fine on its own, but have we quietly accumulated so many small shortcuts that this project is now a systemic failure waiting to happen?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "This project has seventeen individually-defensible compromises stacked on top of each other. The training data was augmented with synthetic examples instead of real-world edge cases. Validation on three input categories was deferred to 'post-launch monitoring.' The confidence threshold was relaxed twice to hit accuracy targets on curated benchmarks. No single one of these killed the system \u2014 but together they mean the model has never been tested against the conditions it will actually face. The aggregate risk profile isn't 'seventeen small risks' \u2014 it's one large systemic risk that each shortcut compounded. The tell was the decision log: eleven of the seventeen entries contained some variant of 'we'll address this in production.' That phrase appearing once is a pragmatic call; appearing eleven times is evidence the team has been trading away reliability for velocity without tracking the running tab.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "User-Centered AI Build", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequential product development process that defers AI code generation until after user research, problem framing, and architecture planning are complete, preventing the 'AI death spiral' of messy, misaligned builds.", "when_to_apply": ["I've been prompting an AI coding tool for hours and the codebase is getting more tangled with every iteration", "I have a product idea and access to Cursor/Bolt/Windsurf \u2014 what's the right sequence to go from idea to working product?", "My AI-generated prototype technically runs but users keep saying it doesn't solve their actual problem", "I'm tempted to just type my app idea into an AI coding tool and see what comes out \u2014 is there a better approach?", "I want to use AI throughout the product development process, not just for code generation"], "principles": ["AI amplifies process quality \u2014 good process becomes great, bad process becomes chaos", "AI cannot infer an entire app from a vague sentence; foundational work must be done explicitly", "Use AI as a thought partner and collaborator before using it as a code generator", "Only move to technical architecture and code after users and problems are deeply understood", "Context-rich, specific prompts produce far better code than vague ones"], "steps": ["Step 1 \u2014 Understand users deeply: Use AI to explore a day in the life of your target user, surfacing emotional triggers, friction points, and mental models before any feature or code work", "Step 2 \u2014 Frame the problem, not the solution: Prompt AI as a researcher to surface unexpected insights about why current solutions fail, challenging your assumptions about the market", "Step 3 \u2014 Use AI as a thought partner: Ask AI what you might be overlooking and where user behavior might differ from assumptions; co-write a PRD with AI at this stage", "Step 4 \u2014 Flesh out the frontend: Use a frontend prototyping tool to translate the PRD into a visual design you are proud of before writing any backend code", "Step 5 \u2014 Design system architecture: Prompt AI to design architecture that solves the specific user challenges identified, identifying key technical decisions and tradeoffs; validate the plan with a reasoning-optimized model if needed", "Step 6 \u2014 Write code with full context: Provide the architecture, PRD, and frontend as context when prompting for code, making requests specific and goal-aligned rather than generic"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "154863522", "title": "Stop Jumping to Build: The Systematic Way to use AI to Build Product in 2025", "date": "2025-01-15T02:16:10.410Z"}], "date": "2025-01-15T02:16:10.410Z", "id": "user-centered-ai-build", "core_question": "I have AI coding tools that can generate code fast, but I keep ending up with a tangled mess that doesn't solve the right problem \u2014 how do I structure my process so the speed actually helps instead of hurts?", "category": "product", "example_application": "The founder has already prompted Cursor three times to 'build a scheduling app for busy parents' and each iteration drifts further from coherence \u2014 features contradict each other, the data model keeps breaking, and there's no clear user need anchoring decisions. The root cause is clear: no user research, no problem frame, no architecture plan. The AI is amplifying the absence of process. Before touching code again, the work is: map the parent's actual daily friction (pickup logistics? medication tracking? partner coordination?), identify which existing tools fail and why, write a PRD that specifies the one workflow being solved, prototype the frontend to lock in interaction design, then feed that entire context stack into the code generation prompt. The code prompt goes from 'build a scheduling app' to 'build the carpool coordination flow described in this PRD, using this architecture, matching this Figma screen' \u2014 and the output quality difference is night and day.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Five High-Impact Domains for AI Agent Use-Case Discovery", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that sorts organizational tasks into five categories based on why humans struggle with them, helping leaders identify where AI agents deliver the most value.", "when_to_apply": ["We have a list of 20 potential AI agent use cases and need a principled way to rank which ones will actually deliver transformative value", "Leadership keeps asking why we chose this process for AI automation instead of that one \u2014 I need a clear rationale tied to the nature of the task", "Some of our workflows are just tedious, others are genuinely error-prone, and a few feel humanly impossible at scale \u2014 how do I sort these into the right buckets for different AI approaches?", "We're trying to distinguish between tasks where AI replaces human limitations versus tasks where it just speeds up something humans already do well enough", "Our AI agent pilot succeeded in one department but flopped in another \u2014 I need to understand what made the first use case structurally better suited"], "principles": ["Not every problem is suited for an AI agent \u2014 categorize first, then decide", "Each domain reflects a distinct reason why human performance falls short, not just generic inefficiency", "The 'Impossible' domain targets tasks structurally beyond human scale, not just difficult ones", "The 'Inconsistent' domain targets bias and fatigue risk, not volume alone", "The 'Take Out the Trash' domain captures human preference and morale, not just capability"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "154511354", "title": "Sailing New Waters: The Complete AI Agent Guide for Builders in 2025", "date": "2025-01-09T23:27:36.007Z"}], "date": "2025-01-09T23:27:36.007Z", "id": "five-high-impact-domains-for-ai-agent-use-case-discovery", "core_question": "We know we want to deploy AI agents somewhere in the organization, but where will they actually deliver outsized value versus just being a marginal improvement over what humans already do?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The compliance review backlog falls squarely in the 'Inconsistent' domain \u2014 the task requires applying the same 47-point checklist to every vendor contract, but human reviewers drift after the fifteenth review, miss clauses when fatigued, and apply subjective judgment unevenly across reviewers. That's not a volume problem or a capability gap; it's a bias-and-fatigue problem, which means the AI agent's value isn't speed \u2014 it's uniform application of criteria every single time. Meanwhile, the team's request to automate executive briefing prep is actually 'Take Out the Trash' \u2014 the analysts can do it fine, they just resent it as busywork that pulls them from higher-value analysis, so the ROI there is morale and retention, not accuracy.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Four-Step Agent Deployment Roadmap", "type": "procedure", "description": "A sequential process for leaders to move from identifying AI agent opportunities to sustained, measurable deployment without overreaching.", "when_to_apply": ["We've picked an AI agent use case but need a concrete plan to go from concept to production without boiling the ocean", "Employees are skeptical of a new AI agent \u2014 we need to define exactly what it decides on its own versus what stays with humans", "Our first AI agent pilot worked in one team and now leadership wants to expand it across the org, but we're not sure how to phase that safely", "We deployed an agent three months ago and haven't set up any real monitoring \u2014 how do we retrofit KPIs and drift detection?", "We keep getting pitched on broad AI transformation but need a disciplined, sequential approach that starts small and proves value first"], "principles": ["Start with a tightly scoped, high-impact pain point rather than deploying AI broadly", "Define human-agent decision boundaries explicitly before launch to build trust", "Pilot in one well-defined domain before expanding scope", "Monitor KPIs obsessively \u2014 AI is not a 'set it and forget it' technology", "Build in recalibration triggers for data drift, market changes, or regulatory shifts"], "steps": ["Identify and prioritize the specific organizational pain point with the most measurable, high-impact outcome", "Define decision boundaries \u2014 clarify what the agent decides autonomously versus what escalates to humans, and make agent logic transparent", "Start with a tightly scoped pilot in one domain; expand scope only as confidence and capability grow", "Establish business-outcome KPIs (time saved, error rates, cost reductions, revenue) and monitor them continuously, recalibrating as conditions change"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "154511354", "title": "Sailing New Waters: The Complete AI Agent Guide for Builders in 2025", "date": "2025-01-09T23:27:36.007Z"}], "date": "2025-01-09T23:27:36.007Z", "id": "four-step-agent-deployment-roadmap", "core_question": "We know we want to deploy an AI agent somewhere in the organization, but how do we go from a promising idea to a live, trusted, measurable deployment without overreaching or losing organizational buy-in?", "category": "ai-adoption", "example_application": "The highest-impact pain point is the claims intake queue \u2014 40% of adjuster time goes to structured data extraction that an agent handles well. Decision boundaries: the agent auto-processes claims under $5K with standard documentation; anything above $5K, flagged for fraud indicators, or missing fields escalates to a human adjuster with the agent's reasoning visible. The pilot runs in the auto-claims division only \u2014 no expansion to property or liability until error rates hold below 2% for 60 days. KPIs are adjuster hours recovered, first-touch resolution rate, and escalation accuracy, reviewed weekly with a recalibration trigger if the denial-overturn rate exceeds baseline by more than 10%.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Three Levers of AI Value Creation in SaaS", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of three distinct mechanisms through which software companies can create and capture value in the AI era, helping operators and investors identify which specific lever applies to their market rather than relying on generic AI narratives.", "when_to_apply": ["We're adding AI features to our SaaS product but I can't articulate why they'd be defensible versus what competitors will also ship", "I'm evaluating a SaaS company as an investor and need to distinguish genuine AI-driven value creation from cosmetic feature additions", "We're an incumbent SaaS player and I need to understand whether AI startups in our space are a real threat or noise", "I'm building an AI-native product and need to decide whether to compete on personalization, cost disruption, or workflow replacement", "Our board keeps asking about our 'AI strategy' and I need a sharper framework than 'we're integrating LLMs everywhere'"], "principles": ["Different levers favor different players: insurgents tend to have the edge on customization, incumbents on cost structure, and workflow breakage is fragmented and domain-specific", "Evaluate opportunities through the lens of specific levers rather than broad AI trends", "Identify which lever is most relevant in your specific market before setting strategy", "Ruthlessly assess your relative advantages in pulling each lever given your position as incumbent or insurgent", "Build coherent strategies aligned with lever-specific strengths rather than generic 'adding AI' initiatives", "Lever 1 (Customization at Scale): Using AI to deliver deeply customized software experiences without traditional customization costs, resetting user expectations set by general-purpose AI chat interfaces", "Lever 2 (Cost of Intelligence): Reimagining software ROI delivery by exploiting a million-fold increase in available general intelligence, reshaping unit economics rather than merely cutting costs", "Lever 3 (Workflow Breakage): Deliberately engineering discontinuous, slip-fault-style changes in how work gets done, erasing existing workflows rather than improving them incrementally"], "steps": [], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "152599376", "title": "SaaS in the Age of AI: The Great Software Overhang", "date": "2024-12-05T05:38:17.562Z"}], "date": "2024-12-05T05:38:17.562Z", "id": "the-three-levers-of-ai-value-creation-in-saas", "core_question": "Which specific mechanism of AI value creation actually applies to my software market \u2014 and does my position as incumbent or insurgent give me the right to win on that mechanism?", "category": "competitive-positioning", "example_application": "This vertical HR tech company is sitting on Lever 2 territory, not Lever 1. Their customers don't need deeply customized experiences \u2014 they need the same compliance checks and benefits administration they already get, but the unit economics of delivering that intelligence are collapsing. The incumbent owns the workflow and the data integrations; an insurgent trying to win here on 'better AI customization' is fighting the wrong battle. The real threat to the incumbent is Lever 3 \u2014 someone who eliminates the benefits-administration workflow entirely by having AI agents negotiate directly with carriers. That's where the insurgent advantage lives: no installed base to protect, no existing workflow to cannibalize.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Aquatic Problem Taxonomy", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification system that names distinct problem archetypes using sea creatures as mnemonics, helping problem-solvers diagnose the structural nature of a challenge before attempting to solve it.", "when_to_apply": ["We've tried three different approaches to this problem and none of them worked \u2014 I think we might be misdiagnosing what kind of problem it actually is", "My team is arguing about the right solution but I suspect we haven't even agreed on the nature of the challenge", "I need to explain to leadership why this problem is structurally different from the last one we solved, even though they look similar on the surface", "We're in a retro and everyone has a different theory about what went wrong \u2014 I need a way to name the actual problem archetype so we stop talking past each other", "A solution that worked perfectly for one team's problem is failing for ours, and I want to understand why the problems aren't actually the same type"], "principles": ["Problems have structural types, and correctly identifying the type is prerequisite to choosing the right solution approach", "Different problem types require fundamentally different strategies rather than a one-size-fits-all method", "Naming a problem type creates shared vocabulary that accelerates team alignment", "The categories are mutually exclusive enough to be diagnostic but the taxonomy is open and extensible"], "steps": [], "categories": [{"name": "Goldfish problem", "definition": "A problem where the answer is dependent on contextual assumptions that aren't defined; the solution space changes depending on which implicit assumption you adopt"}, {"name": "Eel problem", "definition": "A problem where the root cause is unknown and may be fundamentally difficult to discover"}, {"name": "Tuna problem", "definition": "A systemic problem where everyone is micro-incentivized to avoid solving it but the group as a whole would benefit from a solution"}, {"name": "Orca problem", "definition": "A positive-sum problem where there is no upper limit on complexity; treating it as zero-sum leads to underinvestment in solutions"}, {"name": "Snow crab problem", "definition": "A problem with hidden scale breakpoints that yield large, discontinuous changes in solution elasticity when a threshold is crossed"}, {"name": "Shark problem", "definition": "A problem that shows high variance where drivers are understood but exact mechanisms determining outcomes remain a black box"}, {"name": "Blue whale problem", "definition": "A problem where the solution and the measurement of value are disconnected in time, making it hard to know if you've solved it for a very long time"}], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "137302282", "title": "The Goldfish Problem", "date": "2023-09-23T12:00:48.201Z"}], "date": "2023-09-23T12:00:48.201Z", "id": "aquatic-problem-taxonomy", "core_question": "We keep throwing solutions at this problem and they don't stick \u2014 are we even correctly understanding what kind of problem this is?", "category": "problem-diagnosis", "example_application": "The team's quarterly retention initiative keeps failing because they're treating it as a Jellyfish \u2014 an amorphous, hard-to-grasp problem needing more data and clarity \u2014 when it's actually an Octopus: a single root cause with eight visible tentacles across onboarding, pricing, support response time, and feature gaps. The tell is that every 'retention experiment' targets a different symptom without recognizing they share a common origin. The right move is to stop running parallel experiments on tentacles and trace them back to the body \u2014 the broken first-week experience that poisons every downstream metric.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Engineers Bet with Code, Product Bets with Time", "type": "distinction", "description": "A mental model that distinguishes how engineers and product managers take on fundamentally different kinds of bets \u2014 engineers place constrained technical bets executed in code, while product managers place open-ended business bets wagered with team time \u2014 resulting in different risk profiles, hiring stakes, and career dynamics.", "when_to_apply": ["We're debating whether a bad PM hire or a bad engineering hire is more costly to the business", "Our postmortem keeps blaming engineering for a launch failure but the real problem was building the wrong thing", "I'm trying to explain to leadership why product management hiring decisions deserve as much rigor as staff engineer hires", "An engineer on my team is terrified of a deployment failure but seems unconcerned about whether we're building the right feature \u2014 and I want to reframe where the real risk sits", "We're restructuring and need to decide how much autonomy PMs should have over directing engineering capacity"], "principles": ["Engineers place bets with code: they formulate technical options, recommend which to take, and execute to ensure reliable performance at scale", "Product managers place bets with time: they direct the work of many engineers toward a business objective, making the stakes multiplicatively larger", "Engineering bets are chained to product bets \u2014 a good engineering bet raises the ceiling for the product bet that follows it", "Engineering failures tend to be rare, spectacular, and short-lived with limited top-line business impact (security/compliance breaches excepted)", "Product failures can be existential or trajectory-altering for a business because they represent misdirected person-years of effort", "Code risk is roughly two orders of magnitude less risky for the business than product risk, on average", "The risk profile may reverse for individuals: code failures may carry greater personal career risk than product bet failures"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "135878658", "title": "Engineers bet with code, Product bets with time", "date": "2023-08-10T02:23:07.920Z"}], "date": "2023-08-10T02:23:07.920Z", "id": "engineers-bet-with-code-product-bets-with-time", "core_question": "Why does it feel like my engineering and product counterparts are operating with completely different definitions of risk, and who actually has more on the line?", "category": "org-design", "example_application": "The postmortem blamed the engineering team for the failed launch, but the real bet that lost was the product bet \u2014 the PM directed eight engineers for six months toward a feature that customers didn't want. The code worked perfectly; the time was wasted. That's roughly 4 person-years of misdirected effort, which dwarfs the business cost of a typical engineering outage that gets resolved in hours. The engineer who wrote the flaky service feels more career exposure from the visible incident, but the PM's invisible misallocation was the actual business damage.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Five Communication Keys for Stakeholder Management", "type": "procedure", "description": "A five-principle framework for leveling up stakeholder communications to reclaim decision-making bandwidth and improve decision quality in high-velocity roles.", "when_to_apply": ["I'm approving things all day but I can't tell which ones actually deserved my attention", "I keep saying yes to stakeholder requests and now I have no time left for the work that matters most", "People escalate everything to me and I need a way to push decisions back down without seeming disengaged", "I agonize over decisions after I've made them and waste cycles second-guessing things that were already good enough", "I need to say no to senior stakeholders or customers more often but I don't know how to do it without damaging the relationship"], "principles": ["Learn a thousand ways to say no \u2014 tailor the no to the audience (C-suite vs. IC vs. customer) because social expectations differ", "Learn to say yes in tinier pieces \u2014 break ambiguous or high-level asks into components and identify which are quick low-effort yeses vs. higher-effort decisions requiring more diligence", "Prioritize input artifacts that enable high-quality decisions \u2014 focus scrutiny on documents driving one-way-door decisions; devolve autonomy on two-way-door artifacts to empower the team", "Turn your quality meter from a light switch into a dimmer \u2014 determine whether a decision requires 60%, 80%, or 95% quality and bias the dimmer down to preserve capacity", "Learn to fire and forget \u2014 if due diligence was properly applied or risk was honestly assessed, trust the decision and move on rather than monitoring from uncertainty"], "steps": ["Identify incoming asks and classify them as hard no, partial yes, or full yes", "Break ambiguous asks into components to surface quick wins vs. high-effort decisions", "Determine whether each decision is a one-way door (irreversible, high stakes) or a two-way door (reversible, lower stakes)", "Set the appropriate quality threshold (60%, 80%, or 95%) based on decision type and blast radius", "Apply appropriate scrutiny to input artifacts only for high-stakes decisions; devolve the rest", "Make the decision at the chosen quality level and fire and forget \u2014 do not re-litigate unless the risk assessment changes"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "120831970", "title": "The answer to life the universe and everything is actually stakeholder communications", "date": "2023-05-11T23:25:22.765Z"}], "date": "2023-05-11T23:25:22.765Z", "id": "five-communication-keys-for-stakeholder-management", "core_question": "I'm drowning in stakeholder requests and decisions \u2014 how do I protect my bandwidth for the things that actually matter without dropping balls or burning relationships?", "category": "leadership", "example_application": "The VP of Engineering just forwarded a Slack thread where three directors are asking for sign-off on different things: a new vendor contract, a team offsite budget, and a microservice naming convention. The vendor contract is a one-way door \u2014 switching costs are real and the security implications are permanent \u2014 so that gets 95% quality scrutiny on the input artifacts. The offsite budget is a two-way door at moderate stakes, so the right move is a 60%-quality skim and a fast yes with a spending cap. The naming convention is a full devolve \u2014 the directors own that decision entirely, and re-inserting approval authority here would waste everyone's time. That's three decisions triaged in under two minutes, and only one of them actually requires deep attention. The 'no' to the naming convention request isn't phrased as rejection \u2014 it's framed as trust: 'You're closer to this than I am, run with it.'", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Problem Space Mud Wrestling", "type": "procedure", "description": "A roadmap alignment approach that structures stakeholder conversations around broad problem spaces rather than specific features or problems, extracting only the coarse-grained prioritization input needed to build a defensible roadmap.", "when_to_apply": ["Every stakeholder conversation about the roadmap devolves into debating specific features instead of agreeing on direction", "I need to build a roadmap that satisfies sales, engineering leadership, and customers but they all want different things", "Our roadmap planning process is too heavy \u2014 we're running elaborate workshops and still not getting alignment", "I keep getting mid-quarter requests from execs that derail our roadmap and I have no defensible way to push back", "We have rough strategic direction but I can't get the ten people who matter to converge on priorities without it turning into a feature wishlist"], "principles": ["Structure stakeholder engagement around problem spaces, not specific problems or features \u2014 stakeholders don't need to agree on exact problem definitions to move forward", "Limit stakeholder input to the discrete signal you actually need: rough framing of problem spaces and rough prioritization order", "Keep Figma and detailed solutioning downstream with design and engineering, not in stakeholder alignment sessions", "Use a lightweight walking-around deck (3-4 slides) rather than elaborate facilitated sessions to gather input", "After input is collected, return with a short milestone deck built from problem space priorities, giving Product the latitude to define exact problems and solutions", "Pre-established problem space framing gives you defensible grounds to push back on mid-cycle sideways asks"], "steps": ["Identify the ~10 people in the business who truly move the needle, plus key customers", "Build a 3-4 slide walking-around deck that frames strategic challenges as broad problem spaces (e.g., 'Solve working seamlessly across multi-team orgs') rather than features or specific problems", "Have individual conversations asking stakeholders to help prioritize the listed problem spaces and react to the framing \u2014 gather their perspective on framing and relative priority only", "Synthesize input across stakeholders into a rough shared ordering of problem spaces", "Return with a short 2-slide deck listing key milestones that address the prioritized problem spaces", "Build the detailed roadmap from those milestones, keeping exact problem definition and solutioning within the Product, Design, and Engineering team"], "confidence": "high", "source_posts": [{"id": "104552248", "title": "I got 99 product problems and a vision ain't one...", "date": "2023-02-23T14:38:21.477Z"}], "date": "2023-02-23T14:38:21.477Z", "id": "problem-space-mud-wrestling", "core_question": "How do I get alignment from stakeholders on what we're building next without letting their competing agendas and pet features hijack my roadmap?", "category": "execution", "example_application": "The VP of Sales wants 'SSO for enterprise,' the CTO wants 'API v2 rewrite,' and three customers are asking for different reporting features. These all collapse into two problem spaces: 'Make the product work for large orgs with complex access needs' and 'Give customers visibility into what's happening in their account.' Framed at that altitude, every stakeholder agrees large-org readiness is the top priority \u2014 they just had different feature-shaped entry points into the same strategic need. The roadmap now has a defensible first milestone anchored to that problem space, and when Sales comes back mid-quarter asking for a Slack integration, the response is clear: that doesn't land in either of the two problem spaces we collectively prioritized \u2014 it needs to wait or displace something.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "Three Roadmapping Archetypes", "type": "taxonomy", "description": "A classification of three distinct approaches a PM can take when faced with competing stakeholder constraints on a roadmap, each representing a different level of problem framing sophistication.", "when_to_apply": ["My roadmap feels like a negotiated treaty between stakeholders rather than a coherent strategy", "I keep shipping features that satisfy internal requests but don't move customer or business metrics", "I'm about to kick off quarterly planning and I want to avoid the same reactive pattern we fell into last time", "Leadership is asking why our roadmap has 30 items but no clear narrative about what we're solving", "I ran stakeholder interviews and now I have a pile of competing priorities with no obvious way to structure them"], "principles": ["Feature it up buttercup: treat business constraints as the core problem and ship the fastest politically viable feature, leaving tech debt and customer pain unaddressed", "Death by consensus: run listening sessions across stakeholders, synthesize findings, and build a roadmap that pleases multiple groups \u2014 produces more balanced output but lacks a unifying strategic logic", "Problem space mud wrestling: treat constraints as entry points into larger problem spaces, engage stakeholders only for coarse prioritization input, and build the roadmap top-down from those spaces"], "steps": [], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "104552248", "title": "I got 99 product problems and a vision ain't one...", "date": "2023-02-23T14:38:21.477Z"}], "date": "2023-02-23T14:38:21.477Z", "id": "three-roadmapping-archetypes", "core_question": "Am I building a roadmap that actually solves the right problems, or am I just reacting to whoever is loudest in the room?", "category": "product", "example_application": "This roadmap is a Death by Consensus artifact. The tell: every initiative traces back to a specific stakeholder request, the themes are post-hoc groupings rather than genuine problem spaces, and nothing on the roadmap would survive if you asked 'what customer problem does this solve at scale?' The PM ran a thorough listening tour and synthesized well, but the output is a diplomatic quilt, not a strategy. The move is to step back, identify the two or three problem spaces that actually matter for the business, and rebuild the roadmap top-down from those \u2014 using stakeholder input only to coarse-rank between spaces, not to populate the backlog directly.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
{"name": "The Negative Question", "type": "reframe", "description": "A practice of deliberately asking 'What's the best reason NOT to build this?' before greenlighting any product work, to counter groupthink, train product intuition, and treat each feature as one bet among many.", "when_to_apply": ["Everyone on the team agrees we should build this feature and I can't articulate why that makes me uneasy", "We're about to commit a quarter of engineering to something driven by a few loud customer requests or a competitor launch", "I'm writing up a product spec and realize I haven't seriously considered the case for NOT doing this", "We just shipped something that flopped and I want a practice to prevent reflexive greenlighting in the future", "A stakeholder is pushing hard to build something and the team's counter-arguments feel weak or performative"], "principles": ["Ask 'What's the best reason not to build this?' as a required, serious exercise \u2014 not a rhetorical formality", "Build the strongest possible internal rationale against the build, without resorting to external clickbait justifications (e.g., 'we must or competitor X wins')", "Treat every product decision as a bet, not a certainty \u2014 one chip among many on the table", "Avoid false alternatives and straw men when stress-testing your own rationale", "Record your negative rationale and revisit it in retrospectives to identify patterns and train your product gut over time", "Set high standards for what counts as a valid negative rationale \u2014 easy passes don't count"], "steps": ["Before greenlighting a build, write down the single best reason not to build it", "Stress-test that rationale \u2014 make it as strong as possible without invoking purely external justifications", "Compare the negative rationale against the positive case to assess whether this is the right bet right now", "Proceed with the build decision with both cases explicitly considered", "After launch, revisit the negative rationale in a retrospective and observe what actually played out", "Repeat over time to identify personal patterns and calibrate product intuition"], "confidence": "medium", "source_posts": [{"id": "103782020", "title": "The surprising power of negative questions", "date": "2023-02-19T16:37:11.376Z"}], "date": "2023-02-19T16:37:11.376Z", "id": "the-negative-question", "core_question": "Are we greenlighting this because we've actually stress-tested it, or because momentum and consensus made it feel inevitable?", "category": "decision-making", "example_application": "The team is aligned on building a real-time collaboration feature because three enterprise prospects asked for it. The best reason NOT to build it: the product's core value proposition is async-first deep work, and real-time collaboration pulls engineering and design gravity toward a use case that actively undermines the positioning that won those prospects in the first place. The competitive pressure rationale ('Notion has it') is a straw man \u2014 Notion's users aren't this product's users. With both cases on the table, the bet looks worse than it did ten minutes ago: the positive case rests on three anecdotal requests, while the negative case threatens the strategic core.", "related_frameworks": [], "origin": "extracted"}
